const INTERMEDIATE_STORY = [
  { n: 1, title: "The Calibration", text: "**I. Rooke**\nThe bread was four days old, which meant it had to be cut thin and toasted hard or it would sit in your stomach like ballast. Rooke had learned that the winter she turned nine. She had learned a great deal that winter.\n\nShe worked at the loaf with the good knife, the one Mother Halloway kept wrapped in oilcloth under the sink because Tam had a habit of using knives on things that were not food. Six slices. Six was the number that mattered in this house. She could have got seven out of the loaf if she had cut finer, but seven thin slices left everyone hungrier than six honest ones, and there was a difference between feeding people and merely giving them something to chew.\n\nThe pipe behind the wall knocked. Once, twice, then the long shudder.\n\nRooke did not look up. Nobody in the Under looked up at the knock any more than they looked up at their own heartbeat. Somewhere below the floor of the city, below the sewers and the sumps and the black flooded tunnels nobody had names for, the Great Engine turned over, and every pipe in Gifford answered it. Old men called it the Turn. Old women called it the Turn. Rooke had been calling it the Turn since before she could say the word properly, and she had been eleven years old before she understood that it was a machine and not a large and mostly benevolent animal.\n\nFour knocks and a shudder meant the vent was coming. She counted it out of habit and reached over to shut the window.\n\n\"You're up early.\" Mother Halloway stood in the doorway with her shawl held closed at the throat, grey hair loose, one hand flat on the frame because the stairs cost her more than they used to. \"Or you never went down.\"\n\n\"I went down.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" Halloway crossed to the range and put her palm against the iron, testing whether the fire had held through the night. \"Liar.\"\n\n\"I lay down. I never said I slept.\"\n\n\"Well.\" Halloway took her hand off the iron and rubbed the soot off it onto her skirt without noticing. \"It's the same for all of them, first time. Tessa was sick on the step. Do you remember?\"\n\n\"I remember. She said it was the fish.\"\n\n\"There wasn't any fish.\"\n\n\"I know,\" Rooke said. \"That's how I remember.\"\n\nOutside, the four o'clock vent let go. The Under filled up with it the way it always did: a low roar somewhere over Sootgate, then the hot white fog rolling up Ferrow Lane past the window in a wall, and the whole street went the colour of dishwater for a count of forty. Rooke had walked to work in it a thousand times. You held your breath at the front of it, took short ones in the middle, and if you were carrying anything metal you kept it under your coat because vent-fog would put a skin of rust on bare iron in a single morning. That was the whole reason anyone was called Rustbound. Some brass on the Rise had thought it was clever, a hundred years ago, and it had stuck the way names do when the people they are thrown at decide to keep them.\n\nBehind her the house was starting to wake, which in this house meant a slow rolling grumble from the back room where four of them slept in two beds. Nettle's cough went through it like a nail.\n\nRooke heard it and marked it, the way she marked everything, filing it beside the rent and the price of coke and the number of days until the coal-man's boy came round expecting his chalk settled.\n\nThe cough had been three weeks. Sister Maude had listened to Nettle's chest with a brass horn and said *give it time* in the specific tone that meant *give me money*, which was fair enough, because Maude gave away more than she took and the whole Under knew it.\n\nRooke had done the sum. She had done it the first night and she had done it every night since, and it always came out the same.\n\nFive marks and ten cogs a bottle. Two bottles for the full course.\n\nEleven marks.\n\nShe had been carrying that number around for three weeks the way you carry a stone in your boot.\n\n\"Rooke.\"\n\nShe turned. Halloway was looking at her with the particular flatness she used when she had decided to say a thing and had not decided how.\n\n\"You'll do well today,\" Halloway said.\n\n\"I'll do what I do.\"\n\n\"That isn't what I said.\" Halloway lowered herself into the chair by the range, slowly, one hand on the table. \"I've had eleven through this house sit that exam. Eleven. Do you know how many took a bracket worth having?\"\n\n\"Two.\"\n\n\"Two. And how many took the top?\"\n\n\"None.\"\n\n\"None.\" Halloway nodded, as though the number had settled something between them. \"Because none of them were you.\"\n\nThe griddle spat. Rooke turned the bread with her fingers and did not feel it, because her hands had stopped registering that kind of heat years ago.\n\n\"There's no point in it,\" she said.\n\n\"There's every point in it.\"\n\n\"There's no point in it for me.\" She said it evenly, because she had rehearsed it lying in the dark from third bell onward, running it the way she ran a sum. \"If I take the top bracket they come for me the same afternoon. There's a carriage and a form and a signature and then I'm on the clatterlift going up the Rise with a bag, and that's seven years.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Seven years, Mother.\" Rooke turned round. \"Who does the Colvers' pump? Who does Marta's ledgers? Who counts what the coal-man's boy is skimming off the chalk every week, because it's eight grist a week and he thinks nobody's watching and I'm the only one in this house who can hold a column in her head. Tessa can't. Tessa can barely count her own fingers twice the same and I love her, but she can't.\"\n\n\"Tessa manages.\"\n\n\"Tessa manages because I'm here.\" Rooke's voice did not rise. That was the thing people found unsettling about her, and she knew it, and she used it. \"You manage because I'm here. Nettle gets syrup because I'm here. If I go up the Rise, I get fed and clothed and taught the true calculus by men in gowns, and this house is six weeks behind on the rent by the back end of autumn and out on the step by first frost. I've done the sum. I've done it eleven ways. It comes out the same every way.\"\n\nHalloway was quiet for a long moment. Under them the Turn went round and the pipe knocked twice.\n\n\"And what happens in seven years?\" she said. \"When you're a reckoner with a writ and a wage and you could buy this house outright with what you'd make in a season?\"\n\n\"In seven years Nettle's dead.\"\n\nIt came out flat and fast and far too loud, and it hung in the little kitchen with the smell of burning bread, and Rooke heard herself say it and could not take it back.\n\nFrom the back room, the cough. Three times, then the wet catch, then nothing.\n\n\"That's not certain,\" Halloway said quietly.\n\n\"No,\" Rooke said. \"It's likely. There's a difference and I know the difference. It's my whole trouble, knowing the difference.\"\n\nShe took the bread off the griddle. Six slices, black at the edges now, ruined in the way that meant everyone would eat them anyway and nobody would say a word.\n\n\"I'm going to fail,\" she said. \"Properly. Ninth stratum or lower. Nothing they'd want and nothing they'd look at twice. Then I'm coming home and doing the Colvers' pump, because they're paying a mark and eight cogs for it, and that's a mark and eight cogs against eleven marks.\"\n\nHalloway looked at her for a long time.\n\n\"You know what the cruel thing is,\" the old woman said at last. \"It isn't that you'd do it. It's that you worked out how to.\"\n\n---\n\nTallow Street ran along the bottom edge of the Rise, on the seam, in the part of Gifford where the two halves of the city had to touch each other and neither liked it. The examination hall was a great grey slab of a building with the Wrought crest cut into the lintel: the cogwheel, the open eye and the words underneath in the old script that nobody read any more because everyone had been told what they said. *By reckoning, order.*\n\nSix hundred and forty-one fourteen-year-olds queued down the pavement in the sootfall.\n\nIt was not rain. It looked like rain from a distance and it fell like rain and it was black, and by second bell it had put a fine wet grit on every shoulder in the line. Above them the clatterlifts went up and down the face of the Rise on their cables, six of them, brass cars full of people who had never queued for anything, and the noise they made was the noise the whole seam of the city made all day: a clanking, hauling, industrious racket that meant somebody else was going somewhere.\n\nRooke stood between a boy who would not stop cracking his knuckles and a girl who was mouthing her tables with her eyes shut, and she looked up at the open eye on the lintel and thought: they cut that in the year the Turn started. A hundred and three years of children standing in this queue in this filth, and not one of them ever here because they wanted to be.\n\nSomeone up ahead was crying. Someone further up was being sick.\n\nA clerk came down the line with a box of wooden tokens and a ledger and asked each of them their name, their district and their household, and wrote it down, and pressed a token into their hand with a number burned into the wood.\n\n\"Name.\"\n\n\"Rooke.\"\n\nThe clerk's pen stopped. \"Full name.\"\n\n\"That's the whole of it.\"\n\nHe looked up. Wet collar, grey face, a man three hundred names into a job he would be six hundred names into by noon. \"Household, then.\"\n\n\"Halloway. Ferrow Lane.\"\n\n\"Foster?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe wrote it. He did not look at her again. He put the token in her palm and moved on.\n\nFour four seven one.\n\nRooke turned the token over in her fingers all the way up the steps and into the great cold hall with its six hundred desks in their perfect rows, and thought about how a number is only a number until somebody decides what to do with it.\n\n---\n\n**II. Corvain**\n\"Stand straighter,\" said Lady Adelina Corvain. \"You look like a clerk.\"\n\n\"I am, functionally, a clerk.\"\n\n\"You are an officer of the enforcement division of the Council of Gifford and you are wearing the coat, Elspeth, so stand in it properly.\"\n\nCorvain stood in it properly. It was easier than the alternative, and the coat did demand a certain posture; it had been cut for someone with shoulders, and she had been growing into it for four months without quite arriving.\n\nThey were on the observation gallery, a long balcony of dark wood and brass rail running the width of the hall, thirty feet above the floor. Below them six hundred children were being seated by examiners with clipboards. The sound was extraordinary: not loud, but dense, a mass of small movements and small breaths in a room built to carry them.\n\nThe gallery was full. That was the part she had not expected. She had assumed the Calibration would be administered the way a difficult surgery is administered, quietly and by professionals. Instead half the houses of the Rise had turned out in their good coats to watch it happen, and there were servants moving along the gallery with trays of hot spiced wine, and Lady Fenwick was three seats down telling anyone who would hold still about her nephew's tutors.\n\n\"Why is everyone here?\" Corvain said.\n\n\"Don't say *everyone* as though you were raised in a sump.\"\n\n\"Why is anyone here. It's an examination. It takes four hours. There is nothing to see.\"\n\n\"It is the single most important civic occasion in the calendar and attendance is noticed.\" Her mother did not look at her while she said it. She was scanning the gallery, and Corvain understood that the noticing worked in both directions and that her mother was doing her share of it. \"The Chancellor comes at second hour. Kell makes his address. Afterwards there is a reception at which a great many people who matter will be standing in one room, and you, who have been an officer for four months and have made no impression on anybody, will be standing in it with them.\"\n\n\"I'm here to observe the examination.\"\n\n\"You are here to be seen observing the examination.\"\n\n\"Captain Ashcombe assigned me. There's a duty roster.\"\n\n\"Reeve Ashcombe assigned you because I asked him to,\" her mother said. \"Do stop being provincial. Everyone's posting is arranged by somebody. The only question worth asking is whether the somebody was competent.\"\n\nBelow, a bell rang. Six hundred children sat down at once, and the sound of it was like a wave breaking a long way off.\n\n---\n\nGrandmaster Osric Kell walked to the lectern. He was a tall, dry, grey man of about seventy who moved as though every joint had been individually measured and found adequate. He used no notes.\n\n\"You are here,\" he said, and the acoustics of the hall took his voice and laid it flat and clear across every desk, \"because the Turn requires it.\"\n\nCorvain leaned on the rail.\n\n\"A hundred and three years ago this city was mud and fever and eleven thousand people dying of a bad winter. Then twelve reckoners built the Great Engine, and the Engine gave us light and water and heat and carriage and bread, and it asks one thing of us in exchange. It asks to be understood. Every year it asks. Every year we must answer, or it stops, and the mud comes back.\"\n\nHe paused. Nobody moved.\n\n\"You will be tested for four hours. Figures, shapes, the true calculus insofar as any of you have been taught to reach for it. You will be placed in a stratum according to what you show me. Some of you will go up the Rise. Some will be given writs and permissions and useful work. Some will be given nothing, because you will have shown me nothing.\"\n\nA boy in the second row began, quietly, to cry.\n\n\"That is not cruelty,\" Kell said. \"That is arithmetic. The Turn does not care whether you are hungry. It does not care who your mother was. It asks one question and it asks it of every child in this room in exactly the same words. There is no fairer thing in this city than the paper in front of you. Turn it over.\"\n\nSix hundred papers turned over at once.\n\n\"He believes that,\" Corvain said.\n\n\"Of course he believes it,\" said her mother. \"It's true.\"\n\n---\n\nBy third hour Corvain had stopped watching the room and started watching individual children, because the room had settled into a single texture of scratching pens and there was nothing left in it to see.\n\nIt was a bad habit of hers. Ashcombe had told her so, in the voice he used when he meant the opposite. *You look at the one thing instead of the hundred things. It will make you very good and very unpopular.*\n\nSo she looked at the one thing.\n\nThe one thing was in the third row from the back, on the left, under the high windows. A girl, small for fourteen, dark hair cut short and badly, in a coat mended at both elbows by somebody who could sew and had run out of matching thread.\n\nShe was working the paper backwards.\n\nCorvain watched for a full minute to be sure. The Calibration paper ran easy to hard; that was the entire architecture of it. You began at the front and got as far as you got, and where you stopped was your stratum. Every child in the hall was moving front to back at whatever speed they could manage.\n\nThe girl in the third row from the back had opened her paper at the last page.\n\nShe read the final question. She did not write. She turned back three pages, read something, turned forward again. She sat for perhaps ninety seconds without moving her pen at all, which at third hour of the Calibration was an unthinkable extravagance, and then she began to write, quickly and without pausing, and filled a page and a half, and stopped.\n\nThen she turned to the front of the paper and started question one.\n\n\"Mother.\"\n\n\"Mm.\"\n\n\"Who's the examiner on the west floor?\"\n\n\"How on earth would I know?\"\n\nCorvain came up off the rail and went to find out.\n\n---\n\n**III. Marrow**\nThey put him in the overflow room, which was not a room but the old chapel at the back of the hall, and there were forty-one desks in it, and Marrow counted every one of them, because counting was what he did when he was angry and he had learned a long time ago that it was better than the other things.\n\nForty-one desks. Six hundred in the main hall.\n\nSix hundred and forty-one candidates in Gifford this year, and forty-one of them were in a chapel with a leaking roof, and every one of those forty-one had fur or horns or a snout or a tail. There was no rule anywhere that said it had to be so. Nobody had written it down. That was the trick of it. Nobody had written it down, so there was nothing to point at.\n\nHe sat. The desk was too small; they were all too small, built for human children. Two rows over a Laachmi girl had given up on hers entirely and was writing on her knees with the paper against a book, horns tipped forward, working. Marrow knew her a little, or knew her family; the Laachmi in Sootgate mostly worked the drop-forges, because the forges were the one trade in the Under where being seven feet tall and able to lift an anvil was worth actual money, and because nobody else wanted the work. Her people had a word for themselves that went back further than Gifford did. The brass on the Rise called them horns, or bullheads, and thought they were being witty.\n\nMarrow got his elbows arranged and his tail out of the way of the chair legs and told himself, again, the thing he had been telling himself for a month.\n\n*It does not matter where they sit you. It is the same paper.*\n\nKell had said it himself. Marrow had heard it through the wall, faint but clear enough. *There is no fairer thing in this city than the paper in front of you.*\n\nFine. Then let it be the paper.\n\n---\n\nThe invigilator in the chapel was a young man named Pemberton who had said good morning to nobody and who spent the first hour reading a periodical folded inside his ledger.\n\nThe paper was good. That was the strange thing. Marrow had spent two years preparing for it with a stolen syllabus and a slate with a corner off it and Ketu's kitchen table, and he had expected a wall. It was not a wall. It was a staircase. Every question was one step up from the last, and every step was findable if you were standing on the one below it properly.\n\nHe went through the first two thirds without stopping. Around the ninth section it turned hard, properly hard, hard in a way that put a prickle up the back of his neck, and he had to take the questions apart before he could get anywhere with them. Twice he had to leave a thing and come back to it.\n\nHe got there. Section nine, section ten, the long problem at the end about a load carried across an arch, which he answered wrong the first time and then saw, with a lurch of something close to joy, exactly why it was wrong, and answered again.\n\nHe put the pen down.\n\nThirty-five minutes left. He had never in his life had thirty-five minutes left at the end of anything.\n\nMarrow sat in a leaking chapel at a desk built for someone half his size and looked at four hours of his own handwriting, and let himself think the thought he had refused to think for two years, because thinking it had made it possible to lose.\n\n*I have done it.*\n\nNot the top. He was honest with himself about that; he had worked the old papers and he knew what the top of this looked like, and the top of this was somebody who did not have to slow down at section nine. But the upper strata. The real ones, the ones with writs and placements and a wage paid in silver rather than chalked on a slate at Marta's. Work in the Engine houses. A room with one person in it.\n\nHe thought about Ketu, who had come to Gifford forty years ago because her own people had put her out for reasons she had never once discussed, and who had scrubbed the stairs of a house on the Rise for thirty of those years, and who that morning had put her hand flat on the top of his head and said nothing at all, because she was not a woman who said things, and because there had been nothing to say that would not have sounded like hoping.\n\nHe would tell her tonight. Plainly, the way she liked things said.\n\nThe bell rang.\n\n---\n\nOutside, on the step, in the sootfall, a Wrought officer in a grey coat was stopping candidates as they came out and asking them things.\n\nMarrow watched from under the chapel arch and did not go over, because thirty years of Ketu's advice had settled into him as a single instinct: when the brass are asking, be elsewhere.\n\nThe officer was young. Twenty, perhaps. She had stopped a small girl in a mended coat and she was not shouting at her, which was unusual enough to keep him there.\n\nThe girl said something. The officer did not write it down.\n\nThen the officer said something back, and the girl's face did a thing Marrow recognised at once, because he had felt it on his own face inside the last hour: the look of somebody who has just been shown that a door they had carefully locked was never theirs to lock.\n\nMarrow put his hood up and went home through the vent-fog to tell Ketu he had done it.\n\n---\n\n**IV. Second Examiner Voss**\nThe marking ran through the night. It always did. Six hundred and forty-one papers, twenty-two examiners, eleven hours, and by the fourth of those hours the room upstairs at Tallow Street smelled of cold tea and lamp oil and the particular sourness of people who have agreed not to go home.\n\nAda Voss was on the west floor bundle, desks four hundred through five hundred: a hundred papers and a running tally on the strata sheet at her elbow. She was quick at it. Nine years quick. She could take a Calibration paper and put a stratum on it inside four minutes and be right, and the being right was not a small thing. The strata were checked and re-checked, and an examiner who drifted was an examiner who stopped being an examiner.\n\nShe had done sixty-one papers when she came to four four seven one and everything stopped.\n\nVoss read it through once at speed, the way she read all of them.\n\nThen she set her pen down and read it again properly.\n\nCandidate 4471 had scored in the ninth stratum. Ninth of eleven, which was bad. Ninth was where the guidance said *limited aptitude, no placement, no writ*. Ninth was a girl who would be handed a card in three days and told she was of no use to the Turn and could go home.\n\nThe paper was wrong from beginning to end. That was not the strange part.\n\nThe strange part was *how* it was wrong.\n\nVoss went back to the front. Question one: two numbers, add them. Candidate 4471 had added them incorrectly. She had produced a sum that was out by eleven.\n\nQuestion two. Out by eleven.\n\nQuestion three. Out by eleven.\n\nVoss's mouth went dry.\n\nShe got up, took last year's guidance down off the shelf and did not need to open it, because she knew what it said and had never once had cause to use it. Then she sat down and worked forward through four four seven one with a fresh sheet at her elbow, and she stopped correcting the answers and began reading the errors as though the errors were the work.\n\nBy section four it was unmistakable. The candidate had taken the true value of every answer and then moved it. Not randomly. Not the way a struggling child moves a value, which is to say by dropping a digit or reversing a pair or losing a sign. Every answer had been displaced by a quantity that was itself set by the question number, and the rule was clean, and it held across sixty-eight answers, and it held even in the sections where the candidate was required to show her working.\n\nThe working was what made Voss's hands go cold. The working was beautiful. To get an answer wrong that precisely, and to build a plausible chain of reasoning that arrived at exactly that wrong answer, sixty-eight times running, at speed, under invigilation, you had to solve every question correctly first and then solve a second and considerably harder problem about how to hide it.\n\nVoss thought: this is not a child failing an examination. This is a child sitting a different examination, one she set herself, and passing it.\n\nShe sat back.\n\nThe correct procedure was on the shelf. Log the paper as a suspected irregularity and refer it upward to the Grandmaster's office, where it would be assessed, and if the assessment held the candidate would be brought back for supervised re-examination.\n\nVoss looked at the ninth-stratum tally on the sheet at her elbow, and at the name in the register. *Rooke, no other given. Halloway household, Ferrow Lane.*\n\nFerrow Lane was eight streets from where Voss had grown up. She had sat this examination herself, twenty-six years ago, in the main hall, in a coat her aunt had mended, and she had come out fourth stratum, which had been enough for a clerkship and then a training and then this room. Fourth stratum. She had been quietly proud of it for a quarter of a century.\n\nShe knew exactly what a girl from Ferrow Lane was buying, with eleven, sixty-eight times over.\n\nVoss also knew, because she was not a fool and had worked in this building for nine years, that a referral to the Grandmaster's office was a stone dropped down a well. Kell would assess it correctly, and Kell would be right, and somewhere between Kell's rightness and the girl's front door a great many people with a great many interests would get a look at the file.\n\nSo she did not write it up.\n\nShe wrote a note instead, on a plain sheet, and folded it, and at a quarter past fourth bell she carried it down two floors to the duty room, where the young officer from the observation gallery had left her name and her posting and a request, in a hand far too careful to belong to anybody who had been doing the job long, that any irregularity concerning the west floor be brought to her attention directly.\n\nVoss did not know why she chose the girl in the grey coat over the Grandmaster of Examinations.\n\nShe would think about it, on and off, for years.\n\n---\n\n**V. The Retake**\nThey came for her at second bell, which was worse than coming at night. At night nobody sees. At second bell on Ferrow Lane the whole street was out, the fog had lifted, and the carriage was black with the cogwheel on the door. Tam was on the step. Nettle was at the window. Mother Halloway came out with her shawl held closed at the throat and put herself between Rooke and the two men in grey coats and said, \"On what grounds.\"\n\n\"Examination irregularity,\" said the taller one. \"She's not banked. She's requested.\"\n\n\"Requested where?\"\n\n\"Tallow Street.\"\n\n\"For how long?\"\n\n\"Ma'am, I've a form with a name and an address on it. It doesn't say how long.\"\n\nRooke touched Halloway's arm.\n\n\"It's all right,\" she said.\n\nIt was not all right, and Halloway knew it was not all right, and the whole street watched Rooke walk to the carriage on her own two feet as though she had chosen it, because the alternative was being carried, and there was no version of being carried that Ferrow Lane would ever forget.\n\n---\n\nThe hall was empty.\n\nThat was the first blow and she had not braced for it. Yesterday there had been six hundred of them and the noise of six hundred. Today there were six hundred empty desks in their perfect rows, sootfall on the high windows, and nothing else, and the size of the room had gone from a shelter to an accusation.\n\nOne desk had a paper on it. Third row from the back, on the left, under the windows. Her desk.\n\nThere were four people in the hall.\n\nGrandmaster Osric Kell stood at the lectern with his hands behind his back. On the floor beside her desk stood a woman of about fifty in the black coat of a senior examiner, iron-grey hair scraped into a knot, a pocket watch open in her palm. At the back of the room, near the doors, arms folded, stood the young officer in grey who had stopped Rooke on the step yesterday and asked her, mildly, whether she had found the paper difficult.\n\nThe fourth was Second Examiner Ada Voss, standing a little apart from all of them, not looking at Rooke, hands not quite steady.\n\n\"Candidate four four seven one,\" said Kell. \"Do you know why you are here?\"\n\nRooke's mouth was very dry. She thought about eleven marks. She thought about the Colvers' pump and a mark and eight cogs.\n\n\"No,\" she said.\n\n\"That is a lie,\" said the woman with the watch, \"and we have four hours, so let us not spend them on it.\"\n\nShe came forward. Up close she was shorter than Rooke had thought and her face was all straight lines.\n\n\"My name is Vera Kross. I am Senior Examiner of this hall and I have been marking these papers for thirty-one years.\" She closed the watch with a click. \"Do you know what an examiner actually does, girl? Everyone thinks we count. We don't count. Any clerk can count. We read the errors. A right answer tells you almost nothing; it says *I knew this*. A wrong answer says *here is the exact shape of the hole in me*. Thirty-one years I have been reading holes.\"\n\nShe set the watch down on the desk beside the paper.\n\n\"There is no hole in you,\" Kross said. \"I have read your paper four times. There is a hole in the paper, and it is a perfect one, and it is exactly eleven wide from one end to the other, and no child in the history of this hall has ever been stupid in a straight line.\"\n\nRooke said nothing.\n\n\"So.\" Kross tapped the paper. \"Sit down.\"\n\n\"On what grounds,\" Rooke said, and hated how thin it came out.\n\n\"On the grounds that I do not yet know whether you cheated to fail or cheated to pass and turned clever at the last moment, and it happens to be the same procedure for both. Sit *down*.\"\n\nRooke did not move.\n\n\"Why does it matter?\" she said.\n\n\"I beg your pardon.\"\n\n\"Why does it matter which stratum I'm in.\" Her voice was steadier now, which surprised her. \"You had six hundred and forty-one of us. You'll fill the top out of the Rise the way you do every year. You've got what you need. What does it matter what one girl off Ferrow Lane puts on a piece of paper?\"\n\nKross looked at her a moment. Then she said, without any particular heat:\n\n\"Because I do not permit lies in my hall. Not from the Rise, not from Ferrow Lane, not from you. Everything in this city stands on the reckoning being honest. You put a lie into the reckoning. I don't care what you did it for.\"\n\n\"Senior Examiner,\" said Kell from the lectern, mildly.\n\n\"Grandmaster.\"\n\n\"She asked a fair question and you gave her a true answer that was not the answer.\" Kell came down off the step, slowly, and stopped a few feet away. He looked at Rooke, and for the first time she understood that he was very old and very tired and that the tiredness had not come from last night. \"It matters, child, because the Turn does not care what we intend. Every year it puts a question to this city. Every year we must find, out of six hundred and forty-one children, the ones who can hear it. If a girl who can hear it decides to sit at the back of the hall and pretend she cannot, then the city gets a little worse at answering. And it gets worse at answering every year. And one year we will not be able to answer at all, and on that day the lights go out and the water stops and eleven thousand people die of a bad winter. That is why it matters. Not because you lied. Because we cannot afford you.\"\n\nThe sootfall went on against the high windows.\n\n\"And what do I get?\" Rooke said.\n\nKell blinked. \"I'm sorry?\"\n\n\"You said you can't afford me. All right. What do I get.\" She let her voice go hard. \"There's a house on Ferrow Lane with six people in it and one of them is nine and can't stop coughing, and the syrup is five marks ten a bottle and she needs two. Eleven marks. Nobody in that house has eleven marks. I earn it. Me. So if I sit down at that desk and do it properly, what happens to them?\"\n\nNobody answered.\n\n\"That's the sum,\" Rooke said. \"That's the actual sum, and everybody in this room wants to talk about the Turn and eleven thousand dead a hundred years ago and not one of you has done the actual sum. I did it. Eleven ways. It came out the same eleven times. So I sat down and I put eleven on every line, and I would do it again, and if you'd left me alone I'd be at the Colvers' with a valve-key right now earning a mark and eight cogs of it.\"\n\nFrom the back of the hall, quietly, the officer in grey said:\n\n\"A mark and eight.\"\n\nRooke turned.\n\nThe young woman came forward a few steps, hands in her coat pockets, frowning. Not unkindly. In the manner of somebody working through a piece of arithmetic they do not enjoy.\n\n\"That's what you weighed it against,\" she said. \"A mark and eight cogs, and a pump.\"\n\n\"Eleven marks. And a house.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" The officer stopped. \"I understand the house. I want to be clear that I understand the house, because I am about to say something that will sound as though I don't.\" She took a breath. \"I stopped you on the step yesterday because I watched you open your paper at the back. Every child in that hall went front to back. You read the last question first, sat for ninety seconds, wrote a page and a half without pausing, then went to the front and started being wrong. I have thought about that for a day and a night. You did not fail your examination. You solved it, all of it, inside the first ninety seconds, and then you spent four hours building a lie on top of it.\"\n\n\"So?\"\n\n\"So the lie was the hard part,\" said the officer. \"You did the hard part on purpose. For a mark and eight cogs at a time.\"\n\nShe said it without contempt, which was what got through. Rooke had armour for contempt.\n\n\"Elspeth Corvain,\" the officer said. \"I am the one who had you brought in. Not the Grandmaster. Not the Senior Examiner. Me. So if you want to be angry at somebody, and you should be, be accurate about it.\"\n\nRooke looked at her. Twenty at the most. Good coat. Clean hands. Some house up the Rise with a name and a crest and no cough in it anywhere.\n\n\"You've ruined us,\" Rooke said. \"You know that? You came down off the Rise and you were clever and you've ruined us, and you'll go home tonight and have your supper and feel very fine about the reckoning being honest.\"\n\n\"That's possible,\" said Corvain. \"I have thought about that too.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I did it anyway.\" She held Rooke's eyes. \"Because I watched a fourteen year old spend four hours making herself smaller in front of six hundred people, and I have never in my life been so certain that I was looking at something that was not supposed to happen.\"\n\nSilence.\n\nThen Vera Kross picked up the pocket watch, snapped it open and stood it on the desk face-up.\n\n\"Four hours,\" she said. \"The paper in front of you is not yesterday's paper. It is the reserve, which is harder, because the reserve is written for the eventuality of precisely this and nobody has needed it in nine years. You will sit. You will work. I will be at your shoulder for the whole of it, and Second Examiner Voss at the other, and the Grandmaster at the lectern, and Officer Corvain at the door, and nobody will speak to you and nobody will help you.\"\n\nShe stepped back from the desk.\n\n\"You may lie again if you like,\" Kross said. \"You are very good at it. But understand what you would be doing. Yesterday you lied to a room that was not watching. Today there are three people in this hall who have read your working and know exactly what you can do. If you sit down and put eleven on every line, I will not be fooled and I will not be angry. I will write *ninth stratum* on the form, and you will go home to Ferrow Lane with your mark and eight cogs, and in thirty-one years I will still be able to tell you the precise shape of the hole you decided to become.\"\n\nRooke stood in the empty hall with six hundred desks behind her.\n\nShe thought about Nettle's cough, three times and then the wet catch. About Halloway's hand flat on the table, taking her weight on the way to the chair. About six slices, black at the edges, and how nobody would ever say a word.\n\nShe thought: they are all wrong about what the choice is. They think it is honest against dishonest. It is two houses, and I only get to save one.\n\nThen she thought, with a cold clear lurch that felt like the moment in a sum where the shape of the thing finally shows itself:\n\n*Unless I stop letting them set the question.*\n\nRooke walked to the third row from the back, on the left, under the high windows.\n\nShe pulled out the chair. She sat. She picked up the pen, squared the paper to the edge of the desk the way she squared everything, and looked at the first page of a reserve examination nobody had needed in nine years.\n\n\"Four hours,\" said Vera Kross.\n\n\"I won't need four,\" said Rooke, and turned it over.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter One.*" },
  { n: 2, title: "Results Day", text: "**I. Marrow**\nThey posted the strata at Tallow Street at first bell on the third day, on eleven boards under the lintel, and by the time Marrow got there the crowd went back across the road and halfway up the steps of the coke exchange.\n\nHe had not slept. He had not really slept for three nights. He had lain on the pallet in Ketu's back room listening to the knock in the pipe and doing the arch problem again in his head, over and over, checking it, and every time it came out the way it had come out in the chapel, and every time he thought *good*, and then he thought *unless*, and then he did it again.\n\nThe crowd had a shape to it. Marrow had grown up reading crowds and this one had the shape of a crowd around bad news. There were girls sitting on the kerb with their heads down. There was a woman shouting at a clerk who was not listening, in the practised way of a man whose entire profession is not listening. There was a knot of boys near the second board being loudly delighted, which was worse than the crying.\n\nHe worked his way up the left side, where there was more space, because there was always more space where he was.\n\nEleven boards. First stratum at the top of the lintel, in a frame, with gilt on it. Eleventh at the far end, nailed flat, a single sheet.\n\nMarrow did not go to the eleventh. He was not afraid of the eleventh; he had never for one moment thought he was going to the eleventh. He went to the fourth, because the fourth was where he had put himself in every honest reckoning he had done in two years, and he ran his eye down the column looking for his own name.\n\nIt was not there.\n\nHe read it again. Slower. Bottom to top this time, because eyes lie going downward.\n\nNot there.\n\nHis chest did something unpleasant. He went to the fifth board, and then the sixth, and by the sixth he was not reading properly any more, he was scanning for the shape of his own name the way you scan for a face in a crowd, and it was not on the fifth and it was not on the sixth and it was not on the seventh.\n\nThen a hand came down on his shoulder from behind and he nearly took the man's arm off at the elbow.\n\n\"Steady.\" A clerk. Not Pemberton. Older, heavier, with the grey cuffs of the examinations office and a satchel under one arm. \"You're four one nine, aren't you. The Sirrath boy.\"\n\n\"Marrow.\"\n\n\"Right.\" The clerk looked at him with something that was not quite pity and was not quite anything else. \"You'll not find yourself on the boards. You've an amendment. You want the office, round the side, ask for the counter.\"\n\n\"An amendment.\"\n\n\"Round the side,\" the clerk said, and moved on.\n\n---\n\nThe counter was a hatch in a corridor that smelled of damp plaster and lamp oil, and there was a queue at it of nine people, and every one of the nine had a tail or horns.\n\nMarrow stood in that queue for forty minutes and worked out, somewhere around the twentieth, what he was standing in.\n\nThe Laachmi girl from the chapel was three ahead of him. The one who had written on her knees. She came away from the hatch with a paper in her hand and she did not look at any of them as she passed, and Marrow saw her horns come down and her shoulders go up and knew exactly what was on the paper without reading a word of it.\n\nWhen his turn came the woman at the hatch did not look up.\n\n\"Number.\"\n\n\"Four one nine.\"\n\nPaper. Stamp. The scrape of a drawer.\n\n\"Marrow, no household given, Sootgate district. Sirrath.\" She read it off flat, the way you read a docket. \"Your result is amended.\"\n\n\"To what.\"\n\n\"Ninth stratum.\"\n\nMarrow put both hands on the ledge of the hatch because he was not entirely certain his legs were going to keep doing their job.\n\n\"I was fourth,\" he said.\n\n\"Your assessed result was fourth stratum,\" the woman said. \"Your amended result is ninth. The amendment is on grounds of prior conduct. That's a bank, isn't it. Last winter.\"\n\n\"That was a bank for cogging. I was thirteen. I took a pressure gauge off a scrap barrow and the barrow-man got his money back and the magistrate gave me a caution and eight grist' costs and that was the end of it.\"\n\n\"It's a bank.\"\n\n\"It's a *caution*.\"\n\n\"The form says conduct.\" She turned the paper round and pushed it across the ledge at him. \"Prior conduct, adverse, magistrate's record. Amendment applied. There's the stamp.\"\n\nMarrow looked at the paper.\n\nIt was very short. That was the thing that would stay with him for the rest of his life, longer than the injustice, longer even than the anger: that the whole of it had taken four lines. Two years of Ketu's kitchen table. The stolen syllabus. The slate with the corner off. Section nine and the load across the arch and the lurch of joy when he saw why he was wrong. Four lines and a stamp.\n\n\"Where's the appeal,\" he said.\n\n\"There isn't one.\"\n\n\"There's an appeal for everything. There's an appeal if the coal-man overcharges you eight grist. There's a *form*.\"\n\n\"There's no appeal on an amendment.\" She was already reaching past him for the next docket. \"The amendment is the appeal. Assessed result, adverse conduct, amended result. That's the process working. Next.\"\n\n\"Who signed it.\"\n\n\"Next.\"\n\n\"Who *signed* it.\"\n\nThe woman looked up at him then, properly, for the first time, and Marrow watched her take in the whole of him: the height, the pelt, the white of it, the fact that he was leaning on her ledge with both hands and that his voice had gone somewhere he had not authorised it to go.\n\nShe did not look frightened. She looked tired, and then she looked careful.\n\n\"Sir,\" she said, \"you want to take your paper and go home.\"\n\n---\n\nNinth stratum.\n\nHe walked. He did not choose a direction and he went a long way in it. Down off the seam, down the Grieves Steps two at a time and then not at a time at all, down through the arches under the coke exchange where the drays came through in the mornings, down into the Under where the light went brown and the gas came on at three in the afternoon because it never properly got light down there at all.\n\n*Limited aptitude. No placement. No writ.*\n\nThat was what ninth said. He knew it by heart because everybody knew it by heart. Ninth was the stratum they gave to children who could not hold a column, who could not do a fraction, who had come into that hall at fourteen barely able to read the paper. Ninth was not a punishment. Ninth was a description. It was the city writing down, in a ledger that would follow him until he died, that Marrow of Sootgate had sat the Calibration and shown them nothing.\n\nHe thought: they did not even give me eleventh.\n\nEleventh would have been an insult. Eleventh would have been a thing you could point at. Ninth was *plausible*. Ninth was a number a person like him might genuinely have got. Whoever chose ninth had chosen it carefully, and the care was the part that made him want to put his fist through something, because the care meant a person had sat at a desk with his paper in front of them and thought about it.\n\nHe came out on Cinder Row about the time the gas went on.\n\nThere was a notice board at the top of the Row, the way there was in every district in the Under: cast iron frame, glass front, the Council's own boards for the Council's own notices. Rents. Writ hearings. Water interruptions. And this week, the same as every year in the week after the Calibration, a fresh printed sheet behind the glass, the same sheet on every board in the city.\n\nMarrow stood in front of it in the gaslight and read it.\n\n**BY ORDER OF THE COUNCIL OF GIFFORD**\n\n**The Calibration is complete. The strata are posted. To every candidate: your place is the place your work has earned. The reckoning does not err. The reckoning does not favour. Serve the Turn in the station you have shown yourself fit for, and know that the city stands because the reckoning is honest.**\n\n*By reckoning, order.*\n\nThere was a brick loose in the wall by his knee. He had noticed it on the way past, the way he noticed everything, and filed it, the way he filed everything.\n\nMarrow was not a fool. He knew, taking it out of the wall, exactly what it was worth. He knew that the glass in a Council board cost about two marks to replace, and that breaking one was a bank, and that a second bank for a Sirrath in the Under with an adverse conduct amendment already on his record was not a caution and eight grist' costs, it was six months in the works at Grieves and a mark on him for life.\n\nHe knew all of that. He had the whole sum.\n\nHe did it anyway, and it was the only genuinely stupid thing he had ever done, and for about a second and a half it was the best he had ever felt.\n\nThe glass came out of the frame in one piece and went across the cobbles in nine thousand bits, and the sound of it went up Cinder Row and down it, and somewhere on the corner a whistle started.\n\n---\n\n**II. Tessa Brindle**\nThe carriage came at second bell, black, with the cogwheel on the door.\n\nTessa was on the step with the wash bucket, which is where she had been since first bell, because Mother Halloway had said *do something with your hands* and there had been nothing to do with them except the wash.\n\nShe had known. That was the thing nobody understood afterwards, when they talked about it on Ferrow Lane. They talked as though the carriage had been a surprise. It had not been a surprise since the morning three days ago when the two grey coats had taken Rooke to Tallow Street and Rooke had walked to the carriage on her own feet as though she had chosen it. Tessa had watched her go and had known, in her stomach, before her head caught up.\n\nBecause Rooke came back that night and would not say what she had done.\n\nThat was how you knew. Rooke told you everything. Rooke told you what the coal-man's boy was skimming and how the Colvers' pump worked and why the water on the Row went brown on a Tuesday, whether or not you had asked, whether or not you could follow it, because she could not help herself; things went in and had to come out. And she had come home from Tallow Street at gone eighth bell and eaten her bread and gone to bed and said nothing, and in the morning she got up and did the pump at the Colvers' and came back with a mark and eight cogs and put it in the tin and said nothing.\n\nThree days of nothing.\n\nSo Tessa was on the step with the wash bucket when the carriage came, and she had her hands in cold water up to the elbow, and she did not stand up.\n\nTwo men. Grey coats. Not the same two. One of them had a leather case and the other had a clipboard, and the one with the clipboard did all the talking, and he was, Tessa would remember, perfectly polite.\n\n\"Halloway household?\"\n\n\"Inside,\" Tessa said.\n\n\"Is there a Rooke resident here? No other given name?\"\n\n\"Inside.\"\n\nShe still did not stand up. He waited a moment for her to, and then he went in past her, and the other one went in after him, and Tessa sat on the step with her hands in the bucket and listened.\n\nShe could not make out the words. She made out the shape. A man's voice going on for a long time in the level cadence of somebody reading. Then Mother Halloway, low. Then the man again. Then nothing at all for about a count of thirty.\n\nThen Rooke, very short, three or four words.\n\nThen Halloway again, and Halloway's voice broke in the middle of it, and Tessa put both hands over her ears like a child of six.\n\n---\n\nThey gave her twenty minutes to pack.\n\nTessa stood in the doorway of the back room and watched her do it, and it was the worst twenty minutes of Tessa's life, and afterwards she was ashamed of that, because it was obviously not the worst twenty minutes of Rooke's.\n\nRooke packed like she did everything else. She put the good boots in first because boots go at the bottom. She took two shifts and left three, and Tessa said *take them all*, and Rooke said \"Nettle can grow into these,\" and put them back on the shelf, and Tessa had to go and stand in the passage for a minute.\n\n\"First stratum,\" Tessa said, from the passage.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Out of six hundred and forty-one.\"\n\n\"Out of six hundred and forty-one.\"\n\n\"Are you the top?\"\n\nA pause. \"Yes.\"\n\n\"The actual top? The very top?\"\n\n\"There's a mark for it on the form,\" Rooke said. \"It's a printed form and there's a box on it and someone had to write a number in the box and the number was one. Yes.\"\n\nTessa came back to the doorway. Rooke was folding a shirt with a great deal of attention.\n\n\"Then why do you sound like somebody died,\" Tessa said.\n\nRooke stopped folding.\n\n\"Because I could have had this three days ago,\" she said. \"I could have had this on the first morning without any of it. I sat in that hall and I could have put the true answers down and had this exact form, and I chose not to, and I've had three days to work out that the only thing my choosing bought anybody was three days.\"\n\n\"That's not what happened. They caught you.\"\n\n\"They caught me because I was clever about it,\" Rooke said. \"That's the joke. If I'd been *stupid* about it, if I'd just written any old rubbish down like a normal person having a bad day, nobody would have looked at me twice. I'd be in the ninth with the rest of them and I'd be at the Colvers' tomorrow.\" She put the shirt in the bag. \"I was too good at failing. Do you understand how funny that is? I have thought about almost nothing else for three days and I still cannot decide whether it is funny.\"\n\n\"It isn't,\" said Tessa.\n\n\"No,\" said Rooke. \"Probably not.\"\n\n---\n\nOut on the step Mother Halloway held her by both shoulders and did not say anything useful, because there was nothing useful, and Rooke stood there and let her.\n\nNettle was at the window. Nettle had been told to stay in bed and had not stayed in bed, and Rooke looked up at the window and Nettle put her hand flat on the glass, and Rooke lifted a hand about halfway and then put it down again as though she had thought better of it.\n\nThen she got into the carriage.\n\nShe did not cry.\n\nTessa sat on the step with the wash going cold in the bucket long after the carriage had gone up the lane and turned onto the Wick and out of sight, and the thing that would not leave her alone was that: she did not cry.\n\nBecause Rooke was fourteen, and Tessa was sixteen, and Tessa had cried at everything her whole life, at the fish and at nothing and at the pig they killed on the Row three winters ago, and Rooke had gone up the Rise with a bag holding one pair of boots and two shifts and had not cried, and Tessa understood, sitting there in the cold, that she had never once in six years asked herself what that cost.\n\nThere were now five people in the house and one of them was the eldest.\n\nShe was going to have to learn the pump. She was going to have to learn Marta's ledgers, and she could not hold a column, and Rooke had known that, and had said so out loud in the kitchen three days ago, and had been right.\n\nTessa took her hands out of the water and looked at them, and then she got up and went inside and asked Mother Halloway where Rooke kept the tin.\n\n---\n\n**III. Corvain**\n\"Well,\" said Captain Reeve Ashcombe. \"That's a fine start.\"\n\nHe had the file open on the desk between them and he had read it twice, and he was doing the thing he did with his thumb along his jaw when he was deciding how much of what he thought to say out loud.\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"Don't *sir* me, you're insufferable when you're being modest.\" He turned a page. \"First stratum. Out of six hundred and forty-one. And it would have gone down as ninth if you hadn't stood on a gallery for four hours doing what nobody on that gallery has done in a hundred years, which is *look at the children*.\" He shut the file. \"Kell has written to the division. Did you know that?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"He has. Two paragraphs, dry as a docket, and one of them says that the officer's observation was of a quality he has not encountered from the enforcement division in thirty years of administering the examination. Do you know what Osric Kell's praise is worth in this city?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Neither does he. That's what makes it worth something.\" Ashcombe pushed the file back across the desk. \"So. Commendation on your record, four months in. Your mother will be unbearable. Now tell me why you look like a woman who has swallowed a wasp.\"\n\nCorvain took the file and did not open it.\n\n\"Because of the amendment,\" she said.\n\n\"Which amendment?\"\n\n\"That's rather my point, sir.\"\n\n---\n\nShe had gone looking for the girl's file. That was all. She had wanted to see the reserve paper, out of nothing more respectable than curiosity, because she had stood at the back of that hall for four hours and watched a fourteen year old work in total silence and she had wanted to know what had been on the page.\n\nThe file had been in the records office at Tallow Street, and it had been complete, and it had been closed, and it had been *fast*. That was the first thing she noticed. Assessment, referral, reserve examination, result, placement, carriage docket, all of it stamped and sequenced and finished inside three days, and Corvain had been an officer for four months but she had been Adelina Corvain's daughter for twenty years and she knew exactly how long paper took to move in this city when nobody was pushing it.\n\nThree days. In the enforcement division, three days was how long it took to get a requisition for lamp oil.\n\nSo she had looked at what else had moved through that office in the same three days, on the reasonable grounds that a clerk who can be made to move fast for one file might have been made to move fast for others.\n\nThere were forty-one amendments.\n\nForty-one. All of them dated within the same three days. All of them the same form, the same four lines, the same stamp. Assessed result. Adverse conduct. Amended result.\n\nAnd every single one of the forty-one had gone *down*.\n\n\"That's not necessarily anything,\" Ashcombe said.\n\n\"No, sir.\"\n\n\"Conduct amendments exist. They've existed since the thing was invented. If a child's been banked, it's material. That's not a conspiracy, that's a system with a memory.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir. Except.\"\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"Except a conduct amendment is meant to be applied at assessment, sir. That's the guidance. Conduct is a factor in the assessment, so it goes in when the paper is marked, and the marked result already accounts for it.\" Corvain put the file down. \"These didn't. These were applied *after*. Every one of the forty-one has an assessed result and then an amended result, and the gap between them is somebody looking at a number they didn't like and changing it.\"\n\nAshcombe was quiet.\n\n\"And there's the distribution,\" Corvain said.\n\n\"The what?\"\n\n\"Forty-one amendments, sir. Forty-one non-human candidates sat the Calibration this year. They were all put in the chapel behind the main hall, which is a thing I also did not know until Tuesday.\" She met his eye. \"It's not a coincidence rate. It's a hundred per cent.\"\n\nThe office was very quiet. Outside, a clatterlift went up the face of the Rise with its cable hauling, and the whole building took the vibration of it for a moment, the way every building on the seam did, forty times a day.\n\n\"Every one of them,\" Ashcombe said.\n\n\"Every one of them. And the one at the top of the pile, the one whose assessed result was highest, took the biggest fall. Fourth stratum down to ninth. A Sirrath boy out of Sootgate on a caution for taking a pressure gauge off a barrow when he was thirteen.\" She paused. \"Sir, I've read his file. He got section nine. There were four hundred human candidates in the main hall who didn't get section nine, and they're all in the strata where the marking put them, and he isn't.\"\n\nAshcombe put both hands flat on the desk.\n\n\"Corvain,\" he said. \"Listen to me very carefully, because I'm going to give you the only piece of advice that has ever been any use to me. In this city, being right and being safe are two entirely separate questions, and you have to answer them in that order but you cannot skip the second one.\" He looked at her. \"Are you certain?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Good. That's the right answer.\" He straightened up. \"Then be certain. Properly certain. Not *this looks wrong*, which is what you've got. Certain. Because the moment you take a hundred per cent to anybody above me in this building, somebody is going to ask you whether you've considered that forty-one is a small number and the Under is a rough place and non-human candidates do, statistically, get banked more often, and if you have not got an answer to that question you will spend the rest of your career filing lamp oil requisitions in Sootgate.\"\n\n\"They do get banked more often,\" Corvain said. \"I checked. Three times the rate.\"\n\n\"Then that's the answer they'll give you.\"\n\n\"It isn't an answer, sir. It's a reason. Those are different.\" She picked up the file. \"If conduct is genuinely material and it's genuinely applied at assessment, then a higher bank rate should show up as *lower assessed results*. It doesn't. Their assessed results are all over the strata, same spread as anybody. It only appears afterwards. Whatever's happening in that office is not the system remembering that these children have been banked. It's the system finding out how well they did and then doing something about it.\"\n\nAshcombe looked at her for a long moment.\n\n\"Go and be certain,\" he said. \"Quietly. And Corvain.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"If you're right, you understand what it is you'll be holding.\"\n\n\"A records irregularity, sir.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Ashcombe. \"The Calibration is the only thing in Gifford that everybody, top to bottom, brass and rust, actually believes is fair. It's the one honest thing. It's the reason a woman on Ferrow Lane sends her child up those steps instead of putting a brick through a window.\" He turned to the window. \"If it isn't honest, then there is nothing at all holding the lid on this city, and you will be the person who takes the lid off. So be certain.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Silas Vane**\nVane read the strata the way other men read the racing pages, which is to say from the bottom.\n\nHe had them spread on the long table in the back room over the Ashlight with a glass at his elbow and a lamp turned low, and he had the boards' postings, which anybody could get, and he had the amendment dockets, which nobody could get, and had cost him eleven marks and a favour to a clerk with a chalk problem at three houses on the Wick.\n\n\"You're at it again,\" said Growl, from the door.\n\n\"I am always at it. It is the only thing I do.\" Vane did not look up. \"Do you know what the Calibration is, properly? Everyone thinks it is an examination. It is not an examination. It is the single most thorough talent survey conducted anywhere in the civilised world, run at enormous expense, once a year, by people who then throw ninety per cent of the results in a hole.\"\n\n\"Right,\" said Growl.\n\n\"Six hundred and forty-one children, tested to destruction, sorted, ranked and written down. And the brass take the top few dozen up the Rise, and everything below the fourth stratum they hand back to the Under with a card and a shrug.\" Vane turned a page. \"It is the most generous thing they do. They find them for me. They find them, they measure them, and then they tell them they are worthless, and then they let them walk home.\"\n\nGrowl came in and sat down heavily on the bench by the stove. He was Sirrath, older than Marrow by ten years and rougher by considerably more than that, and he had come down off the same boat as half of Sootgate and had never once pretended he was going to do anything other than this.\n\n\"So who is it this year,\" he said.\n\n\"Three worth the walk.\" Vane laid a finger on the sheet. \"A girl in the fifth out of Grieves who put down a method in section eight that the examiner has annotated as *unorthodox but sound*, which is the closest thing to a love letter you will ever find on a Council form. A Laachmi girl in the sixth. And this.\"\n\nHe turned the docket round.\n\n\"Marrow,\" said Growl. \"Sootgate. I know him a bit. Ketu's lodger.\"\n\n\"Assessed fourth stratum,\" said Vane. \"Amended ninth.\"\n\nGrowl leaned over and read it, and Vane watched him read it, and watched the exact moment the older Sirrath's jaw set, because Vane had chosen to have this conversation with Growl rather than with any of the other four men available to him for precisely that reason.\n\n\"Prior conduct,\" Growl said.\n\n\"A caution. Age thirteen. A pressure gauge.\"\n\n\"Turn take them.\"\n\n\"Quite.\" Vane sat back and picked up his glass. \"Now. Understand what I am asking you to do, because I do not want it done clumsily. I am not asking you to recruit that boy. If you go down to Sootgate tonight and offer that boy a place with us, he will spit in your eye, and he will be right to, because he has spent two years believing the thing they told him in that hall and he is not going to stop believing it in three days.\"\n\n\"So what.\"\n\n\"So you are going to do nothing at all,\" said Vane. \"You are going to be somewhere he can find you. You are going to be pleasant, and unhurried, and you are going to say one true thing to him and then you are going to leave.\"\n\n\"Which true thing?\"\n\nVane thought about it.\n\n\"That the brass have already told him what he is worth,\" he said. \"And that he can spend the next forty years asking them to change their minds. Nothing else. Not the work, not the money, not us. He will do the rest himself. They always do the rest themselves; that is the beauty of the arrangement.\" He drained the glass. \"The Council makes them, Growl. I only collect.\"\n\nGrowl stood.\n\n\"And if he doesn't come?\"\n\n\"Then he doesn't come, and he cleans stairs on the Rise for thirty years like the woman who took him in, and in due course somebody hands his own child a card with a nine on it.\" Vane turned back to the sheets. \"Go on. There's no hurry. He'll do something stupid within the week and then he'll need somewhere to be.\"\n\nGrowl was at the door when the boy came up the stairs at a run to say there was a whistle going on Cinder Row and a Council board out on the cobbles in nine thousand pieces.\n\nVane did not look up from the sheets.\n\n\"Under the week,\" he said. \"Well. He is bright.\"\n\n---\n\n**V. The Patrol on Cinder Row**\n**Tracker Sergeant Davin Cross** did not run at first. That was the whole of his method and he had never seen any reason to improve on it.\n\nHe came onto Cinder Row nine minutes after the whistle, from the Wick end, on foot, with his coat open and his hands empty, and he stood over the glass for a moment and looked at what was left in the frame and at the pattern the pieces had made on the cobbles, and then he looked up the Row and down it.\n\nThen he began to walk.\n\nCross was forty-four years old and had been with the enforcement division for twenty-two of them, and for the last nine he had held a warrant that read *tracker* rather than *constable*, which meant that when the Under produced somebody who was genuinely difficult to find, the division sent for him and stopped asking questions about his methods. He did not carry a baton. He had never in his career been in a foot race he had lost, because he had never in his career been in a foot race.\n\nWhat Cross did was funnel.\n\nYou cannot outrun a man in a city, but a city is not open ground. A city is a set of choices, and there are far fewer of them than the man running believes. Cinder Row had two ends and eleven side-turnings, and of the eleven, four were dead, three came back out onto the Row itself, one went up to the Wick where there were four constables standing about because there had been a whistle, and three went down into the sump lanes toward the outfalls.\n\nSo there were three choices. Cross knew that before he took his first step. The man running did not.\n\nHe walked up the Row at an unhurried pace, and he watched the windows, and he watched the doorways, and about two thirds of the way along, without breaking stride, he said conversationally to the alley on his right:\n\n\"That's a bad turning, son. It comes back out forty feet up.\"\n\nSilence. Then a scrape.\n\nThen Marrow came out of the alley going flat out the other way, and Cross began, at last, to run.\n\n---\n\nMarrow ran like something out of a bad dream, which is to say he ran extremely fast and had no idea where he was going.\n\nThat was the trouble and he knew it and he could not fix it. He knew Sootgate. He knew Sootgate the way he knew his own hands, every wall of it, and he could have lost this man in Sootgate in four minutes. He was not in Sootgate. He was in Cinder Row, eleven streets east, because he had walked here without choosing a direction, and the geography under his feet was a stranger's.\n\nHe went right, down into the sump lanes, because down was away from the Wick and away from the whistles, and because down smelled of water and water meant outfalls and outfalls meant holes in walls.\n\nBehind him, footsteps. Not fast. Steady.\n\nThat was somehow worse.\n\nHe came out into a yard with a dead pump in the middle of it and four ways out, and he stopped for two breaths, and he thought, with the part of his brain that had never once stopped working no matter what the rest of him was doing:\n\n*He is not trying to catch me. He is choosing which way I go.*\n\nMarrow put his back against the pump housing and made himself stand still.\n\nHe counted. Four ways out. He had come in by one, so three. The one on the left went uphill and lightward, back toward the Wick, which was where the constables were, and therefore that was the one Cross wanted, which meant that was the one Cross would leave open.\n\nThe middle one was dark and he did not know it.\n\nThe right-hand one went down toward the outfalls, and he did not know that either, but he could smell it. Wet stone and rust and, under that, hot.\n\nHot.\n\nMarrow's head came up.\n\nHe had counted the knock in the pipe every day of his life the way everybody did. It was going now, under the yard, under his feet: *knock, knock, knock, knock,* and the long shudder.\n\nThe vent.\n\nFour knocks and a shudder meant a vent coming, and he had heard the shudder somewhere back on the Row, which meant he had, what, thirty seconds? Twenty? He had never in his life had cause to time it to the second, because nobody times a thing they have never needed. He knew the sequence and not the interval.\n\nThat was the whole problem, and he had it in one clean piece: *I know the order of events and not the spacing of them, and I have to act on the spacing.*\n\nSo work it out.\n\nHe knew the vent went at four bells and four times a day and he knew, because you cannot live in the Under and not know, that the fog took about forty counts to fill a lane and about a hundred and fifty to clear. He knew he had heard the shudder while he was still on the Row and that he had been running since, and running badly, and that he could cover the length of the Row in about sixty counts flat out.\n\nHe knew the fog came out of the outfalls. Everybody knew that. It came up out of the grates and it came out of the low arches by the water and it filled the sump lanes first because the sump lanes were lowest, and *then* it went up.\n\nWhich meant the right-hand turning, the one going down, the one that smelled hot, was about to become the only place in this district where a man who does not know the ground could stand ten feet from another man and not see him.\n\nAnd Marrow could not see in it either. He would be as blind as Cross.\n\nBut Cross needed to see him to funnel him. Marrow only needed to not be seen.\n\nFootsteps in the entry behind him, still unhurried.\n\n\"You've stopped,\" said Cross's voice, from somewhere close. \"That's the first sensible thing you've done. Stay stopped, son, and this is a board and a fine. Run again and I'll put conduct on you, and you'll not like what conduct does to a lad in the ninth.\"\n\nMarrow shut his eyes and made himself count.\n\n*Not yet. Not yet.*\n\n\"You there?\"\n\n*Not yet.*\n\nThe knock came up through the soles of his boots, and the yard drain by the dead pump made a sound like something very large clearing its throat.\n\nMarrow went right, and down, and into the dark hot mouth of the outfall lane, and behind him he heard Cross start to move properly for the first time, fast, faster than Marrow had expected, and much closer.\n\nThen the vent let go under Sootgate and Cinder Row and the whole eastern Under, and forty seconds of white came up out of the ground.\n\n---\n\nCross stood in the middle of it with his hand on a wall and did not move at all, because a man who moves in vent-fog in a sump lane he does not personally own is a man who goes into the water.\n\nHe waited it out. It took ninety counts to thin and another eighty to clear, and when it cleared the lane was empty in both directions and there were four ways out of it that had not been on the survey he had memorised that morning, because the survey had been drawn in the year the Row was built and the outfalls had been rebuilt twice since.\n\nHe stood there a while longer.\n\nThen he went back up to the Row, and stood over the glass, and took out his notebook, and wrote, in a small neat hand:\n\n*Sirrath, male, white pelt, sixteen or thereabouts, six foot four. Cinder Row, board, glass. Ran east then down. Used the vent.*\n\nHe looked at that last line for a moment.\n\nThen he underlined it, because in twenty-two years he had banked nine hundred people in the Under and not one of them had ever thought of it, and a man in his profession learns to write down the day the ground changes under him.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Two.*" },
  { n: 3, title: "Two Worlds", text: "**I. Rooke**\nThe room had a bed in it and nothing else living, and that was the problem.\n\nRooke had been at the University of the Engine for six days and she had slept for perhaps eleven hours of them, and the reason was not the bed, which was better than any bed she had ever been in, and it was not the food, which was so plentiful that on the second day she had been quietly sick from it. The reason was that at night the room was silent.\n\nThere was no cough. There was no Tam talking in his sleep. There was no Pip climbing in at the foot of the bed at second bell because Pip always climbed in at the foot of the bed at second bell and Rooke had spent four years pretending to be asleep when it happened so that Pip would not be embarrassed about it. There was no knock in the pipe, because the buildings on the Rise had their pipes lagged in felt and boxed in behind panelling so that the Turn could not be heard by anybody who mattered.\n\nThey had taken the sound of the Engine out of the walls. They had a hundred and three years of claiming they alone could hear it and they had paid good money to have it silenced in their bedrooms.\n\nRooke lay in the dark and listened to nothing and did the sum she did every night now, which was: two marks a week rent, four and a half marks a week for six people to eat if Tessa shopped as well as Rooke had shopped, which she would not, so five, plus coke, plus the chalk at Marta's, against whatever Tessa and Tam could bring in, which was two marks in a good week.\n\nIt came out at a shortfall of five marks a week and no eleven marks for syrup, ever, on any timeline.\n\nSix days. She had run it about forty times.\n\n---\n\n\"You're not eating,\" said Ivy Thorncastle.\n\n\"I ate.\"\n\n\"You had a roll.\"\n\n\"I had two rolls.\" Rooke put her spoon down. \"I'm not being difficult. I got ill on Tuesday from eating too much and now I'm frightened of it, which is the stupidest thing that has ever happened to me, and I would rather not discuss it in a room with two hundred people in it.\"\n\nIvy considered this. She was fifteen, small, ferociously untidy in a way that took real effort in a place where everybody's clothes were pressed by somebody else, and she had sat down next to Rooke at supper on the first evening for no reason that Rooke had been able to establish since.\n\n\"That's not stupid,\" Ivy said. \"That's a perfectly ordinary physiological response to a change in caloric intake. My aunt could tell you the whole of it. She'd draw you a graph.\"\n\n\"Your aunt.\"\n\n\"Professor Thorncastle.\"\n\nRooke turned and looked at her properly for the first time in six days.\n\n\"Your aunt is Thorncastle,\" she said. \"The Thorncastle who runs the second-year reckoning.\"\n\n\"And the third and the fourth, and she is not going to be pleased to hear you put it in that order. Yes.\" Ivy went back to her supper. \"Before you ask, no, I didn't sit next to you because she told me to. She doesn't know I know who you are. Nobody's supposed to know who you are, they've been very correct about it, and I found out on Monday because I read the placement docket upside down on her desk while she was talking to me about my own results, which were mediocre.\" She said it without any self-pity at all. \"I'm going to be a very good historian and a very bad reckoner and everyone in my family has decided to be gracious about that, which is worse than shouting.\"\n\n\"Then why did you sit next to me?\"\n\n\"Because everyone else was looking at you and nobody was talking to you,\" Ivy said, \"and I have been the person nobody talks to at supper, and it is horrible, and I decided at about the age of nine that when I could stop it happening to somebody I would.\"\n\nRooke looked at her plate for a while.\n\n\"That's the first honest thing anybody's said to me since the carriage,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes, well. The Rise doesn't go in for it much.\" Ivy tore a roll in half and put the larger half on Rooke's plate without appearing to notice she had done it. \"You'll get used to that. Or you won't, and you'll be much more interesting.\"\n\n---\n\nThe diagnostic was on the seventh day, in a small room on the north side with two slate boards and a window looking down the face of the Rise into brown fog, and Professor Linnea Thorncastle set it in front of her and then sat down in the corner with a book and did not look up once for three hours.\n\nRooke had expected an examination. It was not an examination. It was eleven problems on eleven separate sheets and they were not in order of difficulty, and by the third sheet she understood that they were not in order of anything, that they had been chosen the way you choose keys off a ring when you do not know which door you are dealing with.\n\nShe did nine of them.\n\nThe tenth she got most of the way into and then found she was walking in a circle, and she sat with it for forty minutes and then wrote, underneath her working, in her small flat hand: *This is wrong from the fourth line and I cannot see where. The method should hold and it does not, so I have got a thing backwards. I would like to be told which thing.*\n\nThe eleventh she read four times and did not touch at all.\n\nShe turned it over and wrote on the back: *I do not know what this is asking. I do not know most of these words. I know it is not arithmetic and I do not think it is geometry either. If it is the true calculus then I have never seen the true calculus and I would like somebody to say so plainly instead of assuming I have.*\n\nThen she squared the eleven sheets to the edge of the desk and said, \"I'm finished.\"\n\nThorncastle put the book down.\n\nShe read the nine correct ones in about four minutes, which Rooke found insulting until she understood, watching the old woman's eyes, that she was not reading them, she was checking them, the way you check that a wall is straight by looking along it.\n\nThen she read the tenth. She spent a long time on the tenth.\n\nThen she turned over the eleventh and read the back of it, and she sat completely still for a moment, and then she laughed once, sharply, with no humour in it whatsoever.\n\n\"Well,\" said Professor Thorncastle. \"God help us all.\"\n\n\"Is that bad?\"\n\n\"Sit down, child.\"\n\n\"I am sitting down.\"\n\n\"So you are.\" Thorncastle got up and went to the window and stood with her back to the room. \"The tenth. You have got a thing backwards, and the thing is that you have assumed the relation holds in both directions, and it does not, and there is no possible way you could have known that because it is not written down anywhere you would ever have been able to get at. I will teach you in ten minutes on Thursday. That is not the interesting part.\"\n\n\"What's the interesting part.\"\n\n\"You knew you were wrong.\"\n\nRooke waited.\n\n\"Do you understand how rare that is?\" Thorncastle turned round. \"I have taught in this building for thirty-one years. Every year they send me the finest young minds in Gifford, tutored from the age of six, and when they hit a wall they do one of two things. They put down an answer they know is rubbish and hope I am too busy to notice, or they put down nothing and tell me afterwards that they had run out of time. In thirty-one years, I have had four students write *I am wrong and I cannot see where and I would like to be told*. Four.\" She came back to the desk. \"It is the single most useful sentence in the entire discipline and almost nobody can say it, because to say it you have to be able to hold two things at once: that you are capable, and that you are wrong. The Rise raises children who can hold the first one. It does not, on the whole, produce the second.\"\n\n\"Where I'm from,\" Rooke said, \"if you're wrong about a pump you get wet.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" Thorncastle sat down opposite her. \"Yes, I imagine that is exactly it.\"\n\nShe picked up the eleventh sheet.\n\n\"Now this. This is the part where I have to be careful, and you should watch me being careful, because you will want to know later that I was.\" She laid it flat. \"You are right. It is not arithmetic and it is not geometry. It is the true calculus. Or rather, it is a problem set in the notation of the true calculus, which is not the same thing and I want you to notice that I made that distinction.\"\n\n\"I noticed.\"\n\n\"Good. You have never seen it because it is not taught below the fourth year and it is not published, and the reason given is that it is dangerous in untrained hands, and the reason is nonsense, and I have said so in writing twice and been thanked for my contribution.\" Thorncastle's mouth was thin. \"You will begin on it in the spring. You will find it easy. I am telling you now, before you find out, because I would rather you heard it from somebody who likes you: you will find it easy, and a great many people in this building will find that very difficult indeed, and one or two of them will find it intolerable.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you are fourteen and from Ferrow Lane.\"\n\n\"That isn't a reason.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Thorncastle. \"It's a cause. There's a difference and I suspect you know it.\"\n\nRooke sat back.\n\n\"Professor.\"\n\n\"Mm.\"\n\n\"On the docket for my placement. Is there a stipend?\"\n\nThorncastle blinked. It was, Rooke noted, the only time in three hours that she had managed to knock the woman off her line.\n\n\"There is a maintenance allowance,\" she said. \"Fourteen marks a term. It is intended for books and coal and boots.\"\n\n\"Fourteen marks a term.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Four terms a year.\" Rooke did the sum out loud, flatly, because she had already done it and wanted to hear somebody else confirm it. \"Fifty-six marks a year. And I'm here seven years. Three hundred and ninety-two marks, and I don't need books because the library's free and I don't need coal because the rooms are heated and I have boots.\"\n\n\"Child—\"\n\n\"How much of it can I send home?\"\n\nThorncastle looked at her for a long moment. Behind her, out of the window, a clatterlift went down the face of the Rise with its cable hauling, taking somebody down into the brown.\n\n\"All of it,\" she said. \"And it will not be enough, will it.\"\n\n\"Fourteen marks a term is three marks a week and a bit,\" said Rooke. \"They're five short. So no. It's most of the way and it isn't enough, and I've had six days to think about it and I can't make the number bigger by thinking.\"\n\n\"So what will you do?\"\n\n\"Find something that pays,\" said Rooke. \"I always have.\"\n\n---\n\n**II. Marrow**\nHe was three days at Sister Maude's before he could put weight on the ankle, and the ankle was the least of it.\n\nHe had gone into the outfall lane blind and come out of it into a culvert he did not know, and somewhere in the middle of that he had put his foot into a gap between two courses of brick that the fog had hidden, and gone down hard, and had then got up and gone another mile and a half on it because the alternative was Cross. By the time he came up out of the sump lanes into Sootgate the ankle had gone the colour of a bad plum and he could not remember the last four streets.\n\nHe did not go to Ketu's. He was very clear about that afterwards, when people asked. He had stood at the end of her lane in the dark for a long time and had worked out, with the part of his brain that would not stop, that a Sirrath with a fresh bank on him and a Council board in nine thousand pieces behind him was a thing you did not carry into the house of a woman who had scrubbed stairs on the Rise for thirty years and had never once been in trouble.\n\nSo he went to Maude's, because everybody went to Maude's, and Maude asked him no questions at all.\n\nThat was the whole method of the place. Eight iron cots in a room over a dyer's yard, mismatched blankets, a pot-bellied stove, a shelf of bottles, damp on the ceiling in the shape of a country nobody had ever named. Maude took your name if you offered it and did not ask twice if you did not, and she strapped the ankle and told him it was a bad sprain and not a break, and that he would be walking on it in four days and running on it in three weeks and that if he ran on it in four days he would be lame at forty.\n\n\"Understood,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"You'll do it anyway.\"\n\n\"Probably.\"\n\n\"Mm.\" She pulled the strapping tight with a jerk that made him hiss. \"Everybody does. There's a slate on the wall behind you with the names of about two hundred people who did. I scrub one off every few weeks.\"\n\n---\n\nOn the fourth day, when he could get to the stove and back, he sat on the end of the cot with his foot up and did the sum.\n\nHe had eight grist in his coat and no writ and a ninth stratum against his name in a ledger that would follow him until he died, and a bank he had not yet been caught for. That was the position.\n\nThe trade options for a ninth stratum Sirrath in Sootgate were four, and he had known all four of them since he was eleven, the way you know the exits from a room.\n\nThere was the drop-forges, where the Laachmi worked, and they would take him because he was big and there was always a place for big, and it paid three marks a week and took your hearing in nine years and your lungs in fifteen.\n\nThere was portering on the seam. Two marks and a bit, and you were on your feet fourteen hours, and the seam was where the brass could see you, so you got stopped twice a week.\n\nThere was the water gangs in the outfalls, which paid five marks because people drowned.\n\nAnd there was Ketu's trade, which was to go up the Rise every morning at fourth bell and clean a house belonging to people who would not learn your name, for two marks a week and whatever was left on the plates, for thirty years, until your knees went.\n\nThat was the list. That was the whole list. Two years of Ketu's kitchen table and a stolen syllabus and the load across the arch, and the list had not changed by so much as a single item.\n\nMarrow sat with his foot up and understood, with a cold clarity that was worse than the anger, that the amendment had not taken anything away from him. It had simply told him the truth about what he had never had.\n\n\"You're the one broke the board on Cinder Row,\" said a voice.\n\nHe looked up.\n\nThe Sirrath in the doorway was ten years older than him and a good deal rougher, orange-brown, one ear notched, a heavy seam of scar tissue across the muzzle. He had a coat on that was too good for Maude's and he was standing in the door in the specific way that people stood in doors when they had been told not to come in.\n\n\"No,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Right.\" The older Sirrath came in anyway and sat down on the next cot, which creaked. \"Growl. I know Ketu. Cleaned for the Fenwicks on Aster Row about the time my mother did. Small people, small houses.\"\n\nMarrow said nothing.\n\n\"You've an amendment,\" Growl said.\n\n\"Everybody in that room had an amendment.\"\n\n\"Forty-one of you. Yeah.\" Growl leaned back against the wall. \"I had one. Nineteen years ago. Assessed sixth. Amended tenth. Prior conduct.\" He held up his hand and turned it over. \"I was thirteen and I hit a man who had hold of my sister's arm, and there's not one word about the arm anywhere on the docket, and there's four lines about the hitting.\"\n\nThe stove ticked.\n\n\"So you're here to recruit me,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Everyone knows what the Ashlight is.\"\n\n\"Then you know I'd be wasting an afternoon.\" Growl did not move from the wall. \"You've had it three days. You still think there's a version of this where you write a letter to somebody and it gets put right. I'm not going to argue you out of that, because nobody argued me out of it either. Took me about two years.\"\n\n\"Then why are you here.\"\n\nGrowl thought about it. He seemed to be genuinely thinking, which Marrow had not expected.\n\n\"Because the man who runs the Ashlight sent me to say one thing to you and then leave,\" he said, \"and I've decided I'm going to say a different thing, and he'll not know, and if you ever repeat it I'll say you're a liar.\"\n\n\"Go on then.\"\n\n\"The thing he wanted me to say is that the brass have already told you what you're worth, and you can spend forty years asking them to change their minds.\" Growl stood up. \"It's true. That's what makes him good at this. Everything he says to you is going to be true and it's going to be true the way a hook is a piece of metal.\"\n\nHe went to the door.\n\n\"So what's yours,\" said Marrow.\n\nGrowl stopped.\n\n\"Get to Bramwell's,\" he said. \"Off Kettle Yard, behind the dye works, door with a gear nailed to it. Old man, mad as a bag of cats, been cogging Engine parts for fifty years and never once been banked for it. He'll shout at you. Let him.\" He put his hand on the frame. \"He's the last honest room in the Under and there's a reason for that, and the reason is that Vane's asked him four times and been told no four times, and nobody's ever worked out why Vane lets him get away with it.\"\n\n\"Why does he?\"\n\n\"Nobody knows. That's the point of telling you.\" Growl looked back over his shoulder. \"You'll come to us in the end. I'd put money on it and I'm not being unkind, I'd put money on it because I did. But you've maybe got a year, and a year's a year, and there's a thing you can learn in Bramwell's shop that you'll never learn in ours.\"\n\n\"What thing?\"\n\n\"How to make something,\" said Growl, \"instead of how to take it.\"\n\nThen he went out, and Marrow heard him go down the stairs, and heard him stop halfway and say something to Sister Maude in a voice that had entirely changed, and heard the chink of coins going into the box by the door.\n\nMore than eight grist, by the sound.\n\n---\n\n**III. Clerk Pemberton**\nThe trouble with the young noblewoman was that she was polite.\n\nPemberton had worked the examinations records office at Tallow Street for six years and he had developed, in that time, a complete and reliable defence against every category of person who came to his hatch. There were the angry ones, and you gave them the form and the words *there's no appeal on an amendment* and let them shout at the glass. There were the crying ones, and you looked at the docket while they did it. There were the clever ones who tried to catch you out on procedure, and procedure was Pemberton's entire body of knowledge and they never won.\n\nAnd then there was this one, who came in on the Tuesday, and did not shout, and did not cry, and did not try to catch him out. She asked him about seasonal variation in passing rates.\n\n\"Seasonal,\" said Pemberton.\n\n\"Year on year.\" Officer Corvain had her elbows on the ledge of the hatch, which nobody did, and she was looking at him as though he were interesting, which nobody did either. \"The Calibration's a hundred and three years old. There must be variation. Some years the cohort's stronger, some years it's weaker.\"\n\n\"There's variation, yes.\"\n\n\"How much?\"\n\n\"That'd be in the returns.\"\n\n\"Where are the returns?\"\n\n\"Bound. Annually.\" Pemberton heard himself say it and heard how helpful it sounded, and could not for the life of him work out why he had said it that way. \"There's a set in the reading room upstairs. Last thirty years.\"\n\n\"Only thirty?\"\n\n\"Reading room set's thirty. It's what people ask for.\"\n\n\"And the rest?\"\n\nAnd there it was.\n\nPemberton had a small internal experience at this point which he would think about, on and off, for the rest of his life. What happened was that he opened his mouth to say *the rest are in store*, which was true, and completely unremarkable, and the answer he had given perhaps a hundred times to researchers and clerks and one persistent old man from the Historical Society, and instead what came out was:\n\n\"There's a full set.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"Down.\"\n\n\"Down.\"\n\n\"Sub-level. It's not a reading room.\" He was talking too fast now and he knew it. \"It's store. You'd need a requisition.\"\n\n\"From whom?\"\n\n\"Grandmaster's office.\"\n\n\"Which takes how long?\"\n\n\"Eleven days.\"\n\n\"Eleven days,\" said Corvain. \"For a bound volume of numbers that are posted on eleven boards outside this building every summer where anybody can read them.\"\n\n\"That's the procedure.\"\n\n\"I'm not arguing with you,\" she said, and she did not sound as though she were. \"I'm trying to understand why a set of published figures needs eleven days and a Grandmaster's signature. Because that's unusual. In the enforcement division I can requisition a murder file in four days and the murder file has people's names in it.\"\n\n\"I don't set the procedure.\"\n\n\"No.\" She straightened up off the ledge. \"Mr Pemberton, is it?\"\n\n\"Pemberton.\"\n\n\"Mr Pemberton. What's actually down there?\"\n\nAnd Pemberton, who had a complete and reliable defence against every category of person who came to his hatch, said: \"Nothing. Returns. Just returns.\"\n\nHe said it a fraction of a second too fast and about two degrees too loud, and he watched her hear it, and he watched her decide to be kind about having heard it, which was the worst part of the whole exchange.\n\n\"Thank you,\" said Officer Corvain. \"You've been very helpful.\"\n\nShe went out. Pemberton stood at the hatch for some time.\n\nThen he went and sat down at his desk with his ledger open in front of him and the periodical folded inside it, and he did not read either, and what he thought about was this:\n\n*She doesn't want the returns. Nobody has ever wanted the returns. She wanted to know whether there was somewhere the returns were kept that wasn't the reading room. And I told her.*\n\nHe thought about the vault door on the sub-level, three feet of iron with the dial grid on the face of it, and the little brass spring clip below the grid where the current docket slip sat, filled in in pencil, changed every quarter, so that the four clerks who actually had to go down there twice a week could get in without troubling anybody in the true calculus about it.\n\nHe thought about the fact that in six years nobody had ever once asked him how he got in.\n\nThen he thought, with a genuine and rising sense of grievance, that this was not fair, that he had done nothing at all except answer a question honestly, that he was a clerk on eleven marks a week with a chalk at three houses on the Wick, and that whatever was about to happen in this building was going to happen to him first and to the people who had built the vault last.\n\nHe got up and went to find the quarter's docket slip and change it early.\n\nHe was two hours too late.\n\n---\n\n**IV. Chancellor Isolde Prewitt**\nThere were two items on the informal list, which was the list Prewitt kept in her head and had never once written down in twelve years.\n\nThe first was a name.\n\n*Rooke. No other given. Halloway household, Ferrow Lane. Candidate 4471. Assessed ninth stratum on the original paper by deliberate concealment. First stratum on the reserve. Highest reserve score in the recorded history of the examination.*\n\nPrewitt had read Thorncastle's preliminary note twice. It was four lines long and Linnea Thorncastle had been careful in it, which was itself informative, because Thorncastle was not a careful woman and did not become one by accident.\n\n*The candidate's aptitude is not in question. I would prefer not to characterise it further in writing until I have taught her a full term.*\n\nPrewitt turned that sentence over for a while.\n\nYou did not decline to characterise a fourteen year old in writing because she was merely very good. Very good was a thing you put in a report, because very good reflected credit on you. You declined to characterise her in writing when you had looked at what she could do and had understood that the number, written down, would be read by people who would draw a conclusion from it that you were not yet prepared to have drawn.\n\nThere was only one such conclusion available.\n\nThe Wrought held Gifford on a single proposition, which was that the descendants of the Twelve were born able to hear the Turn, and that this was why they must govern it. It had been true of the Twelve. It had been broadly true for two generations after. It had been getting less true for eighty years, in the ordinary way of these things, because a gift does not stay in a bloodline any more than a fortune does; it thins out, and reverts, and the great-grandchildren of geniuses are for the most part perfectly pleasant people who are good at tennis.\n\nPrewitt knew this. Every Chancellor for sixty years had known it. It was the central fact of her working life and it was managed, carefully, annually, by the Calibration, which went out into the city and found the children who could actually do the work and brought them up the Rise and gave them a gown and a title and, in due course, a name that sounded as though it had always been there.\n\nThe system worked. It had worked for a hundred and three years. Prewitt had no moral difficulty with it whatsoever; she had watched what happened in Vaskeld when their council fell and their engine stopped, and she had read the number, and the number was forty thousand.\n\nThe difficulty was one of degree.\n\nA promoted Rustbound child who was very good could be absorbed. It happened every year. Four or five of them, sanded down, married off, absorbed.\n\nA promoted Rustbound child who was demonstrably better at the true calculus than any living member of the Wrought was not an absorption. She was a proof. She was a walking, breathing, fourteen year old proof that the proposition on which the city rested was false, and the only thing standing between that proof and everybody who might draw it was that nobody had yet written the number down.\n\nPrewitt made a small note in her head against the name and moved on.\n\n---\n\nThe second item was also a name, and it annoyed her more, because it was avoidable.\n\n*Corvain, E. Enforcement division, four months. Adelina's girl.*\n\nTwo enquiries at the examinations records office in five days. One requisition submitted for the full historical returns and refused by the Grandmaster's office on procedural grounds without ever reaching Prewitt's desk, which was the system working correctly. One informal conversation with a records clerk.\n\nAnd, this morning, a note from Reeve Ashcombe, who was a good officer and an honest man and who had committed the only unforgivable error available to an honest man in a position of moderate authority, which was to put a thing in writing in order to protect the person who had told him.\n\n*Officer Corvain has identified an anomaly in the application of conduct amendments arising from this year's Calibration and has my authority to establish the facts. I judge her competent and the matter narrow. I would rather it were resolved within the division than raised elsewhere.*\n\nPrewitt read that three times, and each time she liked Ashcombe a little more and found her position a little worse.\n\nBecause he had done exactly the right thing. He had contained it, minuted it, put his own name on it and quietly signalled upward that he intended to handle it himself and would appreciate not being interfered with. It was the act of a decent man managing a subordinate he believed in. And its effect, entirely unintended, was that there was now a piece of paper in this building with the words *anomaly in the application of conduct amendments* on it, signed by a captain.\n\nOnce a thing is minuted it cannot be un-minuted. It can only be answered.\n\nPrewitt sat for a while with her hands folded on the closed ledger.\n\nThere were four ways to answer it and she went through them the way she went through everything, which was slowly and without any particular feeling.\n\nShe could have the girl transferred. Cheap, quick, and it converted a curious officer into an aggrieved one, and aggrieved officers talk.\n\nShe could have the amendments this year quietly reversed. Elegant. It would fix the immediate problem, and forty-one non-human candidates would go up the strata, and it would establish for all time that amendments could be reversed, which would produce, within three years, an appeals process, which would produce within ten years a public argument about what conduct amendments were actually for.\n\nShe could give the girl the answer. The real one. Which was madness and she noted it only to be thorough.\n\nOr she could let her have it.\n\nPrewitt considered the fourth option for a long time, and the shape of it pleased her, in the abstract, the way a well-turned proof pleases anybody.\n\nLet the girl work. Let her be right. Let her establish, over some months, at some cost, with real evidence, that the Calibration is managed. And then, at the moment she brings it in, be the person who receives it.\n\nBecause a young woman with a discovery has two futures and only two. She can be destroyed by it, which makes a martyr and a story. Or she can be believed, and thanked, and told the truth, and shown the number from Vaskeld, and given a chair in the room where the thing is decided.\n\nAnd a person who has been given a chair in the room does not take the lid off the city. A person in the room becomes, within about eighteen months, the most careful custodian of the whole arrangement, because they have seen the alternative and they have signed their name to something.\n\nThat was how Prewitt had come to this desk. Twenty-two years ago. She remembered the afternoon very clearly, and she remembered the man who had shown her the number, and she remembered walking out onto the terrace afterwards and being sick into a flowerbed, and she remembered that by the following spring she had stopped thinking about it more than once a week.\n\nShe reached for the bell to call her secretary, and then did not ring it.\n\nLet her work, then.\n\nBut not down there.\n\nPrewitt drew a single sheet toward her and wrote, in her own hand, six words to the Grandmaster's office, requesting that the sub-level store at Tallow Street be reviewed for its suitability as a records repository at the earliest convenience.\n\nShe dated it. She sanded it. She put it in the tray.\n\nIt would go out on Thursday, and the review would take about nine days, and by the middle of the following month the historical returns would be in eleven crates in a locked room in the Council building with a proper requisition process, and Officer Corvain would be able to look at them any time she liked, once she had asked.\n\nAt the time Prewitt put the note in the tray it was twenty past six in the evening on the Wednesday.\n\n---\n\n**V. The Sealed Archive**\nThe corridor was not on any plan of the building that Corvain had been able to find, which was not sinister; there were four sub-levels under Tallow Street and none of them were on anything, because the building had been extended eleven times in a hundred years and nobody had ever redrawn it.\n\nShe had come down the back stair at half past ten with a candle, a valve-key she had bought that afternoon in the Under for four grist, and her own coat turned inside out so the buttons did not show, which she had thought was rather clever until she had got to the bottom and realised the coat was grey on both sides.\n\nShe was, she was aware, committing a criminal offence. She had decided about that on the stairs and had come to the conclusion that she was going to do it anyway and that it was important to be honest with herself about which of the two possible reasons was operating. The first reason was that a hundred and forty children a year were having their futures amended in a back office. The second reason was that she wanted to know.\n\nThe second one was the true one. Corvain thought that was probably how most people ended up doing the right thing, and that it did not make it less right, and that she would think about the rest of it later.\n\nThen she came round the turn and the candle found the door and she stopped walking.\n\nIt was three feet thick and it filled the corridor. Iron, black with age, and set into the face of it in six rows was a grid of brass dial housings, each dial numbered nought to nine, thirty-six of them, worn bright at the thumb-hold from a century of use.\n\nAbove the grid, cut in a script so small and so weathered she had to hold the candle to it, three lines:\n\n*When the Engine ticks, the city keeps.*\n*When the birds fly, time moves.*\n*When the bells ring, all hear.*\n\nCorvain stood in front of it for a long time.\n\nThen she said, out loud, in the empty corridor: \"Oh, you absolute *frauds*.\"\n\n---\n\nShe had spent the walk down there braced for exactly this. She had spent it telling herself the thing she had been telling herself since Tuesday, which was that she was not a reckoner, that she had come fourth in her year at a very expensive school at a subject she was competent at and no more, that Rooke of Ferrow Lane would look at a lock like this and open it in ninety seconds and Elspeth Corvain would look at it and see thirty-six brass dials and a poem.\n\nAnd that was true. She looked at it and she saw thirty-six brass dials and a poem, and she could not begin to touch it, and she knew she could not.\n\nWhich was the entire point.\n\nBecause Corvain had stood on a gallery for four hours and watched six hundred children take an examination, and she had spent five days in a records office, and she had learned one thing about this city that she had not known before, and it was this: the people who guard the Wrought's secrets are not Wrought.\n\nThey are clerks.\n\nThere were four clerks who went down to this sub-level, twice a week, to file the returns and fetch them. Pemberton was one of them. Pemberton had a ledger with a periodical folded inside it and a chalk at three houses on the Wick and eleven marks a week, and Pemberton had come fourth or fifth stratum at his Calibration twenty years ago and had never seen the true calculus in his life and never would.\n\nPemberton could not open that lock. Not on his best day, not with a week and a slate.\n\nAnd Pemberton went down there twice a week.\n\nCorvain brought the candle down and held it low, under the grid, and looked at the bottom of the door.\n\nThere was a small brass plate screwed to the iron. Mundane, functional, obviously added long after the door was made, the screws a different metal and a different age. In the plate, a spring clip. In the clip, a folded paper slip about the size of a playing card, greasy at the edges from handling.\n\nShe took it out.\n\nIt was a docket slip. She had seen five hundred of them in the last five days. Printed heading, *EXAMINATIONS OFFICE, TALLOW STREET*, a box for the quarter, a box for the series and a long ruled line, and on the line, in pencil, in a small tired hand:\n\n**Q3 — 114 / 2260 / 0709 / 6631 / 1188 / 4471**\n\nCorvain read the last four figures three times.\n\nThen she laughed, once, in the dark, and put her hand over her mouth because the sound went a very long way in a stone corridor.\n\nSix groups of four. Thirty-six dials in six rows of six. No. Six rows, and the numbers were four long, which meant, which meant—\n\nShe held the candle up again and counted properly instead of guessing, which was, she would reflect later, the single most useful thing anyone had ever taught her, and it had been Ashcombe, and he had said it about crime scenes.\n\nSix rows. Six dials in each row. Thirty-six.\n\nAnd six groups of four figures.\n\nNot enough. Two dials spare in every row.\n\nCorvain stood with the slip in one hand and the candle in the other and made herself be slow.\n\n*They are clerks. Whatever this is, four clerks can do it twice a week in a cold corridor holding a lamp and a box of returns. It is not clever. It cannot be clever. It has to be something a tired man can do one-handed.*\n\nShe looked at the slip again.\n\n**Q3.** The quarter. Third quarter.\n\nShe looked at the six dials in the top row and turned the first one to 1, and the second to 1, and the third to 4, and stopped, and looked at what she had done, and then very deliberately did not turn the fourth or the fifth or the sixth.\n\nThen she read the engraving again, and this time she read it the way a clerk would read it, which is to say as an instruction rather than as poetry.\n\n*When the bells ring, all hear.*\n\nThird quarter. Third bell.\n\nCorvain reached up and turned the last two dials of the top row to 0 and 3.\n\nSomething in the door went *click*, very small, like a watch being wound one turn.\n\nShe stood absolutely still.\n\nThen she began on the second row, and her hands were shaking so badly she had to put the candle down on the floor.\n\n---\n\nIt took her nineteen minutes and she got it wrong twice, and both times something in the mechanism made a sound like a swallow and she had to start the row again, and by the fifth row she had stopped being frightened and had started being angry, and the anger was so clean and so total that she could feel it in her teeth.\n\nBecause it was *nothing*.\n\nIt was a quarter-code and a bell-number, six times over. It was the kind of thing a shipping office used. It was changed every quarter and written down in pencil on a slip of paper and kept in a spring clip screwed to the front of the door it opened, six inches below a poem in a dead script about the birds and the bells, and the poem was there so that anybody who came down this corridor would look at the thirty-six dials and the founder's flourish and think: *this is beyond me, this belongs to people who can hear the Turn.*\n\nA hundred and three years of it. A whole city taught from the age of four that the reckoning was a mystery and that the mystery was in the blood, and at the bottom of the last stair the mystery was a docket slip.\n\nCorvain got the sixth row and the mechanism went through a sequence of noises that lasted about four seconds and sounded, unmistakably, like a machine sighing.\n\nThen the door came off its seal with a breath of cold dry air that smelled of paper and dust and old iron, and swung four inches, and stopped.\n\nShe picked up the candle.\n\nBehind the door, receding into the dark further than the light would go, shelf after shelf after shelf of bound volumes, dark green, gilt-stamped by year, in an unbroken run.\n\nA hundred and three of them.\n\nCorvain went in.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Three.*" },
  { n: 4, title: "The Shape of a Thing", text: "**I. Rooke**\nThe worst thing about the Rise was that she was happy there, and she found that out on a Thursday, in the library, at about four in the afternoon.\n\nIt had been six weeks. The library was on the third floor of the west range and it had eleven windows down one side and a gallery running round three walls above, and it held, according to the catalogue, a hundred and forty thousand volumes, of which Rooke had established that four thousand were about the reckoning and about nine hundred were any use.\n\nShe had a table in the corner under the fourth window. It was not her table. She had simply been at it every day for six weeks and by the end of the second week other people had stopped sitting there, in the way that these things happen without anybody deciding.\n\nOn the Thursday she was working through Vasterling on periodic functions, which was two hundred years old and wrong in three places she had found so far, and Thorncastle had given it to her on purpose because it was wrong, and had said, *Find the errors. Do not tell me how many there are. I want to know how many you find.*\n\nRooke found the third one at about four o'clock. It was in a footnote. It had been sitting in that footnote for two hundred years and nobody had troubled it, and she went back and checked it four times because a girl who has been in a university for six weeks does not casually decide she is right and a dead man is wrong, and then she checked it a fifth time, and then she sat back in her chair with a strange feeling in her chest.\n\nShe thought: I have never been this warm and this fed and this quiet at the same time in my life. I have four thousand books and a table and nobody wants anything from me before Thursday. This is what it is like. This is what they all have. Every single one of them in this building has had this since they were six.\n\nAnd then she thought, immediately, in the same breath: Nettle is still coughing.\n\nShe sat with both of those for a while.\n\nThat was the moment she understood the shape of the thing that was going to be wrong with the rest of her life, and it was not guilt, because guilt was easy and she had expected guilt. It was that both were true. The library was good. The library was genuinely, straightforwardly good, and she wanted it, and she was better at being here than she had ever been at anything, and none of that made Ferrow Lane less cold.\n\nShe had been raised to think in sums and she could not make this one balance because it was not a sum. It was two sums about two different things and the answer to one had no bearing at all on the answer to the other, and there was no operation you could perform to bring them together.\n\nThe letter came the same afternoon.\n\n---\n\n*Rooke.*\n\n*Marta wrote this down for me and I am paying her in ledger work which she says is worth two grist an hour and I say is worth more but there we are.*\n\n*Do not send money. That is the first thing and I am putting it first because Mother said to.*\n\n*Mother is not well. It is her chest and it is not the same as Nettle it is the old thing that comes in the autumn, but this year it has come in September and she has not been down the stairs in nine days. Sister Maude has been twice and would not take anything either time which I have not told Mother.*\n\n*We are four weeks on the rent. Barrow has been round twice and he was not unkind the first time. Tam is portering on the seam and brings a mark and four most weeks. I have the ledgers at Marta's and I am slow but I am getting less slow and she has not shouted at me since the second week.*\n\n*Nettle is the same. Not worse. She asks about you.*\n\n*Do not send money Rooke. Mother says and I say. You sent eleven marks in August and I know what you get and I did the sum which I know is a stupid thing to say to you but I did it and eleven marks was most of what you had for the whole term. Mother cried when it came and it was not the good kind.*\n\n*We are not going to be out on the step. Barrow has a chalk with everybody on this lane and he cannot put us all out. Tessa.*\n\n*P.S. Marta says to tell you that her nephew has stopped skimming since I started on the ledgers which is not because I am good it is because he thinks I might be.*\n\n---\n\nRooke read it three times in the library and then took it out into the corridor and read it twice more by the window at the end, where nobody went.\n\n*Do not send money.*\n\nShe put the letter in her coat and stood looking down the face of the Rise at the brown fog over the Under and did the arithmetic she had been doing every night for six weeks, and it had not changed.\n\nFourteen marks a term. Four terms. Fifty-six marks a year, and the shortfall on Ferrow Lane was five marks a week, which was two hundred and sixty a year.\n\nShe had sent eleven marks in August because eleven was the number and she had wanted, very badly, to send eleven exactly. It had bought two bottles of syrup, and it had not bought the rent, and it had made Mother Halloway cry the bad way.\n\nTwo hundred and sixty marks a year. There was no version of a maintenance allowance that touched it. There was no version of any legitimate thing a fourteen year old student could do in a term that touched it.\n\nRooke stood at the window with her forehead nearly on the glass and thought, quite calmly:\n\n*Then I need a different source.*\n\n---\n\n**II. Marrow**\nThe door had a gear nailed to it, which turned out to be the least eccentric thing about the entire premises.\n\nMarrow had gone to Kettle Yard four times in a fortnight before he got in. The first time nobody answered. The second time somebody shouted through the door that they were not buying. The third time the door opened four inches, an eye came to the gap, and the door shut again.\n\nThe fourth time he brought the pressure gauge.\n\nIt was not the pressure gauge he had been banked for; that one had gone back to the barrow-man. It was one he had spent nine days building out of two broken ones, a length of brass tube, a spring out of a dead clock and a glass face he had ground down himself on a wet stone with a great deal of swearing. It read low by about a twelfth across its whole range, consistently, which he had established by comparing it against the works gauge on the standpipe at the end of Ketu's lane over four days.\n\nHe held it up to the eye at the gap.\n\nThe door opened.\n\n---\n\n\"It's wrong,\" said Old Bramwell.\n\n\"By a twelfth. Low. Right the way across.\"\n\n\"Then it's *wrong*.\"\n\n\"It's wrong the same amount everywhere,\" said Marrow. \"So it's not wrong, it's offset. You can read it. You just have to know.\"\n\nBramwell looked at him over the top of a pair of brass-rimmed magnifying goggles pushed up onto his forehead. He was about seventy, wild grey hair going in eleven directions, a leather apron burned through in three places, hands ruined the way hands get ruined by forty years of doing something properly.\n\n\"Say that again,\" he said.\n\n\"You can read it. You just have to know.\"\n\n\"No, the other bit.\"\n\n\"It's not wrong, it's offset.\"\n\nBramwell took the gauge and turned it over twice and tapped the glass and handed it back.\n\n\"Come in,\" he said. \"Don't touch anything and don't sit on the bench with the cloth on it, and if I shout it isn't about you.\"\n\n---\n\nThe workshop had eaten its own building. That was the only way Marrow could ever describe it to anybody afterwards. Three storeys with the floors mostly taken out, and salvaged Engine components hanging from the joists on chains, and six part-cannibalised machines on six benches in various stages of being either murdered or resurrected, and a wall of labelled jars containing nothing but gears, sorted by tooth count, and no clear floor anywhere at all.\n\nAnd it was warm. That was the thing that surprised him most. There was a stove going at the back and a kettle on it and two mismatched chairs, and somebody lived here, actually lived here, in among all of it.\n\n\"Ninth stratum,\" said Bramwell, over his shoulder.\n\nMarrow stopped in the doorway.\n\n\"Growl told you.\"\n\n\"Growl tells me everything, he's been telling me everything since he was nine, it's a curse.\" Bramwell was doing something to a spring vice with a file. \"Assessed fourth. Amended ninth. Prior conduct. Sirrath.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Right.\" The old man did not turn round. \"Now listen, because I'm going to say this once and then we're never going to discuss it again and if you bring it up I'll throw you out.\n\n\"There's two things you can do with that. One is you can spend the rest of your life being a man who was robbed. It's a whole trade, that. There's men in the Ashlight been doing it thirty years and they're very good at it and they've got the whole thing off by heart, and every one of them is exactly as clever now as he was at fourteen, because he stopped.\" He put the file down. \"The other thing you can do is get *better*. Not to show them. Not to prove anything. There's no proving anything, they've got the ledger and the ledger doesn't care. Just get better, because it's the only thing in this world that nobody can take off you with four lines and a stamp.\"\n\n\"That's the same speech Vane gives, isn't it,\" said Marrow. \"Just the other way round.\"\n\nBramwell turned round.\n\nHe looked at Marrow for a good four seconds and then he laughed, a horrible barking laugh, and slapped the bench.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"Yes it is, isn't it. Same speech. Same true thing at the bottom of it. And that's why he's had four goes at me and I've said no four times, and it isn't because I'm a better man than Silas Vane, because I'm not, I've done things you'd cross the road about.\" He pushed the goggles further up his forehead. \"It's because he uses it to point people at something and I use it to point people at a bench. That's the whole difference. It's not a big difference. Some days I can't find it.\"\n\nHe picked the file back up.\n\n\"Sweep,\" he said. \"Start at the back. There's forty years of it and I'll want the brass swarf kept separate, it's worth eight grist a pound.\"\n\n---\n\nHe swept for nine days. On the tenth day Bramwell let him hold a lamp while the old man worked, and asked him what he was looking at, and Marrow said, \"You're taking the governor out backwards,\" and Bramwell said, \"Why's that stupid?\" and Marrow told him, and Bramwell said, \"Right,\" and carried on taking it out backwards, and it came out, and it was the wrong way to do it and it worked.\n\n\"Why?\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Because the collar's swelled. You couldn't know that. You could know it if you'd had your hands on nine hundred of them.\" Bramwell put the governor on the bench. \"That's the whole of it, son. That's the only thing I've got that you haven't. It isn't cleverness. You're cleverer than me and we both know it and I'd not be able to sit in that hall for four hours doing what you did. It's nine hundred governors.\"\n\nMarrow held the lamp.\n\n\"They don't teach that up the Rise either,\" he said.\n\n\"No, they do not, and it's the reason half of them can't change a washer.\" Bramwell reached for a scraper. \"Now. You've been sweeping ten days and I've been watching you look at things, and I've got a question and you're to answer it honestly. What do you actually want?\"\n\nMarrow thought about it for a long time. The lamp hissed.\n\n\"I want a room with one person in it,\" he said. \"And I want to be paid in silver.\"\n\n\"That's not an answer, that's a wage.\"\n\n\"It's the only answer they'd let me have.\"\n\n\"That wasn't what I asked, was it.\" Bramwell held out his hand for the lamp. \"Try again. Not what you'd settle for. What do you *want*.\"\n\nAnd Marrow, who had not said it out loud to anybody, not to Ketu and not to the woman at the hatch and not to Growl on the end of a cot at Sister Maude's, heard himself say:\n\n\"I want to know what's actually in the Turn.\"\n\nBramwell went very still.\n\n\"Come again.\"\n\n\"Nobody knows.\" Marrow's voice came out faster than he meant it to. \"That's what I worked out, doing the old papers. Everybody talks about the Engine like it's a thing they understand and then when you get to the actual questions about it, the real ones, about what it's doing and how it's doing it, there's *nothing*. There's four books and they're all a hundred years old and they all say the same eleven pages and none of them says what happens inside. And I thought, right, that's because it's the true calculus and that's up the Rise and it's not for me. But it isn't up the Rise either, is it? I've read what they publish. They publish nothing. Not because it's secret. Because there's nothing to publish.\"\n\nThe stove ticked at the back of the shop.\n\n\"They don't know,\" said Marrow. \"Do they.\"\n\nBramwell put the lamp down on the bench very carefully, as though it had become heavier.\n\n\"Sit down,\" he said. \"The chair, not the bench with the cloth.\"\n\n---\n\n**III. Captain Reeve Ashcombe**\nShe had not slept, and she had stopped bothering to hide it.\n\nAshcombe had been watching officers stop sleeping for twenty-six years and he had catalogued the ways it showed. There was the drinking sort, and the loud sort, and the sort that got careless with their kit. Corvain had gone the other way, which was the way he had always found most difficult to manage, because it did not look like a problem. She had gone quiet and extremely precise. Her paperwork had got better. She had started coming in at fifth bell.\n\n\"Sit down,\" he said.\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"Sit *down*, and put the file on the desk, and don't open it.\"\n\nShe sat down. She put the file on the desk. She did not open it. She looked at him and waited, and the waiting was patient and absolutely immovable, and Ashcombe thought, not for the first time in six weeks, that Adelina Corvain had accidentally produced something considerably more dangerous than she had intended.\n\n\"Tell me what you've got,\" he said. \"Without the paper. Out loud, like a person.\"\n\n\"There are a hundred and three volumes of annual returns,\" said Corvain. \"I've read fifty-one. That's from the founding forward, and I did it that way round on purpose.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because if I'd started at this year I'd have known what I was looking for and found it.\"\n\nAshcombe grunted. That was the correct answer and it was not a common one.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"Conduct amendments don't appear in the returns for the first thirty-eight years. Not once. The word isn't in the volumes.\" She had her hands flat on her knees. \"They start in the thirty-ninth year, which is eighteen years after the founding generation died out. There are four of them in the first year they appear. Nine the next. Then it goes up, and it keeps going up, and by the sixtieth year it's running at about thirty a year, and it's never gone below thirty since.\"\n\n\"And this year?\"\n\n\"Forty-one. Same as the number of non-human candidates. But that's this year and it's the least interesting part.\" She paused. \"Sir, the amendments aren't about conduct. I can prove that now and I couldn't a fortnight ago.\"\n\n\"Prove it to me, then.\"\n\n\"An amendment has an assessed result and an amended result. Both are recorded, which was careless of them, but they had to be, because the examiners' pay used to be tied to assessment volume.\" She said it drily. \"So for a hundred and three years there is a written record of what every amended candidate actually scored before somebody changed it. Fourteen thousand of them.\"\n\nAshcombe sat forward.\n\n\"Fourteen thousand.\"\n\n\"Fourteen thousand two hundred and six. I counted.\" She met his eye. \"Sir, if conduct amendments were about conduct, then the assessed results of amended candidates would be spread across the strata the way anybody's are. Some high, most middling, some low. They are not. Ten thousand of the fourteen thousand were assessed in the top four strata.\"\n\nThe office was very quiet.\n\n\"Say that again,\" said Ashcombe.\n\n\"Seventy-one per cent of every conduct amendment in the history of the Calibration was applied to a child who had scored in the top four strata.\" Corvain's voice did not change at all. \"It is not a punishment system. Punishment doesn't care how well you did. It is a filter. It goes looking for the ones who did *best*, and it takes them out, and the record of what they actually scored is sitting in a green volume with gilt on the spine two floors under Tallow Street where nobody has looked at it since it was bound.\"\n\nAshcombe got up and went to the window.\n\nThe clatterlift cable ran up past the glass, hauling, and the building took the vibration the way it did forty times a day, and he stood there with his hands behind his back and let himself understand it properly, because there was no point in being a captain if you could not stand still for ten seconds and let a thing be true.\n\n\"Rustbound,\" he said.\n\n\"Sixty-eight per cent Rustbound. Twenty-two per cent non-human, which is enormously out of proportion. Ten per cent from the Rise, and I looked at those, sir, and every one of the ten per cent is from a minor house, and eleven of them are servants' children raised in Wrought households.\" She stopped. \"There is not one single amendment in a hundred and three years applied to a child of a major house. Not one. I checked twice and then I got a clerk to check it, and I did not tell him what he was checking for.\"\n\n\"Which clerk?\"\n\n\"Nobody who'll talk to anybody, sir.\"\n\n\"That's not an answer.\"\n\n\"No, sir.\"\n\nAshcombe turned round.\n\n\"Right,\" he said. \"Now the other question. The one you haven't asked yourself, and I want to see you not be able to answer it, because you need to know it's there before somebody else shows it to you.\" He came back to the desk. \"Why?\"\n\nCorvain opened her mouth and stopped.\n\n\"You've got the *what*,\" said Ashcombe. \"The what is beautiful and it's airtight and I'd take it to a magistrate tomorrow. Now tell me why. Why does the Council of Gifford spend a hundred and three years and God knows how much money finding the cleverest children in this city and then reaching into the ledger and pushing them back down. Because if the answer is spite, or snobbery, or keeping the rust in the Under, then it's a bad system run by bad people and we go to the magistrate and win. And you don't believe that, do you.\"\n\n\"No, sir.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\n\"Because it's too expensive,\" said Corvain. \"Spite doesn't build a filing system. Somebody has been maintaining this for a hundred and three years across, what, twenty Chancellors, and it has never once leaked, and the mechanism is fussy and administrative and it costs real money to run. People don't do that out of contempt. They do that because it's load-bearing.\" She looked up. \"Somebody thinks the city falls down without it. That's the only thing that fits.\"\n\nAshcombe sat down heavily.\n\n\"Good,\" he said. \"Then you're where I am. And Corvain, I want you to hear the next bit properly, because I'm going to say it once.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"When you find out why, and you will, you are going to be offered something.\" He said it flatly. \"Not a threat. They don't threaten, at that level, it's vulgar and it leaves a mark. You're going to be offered a chair. Somebody very calm is going to sit you down and show you a number, and the number is going to be real, and they're going to say: now you know, and you can see why, and we'd like you to help.\n\n\"And here is the thing, and this is the only piece of it I have ever had to work out for myself. The number will be real. The reason will be *good*. It'll be a proper reason, not an excuse, and you'll believe it, because it'll be true.\" He looked at her. \"And it still won't be the answer. Because a good reason for doing a wrong thing quietly for a hundred years is how you get a city where four clerks and a docket slip hold up the whole arrangement and nobody has thought about it since before your grandmother was born.\"\n\n\"Sir,\" said Corvain. \"How do you know what they'll offer?\"\n\nAshcombe was silent for a moment.\n\n\"Because it's a small city,\" he said, \"and I'm fifty-five, and I've been in this coat a long time. Take the file. Get some sleep. And Corvain.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"When you go up against it, come to me first. Not because I can protect you. I can't, and you should be clear about that.\" He pushed the file across. \"Because I'd like the chance to decide what I am before somebody decides it for me.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Whistle**\nThe shop on Cull Alley had a low ceiling, and Whistle had not stood up in it since the second year, which was nineteen years ago.\n\nThat was fine. Whistle sat. The counter had been built to be worked at while sitting, and the pigeonholes went floor to ceiling on every wall, four thousand of them, and every one of them held folded paper, and the arrangement of that paper was not written down anywhere and never would be.\n\nIt had been a busy month.\n\nThree separate parties had come asking about the Calibration returns, and none of the three knew about the other two, and Whistle had served all three, and the pleasure of it was not the money.\n\n**The first** had been the young Sirrath, and he had come in September with eight grist and a homemade pressure gauge he was clearly not willing to part with, and he had asked, straight out, with no attempt at cleverness at all: *Who signed my amendment.*\n\nWhistle had told him: nobody signs an amendment. That is the answer and it is worth what he paid, which was nothing, because Whistle had told him for free. There is no signature block on the form. It is initialled by a clerk under a delegated authority and the delegation is renewed every quarter by an office rather than a person. There has not been a name on an amendment since the fifty-first year.\n\nThe boy had stood in the shop for a long moment and then said, \"That's on purpose.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then who set it up that way?\"\n\nAnd Whistle had said: *that is a different question, and it costs.*\n\n**The second** had been the officer. Grey coat, twenty, far too polite, and she had come in November and asked something so specific that Whistle had put down the pen.\n\n*Has anybody ever, at any time, tried to publish the assessed results.*\n\nNot the amendments. The assessed results. She had already worked out that the crime was recorded in the ledger, and what she wanted to know was whether anybody had ever tried to take it out of the ledger and into the street, and what had happened to them.\n\nWhistle had quoted her four sovereigns, which was deliberately outrageous, to find out whether she had four sovereigns and whether she would pay them.\n\nShe had paid.\n\n**The third** had come in December, and the third was the interesting one, because the third had not come at all. The third had sent a letter, from an address on the Rise, in a hand that had been taught to write properly by somebody who was paid to teach it, and the letter had contained no name and one question and a folded note for six marks.\n\n*What does a person on the Rise buy, that is legal, that pays well, and that they cannot be sent home for?*\n\nWhistle had read that four times.\n\nIt was not the question of somebody wanting to break a rule. That was the ordinary trade; Whistle sold rule-breaking eleven times a day. This was somebody who had established, carefully, in advance, that they could not afford to be sent home, and who was therefore looking for the narrow band of things that were both lucrative and defensible, and who had worked out that such a band existed and that somebody would know where it was.\n\nThat was a fourteen year old asking an adult question.\n\nWhistle had turned the six marks over on the counter for a while and had then done something not done in nineteen years of trading, which was to answer at less than the going rate.\n\nThe reply had been three lines.\n\n*Contentions. The university permits them and the Council does not regulate them because they are a tradition of the Rise and nobody has ever needed to. Stakes are set by the parties and are enforceable as a debt of honour, which in that building means enforceable in fact.*\n\n*Nobody has staked money in one for forty years. There is no rule against it.*\n\n*You will only be able to do this twice.*\n\n---\n\nWhistle logged all three in the pigeonholes, in three different places, under three systems.\n\nThen, at the end of the month, sitting alone in the shop with the lamp hooded and the slate wiped, Whistle did the thing that made the money worth having, which was to lay the three of them side by side.\n\nA Sirrath boy asking who built the amendment mechanism.\n\nAn officer asking whether anybody had ever tried to publish the assessed results, and what had become of them.\n\nA child on the Rise asking how to earn money without asking permission.\n\nThree people, all fourteen or thereabouts, all set in motion by the same examination in the same summer, all pulling on the same rope from three different ends, and not one of them aware that the others existed.\n\nWhistle sat with that for a long time and then wrote a single line on a fresh slip and put it in a pigeonhole that had nothing else in it.\n\n*They will all three come back. Raise the price.*\n\n---\n\n**V. The Calculus Duel**\nA Contention was declared in the Long Hall, at supper, in front of everybody, because that was the entire point of it and had been for two hundred years.\n\nLord Antony Vex stood up between the second and third courses and did it perfectly, and Rooke, who had known it was coming since Tuesday because Ivy Thorncastle heard everything, sat with her hands in her lap and watched him do it and thought: he has practised this.\n\n\"Fellows and scholars.\" He had a good voice. Eighteen, tall, immaculately turned out in a way that took either forty minutes or a servant. \"I ask leave to declare a Contention.\"\n\nThe Long Hall did the thing halls do, which is go quiet in patches and then all at once.\n\n\"Against whom?\" said the Proctor, wearily, from the high table.\n\n\"The first-year scholar Rooke.\"\n\nThere was a sound in the room, a kind of collective intake, and Rooke heard about four separate people laugh and stop.\n\n\"On what matter?\"\n\n\"On the matter of the fourth-year seminar.\"\n\nAnd there it was. Rooke had wondered how he would frame it and he had chosen well, because it was the only genuine grievance available and everybody in that hall knew about it. Thorncastle had moved Rooke into the fourth-year seminar in the ninth week of her first term. It had never been done. It had never been done because it could not be done; you could not sit in the fourth-year seminar without three years of the notation, and Rooke had walked in with eleven weeks of it and had, on the third Thursday, corrected the Reader.\n\n\"The scholar Rooke has been advanced past three years of study on the recommendation of one Professor,\" said Vex, \"and the rest of us are asked to take that on faith. I don't ask her to be removed. I don't wish her removed. I ask her to demonstrate it, in the hall, in the old way, so that nobody has to take anything on faith at all.\"\n\nHe said it reasonably. He was very good at reasonably.\n\n\"That's actually fair,\" Ivy muttered.\n\n\"I know,\" said Rooke. \"That's what's clever about it.\"\n\n\"Scholar Rooke,\" said the Proctor. \"Do you accept?\"\n\nRooke stood up.\n\nSix weeks ago she would have said yes out of temper, and she knew that about herself now, and she had spent three days making very sure that she was not going to.\n\n\"I accept,\" she said. \"I'd like to name the stake.\"\n\nThe Proctor blinked. \"The stake is traditionally—\"\n\n\"The stake is traditionally set by the parties,\" said Rooke. \"I looked it up. Statute of the Long Hall, article nine. *Stakes to be agreed between the contending parties before the setting of the problems, and thereafter enforceable upon the honour of the houses concerned.* There's no restriction on what the stake can be. There used to be a Contention over a horse.\"\n\nA long silence.\n\n\"Well,\" said Vex, with a small smile, because he could feel the room enjoying itself and he was not stupid. \"Name it.\"\n\n\"Sixty marks.\"\n\nThe Long Hall made a noise.\n\nIt was, Rooke would think later, one of the more interesting noises she had ever caused. It was not outrage. It was worse than outrage. It was embarrassment: two hundred people all simultaneously experiencing the specific social discomfort of hearing money mentioned by name at supper.\n\n\"Money,\" said Vex.\n\n\"Sixty marks.\"\n\n\"You want to stake *money*.\"\n\n\"I want you to stake money,\" said Rooke. \"I haven't got sixty marks. If I lose I'll take a forfeit, whatever you like, I'll clean the seminar boards for a year, I don't care. If I win, sixty marks.\"\n\n\"That is not—\" Vex laughed, and looked round for the room, and got it, and Rooke watched him get it and felt something cold and useful settle in her stomach. \"Forgive me. That is not what a Contention is *for*.\"\n\n\"No,\" Rooke agreed. \"It's for one person proving something to a room. I've read the statute and it doesn't say what either party has to want out of it. You want to make a point. I want sixty marks. There's no rule that says we have to want the same thing.\"\n\n\"You are asking me to *pay* you.\"\n\n\"I'm asking you to risk sixty marks on being right about me,\" said Rooke. \"You've just stood up in front of two hundred people and said you're sure. Sixty marks is nine days' allowance to you. I looked that up as well.\"\n\nThe room went very quiet indeed.\n\nAnd Vex, who had been raised from birth by people who had taught him that the one unforgivable thing is to be seen to flinch, said:\n\n\"Done.\"\n\n---\n\nThey cleared the floor of the Long Hall and brought in the two slate boards, back to back, which was how it had always been done so that neither party could see the other's working, and the Proctor set the problems from a sealed envelope prepared by two Readers who were not in the room.\n\nBest of three. No books, no tables, no instruments.\n\nRooke took off her coat and rolled her sleeves back and picked up the chalk, and while the Proctor was reading the preamble she did the only piece of thinking that actually mattered, which was this:\n\n*He is very good. He is properly, genuinely good, and everybody keeps telling me he's arrogant as though that makes him bad at it, and it doesn't. He has had twelve years of the best teaching in Gifford and he does not have to slow down at section nine.*\n\n*So do not race him. He is faster than me at the things he has practised, and he has practised everything on the syllabus.*\n\n*Beat him at the shape of it.*\n\n**The first problem** went to Vex in four minutes. It was a standard reduction. He did it beautifully and he did it fast and there was applause and he turned round and inclined his head to it, and Rooke was still on her third line.\n\nShe had known that would happen. It still hurt more than she had budgeted for.\n\n**The second problem** was longer and had a trick in it, and Rooke found the trick at ninety seconds and Vex found it at about two and a half minutes, and she took it by a margin of perhaps forty seconds, and there was almost no applause at all because two hundred people were now paying a completely different kind of attention.\n\n**The third problem** the Proctor read out twice, because it was long.\n\nIt concerned a periodic system with a driving force applied at an interval, and it asked for the condition under which the system remained stable, and it was fourth-year work and it was on the syllabus and Antony Vex had done nine of them.\n\nHe started immediately. Rooke heard the chalk go on the other side of the board and did not move.\n\nShe stood there for a hundred and ten seconds with her hands at her sides.\n\nSomebody in the hall started to whisper and was hushed.\n\nBecause there was something wrong with the problem, and she could see it, and she had seen it in the first fifteen seconds, and she had spent the next ninety-five making absolutely certain, because a girl who has been in this building eleven weeks does not casually decide that two Readers have set a broken question.\n\nThe interval in the driving force. It was not arbitrary. It had been chosen to look arbitrary, and it was in fact exactly the interval that made the standard method fail, because the standard method assumed you could treat two things as independent and at that specific interval they were not independent, they were the same thing looked at twice, and if you ran the standard method you would get a clean, plausible, entirely wrong condition, and it would look right, and it would be wrong in the fourth line.\n\n*You have assumed the relation holds in both directions and it does not.*\n\nThorncastle had taught her that in ten minutes on a Thursday in September, off the back of a diagnostic sheet, because Rooke had written *I am wrong and I cannot see where and I would like to be told*.\n\nIt was not on the syllabus. Rooke was suddenly and completely certain of that. It was not on the syllabus, and Vasterling had it wrong in a footnote two hundred years ago, and it had sat there ever since, and the two Readers who set this problem had almost certainly set it out of Vasterling.\n\nShe thought: they don't know either.\n\nThen she thought: if I write the right answer, I lose.\n\nBecause she would be marked against the model solution, and the model solution would come out of the same book, and the Proctor would read it and see that Vex's answer matched and hers did not, and she would lose sixty marks and Ferrow Lane would be four weeks on the rent going on five.\n\nRooke stood in front of the slate with the chalk in her hand and the whole hall watching, and she made the decision in about two seconds, and it was not a noble decision and she was clear-eyed about that afterwards. It was arithmetic. She had run it and the honest answer paid better.\n\nShe wrote the wrong solution first.\n\nShe wrote it out in full, the standard method, cleanly, quickly, exactly as the model would have it, and she wrote it small and she wrote it high on the board so there was room underneath.\n\nThen she drew a line under it.\n\nThen, underneath, she wrote:\n\n*The above is the answer to the question as set. It is wrong. The interval given makes the fourth line invalid, because at that interval the two terms are not independent. The problem as set has no stable condition of the form requested. Below is the actual condition. It does not appear in Vasterling, who has the same error in a footnote on page 340.*\n\nAnd then she did it properly, and it took her nine minutes, and it filled the rest of the board.\n\nShe put the chalk down.\n\n\"Finished,\" said Antony Vex, from the other side.\n\n\"Finished,\" said Rooke.\n\n---\n\nThey turned the boards.\n\nThe Proctor read Vex's board and nodded, because it was clean and it was correct against the model, and there was applause, and Vex stood with his hands behind his back and took it well.\n\nThen the Proctor came round to Rooke's board.\n\nHe read the top third, and his face did nothing.\n\nHe read the line.\n\nThen he read the paragraph underneath it, and stopped, and read it again, and then turned and looked at Rooke, and then turned back and read the nine minutes of working below it, all of it, slowly, in front of two hundred silent people, and it took him a very long time.\n\nThen he said, to nobody in particular: \"Fetch Professor Thorncastle.\"\n\n\"She's at dinner in the Deanery,\" said somebody.\n\n\"I am aware of where she is.\"\n\n---\n\nThorncastle came in with her napkin still in her hand.\n\nShe read the board from four feet away without moving. Then she came closer and read the paragraph. Then she said, out loud, in a voice that carried to the back of the Long Hall:\n\n\"Page three hundred and forty.\"\n\n\"Professor,\" said the Proctor.\n\n\"I have been telling this university about page three hundred and forty for nineteen years.\" Thorncastle turned round. Her colour was very high. \"I have raised it in three faculty meetings. There is a note from me in the seminar file dated the year before this child was born. And it is *still* being set out of Vasterling, in the fourth year, by two Readers who have both been through my seminar, and it took a first-term scholar eleven weeks off Ferrow Lane one hundred and ten seconds to see it from the far side of a slate board.\"\n\nShe turned to the high table.\n\n\"I should like it minuted,\" she said, \"that the problem was defective, and that the model solution is defective, and that if this Contention is decided against her then this hall will have formally resolved that the correct answer loses.\"\n\nThe silence went on for some time.\n\nThen Antony Vex said, from the other side of the boards: \"May I see it?\"\n\nHe came round. He read the top third. He read the line and the paragraph. Then he read the working, and he read it properly, with his hands behind his back, and it took him about three minutes, and nobody in the hall said a word.\n\nThen he straightened up.\n\nRooke watched his face and understood, with a lurch of something that was not at all what she had expected to feel, that Antony Vex was not stupid, and was not a coward, and had just been shown a thing he was going to have to carry around for the rest of his life.\n\n\"The Contention is hers,\" he said.\n\n\"Lord Antony—\"\n\n\"It's hers, Proctor. I answered the question. She answered the *problem*.\" He said it steadily, and only somebody standing where Rooke was standing could have heard what it cost. \"And I would rather be the man who lost sixty marks than the man who stood in this hall and argued that being right was outside the terms.\"\n\nHe turned to Rooke.\n\n\"Sixty marks,\" he said. \"You'll have it in the morning.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"I don't like you,\" said Vex.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I want to be clear that this has not changed that at all.\"\n\n\"That's all right,\" said Rooke. \"I need the money, not the friend.\"\n\nAnd Antony Vex laughed, once, entirely without meaning to, and then looked extremely annoyed about having done it, and walked out of the Long Hall with his shoulders back.\n\n---\n\nIvy found her afterwards, sitting on the steps outside with her coat over her knees, in the cold, doing something with a pencil on the back of Tessa's letter.\n\n\"You won sixty marks,\" said Ivy.\n\n\"Twelve weeks,\" said Rooke.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Sixty marks. The shortfall's five marks a week.\" Rooke did not look up. \"Twelve weeks. That's what it is. Everyone in there thinks I just won an argument about a footnote and what I actually won is that nobody gets put out on the step until March.\"\n\nIvy sat down on the step next to her.\n\n\"You said you'd only be able to do that twice,\" she said.\n\nRooke's pencil stopped.\n\n\"I never said that to you.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Ivy. \"You said it to yourself on Tuesday, out loud, in the library, and I was in the gallery looking for something on the Vaskeld famine and you didn't know I was up there.\" She pulled her knees up. \"Why twice?\"\n\nRooke was quiet for a moment.\n\n\"Because it only works while they think I might lose,\" she said.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Four.*" },
  { n: 5, title: "Hungrier Science", text: "**I. Rooke**\nShe went down on the clatterlift with sixty marks in a cloth bag against her ribs and a lie already prepared, and she used the lie on the porter at the university gate and it worked, and that was the first thing that told her she had changed.\n\nSix months ago she could not have lied to a porter. Six months ago she had been a girl who put eleven on sixty-eight lines because she could not think of a cheaper way to be dishonest. Now she said, \"Professor Thorncastle's sent me to the instrument-maker's on Fenn Street,\" without a flicker, and the porter wrote it in his book, and she was through.\n\nThe lift went down the face of the Rise in eleven minutes. Rooke stood at the front with her forehead nearly on the glass and watched the light change.\n\nIt went in stages. That was the thing you did not notice going up, because going up you were being taken away from something and you did not look. Going down you watched it happen: clean grey daylight at the top, then the first level of the fog, thin, and everything went sepia, and then thicker, and the gaslights started appearing in the murk below you like things underwater, and by the last two minutes the windows had gone brown and you could see the lamps on the cables and nothing else at all.\n\nThe doors opened onto the seam and the smell hit her, and Rooke had not once in eleven weeks thought about the smell, and she stood in the doorway of the lift car with her hand on the frame and understood that she had forgotten it.\n\nCoke smoke. Wet iron. The dyer's works. Something rotten in the sumps under the arches. Bread. It was the smell of her entire life and she had lost it in eleven weeks, and getting it back should have felt like coming home and did not. It felt like the first mouthful of food on the second day at the university, when she had eaten too much and been sick.\n\nShe was wearing a coat that fitted her.\n\nThat was the other thing, and she did not work it out until Ferrow Lane. She had come down in her own clothes, deliberately, the mended coat, the boots, and she had thought that was enough. It was not. Because the coat had been washed properly eleven times by somebody who was paid to wash things, and she had been eating four times a day for eleven weeks, and she had grown three quarters of an inch, and the coat which had been two sizes too large in the summer now nearly fitted her, and Rooke came round onto Ferrow Lane and the first person she saw was Barrow the rent man and he did not recognise her.\n\n---\n\nTam knew her from the end of the lane and came at her at a dead run and hit her about waist height and would not let go, and that was the only easy thing that happened all day.\n\nPip hung back in the doorway. Pip had been six weeks off seven when Rooke left and had climbed into the foot of her bed at second bell every night for four years, and Pip stood in the doorway with one hand on the frame and looked at her and would not come out.\n\n\"Pip.\"\n\nNothing.\n\n\"Pip, it's me.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Pip.\n\nAnd went inside.\n\n---\n\nMother Halloway was in the front room, because they had brought the bed down. That was the thing Rooke saw before she saw anything else and it went into her like a nail: they had brought the bed down from the back room, which meant nine days on the stairs had become a decision about the stairs, and nobody had put that in a letter.\n\n\"There she is,\" said Halloway.\n\nHer voice had gone. Not weak; wrecked. There was a thing in the bottom of it that rattled.\n\nRooke sat down on the edge of the bed and took her hand and did not say anything for a while, which was, she understood later, the only genuinely right thing she did that day.\n\n\"You've grown,\" said Halloway.\n\n\"Three quarters of an inch.\"\n\n\"Course you've measured it.\" The old woman laughed and then paid for the laugh for about fifteen seconds. \"Well. That's what it's for, isn't it. Feeding you.\"\n\n\"Mother, I've got sixty marks.\"\n\nHalloway's hand tightened on hers.\n\n\"Rooke.\"\n\n\"It's *mine*. Nobody's given it to me and I haven't done anything wrong to get it, I won it, there's a thing they do up there called a Contention and it's a formal thing with a statute and a Proctor and it's older than the Engine and there's no rule against staking money and I won it in front of two hundred people.\" It came out fast and she heard it come out fast. \"Sixty marks. That's the rent clear and the arrears clear and Nettle's syrup and something over.\"\n\n\"You won it.\"\n\n\"I beat a boy called Antony Vex at the fourth-year reckoning in front of the whole hall and he paid me the next morning in silver and he was decent about it, which I did not expect.\"\n\nHalloway lay still for a moment.\n\n\"Sixty marks,\" she said. \"In front of two hundred people.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So two hundred people up there now know that the girl from Ferrow Lane needs money.\"\n\nRooke stopped.\n\nShe had run that sum eleven ways. She had run it for three days before she accepted the Contention, and she had run it while she was standing in front of the slate, and she had run it on the steps afterwards with Ivy, and she had not run it that way. Not once.\n\n\"Yes,\" she said.\n\n\"Mm.\"\n\n\"That's a cost. I hadn't counted it.\"\n\n\"No, love.\" Halloway's thumb moved on the back of her hand. \"You never count that one. None of you ever do, and I've had eleven of you.\" She turned her head on the pillow. \"You always count the money and you never count what people know about you. It's the same in a lane and it's the same up a hill. What people know about you is the whole of it.\"\n\n---\n\nNettle came down at four, wrapped in the blanket, and sat on the end of the bed and did not cough for nine whole minutes, and Rooke sat and counted them, and then Nettle coughed, three times and then the wet catch, and Rooke's whole body went cold in a way it had not done in eleven weeks.\n\n\"You've still got it.\"\n\n\"It's better,\" said Nettle.\n\n\"You've had the syrup?\"\n\n\"Two bottles. In August.\" Nettle picked at the blanket. \"Tessa said.\"\n\n\"And it's better?\"\n\n\"It's better than August.\"\n\nRooke looked at her. Nine years old, thin, flushed across the cheekbones in a way that was not health.\n\n\"Nettle. Look at me and tell me honestly. Is it better or is it the same and you've stopped mentioning it?\"\n\nNettle looked at the blanket.\n\n\"It's the same,\" she said.\n\n---\n\nShe did not tell them she was going. She got up at half past eight to catch the last lift and Tessa followed her out onto the step.\n\n\"Say it,\" said Rooke.\n\n\"I wasn't going to say anything.\"\n\n\"You've been going to say something since two o'clock. Say it.\"\n\nTessa stood in the doorway with her arms folded against the cold. She was seventeen now and she looked about thirty and she had ink on the side of her right hand, which had never in her life been true before.\n\n\"Don't come back for a while,\" she said.\n\nRooke did not move.\n\n\"Not like that. I'm saying it wrong, I always say it wrong, that's why you did the letters.\" Tessa pushed her hair back. \"Pip wouldn't come out of the doorway. Did you see?\"\n\n\"I saw.\"\n\n\"Pip cried for two hours after you went in August. Not while you were here. After.\" Tessa's voice was steady and it was costing her. \"And Pip's fine most days now, and then you come down for six hours and the whole thing starts again, and you go up on the lift, and I'm the one that's here at second bell. So don't come back for a while. Come back at the end of the year. Write. Write more, write every week, Marta'll read them out and she doesn't charge for that, she only charges for writing.\"\n\n\"That's not—\"\n\n\"And take the money back.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Take some of it back.\" Tessa's chin came up. \"Not all of it. Keep forty down here, that's the rent clear and the arrears and Nettle. Take twenty back up with you.\"\n\n\"I don't need twenty marks. Everything's paid for up there, that's the whole point of it—\"\n\n\"Rooke.\" Tessa stepped down off the step. \"You went up that hill with two shifts and one pair of boots. And you've been down here six hours and you've told us about a Contention and a Proctor and a statute and a boy called Vex, and I've been listening, and I don't understand one word in four, and I'm not stupid, I'm slow at columns and I'm not stupid.\" She was crying and ignoring it. \"You had to fight a lord in front of two hundred people to get sixty marks. What happens the next time we need money? Are you going to fight another one?\"\n\nRooke said nothing.\n\n\"That's what I thought.\" Tessa wiped her face with the back of her wrist. \"So take twenty. Because you're going to need to be able to say no to something up there, one day, and you can't say no to anything if you haven't got a penny of your own. That's not me being clever. That's just what happens to people.\"\n\nRooke stood in the lane with the fog coming up.\n\n\"Where did you learn that?\" she said.\n\n\"Marta,\" said Tessa. \"Forty years behind a counter. She says everyone who's ever gone under did it because they couldn't afford to walk out of a room.\"\n\n---\n\nRooke went up on the last lift with twenty marks in the cloth bag and Ferrow Lane going brown and then sepia and then grey below her, and about halfway she stopped looking down, and she was not proud of that, and she did it anyway.\n\n---\n\n**II. Marrow**\n\"Nobody knows what's in it,\" said Bramwell. \"That's not a secret. It's just not a thing anybody says out loud, which isn't the same. Sit still and hold the light.\"\n\nThey were four hours into a job on a pressure governor off a dray, and the shop was warm, and it was the fourth week and Marrow had learned by now that Bramwell only talked properly when his hands were busy.\n\n\"You've been inside it,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Twice. Nineteen and thirty-one years ago. Both times on a contract, both times with a gang of eleven, both times in and out in a shift and never past the second gallery.\" Bramwell held out his hand for the scraper. \"And I'll tell you what I saw, because it's not much and it's got about in the trade anyway. It's big. That's the first thing and everybody says it and nobody's ready for it. You go in expecting a machine and it's a *building*, and the building's the machine, and there's cogwheels in there the size of this shop turning about as fast as an hour hand and you can't hear them, you can *feel* them, in your teeth.\"\n\n\"And the second thing?\"\n\n\"The second thing.\" Bramwell worked at the collar for a while. \"Half of it's not on the plans.\"\n\nMarrow shifted the lamp.\n\n\"Say that again.\"\n\n\"There's a plan set. There's four copies and they're in the Engine houses and they're a hundred and three years old and I've had my hands on a page of one, which is not a thing I'm going to tell you how.\" Bramwell squinted. \"And the machine that's in that hole is not the machine on the plan. It's bigger. There's whole banks of it that aren't drawn anywhere, and they're not new, they're as old as the rest of it, they're the same brass and the same casting marks, they were built at the same time by the same people. They just aren't on the plan.\"\n\n\"Why would you build a thing and not draw it?\"\n\n\"You wouldn't.\"\n\n\"Then—\"\n\n\"Unless you drew it,\" said Bramwell, \"and then somebody took the drawing out.\"\n\nHe set the scraper down.\n\n\"Third thing,\" he said. \"And this one's mine, and I've never said it to anybody but Growl, and Growl thinks I'm mad, which is fair.\"\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"The year before you were born, the eastern feed went down. You'll not remember it, nobody does, it was nine hours and they said it was a valve.\" Bramwell wiped his hands. \"It wasn't a valve. I was on the gang that went in after. What had happened was one of the Engine house reckoners had set a governor wrong, up on the fourth gallery, and it had put the whole eastern bank about a twelfth out of true, and nine hours later it came back on its own and it came back *right*.\"\n\n\"They fixed it.\"\n\n\"They did not fix it. That's the point. Nobody touched it. There were forty of us stood in that gallery for nine hours and the reckoners were up there arguing about the calculus and not one hand went on that machine, and at the eighth hour the whole eastern bank ran itself back through about eleven positions, one at a time, slow, like somebody counting, and stopped, and the feed came up.\"\n\nThe stove ticked.\n\n\"That's a governor resetting,\" said Marrow. \"That's just a feedback loop. That's not—\"\n\n\"It ran back eleven positions and stopped at the *right one*, son. Not at the middle. Not at the stop. At the one position out of a hundred and something that made the whole eastern bank true, and it did it in eleven separate moves, and there's no linkage in a governor that does that, and I have taken nine hundred governors apart.\" Bramwell picked the scraper up again. \"So either there's a piece of that machine that isn't on the plan and isn't in the books and does something nobody's written down, or I'm an old man who saw what he wanted to see in a bad shift thirty-one years ago.\"\n\n\"Which do you think?\"\n\n\"I think both, most days.\" Bramwell went back to the collar. \"But I'll tell you the bit that keeps me up. It took eight hours. And there were eleven reckoners on that gallery with the best education in this city, shouting at each other in the true calculus, and not one of them worked it out in eight hours. And the machine did it in eleven moves.\"\n\nMarrow held the lamp and did not say anything for a long time.\n\n\"Bramwell.\"\n\n\"Mm.\"\n\n\"What if it wasn't doing it on its own?\"\n\n\"Meaning?\"\n\n\"What if it was waiting for somebody to ask it properly,\" said Marrow, \"and nobody in that gallery could, so after eight hours it gave up and did it itself.\"\n\nBramwell stopped working.\n\nHe sat back on his stool and looked at Marrow for a long moment with the goggles pushed up on his forehead, and there was something in his face that Marrow could not read at all.\n\n\"Don't say that to anyone,\" he said.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because I've had that thought,\" said Bramwell, \"and I've had it for thirty-one years, and I've never once said it out loud, and you got to it in four weeks off the back of me telling you a story about a valve.\" He turned back to the bench. \"And because the last man I know of who said that sort of thing in public was a reckoner named Halloran and he was writing a book about it, and the book never came out, and he went to a chair at the university in Vaskeld in the summer of the year I turned forty.\"\n\n\"What happened to him?\"\n\n\"Nothing,\" said Bramwell. \"That's the trouble. Absolutely nothing happened to him at all. He went to Vaskeld and he had a very nice career and he died at seventy-six and he never wrote another word about the Engine.\"\n\n---\n\n**III. Ratchet**\nRatchet liked the boy, which was inconvenient, and he had known it was going to be inconvenient from about the second week.\n\nHis actual name was Ratcliffe and nobody had called him that since he was eleven, and he was twenty, and he had been with the Ashlight for four years, which made him neither new nor anybody. He ran messages. He kept the book on the Tuesday game. He sat on a crate in the yard behind the Ashlight most afternoons eating something out of paper, and because he sat in the yard he saw everybody who came and went, and because he was harmless nobody minded what he saw.\n\nMarrow came through the yard three times in December, and never once to see Vane.\n\n\"He's not here,\" Ratchet said, the first time.\n\n\"I'm not looking for him.\"\n\n\"Right.\" Ratchet moved his legs off the crate. \"Growl's inside.\"\n\n\"I'm not looking for Growl either. There's a man called Ferris does castings out of the shed on the far side and Bramwell wants a pattern off him and won't come himself.\"\n\n\"Why won't he come himself?\"\n\n\"Because Vane's asked him four times,\" said Marrow, \"and he says if he walks through this yard it counts as an answer.\"\n\nRatchet laughed at that for about a minute.\n\n---\n\nBy the third time they had got as far as sitting on the crate together, and Ratchet had a bag of chestnuts and gave him half, and Marrow told him about the eastern feed going down the year before he was born, and Ratchet did not understand one word in three of it and enjoyed himself enormously, because nobody in the Ashlight had ever once talked to him about anything except the Tuesday book.\n\nThen Marrow said, \"What does Vane actually do?\"\n\nAnd Ratchet stopped chewing.\n\nBecause that was the question, and Ratchet had been in the yard four years and had watched perhaps nine boys come through it, and every single one of them had asked that question at some point, and the ones who asked it early were the ones who were going to be all right and the ones who asked it late were already in.\n\n\"Depends who's asking,\" said Ratchet.\n\n\"I'm asking.\"\n\n\"Right. Then, cogging. Parts. Everybody knows that.\" Ratchet turned a chestnut over. \"There's a market in Engine components because the Engine houses hold the writs and if you haven't got a writ you can't buy a bearing, and there's about four thousand small workshops in this city that need bearings and can't have them, so there's a trade. He runs the trade. That's about sixty per cent of it.\"\n\n\"And the rest?\"\n\n\"The rest is that he lends money,\" said Ratchet. \"That's the bit nobody says. And he lends it cheap, and he lends it to people the chalk won't cover, and he does not break your fingers, and that's why half the Under would put him in the Council tomorrow.\"\n\n\"So what's wrong with it?\"\n\nRatchet looked at his chestnuts for a while.\n\n\"He doesn't want it back,\" he said.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"The money. He doesn't want it back. He'd rather you couldn't pay.\" Ratchet put the paper down. \"Because if you can't pay he doesn't do anything about it, he just doesn't do anything about it *for years*, and then one day there's a thing he needs doing and it's you that does it, and you say yes, and you don't say yes because you're frightened, you say yes because you're *ashamed*, and that's a much better hold on a man than frightened. Frightened wears off.\"\n\n\"How do you know that?\"\n\n\"Because I owe him nine marks from when I was sixteen,\" said Ratchet cheerfully, \"and he's never mentioned it, not once, not in four years, and I think about it about nine times a day.\"\n\nMarrow was quiet.\n\n\"You should stay at Bramwell's,\" said Ratchet.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"No, you don't. Everybody says they know.\" Ratchet was not looking at him. \"Growl told you it's the last honest room in the Under, right? He tells everybody that. And what he doesn't tell you is why he says it, and it's not because he wants you saved. It's because Growl came up through this yard at fifteen and there was no Bramwell's for him, and every time he sends someone up there it's a bet he's placing against himself nineteen years too late.\"\n\n\"That's not a bad thing.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Ratchet. \"But it means he'll only send you once. He's not going to come and get you.\" He picked the paper back up. \"Nobody's going to come and get you. That's the actual thing about the Ashlight and it took me four years. There's no door. Nobody drags you in. You just keep coming through the yard because the yard's warm and people talk to you, and then one day there's a thing that needs doing.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Corvain**\nShe stopped reading the volumes in the middle of December and started finding the people, and it took her three weeks to understand that this had been a mistake, and by then she could not stop.\n\nThe first was a woman called Ada Marchbank, sixty-one, who kept a pipe-fitter's yard off the Grieves Steps and who had been assessed in the second stratum in the sixty-fourth year and amended to the tenth.\n\nCorvain sat in her office among the fittings with a cup of tea she did not want and asked her, carefully, what she remembered about the Calibration.\n\n\"Nothing,\" said Ada Marchbank. \"It was forty-seven years ago.\"\n\n\"Nothing at all?\"\n\n\"I remember I was sick on the way in.\" She shrugged. \"I remember the card. Tenth. And my mother took it very hard and I told her not to be daft because we all knew I wasn't clever.\"\n\n\"Did you think you weren't clever?\"\n\n\"I *knew* I wasn't. I'd had the card.\" Marchbank laughed. \"That's what the card's for, love.\"\n\nAnd Corvain sat there with the volume in her bag, in which it was written in a clerk's hand that Ada Marchbank had scored second stratum out of six hundred and eighty candidates in the sixty-fourth year, second, out of the entire city, and that eleven days later somebody had made it tenth on grounds of adverse conduct, and the conduct was that her father had been banked twice for drink.\n\nShe did not show her.\n\nShe had gone to that yard fully intending to show her. She had the volume in her bag and she had the page marked, and she sat in the office and she looked at a sixty-one year old woman laughing about not being clever, and she understood that if she opened the bag she would be handing over forty-seven years, and that Ada Marchbank had built an entire life on top of that card and had built it *well*, and that there was no version of the next ten minutes that did not take a hammer to it.\n\nShe said thank you for the tea and she left.\n\nShe was sick in an alley off the Grieves Steps and then she went home.\n\n---\n\nThe second was a man called Corrin Sallow, fifty-three, who had been assessed third and amended ninth, and who now worked the water gangs in the outfalls, and who had a wet cough and eleven fingers' worth of scars and who asked her twice whether she was from the Council about the money.\n\nCorvain did not show him either.\n\n---\n\nThe third one she did not choose. The third one chose her, because on the eleventh of January a note came to the duty room at Tallow Street in a careful hand, and it said:\n\n*You have been to see two of them. There will be more. Before you go to a third, come and see me, and I will tell you the thing that neither of them can, which is what it looks like from the other side of the hatch.*\n\n*Ada Voss. Second Examiner. I will be in the reading room on Thursday from sixth bell.*\n\n---\n\nThe reading room on the third floor at Tallow Street was empty on a Thursday evening except for the woman at the far table with thirty green volumes stacked in front of her and the lamp turned low.\n\n\"Sit down, Officer.\"\n\nCorvain sat.\n\n\"I sent you the note about the anomaly in September,\" said Voss.\n\n\"I know. Thank you.\"\n\n\"Don't thank me. I want to be extremely clear about that.\" Voss's hands were flat on the table. \"I did not send you that note because I am brave. I sent it to you rather than to the Grandmaster because sending it to the Grandmaster would have put my name on a file, and sending it to a four-month officer from a good house put *your* name on a file. I chose you because you were expendable and well-connected, which is the safest possible thing for somebody like me to aim at. I have thought about that every day for four months and I would like it said out loud once.\"\n\nCorvain sat with that.\n\n\"All right,\" she said.\n\n\"All right.\" Voss let out a breath. \"Now. You have been to see Marchbank and Sallow, and you did not tell either of them, and you have been sick at least once, and you are about to decide that the humane thing is to stop looking at people and go back to the volumes.\" She looked up. \"Don't. The volumes are the crime. The people are the *reason*, and if you lose the reason you will end up like everybody else who has ever got this far, which is to say you will end up thinking of it as a records problem.\"\n\n\"Has anybody got this far?\"\n\n\"Eleven times,\" said Voss.\n\nCorvain went cold.\n\n\"There is a list,\" Voss said. \"It is not a secret list, that is the extraordinary thing about it, it is simply not a list anybody has ever compiled. But if you sit in the examinations office for nine years and read the internal minutes going back a century, which nobody does because they are the dullest documents ever produced by human hand, you find eleven occasions on which somebody inside this building noticed the amendment pattern and wrote it down.\"\n\n\"What happened to them?\"\n\n\"Nothing,\" said Voss. \"Nothing at all. That is the answer and I am sorry, but you had better have it now.\" She opened the top volume. \"Two of them were promoted. Three were given the review they asked for and the review concluded that the amendment process was operating as designed, which was true. One resigned and went into insurance. One took it to a magistrate and the magistrate found there was no offence, which was also true, because there is no law in Gifford against amending an examination result and there never has been. Four dropped it.\"\n\n\"Four just dropped it.\"\n\n\"Four just dropped it, and I have read what they wrote and I would like you to understand that all four of them were better people than me.\" Voss turned the volume round. \"Here. Sixty-first year. A Second Examiner named Halloran. He wrote nine pages. It is the best thing anybody has ever written about this and it is in an internal minute file that has been opened, by the log, three times in forty-five years, twice by me.\"\n\nCorvain read it.\n\nIt took her twenty minutes and she did not speak once.\n\nThen she said: \"He knew why.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"He knew *why*, and he doesn't say it, he circles it four times and then he stops, and on page seven he says—\" she found it— \"*I have satisfied myself that the practice is not malicious and that its purpose is protective, and I find that I am not able to write down what it protects.*\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"I have had forty-five years of other people's spare time to think about that sentence,\" said Voss, \"and I think it means somebody had already told him.\"\n\nShe closed the volume.\n\n\"He left in the summer of that year. Chair of reckoning at the university in Vaskeld. Very fine appointment, everybody said so.\" Voss stood and gathered her coat. \"And I will give you the last thing for nothing, Officer, and then I am going home and I would rather you did not write to me again for a while.\n\n\"Halloran went to Vaskeld in the sixty-first year. Nine years later the Vaskeld council fell and their engine stopped, and it was winter.\"\n\n\"I know about Vaskeld,\" said Corvain. \"Everybody knows about Vaskeld.\"\n\n\"Everybody knows the *number*,\" said Voss. \"Do you know what the number is?\"\n\n\"Forty thousand.\"\n\n\"Forty-one thousand six hundred.\" Voss put her coat on. \"And Halloran was there for it. He was sixty-one years old and he was in that city for the whole of that winter and he survived it, and he never wrote another word about engines for the rest of his life.\"\n\nShe stopped at the door.\n\n\"So when you find out why, Officer Corvain,\" she said, \"and you will, I think, because you are better at this than the eleven were. When you find out why, you want to have decided in advance what you are going to do with a good reason. Because that is what is waiting at the end of this. Not a villain. A perfectly good reason, held by tired people, that has eaten fourteen thousand children.\"\n\n---\n\n**V. Bramwell's Trial**\nThe Resonance Regulator sat on the end bench under the caged lamp, and it had been sitting there since the day Marrow first came through the door, under a cloth, which was the bench Bramwell had told him not to sit on.\n\n\"Right,\" said Bramwell, on the second Thursday in January. \"Come here.\"\n\nHe pulled the cloth off.\n\nIt was about the size of a dog: brass and iron, all exposed springs and calibration screws, a cracked glass dial face and a bell housing on top with the bell taken out of it. Filthy. Beautiful, underneath the filth, in the way that things made by people who cared are beautiful whatever state they end up in.\n\n\"What is it?\"\n\n\"Resonance regulator off a compressor house. Sootgate, number four house, and it's been dead nine years and they've been running that house nine years without it, which is why everybody within four streets of Kettle Yard has a headache all day and doesn't know why.\" Bramwell folded the cloth. \"There were eleven of these made. This is the last one anybody's got their hands on and the pattern's gone. Nobody can make another because nobody knows what the pattern was.\"\n\n\"Can you fix it?\"\n\n\"No.\" Bramwell said it flatly. \"I've had it eleven years. I've had it apart nine times. I know what every part of it does and I know exactly what's wrong with it and I cannot fix it, and I'm going to tell you why in a minute, and then you're going to fix it, and then I'm going to decide whether you can stay.\"\n\nHe laid out six components on the bench in a row. He did it slowly and deliberately, and Marrow understood that the order was not accidental.\n\n\"Six,\" said Bramwell. \"The regulator wants three parts I haven't got: a spring, a collar and a cam. Those six are what forty years of cogging has turned up.\n\n\"These two are right.\" He touched the first two. \"Spring and collar. Right size, right material, right taper. Don't thank me, that's the easy end.\n\n\"This one's the cam and it's *close*. Eleven thousandths over on the throw. It'll go in. It'll work. It'll put the whole regulator about a twelfth out and it'll wear the seat in about four years.\n\n\"This one's a cam and it's *right*, and it's cast wrong, there's a void in it, and it'll shear. Might be a month, might be nine years.\n\n\"This one's a cam out of a marine governor and it's the wrong profile entirely and I've only kept it because it's bronze.\n\n\"And this one.\" He picked up the last and turned it in the light, and it was, unmistakably, the finest object on the bench: bright, sharp, unworn, the casting marks crisp. \"This one is a perfect cam. Right profile, right throw, right steel, and it came off a Council dray in November and it is nine sizes too big and it will not go anywhere near that regulator and it never will.\"\n\nHe put it down.\n\n\"There,\" he said. \"That's the whole of it. Go on.\"\n\nMarrow looked at the bench for a long time.\n\n\"There's no right answer,\" he said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"You want me to pick which wrong.\"\n\n\"I want to know *how you pick*,\" said Bramwell. \"That's the only thing I've ever wanted to know about anybody. Any fool can do a job when there's a right answer, the right answer does the work for you. Sit down and don't touch it for a bit. Think.\"\n\n---\n\nMarrow sat down on the stool and looked at six pieces of metal for two and a half hours, and Bramwell went and did something at the far end of the shop and did not come back once.\n\nAt the end of the two and a half hours Marrow said:\n\n\"I need to know what it's for.\"\n\nBramwell's voice came from the dark. \"It's a resonance regulator.\"\n\n\"That's what it is. I asked what it's *for*.\" Marrow did not turn round. \"You said number four compressor house has been running nine years without it and everybody within four streets has a headache. So it's not keeping the house safe, because the house hasn't blown up in nine years. It's keeping the house *quiet*. It's damping the beat.\"\n\nA pause.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"So if I put the close cam in, the one that's eleven thousandths over, the regulator runs a twelfth out of true. Which means the damping's a twelfth out. Which means it damps most of it and misses a bit.\" Marrow leaned forward. \"And a headache you can nearly not hear is worse than one you can. That's the thing about a beat. If it's there and you can hear it you get used to it. If it's nearly gone you spend all day listening for it.\"\n\nThe scraping at the far end of the shop had stopped.\n\n\"So not that one,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"Not that one. And not the void one, obviously, because it shears, and when a regulator shears on a live compressor house the housing goes and there's four men on that floor.\"\n\n\"Might be nine years.\"\n\n\"Might be a month, and I'd know I'd done it.\" Marrow picked up the fifth one, the marine cam, and turned it over. \"This one's the wrong profile.\"\n\n\"Completely wrong.\"\n\n\"How wrong?\"\n\nBramwell came out of the dark and stood at the end of the bench.\n\n\"Say what you're thinking,\" he said.\n\n\"I'm thinking a profile's a shape and a shape is a set of numbers,\" said Marrow slowly, \"and this one's bronze, and bronze cuts. And I've swept up eleven pounds of brass swarf out of this shop in three months so there's a lathe in here somewhere under all of it, and the profile I want, I can get. It's on the old cam. The old cam's cracked but the *shape* isn't cracked. I can take the shape off the broken one and put it on this one, and it'll take me about nine days, and I'll ruin it twice first.\"\n\n\"It's bronze,\" said Bramwell. \"Bronze wears. Steel's better. Steel's what it was made in.\"\n\n\"Bronze wears *predictably*.\" Marrow looked up. \"That's the whole thing, isn't it. That's the twelfth on my pressure gauge. Steel that's wrong is wrong and you don't know how much. Bronze that's right wears about a thousandth a year and you can *measure* it, and in nine years somebody comes and puts a shim in, and I'll write down how thick the shim wants to be and leave the note in the housing.\"\n\nBramwell said nothing at all.\n\n\"And the perfect one,\" said Marrow, \"the beautiful one, off the Council dray, I'd sell. Because it's nine sizes too big and it's never going in anything in this shop, and it's worth about four marks to somebody with a dray, and four marks buys the bronze stock for the next one.\"\n\nHe put the marine cam down.\n\n\"That's my answer,\" he said. \"The wrong-shaped one that I can make right, over the right-shaped one that's wrong in a way I can't see. And I keep the note in the housing because in nine years I won't be here and somebody's going to open it up and want to know why there's a bronze cam in a steel machine and think whoever did it was an idiot.\"\n\n---\n\nIt took him eleven days, not nine, and he ruined two blanks, and on the eighth day he had to stop and re-cut the whole profile because he had taken the shape off the cracked cam without accounting for the fact that it was cracked, and the crack had opened the throw by four thousandths, and he had faithfully reproduced the fault.\n\nBramwell watched him find that and said nothing and did not help.\n\nOn the eleventh day they took it up to number four compressor house at fourth bell with the regulator in a sack and Bramwell's writ in his pocket, which was the only writ in the whole of Kettle Yard and which he had held for forty-one years and never once explained.\n\nIt went in.\n\nThey stood on the floor of number four with four fitters and a foreman and watched the bell housing, and the beat came up through the boards the way it had come up for nine years, and then the regulator took hold, and over about forty seconds the beat went out of the floor.\n\nNot most of it. All of it.\n\nThe foreman put his hand flat on the wall and stood there for a bit.\n\n\"That's gone,\" he said.\n\n\"That's gone,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"Nine years.\" The foreman looked round at the fitters. \"Nine years I've had a headache. I thought that was me.\"\n\nMarrow stood in the middle of the compressor floor with a sack in his hand and his ears full of the absence of something, and he did not say anything, because there was a thing happening in his chest that he did not entirely have a name for.\n\nOn the way down the stairs Bramwell said, without looking at him:\n\n\"There's a room upstairs at the shop with a bed in it. It's full of gears. You can have it if you clear it out, and I'll pay you two marks a week and you'll be worth about four, and I'll put it up when you're worth six and not before.\"\n\n\"All right.\"\n\n\"And the note in the housing.\" Bramwell stopped on the landing. \"You wrote it in the third person. *The cam is bronze. The shim allowance is as follows.* Why?\"\n\nMarrow thought about it.\n\n\"Because if I sign it,\" he said, \"whoever opens it in nine years reads the name first, and if it's a Sirrath name off a ninth stratum they'll stop reading.\"\n\nBramwell looked at him for a long moment.\n\n\"Aye,\" he said. \"Well. That's the first thing you've got wrong in eleven days.\" He went on down the stairs. \"Sign it. Sign the bloody thing. Let them stop reading. It'll still be in there in ninety years and there'll come a day when somebody opens up a machine in this city and finds a Sirrath name on a repair that held, and you'll not be there to see it, and that's the whole reason to do it.\"\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Five.*" },
  { n: 6, title: "Pressure", text: "**I. Marrow**\nHe found out about Ketu on a Tuesday in February, and he found out because he had money.\n\nThat was the joke of it and he saw the joke immediately and it did not help. Four months at Bramwell's, two marks a week and then three, and a room upstairs he had cleared of nine hundredweight of gears, and for the first time in his life Marrow had a little that was his. So on the Tuesday he went up to Ketu's lane with four marks in his coat and a bag of coke and the specific intention of settling her chalk at Marta's, because he had worked out that she was carrying about two marks of it and had been carrying it since the autumn.\n\nMarta looked at the money and then looked at him and then wiped her hands on her apron in a way that took slightly too long.\n\n\"There's no chalk on Ketu,\" she said.\n\n\"There is. Since October.\"\n\n\"There was. It got settled.\" Marta put both hands on the counter. \"Fifth of January. Twelve marks, cleared, the whole of it, and something over for the coke through to March.\"\n\nMarrow stood in the shop with four marks in his hand.\n\n\"Twelve,\" he said.\n\n\"Twelve and four.\"\n\n\"She hasn't got twelve marks. She's never had twelve marks in her life, she cleans stairs, she gets two a week and she's sixty-eight—\"\n\n\"Marrow.\" Marta said it quietly. \"Don't make me be the one.\"\n\n---\n\nHe walked to the end of the lane and stood there for a while and then went in and asked her.\n\nKetu was at the table with the lamp on and a bowl of something. She was very small, these days. She had been enormous when he was nine.\n\n\"Who paid Marta,\" he said.\n\nShe did not look up.\n\n\"Ketu.\"\n\n\"Sit down.\"\n\n\"Who paid Marta.\"\n\n\"Sit *down*, boy, I'm not shouting up at you like a bird in a chimney.\"\n\nHe sat down.\n\nKetu put her spoon in the bowl and folded her hands, and Marrow watched her do it and understood that she had been expecting this conversation for some time and had prepared for it, and that the preparation had not made it any better.\n\n\"Nine years ago,\" she said, \"you had a fever in the March and you were seven and you did not eat for eleven days.\"\n\n\"I remember.\"\n\n\"You remember the fever. You do not remember the eleven days, because I made sure you did not.\" She turned her hands over. \"There was a woman on Aster Row wanted a night girl as well as a day one and I took it, and I was doing five in the morning to nine at night for six weeks, and it was not enough, because the medicine was six marks a bottle and you had four bottles.\"\n\nMarrow said nothing.\n\n\"Twenty-four marks. In 1889.\" Ketu's voice did not change. \"I did not have twenty-four marks. I did not have four. I went to the Ashlight and I asked, and a man I had never met before came out and sat down with me and did not laugh at me and did not ask me anything about myself, and he lent me twenty-four marks at no interest at all, and he said: pay it when you can.\"\n\n\"And you never paid it.\"\n\n\"I have paid it four times over,\" said Ketu. \"In pieces. Ten grist here and a mark there for nine years, whenever there was anything, and every time I took it up there they wrote it in the book and thanked me and every time the number at the bottom of the book was the same number.\" She looked up at him for the first time. \"Because it is not a debt, boy. I worked that out about the fourth year. It is a *rope*. And I have been paying rope-money into that book for nine years so that nobody ever came to this house and said your name.\"\n\nThe lamp hissed.\n\n\"And on the fifth of January,\" said Marrow, \"somebody went to Marta's and paid twelve marks.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"He came himself,\" said Ketu.\n\n---\n\nThe room over the Ashlight was warm and smelled of oil and tobacco and there was a long table with paperwork on it, and Silas Vane looked up when Marrow came in and did not stand.\n\n\"Sit down.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then stand.\" Vane pushed a chair out anyway with his foot and turned back to the table. \"You'll want the ledger. Third book, on the shelf behind you, the green one. Take it down and read it yourself, because if I read it to you you'll think I'm shaping it.\"\n\nMarrow took it down.\n\nIt was all there. Nine years of it, in four different hands. *Ketu, no other given, Sootgate. 24m advanced 12/3/89. No term.* And then nine years of payments in and the running total at the bottom of every page unchanged, and then, on the fifth of January, in a fifth hand, an entry for twelve marks four out, marked *Marta's, cleared*, and beneath it, and this was the thing that made Marrow's hands go cold on the covers, a note in the margin.\n\n*Do not accept further payment from this account.*\n\n\"You closed it,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"I closed it.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because she is sixty-eight and her knees have gone and she has been putting ten grist a week into that book for nine years out of fear of me, and I have taken it, because taking it was the arrangement.\" Vane put his pen down. \"And because I am going to ask you for something, and I did not want you to be able to tell yourself that you were doing it for her.\"\n\nMarrow shut the ledger.\n\n\"That's a lie,\" he said.\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"All of it. It's true and it's a lie. You closed it in January and she found out in January and I found out in February off Marta, which means you didn't tell me, which means you waited for me to find out on my own, which means you *wanted* me to find out on my own, because if you'd come to Bramwell's and told me, I'd have known what it was.\" He put the ledger on the table. \"You paid twelve marks and you bought a Tuesday afternoon where I walk in here on my own feet. That's what you bought. Twelve marks four.\"\n\nVane looked at him for a moment.\n\nThen he smiled, and it was not a performance, and that was somehow worse.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"That is exactly what I bought, and you are the first one in nineteen years to say it to my face inside the first four minutes, and you have no idea how tiring it is being right about people.\" He sat back. \"So. Now you know what it is. Does it work?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because it's still twelve marks she hasn't got to find.\"\n\n\"Quite.\" Vane reached for the decanter. \"Sit down. You may as well hear the job before you say yes to it. There's no dignity in agreeing to something you haven't heard.\"\n\n---\n\nThe job was nothing.\n\nThat was the whole of Vane's method and Marrow could see the method operating and sat in the chair and watched it operate on him.\n\nThere was a compressor house on the Wick, number nine, and it had a writ inspection coming on the Thursday, and Vane needed the inspection moved by eleven days, and to move it by eleven days somebody had to be in the yard behind the Engine house at Aster Row at fourth bell on the Wednesday and put a wedge in the bottom of a particular gate so that a particular dray could not get out until the afternoon post had gone.\n\n\"Nobody is hurt,\" said Vane. \"Nothing is stolen. The dray goes out at two instead of nine. A clerk misses a post. An inspection moves eleven days. There are four men on that compressor floor who keep their places for another quarter and a foreman who does not have to explain a gauge that has been reading low since November.\"\n\n\"Whose gauge?\"\n\n\"Mine.\"\n\n\"So it's a lie to a writ inspector.\"\n\n\"It is a wedge in a gate,\" said Vane. \"The lie is somebody else's and it was told in November. You are being asked to delay a dray.\"\n\nMarrow sat with it.\n\nHe knew exactly what it was. He had sat on a crate in the yard with Ratchet and had it explained to him in December in words of one syllable: *there's no door, nobody drags you in, and then one day there's a thing that needs doing.* He had heard it and he had understood it and he had walked out of that yard thinking that being able to see the shape of a trap was the same as being outside it.\n\n\"Say no,\" said Vane.\n\nMarrow looked up.\n\n\"Say no. Now. Out loud, in this room, and I will not ask you again, and nobody will come to Ketu's house, and the twelve marks are gone and I will not mention them for the rest of my life.\" Vane held his eye. \"I mean it entirely. Go back to Bramwell's, get very good at machines, be a decent man. It is a real offer and it is on the table and it will be off it in about a minute.\"\n\n\"Why would you do that?\"\n\n\"Because I have run this for nineteen years and the ones who are dragged in are worth nothing to me.\" Vane turned his glass. \"They are frightened, and frightened men are stupid and they talk. I have no use whatever for a boy who is here because of his grandmother's chalk. I want the one who does the arithmetic and puts a wedge in a gate on a Wednesday because he has looked at every other option available to a ninth stratum Sirrath in this city and found that they are the drop-forges, the outfalls, portering on the seam, or scrubbing stairs on the Rise until his knees go.\"\n\n\"There's Bramwell's.\"\n\n\"There is Bramwell's,\" Vane agreed. \"There is one Bramwell's, and he is seventy-one, and he has a bad chest, and when he dies the writ dies with him because a writ does not descend to a Sirrath with an amendment. And you know all that, because you are the only person in the Under who has ever worked out that the writ is the whole of what Bramwell has that you cannot get.\"\n\nThe stove ticked.\n\n\"So,\" said Vane. \"Say no.\"\n\nMarrow sat in the warm room over the Ashlight and did the sum, and it came out the way it came out, and he was completely clear-eyed about it, and the clear-sightedness did not help at all.\n\n\"What's the wedge made of?\" he said.\n\n---\n\nHe did it on the Wednesday at fourth bell and it took eleven seconds and nobody saw him and nothing happened.\n\nThat was the part nobody had warned him about. He had braced for the guilt and he had braced for the fear and what he had not braced for was the *nothing*. He put a piece of oak in the bottom of a gate and walked out of the yard and went home and ate his supper, and on the Thursday the inspection moved to the twenty-first, and on the Friday Bramwell shouted at him for four minutes about a bearing race and he laughed about it, and by the Saturday he had noticed that he had not thought about it since Wednesday night.\n\nHe sat on the end of the bed in the room over the shop with a bronze blank in his hands and thought:\n\n*That is how it works. It is not that it feels bad and you do it anyway. It is that it feels like nothing at all, and then the next one is slightly bigger, and it also feels like nothing.*\n\nHe thought: I should stop.\n\nThen he thought, and this was the sentence that he would come back to in a cellar in August with sawdust under his feet:\n\n*I will stop when it stops feeling like nothing.*\n\n---\n\n**II. Corvain**\nThey took the case off her on the twelfth of March at eleven in the morning, and they did it properly, which she had not expected.\n\n\"Sit down, Officer,\" said Senior Detective Marcus Vale.\n\nHe was sixty-two and heavy through the shoulders and beautifully dressed in a way that had nothing to do with fashion, and he had a file open in front of him, and the file was hers. Not a copy. Hers, with her own pencil in the margins.\n\nAshcombe was standing at the window with his back to the room, which told Corvain everything about the last hour that she needed to know.\n\n\"You've had my file,\" she said.\n\n\"I've had your file since Tuesday. Sit down.\" Vale waited until she did. \"I've read all of it. Sixty-one pages, forty-two of them annexes, and the annexes are better than the report, which is the correct way round and almost nobody does it.\" He closed it. \"It's very good work. I want to say that first, because you're about to decide that I'm your enemy and I'd rather you did it on the evidence.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"You've established that conduct amendments are applied disproportionately to high-scoring candidates. Fourteen thousand two hundred and six cases, hundred and three years, and you've got the assessed results, which nobody's ever pulled together.\" Vale folded his hands. \"It's the best piece of records work this division has produced in my career. Now tell me what offence it discloses.\"\n\nCorvain did not answer.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"None,\" she said.\n\n\"None.\" Vale nodded slowly. \"None. There is no statute in Gifford governing the amendment of an examination result. There is no statute requiring the Calibration to be conducted at all. The Council administers it under prerogative and it may administer it in any manner it chooses, and it has published the strata every year for a hundred and three years, which is more than it is obliged to do.\" He tapped the file. \"You have documented, at very great length, something that is entirely lawful. You know that. It's on page nine, in your own hand: *no offence appears.*\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"Then why did you keep going?\"\n\n\"Because it's wrong, sir.\"\n\n\"Right,\" said Vale, and something in his face changed very slightly, and for one moment Corvain thought she had reached him. \"Yes. That's the answer. Hold on to it. Now here's mine.\"\n\nHe drew a second file out from under the first.\n\n\"Ninth of March. A writ inspection at number nine compressor house on the Wick was delayed eleven days by interference with a gate at the Aster Row Engine house yard. Second of February, four hundredweight of bearings out of the Grieves store. November, a gauge substitution at number nine that's been running low four months with four men on the floor.\" He set it down. \"There is an organisation in the Under trading in Engine components without writ and it is turning over something in the region of nine hundred sovereigns a year and I have been trying to put a case on Silas Vane for eleven years. That is an *offence*, Officer. That is about nine of them. And it is going to kill somebody in a compressor house inside two years, and when it does there will be an inquiry and the inquiry will find that this division knew.\"\n\n\"Sir, those aren't connected—\"\n\n\"They are now,\" said Vale. \"As of this morning your file is annexed to mine.\"\n\nThe room went very quiet.\n\n\"Say that again,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Your work is being folded into the Ashlight investigation.\" Vale said it without any pleasure at all, which was the worst thing about him. \"It's a natural fit and it will survive any review. Fourteen thousand children pushed down the strata over a century, disproportionately Rustbound, disproportionately non-human: that is the recruiting ground. It's motive. It's the best explanation anybody has ever produced for why the Under keeps handing Vane its cleverest boys, and it's *true*, Officer, that's the thing you'll want to sit with. Every word of it is true.\"\n\nCorvain sat absolutely still.\n\n\"And in the case as annexed,\" she said, \"the amendments stop being the crime.\"\n\n\"The amendments become context.\"\n\n\"They become the reason a Rustbound gang exists.\" Her voice was perfectly level. \"So the finding is that the Under is dangerous because its clever children are embittered. Which means the recommendation is more enforcement in the Under. Which means the Council reads a report that begins with fourteen thousand amended children and ends by asking for eleven more constables in Sootgate.\"\n\nVale did not deny it.\n\n\"That is a possible outcome,\" he said.\n\n\"It's the only outcome.\"\n\n\"It is the *likely* outcome, and I'd have thought you'd take it, because it is eleven more constables than there were yesterday and about four hundred people in Sootgate get a functioning compressor house.\" He stood up. \"I've been doing this thirty years and I'll tell you what I know, and you may take it or leave it. You cannot indict a system. There's no dock big enough. What you can do, on a good day, with a great deal of paper, is put one man in one dock for one thing. And the price of that is always, every single time, that the thing you actually cared about goes in as an annexe.\"\n\n\"And in thirty years how many times have you paid that price?\"\n\n\"Every time,\" said Vale. \"Every single time, and it is why the compressor houses in this city are safer than they were and why the Under is not, and I am not going to pretend to you that those two facts are unrelated.\" He picked up both files. \"You're seconded to me for the duration. You'll write the annexe yourself, because you know the material and because you'd never forgive anybody else doing it. And Officer.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"You have got about four days,\" said Marcus Vale, \"before the volumes go into evidence, at which point they are sealed and neither of us can look at them again without a court order. I'm not telling you that as a kindness. I'm telling you because I'd rather you spent those four days doing something useful than deciding what you think of me.\"\n\nHe went out.\n\nAshcombe stayed at the window a moment longer.\n\n\"Sir,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"He's not lying to you,\" said Ashcombe. \"Not once, not in any part of it. He's the best detective in this building and he means every word and if you go up against him you will lose, and in nine years you'll be him.\" He turned round. \"So don't go up against him. Go round.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"How long have you got?\"\n\n\"Four days.\"\n\n\"Then it's not going to be the volumes, is it,\" said Ashcombe. \"Think, Corvain. What have you got that's not in that room?\"\n\n---\n\n**III. Ivy Thorncastle**\nShe burned them.\n\nIvy had noticed it in November and had said nothing for four months, which for Ivy was an act of self-restraint on roughly the scale of a religious vocation.\n\nRooke got a letter from Ferrow Lane most weeks. Marta the Grocer wrote them and Tessa dictated and the hand was large and careful and the spelling was doing its best. They came up on the Thursday post. Rooke would take hers and put it in her coat and not open it, and then at some point in the evening she would go to the window at the end of the third-floor corridor where nobody went, and read it twice, and then go along to the stair-head where there was a lamp, and burn it.\n\nEvery week. Since October.\n\n\"Why do you burn them?\" Ivy said, finally, on a Thursday in March, having decided at about two o'clock that afternoon that four months was enough.\n\nRooke did not jump. She had known Ivy was there, because she always knew.\n\n\"Because I read them twice,\" she said.\n\n\"That isn't an answer, that's a sentence with the same shape as an answer.\"\n\n\"It's the answer.\" Rooke turned the letter over. \"If I keep them I read them four times. Then six. There was a fortnight in December where I read the November ones every night, all five of them, in order, and by the end of it I could say them, and I was sitting in the fourth-year seminar saying *Nettle asks about you* in my head while a Reader was talking about periodic systems.\" She folded it. \"So I read them twice and burn them. I get the whole of it and then it's gone and I do my work. It's the only thing that's worked.\"\n\nIvy sat down on the window seat.\n\n\"That's the most frightening thing anybody's ever said to me,\" she said.\n\n\"It's just management.\"\n\n\"That's *why* it's frightening.\" Ivy pulled her knees up. \"Rooke, do you know what the rest of us do when we get a letter from home? We read it, and we put it in a box, and in nine years we get it out and we're sentimental about it. That's the normal thing. You've built a *procedure*.\"\n\n\"The normal thing assumes you can afford to be sentimental.\"\n\n\"Everyone can afford to be sentimental.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Rooke. \"You can. There's a difference and I keep having to explain it and I'm getting tired of explaining it, so I'll do it once more and then I'd like to stop. If you're sentimental about your mother's letter it costs you an evening. If I'm sentimental about that letter it costs me the seminar, and if I lose the seminar I lose the placement, and if I lose the placement there is no maintenance allowance and no Contentions and no sixty marks in March, and Barrow the rent man puts five people out on a step. That's the actual chain. It isn't self-pity, it's a chain, and every link in it is real, and you can go and check any of them.\"\n\nIvy was quiet for a while.\n\n\"I looked up the Contention statute,\" she said.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you said you could only do it twice and I wanted to know why, and you wouldn't tell me on the steps in December, and I've had three months.\" Ivy picked at the seam of the cushion. \"Article nine. Stakes agreed between the parties. And article eleven, which nobody's read since about 1830, which says a Contention may be refused without dishonour where the declaring party has grounds to believe the matter is not genuinely contested.\"\n\nRooke said nothing.\n\n\"That's it, isn't it,\" said Ivy. \"You can do it twice. The first time because nobody knew. The second time because somebody with something to prove will still take the bet. And after that everybody in that building knows that a Contention against you is just handing over money, so they invoke article eleven and refuse, and it's not even cowardice, it's *in the statute*.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"You've had one.\"\n\n\"I've had one.\"\n\n\"So you've got one left,\" said Ivy, \"and after that there's nothing.\"\n\n\"After that there's nothing.\"\n\nIvy looked at her.\n\n\"Then don't spend it on money,\" she said.\n\nRooke's head came up.\n\n\"Sixty marks was twelve weeks,\" said Ivy. \"You told me that on the step. Twelve weeks, and it's March, and you've had one letter in four months that said the arrears were clear and three that didn't mention the rent at all, and you burned all of them, and I bet you anything you like that you know exactly which week the sixty marks runs out.\"\n\n\"The nineteenth of April.\"\n\n\"Right.\" Ivy nodded. \"So the next sixty marks is another twelve weeks and then you're standing in the same corridor in July with nothing left to stake, and that's if anybody even takes it. You cannot get out of this by winning it twice.\"\n\n\"Then what would you have me do?\"\n\n\"I don't know,\" said Ivy. \"I'm a mediocre reckoner and I'm going to be a very good historian and this is the first time in my life those two facts have been useful at once.\" She sat forward. \"But I'll tell you what a historian would say. You keep trying to buy time. Twelve weeks, twelve weeks, twelve weeks. Nobody in the history of anything has ever got out of a hole by buying twelve weeks. What you want is a *change in the terms*, and you haven't once gone looking for one, because you're the best reckoner in this building and you've spent five months solving the wrong problem.\"\n\nRooke stood at the window with the letter in her hand.\n\n\"Say the problem,\" she said.\n\n\"Your problem isn't that you haven't got five marks a week,\" said Ivy. \"It's that the only thing anybody in Gifford will pay you for is being a curiosity. They'll pay to watch you beat a lord. They will not pay you for the actual work, because you're fourteen and you're from Ferrow Lane and there's no writ in this city that has ever been issued to either of those things.\"\n\nShe stopped.\n\n\"Oh,\" said Ivy.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Nothing. It's a stupid thought.\"\n\n\"Say it.\"\n\n\"There's no writ,\" said Ivy slowly, \"issued to *either of those things*. But you're not either of those things any more, are you? You're a first stratum scholar of the University of the Engine, and the university holds its own writs, and it issues them under its own seal, and it has done since about eleven years before the Council existed.\" She looked up. \"My aunt holds four.\"\n\nRooke did not move for about nine seconds.\n\nThen she said: \"Where's the library index for university statutes?\"\n\n\"Second gallery, north end, and it's forty volumes and there's no catalogue.\"\n\n\"Right.\" Rooke put the unread letter in her coat pocket, and did not go to the lamp at the stair-head, and Ivy noticed that and said nothing at all about it. \"I'll want your help. You're better at forty volumes with no catalogue than I am.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Ivy, extremely pleased. \"I am.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Grandmaster Osric Kell**\nKell reviewed the year's amendments in the last week of March, as he had done every year for thirty-one years, and it took him a full day, and he did it alone with the door shut.\n\nForty-one files. He read all of them.\n\nHe had a system, and the system was that he read each file completely before he looked at the amendment, so that he had the child in his head before he had the decision. He had built that system in his first year in the post because it had seemed to him then that the least a man could do was know what he was signing.\n\nIt had not occurred to him for about twenty-five years that a system designed to make a thing harder to do, which does not stop you doing it, is not a conscience. It is a ritual.\n\nThe thirty-fourth file was a Sirrath boy out of Sootgate. Assessed fourth stratum. Amended ninth, prior conduct, magistrate's caution at thirteen for the theft of a pressure gauge from a scrap barrow, restitution made, costs eight grist.\n\nKell read the paper.\n\nHe was not required to read the paper. Nobody had read a candidate's actual paper at the review stage in the history of the office; the review was of the amendment, not of the assessment. But the papers were in the file and Kell had read them for thirty-one years, and he opened this one and worked through it as he would have worked through any of them.\n\nIt took him fifty minutes.\n\nThe boy had got section nine. He had got it slowly, with two false starts that he had marked himself and gone back to, and one of the false starts was a genuinely interesting error, the kind of error you can only make by understanding what you are doing.\n\nAnd he had got the load across the arch.\n\nKell sat back in his chair and looked at the last page of a fourteen year old's examination for some time.\n\nFour hundred and eleven human candidates in the main hall had not got the load across the arch. Kell knew that number because he had read the marking returns that morning. Four hundred and eleven, and every one of them was sitting today in the stratum the marking had put them in, and this boy was in the ninth, which said *limited aptitude*, and would be in the ninth in the ledger in a hundred years.\n\nHe drew the amendment schedule toward him and uncapped his pen and did not sign it.\n\nHe sat with the pen in his hand for a long time.\n\n---\n\nWhat Kell thought about, in that hour, was the sixty-first year.\n\nHe had been thirty-one. He had been Second Examiner then, on the west floor, and there had been a First Examiner named Halloran who had written nine pages about the amendment pattern and put them into the minute file, and Kell had read them, because Halloran had asked him to.\n\nHe remembered the conversation with complete clarity. They had been in the reading room. Halloran had been sixty-one, which Kell had thought at the time was extremely old, and he had said:\n\n*I have written it down, Osric. That's all I can do. I have written it down properly and put it where it can be found, and one day somebody will find it, and I'd like there to be a name on it.*\n\nAnd Kell had said: *Why not publish?*\n\nAnd Halloran had looked at him for a while and had then said: *Because they've told me why.*\n\n*Then tell me.*\n\n*No.*\n\n*Why not?*\n\nAnd Halloran had said: *Because you're thirty-one and you're going to run this office one day, and if you know why, you will do exactly what I am doing, and you will do it for forty years, and you will do it well. And if you don't know why, there is a chance, a small one, that you will do something stupid.*\n\n*I'd rather know.*\n\n*I know you would,* Halloran had said. *That's precisely why I'm not telling you.*\n\nHe had gone to Vaskeld in the summer.\n\nKell had spent about nine years being angry about that conversation and about twenty-two years understanding it, and had never once, in either period, gone looking for the answer.\n\nThat was the thing he thought about with the pen in his hand. Not the amendments. He had made his peace with the amendments in the sixty-fourth year and the peace had held, on the grounds that the practice was not malicious and that its purpose was protective, which was Halloran's own phrase and Kell had borrowed it and used it on himself for thirty-one years like a man borrowing a coat.\n\nWhat he thought about was that he had had a whole career and had never asked.\n\nHalloran had been offered the answer and had taken it and had let it stop him. Kell had been offered the choice of not knowing and had taken that instead, and had told himself for three decades that it was integrity, and it had been convenience, and there was a Sirrath boy in the ninth stratum with a note in the margin about a pressure gauge and the load across the arch answered correctly on the last page.\n\nKell put the pen down.\n\nHe did not tear up the schedule. He was seventy years old and he was not a fool and he understood, entirely, that a Grandmaster of Examinations who refused to sign the annual amendment review would be a former Grandmaster of Examinations by Thursday and the schedule would be signed by somebody else on Friday, and forty-one children would be in exactly the same place with the difference that nobody in that office would ever read a candidate's paper again.\n\nInstead he did two things, and both of them took about four minutes, and one of them was cowardice and one of them was not, and he was clear with himself about which was which.\n\nThe first thing was that he signed the schedule.\n\nThe second thing was that he wrote a note, in his own hand, on examinations office paper, and sealed it, and addressed it to the officer whose name he had seen on a requisition refusal in November and again on a records access log in December.\n\nIt said:\n\n*Officer Corvain.*\n\n*You have been refused the historical returns twice on procedural grounds and I signed both refusals. The second refusal was correct. The first was not, and I signed it because I did not wish to spend the attention.*\n\n*The internal minute file of this office is not covered by the requisition procedure, because when the procedure was drafted in the fifty-ninth year nobody thought of it. It is held in the reading room on the third floor and it is open to any officer of the Council on production of a warrant card. It has been open for forty-five years. Nobody has ever asked.*\n\n*Sixty-first year. First Examiner Halloran. Nine pages.*\n\n*I am seventy years old and I have signed thirty-one of these and I am not going to pretend to you that this note constitutes anything. But you are going to be told, quite soon, that a great many careful people have looked at this and concluded that it is necessary, and when you are told that I would like you to know that at least one of them never looked at all.*\n\n*O. Kell.*\n\nHe sealed it and put it in the tray for the Thursday post.\n\n---\n\n**V. The Case Race**\nShe had four days and she spent the first of them doing nothing, which was the hardest thing she had ever made herself do.\n\nAshcombe's question sat in her chest all Tuesday. *What have you got that's not in that room?*\n\nThe volumes were going into evidence on the Friday. A hundred and three of them, sealed, and after that nobody could open one without a court order, and the court order would go through the Council, and Corvain had spent enough time in that building now to know exactly what would happen to it.\n\nSo: not the volumes.\n\nShe had her own report, and Vale had that. She had her annexes, and Vale had those. She had her notes, and they were copies of the volumes and worthless without them, because a set of figures in a young officer's handwriting is not evidence of anything, it is an allegation about a book.\n\nThat was the trap and it was a good one and Vale had not even had to set it. In four days the entire evidential basis of a hundred and three years of amendments would be sitting in a sealed evidence room in this building, lawfully, as an annexe to a case about stolen bearings.\n\nOn Wednesday morning Corvain went up to the reading room on the third floor at Tallow Street and started on the thirty volumes that anybody could read.\n\n---\n\nThe reading room set was thirty years. That was the whole of it: the last thirty annual returns, bound, on an open shelf, available to any member of the public who signed the book.\n\nAnd the reading room returns did not contain the assessed results.\n\nThat was the thing that had sent her down to the sub-level in the first place. The published returns gave the strata: how many candidates in each of the eleven, by district, by year. They did not give amendments at all. The word did not appear. Whatever had been done to fourteen thousand children over a century had been done entirely inside the building, and outside the building there was simply a list of how many children had come out in each stratum.\n\nCorvain sat down at a table with thirty volumes and a sheet of paper and no assessed results and thought: then there is nothing here.\n\nAnd then, at about four in the afternoon, she thought: unless there is.\n\n---\n\nIt took her nine hours over two days and she got it at about two in the morning on the Friday, and when she got it she sat in the empty reading room and laughed until she had to put her head on the table.\n\nBecause the published returns gave the strata *by district*.\n\nEleven strata. Nineteen districts. Thirty years. Six and a half thousand numbers, all of them public, all of them printed by the Council itself every summer for a hundred and three years and posted on eleven boards on Tallow Street where anybody could read them, and Corvain had walked past those boards forty times.\n\nAnd here was the thing.\n\nIf the Calibration was what Kell said it was in the hall every year, if it asked one question of every child in the same words and sorted them by what they showed, then the strata were a *sorting*. And a sorting has a shape. A big cohort sorted by ability produces the same shape every time, in every district, with the middle fat and the ends thin, and the districts differ in where the middle sits, because Ferrow Lane children are hungrier and worse taught than Aster Row children and that shows up, and it should show up, and it does.\n\nBut the shape stays the same shape. Poorer district, same shape, shifted. That is what sorting does.\n\nCorvain laid out nineteen districts by thirty years and looked at the shapes.\n\nEleven of the nineteen were fine.\n\nEight were not.\n\nIn eight districts, all eight of them in the Under, all eight of them the eight with the highest non-human population in the city, the shape had a *dent* in it. Not at the bottom. Near the top. In the fourth, third and second strata there were consistently, year after year, for thirty years, fewer children than the shape required, and in the ninth and tenth there were consistently more.\n\nThe dent was small. In any one district in any one year it was three or four children and it looked like nothing at all, which is why it had sat on eleven public boards for a hundred and three years.\n\nAcross eight districts and thirty years it was four thousand one hundred and eleven children.\n\nAnd it was in the *published* figures. Under the Council's own seal. Available to anybody who signed the book.\n\nCorvain sat in the reading room at two in the morning with six and a half thousand public numbers in front of her and understood that she did not need the volumes at all.\n\nThe volumes proved *who*. She had lost the volumes and she was not going to get them back.\n\nThe public returns proved *that it happened*, and they proved it out of the Council's own printing office, and they could not be sealed into an evidence room, because there were sets of them in nine libraries and the university held one and the boards went up outside on Tallow Street every summer in front of six hundred children and their mothers.\n\nYou cannot seal a thing you have already posted on a wall.\n\n---\n\nAt half past four in the morning she wrote it up. Eleven pages, no annexes, no rhetoric, the arithmetic and nothing else, and she checked it four times and then walked across the seam in the dark to the enforcement building and put it on Ashcombe's desk with a note.\n\n*Sir. I went round.*\n\n*This is entirely from the published returns. Every figure in it is on the open shelf in the third floor reading room at Tallow Street and has been printed by the Council every summer since the founding. There is nothing in it from the volumes. Detective Vale can seal the evidence room on Friday and it will not touch one line of this.*\n\n*It does not prove who did it or why. It proves that it was done, and it proves it out of the Council's own numbers, and it cannot be taken back.*\n\n*You said you would like the chance to decide what you are before somebody decided it for you. I think this is that. I am sorry. E.C.*\n\nThen she went and stood on the step of the enforcement building in the cold as the fog started coming up off the Under, and she thought about Ada Marchbank laughing in a pipe-fitter's yard about not being clever, and she was not sick this time, and she thought that was probably the worst thing that had happened to her all year.\n\nAt nine in the morning a boy from the Tallow Street post brought her a sealed note in a seventy year old hand.\n\n*Sixty-first year. First Examiner Halloran. Nine pages.*\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Six.*" },
  { n: 7, title: "Whistle's Price", text: "**I. Three Doors**\nBy the middle of April all three of them had arrived at the same wall from three different sides, and none of them knew the others existed.\n\n**Rooke** had spent nine days in the second gallery of the library with Ivy Thorncastle and forty volumes of university statute and no catalogue, and had found what she was looking for on the seventh day, and had then spent two days establishing that it was useless.\n\nThe university did hold its own writs. It had held them since eleven years before the Council existed. It could issue them under its own seal to any member of the university for the purposes of instruction or enquiry, and there was no age qualification anywhere in the statute, and Ivy had been so pleased about that she had actually shouted in a library.\n\nAnd then Rooke had read the schedule of writs the university had actually issued in the last forty years, and there were nine hundred and eleven of them, and every one was for instruction, and not one was for trade.\n\n*Enquiry* was the word she needed and *enquiry* did not mean what she needed it to mean. It meant you could buy a barometer.\n\nShe sat in the gallery with the ninth volume open on her knees and understood that she had spent nine days finding a door that led into the same room.\n\nSo she wrote to the address on Cull Alley for the second time.\n\n**Corvain** had read Halloran's nine pages four times and then done what Halloran had not done, which was to go looking for the mechanism rather than the pattern.\n\nShe had her eleven pages. Ashcombe had them, and had read them, and had said nothing for two days and then said *right*, which she had learned by now was the most alarming thing he said. And the eleven pages proved that it happened, out of the Council's own printing, and could not be sealed.\n\nBut Vale had been right about one thing, and it had gone into her like a splinter and stayed. *You cannot indict a system. There's no dock big enough.*\n\nTo put a thing in a dock you need a hand on it. And Corvain had a hundred and three years of amendments and fourteen thousand children and not one single name, because there had not been a signature on an amendment since the fifty-first year. It was initialled by a clerk under a delegated authority, and the delegation was renewed every quarter by an office rather than a person.\n\nWhich office?\n\nShe had spent three weeks on that and had got nowhere at all, because the answer was recorded nowhere she could reach, and the four people who knew it were clerks, and the last clerk she had spoken to had changed a docket slip two hours too late and had not slept properly since.\n\nSo she took four sovereigns out of her own account and went back to Cull Alley.\n\n**Marrow** had put a wedge in a gate in March and had not thought about it since the Wednesday night, and that fact had frightened him more than anything in his life.\n\nSo he had gone at it the only way he knew, which was the bench. He had asked Bramwell one question and Bramwell had refused to answer it, which was a first, and the question was: *how many people work inside the Engine houses?*\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because you said there were eleven of you on the gang and you never got past the second gallery, and you said half of it's not on the plan, and you said the eastern bank reset itself in eleven moves and eleven reckoners on a gallery couldn't do it in eight hours.\" Marrow had put the file down. \"Eleven reckoners. On one gallery. On one shift. How many are there altogether?\"\n\nAnd Bramwell had said, \"Don't,\" and gone to the far end of the shop, and that had been the whole of it.\n\nSo Marrow walked to Cull Alley on a Sunday with three marks in his coat, which was everything he had that was not owed to anybody.\n\n---\n\n**II. What Whistle Charged**\nThe shop had a low ceiling and Whistle had not stood up in it for nineteen years.\n\nThey came on three separate days in the same week, which Whistle noted, and did not mention to any of them.\n\n\"You have been here before,\" Whistle said, to each of them.\n\n\"Yes,\" said Rooke.\n\n\"Twice,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Once, in September, and you told me nobody signs an amendment,\" said Marrow. \"You told me that for nothing and I've thought about it for seven months and I'd like to buy the rest.\"\n\nTo each of them Whistle said the same thing, in the same voice, which was low and slow and came from a long way down inside a very large chest:\n\n\"I do not sell answers. I sell what it costs me to have found them out, and what it costs me is different for each of you, because the thing I would have to hand over is different.\" A pause. \"So I will set you a problem instead. If you can answer it, I will tell you what you came for, and you will pay me half. If you cannot answer it, you will pay me nothing and go home, and that is not a kindness, it is that you would not be able to use the answer and I do not sell things to people who cannot use them.\"\n\n\"That's not how brokers work,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"It is how this one works. Sit down. The stool, not the crate, the crate has a ledger under the cloth.\"\n\n---\n\n**III. Rooke's Problem**\nWhistle put a bound volume on the counter. Green, gilt-stamped, old.\n\n\"You will know what that is.\"\n\n\"It's a published return.\" Rooke turned it round. \"Sixty-fourth year. That's the summer figures, they post them on the boards. Anybody can read this.\"\n\n\"Anybody can. Nobody does.\" Whistle laid a hand flat on it, and the hand covered most of the cover. \"In there is the strata by district for the sixty-fourth year. I have the full run. A hundred and three of them. I have had them for nineteen years and I bought them one at a time from a bindery on the Wick because nobody else wanted them.\"\n\n\"What's the problem?\"\n\n\"The problem is one question,\" said Whistle, \"and you may have the run of the volumes for as long as you like, and you may have a slate, and I will make tea and not speak to you. And the question is this.\n\n\"There is one thing in a hundred and three years of those returns that has never happened. Tell me what it is.\"\n\nRooke looked at the volume.\n\n\"That's not a problem, that's a search,\" she said. \"There's six and a half thousand numbers per volume. A hundred and three volumes is most of a million. You want me to find the absence of something in a million numbers and you haven't told me what kind of thing.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"That could take a year.\"\n\n\"It took me eleven months,\" said Whistle, \"and I am not going to pretend otherwise, and I did it with a slate and no instruction and I was thirty-four. You are fourteen and you came first stratum out of six hundred and forty-one and Professor Thorncastle has moved you into the fourth-year seminar in your first term, which I know because I know what is worth knowing.\" The great head tilted. \"I do not expect you to search. Searching is what I did. I expect you to think about what kind of thing could be missing, and then look in one place.\"\n\n---\n\nRooke sat on the stool for forty minutes and did not open a volume.\n\nWhistle made tea and did not speak.\n\nWhat she thought about was Ivy in the corridor in March, saying: *you're the best reckoner in this building and you've spent five months solving the wrong problem.*\n\n*So do not search. Think about the shape.*\n\nA published return is a sorting. Six hundred and forty-one children go into eleven strata. Nineteen districts. That is the whole object. And the question is not *what is the smallest number in there*, because Whistle would not set a question with an arithmetic answer, and it is not *which district does worst*, because everybody in Gifford already knows the answer to that and it is Sootgate.\n\n*Something that has never happened. Not never happened in one district. Never happened at all, in a hundred and three years, in a million numbers.*\n\n*Which means it must be something structural. Something that the shape of a sorting says should happen sometimes, by accident, in a hundred and three tries.*\n\nRooke put the tea down.\n\n*The strata are eleven. There are six hundred-odd children a year. If you sort six hundred children by ability into eleven bands, the top band is going to have — what?*\n\n*It depends where you put the cuts. And the cuts are the Council's business. But if the cuts are fixed, then the number in the top band varies year on year, because cohorts vary. Some years you get three brilliant children and some years you get none, and over a hundred and three years you would expect both.*\n\nShe reached over and opened the sixty-fourth year at the strata table.\n\nFirst stratum: **1**.\n\nShe turned to the sixty-fifth. First stratum: **1**.\n\nSixty-sixth. **1**.\n\nRooke went very still.\n\nShe opened four more volumes at random, from four different decades, and then she got up off the stool and went along the counter and opened eleven more, fast, and then she stopped and stood with both hands flat on the wood.\n\n\"It's always one,\" she said.\n\nWhistle said nothing.\n\n\"A hundred and three years.\" Rooke's voice had gone strange. \"There has never been a year with two people in the first stratum. There has never been a year with none. It is one, every single year, since the founding.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"That's impossible.\" She was talking fast now. \"That is not what sorting does. If the cut is fixed you get variation. You get a year with three and a year with none, you get *ties*, there are six hundred children and the top two are going to be within a mark of each other about one year in four, and in a hundred and three years there should be twenty-odd years where you cannot separate them and you'd have to put two in. There are none. And there should be five or six years where the cohort was poor and nobody reached the cut. There are none.\" She looked up. \"It's one. Exactly one. Every year for a hundred and three years.\"\n\n\"Say what that means,\" said Whistle.\n\nRooke stood in a shop on Cull Alley with a low ceiling and heard herself say it.\n\n\"It means the first stratum isn't a stratum,\" she said. \"It's a *post*. It's one appointment a year. They're not measuring where the top of the cohort falls, they're filling a chair, and if two people are good enough then one of them doesn't get in and if nobody's good enough then somebody gets in anyway.\"\n\nWhistle put the tea things away.\n\n\"That is the answer,\" Whistle said. \"You took fifty-one minutes and I took eleven months. Now you may ask me what you came for.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Corvain's Problem**\n\"Four sovereigns,\" said Corvain, and put them on the counter.\n\nWhistle did not touch them.\n\n\"Your question is which office renews the delegated authority for conduct amendments.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"You have spent three weeks on it.\"\n\n\"Twenty-three days.\"\n\n\"And you have got nowhere, and you know why you have got nowhere, and I want you to say it out loud before we go on.\"\n\nCorvain sat on the stool.\n\n\"Because I've been looking for it in the examinations office,\" she said, \"and it isn't there.\"\n\n\"Why isn't it there?\"\n\n\"I don't know. That's what I'm buying.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Whistle. \"That is what you think you are buying. Here is your problem, Officer, and it is not a problem about examinations at all.\"\n\nThe great hand went into a pigeonhole and came out with a folded sheet.\n\n\"That is a list of every standing delegated authority in the Council of Gifford. All of them. Four hundred and eleven. It is not a secret document; it is printed annually for the audit and there are nine copies and one of them is in the Council library and you could have had it in November for the asking.\" Whistle laid it flat. \"Every entry gives the authority, the office that holds it, and the renewal interval. The amendment authority is on that list. I am not going to tell you which line it is.\n\n\"Your problem is to find it without reading four hundred and eleven lines, and you are going to do that by thinking about what an office is.\"\n\nCorvain looked at the sheet.\n\n\"That's a bureaucratic problem,\" she said.\n\n\"It is the only kind you are good at,\" said Whistle, \"and you should stop being ashamed of that. The girl who was in here on Tuesday would look at this sheet and see four hundred and eleven lines and start reading. You will not, because you have spent five months in a records office and you know something she does not, which is that a delegated authority is not a permission. It is a *liability transfer*.\"\n\nCorvain sat forward.\n\n\"Say that again.\"\n\n\"A delegation exists so that a decision may be taken without the person who is answerable for it being present.\" Whistle folded both hands. \"So. Do not ask who wants the power. Ask who cannot afford to be in the room.\"\n\n---\n\nCorvain looked at the four hundred and eleven lines for about ninety seconds and then stopped looking at them.\n\n*Do not ask who wants the power. Ask who cannot afford to be in the room.*\n\n*The amendments are ordered. Somebody decides that a fourth stratum Sirrath boy becomes a ninth stratum Sirrath boy, and the decision is real, and it is taken every year forty-odd times, and it is initialled by a clerk under a delegation. And the delegation is renewed quarterly.*\n\n*Quarterly is fast. Quarterly is not how you renew a permission you are comfortable with. Quarterly is how you renew a thing you want to be able to stop.*\n\n*And who is not in the room?*\n\nShe thought about Kell. Kell was in the room; Kell signed the annual review, and the review was of the amendments. So the examinations office was in the room, and the examinations office was the one everybody would look at, which meant the examinations office was the front.\n\n*Who has never appeared once in fourteen thousand files?*\n\nAnd then she had it, and it was so simple and so enormous that she said it out loud in a flat voice like somebody reading a weather report.\n\n\"The Engine houses.\"\n\nWhistle said nothing.\n\n\"It's the Engine houses.\" Corvain's hands were flat on the counter. \"Not the Council. Not the examinations office. The amendments are ordered by the people who run the machine.\"\n\n\"Find the line,\" said Whistle.\n\nCorvain turned the sheet round and ran her finger down it, and she did not read four hundred and eleven lines, she read the twenty-nine that belonged to the Engine houses, and it was the ninth of those.\n\n*Authority: assessment amendment, Calibration, all strata. Held: Office of the Chief Engineer, Engine Houses, Aster Row. Renewal: quarterly. Exercised by: delegated clerks, Examinations Office, Tallow Street.*\n\nIt had been printed every year for the audit. There were nine copies. One of them was in the Council library.\n\nCorvain put her head in her hands.\n\n\"A hundred and three years,\" she said, into her palms.\n\n\"Nineteen years for me,\" said Whistle. \"I found it in the fourth. I have been sitting on it for fifteen and nobody has ever asked me the right question until this afternoon.\"\n\n\"Why didn't you tell somebody?\"\n\nThe Laachmi looked at her for a long moment over the half-spectacles.\n\n\"Officer,\" said Whistle. \"I am seven feet tall and I have horns and I live in a shop with a ceiling I cannot stand up under, and I came to this city because my own people put me out, and the strata figures for my district have a dent in them that I could see from the age of nineteen. Who, precisely, do you imagine I was going to tell?\"\n\n---\n\n**V. Marrow's Problem**\n\"Three marks,\" said Marrow. \"That's everything I've got that isn't owed.\"\n\n\"Keep it.\"\n\n\"I'm not taking charity off you.\"\n\n\"I did not say I would not charge you. I said keep it, because I am going to charge you something else.\" Whistle reached under the counter. \"You asked Bramwell how many people work inside the Engine houses and he refused to answer, and you came here, and you want the number.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"You will have the number for nothing, because the number is published. Four hundred and eleven reckoners hold writs to work inside the Engine houses. It is in the Council register and it has been in the Council register every year since the fifty-first.\"\n\nMarrow sat down slowly.\n\n\"Four hundred and eleven.\"\n\n\"Now here is your problem,\" said Whistle, \"and it is the one I have been keeping for nine years for somebody with your hands.\n\n\"In the founding generation there were twelve. The Twelve built it and the Twelve ran it, and for the first eleven years of the Turn there were twelve men and women with their hands on that machine and nobody else at all. It is in the same register. Volume one, page one.\n\n\"Twelve, then. Four hundred and eleven now. And the Engine has not been extended since the nineteenth year; every plan set is a hundred and three years old and every bank in that hole was cast in the founding decade. The machine is the same machine.\n\n\"Your problem is this. Nothing about the work has changed. Nothing about the machine has changed. Explain the number.\"\n\n---\n\nMarrow sat in the shop and looked at his own hands for a long time.\n\nHe thought about Bramwell saying *it isn't cleverness, son, it's nine hundred governors.*\n\nHe thought about eleven reckoners on the fourth gallery for eight hours, shouting at each other in the true calculus, while a bank of brass ran itself back through eleven positions and stopped at the right one.\n\n\"There's four ways it could go,\" he said.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"One: the machine's got harder to run. It hasn't, because it's the same machine and it's a hundred and three years old and it's been serviced, so if anything it's better understood, not worse.\n\n\"Two: they've got more to do. They haven't. Output's flat. I've read the output figures, they're on the writ notices in every compressor house in the Under, the Engine's put out the same in every decade since the twenties.\n\n\"Three: they're doing it better. Four hundred and eleven people doing a twelve-person job to a much higher standard.\" He looked up. \"Except you don't get a higher standard out of thirty-four times the people. That's not how work goes. You get thirty-four times the arguing. And the eastern feed went down for nine hours the year before I was born and eleven of them on a gallery couldn't put it right.\"\n\n\"And four?\"\n\nMarrow was quiet.\n\n\"Four is that it takes four hundred and eleven of them now,\" he said, \"to do what twelve of them used to do.\"\n\nThe stove ticked.\n\n\"Which means they can't do it,\" said Marrow. \"Individually. Not one of them can do what one of the Twelve could do, so they do it in committee, four hundred and eleven of them, with procedures and standing orders and a house full of clerks, and it *works*, mostly, because you can get a long way on procedure if the machine is stable and somebody wrote the procedures down properly a hundred years ago. And when something happens that isn't in the procedures, like the eastern feed, they stand on a gallery for eight hours and shout.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\n\"They don't know how it works,\" he said. \"That's not a suspicion any more, is it. Four hundred and eleven is the *measurement*. That's what it costs to run a machine you don't understand.\"\n\nWhistle sat back, and the crate creaked under seven feet of Laachmi.\n\n\"That is the answer,\" Whistle said. \"You took nine minutes. I want you to know that, because you will spend the rest of your life being told what a ninth stratum is worth. Nine minutes.\"\n\n\"What are you charging me?\"\n\n\"You are going to fix my ceiling,\" said Whistle. \"There is a joist over the back of this shop that has been three inches too low for nineteen years and I have not been able to stand up in my own premises since I was thirty-four, and every builder in the Under has told me it is load-bearing and cannot be moved, and I do not believe them, and neither will you when you look at it.\"\n\nMarrow looked up at the joist.\n\nHe looked at it for about a minute and a half.\n\n\"It's not load-bearing,\" he said. \"It's a strut off the old dye-works frame and the frame came out in about the sixties, you can see where they cut it. That's holding nothing at all. It's been holding nothing for forty years.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Whistle.\n\n---\n\n**VI. What They Bought**\nTo each of them, Whistle told the same thing.\n\nNot the same words. Whistle was a professional and gave each of them the piece they had paid for and the piece they could use, and let them put the rest of it together in the street on the way home, which they all three did, within about eleven minutes, in three different districts, at three different times in the same week.\n\nTo **Rooke**, who had found that the first stratum was one appointment a year:\n\n\"You are this year's.\"\n\n\"This year's what?\"\n\n\"You are not a scholar. There are nine hundred scholars in that building and they are all scholars.\" Whistle folded the volume shut. \"There is one first stratum a year and it has been one a year for a hundred and three years, and every one of them goes up the Rise, and every one of them takes seven years of instruction, and every one of them ends in the Engine houses. Every one. I have the list. A hundred and three names, and there are four on it who died and two who went abroad and ninety-seven who went to Aster Row.\"\n\nRooke stood very still.\n\n\"You are the appointment,\" said Whistle. \"You were the appointment on the morning they made you sit again.\"\n\nTo **Corvain**, who had found the office:\n\n\"Now ask yourself what the Chief Engineer wants.\"\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"Yes you do. You have known for a month and you have not let yourself finish the sentence, because you are a decent person from a good house and you have been raised to think this is about contempt.\" Whistle put the audit sheet away. \"Officer. If you ran a machine you could not repair, and your right to run it rested on a claim that your family could hear it, and once a year the city held a public examination that found the child in Gifford who could actually hear it best, what would you need to do about the other forty who could nearly do it?\"\n\nCorvain did not answer for a long time.\n\n\"You'd need there to be one,\" she said.\n\n\"You would need there to be exactly one,\" said Whistle, \"every year, for ever. Not none, because you need her. Not two, because two is a comparison. One.\"\n\nTo **Marrow**, who had found the number:\n\n\"You asked me in September who set up the amendments so that nobody signs them.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It was not the Council and it was not the examiners. The amendment authority is held by the Office of the Chief Engineer at Aster Row and has been since the fifty-first year, and it is renewed every quarter, and it is exercised by clerks at Tallow Street who have never seen the true calculus in their lives.\" Whistle's voice did not change. \"It was not done to you out of spite, and I am sorry, because spite would be easier to carry. Your paper was read by somebody who understood exactly what section nine was worth, and they moved you down five strata because you had done well, and they did it in four lines because four lines is all it takes and because nobody has ever had to explain it to anybody.\"\n\nMarrow sat with his hands on his knees.\n\n\"How many of us?\" he said. \"Not this year. All of it.\"\n\n\"Fourteen thousand two hundred and six.\"\n\n---\n\n**VII. Whistle, After**\nThey went out one at a time, on three days, and Whistle sat in the shop with the lamp hooded and did the accounts.\n\nFifty-one minutes, twenty-three days and nine minutes.\n\nThe girl had seen the shape of it out of a single number repeated a hundred and three times. The officer had found the mechanism by asking who could not afford to be in the room. The Sirrath had measured the whole collapse of a ruling class out of a staffing figure printed in a public register.\n\nThree ways in. One room.\n\nWhistle had known all of it for fifteen years and had told nobody, because there had been nobody to tell, and because a Laachmi who publishes the Council's arithmetic is a Laachmi who is found in an outfall, and because, if Whistle was honest, and Whistle was occasionally honest late at night, the knowing had been worth something in itself. Nineteen years of being the only person in Gifford who had done the sum.\n\nThat was over now.\n\nWhistle got up, and stood up, which took a moment, because it had not been done under this ceiling for nineteen years and the body had made its own arrangements.\n\nThe joist had come out on the Thursday. It had taken the boy four hours and two props and a note left in the wall cavity, signed, in a Sirrath name.\n\nWhistle stood in the middle of the shop at full height with the top of the horns eleven inches clear of the ceiling and looked at four thousand pigeonholes from a place nobody had ever looked at them from.\n\nThen Whistle wrote one line on a slip, and put it in the empty pigeonhole where the other slip had been sitting since December, and the other slip said *they will all three come back, raise the price.*\n\nThe new slip said:\n\n*They did not need me. They needed each other and they do not know it. Tell them.*\n\nWhistle looked at that for a while.\n\nThen took it out again, and tore it in half, and wrote instead:\n\n*Do not tell them. They will get there, and it will be theirs, and that is worth more than the fee.*\n\nAnd put it in the hole, and hooded the lamp, and went to bed under a ceiling three inches higher than it had been the week before.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Seven.*" },
  { n: 8, title: "The Line", text: "**I. Marrow**\nThe seduction was not money and it was not fear. It was being asked a good question.\n\nMarrow understood that afterwards, in the cellar, with sawdust in his mouth, and he understood it completely, and understanding it did not undo one single thing.\n\nIt happened in June, in the room over the Ashlight, and it took about nine minutes.\n\n\"Sit down,\" said Vane. \"I want to ask you something technical and I want you to answer it as a technical question, because that is what it is.\"\n\n\"Right.\"\n\n\"Suppose a man wanted to put the Engine out.\"\n\nMarrow's hands went still on his knees.\n\n\"I am not asking you to do anything,\" said Vane, \"and I want that on the table before we start. I am asking you a question in the way a man asks an engineer a question, and you may refuse to answer it and I will not raise it again. But I would rather ask you than the four fools downstairs who think a bomb is an argument.\"\n\n\"Why would you want the Engine out?\"\n\n\"I do not want the Engine out.\" Vane poured two glasses and pushed one across, and Marrow did not touch it. \"I want it out for *nine hours*, at a time of my choosing, in a way they cannot fix, and I want four hundred and eleven of the best-paid men in this city stood on a gallery in front of witnesses being visibly, publicly, undeniably unable to fix it.\"\n\n\"Then people die.\"\n\n\"In winter, yes. In July, no.\" Vane sat back. \"That is precisely the calculation and I have done it and I would like to walk you through it, because you are going to check it anyway.\n\n\"Vaskeld was February. Their engine went down in February and stayed down for eleven days and it killed forty-one thousand six hundred people, and every one of them died of cold or of thirst or of what happens when a city's water stops moving. Nobody died in the first day. Almost nobody died in the first three. The dying was the eleven days and the February.\n\n\"Gifford in July, for nine hours.\" He held up a hand and counted it off. \"The water towers hold thirty hours. The gas holders hold two days. The hospitals have their own compressors and the compressors have coke on site. Nobody freezes in July. What happens in nine hours in July is that the trams stop, the lifts stop, six thousand people walk home, the coke exchange loses about nine hundred sovereigns and the whole of the Rise stands in the dark for one evening.\"\n\n\"And people on the outfall gangs drown when the sumps back up.\"\n\nVane paused.\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \"Between four and eleven, on my figures, and I will not pretend to you that my figures are good, because nobody has ever measured it. Say eleven. Say twenty.\"\n\n\"You'd kill twenty people to make a point.\"\n\n\"I would kill twenty people to end a hundred and three years of it.\" Vane said it without any heat at all, and that was what made it terrible. \"And I want you to notice that you have not asked me the actual question, which is: how many has the amendment killed? Fourteen thousand two hundred and six children pushed down the strata. Ada Marchbank in a pipe-fitter's yard off the Grieves Steps who thinks she is stupid. Corrin Sallow on the water gangs with a wet cough at fifty-three who was assessed third stratum in a year when six hundred and eighty children sat. Your Ketu on her knees on a staircase on Aster Row for thirty years.\" He turned his glass. \"Nobody counts those, because they are not deaths, they are just lives that were made smaller by four lines and a stamp. Twenty against fourteen thousand. I have done that sum and I have done it every week for nineteen years and it comes out the same way every time and I would be delighted to be shown that it does not.\"\n\nMarrow sat in the warm room and did not say anything for a long moment.\n\nThen he said: \"You'd need it to be a fault they can't fix.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Not damage. Damage they can fix, damage is just a part and a shift and they've got four thousand spares at Aster Row.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It'd have to be something where the machine's still whole and still running and it's *wrong*, and they can't find why.\" Marrow was talking to himself now and he heard himself doing it and did not stop. \"Because that's the only kind of fault that makes them look like what they are. If you break something they look competent, they turn up with a crane and they mend it and everybody claps. If you put it *out of true* they've got to reason about it, and reasoning about it is the thing they can't do.\"\n\nVane did not move at all.\n\n\"Go on,\" he said.\n\nAnd Marrow, sitting in a chair in a room over a public house at nineteen minutes past six on a Tuesday evening in June, said:\n\n\"The eastern bank went out of true the year before I was born because a reckoner set a governor wrong on the fourth gallery. A twelfth out. Nine hours, eleven of them on the gallery, couldn't find it. So you don't need to break anything. You need to set one governor about a twelfth out somewhere they don't look, and you need to do it on the *feed side*, not the load side, because a load-side fault shows up on the dials in the house and a feed-side fault shows up as everything being slightly wrong everywhere, and they'll spend the first four hours checking the dials.\" He was leaning forward. \"And you'd want it on a bank that's not on the plans. Bramwell says half of it isn't on the plans. If it's not on the plan they can't even get to it in a drawing, they'd have to walk it, and walking it is a day and a half.\"\n\nSilence.\n\nMarrow heard the silence about a second and a half after he stopped talking.\n\nHe sat back very slowly.\n\n\"That was the question,\" he said.\n\n\"That was the question.\"\n\n\"You already knew the answer.\"\n\n\"I knew a version of it. I have had nine men look at this in eleven years and the best of them got as far as *put a governor out* and could not tell me which side or which bank or why.\" Vane picked up his glass. \"You did the whole of it in four minutes and you did it because I asked you nicely and you enjoy being good at something. That is not a criticism, son. That is the most human thing about you.\"\n\nMarrow stood up so fast the chair went over.\n\n\"Take it back,\" he said.\n\n\"I can't take it back. You said it out loud in a room.\"\n\n\"Then don't use it—\"\n\n\"Sit down.\"\n\n\"*Don't use it.*\"\n\n\"Sit *down*, Marrow, and listen, because there are two ways this goes and only one of them has you in the room.\" Vane had not raised his voice once. \"I am going to do this in the autumn. It was going to be November and it is going to be September now, because of what you have just told me. That is settled and it was settled before you came up those stairs.\n\n\"What is not settled is whether the man who understands the fault is in the gallery when it happens, or four streets away at a bench.\n\n\"Because a governor set a twelfth out by somebody who knows what he is doing is nine hours and twenty dead on the outfalls. And a governor set a twelfth out by Scarrow with a bar, because I sent Scarrow, because you would not come, is God knows what. Might be nothing. Might be a bank shearing and a hundred and eleven men on a floor.\"\n\nHe let that sit.\n\n\"So,\" said Vane. \"That is the shape of it, and I want you to know I am doing to you exactly what was done to me, and that I am aware of it, and that it works.\"\n\n---\n\n**II. Bramwell**\nHe knew within the week, because the boy stopped talking.\n\nBramwell had spent fifty years reading machines and about the same reading the eleven or twelve people he had let into the shop, and it was the same skill and he had never once bothered to hide that he thought so. A machine tells you it is wrong before it fails. It tells you in the noise, in the swarf, in a bearing running four degrees warm. You do not need to know what is wrong. You need to notice that the noise has changed.\n\nThe noise changed on the Thursday.\n\nMarrow came in at fifth bell and worked ten hours on a compressor governor and did it beautifully and did not say one unnecessary word, and at the end of it he cleaned down and went upstairs, and Bramwell sat on his stool in the dark with the lamp off and thought: right.\n\nHe gave it four days, because a thing that is going wrong sometimes comes right on its own and a man who leaps in on the first day is a man who never finds out what was actually happening.\n\nOn the Monday he said, \"You've gone quiet.\"\n\n\"I'm working.\"\n\n\"You've been working seven months and you've never once been quiet, you talk like a burst main, it's the single most tiring thing about you.\" Bramwell put down the file. \"Out with it.\"\n\n\"It's nothing.\"\n\n\"Son.\" Bramwell took the goggles off, which he almost never did. \"I'm seventy-one. I've got a chest like a wet sack and I'll not see eighty. There's about four things left in this world I'm any use for and one of them is this, so don't waste it.\"\n\nMarrow stood at the bench with his back to him.\n\n\"If I told you,\" he said, \"you'd have to do something about it.\"\n\n\"Aye.\"\n\n\"That's why I'm not telling you.\"\n\n\"That's not a reason, that's a *courtesy*.\" Bramwell got off the stool. \"And I'll tell you what a courtesy is, it's what people do to each other when they've already decided how it ends. You've decided. You're stood at my bench being polite to me because you've worked out what happens next and you've put me on the outside of it.\"\n\nMarrow did not turn round.\n\n\"He's asked you for something,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"He's already got it.\"\n\n\"Right.\" Bramwell breathed out slowly. \"Then that's a different conversation and it's worse. What did you give him?\"\n\n\"An answer.\"\n\n\"To what?\"\n\nAnd Marrow told him.\n\nHe told him all of it, in about four minutes, standing at the bench with a governor in bits in front of him, and Bramwell listened to the whole of it without once interrupting, which he had not done in the entire seven months.\n\nAt the end Bramwell sat down on the crate by the stove and put both hands on his knees.\n\n\"The feed side,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"On a bank that's not on the plan.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Because I told you it wasn't on the plan.\" Bramwell's voice was perfectly steady. \"In January. Sat right there, holding a lamp for me while I took a collar off, and I told you about the eastern feed going down and the eleven of them on the gallery, and I told you about the plans, and I have been carrying that story round for thirty-one years thinking it was a story about how nobody knows anything.\" He looked up. \"And you took it and made it a method.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"No, you don't, because you're seventeen, so I'll say the whole of it.\" Bramwell leaned forward. \"It's my fault. That's the first thing. I put that in your head and I did it because it made me feel clever at ten o'clock at night with a lamp in my hand, and I've done that to about nine people in fifty years and this is the first time it's come back. So don't stand there being noble at me. We're both in it.\"\n\nMarrow turned round for the first time.\n\n\"Second thing,\" said Bramwell. \"You've not done anything yet.\"\n\n\"I've told him how.\"\n\n\"You've told him a fact about a machine. That's not a crime and it's not a sin and if it were, every man who ever taught anybody anything would be in the works at Grieves.\" Bramwell coughed, and it went on a while, and he waved a hand at it. \"It's a bad thing you've done and it's not the thing you think it is. You've not made him able to do it. He was going to do it in November with Scarrow and a bar and it was going to be worse. What you've done is make it *work*, and made yourself necessary, and those are two different sins and only one of them is fixable.\"\n\n\"Which one?\"\n\n\"Necessary,\" said Bramwell. \"That's the one you can undo. And there's precisely one way and you're not going to like it.\"\n\n\"Tell him no.\"\n\n\"No. You've tried that and he's got you by the fact that Scarrow with a bar is worse, and he's right, and that's a genuinely good hook and I'd not get off it either.\" Bramwell hauled himself upright. \"You make yourself *unnecessary*. You give the fault to somebody who can stop it.\"\n\nMarrow stared at him.\n\n\"That's grassing.\"\n\n\"Aye.\"\n\n\"On Vane. In the Under.\" Marrow's voice cracked on it. \"Bramwell, you know what happens. Not to me, I don't care about me, to Ketu. She's sixty-eight and she's in a house at the end of a lane with one door.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"So what, I take her out? Where? Up the Rise? She hasn't got a writ and neither have I. Sister Maude's? He *funds* Sister Maude's, he's been funding it eleven years, everybody knows and nobody says.\"\n\n\"I know all of it, son.\"\n\n\"Then don't tell me to do it!\"\n\n\"I'm not telling you to do it,\" said Bramwell. \"I'm telling you it's the only way, and that it costs what it costs, and that you're going to have to decide, and that whichever way you decide I'll not throw you out of this shop.\" He picked up the file again. \"That's what I'm for. I'm not for telling you which. I've had seventy-one years and I've made about four decisions of that size and I got two of them wrong, and one of the two was in 1867 and there's a man in the ground because of it.\"\n\nHe put the goggles back on.\n\n\"Now go and finish that governor,\" he said. \"Because whatever happens in September, that dray goes out on Friday and there's a family in Sootgate that eats on what that dray earns, and it is possible to hold a very large thing and a very small thing in your head at the same time. It's most of what the work is.\"\n\n---\n\n**III. Corvain**\nAshcombe read the eleven pages for the ninth time and then put them face down on the desk and said:\n\n\"Right. Here's where we are, and I want you to hear all of it before you say anything, because you're going to want to interrupt at about the third sentence.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"Vale sealed the volumes on the Friday, exactly as he said he would, and he did it lawfully and there's no fault in it. Your annexe goes into his case and his case goes to the Council in September and the finding will be more constables in Sootgate. That's fixed. That's not the interesting part.\"\n\nHe turned the pages over again.\n\n\"This,\" he said, \"is not in his case. This is eleven pages built entirely out of published returns, none of it privileged, none of it seizable, and the arithmetic is sound. I've had it checked.\"\n\n\"By whom?\"\n\n\"That's the third sentence.\" Ashcombe met her eye. \"I sent it to Professor Linnea Thorncastle at the university, under my own name, on the fourteenth of April, and I asked her one question, which was: is the arithmetic sound. And she wrote back in nine days and said yes, and then she wrote back again a fortnight later, unasked, with four more pages.\"\n\nCorvain sat down without being told to.\n\n\"Sir, you can't—\"\n\n\"I've done it, so we're past can't.\" He pushed a folder across. \"And before you tell me I've exposed you, understand what I did and why. Your eleven pages prove the shape is wrong. They don't prove anybody meant it. A Council lawyer would stand up and say: districts differ, cohorts vary, the Under is poorly taught, this young woman has found a sad fact about poverty and called it a conspiracy. And they'd win, because there's no signature on anything.\"\n\n\"I know. That's why I went after the delegation.\"\n\n\"Which you've found. Office of the Chief Engineer.\" Ashcombe nodded slowly. \"And that's the single most dangerous piece of paper anybody in this building has ever put in front of me, and we'll come to it. But first, read what Thorncastle sent.\"\n\nCorvain opened the folder.\n\nIt was four pages in a hard, impatient hand, and the first line was:\n\n*Captain. Your officer is right and she has been modest, which is a fault she should be encouraged out of.*\n\nShe read it twice.\n\nThorncastle had taken the eight districts with the dent and she had done something Corvain had not thought to do, because Corvain was an investigator and not a mathematician: she had worked out how *large* the missing group was, and then she had worked out what it implied about the population of Gifford.\n\n*The deficit in the upper strata across the eight districts, over the thirty years available in the reading room, is 4,111 children. Your officer states this figure and stops. I will not stop.*\n\n*The proportion of a cohort that reaches the second stratum in a district with no deficit is stable to within a few per cent across a century. Apply that proportion to the eight districts and the shortfall is not a shortfall of promotion. It is a shortfall of people. Gifford has, on its own published figures, mislaid something in the order of four thousand reckoners in thirty years, and if the full run behaves as the last thirty do, something in the order of fourteen thousand in a century.*\n\n*I want to be precise about what that means, because it is not a moral point. Four hundred and eleven persons hold writs to work inside the Engine houses. The city has, in one century, identified and then discarded thirty-four times that number.*\n\n*I have been employed by this university for thirty-one years to produce reckoners. I have produced, at a generous estimate, eleven who were any good. It has never once occurred to me to ask where the others went, and I find that I am not able to write down how that feels.*\n\n*The arithmetic is sound. Tell your officer so. Then tell her that the arithmetic is the least of it, and that the interesting question is not why they did it but why nobody has ever added it up, and that I am fifty-nine years old and I am one of the people who did not.*\n\n*L. Thorncastle*\n\nCorvain put the pages down.\n\n\"Sir,\" she said. \"Why did you send it to her?\"\n\n\"Two reasons and only one of them's respectable.\" Ashcombe sat back. \"The respectable one is that I needed it checked by somebody whose name would stop a Council lawyer, and there are four such people in Gifford and three of them are on the Council.\"\n\n\"And the other?\"\n\n\"The other is that Linnea Thorncastle has taught the first stratum scholar every year for thirty-one years,\" said Ashcombe, \"and I have known her since we were both twenty-two, and I wanted to find out whether she was in it.\"\n\n\"And is she?\"\n\n\"No.\" He said it flatly. \"And I know that because of what she wrote in the last paragraph, and because nobody who was in it would have written that paragraph, and because I'd have been able to tell in the first line and so would you.\" He leaned forward. \"Which brings us to the Chief Engineer, and now I'm going to tell you what I've decided, and then you're going to tell me what you think of me.\"\n\n\"Sir.\"\n\n\"I'm not taking it up,\" said Ashcombe.\n\nCorvain did not move.\n\n\"Not because I'm frightened, though I am, and you should be too and I'd worry about you if you weren't. Because it won't work.\" He counted it on his fingers, the way he had done in the office in April. \"You have a printed audit sheet saying the amendment authority sits with the Engine houses. Fine. That authority is lawful. There is no offence. If I take that to the Council in September, what happens is a review, and the review reports in eleven months that the amendment process is operating as designed, which is *true*, and the delegation is quietly moved to a committee, and the audit sheet stops being printed, and you and I are in Sootgate counting lamp oil for the rest of our careers, and there is no longer any document in Gifford that connects the machine to the examination. That's the outcome. That's not pessimism, I've watched it happen twice.\"\n\n\"Then what?\"\n\n\"Then we go at the only thing they can't review away.\" Ashcombe stood up and went to the window. \"Corvain, you've been going at this like a case for eight months and it isn't a case, and I've let you, because you needed to find that out by doing it. There's no dock big enough, Vale was right. But there's a thing Vale hasn't got and it's the thing I've been sitting on since April.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"The reason.\" He turned round. \"You've got the shape and the mechanism and the arithmetic, and you have not got the reason, and neither have I, and neither did Halloran, and Halloran was offered it and took it and went quiet for fifteen years. Voss told you that in January.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So the reason is a thing that a good man, given it, accepts.\" Ashcombe put both hands on the back of his chair. \"Which means it is either a very good reason or a very good lie, and there is exactly one way to find out which, and it is not in a volume and it is not on an audit sheet. Somebody has to be told it. To their face. By whoever has been doing the telling for a hundred years.\"\n\nCorvain went cold.\n\n\"You want me to make them offer me the chair.\"\n\n\"I want you to *be offered it*, and I want you to be sat in that room with your eyes open, knowing in advance that it is coming, having decided in advance what you will do with a good reason.\" Ashcombe's voice did not change. \"Because that is the only part of this that they cannot review, seal, annexe or lose. The reason has never been written down. It only exists in a room, said out loud, by one tired person to one young one, about once a generation. That's why it's held for a hundred and three years. And it means the only way to get it into the world is for somebody to be in the room and come back out again still meaning to say it.\"\n\n\"Eleven people have been in that room, sir. All eleven stopped.\"\n\n\"Twelve,\" said Ashcombe.\n\nCorvain looked up.\n\n\"I told you in April I'd like the chance to decide what I am before somebody decided it for me.\" He said it without any drama whatsoever. \"I was twenty-nine. It was the seventy-ninth year. And I have thought about it every week for twenty-six years and I have never told another living soul, and the reason I sent your pages to Linnea Thorncastle is that she is the only other person alive who was in a room like that in the same decade, and I needed to know whether she had gone the same way as me.\"\n\n\"And had she?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Ashcombe. \"She was never offered it. That's the whole of the difference between us. She was too useful where she was.\"\n\nHe sat down heavily, and for the first time in eight months Corvain saw him look every one of his fifty-five years.\n\n\"So no,\" he said. \"I'm not taking it up. I'm going to write to the Chancellor's office and request that Officer E. Corvain be permitted to present her findings in person, and I am going to sign it, and it will be granted inside a fortnight because they will want to know exactly how much you have and they cannot find that out any other way. And then you will be in the room, and I will be outside it, and I will not be able to help you at all.\"\n\n\"Sir.\" Corvain's voice was not entirely steady. \"What did they tell you? In the seventy-ninth year?\"\n\nAshcombe looked at her for a long moment.\n\n\"If I tell you,\" he said, \"then you go into that room already knowing, and you will be able to sit there and be clever and unmoved, and you will come out with nothing, because they will see it in the first minute and they will talk about the weather.\" He shook his head. \"You have to hear it cold. It only works once. I'm sorry.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. Rooke**\nShe worked it out on the ninth of July in the fourth-year seminar, from a timetable.\n\nThat was the humiliating part and she never entirely got over it. She had spent nine weeks since Cull Alley chewing on what Whistle had told her, *you are the appointment*, and she had gone at it the way she went at everything, which was to look for the mechanism, and she had got nowhere, and then it fell out of a piece of administration.\n\nThe fourth-year seminar met on Thursdays. In the ninth week of Trinity the Reader announced that the seminar would move to Tuesdays from Michaelmas, and there was a general groan, and Ivy, who was not in the seminar and had come to meet her afterwards, said, \"Why?\"\n\n\"Fourth-years go to Aster Row on Thursdays in their last year,\" said one of the others. \"Attachment. It's on the schedule.\"\n\nAnd Rooke said, \"What attachment?\"\n\nNine faces turned to look at her, and there was a particular quality to the silence that she had learned to recognise in eleven months, which was the silence of a room realising that the girl from Ferrow Lane has not been told something everybody else has known since they were six.\n\n\"The Engine houses,\" said Antony Vex.\n\nHe said it flatly, without cruelty. They had not become friends. They had become something else which Rooke could not name and which involved him telling her true things in front of other people about twice a term.\n\n\"Fourth-year attachment. Four days a week for a year, then the writ, then you're in.\" He was watching her. \"That's what this is. That's what all of it is. You did know that.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Rooke, it's the *course*.\"\n\n\"I know it's the course. I know where the fourth years go.\" She heard her own voice and did not like it. \"I thought it was one route. I thought there was, I don't know, teaching, and the instrument trade, and the university, and the Engine houses were the one everybody wanted—\"\n\n\"There's no other route,\" said Vex.\n\n\"There has to be.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nAnd Rooke stood in the seminar room in July and could not answer that, because there did not have to be, and she had assumed there was, and she had assumed it for eleven months without ever once checking, and she was the girl who had checked a footnote five times before contradicting a dead man.\n\nShe went to the library. It took her forty minutes.\n\nA hundred and three first stratum scholars. Ninety-seven of them at Aster Row, four dead, two abroad. Whistle had told her that in April and she had heard it as a statistic about other people.\n\nAnd underneath it, in the university calendar, printed every year, available to anybody:\n\n*The first stratum scholarship is founded upon the endowment of the Engine Houses and is held subject to the conditions thereof.*\n\nFounded upon the endowment.\n\n*Subject to the conditions thereof.*\n\nRooke sat in the second gallery with the calendar open on her knees and understood, with a completeness that made her feel physically ill, that she had not been rescued.\n\nShe had been *bought*.\n\nThe carriage on Ferrow Lane, the twenty minutes to pack, Pip in the doorway, Mother Halloway's hand shaking on her shoulder, Tessa on the step in the cold saying *take twenty back*, all of it, the whole of the worst day of her life, had been a procurement. Somebody at Aster Row had a chair to fill, and once a year the city ran an examination to find them somebody who could actually do the work, and this year it had been her, and the *reason* she had been chased down and made to sit again was not that Elspeth Corvain had watched a girl make herself smaller and found it intolerable.\n\nIt was that the chair was empty and there was nobody else.\n\nShe sat with that for a long time.\n\nAnd then she thought the thing that turned her, and it did not arrive as anger; it arrived as arithmetic, cold and clean, the way it always did.\n\n*One a year. For a hundred and three years. And they need her, or they would not come and get her.*\n\n*Which means the appointment is the only thing in this entire city that the Wrought cannot do without and cannot make.*\n\n*Which means every single one of the hundred and three had something, for about a year, that nobody has ever used.*\n\nRooke got up and went to find Ivy.\n\n\"I need everything you can get me,\" she said, \"on what the university's writs can and can't do. Not instruction. Not enquiry. The other schedule, the one we didn't read, the one about the Engine houses' endowment.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because I've been trying to sell them my time,\" said Rooke, \"and I've had it the wrong way up for eleven months. They're not doing me a favour. They've got a chair they can't fill and I'm the only person in Gifford who can fill it, and I have spent a *year* asking that building for sixty marks like a girl fixing a pump.\"\n\n---\n\n**V. Scarrow's Test**\nThey did it in the cellar under the Ashlight on a Tuesday in August, with sawdust down and eleven men round the walls, and Marrow understood as he came down the steps that he had made his decision four days ago without noticing.\n\nHe had gone to Bramwell. He had asked what unnecessary looked like. Bramwell had told him the price. Marrow had spent four days looking at the price and had not paid it, and had not refused to pay it either, and had come to this cellar instead, which was itself an answer.\n\nVane was at the back with a glass, sitting, not participating.\n\nScarrow was in the middle of the floor.\n\nHe was Sirrath, thirty-five, dark brindled, and enormous in a way that was not about height; he was Marrow's height and about half again his width. One ear gone entirely. Scarring across the throat that had changed his voice years ago into something that came out flat and wrong and made everything he said sound like a statement of fact.\n\n\"Nothing personal,\" said Scarrow.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"He does this.\" Scarrow rolled his shoulders. \"It's not a fight. It's a look at you. Don't try and win, you'll not win, just don't go down soft. Nine of them went down soft and he sent them home.\"\n\n\"That's decent of you.\"\n\n\"It's not decent, it's that I've done nine of these and the soft ones take longer.\" Scarrow put his hands up. \"Right.\"\n\n---\n\nMarrow lost the first ninety seconds badly and he lost them because he tried to fight.\n\nHe was seventeen and six foot four and he had never in his life been in a proper fight, because he was six foot four and a Sirrath, and the whole of his experience of violence was that people did not start them with him. He went in with his weight forward and his hands up like something out of a picture, and Scarrow took him apart with a kind of bored efficiency: two into the body, one into the side of the knee that was not hard but which arrived in the exact place where the ankle he had wrecked in the outfall lane in December had rebuilt itself slightly wrong, and Marrow went down on the sawdust and could not get air.\n\nHe got up. Scarrow put him down again.\n\nThe third time he got up Scarrow said, not unkindly, \"Stay there.\"\n\nAnd Marrow, on his knees in the sawdust with blood coming out of his nose and one eye already going, thought:\n\n*Stop fighting. You cannot fight. You have never been able to fight and you are not going to learn it in a cellar.*\n\n*Look at it.*\n\n*It's a mechanism.*\n\n---\n\nHe watched Scarrow for the next forty seconds and did nothing at all except get hit, which cost him a rib.\n\nBut he watched.\n\nAnd what he saw was this: Scarrow had done nine of these, and Scarrow was thirty-five and had a throat that had been opened once, and Scarrow's whole method was a repeating cycle of four movements. Left into the body, right into the body, step, then the heavy one. Left, right, step, heavy. He had done it eleven times in two minutes and he had done it in the same order every single time, because he had done it nine hundred times in nineteen years and it worked, and nobody had ever made him change it.\n\n*Nine hundred governors,* Bramwell had said. *That's the only thing I've got that you haven't.*\n\n*And the trouble with nine hundred of anything,* thought Marrow, on his knees, *is that you stop measuring.*\n\nThe step was the fault.\n\nNot the punches. The step. Because on the step Scarrow's weight went across onto the left foot for about a third of a second before the heavy one came over, and for that third of a second every ounce of him was standing on one point, and it was a point about eleven inches inside his own centre, and he had been getting away with it for nineteen years because everybody he had ever fought had been busy being hit.\n\nMarrow did not have to hit him. He had learned about levers off a compressor governor in January and he had cut a cam profile in bronze in eleven days and he knew, with total precision, what happens to a loaded structure when you take away the point it is standing on.\n\n*Left. Right. Step.*\n\nHe went low and took the ankle.\n\nIt was not a fighting move at all. There was nothing in it of technique. It was a seventeen year old Sirrath who weighed fourteen stone putting the whole of that fourteen stone through one point on the outside of a man's left ankle at the exact third of a second when that ankle was carrying everything.\n\nSomething in it went with a sound that Marrow heard through his hands.\n\nScarrow came down like a wall.\n\n---\n\nThe cellar was completely silent.\n\nMarrow got up. It took him a while. He stood over Scarrow, who was not unconscious, who was in fact entirely conscious and looking up at him with an expression of pure uncomplicated astonishment, and whose left leg was at an angle that leg does not go.\n\n\"Finish it,\" said somebody by the wall.\n\nMarrow looked at the man who had said it. He did not know him. He was a man of about thirty in a good coat with a glass in his hand, and he had said it the way you would say *pass the salt*.\n\n\"Finish it,\" he said again.\n\nAnd here is what Marrow felt, standing in a cellar in August over a man with a broken leg, with a rib gone and one eye closing and sawdust in his mouth:\n\nNothing.\n\nHe felt nothing at all.\n\nHe had been waiting for it. That was the thing. Ever since March, since the wedge in the gate and the terrible discovery that a bad thing can feel like nothing, he had been carrying a promise around like a coin in a pocket: *I will stop when it stops feeling like nothing.*\n\nAnd he had just broken a man's leg with his whole body weight in front of eleven witnesses and he felt exactly as much as he had felt putting a piece of oak in a gate, which was nothing, and the nothing did not arrive as numbness. It arrived as *competence*. It felt like a good cut on a bronze blank. It felt like the beat going out of the floor at number four compressor house.\n\nHe had solved a mechanism and it had worked and there was a man on the floor.\n\nMarrow stood there and understood that the promise had been worthless, that it had always been worthless, that there was never going to come a day when it stopped feeling like nothing, because the whole architecture of him was built to find the fault and take the point away and feel good about it, and that was the thing Bramwell had trained and it was the thing Vane had bought, and it was the same thing.\n\nHe turned round and looked at Silas Vane at the back of the cellar.\n\nVane was on his feet. He was not smiling. He had the expression of a man who has been shown a piece of work.\n\n\"Well,\" said Vane.\n\n\"Get him a surgeon,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"He'll have one.\"\n\n\"Now. Not after. Now, while I'm stood here, because if you're going to have him carried up those steps and left in a room I want to be the one that says no.\" His voice came out flat and wrong and he barely recognised it. \"Ratchet. Go and get Maude.\"\n\nRatchet went, fast, and nobody stopped him.\n\n\"You've made your point,\" said Vane.\n\n\"I haven't made any point at all.\" Marrow spat sawdust and blood onto the floor. \"That's what I'm trying to tell you. There wasn't a point in it. I looked at him and he was a mechanism and I found the fault and I took the point away and I'd do it again in four seconds and it wouldn't cost me anything.\" He wiped his mouth. \"You asked me in June whether you had me. You have. That's what this was for and it's worked. I'm in.\"\n\nVane studied him.\n\n\"You do not sound like a man saying yes,\" he said.\n\n\"No,\" said Marrow. \"I sound like a man who's just found out what he is.\"\n\nHe walked to the bottom of the steps and stopped, and put his hand on the wall, because the rib had begun to arrive properly.\n\n\"September,\" he said. \"I'll do the governor. Not Scarrow, not any of them, me, because me is nine hours and twenty on the outfalls and them is a bank shearing and a hundred and eleven men on a floor, and I've run both and I'd rather carry the twenty.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"And I want three things and they're not negotiable, and if you say no to any of them I'll walk up these stairs and go to the enforcement division tonight, and you know I mean it, because you've just watched me put a man down without feeling anything and you're clever enough to be frightened of exactly that.\"\n\nVane's glass stopped halfway.\n\n\"Name them.\"\n\n\"Ketu goes up the Rise in August. Not to a house, to a room, paid for a year, in a district where you've got nobody, and I'll want the address and I'll check it.\" Marrow held up a finger. \"Two: the outfall gangs come out. All of them, all eight sumps, for the whole nine hours. I don't care how you do it and I don't care what it costs you, you get eleven hundred men out of the outfalls before that governor moves, and if you can't do that then there's no operation because the whole of your arithmetic is the twenty and I've just taken the twenty off you.\"\n\n\"And the third?\"\n\nMarrow looked back over his shoulder.\n\n\"When it's done,\" he said, \"and they're stood on that gallery in front of the witnesses and they can't fix it, I put it right.\"\n\n\"You what?\"\n\n\"I fix it. Me. In front of them.\" Marrow's one open eye did not move. \"Nine hours. That's what you said. Nine hours and then the city gets its Engine back, and it doesn't get it back because it corrected itself and it doesn't get it back because four hundred and eleven of them worked it out. It gets it back because a ninth stratum Sirrath off Sootgate walks onto the fourth gallery and puts a governor back a twelfth of a turn.\"\n\nThe cellar was silent.\n\n\"That's not your point,\" said Marrow. \"That's mine. You want to prove they can't. I want to prove *we can*. And I'll do your half to get my half, and I want you to understand that I know exactly what that makes me.\"\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Eight.*" },
  { n: 9, title: "The Ledger", text: "**I. Rooke**\n\"Ask me the question you came to ask,\" said Professor Thorncastle, \"and stop circling it, you've been in this room eleven minutes and you've asked me about the endowment schedule three times in three different ways.\"\n\nRooke put the calendar down.\n\n\"What happens if I say no?\"\n\nThorncastle's hands went still on the desk.\n\nIt was the second week of August and the room on the north side was hot and the window was open onto brown air, and the Professor of Reckoning of the University of the Engine sat behind her desk and did not answer for a very long time.\n\n\"Say the whole of it,\" she said eventually.\n\n\"There is one first stratum a year. There has been one a year for a hundred and three years, never two, never none, which means it isn't a measurement, it's an appointment.\" Rooke had rehearsed this and heard herself deliver it and did not care. \"The scholarship is founded on the endowment of the Engine houses and held subject to the conditions thereof. Ninety-seven of the hundred and three went to Aster Row. The fourth-year attachment is four days a week and then the writ and then you're in.\n\n\"So I'm not a scholar. I'm a seven-year procurement with a chair at the end of it. And I have spent eleven months in this building being grateful, and I would like to know what happens if I decline.\"\n\n\"Nothing happens,\" said Thorncastle.\n\n\"That's not—\"\n\n\"I mean it literally. Nothing happens, and that is the answer, and it is not a comfortable one.\" Thorncastle got up and went to the window. \"There is no penalty in the statute. There is no bond. You are not indentured, nobody has ever been indentured, and if you walked out of this building tomorrow the only thing that would happen is that your maintenance allowance would stop and you would be a fourteen year old girl on the Rise with no writ and nowhere to sleep. That is the whole of the enforcement and it has been sufficient for a hundred and three years, because it has never once been tested.\"\n\n\"Never?\"\n\n\"Never.\" Thorncastle turned round. \"Not once. A hundred and three first strata and not one has ever said no, and I have taught thirty-one of them and it has never occurred to a single one of them that no was available, and it did not occur to me either until about four minutes ago.\" She sat down on the window seat, which Rooke had never seen her do. \"Because they come up that hill at fourteen out of a house where they were hungry, and this building gives them books and heat and four meals and the first adult who has ever taken them seriously, and by the time they are old enough to ask what it is for, they want it. Every one. Including the eleven who were any good.\"\n\n\"That's the mechanism,\" said Rooke. \"That's not a trap, it's a *kindness*, and it works better than a trap because you can't be angry at it.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did you know?\"\n\n\"I knew every part of it and I had never once put the parts together, which I have recently been told is the characteristic crime of my generation.\" Thorncastle's mouth went thin. \"A captain of the enforcement division sent me eleven pages in April and asked me whether the arithmetic was sound, and I checked it, and it was, and then I sat in this room for a fortnight and did the rest of the sum myself and wrote him four pages I have regretted every day since. Not because they were wrong.\"\n\n\"What was in them?\"\n\n\"That is not mine to give you and I will not, so do not press it.\" Thorncastle looked up. \"But I will tell you the one line I could not get out of my head, because you may as well have it. I have been employed here for thirty-one years to produce reckoners. I have produced eleven who were any good. And it had never once occurred to me to ask where the others went.\"\n\nRooke sat very still.\n\n\"There aren't any others,\" she said. \"That's what you mean. There's one a year and the others are in the ninth.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So you've spent thirty-one years teaching the one they sent you and the whole rest of your subject is in Sootgate with a card.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Thorncastle. \"And I would ask you to understand that I am fifty-nine and I found that out in April and I have not yet worked out what to do with it, and that a person who has just discovered they have been complicit for thirty-one years is not a reliable ally. I would rather tell you that now than have you find it out at a bad moment.\"\n\n---\n\nRooke went away and thought about it for four days.\n\nWhat she kept coming back to was not the injustice, because the injustice was enormous and old and she had known about it in some form since she was nine years old on Ferrow Lane. It was the *thinness*.\n\nA hundred and three years, and the entire enforcement was that a fourteen year old would not want to sleep outside.\n\nThat was all. There was no bond, no penalty, no statute, nothing. The Wrought of Gifford held the Engine on one thread and the thread was that they got to the clever children early enough to make them grateful.\n\nAnd Rooke had been made grateful, and it had worked, and she was clear-eyed about that. She wanted the library. She wanted the fourth-year seminar. She wanted, with an intensity that frightened her, to find out what was actually inside the Turn, and Aster Row was the only door.\n\n*So do not refuse. Refusing gets you a step on Ferrow Lane and an empty chair for one year and they fill it the next summer and nothing changes at all.*\n\n*Name terms.*\n\nShe sat in the second gallery with Ivy on the fourth day and said, \"How does a person get an audience with the Chancellor?\"\n\n\"You don't. Nobody does.\" Ivy did not look up from her volume. \"My family's been on the Rise for four generations and my aunt holds a chair and she's been in a room with Isolde Prewitt about six times in her life and four of them were at funerals.\"\n\n\"Then how does the Chancellor get an audience with me?\"\n\nIvy's head came up slowly.\n\n\"Say that again.\"\n\n\"They came and got me,\" said Rooke. \"In a carriage. On the third day, with a form and two men in grey coats, twenty minutes to pack. That is not what a city does for a scholarship. That is what a city does when a chair is empty.\" She closed the calendar. \"I've spent eleven months trying to get someone up here to pay attention to me and I've been going about it like a girl fixing a pump. The way you get the Chancellor of Gifford to sit down with you is that you become a problem she has to solve before September.\"\n\n\"How?\"\n\n\"By putting it in writing,\" said Rooke, \"and sending it to the wrong people first.\"\n\n---\n\nWhat she wrote was four lines and she wrote nine drafts of it.\n\n*To the Office of the Chief Engineer, Engine Houses, Aster Row.*\n\n*I am the first stratum scholar of this year. I understand from the university calendar that my scholarship is founded upon your endowment and held subject to your conditions.*\n\n*Before I begin the fourth-year attachment I should like to know what the conditions are, since they are not printed anywhere I can find and nobody in this university appears to have read them. I should also like to know the terms on which I may decline, which I am told have never been established.*\n\n*I am fourteen. I would rather ask now than at twenty-one.*\n\n*R. (first stratum, this year)*\n\nShe sent one copy to the Chief Engineer.\n\nShe sent one copy to the Proctor of the Long Hall, formally, requesting that it be minuted, because a Contention had taught her that the Rise could not ignore a thing once it was in a minute book.\n\nAnd she sent one copy, unsigned, in a plain hand, to the enforcement division at the seam, addressed to *the officer who observed the Calibration from the gallery*, because eleven months ago a young woman in a grey coat had stood at the back of an empty hall and said *I have never in my life been so certain that I was looking at something that was not supposed to happen*, and Rooke had spent eleven months hating her for it and had recently begun to suspect that hating her had been a mistake.\n\nThe reply from the Chancellor's office came in nine days.\n\n---\n\n**II. Ketu**\nThey moved her on the fourteenth of August and she went, and she went because he asked, and she would like it recorded that she knew exactly what she was agreeing to.\n\nThat was the thing the boy did not understand and would not understand for years. He thought he was protecting her. He thought he had negotiated her out of a house with one door, in a lane where a man could send somebody, and up onto the Rise into a room paid for a year in a district where the Ashlight had nobody.\n\nHe had. That was all true.\n\nWhat he had not worked out, because he was seventeen and because he had been raised by a woman who had made a lifetime's work of not being a burden, was that a room on the Rise paid for a year by Silas Vane was not an escape. It was a *deposit*. She was collateral now. She had been collateral before in a different way, on a slate in a ledger at ten grist a week for nine years, and at least on the slate she had been paying it herself.\n\nKetu did not say any of that to him. She had thought about it for four days and decided not to, and the decision had cost her more than the move.\n\nBecause he had come to her with one eye closed and a rib strapped and his hands shaking, and he had said, \"You're going up the Rise on Thursday, it's a room, it's paid, it's Cullen Street,\" and his voice had been full of something she recognised, which was a young person who has done a terrible thing and has found one good consequence to hold up in front of it.\n\nIf she had said *this makes it worse, boy, this ties you tighter*, he would have believed her, because he was clever and it was true.\n\nAnd then he would have had nothing at all.\n\nSo Ketu packed a bag with eleven things in it, and locked a door she had opened every morning for forty-one years, and let a Sirrath boy carry her box up the Grieves Steps to the clatterlift, and said nothing.\n\n---\n\nThe room was on the fourth floor of a house on Cullen Street. It was clean. It had a window with sash panes and a range and a bed and a chair and a table, and it had a boxed-in pipe behind the panelling, which meant there was no knock in the wall, which meant that for the first night in sixty-eight years Ketu lay in a bed in Gifford and could not hear the Turn.\n\nShe did not sleep. She got up at fourth bell out of forty-one years of habit and stood at the window in the dark, and she could see the whole of the Under from up there.\n\nIt was extraordinary. She had not known. She had gone up that hill every working morning of her adult life and she had gone up it to scrub, with her eyes down, in the servants' stair, and she had never once in thirty years stood at a window on the Rise at fourth bell and looked down.\n\nThere was the fog. She had lived under it her whole life and she had never seen the top of it. It lay across the whole of the Under like something breathing, and where the vents went off it heaved, and the gaslights underneath it made it glow in patches, orange and dirty, so that the entire lower city looked like a fire seen through a blanket.\n\nAnd down there, somewhere under that, was a lane with a door she had locked.\n\nKetu stood at the window until it got light.\n\nThen she washed, and dressed, and went out, and asked the woman on the second floor where the nearest grocer was, and walked eleven minutes to it, and bought bread and tea and a quarter of dripping, and paid in coin, and came back.\n\nAnd on the way back she worked out what she was going to do, and it took her the length of Cullen Street.\n\nShe was sixty-eight. Her knees had gone. She had no writ and no trade beyond stairs and no money that was not somebody else's. She could not stop him, and she could not protect him, and she was not going to spend the year she had left in this room being kept.\n\nBut she had scrubbed the stairs of eleven houses on the Rise for thirty years, and there is nothing on this earth that hears more than a woman on her knees on a landing with a brush, whom nobody has ever bothered to learn the name of.\n\nKetu knew things. She had known things for thirty years and had never had the slightest use for any of them.\n\nShe sat down at the table in the clean room with the light coming in the sash window and began, slowly, in a hand she had taught herself at fifty because nobody had taught her at five, to write a list of what she knew about the houses on Aster Row.\n\n---\n\n**III. Marrow**\nWhistle broke a nineteen-year rule on the twenty-first of August and did it without any particular ceremony.\n\nMarrow came in to say the ceiling was still holding, which was a lie and both of them knew it, and Whistle looked at the closed eye and the strapping and said:\n\n\"Sit down. You are going to be angry with me and I would like you sitting.\"\n\n\"I'm not—\"\n\n\"In April I sold three people three answers.\" Whistle folded both hands on the counter. \"You, an officer of the enforcement division, and a first stratum scholar. Within the same week. Not one of you knew about the others, and I wrote myself a note saying I should tell you, and then I tore it up and wrote another one saying I should not, on the grounds that you would find each other eventually and that it would be worth more if it were yours.\"\n\nMarrow stared at him.\n\n\"That was four months ago,\" he said.\n\n\"It was four months ago.\"\n\n\"You've known for four months that there's—\"\n\n\"I have known for fifteen years that there was one of you at a time,\" said Whistle. \"That is the whole of my trade and I want you to understand what it has consisted of. Every four or five years somebody comes into this shop and asks a good question, and I answer it, and they go out and they do nothing, because one person with the truth in Gifford is a person with an interesting grievance.\" The great head came forward. \"Three at once has not happened before. Not in nineteen years. And I decided in April that it would be better if you found each other, and I was wrong, and I have been wrong since about June, and I am too old to sit on a thing out of vanity for a second time.\"\n\n\"What changed?\"\n\n\"You came in here on Monday and asked me what the water towers hold,\" said Whistle.\n\nMarrow went very still.\n\n\"Thirty hours,\" said Whistle. \"That is the answer and I gave it to you and I charged you nothing, and then I sat here on Monday night and asked myself why a seventeen year old Sirrath with a broken rib wanted to know the reserve capacity of the municipal water towers in August. And I could think of four reasons and three of them were nothing and the fourth one was the eastern feed.\"\n\nThe shop was quiet.\n\n\"I'm not going to tell you anything,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"I am not asking. I have never once asked a question in this shop, it is a matter of professional pride.\" Whistle reached into a pigeonhole. \"I am going to give you two things and then you may go, and what you do with them is not my business.\n\n\"The first is a name. Elspeth Corvain, enforcement division, twenty-one years old, four months in the coat when she came in here and eleven months in it now. She is the officer who had you brought in front of the strata boards in September, in the sense that she is the officer who broke the girl's concealment and started the whole of this, and she has spent a year proving what was done to you and she has got further with it than anybody in a hundred and three years, and eight days ago she was granted an audience with the Chancellor of Gifford, which is a thing that does not happen.\"\n\n\"And the second?\"\n\n\"The second is that she does not know about you.\" Whistle laid a folded slip on the counter. \"She knows there is a Sirrath boy out of Sootgate whose file she read in November. She does not know that he is seventeen and working at Bramwell's and that he understands the Engine better than four hundred and eleven men on Aster Row, and she does not know that he has been asking about water towers.\"\n\nMarrow looked at the slip and did not pick it up.\n\n\"You want me to go to a coat,\" he said.\n\n\"I want nothing. I am a broker and I have made a bad decision and I am correcting it.\" Whistle pushed the slip an inch closer. \"But I will say one thing to you as a person rather than as a trade, and then I will not mention it again.\n\n\"You are about to do something in September. I do not know what it is and I do not wish to. And whatever it is, you have arranged it so that you carry it, alone, because you have worked out that you can carry it better than anybody else available, and that is true, and it is the reason you will do it, and it is exactly what the man over the Ashlight has been counting on since the day he closed Ketu's account.\"\n\n\"That's not—\"\n\n\"He does not recruit angry boys, Marrow. Anger burns out in two years.\" Whistle's voice was very low. \"He recruits *capable* ones, and he does it by arranging matters so that the capable thing and the terrible thing are the same act. And I have watched him do it for nineteen years and the only ones who ever got out were the ones who found somebody else to be capable with.\"\n\n---\n\nMarrow took the slip.\n\nHe walked home across the seam with it in his coat and he did not open it for two days, and on the second night he sat on the end of the bed in the room over the shop with a bronze blank in his hands and thought about it properly.\n\n*Nine hours. Twenty dead on the outfalls, and I have taken the twenty off him, so it should be nobody, and it will not be nobody, because there is no operation in the history of the world that has gone the way it was drawn.*\n\n*And then I walk onto the fourth gallery in front of the witnesses and put a governor back a twelfth of a turn.*\n\n*And that is the part I want. That is the part I have wanted since I was fifteen years old with a stolen syllabus on Ketu's kitchen table, and Vane has understood that about me for four months, and he has built the whole operation so that the only way I get it is through him.*\n\nHe turned the blank over.\n\n*Unless there is another way to be in that gallery.*\n\nMarrow sat in the dark for a long time.\n\nThen he opened the slip.\n\n---\n\n**IV. The Chancellor's Ledger**\nThe Chancellor's office was on the fourth floor of the Council building and it had a window that looked out over the whole of the Rise, and the first thing Corvain noticed was that there was nothing in the room.\n\nShe had expected grandeur. There was a desk, four chairs, a wall of brass filing drawers, a green-shaded lamp and a carpet. The walls were plain. There was not one single object in that room that was there to impress anybody, and Corvain understood immediately that this was the most expensive room she had ever been in, and that the expense was the emptiness.\n\n\"Sit down, Officer,\" said Isolde Prewitt. \"Would you like tea? I have discovered that people always say no and always want it, so I have had it brought anyway.\"\n\n\"Thank you, ma'am.\"\n\n\"Isolde is fine in this room. Chancellor everywhere else.\" Prewitt poured. She was fifty-three and dressed simply and looked, Corvain thought, exactly like a woman who had been running something difficult for twelve years and had stopped noticing that this was unusual. \"Now. You have eleven pages built out of published returns, four pages from Linnea Thorncastle which she sent to Reeve Ashcombe unasked, an audit sheet establishing that the amendment authority sits with the Office of the Chief Engineer, and a hundred and three volumes of assessed results which Marcus Vale sealed into an evidence room in March and which I could have unsealed for you at any time in the last five months if you had asked me.\"\n\nCorvain put the tea down.\n\n\"You've known the whole time.\"\n\n\"I have known since November that Adelina Corvain's daughter was in the records office at Tallow Street, and I have known the extent of it since about April.\" Prewitt sat back. \"I want to establish that at the start because I am not going to spend this conversation being caught out, it is undignified and it wastes the afternoon. You have done a year of extremely good work and nobody has stopped you, and you have presumably noticed that nobody has stopped you, and you have presumably wondered why.\"\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"Say why.\"\n\n\"Because being stopped would have made me certain,\" said Corvain, \"and being left alone made me thorough, and thorough is slower.\"\n\n\"Partly.\" Prewitt inclined her head. \"Mostly it is that I have been waiting for you to get all of it, because there is no use whatever in telling this to somebody who has half. Halloran had half, in the sixty-first year, and it took him nine pages to circle it and he never wrote it down, and I have read those nine pages about forty times.\" She reached down and lifted something onto the desk. \"Now. Before I tell you, you are going to prove to me that you can read it, because I am not going to explain a hundred and three years to somebody who cannot follow the arithmetic. Ashcombe will have warned you that this was coming.\"\n\n\"He said I'd be offered a chair.\"\n\n\"He would know.\" Prewitt did not react at all. \"This is not the chair. The chair comes afterwards and you will not be surprised by it. This is the entrance examination, and it is the only one in this city that has never been amended.\"\n\nShe opened the ledger.\n\n---\n\nIt was enormous and it was old and it was not a book so much as a bound accumulation, and both facing pages were dense with figures in six different hands.\n\n\"Engine house output and establishment,\" said Prewitt. \"Annual, since the founding. It is not secret and it is not published. Nobody has ever asked for it because nobody has ever had a reason to want output and establishment on the same page.\" She turned it round to face Corvain. \"There are four columns. Output in units of delivery, which you may treat as a black box, it is the number the Chief Engineer reports. Establishment, being the number of writ-holding reckoners. Corrections logged, being faults found and rectified. And interval, being the mean time between scheduled recalibrations.\n\n\"A hundred and three rows. You may have as long as you like and you may have a pencil and paper. Tell me what has happened to this city.\"\n\nCorvain looked at the page.\n\nThen she took out her notebook and did what she had learned to do in a reading room in March, which was to stop looking at the numbers and start looking at the shape.\n\nIt took her twenty-six minutes.\n\n\"Output is flat,\" she said. \"It rises for the first nineteen years and then it is flat within about two per cent for eighty-four years. That's not a machine at capacity, that's a machine being run at a fixed setting because nobody dares change it.\"\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"Establishment is twelve for eleven years and then it climbs, and it climbs steadily, and it does not stop. Twelve, forty-one, a hundred and nine, two hundred and sixty, four hundred and eleven.\" She turned a page. \"And it is not correlated with output at all. They are doing the same amount of work with thirty-four times the people.\"\n\n\"Which means?\"\n\n\"Which means the work has got harder or the people have got worse, and the machine hasn't changed, so it's the people.\"\n\n\"And corrections?\"\n\nCorvain ran her finger down the third column and stopped.\n\n\"Corrections logged goes *up* until about the fortieth year,\" she said slowly. \"Nine, fourteen, twenty-two, thirty-one. And then it comes down. And it keeps coming down. And in the last thirty years it's...\" She checked it twice. \"It's between nought and two. It's been nought in eleven of the last thirty years.\"\n\n\"And what does a falling correction rate mean?\"\n\n\"Either the machine has stopped developing faults.\" Corvain's mouth had gone dry. \"Or they have stopped being able to find them.\"\n\n\"Which?\"\n\n\"The interval.\" She went to the fourth column. \"Mean time between recalibrations. It's eleven days at the founding. It's four years now.\" She looked up. \"You can't find a fault you only look for every four years. They didn't stop finding faults because there were none. They stopped looking, because looking meant taking the thing apart, and taking the thing apart meant somebody had to be able to put it back.\"\n\nPrewitt did not move.\n\n\"Say the sentence, Officer.\"\n\nCorvain sat back in the chair in the empty room.\n\n\"The Wrought have not been able to run the Engine for about sixty years,\" she said. \"You have been operating a machine you do not understand by not touching it, and you have got away with it because it is a very good machine, and the entire establishment of four hundred and eleven exists to maintain a procedure written down by people who *did* understand it, and every year the procedure works and nobody has to find out what would happen if it stopped working.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And the amendments—\" She stopped. \"Oh.\"\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"The amendments aren't about keeping the Under down at all.\" Corvain heard her own voice go strange. \"You need one. Every year you need one person who can actually do it, because the procedure isn't enough on its own, there has to be somebody who can *reason* about the machine when something happens that isn't in the book. And you can't produce one out of your own families any more, so you go out into the city once a year and find one, and you take her.\n\n\"And the forty others who nearly could—\"\n\n\"Are the problem,\" said Prewitt.\n\n\"Are the *proof*.\" Corvain put both hands flat on the desk. \"If you promoted all forty-one, then within one generation there would be four hundred Rustbound reckoners in the Engine houses, and every one of them would be visibly better at it than every Wrought child in the building, and the claim that the true calculus runs in the blood of the Twelve would be dead inside ten years. So it isn't spite and it isn't snobbery. It's that two is a comparison.\"\n\n\"That is the whole of it,\" said Prewitt. \"You are the first person to get there in this room without being told, and it took you twenty-six minutes and a ledger, and I would like you to know that I am not being kind when I say that.\"\n\n---\n\nPrewitt closed the ledger.\n\n\"Now,\" she said. \"The reason. Because you have the mechanism and the motive, and you think the motive is legitimacy, and legitimacy is only the *shape* of it. Nobody maintains a system for a hundred and three years to protect their own vanity. They maintain it because they are frightened of something.\"\n\n\"Vaskeld,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Vaskeld.\" Prewitt got up and went to the window. \"Everybody in Gifford knows the number and nobody in Gifford knows the story, and I am going to tell it to you properly because it is the only argument I have got and it deserves to be made well.\n\n\"Vaskeld had an engine. Not ours; theirs was smaller and later and built by people who had read about ours. And Vaskeld had a council of about the same sort as ours, holding it on about the same sort of claim, and in the sixty-eighth year Vaskeld had a reformer.\n\n\"He was not a bad man. I want to be extremely clear about that, because it would be much more comfortable for me if he had been. His name was Aurel Sant and he was a lawyer and he was *right*. He established, publicly, with documents, that the Vaskeld council could not run their engine, that the qualification was a fiction, and that the examination by which they recruited had been managed for forty years. All of it true. All of it provable. He published in the summer.\n\n\"The council fell in nine weeks. There was no violence to speak of; they simply stopped being obeyed, which is what falling is. And a committee of the qualified took over the engine houses, and the committee was drawn from the people who could actually do the work, which was the correct principle and I have never once disputed it.\"\n\nShe turned round.\n\n\"And then in February the engine went out of true, and the committee could not fix it, and it took eleven days, and it killed forty-one thousand six hundred people.\"\n\nCorvain said nothing.\n\n\"Do you understand what I am telling you?\" said Prewitt. \"Sant was right. The reform was right. The people who took over were the best available and they were better than the ones they replaced. And the engine still went down, because knowing that a lie is a lie does not teach you how a machine works, and the forty years of managed examinations had already happened, and the people who could have fixed it in February had been sorted into the ninth stratum in the fifties and were forty-five years old and working in yards.\"\n\n\"So the crime killed them,\" said Corvain. \"Not the reform.\"\n\nPrewitt stopped.\n\n\"Say that again.\"\n\n\"The forty years of amendments are the reason the committee couldn't fix it.\" Corvain's voice was perfectly level. \"You have just told me that Vaskeld fell because it had spent forty years throwing away everybody who could run its engine. And your conclusion from that is that the amendments must continue.\"\n\n\"My conclusion,\" said Prewitt, \"is that you cannot stop in the middle.\"\n\nShe came back and sat down.\n\n\"That is the whole of it, Officer, and I want you to sit with it, because it took me about four years. Yes. The amendments are the disease. I have never once pretended otherwise, not to myself and not to the eleven other people in this city who know. And a city that has been doing this for a hundred and three years is a city where the only person alive who can reason about the Engine is a fourteen year old girl who was pulled out of Ferrow Lane eleven months ago, and there is precisely one of her, because we made sure there was precisely one of her.\n\n\"So if I publish tomorrow, this council falls in nine weeks, exactly as Vaskeld's did, and it falls with my full support, and I would go quietly and I would be glad. And then the Engine goes out of true, in some February, in eleven years or forty, and there will be one person in Gifford who might be able to reason about it, and she will be a woman in her fifties by then, and if she is dead or abroad or simply not clever enough on that particular day, then this city puts forty thousand people in the ground.\n\n\"That is the reason. It is not a good reason. It is a *true* one. And every single person who has ever been in this room has taken it, and I have never once thought worse of any of them.\"\n\nThe room was quiet for a long time.\n\n\"How long?\" said Corvain.\n\n\"I'm sorry?\"\n\n\"How long does it take to stop?\" Corvain leaned forward. \"That's the question and I don't think anybody has ever asked it in this room, because everybody who has ever sat in this chair has been offered a choice between *publish tomorrow* and *carry on*, and those aren't the only two things. You said you cannot stop in the middle. All right. How long is the *end*?\"\n\nPrewitt looked at her.\n\n\"Say what you mean.\"\n\n\"You need one a year because you cannot make one.\" Corvain was talking fast now. \"Fine. Then stop unmaking them. Not publish. Not reform. Stop the amendments, quietly, this year, forty-one of them, and promote the lot. That's forty-one instead of one. And do it again next year, and the year after.\"\n\n\"And in ten years there are four hundred Rustbound reckoners in the Engine houses who are all better than my grandchildren, and the claim dies, and the council falls, exactly as it did in Vaskeld, only slower.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Corvain. \"It falls in ten years instead of nine weeks, and when it falls there are four hundred people who can run the machine instead of one. That's the difference. That's the entire difference and it is the only thing that matters in the whole story.\" She put her hand flat on the ledger. \"Vaskeld didn't die of reform. It died of reforming a city that had already thrown its reckoners away. You've got eleven years, or forty, before your February. You cannot use them to keep the lie alive, because the lie is what will kill you. You can use them to build the four hundred.\"\n\nPrewitt sat absolutely still.\n\n\"You are proposing,\" she said slowly, \"that this council should spend the next ten years deliberately manufacturing the conditions of its own destruction.\"\n\n\"I'm proposing that it should spend ten years making itself unnecessary,\" said Corvain, \"which is the only honourable thing left available to it, and you know that, because you have known for twenty-two years that it is a lie and you have spent twenty-two years keeping it warm.\"\n\n\"And you imagine the Chief Engineer will agree to this.\"\n\n\"No, ma'am. I imagine the Chief Engineer will fight it to the last breath, because the Chief Engineer holds a quarterly delegation and four hundred and eleven writs and a claim on a bloodline.\" Corvain sat back. \"But you're the Chancellor. And you asked me here to offer me a chair, and I'd like to say yes, and I'd like to say yes to *that* chair.\"\n\n---\n\nPrewitt got up and went to the window and stood there for a very long time.\n\n\"You are twenty-one,\" she said, without turning round.\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"When I was twenty-one I sat in this room in the chair you are sitting in, and a man called Everard Kell showed me the Vaskeld figures, and I went out onto the terrace afterwards and was sick into a flowerbed, and by the following spring I had stopped thinking about it more than once a week.\" She put her fingertips on the glass. \"Osric Kell's father. Nobody has ever known that, including his son, and I have carried it for twenty-two years.\n\n\"And I have spent twenty-two years believing there were two options, and you have been in this room for eighty minutes and produced a third, and it is not a good third, it is slow and it is dangerous and it will very probably fail. And it is the first new thing anybody has said to me about this since I was twenty-one years old.\"\n\nShe turned round.\n\n\"I am going to say no,\" said Isolde Prewitt.\n\nCorvain's stomach dropped.\n\n\"For eleven days,\" Prewitt went on, \"because that is how long it will take me to find out which of the eleven other people who know will hold, and because if I say yes to you this afternoon in the heat of having been out-thought by a girl in a grey coat, I will unsay it by Friday and you will be in Sootgate counting lamp oil. So: no, today. Come back on the second of September and I will give you an answer I can keep.\"\n\n\"Ma'am—\"\n\n\"And in the meantime you are going to do something for me, and it is not a test, and I want you to understand that I am asking rather than instructing, because you have earned that.\" Prewitt came back to the desk. \"There is a letter on my desk from the first stratum scholar. Four lines. She has written to the Chief Engineer asking what the conditions of her scholarship are and on what terms she may decline, and she has sent copies to the Proctor of the Long Hall to be minuted and to an unnamed officer of the enforcement division, and she is fourteen years old, and it is the single most dangerous piece of paper in this building.\n\n\"You know her. You are the reason she is here. Go and talk to her, and find out what she wants, and tell her the truth, all of it, including the part about there being precisely one of her, because she is going to work it out inside a month and I would rather she heard it from somebody who is on her side.\"\n\nCorvain stood up.\n\n\"Ma'am,\" she said. \"May I ask one thing?\"\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"Ashcombe was in a room like this in the seventy-ninth year and he wouldn't tell me what he was told. He said I had to hear it cold.\" She hesitated. \"Was it you?\"\n\n\"No,\" said Prewitt. \"It was Everard Kell, and it was the same afternoon he did me, and Reeve Ashcombe was twenty-nine and he sat in that chair and said nothing at all for four minutes and then said *thank you for telling me* and went out.\" She sat down and drew a sheet toward her. \"And he has spent twenty-six years being a very good captain in a division that cannot touch it, and he has just spent the whole of that credit sending me you, and I want you to know that, because he will never tell you and it is the largest thing anybody has done in this business in my lifetime.\"\n\n---\n\nCorvain got as far as the door.\n\n\"Officer.\"\n\n\"Ma'am.\"\n\n\"The second of September.\" Prewitt did not look up. \"Between now and then, keep your evidence somewhere I cannot reach and do not tell me where. I say that as a friend and I mean it entirely, because there are eleven other people who know, and I do not yet know which of them will hold, and one of them is the Chief Engineer.\"\n\n---\n\nThe Engine went out of true at nine minutes past six on the evening of the thirty-first of August.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Nine.*" },
  { n: 10, title: "The Engine's Answer", text: "**I. Nine Minutes Past Six**\nThe thing nobody in Gifford ever understood about the thirty-first of August was that four separate plans were running that evening and not one of them was the plan that happened.\n\n**Silas Vane's plan** was nine hours in the last week of September, with the outfall gangs pulled out and eleven hundred men accounted for and a governor on the fourth gallery set a twelfth out on the feed side by a Sirrath who knew exactly what he was doing. That plan was three weeks away and it was good and it would probably have worked.\n\n**Marrow's plan** was to be in the gallery at the end of it.\n\n**Corvain's plan** was the second of September.\n\n**The Chief Engineer's plan** was the only one that moved early, and it moved because a fourteen year old had written four lines on a piece of paper and sent one copy to be minuted.\n\n---\n\nHis name was Halvard Crale and he had held the Office of the Chief Engineer for nineteen years, and he was not a wicked man and he was not clever either. He was the fourth Crale to hold it. He had come out of the university in the ordinary way, fifth stratum in his year, promoted on the strength of an uncle, and he had run the Engine houses for nineteen years exactly as they had been run for the previous eighty, which was by not touching anything and by making certain that the annual recalibration schedule slipped a little further every year, because a recalibration was the one occasion on which somebody might have to explain something.\n\nHe had received Rooke's letter on the twenty-fourth of August.\n\nHe had read it four times and had understood, correctly, that the girl had worked out that the first stratum was an appointment. He had understood, also correctly, that the letter had been minuted at the Long Hall and could not be recalled. And he had understood, entirely incorrectly, that the Chancellor's granting of an audience to an enforcement officer on the second of September and the arrival of that letter in the same fortnight were one thing rather than two.\n\nSo Halvard Crale did what frightened men in old offices have always done, which was to reach for the only instrument he was completely confident of.\n\nOn the twenty-ninth of August he ordered a full recalibration of the eastern bank.\n\nIt was, on paper, an unimpeachable act. The eastern bank was four years overdue. He minuted it as routine. And what he intended by it was to be able to stand in front of the Council on the third of September and say: *the amendment authority exists because the Engine requires unbroken competence and here is the competence, we have this month completed a full recalibration of the eastern bank on schedule.*\n\nHe gave the order to a committee of nine.\n\n---\n\nThe committee of nine began work at second bell on the thirty-first of August, on the fourth gallery, on a bank that had not been opened in four years and about a third of which was not on any plan.\n\nThey followed the procedure. The procedure was a hundred and three years old and it was excellent and it had been written by people who understood the machine, and it ran to four hundred and eleven pages, and every one of the nine had been examined on it.\n\nAt nine minutes past six one of them, a reckoner of thirty-one named Sella Vaunt who was the best of the nine and who would spend the rest of her life going over this, reached the end of section eleven of the procedure and found that section eleven referred forward to a bank of rods that was not there.\n\nNot missing. Not broken. Not there, in the sense that the procedure described eleven rods in a housing and the housing in front of her had fourteen.\n\nSella Vaunt did the right thing. She stopped, and she called the senior, and the senior looked at it for four minutes and could not account for it either, and then the senior did the wrong thing, which was to reason that three additional rods must have been fitted at some later date and were therefore not load-bearing in the original design, and to proceed with section twelve.\n\nSection twelve of the procedure required them to slacken the feed governor by a quarter turn.\n\nThey slackened it by a quarter turn.\n\nWith fourteen rods in the housing instead of eleven, a quarter turn on the feed governor was not a quarter turn on the feed. It was a twelfth of a turn out of true, on the feed side, on a bank that was not on the plans.\n\nThey had done it to themselves, following a hundred and three year old procedure, with the best of the nine standing there saying *wait*, in the exact manner that a man drowns four feet from a rope.\n\nNobody sabotaged the Engine of Gifford. That was the thing that Vane could never afterwards be told and would never have believed. The city had been holding a loaded pistol against its own head for sixty years and on the thirty-first of August it finally got round to pulling the trigger by the book.\n\n---\n\nThe knock stopped at eleven minutes past six.\n\nIt did not stop everywhere at once. That was what people remembered. It came out of the pipes in waves, district by district, and it took about ninety seconds to cross the Under, and everybody who was in the Under that evening felt it happen in their own wall before they knew what it was, because the knock is the thing you do not notice until it is gone.\n\nMarrow was at the bench.\n\nHe had a compressor governor in bits in front of him and he had been arguing with Bramwell about the ceiling joist on Cull Alley for eleven minutes, and the pipe behind the shop wall went *knock, knock, knock,* and then did not do it a fourth time.\n\nBoth of them looked up.\n\n\"That's not a vent,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey stood in the middle of the shop in the dark and listened to a hundred and three years of noise not happening.\n\nThen the gas went down. Not out; down, to about a third, all along Kettle Yard and then further, and outside somebody started shouting and then a lot of people started shouting, and Bramwell put his hand flat on the wall the way the foreman at number four compressor house had done in January.\n\n\"Aye,\" he said. \"Well.\"\n\nMarrow was already getting his coat.\n\n\"It's not September,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"I know it's not September.\"\n\n\"Then it's not him.\"\n\n\"I know it's not him.\" Marrow was at the door. \"That's what's wrong with it. If it were him I'd know which bank and which governor and how far, and I'd know the outfalls were clear, and I'd know it was nine hours.\" He looked back. \"It's not nine hours. Nobody's counting. Nobody's pulled anybody out of anything. It just went.\"\n\nBramwell stood in his dark shop and said the only thing there was to say.\n\n\"The water towers hold thirty hours,\" he said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then you've got thirty hours, son, and you've not got a writ, and there's four hundred and eleven men on that hill who'll have you in the works at Grieves if you go within a street of it.\"\n\nMarrow stopped in the doorway.\n\n\"There's a woman on Cullen Street who scrubbed those stairs for thirty years,\" he said. \"And there's a coat at the seam who's spent a year proving what they did to me. And there's a girl in that university who came first stratum out of six hundred and forty-one and is fourteen.\"\n\nHe pulled the door.\n\n\"I'm not going alone,\" he said. \"That's the whole thing I worked out in August. I'm not going alone.\"\n\n---\n\n**II. Three Doors, Opening**\n**Rooke** was in the second gallery of the library when the lights went down, and she was the only person in the building who understood what she was looking at within four seconds, and she understood it because she was the only person in the building who had read the output ledger.\n\nShe had not been given the output ledger. She had asked Thorncastle for it in the second week of August, in the same conversation, and Thorncastle had looked at her for a long moment and then said *God help us all* for the second time in eleven months and sent for it.\n\nEstablishment climbing. Output flat. Corrections falling to nought. Interval stretching to four years.\n\nRooke had read all four columns in about nine minutes and had said: \"They've stopped opening it.\"\n\nAnd Thorncastle had said: \"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then the first time they open it, it'll go.\"\n\nSo when the gas went down over the whole Rise on the thirty-first of August, Rooke did not think *what has happened*. She thought, with a cold and total clarity, *they have opened the eastern bank*, and she was out of her chair and running before the second lamp had dimmed.\n\nShe got to Thorncastle's rooms at eleven minutes past six and the old woman was already standing in the middle of the floor with her coat in her hand.\n\n\"Aster Row,\" said Thorncastle.\n\n\"They won't let me in.\"\n\n\"No. They will not let you in and they will not let me in, and I have held a chair in this university for thirty-one years.\" Thorncastle was pulling the coat on. \"Which is why we are not going to Aster Row. We are going to the Council building, and we are going to find the one person in Gifford who has spent a year assembling a document that says the Engine houses cannot do this, and we are going to make her take us up that hill.\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Her name is Elspeth Corvain,\" said Professor Thorncastle, \"and I owe her four pages and an apology.\"\n\n---\n\n**Corvain** was in the duty room at the seam when the knock stopped, and she was the only person in that building who did not spend the first four minutes asking what had happened, because Ashcombe took one look at the ceiling and said, \"Get your coat,\" and she got her coat.\n\n\"Sir, what—\"\n\n\"The Engine's out of true.\" He was moving fast for a man of fifty-five. \"Not stopped. Out of true. Listen to the gas.\"\n\n\"How can you tell?\"\n\n\"Because when an engine stops it stops, and that gas is running at a third and steady, which means the thing is turning and it is turning *wrong*, and I have been waiting twenty-six years for somebody to tell me it had happened.\" He was at the stairs. \"The second of September has just become tonight. Where's your evidence?\"\n\n\"In four places, sir, and one of them is a bindery on the Wick.\"\n\n\"Good girl.\"\n\nThey came out onto the step of the enforcement building at seventeen minutes past six into a city with the lights at a third, and there were two people standing at the bottom of the steps.\n\nOne was a woman of fifty-nine in an academic coat with her hair coming down.\n\nThe other was a girl of fourteen in a coat that nearly fitted her.\n\nCorvain stopped dead.\n\n\"You,\" she said.\n\n\"You,\" said Rooke.\n\nAnd then, because neither of them had anything prepared and there was no time and the whole city was going down around them, Rooke said the thing she had been carrying for eleven months and had rehearsed about four hundred times and had never once expected to get to say:\n\n\"You ruined us.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"You came down off the Rise and you were clever and you put me in a carriage and my foster mother has been nine days on a bed in a front room since last autumn and there's a nine year old on Ferrow Lane who's had two bottles of syrup in a year, and I have hated you for eleven months, properly, every day, like a job.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" said Corvain. \"I know. I've read your file about nine hundred times.\"\n\n\"And I was wrong,\" said Rooke.\n\nCorvain blinked.\n\n\"I worked out in July why they came and got me. It wasn't you being certain in an empty hall. It was that there's one chair a year and it was empty and I was the only one in Gifford who could fill it.\" Rooke's voice did not shake. \"You were the *instrument*. And I've spent eleven months hating the instrument, which is a stupid way to do arithmetic, and I don't have time to do it properly now so I'm doing it badly. I don't forgive you. I've stopped billing you.\"\n\n\"That'll do,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Right,\" said Ashcombe. \"Now, if the four of us have finished, will somebody tell me why a professor and a first-year are on my steps.\"\n\n\"Because they have opened the eastern bank,\" said Thorncastle, \"and the eastern bank is a third not on the plans, and this child has read the output ledger and can tell you what is about to happen to the water in about thirty hours, and neither of us can get onto Aster Row without an officer of the Council.\"\n\n\"And I can't get onto Aster Row without a reason,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"You have got eleven pages, four pages from me, an audit sheet and a hundred and three volumes,\" said Thorncastle, \"and an engine that has been out of true for nine minutes.\"\n\n\"That's not a reason, that's a report.\"\n\n\"Then take a *warrant*,\" said a voice from the bottom of the steps.\n\nThey all turned round.\n\nThere was a Sirrath standing at the kerb in the half-dark, six foot four, white pelt, one eye still not entirely open from August, breathing like a man who had come across the seam at a run.\n\n\"Take a warrant,\" said Marrow, \"for me.\"\n\n---\n\n**III. What Marrow Brought**\nHe gave it to them on the steps in four minutes because there was no time to do it properly, and it was the worst four minutes of his life and he did it standing up.\n\n\"There's an operation,\" he said. \"Last week of September. A governor on the fourth gallery, feed side, a twelfth out, on a bank that isn't on the plans. Nine hours. I gave him the method in June because he asked me a good question and I liked being asked.\"\n\nAshcombe's face did not change at all.\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Silas Vane.\"\n\n\"And you're telling me this on a step.\"\n\n\"I'm telling you this on a step because it's the thirty-first of August and it's happened three weeks early and it's *not him*.\" Marrow's voice cracked and he did not stop. \"That's the whole reason I'm here. If it were him I could tell you which bank and which governor and how far and I'd have it fixed in an hour. It isn't him. They've done it themselves. They've opened the eastern bank on a hundred and three year old procedure with fourteen rods in an eleven rod housing and they have done the exact thing I told him to do, by the book, in front of nine witnesses, and there is nobody up there who can find it.\"\n\n\"How do you know about the rods?\" said Rooke.\n\nMarrow looked at her properly for the first time.\n\n\"Because I've had my hands on the housing,\" he said. \"Bramwell cogged three rods out of an eastern bank spare in the seventies and they're hanging on a chain in his shop and I've measured them, and they're not on the plan sheet he's got a page of, and I asked him in January why there were fourteen and he said he'd never counted them.\"\n\n\"And you counted them.\"\n\n\"I count everything,\" said Marrow. \"It's all I've got.\"\n\nRooke stood in a dying street and looked up at a Sirrath she had never met.\n\n\"What's your name?\" she said.\n\n\"Marrow.\"\n\n\"Fourth stratum, amended ninth.\"\n\nHe went very still.\n\n\"How do you—\"\n\n\"Because there's forty-one of you and I've had your files since April, and you were the top of the forty-one, and I've read your paper.\" Rooke's chin came up. \"You got the load across the arch. Four hundred and eleven human candidates in the main hall didn't get the load across the arch and I've been in the fourth-year seminar for a year with nine of them.\"\n\n\"I got it wrong first.\"\n\n\"Everyone gets it wrong first. You marked your own false start and went back. That's the whole of it, that's the only thing that matters, that's the—\" She stopped. \"There isn't time. Captain, take the warrant.\"\n\n\"On what charge?\" said Ashcombe.\n\n\"On his own information,\" said Corvain. \"Sir, he's just confessed to conspiracy against the Engine in front of four witnesses on a public step. That's a warrant. That's a *good* warrant, it's the best warrant this division has had in eleven years, and it takes us onto Aster Row tonight with an officer, a suspect, a material witness and an expert, and there is not one man in that building who can refuse it.\"\n\n\"And when we get up there,\" said Ashcombe, \"you are walking a Sirrath with a confession into a room full of people who would very much like there to be a saboteur.\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\n\"They will hang him for this.\"\n\n\"I know,\" said Marrow.\n\nEverybody looked at him.\n\n\"I've done the sum,\" he said. \"I did it in August on the end of a bed with a bronze blank in my hands and I've done it about nine hundred times since. If I stay in Kettle Yard tonight nobody ever knows and I keep my hands and in three weeks I put a governor out for a man I'm frightened of, and twenty people drown in the sumps, and I fix it afterwards in front of witnesses and that's the thing I've wanted since I was fifteen.\" He wiped his mouth. \"And if I get in that carriage I get the same gallery and the same governor and the same twelfth of a turn, tonight, for nothing, for a city that gave me a nine, and then they hang me for a conspiracy that never happened.\n\n\"And it's the same gallery. That's what I worked out. It is the exact same gallery either way. The only difference is who's in the water.\"\n\n---\n\n**IV. The Fourth Gallery**\nThey went up on the works lift at Aster Row at nine minutes to eight and it took eleven minutes and nobody spoke.\n\nCorvain had the warrant. Ashcombe had his rank and twenty-six years and a face that had stopped four arguments at the gate. Thorncastle had a chair in the university and an expression that had stopped the fifth. Rooke had nothing at all and came in behind Thorncastle's elbow, and nobody looked at her twice, because she was fourteen and small and there were four hundred and eleven men on that hill with something considerably more urgent to think about.\n\nMarrow came in under warrant, between two constables, with his hands loose in front of him.\n\nThat was the arrangement Ashcombe had made at the gate in about forty seconds, and it was the single cleverest thing Corvain ever saw him do, and it worked entirely on one principle: a man in custody is allowed onto a site where an expert is not.\n\n---\n\nThe fourth gallery was two hundred feet long and it hung in the dark over a drop nobody had ever measured, and the whole of the eastern bank stood behind it: eight thousand square feet of brass and blackened iron, rod banks stacked to a vanishing point overhead, and the great cogs turning somewhere below at the speed of an hour hand, and the sound of it in the chest rather than in the ears.\n\nIt was turning. That was the thing that emptied Rooke's lungs when she came out onto the walkway. It had not stopped. It was running, enormous and patient, and it was running *wrong*, and you could feel that too, a kind of stagger in the floor about once every four seconds, like a man walking with one leg shorter than the other.\n\nThere were about sixty people on the gallery.\n\nThe Chief Engineer, Halvard Crale, was at the control stand with eleven of them round him. The committee of nine were in a knot further along, and one of them, a woman of thirty-one, was standing slightly apart with her arms wrapped round herself, looking at a housing.\n\nAnd there were witnesses, which was the part nobody had planned and which changed everything: four Council members, two Council clerks with notebooks, and a reporter from the *Gifford Intelligencer* who had been at Aster Row that afternoon for a piece about the endowment and had not been asked to leave because in a hundred and three years nobody had ever needed a procedure for asking a reporter to leave the fourth gallery.\n\n\"Two hours,\" said Ashcombe quietly. \"They've had two hours.\"\n\n\"They've had a hundred and three years,\" said Thorncastle.\n\n---\n\nWhat happened in the next forty minutes was the single most humiliating episode in the history of the Wrought and it was witnessed and minuted and printed, and none of it was cruel and all of it was worse for that.\n\nCrale had them on the dials. That was where he had put them at half past six and it was where they still were at twenty past eight, because the dials were in the book and the book was what he had. Nine reckoners walking the dial banks in pairs, reading and calling and writing, four hundred and eleven pages of procedure open on the stand.\n\n\"He's checking the load side,\" said Marrow, from between his constables.\n\n\"Say it louder,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"He's checking the *load side*.\" Marrow's voice went down the gallery. \"It's not on the load side. If it were on the load side the dials would say so and they've read them twice. It's on the feed.\"\n\nSixty people turned round.\n\nHalvard Crale looked along the gallery at a Sirrath in the custody of two constables and said, \"Who let that in here?\"\n\n\"Officer Corvain, enforcement division, on warrant.\" Corvain had her card out. \"That's a material witness in a live conspiracy and he stays where he is.\"\n\n\"Take him out.\"\n\n\"No, sir.\"\n\n\"I am the Chief Engineer of this—\"\n\n\"And I'm an officer of the Council executing a warrant on Council premises,\" said Corvain, \"and you can have this argument with me at length tomorrow morning and I'll lose it, and tonight he stays where he is.\"\n\n\"The feed side,\" said Sella Vaunt.\n\nEverybody turned again.\n\nShe was the woman of thirty-one who had been standing apart. She had a procedure book open in both hands and her face had gone the colour of paper.\n\n\"Section eleven,\" she said. \"Sir. I said at nine minutes past six that section eleven referred to eleven rods and there were fourteen in the housing, and I said we should stop, and it's in the log, and then we did section twelve, which is a quarter turn on the *feed governor*.\" She was shaking. \"If the housing is wrong then the quarter turn wasn't a quarter turn.\"\n\nThe gallery was completely silent except for the machine.\n\n\"Fourteen rods,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Housing nine or housing eleven?\"\n\n\"Nine.\"\n\n\"Then you're a twelfth out,\" said Marrow, \"not a quarter, because three extra rods in housing nine is a three to one on the linkage and a quarter of a turn through a three to one on the feed side is a twelfth on the bank. And it'll be reading true on every dial in this house, because the dials are downstream of housing nine and the whole bank went out together, so everything's agreeing with everything and all of it's wrong.\"\n\nSella Vaunt put the book down on the rail and put both hands over her face.\n\n\"Two hours,\" she said. \"I've been saying housing nine for two hours.\"\n\n\"You said the housing,\" said Marrow. \"You didn't say the linkage. Nobody'd say the linkage unless they'd had one in bits. It's not in the book.\"\n\n\"Have you had one in bits?\"\n\n\"I've had three rods off one on a chain in a shop in Kettle Yard for seven months,\" said Marrow. \"And a pressure governor off a dray about nine hundred times.\"\n\n---\n\nHalvard Crale did the thing that ended him, and he did it out of nineteen years of habit, and it took four seconds.\n\n\"This is a Sirrath in custody on a sabotage warrant,\" he said, \"who has just demonstrated a detailed knowledge of the eastern bank linkage, and I want him removed and I want the gallery cleared of enforcement personnel, and I will not have the Engine of Gifford diagnosed by a *criminal* in front of the press.\"\n\nAnd the reporter from the *Gifford Intelligencer* wrote that down.\n\nAshcombe stepped forward.\n\n\"Chief Engineer,\" he said. \"How long have you got?\"\n\n\"I beg your pardon?\"\n\n\"The water towers hold thirty hours. It is now twenty-six minutes past eight and the Engine has been out of true since nine minutes past six, which is two hours and seventeen minutes, so you have twenty-seven hours and forty-three minutes.\" Ashcombe's voice was completely level and it carried the length of the gallery. \"You have used two hours and seventeen minutes checking the dials. There are nine of you and four hundred and eleven pages, and the woman who found it at nine minutes past six has been telling you for two hours and seventeen minutes and you have not heard her.\n\n\"So I am going to ask you a question in front of four Council members, two clerks and a newspaper, and I would like you to answer it, because it is going to be printed either way.\n\n\"Can you fix it?\"\n\nHalvard Crale stood at the control stand of the Engine of Gifford with eleven of his own people around him.\n\n\"We are proceeding in accordance with the procedure,\" he said.\n\n\"That isn't the question.\"\n\n\"The procedure is—\"\n\n\"Chief Engineer,\" said one of the Council members, quietly, from the rail. \"Answer the officer.\"\n\nAnd Halvard Crale, fourth of his name to hold the office, fifth stratum in his year, promoted on the strength of an uncle, said:\n\n\"Not tonight.\"\n\n---\n\n**V. The Engine's Answer**\nIt took the four of them eleven minutes to agree the fix and forty minutes to do it, and it should be recorded that all four of them were necessary and that afterwards none of them could ever separate out whose part had been whose.\n\n**Marrow** had the linkage. He got the housing open and he was inside it to the shoulder within four minutes and he found the three extra rods by hand in the dark, and he could say what they were: not additions, originals, cast in the founding decade with the same marks, put in by somebody who had wanted a three to one where the plan said one to one.\n\n**Rooke** had the number. That was the part nobody else in the building could have done and there were sixty witnesses to her doing it. Because a twelfth out was what Marrow could say from the linkage, and a twelfth out was not enough; the bank had been running out of true for two hours and twenty minutes and a bank running out of true does not simply sit there being wrong, it *drifts*, and the correction required was not a twelfth of a turn back, it was a twelfth of a turn back plus the drift, and the drift was a function of how long it had been running and how hard.\n\nRooke sat down on the walkway with her back against the rail and Thorncastle's notebook on her knees and did it in nine minutes with sixty people watching her, and got an answer, and then said, out loud, in the silence:\n\n\"That's wrong.\"\n\n\"What?\" said Crale.\n\n\"That's wrong, I've assumed the drift's symmetrical and it isn't, it's a feed-side fault so the drift only runs one way.\" She was already crossing it out. \"Give me four minutes.\"\n\nNobody in that gallery ever forgot it. Not the answer. The crossing out. A fourteen year old girl in a mended coat sitting on the floor in front of the Chief Engineer and four Council members and a newspaper, saying *that's wrong* about her own work, out loud, with the whole city going dark, and then doing it again.\n\nIt took her six minutes, not four.\n\n\"A twelfth back, and then eleven sixty-fourths of a turn beyond it,\" she said. \"And then it'll hunt for about ninety seconds and you have to leave it alone while it hunts, and it'll settle short, and then you give it a sixty-fourth.\"\n\n\"How do you know it'll settle short?\"\n\n\"I don't,\" said Rooke. \"It should settle long. Everything I know says it should settle long.\" She looked up. \"But in the year before he was born the eastern bank went out of true and it came back on its own in eleven moves, and it stopped at the right one, and eleven moves is the number you get if it's settling short every time and correcting. So either there's something in this machine that hunts, or a man in Kettle Yard misremembered a bad shift thirty-one years ago.\"\n\nThorncastle, standing at the rail, said, \"Which do you think?\"\n\n\"I think we find out in ninety seconds,\" said Rooke.\n\n**Corvain** had the authority, and it was the only reason any of it was permitted to happen. She stood between Marrow and Halvard Crale for forty minutes with a warrant in her hand and made the space, and she did it by knowing, precisely, the four hundred and eleven lines of a Council audit sheet and which delegated authorities Crale did and did not hold on a gallery in an emergency, and she was correct four times running in front of Council members who could check.\n\n**Thorncastle** did the last thing, which was the smallest and possibly the largest.\n\nWhen Crale said, for the fourth time, that he would not have the Engine adjusted on the arithmetic of a first-year scholar, Linnea Thorncastle walked to the control stand, took the notebook, read Rooke's nine minutes and her six minutes, and said:\n\n\"I have held the chair of reckoning in this university for thirty-one years and I have examined every reckoner in this building including you, Halvard, and I got you through it. The arithmetic is sound. Turn the governor.\"\n\n---\n\nThey turned it a twelfth back and eleven sixty-fourths beyond, and Marrow did it, with his hand on the wheel and Sella Vaunt's hand next to his on the lock, because it needed two.\n\nAnd then the eastern bank of the Engine of Gifford began to hunt.\n\nIt went eleven positions.\n\nIt went them one at a time, slowly, with about four seconds between each, and the whole gallery felt every one of them come up through the floor, and nobody on that walkway moved or spoke for the entire ninety seconds. Sixty people stood in the dark over a drop nobody had measured and listened to a machine built by twelve dead people count.\n\nIt settled short.\n\n\"Sixty-fourth,\" said Rooke.\n\nMarrow gave it a sixty-fourth.\n\nThe stagger came out of the floor.\n\nIt did not happen dramatically. That was what everyone said afterwards, all sixty of them, in about sixty different ways: it was not dramatic at all. The floor had been walking with one leg shorter than the other since nine minutes past six, and at twelve minutes past nine it stopped, and the whole enormous patient sound of the thing came back into true, and about four seconds later the gaslights on the gallery came up from a third to full.\n\nSomewhere a very long way below and outside, across the entire Under, in eleven thousand houses, a pipe went *knock*.\n\n---\n\nRooke was still sitting on the walkway with her back against the rail.\n\nMarrow came and sat down next to her because his legs had stopped being reliable, and the two of them sat on the floor of the fourth gallery with their backs to the rail while sixty people had four separate arguments over their heads.\n\n\"It hunted,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Eleven moves.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So it's not a governor resetting.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Rooke. \"A governor doesn't hunt like that. A governor goes to a stop. That went to the *answer*, and it went there by trying and correcting, eleven times, and stopping short each time, and I have been sitting here for four minutes trying to work out what in the world has a settling behaviour like that.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"There's one thing I know of,\" said Rooke.\n\n\"Go on.\"\n\n\"A person,\" she said. \"That's how a person does a sum they can't do in their head. You try it, you see you're short, you go again. Eleven times.\" She turned her head and looked at him. \"That machine did not correct itself. It *worked it out*. And it took ninety seconds, and it took eleven attempts, and it stopped short every single time, which means it is not good at this. Which means it is doing it the hard way. Which means—\"\n\nShe stopped.\n\n\"Say it,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Which means it's been doing it the hard way for a hundred and three years,\" said Rooke, \"every time one of them set something slightly wrong, quietly, in the dark, eleven moves at a time, and nobody ever knew, and the reason four hundred and eleven men can run a machine they don't understand is not that the procedure is good.\"\n\nThe gallery was very loud above them and neither of them heard any of it.\n\n\"It's been covering for them,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"For sixty years.\" Rooke's voice had gone almost to nothing. \"And in February, in Vaskeld, when their engine went out of true and the committee couldn't fix it and it took eleven days—\"\n\n\"It wasn't refusing.\"\n\n\"It was *trying*,\" said Rooke. \"It was trying for eleven days and it couldn't get there, because whatever's in it that hunts isn't clever, it's just patient, and eleven days of it wasn't enough, and forty-one thousand six hundred people died while a machine sat in the dark under a city doing a sum it wasn't good enough to do.\"\n\nThey sat on the floor of the fourth gallery.\n\n\"It waited two hours tonight,\" said Marrow.\n\n\"Two hours and twenty-three minutes.\"\n\n\"With sixty of them stood up here reading dials.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And it didn't start hunting till we turned the governor.\" He put his head back against the rail. \"It was waiting for somebody to *start it off*. It got the last sixty-fourth wrong and we had to give it that too.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then it's not covering for them,\" said Marrow. \"It can't do it on its own. It never could. It needs somebody to go first.\"\n\nAnd Rooke, fourteen years old, sitting on a steel walkway two hundred feet above a drop nobody had measured, with her back against a brass rail and a borrowed notebook on her knees, said the sentence that the next thirty years of her life would be about.\n\n\"Then there's nothing in the blood at all,\" she said. \"There never was. There's a machine down there that can nearly do it, and once a year for a hundred and three years this city has gone out and found one person who can do the last sixty-fourth.\"\n\n---\n\n**VI. Ten Past Ten**\nAt ten past ten Silas Vane was standing in the yard behind the Ashlight with a glass in his hand looking up at a city where the lights had just come back on.\n\nRatchet came out to him.\n\n\"It's on the street already,\" said Ratchet. \"There was a paper man on the gallery. Crale said *not tonight* in front of four Council and it's going in the morning edition.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"And they're saying it was a Sirrath. In custody. On a warrant.\" Ratchet was not looking at him. \"And a first stratum, and a professor, and a coat.\"\n\nVane did not say anything for a while.\n\nThen he laughed, once, quietly, and poured the rest of his glass out onto the cobbles.\n\n\"Nineteen years,\" he said. \"Nineteen years of pulling the cleverest boy in the Under through that yard, and I got him, and I had him, and I built the whole thing so that the only door out was through me.\"\n\n\"He went to a coat.\"\n\n\"He did not go to a coat,\" said Vane. \"That is what everybody in this yard will say by Thursday and it is wrong, and I would like one person in this city to be accurate about it, so it may as well be you.\" He set the glass on the wall. \"He found three other people who were as capable as he was, and he went with them, and I have spent nineteen years making very sure that never happened, and I did not fail because I was careless.\n\n\"I failed because a Laachmi with a low ceiling sold three answers in the same week in April and then changed its mind.\"\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Ten.*" },
  { n: 11, title: "Reversion to the Mean", text: "**I. The Morning Edition**\nThe *Gifford Intelligencer* of the first of September ran it on the second page, under the shipping, in eleven column inches, with the headline:\n\n**ENGINE FAULT RECTIFIED. CHIEF ENGINEER ANSWERS QUESTIONS.**\n\nThat was the whole of it. Eleven inches. The Engine had gone out of true for three hours and three minutes, and the Chief Engineer had said *not tonight* in front of four Council members and a reporter, and the paper put it on page two under the shipping, and the phrase *not tonight* appeared in the ninth paragraph without comment.\n\nCorvain read it at half past six in the morning at a coffee stall on the seam and understood four things in about ninety seconds.\n\nThe first was that nobody had suppressed it. There had been no order, no visit, no quiet word. The reporter had filed what he saw and an editor of thirty years' standing had looked at eleven inches about a machine and put it under the shipping, because a fault that had been rectified in three hours was not news, and because in a hundred and three years the Engine had never once been news, and because there was no shape in that man's head for the story he was holding.\n\nThe second was that this was the whole mechanism. Not conspiracy. *Category*. The thing had been safe for a century not because it was hidden but because nobody had a place to put it.\n\nThe third was that by Thursday it would be gone entirely.\n\nAnd the fourth was that page two, under the shipping, in eleven inches, was the single most useful document that had ever existed on this subject, because it was *printed*, and it was *dated*, and in the ninth paragraph the Chief Engineer of Gifford was on the record saying that he could not fix the Engine.\n\nCorvain bought nine copies.\n\n---\n\n**II. The Second of September**\n\"Sit down, Officer,\" said Isolde Prewitt. \"Would you like tea. I have discovered that people always say no.\"\n\n\"Yes, ma'am. Please.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Prewitt poured. She had not slept; it did not show in her face and it showed entirely in the room, because there were papers on the desk, four separate stacks of them, in a room that had contained nothing at all eleven days ago. \"You will have worked out that I have had a difficult week.\"\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"Eleven other people knew. I said that to you on the twenty-second of August and I said I did not yet know which of them would hold.\" Prewitt sat down. \"Six of them will. Three of them will not and are already moving. One of them died in 'ninety-four and I have been counting him for four years out of habit, which tells you something about how often this is discussed.\n\n\"And one of them was Halvard Crale, and on the thirty-first of August Halvard Crale ordered a full recalibration of the eastern bank because a fourteen year old wrote him four lines asking what the conditions of her scholarship were.\" Prewitt put the pot down. \"So we may take it that the eleven days did not go as I intended.\"\n\n\"No, ma'am.\"\n\n\"Say the rest of it. You have been in the building four minutes and you have something in your coat and you are being polite about it.\"\n\nCorvain took out the newspaper and laid it on the desk, folded to page two.\n\nPrewitt read the ninth paragraph.\n\nShe read it twice, and then she sat back in her chair, and for about four seconds Corvain watched the Chancellor of Gifford look genuinely, undefendedly old.\n\n\"Under the shipping,\" said Prewitt.\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"A hundred and three years.\" She put her fingertips on it. \"And it goes on page two under the shipping, and by Thursday nobody will remember it, and you have brought me nine copies.\"\n\n\"Four copies. Five are elsewhere.\"\n\n\"Of course they are.\" Prewitt almost smiled. \"So. You are here to tell me that the thing I said could not be published has been published, in the Council's own approved organ, without anybody noticing, and that the Chief Engineer of Gifford is on the printed record admitting the whole of it in the ninth paragraph, and that this is now a fact about the world rather than an allegation in a young officer's file.\"\n\n\"Yes, ma'am.\"\n\n\"And what do you want?\"\n\n\"The same thing I wanted on the twenty-second of August,\" said Corvain. \"Ten years. Stop the amendments and build the four hundred.\"\n\n---\n\nPrewitt got up and went to the window and stood there for a long time, and the city below her had its lights on and its trams running and its pipes knocking, and eleven thousand people had gone to work that morning without knowing that anything had nearly happened at all.\n\n\"I am going to tell you what I can do,\" she said, \"and it is going to be very much less than you have asked for, and I want you to hear the whole of it before you decide what you think of me, because I have had eleven days and this is genuinely the most I can carry.\"\n\n\"Ma'am.\"\n\n\"I cannot stop the amendments.\" Prewitt turned round. \"I want to be exact. I cannot stop them because the amendment authority is not mine. It has sat with the Office of the Chief Engineer since the fifty-first year and it is renewed quarterly and it is lawful, and a Chancellor who attempts to take a lawful delegated authority away from a Chief Engineer without cause is a Chancellor who is testing whether the Council will follow her, and I have counted, and it will not. Six of eleven is not a majority of anything.\n\n\"What I can do is three things.\n\n\"Halvard Crale resigns on the eleventh. Not for the amendments; for the eastern bank, on the record, on his own words in a newspaper, and I have his letter in that drawer and it cost me two of the six.\" She held up a finger. \"Understand what that is worth. It is not justice and it does not touch the system. It is that for the first time in a hundred and three years the Office of the Chief Engineer will be filled by somebody who was put there by a Chancellor rather than by an uncle.\n\n\"Second. The forty-one amendments of this year are reversed.\"\n\nCorvain's hands went still on her knees.\n\n\"All of them?\"\n\n\"All forty-one, effective the first of October, retrospective to the summer. They will be placed in the strata their assessed results earned, which for eleven of them means promotion to the Rise and for the rest means writs and placements they were entitled to in July.\" Prewitt sat down. \"That is not generosity, Officer. That is the price of Crale's letter, and it is defensible in Council as an administrative correction arising from an operational failure, which is a sentence I spent nine hours constructing.\n\n\"And it is forty-one out of fourteen thousand two hundred and six, and I would like you to say that number out loud before you thank me for anything.\"\n\n\"Forty-one out of fourteen thousand two hundred and six,\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And the third thing?\"\n\n\"The third thing is the only one that matters and it is the one that will look like nothing,\" said Prewitt. \"From this year, the annual returns will publish assessed results alongside final strata. Both columns. In the volume, on the boards, on Tallow Street, every summer, where anybody can read them.\"\n\nCorvain sat back slowly.\n\n\"That's it,\" she said.\n\n\"That is it.\"\n\n\"That's the whole thing. That's everything.\" Corvain was not entirely in control of her voice. \"Ma'am, if both columns are on the boards then the amendments are *visible*. Not in a sealed volume, not in a records office. On the wall outside the hall, in front of six hundred children and their mothers, every summer, for ever. Anybody can count them. A grocer could count them.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"You've just ended it.\"\n\n\"No,\" said Isolde Prewitt. \"I have made it *countable*, which is a different thing and a much slower one, and I want you to be extremely clear about the difference, because you are twenty-one and you will otherwise spend the next four years disappointed.\n\n\"The amendments will continue. Crale's successor will make them, and the successor after that, and they will go on the boards in both columns, and for the first three or four years absolutely nothing will happen, because nobody reads the boards. Then somebody will. A clerk, or a grocer, or a mother on Ferrow Lane with a child in the ninth, and it will take another few years for enough of them to be angry at the same time, and then this council will fall, and it will deserve to.\n\n\"That is what I have bought. Not an end. Ten or fifteen years of daylight, and a public number, and a Chief Engineer I chose. And in those ten or fifteen years there will be forty-one promoted this October and forty-one next year if I can hold the argument, and the year after, and if it goes on long enough there will be four hundred of them, and when this council falls there will be somebody to run the machine.\"\n\nShe folded her hands.\n\n\"You proposed on the twenty-second of August that this council should spend ten years making itself unnecessary. I said I would give you an answer I could keep. That is the answer. It is a worse plan than yours and it is the best one I can actually deliver, and it will outlive me, and I will not see the end of it.\"\n\n---\n\n\"And the chair?\" said Corvain.\n\n\"Ah.\" Prewitt drew a sheet from the second stack. \"Yes. I am obliged to offer you that, and I am going to, and I would like you to refuse it.\"\n\n\"Ma'am?\"\n\n\"You are twenty-one years old and you have just spent a year doing the only piece of original work anybody in this city has done on this subject in forty-five years, and you did it because you were outside.\" Prewitt pushed the sheet across. \"That is a secondment to my office. It is a considerable promotion, it would delight your mother, and it would put you in this room for the next thirty years, and inside eighteen months you would be the most careful custodian of the whole arrangement in Gifford, because you would have seen the Vaskeld figures and you would have signed your name to something. That is not a slur. It is what happened to me and it is what happened to Reeve Ashcombe and it is what happened to eleven other people, and it works every single time.\"\n\nCorvain looked at the paper.\n\n\"Then why offer it?\"\n\n\"Because if I do not offer it, somebody else will, and it will be one of the three who are already moving, and they will not warn you first.\" Prewitt met her eye. \"Take it or refuse it. But decide it here, in front of me, having been told exactly what it does.\"\n\nCorvain sat in the empty room for a long moment.\n\n\"I'll take it,\" she said.\n\nPrewitt's eyebrows went up about a millimetre.\n\n\"On one condition, ma'am, and it isn't negotiable, and if you say no I'll go back to the division and I'll be a nuisance for thirty years instead.\"\n\n\"Name it.\"\n\n\"You publish both columns this summer, in September, before the Council can debate it, and you do it by administrative notice.\" Corvain leaned forward. \"Because the moment I sit in this room I become somebody who can be reviewed, and moved, and given a seat on a committee, and I know exactly what happens to me in eighteen months, and so do you. So the boards go up first. Then I'll come and be careful for you.\n\n\"That's what Ashcombe would have wanted twenty-six years ago and there was nobody to make the trade with. I'd like to make it.\"\n\nIsolde Prewitt looked at her for a long time.\n\nThen she reached for a pen, and wrote the administrative notice out in her own hand, four lines, dated the second of September, and sanded it and put it in the tray.\n\n\"It goes out on Thursday,\" she said.\n\n---\n\n**III. Aldous**\nThe journals turned up in October, and they turned up because Ketu could write a list.\n\nShe had been at it since August. Eleven pages, in a hand she had taught herself at fifty, of what a woman on her knees on a landing for thirty years knows about the houses on Aster Row: which families had sold what, which had a room nobody went into, which had a great-grandmother who talked, and where things went when a house was cleared.\n\nItem nine on the fourth page was this:\n\n*Fenwick house, Aster Row. Cleared 1881 on death of old Lady F. Papers went to the bindery on the Wick with the books because the family took the silver and did not want the paper. The bindery kept what would not sell. I know this because I carried four boxes of it down the area steps myself and the bindery man gave me a shilling.*\n\n*Old Lady F was a Wrought before she married. Her grandmother's name was Wrought. I do not know if this matters.*\n\nRooke read that on a Tuesday, out loud, in Bramwell's shop, to four people and a stove.\n\nThe bindery on the Wick was the one Whistle had bought a hundred and three published returns from, one at a time, over nineteen years, because nobody else wanted them.\n\nIt took Ivy Thorncastle nine days in the back room of that bindery, and she found them in a tea chest under a run of parish registers, and she came out into Kettle Yard with her hair full of dust and four leather books in her arms, and she was fifteen years old and she was crying, and what she said was:\n\n\"I told you I was going to be a very good historian.\"\n\n---\n\nThey were not what anybody had hoped for.\n\nThat was the first thing and it took Rooke about a week to stop resenting it. There was no revelation in them and no confession. Aldous Wrought had been sixty-one when he wrote the last of it and he had been a working reckoner all his life, and four fifths of the four volumes were working: page after page of it, in a small furious hand, and about a third of it was wrong in ways that had been sorted out fifty years later, and about a sixth of it was ahead of anything the university had taught anybody in 1897.\n\nBut there were eleven pages at the back of the fourth volume, dated the winter before he died, and they were not working. They were a memorandum, addressed to nobody, and the handwriting had got worse.\n\n*On the matter of the qualification, which they have begun to speak of as though it were settled.*\n\n*It is not settled and it is not true and I have said so at four meetings and I am tired of saying it.*\n\n*There is nothing in the blood. I would know. Of the Twelve, four were the children of gentlemen and eight were not, and the best of us was Halloran's mother, who could not read until she was nineteen. There is no faculty. There is a habit of mind, which is that one is willing to be wrong out loud, and it can be taught to almost anybody who is fed and left alone with paper, and it is destroyed by hunger, humiliation and being told at fourteen what one is worth.*\n\n*They will not accept this because it is not useful to them. They wish there to be a faculty, because a faculty may be inherited and a habit may not, and a man who has inherited a faculty need not go on demonstrating it.*\n\n*The Calibration was my proposal and I record here that it was a bad one.*\n\n*I proposed it in the eleventh year for one purpose, which was to find, out of the whole city, the children who had the habit, so that we might feed them and leave them alone with paper. It was to be temporary. It was to run until we had built enough schools that we could stop looking for the habit and start making it, which I estimated at forty years.*\n\n*It has been running nineteen and there are no schools and there is now a schedule of eleven strata, which I did not propose and which was added by the Council in my absence, and I am told it is administratively necessary.*\n\n*I understand what it will become. It will become a machine for finding the habit in other people's children and then, because two of them is inconvenient, for putting all but one of them back.*\n\n*I am sixty-one and I have a bad chest and I will not see it. I record it so that when it is happening somebody may find this and know that it was foreseen, and that it was foreseen by me, and that it is therefore mine.*\n\n*The Engine will manage. It is better than we are at almost everything and it is patient, and it will cover a great deal of incompetence for a very long time, and that is the worst thing about it, because it means nobody will find out until it is far too late to teach anybody anything.*\n\n*It cannot do the last part. Whoever reads this: it cannot do the last part. It will get within a hair and then it will stop and wait, and somebody must go first. There must always be somebody who can go first, and there must never be only one of them.*\n\n*A. Wrought*\n\n---\n\n**IV. What Each of Them Got**\n**Marrow** was charged.\n\nThat was the thing nobody outside the four of them ever quite understood, and it was the correct outcome, and Ashcombe had told him it would be on the steps of the enforcement building on the thirty-first of August and had not softened it once.\n\nHe had confessed to conspiracy in front of four witnesses. Vane's operation was real, the method was his, and there was a man in Kettle Yard with three rods on a chain who could confirm every part of it. Marcus Vale prosecuted, and did it thoroughly, and did not once use the word Sirrath.\n\nHe was not hanged. He was not hanged because Marcus Vale, on the ninth day, in front of Magistrate Colworth, put into evidence the fact that the accused had come forward voluntarily on the night of the fault, in the middle of the emergency, in the certain knowledge of what it would cost him, and had then diagnosed the eastern bank linkage on the fourth gallery in front of sixty witnesses including four Council members.\n\n\"I am obliged to prosecute him,\" Vale told the court, \"and I am obliged to tell you what he did, and I am not going to pretend that both of those things sit comfortably. They do not. I have been doing this for thirty years and it is the first time.\"\n\nEleven months in the works at Grieves. He served nine.\n\nAnd on the day he came out there was a letter waiting at Bramwell's shop from the Office of the Chief Engineer, signed by Crale's successor, offering him a probationary place on the eastern bank maintenance establishment, subject to writ, subject to conduct.\n\nMarrow read it twice and put it on the bench and did not answer it for four days.\n\n\"Take it,\" said Bramwell.\n\n\"It's the same building.\"\n\n\"Aye.\"\n\n\"It's the same four hundred and eleven men.\"\n\n\"Four hundred and fifty-two, now,\" said Bramwell. \"They took the forty-one in October, and eleven of them went up the Rise and the rest went in with writs, and there's a Laachmi girl out of the chapel behind the hall in your year who's on the *third gallery*.\" He coughed for a while. \"So it's not the same building. It's the same building with fifty-two of us in it, and in nine years it'll be a hundred, and there'll be a day when somebody opens a machine in this city and finds a Sirrath name on a repair that held.\"\n\n\"You said that in January.\"\n\n\"I say it about once a year, it's one of about four things I've got left.\" Bramwell handed him the pen. \"And there's the other reason and I'll say it once. I'm seventy-two and I've a chest like a wet sack and the writ dies with me. It does not descend to a Sirrath with an amendment. That's still the law and it'll be the law when I'm in the ground.\"\n\nHe tapped the letter.\n\n\"But a writ *issued* to a Sirrath on the eastern bank establishment is a writ, and a man with a writ can hold a shop, and I'd like there to be a shop in Kettle Yard in ninety years, and I'd like the note in the housing at number four compressor house to be signed by the man who owns it.\"\n\n**Rooke** did not go to Aster Row in her fourth year, and she did not decline the appointment either.\n\nWhat she did was write a second letter, in November, four lines again, and this one had nine drafts as well.\n\n*To the Office of the Chief Engineer.*\n\n*I understand that the conditions of my scholarship require the fourth-year attachment and the writ. I do not decline them.*\n\n*I decline to be the only one. In the year of my Calibration forty-one candidates were amended down and eleven of them are now on the Rise, and there is no reason whatever that the first stratum should be a single appointment, and there never was.*\n\n*I will take the attachment in the same year as ten others, or I will take it in nine years' time when there are ten others, and I am fifteen and I can wait longer than the Office can.*\n\n*R.*\n\nShe sent one copy to the Chief Engineer, one to the Proctor to be minuted, and one to a secondee in the Chancellor's office.\n\nIt took four years.\n\nShe took the attachment at eighteen, in a cohort of nine, and one of the nine was a Laachmi and two were Sirrath and four were out of the Under, and Rooke was not the best of them, which she said out loud, twice, in front of witnesses, and meant.\n\nShe sent money down the hill every month of those four years and it was never enough and it was never nothing. Mother Halloway died in the February of Rooke's third year, at seventy-four, upstairs in her own bed in the back room, which had taken eleven months of arguing with a landlord and four marks a week that Rooke did not really have. Nettle's cough went in the spring of the second year and did not come back. Tessa Brindle kept Marta's ledgers for six years and then bought the shop, slowly, in pieces, on a chalk, and never learned to hold a column in her head and never once needed to.\n\nPip came out of the doorway eventually. It took about two years.\n\n**Corvain** sat in the room.\n\nShe was thirty-two before she understood that Prewitt had been right about the eighteen months, and thirty-four before she understood that Prewitt had also been right that this did not make the trade a bad one, and thirty-nine when Isolde Prewitt died and Elspeth Corvain became the youngest Chancellor of Gifford in sixty years and the first who had ever entered that room as an accusation.\n\nThe boards went up in September, both columns, by administrative notice, and nobody noticed for four years.\n\nIn the fifth year a grocer on Ferrow Lane called Tessa Brindle, who could not hold a column in her head and had spent six years doing somebody else's ledgers with a finger and a slate, stood in front of the boards on Tallow Street with her nephew's card in her hand and counted the difference between the two columns for the eight Under districts, out loud, slowly, in front of about forty people who were waiting to find out what their children were worth.\n\nIt took her most of an hour.\n\nThat was the beginning of it, and it was nothing to do with any of them, and it was exactly what Prewitt had said would happen and it happened eleven years earlier than she had estimated, and Corvain was in the room when the argument reached the Council and she did not have to say very much at all.\n\n---\n\n**V. The Boards**\nOn the last morning of the eleventh summer after, six hundred and eighty-one fourteen year olds queued down the pavement on Tallow Street in the sootfall.\n\nThe eye was still cut into the lintel. *By reckoning, order.* Nobody read it. Everybody had been told what it said.\n\nThere were eleven boards under the lintel and there would be eleven boards next year, because the strata had not gone; eleven years of daylight had not abolished the strata and nobody in that queue had been promised that they would. What there was, now, was two columns on every board instead of one, and a printed line at the foot of each in type nobody had bothered to make small:\n\n*Where the assessed and final columns differ, the difference has been made by administrative amendment. Enquiries to the Office of the Chief Engineer.*\n\nThere were nine hundred and eleven enquiries that year. There had been four in the first year.\n\nA clerk came down the line with a box of wooden tokens and a ledger and asked each of them their name, their district and their household.\n\n\"Name.\"\n\n\"Halloway.\"\n\nThe clerk's pen stopped. \"First name?\"\n\n\"Just Halloway, sir. It's the household name and I've took it.\"\n\n\"Right.\" He wrote it. \"District.\"\n\n\"Ferrow Lane.\"\n\n\"Foster?\"\n\n\"Yes, sir.\"\n\nHe put the token in her palm and moved on, and she stood in the queue in the black rain turning it over in her fingers, and up on the boards in the gilt frame at the top of the lintel there were two names in the first stratum this year, and there had been three the year before that, and nobody in the queue knew that this had once been the single most impossible thing in the city.\n\nSomewhere a very long way below the pavement, under the sumps and the black flooded tunnels nobody had names for, the Turn went round.\n\nIt got within a hair of the answer.\n\nThen it stopped, and waited, the way it had waited for a hundred and fourteen years, for somebody to go first.\n\n---\n\n*End of Chapter Eleven.*\n\n*End of Book One.*\n\n**THE END**" },
];
const INTERMEDIATE_ADVENTURES = {
gf_tambrindle: {
  id: "gf_tambrindle",
  title: "The Vent-Count Watch",
  tagline: "A rush job across the seam, a rival porter's dare, and forty counts before the fog comes down. Tam only has to be quicker than usual.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_tambrindle_item1", name: "The Weighed Blade", stat: 4, how: "found", desc: "+1 Speed. Won with a sharp Speed check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_tambrindle_item2", name: "The Reckoned Chit", stat: 0, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_tambrindle_item3", name: "The Vent-Count Watch", stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { mainspring: "A Tensioned Mainspring", countwheel: "A True Count-Wheel", crystal: "A Sliver of Watch-Crystal" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🔪", text: "Tam heard it through the floorboards before anyone said it out loud: Ma Halloway at the table with the rent docket and the chalk tab from Marta's laid side by side, not adding up, going quiet in the particular way she went quiet when she was doing sums she didn't like the answer to.\n\nThree marks short. Maybe three and a half. Nobody had said it to him. Nobody needed to.\n\nHe was supposed to be mending the barrow wheel, and he was, technically, in that he had a knife out and a peg half whittled and had been for two days, because the knife kept finding better things to do than the peg. He put it away, laced his boots twice each, and went to see what the yard had going spare that morning. Portering paid by the load and the hour, and today he intended to be the fastest thing on the seam.", next: "speed_check" },
    speed_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "⏱️", stat: 4, dc: 12,
      text: "Roll-call at the porters' yard closed on the second bell, not a breath after, and the second bell was already clearing its throat somewhere over the Under's chimneys. Ferrow Lane was full of the usual morning clutter: barrows, washing lines, a dog that owned the middle of the street and knew it.\n\nTam had run this lane since he was six. He knew which wall you could vault instead of round, which gate stuck if you didn't kick the bottom corner, and exactly how much of a lie it was to tell himself he had time to spare.\n\nSomebody quick had better make that bell.",
      success: "speed_found", fail: "speed_miss" },
    speed_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "He misjudged the gate. It stuck on the bottom corner the way it always did when you forgot to kick it, and by the time he'd wrestled it open the bell had finished and the yard clerk had drawn a line under the list.\n\n'Late's late,' she said, not looking up, which was somehow worse than if she had. 'There's still the leavings. Go and see what nobody wanted.'\n\n(The Weighed Blade stays lost for now — another run at this morning might turn it up.)", next: "gate_fight" },
    speed_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🔪", grantItem: "gf_tambrindle_item1", text: "He cleared the gate on the vault, no kicking required, and landed running past a jeweller's boy who'd dropped his tray in the scramble and was scrabbling after the pieces with more panic than sense.\n\nTam scooped up the one thing rolling toward the drain before it could go down it: a boot-knife, plain handle, no maker's mark, balanced so true it barely felt like it was there at all. He held it out. The boy, red-faced and grateful, told him to keep it, he'd never even liked the thing, it wouldn't sit right in any sheath he owned.\n\nIt sat right in Tam's. The Weighed Blade is his now. (+1 Speed, kept forever.)", next: "gate_fight" },
    gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "⚙️", qLevel: 2,
      text: "He made the bell with three heartbeats to spare, which the yard clerk noted with the enthusiasm of somebody noting rain, and then the seam gate itself gave him trouble: a Warden, brass and dull iron, the kind the Rise leased out to check writs at the boundary crossings, planted square in the middle of the crossing with its lamp-eye fixed on him.\n\n'DOCKET,' it said, in the flat voice they all had. 'OUT OF SEQUENCE. STATE PURPOSE.'\n\nHe had a docket. It just wasn't the docket for this crossing at this hour, on account of him being early on purpose, and the Warden's clockwork did not have a slot in it marked keen.",
      enemy: { name: "The Seam Warden", emoji: "⚙️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "The working came out clean and quick, and the Warden's lamp-eye stuttered, its objection running out of steam a half-second before it finished stating it.",
        strikeText: "The Warden's arm swept the crossing in a slow, certain arc, and Tam caught the edge of it across the shins, which is a bruise that argues with you all day.",
        defeatText: "Something in the Warden's chest gave a soft, defeated click, and it stood aside with the mechanical dignity of a thing that has never once admitted to losing." },
      victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "It let him through without another word, because Wardens don't apologise, they just stop objecting, and that's the closest thing to an apology their sort ever manages.\n\nAt the counter beyond, Coop was waiting: the Weighmaster's clerk, a Laachmi, horns filed blunt as the office required, standing very still the way tall dignified people do when they'd rather be sitting. Coop had a rush job on the board that nobody else wanted, on account of the deadline attached to it, and had apparently decided Tam looked exactly foolish enough to take it.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "He backed off before the second strike landed, which was sense, not cowardice, though it took a while to feel like the first one. The long way round the crossing cost him a good ten minutes and a scraped elbow off a low wall he'd misjudged out of temper.\n\nBy the time he reached the counter Coop had already half filled in someone else's name on the board, and looked, if a Laachmi's face can be said to look anything, mildly disappointed to have to cross it back out again.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "'Valve gaskets,' said Coop, sliding a sealed crate and a docket across the counter. 'Rise workshop on Founders' Row wants them before their afternoon shift starts retooling, or the whole line stands idle and somebody's fee gets docked, and it will not be mine. Triple the usual rate if it's there before the third bell. Nothing at all if it isn't.'\n\nCoop's eyes flicked, once, to the board. 'Sella Vane's already gone. Down the Scullery Passage, if you're asking, which you weren't, but you'll want to know it.'\n\nThree ways ran from the yard toward Founders' Row: the Cutthroat Stairs, steep and mean and fastest if your knees agreed to it; the queue for the clatterlift, which climbed the cliff proper but only some of the cars stopped at his landing; or the Scullery Passage, straight through the back kitchens, the way Sella had gone, if he fancied finding out firsthand how fast she really was.", options: [
      { label: "Take the Cutthroat Stairs", next: "stairs", preview: "Work out, on the way down, whether you'll actually beat the vent. A real speed puzzle." },
      { label: "Queue for the clatterlift", next: "lift", preview: "Only some of the cars stop at your landing. A dice roll on the odds." },
      { label: "Race Sella down the Scullery Passage", next: "race", preview: "Catch her before the workshop door. A head-to-head footrace." },
    ] },
    stairs: { type: "story", emoji: "🪜", text: "The Cutthroat Stairs dropped two hundred and forty feet of cliff face in one straight ugly run, and every porter who'd ever used them called them exactly what they were: quick, if you didn't think too hard about the outfall grate waiting at the bottom.\n\nThe grate had already started to hiss. Somewhere below, the Turn had begun its afternoon vent, and the fog would come up through that grate and fill the foot of the stairs whether Tam had finished his business there or not.\n\nHe had the crate on his back, the numbers in his head, and about four seconds to decide if the numbers were good enough.", next: "stairs_puzzle" },
    stairs_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⏱️", text: "The vent fills a lane in forty counts flat, and the counts don't wait for anybody's opinion of them. Tam knew his own pace on these stairs going down loaded, and he knew it going back up empty, because he'd run this exact stretch a hundred times chasing pennies. The question was whether the two together added up to a stair he could still be standing on when the grate opened.", question: "Loaded, Tam covers the 240-foot run of the stairs at 6 feet a second. Empty, on the way back up, he manages 12 feet a second over the same 240 feet. What is his true average speed for the whole there-and-back run, in feet per second?", options: ["8", "9", "10", "7.5"], answer: 0,
      hint: "Average speed is total distance divided by total time, not just the average of the two speeds. Work out how long each leg takes on its own first.",
      solution: "Distance each way is 240 feet, so total distance = 480 feet. Time down = 240 ÷ 6 = 40 seconds. Time up = 240 ÷ 12 = 20 seconds. Total time = 60 seconds. Average speed = 480 ÷ 60 = 8 feet per second. (Simply averaging 6 and 12 gives the tempting but wrong answer of 9 — more time is spent at the slower speed, which drags the true average down.)",
      good: "stairs_good", mid: "stairs_mid" },
    stairs_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔧", grantComponent: "mainspring", text: "Eight feet a second, near enough, and the grate hissed its first breath of fog just as his boots hit the flat below, close enough that the hem of his coat came away smelling of hot metal.\n\nWedged in the drain grille, too corroded to be worth anyone's trouble, sat the guts of somebody's dropped chronometer: rusted through, useless as a whole, but the mainspring inside had been sealed tight enough to survive. He dug it out with the Weighed Blade, wiped it on his sleeve, and pocketed it without quite knowing yet what it was for.\n\nComponent found: A Tensioned Mainspring.", next: "converge" },
    stairs_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", setMid: true, text: "He hedged his numbers wrong, took the last flight two steps at a time out of pure nerve instead of arithmetic, and the fog caught the back of his boots on the last landing. It didn't touch skin. It touched leather, and the leather would smell of it for a week.\n\nWhatever was rusting in that drain grille stayed exactly where it was. Some grates you only get one clean pass at, and he'd spent his on getting down alive, which was, on reflection, the correct spending.", next: "converge" },
    lift: { type: "story", emoji: "🛗", text: "The clatterlift queue wound twice round the winding-house, cable cars clanking up the cliff face on their long brass chains, and the trouble with the clatterlift was well known to every porter on the seam: only one car in three actually stopped at his landing. The rest sailed straight past, chains singing, bound for stops with better connections and better manners.\n\nHe hadn't the time to wait for a guaranteed one. He had the time for exactly one roll of the dice the queue always was.", next: "lift_dice" },
    lift_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 5, text: "Roll 5 or more on a six-sided die and the next car is one of the ones that stops at your landing. Anything less, and you watch it clank on past with your fee riding away on it.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the true chance of rolling a 5 or a 6 on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/3", "1/2", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "Two faces out of six succeed: the 5 and the 6. That's 2/6, which simplifies to 1/3 — roughly one car in three, exactly as the queue's reputation says. Knowing the true odds before you commit steadies your nerve: +1 to your roll." },
      success: "lift_good", fail: "lift_fail" },
    lift_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔧", grantComponent: "mainspring", text: "The car that clanked into the landing was one of the good ones, chain taut, door already swinging. He was in and climbing before the operator had finished calling the stop.\n\nSomeone had left a dead chronometer wedged behind the bench seat, years of dust on it, forgotten the way small useless things get forgotten in places people only ever pass through. He worked the mainspring loose from the ruined casing while the cliff face slid past the window, and had it in his pocket before the car reached the top.\n\nComponent found: A Tensioned Mainspring.", next: "converge" },
    lift_fail: { type: "story", emoji: "🛗", setMid: true, text: "The car clanked straight past, chain singing, bound for somewhere he wasn't going. He watched it go with the particular patience of somebody doing sums about how much of his morning that had just cost him, and joined the back of the queue again because there was nothing else honest to do with his hands.\n\nThe second car was one of the good ones. It got him up the cliff eventually. It did not have anyone's forgotten chronometer wedged behind the seat.", next: "converge" },
    race: { type: "story", emoji: "🏃", text: "The Scullery Passage ran low and steaming through the back of a dozen Rise kitchens, cooks shouting, pot-boys flattening themselves against the walls, and forty yards ahead of him, moving like she had personally arranged for the passage to be empty, was Sella Vane.\n\nSella portered the seam same as he did, same wages, same worn boots, and had made it plain on more than one occasion that she considered Tam's crate-carrying technique an insult to the profession. She hadn't seen him yet. That wouldn't last.\n\n'Oi,' he called, because subtlety had never once got him anywhere. She looked back, clocked the crate on his shoulder, and grinned like this was the best news she'd had all week.", next: "race_fight" },
    race_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🏃", qLevel: 2,
      text: "'Same crate, same docket, same rate,' Sella called over her shoulder, not slowing down. 'Whoever's foreman signs first gets paid. Whoever's second gets a story to tell about it.'\n\nIt wasn't a fair race. It wasn't meant to be. It was two porters, one passage, and a dozen quick reckonings about corners, cook's carts, and how much of a lead a person could actually hold onto through a kitchen full of boiling pans.",
      enemy: { name: "Sella Vane", emoji: "🏃", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "He cut the corner a half-step tighter than she did, and the gap between them closed enough to hear her swearing about it.",
        strikeText: "She barrelled past a cook's cart at exactly the wrong moment for him, and he lost three good strides untangling himself from a rack of ladles.",
        defeatText: "He came out of the last turn a full body-length ahead, and didn't look back to check, because looking back is how you lose the strides you've just won." },
      victory: "race_win", retreat: "race_retreat" },
    race_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🔧", grantComponent: "mainspring", text: "He beat her to the workshop door by less than the length of a barrow, and she arrived laughing, which was worse than if she'd been furious about it, because now he owed her a rematch and both of them knew it.\n\n'You dropped this back at the third turn,' she said, tossing him something small and cold before she'd even caught her breath properly. 'Went straight past a stall selling scrap chronometers for parts. Didn't stop. You're welcome.' It was a mainspring, still coiled tight, worth more to him right now than the coin it might have fetched her.\n\nComponent found: A Tensioned Mainspring.", next: "converge" },
    race_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", setMid: true, text: "She took the last turn wide and mean and left him nowhere to go but wide and slow, and she reached the workshop door with enough of a lead that following her in felt less like a race and more like an apology.\n\n'Tell your foreman you were second,' she said, not unkindly, catching her breath against the doorframe. 'Tell him you were fast about it, at least. That's not nothing.' It wasn't nothing. It also wasn't a mainspring, and she hadn't found one to spare him.", next: "converge" },
    converge: { type: "story", emoji: "📦", text: "Whichever way he'd come, he came through the workshop door with the crate still on his shoulder and time still, just, on his side. The foreman on shift was a broad, soot-streaked man called Hask, who took one look at the state of Tam and the crate and said, not unkindly, 'You'll want to sit down before you fall down, lad, but the docket wants signing first.'\n\nHask didn't sign anything without a count. That was the whole point of Hask.", next: "dock_puzzle" },
    dock_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📋", text: "'Manifest says gaskets and washers ship in a ratio of five to three,' Hask said, tipping the crate's contents onto the bench without much ceremony. 'Ninety-six pieces in there altogether, gaskets and washers both. Tell me how many gaskets I should be counting, and I'll believe the crate before I've finished counting it myself.'", question: "Gaskets and washers must ship in the ratio 5:3. The crate holds 96 items in total, all gaskets and washers. How many gaskets should there be?", options: ["36", "40", "60", "72"], answer: 2,
      hint: "Add the parts of the ratio together first to find out how many equal shares the total splits into.",
      solution: "The ratio 5:3 makes 5 + 3 = 8 equal shares. 96 ÷ 8 = 12 per share. Gaskets get 5 of those shares: 5 × 12 = 60.",
      good: "dock_good", mid: "dock_mid" },
    dock_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔧", grantComponent: "countwheel", text: "Sixty gaskets, thirty-six washers, exactly as counted, and Hask signed the docket without another word of argument, which from Hask was practically a testimonial.\n\n'Scrap bin's there if you want it,' he added, nodding at a crate of broken instrument parts by the door. 'Nobody's claimed it in months.' Tam found a small brass count-wheel in among the ruin, teeth still true, and pocketed it next to the mainspring, which was starting to look, in his head, less like two odd bits of scrap and more like the beginnings of something.\n\nComponent found: A True Count-Wheel.", next: "vent_setup" },
    dock_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📋", setMid: true, text: "Hask counted it out himself, slower, muttering the eights under his breath, and got to sixty gaskets and thirty-six washers with a small satisfied grunt that had nothing to do with Tam at all.\n\n'Good enough,' he said, signing the docket. He did not mention the scrap bin, and Tam, half asleep on his feet by then, didn't think to ask.", next: "vent_setup" },
    vent_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "💨", text: "Docket signed, fee promised, nothing left but the walk home, which should have been the easy part of the day and generally was, except the Turn didn't care whose day it had been. Somewhere under the streets it ticked once, hard, a knock he felt through the soles of his boots, and every porter on the seam knew what that meant: the second vent of the afternoon, right on schedule, whether he'd finished his business or not.\n\nA lane fills with fog in forty counts. It doesn't clear again for a hundred and fifty. Get the reading wrong either way, and you're either scalded or standing about half an hour longer than you can afford to.", next: "vent_check" },
    vent_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🌡️", stat: 3, dc: 12,
      text: "The grates along Founders' Row were already ticking warmer underfoot, that particular shiver in the ironwork that came just before the hiss started properly. Reading it right meant knowing the difference between a grate that had forty counts left in it and one that had four.\n\nGet it wrong, and you learn the difference the hard way, which is also a kind of knowing, just an expensive one.",
      success: "vent_good", fail: "vent_fail" },
    vent_fail: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "He misjudged it by a breath, ducked into the shelter of an old, boarded-up watch-repair kiosk a half-second after the fog had already caught the cuff of his sleeve, and spent the next hundred and fifty counts nursing a scorched wrist and cursing quietly at the dark inside the kiosk, which had nothing useful in it that he could find in that light.\n\n(A Sliver of Watch-Crystal stays lost for now — another run at this stretch of the day might turn it up.)", next: "forge" },
    vent_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔧", grantComponent: "crystal", text: "He read the shiver in the grate exactly right, ducked into the shelter of an old, boarded-up watch-repair kiosk with half a breath to spare, and sat out the hundred and fifty counts in comfortable, unscorched boredom, poking through a dusty tray of parts nobody had bothered to clear out when the place shut.\n\nAmong the dead springs and stripped screws sat a single sliver of watch-crystal, thin as a fingernail and, against all the dust around it, still perfectly clear.\n\nComponent found: A Sliver of Watch-Crystal.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Old Byrne kept a bench at the seam gate, mending porters' pocket-watches for whatever they could spare, on the grounds that a porter who couldn't tell the time was a porter who'd be scalded eventually and it might as well not be on her conscience.\n\n'Turning up with parts instead of coin,' she said, looking over what he tipped onto her bench: a mainspring, a count-wheel, a sliver of crystal. 'That's either very clever or very broke. Show me all three properly matched, and I'll see what a good knife and a steady hand can do about the rest.'",
      forged: "Byrne turned the pieces over in the lamplight, matched the spring to the wheel and the wheel to a scavenged case, and set the crystal over the face with a small satisfied click that meant it had seated true first time.\n\n'There,' she said, sliding it across the bench. 'Counts your seconds honester than any bell in this city, and none of them are for sale to the Rise. Mind the knife you built half of this with stays for whittling and not for anything that bleeds.'\n\nThe Vent-Count Watch is his! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
      notforged: "Byrne laid the pieces out and counted them, then counted them again, which cost her nothing because there weren't enough of them to take long.\n\n'Short a piece,' she said, not unkindly, tapping the gap. 'A watch wants all three or it's just a very optimistic paperweight. Keep what you've got. The bench isn't going anywhere, and neither, worse luck, is the Turn's schedule.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
      grantItem: "gf_tambrindle_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "🏠", text: "He came up Ferrow Lane past dusk with his fee in his pocket, his knees complaining, and something new and ticking against his ribs that he hadn't had that morning. Somebody said the Halloway girl had gone up the Rise again that week, on some business of her own that wasn't his to ask after, and he filed it away the way you file most things you can't do anything about.\n\nMa Halloway was still at the table when he got in, docket and chalk tab laid side by side, and this time, when he put the marks down next to them, the sums came out the way sums are supposed to: even, and quiet, and nobody's fault at all.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end",
      goodText: "Marta struck three marks off the chalk tab without a word of comment, which from Marta was practically a hug, and the rent docket got paid clean for the first time in a month. Sella found him at the yard two days later and demanded her rematch, and got it, and lost it, loudly, in front of witnesses she'd specifically brought along to see her win.\n\nHe still hadn't finished the peg for the barrow wheel. But he could tell you, now, to the count, exactly how long he had before the next vent came up through any grate on the seam, and in a city built on the Turn's good time, that was worth more than most people's writs.",
      midText: "Marta took what he had toward the tab and didn't press for the rest, which was its own kind of kindness, the kind that costs the giver something and never says so. The rent held for another week. That was the job, done, and nobody had asked it to be more than that.\n\nHe still hadn't finished the peg for the barrow wheel, and the knife had, if anything, found even more things that weren't food to occupy itself with. But he'd learned the shiver of every grate between the yard and Founders' Row, and that knowledge doesn't go stale, and it doesn't get taken off you at the door.",
      grantItem: "gf_tambrindle_item2" },
  },
},

gf_nettle: {
  id: "gf_nettle",
  title: "The Pattern in the Fog-Glass",
  tagline: "Kept in from the cold by a cough that won't quit, Nettle finds the Engine's fog keeps better time than anyone in Ferrow Lane has noticed.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_nettle_item1", name: "A Stub of Borrowed Chalk", stat: 3, how: "found", desc: "+1 Science. Won with a sharp Science check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_nettle_item2", name: "The Post-Line's Brass Hook", stat: 2, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_nettle_item3", name: "Nettle's Tally-Pane", stat: 0, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { clean_pane: "A Clean Scrap of Lamp-Glass", waxed_paper: "A Dry Scrap of Waxed Paper", bent_wire: "A Bent Curtain-Wire" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🪟", text: "Nettle was meant to be asleep. Mother Halloway had said so twice, once at supper and once again with the candle held close to check the cough hadn't come back sharp, and both times Nettle had nodded and meant it, mostly. But the room was cold in the particular way that told her the Engine was about to knock, and there was a stretch of bare glass above her bed that no one else in the house ever bothered to watch. So she got up on her knees on the cold boards, breathed a small patch of warmth onto the pane, and waited. Down at the end of Ferrow Lane the outfall grate gave its first low knock, felt more than heard, right through the floor and the bedframe and her own chest. Four times a day the Turn breathed its fog up through that grate, and four times a day everyone else in the lane simply waited it out. Nettle had started counting instead, weeks ago, chalking the knocks on the underside of her sill where Mother Halloway's candle never reached. Tonight the knocks felt sharper than usual, more countable, as though the Engine wanted, just this once, to be understood.", next: "skillcheck1" },
    skillcheck1: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "❄️", stat: 3, dc: 11, text: "The frost bloomed up from the corner of the glass the second the fog began to rise outside, feathering into a shape before her own breath fogged it away again. It was the same shape as last time, she was almost sure, and the time before that, but 'almost sure' was not a thing you could chalk on a sill and trust. She pressed her sleeve to her mouth to keep her breath off the pane a moment longer and looked hard: was it four points on that little frost-fern or five, and did the middle sit round or square. A proper noticing, the kind her chest ached less for making than for arguing about afterwards.", success: "item1_found", fail: "item1_missed" },
    item1_found: { type: "story", emoji: "✏️", grantItem: "gf_nettle_item1", text: "Five points, round in the middle, same as always. Nettle grinned in the dark and went to chalk it on the sill, and her fingers found the corner board looser than she remembered, tipping up under her nail like a lid that wanted opening. Underneath, in a shallow dusty hollow, lay a stub of proper slate-chalk worn down to almost nothing, and scored into the wood beneath it, small and careful and unmistakably not her own hand, a little row of tally-marks someone had kept and then stopped keeping. She knew the hand. She didn't say the name out loud, not even to herself, but she took the chalk stub and held it a moment before she put it to use.", next: "gate_fight" },
    item1_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The frost-fern blurred under her own breath before she could be sure of it, gone to a smear before she'd counted the points properly. Nettle huffed, more cross with her own lungs than with the glass, and let it go. There was a stub of ordinary chalk in her apron pocket from lessons, not as fine as she'd have liked, but chalk is chalk, and a tally kept in ordinary chalk still keeps. Whatever had been waiting behind the loose sill-board stayed there, forgotten, for another night.", next: "gate_fight" },
    gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🐈", qLevel: 2, text: "A scrabble at the sill made her jump half out of her skin. Pressed flat against the outside ledge, tail lashing, was the lane's stray tabby, the one the children called nothing in particular and fed scraps to anyway. She'd picked the worst possible spot to shelter, right above the outfall's mouth, and the knocking pipes had her in a proper state, ears flat, claws hooked into the rotten wood, not going anywhere sensible on her own.", enemy: { name: "Tuppence, in a Fright", emoji: "🐈", hits: 2, toHit: 8, hitText: "The cat flattens herself harder against the glass and the ledge creaks, close enough to the outfall's edge that Nettle's stomach lurches with her.", strikeText: "Nettle taps the same steady beat on the inside of the pane that the pipes are knocking outside, one, two, three, slow and sure, and murmurs the numbers to her like a lullaby.", defeatText: "On the third steady count Tuppence's ears come up out of their flatten, and she shuffles, unhurried now, along the ledge to the drier corner away from the grate's mouth, exactly where Nettle had been willing her to go. She's still cross about it. She's also, for the moment, safe." }, victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🐾", text: "Tuppence settled into the dry corner with the particular dignity of a cat pretending she'd meant to go there all along, and Nettle let her breath out slow. Down the lane, muffled through glass and fog both, she could just make out voices. Someone else was up far too late as well, by the sound of it, and by the sharpness of the voices, not entirely happy about it.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "Tuppence wasn't in a mood to be talked round tonight, fright being the sort of thing that doesn't always listen to a good beat kept on the glass. Nettle let her be rather than pester a frightened animal, and after a minute the cat worked her own way along the ledge regardless, muttering in cat the whole time. Down the lane, muffled through glass and fog both, voices carried, sharp with the particular worry of people who are up far too late.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🧺", text: "Ferrow Lane had a post-line strung window to window and lamp-post to sill, a length of good cord the children kept for passing things along without going out in the wet, and someone down the lane was tugging on it now, three short pulls, which meant urgent. Nettle reeled the little tin basket in along the sill and found three separate scraps of trouble waiting for her attention, chalked in three different hands. She could only send one chalked answer back down the line before the next knock came.", options: [
      { label: "Cobb's coal round", next: "routeA_setup", preview: "The coal-boy needs to know exactly when it's safe to set off down the lane." },
      { label: "Old Fenn's ladder", next: "routeB_setup", preview: "The lamplighter's trusting her luck against the fog. Reckon the odds for her." },
      { label: "The grate by the outfall", next: "routeC_setup", preview: "Something small is stuck down there, and Tuppence has gone frantic about it." },
    ] },
    routeA_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🪣", text: "The first note was in Cobb's careful, blotchy hand. He did the coal round for six houses on Ferrow Lane, hauling a handcart heavier than he was through weather that never once did him a favour, and tonight his round would take him straight past the outfall grate right as it was due to knock its warning. Get it wrong and he'd be standing in scalding fog with a cart he couldn't leave and coal that couldn't get wet. Nettle chalked back that she needed a minute, and set to counting properly.", next: "routeA_challenge" },
    routeA_challenge: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⏱️", text: "The outfall's pipes had a pattern Nettle had been keeping for weeks now, three knocks before every vent, always the same shape: short, short, long, and then the fog came. She'd counted forty knocks since the house had gone quiet tonight, and Cobb needed to know, before he so much as lifted the cart handles, whether that fortieth knock was the safe short kind or the long warning kind.", question: "The Engine's warning knocks always run in a repeating pattern of three: short, short, long, and then it vents. If the pattern has run without a single missed beat, is the 40th knock since the pattern began a short knock or a long knock?", options: ["A short knock", "A long knock", "There's no way to tell without counting all forty", "It changes depending on the night"], answer: 0, hint: "The pattern repeats every three knocks. Divide 40 by 3 and see what's left over. That leftover tells you where in the pattern the 40th knock falls.", solution: "The pattern is short, short, long, repeating every three knocks. Dividing 40 by 3 gives 13 whole patterns, since 3 x 13 = 39, with 1 knock left over. That leftover of 1 lines up with the very first knock in the pattern, which is short. So the 40th knock is a short knock, not the long warning knock, and there's still time before the vent.", good: "routeA_good", mid: "routeA_mid" },
    routeA_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔍", grantComponent: "clean_pane", text: "Nettle chalked back a single word, SAFE, and sent the basket rattling down the line. Cobb read it, nodded once at nothing in particular the way boys do when they're too proud to say thank you to a windowpane, and got the cart moving well clear before the long knock ever came. In thanks, or maybe just because he was glad to be past it, he sent the basket back up with a shard of spare lamp-glass from his cart lantern tucked inside, its edges clean and unbroken. Nettle turned it over in the candlelight. Perfectly clear. Exactly the sort of thing a person keeping a careful watch on the fog might find a use for.", next: "converge1" },
    routeA_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "Nettle's count snagged somewhere around the thirty-third knock, and by the time she'd worked her way back to sureness the basket had already gone, tugged down the line by a Cobb too impatient to wait on an answer he wasn't sure was coming. He made it past the grate anyway, more by good luck than good arithmetic, hauling his cart at a dead run the last stretch and arriving red-faced but dry the other end. No glass came back up the line this time. Some things, Nettle told herself, you only get the once.", next: "converge1" },
    routeB_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🏮", text: "The second note was in a spidery, elderly hand: Old Fenn's, the last lamplighter left on Ferrow Lane now the young ones had all gone for engine-work instead. She was three rungs up her ladder against the lamp-post, mantle unlit, fog already curling round her ankles, and she needed both hands free to strike the light before the damp fouled the gas for good. She could risk one hand off the ladder for exactly one try. Whether it came off clean was mostly down to luck, and Old Fenn, being a sensible sort, wanted the true odds before she trusted her grip to them.", next: "routeB_challenge" },
    routeB_challenge: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 5, text: "Fenn had a little bone die in her apron pocket for exactly these decisions, half in earnest and half for comfort, and she called down through the murk that she'd trust the strike if the die came up 5 or better. Nettle chalked out the numbers before anyone rolled anything, because Fenn had asked for the true odds, not a guess dressed up as one.", predict: { question: "Before the roll: what is the chance of rolling a 5 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/3", "1/2", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "Only the rolls of 5 and 6 succeed, and that is 2 outcomes out of 6, which simplifies to 1/3. Knowing the true odds before you commit to the risk steadies the hand: +1 to the roll." }, success: "routeB_good", fail: "routeB_mid" },
    routeB_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", grantComponent: "clean_pane", text: "The die came up kind, and Fenn's free hand struck true on the first go, the mantle catching with a soft pop just as the fog thickened past her knees. She climbed down steadier for having known her chances rather than merely hoped at them, and before she went off to the next lamp she called up that she owed the sill-watching Halloway girl a good turn. It came, a little while later, wrapped in a twist of paper and sent up the post-line: a spare glass roundel from her repair kit, clean and round and exactly the sort of thing that might sit well in a frame.", next: "converge1" },
    routeB_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🌑", text: "The die came up low, and Fenn's hand slipped on the wet rung rather than the mantle, costing her nothing worse than a bruised palm and a lamp left dark one street a little longer than it should have been. She wasn't cross about it, only tired, and called up that true odds don't owe you a good result, only an honest one. No glass came back up the line, and Nettle chalked a small, private note to herself that some risks simply don't pay out, however fairly you reckon them.", next: "converge1" },
    routeC_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🐾", text: "The third note wasn't chalk at all, just Tuppence herself, back at the sill and yowling now instead of frightened quiet, pacing the ledge and refusing to be comforted by any amount of tapping. Down below, half under the grate's rusted cover where it had come loose from its frame, three small shapes were mewing in a huddle too far in for any grown hand to reach easily. Tessa had come out onto the step in her nightclothes to see what the noise was, and was crouched by the grate working her fingers under the cold iron edge, but the cover was stuck fast and would need lifting at exactly the right moment, not a knock too soon or too late.", next: "routeC_challenge" },
    routeC_challenge: { type: "combat", emoji: "🐾", qLevel: 2, text: "The grate cover had rusted half into its frame and would only give if Tessa timed her heave between two knocks rather than through one, or she'd catch her fingers in the iron the instant it shifted. Tuppence circled the step, beside herself, swiping at nothing, unable to help and unwilling to leave. Nettle pressed her face to the cold glass and began counting the knocks aloud for Tessa to hear through the pane, steady and clear, the same rhythm that had settled the cat on the ledge not half an hour before.", enemy: { name: "Tuppence, Beside Herself", emoji: "🐈", hits: 2, toHit: 8, hitText: "Tuppence darts between Tessa's ankles with a yowl, nearly tipping her off balance right as she's got her fingers under the iron.", strikeText: "Nettle counts the knocks through the glass in the same slow beat as before, loud enough for both a cat and a half-asleep sister to hear and trust.", defeatText: "On the count Tuppence knows, the one she learned on the ledge, she finally sits, tail wrapped tight, and waits instead of scrabbling. Tessa heaves the cover clear between two knocks exactly as counted, and three damp, furious kittens tumble out into the light, straight into their mother's very relieved attention." }, victory: "routeC_good", retreat: "routeC_mid" },
    routeC_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🐾", grantComponent: "clean_pane", text: "With the kittens safe in a huddle against their mother and the cover set back loose but level, Tessa knelt to catch her breath and noticed something else glinting where the grate had lifted clear: an old shard of glass, long since fallen and forgotten down there, rinsed clean by every vent that had ever passed over it and somehow never once cracked. She climbed the step, wrapped it in a scrap of her own shawl, and sent it up the post-line to the one person on Ferrow Lane who'd know exactly what to do with a clean piece of glass.", next: "converge1" },
    routeC_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🌿", text: "The timing slipped by half a knock, no one's fault exactly, and the cover jammed harder than before rather than lifting clear. Tessa drew her fingers back sharp and safe rather than risk them, and decided the kittens would simply have to wait out one more venting where they were, huddled but out of the fog's direct path, until calmer hands and a steadier count could try again. No glass came up the line this time, only Tessa's tired, grateful wave through the murk.", next: "converge1" },
    converge1: { type: "story", emoji: "🧺", text: "Whatever had happened at the end of the lane, the post-line eventually went quiet and the little tin basket came to rest against Nettle's sill for good, the night's small crises settled one way or another. She sat back against her pillow with her chalk stub and her borrowed odds and her tallies, and found she wasn't quite ready to stop. If a pattern this useful was hiding in the Engine's knocking, she wanted more than a chalked warning shouted down a cord. She wanted something she could hold up to the glass itself, steady, so the fog's own shape couldn't slip away from her the way the frost-fern kept doing.", next: "dice2_setup" },
    dice2_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🪝", text: "A proper watching-frame needed something dry to trace on, and Nettle had her eye on a scrap of good waxed paper snagged on a nail across the lane, blown there off somebody's parcel and forgotten. The post-line's little brass hook could just about reach it if she swung the basket right, but the nail was slick with damp and the paper thin enough to tear if she mistimed the pull.", next: "dice2" },
    dice2: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 4, text: "She needed the basket to swing true and catch the paper clean rather than glance off it, which meant trusting the hook to a good firm throw, a 4 or better on the little bone die kept in her apron for exactly this sort of decision.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 4 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/3", "1/2", "2/3"], answer: 2, why: "Rolls of 4, 5 and 6 all succeed, which is 3 outcomes out of 6, and 3/6 simplifies to 1/2. Knowing the odds before you throw steadies the hand: +1 to the roll." }, success: "comp2_good", fail: "comp2_mid" },
    comp2_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📜", grantComponent: "waxed_paper", text: "The basket swung, caught, and came back with the whole scrap of waxed paper still attached to its nail-torn corner, dry and sound and just the size to sit behind a small pane of glass. Nettle smoothed it flat on her knee, delighted. Two pieces of whatever she was building, and both of them honestly won.", next: "skillcheck3_setup" },
    comp2_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "💧", setMid: true, text: "The hook glanced off the nail instead of catching, and the paper, damp already from the fog, tore clean in half and fluttered down into a puddle beyond any rescuing. Nettle sighed, wound the cord back in, and decided a proper watching-frame could manage without paper for now if it truly had to. It would just have to be built the longer, plainer way.", next: "skillcheck3_setup" },
    skillcheck3_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🍞", text: "Down in the kitchen, through the thin floorboards, Mother Halloway had started muttering sums to herself over the evening loaf, the way she did most nights, only half-aware anyone could hear. Eight little ones still home tonight meant eight slices, and one whole slice set aside, she was saying, to crumble into a saucer for that wretched cat and her wretcheder kittens, and then hadn't she gone and put half of it back for the children after all, and now she couldn't for the life of her say what that left the cat with. Nettle, lying very still and listening hard, thought she could.", next: "puzzle3" },
    puzzle3: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🍞", text: "Nettle called the answer down through the floor-grate in her best clear-in-class voice, working it through on her fingers first to be sure.", question: "Mother Halloway cuts the evening loaf into 8 equal slices. She sets aside 1 whole slice for the cat's saucer, then changes her mind and puts back half of that slice for the children after all. What fraction of the whole loaf ends up in the cat's saucer?", options: ["1/16", "1/8", "1/4", "3/16"], answer: 0, hint: "First work out what fraction of the whole loaf just one slice is. Then take half of that fraction, since half the slice went back.", solution: "One slice out of 8 equal slices is 1/8 of the loaf. Putting half of that slice back for the children leaves the other half in the saucer, and half of 1/8 is 1/2 x 1/8 = 1/16. So 1/16 of the whole loaf ends up in the cat's saucer.", good: "comp3_good", mid: "comp3_mid" },
    comp3_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔧", grantComponent: "bent_wire", text: "'A sixteenth,' Nettle called down, and there was a pause below the sort that meant Mother Halloway was checking it on her own fingers before she'd believe a nine-year-old through a floorboard. 'Well,' she said at last, gruffly pleased, 'that's settled, then.' A few minutes later the stairs creaked, and a hand came round Nettle's door not with a scolding but with a bent length of curtain-wire pulled straight from the mending basket. 'Seeing as you're clearly building something up here instead of sleeping,' Mother Halloway said, not quite hiding a smile, and left before Nettle could work out what to say to that.", next: "forge" },
    comp3_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🍞", setMid: true, text: "Nettle's fraction came out wrong on the first go, a quarter instead of a sixteenth, and she caught her own mistake a breath too late to call it down clearly. Mother Halloway settled the matter herself before the correction reached her, portioning the saucer by eye the way she always had before any of this counting business started. No wire came up the stairs tonight. Nettle chalked the right working out properly for next time and let it be.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔨", text: "Nettle laid out what the night had brought her on the blanket: a clean scrap of lamp-glass, a dry piece of waxed paper if she'd managed to keep it whole, and a length of bent curtain-wire, and turned them over by candlelight, working out in her head how a person too poorly to leave her own bed might still build something worth having.", forged: "The wire bent easily round the glass to make a small frame, the waxed paper backing it soft enough not to scratch, and by the time she'd bound the corners with a scrap of thread the whole thing sat neat and light in her palm: a little hinged pane she could press flush to the cold window glass, holding the frost's true shape steady behind clear glass long enough to read it properly, breath or no breath. She turned it over once, pleased right down to her toes. Nettle's Tally-Pane, she decided it should be called, because it was hers and it told the truth.", notforged: "Two pieces out of three isn't nothing, Nettle told herself, turning what she had over in the candlelight, but a frame with no paper behind it, or no wire to hold it, was really just a piece of glass with plans. She wrapped the pieces carefully in her handkerchief and tucked them under the loose sill-board where the chalk stub had been, for a night when the lane had fewer urgent tugs on its post-line and she had steadier hands.", grantItem: "gf_nettle_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "❄️", text: "By the time the last of the night's fog cleared, properly cleared, the whole hundred and fifty counts of it, Ferrow Lane had gone quiet in the particular way it did just before the grey light came up. Nettle held her new little pane to the glass and watched the next frost-fern bloom clean and steady behind it, unsmudged by her own breath for the first time, five points, round in the middle, exactly as always. Somewhere below, Cobb's cart stood empty outside the last house on his round, Old Fenn's lamp burned small and gold against the grey, and on the step Tessa sat with three kittens asleep in her lap and their mother keeping a suspicious watch over all of them. Nettle thought, as she sometimes did at this hour, that Rooke would have liked seeing it laid out like that, all the small trouble sorted at once. She didn't know where Rooke was tonight. She hoped, wherever it was, there was a window.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end", goodText: "Word got round Ferrow Lane quietly, the way useful things do, that if your business needed timing against the Engine's knock, you sent word up to the Halloway girl at the window, the one with the little glass pane and the chalk stub and the cough that never quite let her go far. Cobb started tapping his cart-wheel twice on the cobbles before every round, a small salute she couldn't hear but liked to imagine anyway. Mother Halloway never did tell her off for the loose sill-board, or the wire gone missing from the mending basket, and once, just once, she left an extra sliver of bread on Nettle's plate without a word about fractions at all. Nettle slept that night with the tally-pane on the pillow beside her, and dreamed, for once, of nothing needing counting.", midText: "It was a scrappier night than Nettle would have liked, a torn scrap of paper here, a mistaken fraction there, and the little tally-pane that came out of it held together with more hope than wire in places. But Cobb got home dry, Old Fenn's lamp got lit eventually, and three kittens slept safe in Tessa's lap by the time the fog properly cleared, which was really the whole of what the night had asked of her. Nettle tucked the unfinished pane under the loose sill-board for mending another evening, pulled her blanket up over the cough that hadn't quite let go yet, and told herself, not unreasonably, that a pattern half-caught was still more than no pattern at all.", grantItem: "gf_nettle_item2" },
  },
},

gf_higgins: {
  id: "gf_higgins",
  title: "The Seven and a Half Rule",
  tagline: "Sump Gate Seven vents four times a day, on the tick, without exception. Corporal Higgins has never once needed to wonder what an exception would look like.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_higgins_item1", name: "The Watchman's Count-Whistle", stat: 4, how: "found", desc: "+1 Speed. Won with a sharp Speed check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_higgins_item2", name: "The Amended Logbook", stat: 2, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_higgins_item3", name: "The Reckoning Chronometer", stat: 0, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { sump_glass: "Sump-Glass Lens", verge_spring: "Verge Spring", tally_chain: "Tally Chain" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "Sump Gate Seven sat where Tallow Street stopped pretending the Rise and the Under were the same city, and admitted it outright. Oxblood brick above the seam, soot brick below it, and between the two a grate the size of a dining table, bolted into the road like something that expected trouble and intended to be ready for it.\n\nCorporal Higgins had stood this post for six years. In six years he had never once been late for the click.\n\nThe click came first, a knock felt up through the boots, the Turn clearing its throat before it spoke. Then the count of forty, while the fog gathered itself in the culvert below. Then the seal, both grates down, nobody crossing for love, money or a superior's writ. Then the long count, one hundred and fifty, while the fog got on with its scalding, iron-eating business and thought better of coming up any further. Then the reopening, and the docket check, and the next lawful soul across the seam.\n\nIt was written down. It had always been written down. Higgins had read the Vent Order so many times he could recite it walking backwards in the dark, which he had in fact done once, for a bet he did not remember losing, to a colleague he did not much like.\n\n'Correct in every particular,' his old training sergeant used to say, tapping the card, 'and you will never once have to decide anything for yourself.' Higgins had found this the single most restful sentence in the language, and had built six years of a career on it.\n\nThis particular morning the eight o'clock queue for the boiler sheds was backed up past the second bollard, because the Rise-side clatterlift had thrown a cable in the night, and eight o'clock was also klaxon time, and the crowd did not know that yet. Which was, in its entirety, Higgins's job to fix before the click.", next: "count_check" },
    count_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "⏱️", stat: 4, dc: 12,
      text: "Forty workers to a lane and a click already ringing up through the cobbles. Higgins had exactly as long as the count of forty to clear the crowd behind the yellow line, and the counting had to be exact, because 'about forty' is a fine number for a poem and a hanging offence for a gatekeeper.\n\nHe called it aloud, the way the manual said to, one number to a step, the crowd shuffling back on his voice like a tide that had finally met somebody more stubborn than it was.",
      success: "whistle_found", fail: "count_miss" },
    whistle_found: { type: "story", emoji: "📯", grantItem: "gf_higgins_item1", text: "The count landed on thirty-nine, the lane cleared with a full second to spare, and the grate housing gave a small metallic cough as the seal dropped. Something rattled loose inside it and skittered to Higgins's boot.\n\nA whistle. Brass, dented, the cord long since rotted away, with DOONE scratched into the barrel in a hand that had clearly done it in a hurry, forty years before Higgins was born. Old Gatekeeper Doone had stood this exact post once, by the look of it, and had also, at some point, stopped needing the whistle.\n\nHiggins blew it once, out of professional curiosity. It worked perfectly. He put it on his own cord and did not consider the matter further, which was the correct amount of consideration for a Wednesday.\n\nThe Watchman's Count-Whistle is yours. (+1 Speed, kept forever.)", next: "gate_fight" },
    count_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The count came apart at thirty-one, when a boiler-shed foreman tried to push one last apprentice through the yellow line for luck. Higgins put a hand flat on the man's chest, which was the entire argument, and the lane cleared two seconds late and considerably less politely than usual.\n\nThe seal dropped anyway, in time, because the seal did not care how the count had felt about it. Whatever had been rattling loose in the grate housing stayed lost in there for another day.", next: "gate_fight" },
    gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🪨", qLevel: 2,
      text: "Something in the sealed culvert did not want to stay sealed. A lump of hardened slag and old fog, about the size of a coal sack, had wedged itself sideways in the outfall throat, and the pressure building behind it was making the whole grate hum a note Higgins did not care for.\n\nThe manual had a name for these. A Clinker: cooled fog and grit, fused by heat into something that could think just enough to be stubborn about doorways. Left alone it would either blow the seal early or crack the grate outright, and neither of those was going to happen on Higgins's watch, on Higgins's grate, on a Wednesday.\n\n'Move along,' said Higgins, in the flat tone the manual recommended for uncooperative masonry. The Clinker did not move along. The Clinker glowed a shade angrier and pushed.",
      enemy: { name: "The Grate Clinker", emoji: "🪨", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "The answer struck home, and a fist-sized chunk of the Clinker sheared off with a sound like a kettle giving up an argument.",
        strikeText: "The Clinker vented a gout of scalding fog through the grate bars, and somebody in the party found out exactly what the manual meant by 'corrosive'.",
        defeatText: "The Clinker cracked down the middle and slumped into ordinary slag, all its stubbornness gone out of it at once. The grate stopped humming that note. Higgins made a note of his own, in the log, in block capitals: CULVERT THROAT, CLEAR." },
      victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The Clinker's remains were swept into a bucket for the cinder cart, and the grate went back to humming its usual, entirely acceptable hum.\n\n'Third one this month,' said Higgins, to nobody, because there was nobody else on duty to say it to. Third Clinker, third grate, third little fault the Turn hadn't quite managed to correct on its own. It always managed the first ninety-nine steps by itself, the tinkers said. It was the hundredth step it couldn't do without a person going first. Higgins found this a reasonable division of labour and did not lose any sleep over which of the two of them was doing the dangerous part.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The Clinker got the better of the exchange, vented a last spiteful gust through the bars, and Higgins pulled the party back to the yellow line to reconsider the approach with slightly less exposed skin.\n\nThey went round by the footbridge instead, which added the better part of an hour and a great deal of standing in a queue that had opinions about queue-jumpers. Behind them the grate kept humming its unhappy note, and would go on doing so until somebody came back to finish the job properly.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🗺️", text: "The morning's real trouble, once the Clinker was dealt with, turned out to be entirely human, and arrived in triplicate.\n\nAt the head of the boiler-shed queue, foreman Crick was doing arithmetic under his breath and not liking the answer. Further up the lane, a Rise widow by the name of Anstruther was explaining, at volume, exactly whose grate this was going to stop being if somebody didn't move faster. And down by the old culvert stile, a Laachmi courier stood very still, the way people stand when they have noticed they are being followed and are hoping it isn't true.\n\nSomewhere in that lot, tinkers' gossip said, a spare Sump-Glass Lens was going to change hands today. Higgins couldn't be in three places, so he went where the day sent him first.", options: [
      { label: "Sort out foreman Crick's queue", next: "queue_setup", preview: "A rate-and-remainder puzzle about clearing the boiler-shed queue before the klaxon. Guards a Sump-Glass Lens." },
      { label: "Deal with Widow Anstruther's demand", next: "wrought_setup", preview: "A judgement call on the override valve, weighed as a probability. Guards a Sump-Glass Lens." },
      { label: "Check on the Laachmi courier", next: "courier_setup", preview: "Somebody is stalking the courier for her docket. A fight guards a Sump-Glass Lens." },
    ] },
    queue_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "👥", text: "Foreman Crick had ninety-five boiler-shed hands queued behind the yellow line and a clatterlift that wasn't coming, which meant every one of them had to go through Higgins's grate on foot, in groups, dockets checked, before the klaxon.\n\n'I've counted it four times,' Crick said, showing Higgins a slate covered in crossings-out. 'I keep getting a different number of poor souls left standing in the cold, and none of the numbers is zero.'\n\nHiggins took the slate. Ten counts to unlock the gate mechanism properly, always. Groups of five through the docket desk, eight counts a group, no faster, because faster is how dockets get missed and missed dockets are how the wrong people end up on the wrong side of a fog vent. One hundred and fifty counts until the klaxon. It was, Higgins thought, quite a tidy little problem, for a Wednesday.", next: "queue_puzzle" },
    queue_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧮", text: "'Work it out properly and I'll believe you,' said Crick, handing over the slate and a stub of chalk. 'Work it out wrong and I'll still believe you, because you're wearing the coat. But I'd rather you were right.'", question: "The gate mechanism needs 10 counts to unlock before anyone can pass. After that, workers cross in groups of 5, and each group takes 8 counts at the docket desk. The klaxon sounds in 150 counts. If 95 workers are queued, how many are still waiting when the klaxon sounds?", options: ["0", "5", "10", "15", "20"], answer: 2,
      hint: "First take away the 10 counts spent unlocking the gate. Then work out how many complete groups of 8 counts fit into what's left, and how many workers that clears.", solution: "After the 10-count unlock, there are 150 − 10 = 140 counts left for processing. Each group of 5 workers takes 8 counts, and 17 × 8 = 136 counts, which fits inside 140 (18 groups would need 144, which doesn't fit). So 17 groups get through, clearing 17 × 5 = 85 workers. That leaves 95 − 85 = 10 workers still queued when the klaxon sounds.",
      good: "queue_good", mid: "queue_mid" },
    queue_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "sump_glass", text: "Higgins ran the sums exactly as worked, called the last safe group forward with eleven counts still in hand, and had the remaining ten workers stood back behind the line before the klaxon so much as cleared its throat.\n\nCrick, in the sort of gratitude that embarrasses both parties, pressed something wrapped in oilcloth into Higgins's hand. 'Old Doone's kit,' he said. 'Cleared out the boiler-shed store last spring, found this, didn't know who else it'd fit better.' Inside was a Sump-Glass Lens, ground thick and clear, exactly the sort of thing a careful man keeps for looking through fog and seeing what's actually there.\n\nComponent found: Sump-Glass Lens.", next: "converge1" },
    queue_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📋", setMid: true, text: "Higgins's first working put eighteen groups through the gate, which felt right and was wrong, because eighteen groups need a hundred and forty-four counts and there were only a hundred and forty going spare. The klaxon caught the eighteenth group half-processed, and the desk clerk had to slam the docket book shut on a very undignified scramble.\n\n'Close enough,' said Crick, who did not mean it as a compliment and was not offering one. Ten workers went home in the cold to try again tomorrow, and the oilcloth parcel in the boiler-shed store stayed exactly where it was, unclaimed and unmentioned.", next: "converge1" },
    wrought_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "Widow Anstruther had a writ appointment on the Rise side, a lawyer who charged by the quarter-hour, and absolutely no patience for a grate that thought it outranked her.\n\n'There is an override valve,' she said, tapping the manual's cover with one gloved finger, as though she had written it herself. 'I have seen it used. My late husband had it used for him twice.' Which was true, and also, Higgins reflected, exactly the sort of true that got people killed eventually, because a valve used twice safely is not a valve proven safe, it is a valve that has been lucky twice.\n\nThe override cracked the seal ten counts early, venting the last of the built-up fog through a side stack instead of the main one, provided the residual pressure had already dropped low enough. If it hadn't, the side stack gave everyone nearby a very bad afternoon. Higgins had never once needed to make this particular call. He supposed a Wednesday had to be the first time for something.", next: "wrought_dice" },
    wrought_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 4, text: "There was no gauge for residual pressure that told you plainly. There was only the manual's rule of thumb, a listening tube pressed to the side stack, and a roll of the dice against how the morning's fog had actually behaved, which the manual admitted, in small print, varied. Roll 4 or more on a six-sided die to judge the pressure correctly and crack the valve clean.", predict: { question: "Before you decide: what is the probability of rolling 4 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/3", "1/2", "2/3"], answer: 2, why: "The rolls 4, 5 and 6 succeed, which is 3 outcomes out of 6, and 3/6 = 1/2. An even bet, no better, which is exactly why the manual leaves it to a rule of thumb and not a promise. Judging the odds correctly steadies your hand: +1 to your roll." },
      success: "wrought_good", fail: "wrought_mid" },
    wrought_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "sump_glass", text: "The listening tube hummed low and even, exactly the note the manual described as safe, and Higgins cracked the valve with a satisfying clunk. The side stack took the last of the fog quietly, Widow Anstruther swept through eight minutes ahead of her lawyer's clock, and did not thank Higgins, which he had not particularly expected.\n\nWhat she did do, on her way through, was drop a small case from her late husband's coat pocket into Higgins's hand. 'He kept this by the valve, always,' she said, already walking. 'Man who invented rules for himself. You'll know what to do with it.' Inside, wrapped in felt, sat a second Sump-Glass Lens.\n\nComponent found: Sump-Glass Lens.", next: "converge1" },
    wrought_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🗞️", setMid: true, text: "The tube hummed uneven, a note the manual filed under 'wait', and the side stack, cracked anyway, gave back a short ugly cough of fog that singed the paintwork and everyone's tempers in equal measure.\n\nWidow Anstruther got through eventually, ten minutes later than promised and considerably less gracious about it, having watched the whole business from a safe distance with her handkerchief over her nose. Whatever her late husband had kept by the valve stayed in her coat pocket, and she did not offer to show it to anyone who had just proved they might drop it.", next: "converge1" },
    courier_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🧳", text: "The Laachmi courier by the culvert stile gave her name, reluctantly, as Ondine Rell, and her business, more reluctantly still, as carrying a sealed docket for the Turn's overseers that she would very much like to still be carrying in five minutes.\n\n'There's a man been matching my pace since the Tallow Street corner,' she said, not looking round, in the disciplined way of someone who has been taught exactly how to watch a threat without watching it. 'Ordinary coat. Extraordinary interest in exactly what I'm holding.'\n\nHiggins had seen the type before. Docket forgers didn't usually want your docket to keep. They wanted ten quiet minutes with it, a steady hand and a very good pen, and to hand you back something that looked identical and read completely differently three signatures down. It was the sort of small, patient theft that never made the broadsheets, and ruined exactly the sort of person nobody important went looking for.", next: "courier_fight" },
    courier_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🗂️", qLevel: 2,
      text: "The ordinary coat stopped being ordinary the moment it produced a knife with a very unordinary point, angled not at Ondine but at the satchel strap over her shoulder. Cut the strap, take the bag, and be three streets gone before anyone noticed it wasn't a mugging.\n\n'Fenno Grale,' the man said, by way of introduction, as if the name should mean something. It meant, to Higgins, exactly one thing: a name three lines down on a list of persons the Under watch would very much like a quiet word with. 'Off duty, are we, Corporal? Wonderful timing.'\n\n'Never off duty at my own gate,' said Higgins, which was, strictly, in the manual.",
      enemy: { name: "The Docket-Snatcher", emoji: "🗂️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "The answer landed square, and Grale's knife hand jerked wide, the strap uncut.",
        strikeText: "Grale feinted low and got a boot into somebody's shin, buying himself half a step of the room he'd wanted all along.",
        defeatText: "Grale went down under his own tangled feet as much as anything, dropped the knife, and put both hands up with the weary speed of a man who had done this calculation before and knew exactly how it ended." },
      victory: "courier_good", retreat: "courier_mid" },
    courier_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "sump_glass", text: "Grale went into custody still complaining, mostly about the unfairness of a gate officer being armed with arithmetic on his own doorstep, which Higgins felt was rather the point of the doorstep.\n\nOndine Rell checked her satchel twice, found the seal unbroken, and allowed herself exactly one breath of relief before her professional face came back down like a shutter. 'You'll want this for the trouble,' she said, and pressed a lens into his palm, thick glass in a plain brass ring. 'Overseers issue them to couriers who work the fog routes. I've a spare. You clearly work a fog route too.'\n\nComponent found: Sump-Glass Lens.", next: "converge1" },
    courier_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", text: "Grale was quicker than he looked, cut wide of the fight entirely, and vanished down a soot alley with the satchel strap half severed and Ondine's shoulder badly wrenched from holding on regardless.\n\nThe docket itself, thank the Turn, was sewn into her coat lining and not the satchel, which Grale would discover with some irritation once he'd emptied his prize of nothing but a spare pair of gloves and a cold meat sandwich. Ondine did not offer Higgins anything for the trouble. She had, after all, kept the only thing that mattered by not trusting the satchel in the first place.", next: "converge1" },
    converge1: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "The three small fires of the morning were out, one way or another, and Higgins logged each of them in his careful block capitals, because a thing that isn't written down, in his considered professional opinion, hasn't actually finished happening yet.\n\nHe noticed, filling in the log, that a young Rise officer named Corvain had signed the seam-gate register twice already that month, asking pointed questions about seal timings that weren't, strictly, his patch. Officers didn't usually spend their afternoons on other men's grates. Higgins made a note of the pattern and no further comment, on the grounds that noticing things was his job and having opinions about them was somebody else's.\n\nWhat concerned him rather more was the grate mechanism itself, which had been sticking on its return stroke since the Clinker jammed it, and a sticking gate on Sump Seven, on a day when everything else had already gone slightly sideways, was not a coincidence Higgins felt inclined to trust.", next: "spring_puzzle" },
    spring_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚙️", text: "The maintenance ledger for Gate Seven's mechanism went back eleven years, in four different hands, and it recorded exactly what Higgins needed and nothing he wanted: two old readings of the escapement's advance rate at two different tension settings, and no reading at all for the setting the gate needed now.\n\nAt tension 4, the ledger said, the arm advanced 52 counts a minute. At tension 7, it advanced 88 counts a minute. The relationship, the old engineer's note insisted, was perfectly straight-line, tension to rate, no surprises. Gate Seven needed exactly 100 counts a minute to seal on schedule.", question: "The gate arm's advance rate is a linear function of the tension setting. At tension 4 the rate is 52 counts per minute, and at tension 7 the rate is 88 counts per minute. What tension setting gives a rate of exactly 100 counts per minute?", options: ["6", "7", "8", "9", "10"], answer: 2,
      hint: "Work out how much the rate changes for each single step of tension between the two known readings, then use that to build an equation for the rate at any tension.", solution: "Between tension 4 and tension 7, the rate rises from 52 to 88, a change of 36 over 3 tension units, which is 12 counts per minute for every extra unit of tension. So the rate at tension t is 12(t − 4) + 52 = 12t + 4. Setting 12t + 4 = 100 gives 12t = 96, so t = 8.",
      good: "spring_good", mid: "spring_mid" },
    spring_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "verge_spring", text: "Higgins set the tension dial to eight, exactly, and the arm's stutter smoothed out into the same patient tick it had kept for eleven years of engineers before him.\n\nThe old spring came out clean in his hand once the new setting had taken the load off it, a verge spring worn thin but not yet worn out, the kind of part a careful man keeps rather than scraps, on the grounds that a spring that has kept honest time for eleven years has earned a second job somewhere.\n\nComponent found: Verge Spring.", next: "chain_check" },
    spring_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", setMid: true, text: "Higgins tried tension seven first, on the reasonable but wrong assumption that the relationship curved rather than ran straight, and the arm advanced at eighty-eight when it needed a hundred, which meant a slow gate and a slow gate on Sump Seven meant a longer window of fog than the Under side had any reason to expect.\n\nHe corrected the dial by evening, working it out properly the second time, but the old spring had already been wrung past its patience by the wrong setting and came out in two pieces rather than one, fit for nothing but the scrap tin.", next: "chain_check" },
    chain_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "⛓️", stat: 2, dc: 13,
      text: "The equipment store behind the gatehouse held nine tally chains on a single hook, every one of them near identical, and exactly one of them calibrated to mark Gate Seven's counts in true tens up to a hundred and fifty. The other eight belonged to other gates entirely, with other rhythms, and would seal Gate Seven's mechanism a shade fast or a shade slow depending on which liar's chain you trusted.\n\nEach chain's notches told a story if you were patient enough to read it: wear patterns from years of the same count, over and over, worn deeper at the tens than the ones and deeper still at the fifties, where a gate's cycle habitually pauses to check itself. Somebody with a proper eye for that sort of pattern could tell Gate Seven's true chain from the other eight without a single label.",
      success: "chain_good", fail: "chain_mid" },
    chain_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "tally_chain", text: "Higgins worked it out from the wear alone, the way he worked out most things, by refusing to be in a hurry about it. The fourth chain along had its deepest wear at forty and one hundred and fifty, precisely where Gate Seven's cycle paused to think, and none of the others matched that rhythm at all.\n\nHe unhooked it, coiled it properly and pocketed it, leaving a note pinned to the hook for whichever colleague came looking, because a man who finds the right chain and doesn't say so has only solved half the problem.\n\nComponent found: Tally Chain.", next: "forge" },
    chain_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "Higgins picked the sixth chain along, whose wear pattern looked close enough at a glance, and did not discover his mistake until the gate mechanism ran eleven counts fast on the next cycle, sealing four seconds before the crowd behind the line had actually cleared.\n\nNobody was hurt. Four seconds is not much of a margin to lose, but it is not nothing either, and Higgins filed an incident note about it that was, in every particular, entirely correct and entirely too late to matter to the people who'd felt those four seconds happen to them.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "The gatehouse workshop belonged to old Instrument-Sergeant Vail, who had fitted more gauges and dials into more brass housings than any three younger men combined, and who looked at Higgins's collected parts with the particular satisfaction of someone about to be usefully occupied.\n\n'A Reckoning Chronometer, is it,' Vail said, turning the lens, the spring and the chain over on the bench one at a time. 'Haven't built one since the old Vent Order was first drawn up. Lens, spring, chain, all three, and I'll set you something that counts truer than any man's mouth.'",
      forged: "The workshop forge ran hot and steady while Vail set the Sump-Glass Lens into a brass face, wound the Verge Spring into a housing ground smooth as a promise, and hung the whole mechanism from the Tally Chain so it could sit against a watchman's coat and be trusted at a glance.\n\nThe Reckoning Chronometer read counts the way Higgins had always wished the world would: exactly, immediately, and without a single opinion about the matter.\n\nThe Reckoning Chronometer is yours! (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)",
      notforged: "Vail laid the parts out and counted them twice, which did not take long, because there were not very many of them.\n\n'Short a piece or two,' Vail said, without unkindness, nudging the gaps into a neat row. 'A chronometer wants the full set, or it's just a good-looking brass box that tells you nothing you didn't already suspect. Come back with what's missing. The forge keeps its own hours, same as everything else round here.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
      grantItem: "gf_higgins_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "It was Higgins's own count that caught her, not the manual's: a Rustbound girl of perhaps twelve, standing dead still at the yellow line exactly as the klaxon sounded, a chemist's chit clutched in one fist gone white at the knuckle.\n\n'Please,' she said, in the flat voice of somebody who has already been told no several times today and is saving her tears for later, on the grounds that they wouldn't help now. 'My mother's chest. The only draught that'll shift it is at the Rise apothecary, and the counter shuts the minute the fog clears, and the fog won't clear for a hundred and fifty counts, and by then—' She stopped, because the arithmetic finished the sentence for her, the way arithmetic does, without any interest in how it landed.\n\nThe Vent Order was quite clear. The lane was sealed. Nobody crosses for love, money or a superior's writ, and Higgins had built six years on that sentence being the whole of the job.\n\nBut Higgins had also spent the last several hours with his nose in Gate Seven's mechanism, and a man who has just relearned every joint of a thing sometimes remembers, entirely by accident, a fact that isn't strictly his to know. The old culvert stile past the courier's post, the one nobody used for foot traffic any more, had never been renumbered when the two lanes merged forty years back. On paper, in the Vent Order, in the only document that mattered, it was still Gate Seven and a Half, fed by a smaller secondary valve, venting on its own offset schedule roughly twenty-five counts behind the main stack. And the Vent Order, read exactly as written, forbade crossing THE LANE. It said nothing whatsoever about a lane it didn't know existed.\n\nHiggins looked at the chit. He looked at the girl. He looked, for slightly longer than strictly necessary, at absolutely nothing at all, which was, for once, doing quite a lot of work.\n\n'What's your name,' he said, already moving.\n\n'Tansy Vail,' she said, half a step behind him and closing the gap fast.\n\n'Any relation to the Instrument-Sergeant?'\n\n'My great-uncle.'\n\n'Convenient,' said Higgins, in a tone that was almost, but not quite, dry. 'Keep up. We've got about a hundred and twenty counts before the second valve fires, and I intend to use every one of them counting, not talking.'", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end",
      goodText: "They made the apothecary counter with eleven counts to spare, Tansy's chit stamped, the draught paid for out of a coin purse the apothecary clearly kept for exactly this sort of morning, and Higgins escorted her back through the same unlisted culvert before its own valve so much as thought about firing.\n\nHe did not mention it to anyone that evening. What he did do was open the logbook, in his own careful block capitals, and add a line that nobody had asked him to add and the manual did not require: SUMP GATE SEVEN AND A HALF, OFFSET SCHEDULE, UNLISTED. FOR THE NEXT MAN'S BENEFIT.\n\nIt was not a rebellion. Higgins would have been genuinely offended by the suggestion. It was, as far as he was concerned, simply a correction to an incomplete record, made by a man who happened to notice the record was incomplete, on a Wednesday when noticing had, for once, mattered rather a lot.",
      midText: "They made the apothecary counter with barely a handful of counts to spare, breathless and singed at the cuffs from a morning that had already asked more of the mechanism, and of Higgins, than a Wednesday usually did. The draught got bought. The chit got stamped. Nobody official ever found out quite how.\n\nHiggins added his line to the logbook regardless, a little unsteadier in the hand than usual, a little later than he'd have liked: SUMP GATE SEVEN AND A HALF, OFFSET SCHEDULE, UNLISTED. FOR THE NEXT MAN'S BENEFIT. It had been a rough morning by any measure. He found, filing the entry away, that he minded rather less than he'd have expected to about the parts of it that hadn't gone entirely by the book.",
      grantItem: "gf_higgins_item2" },
  },
},

gf_marta: {
  id: "gf_marta",
  title: "The Quarter-Day Reckoning",
  tagline: "Four marks don't lose themselves. Someone put them there, and Marta means to find out who.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_marta_item1", name: "The Notched Rule", stat: 0, how: "found", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won with a sharp Arithmetic check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_marta_item2", name: "A Lighter Hand at the Till", stat: 4, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Speed. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_marta_item3", name: "The Reconciled Ledger", stat: 2, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Logic. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { gf_marta_comp_page: "The Corrected Page", gf_marta_comp_ratio: "The Split Reckoning", gf_marta_comp_carry: "The Uncarried Figure" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🧾",
      text: "The chalk-book balanced every quarter-day for eleven years, and then, on a Tuesday that smelled of the Engine's fourth venting, it did not. Marta found the gap by candlelight: four marks unaccounted for in a column she had checked twice already, which meant either her arithmetic had failed her for the first time in her life, or somebody had been busy. She did not believe the first explanation. She rarely did. Outside, the outfall grate on Ferrow Lane coughed its scalding fog into the gutter and the whole street held its breath until the knock came, felt through the soles of every boot at once, the way it always did. Marta shut the book, latched it, and thought carefully about who had been standing near the counter on collection days.",
      next: "skillcheck1",
    },
    skillcheck1: {
      type: "skillcheck",
      stat: 0,
      dc: 12,
      emoji: "🕯️",
      text: "She went back through every entry since the last reconciling, matching docket to docket by lamplight while the queue outside grew restless and Mrs Ochre rapped her knuckles on the counter for effect. Four marks does not sound like much until you understand that four marks is a family's bread for two days, and that somebody in this street is going without because of it. Marta set her jaw and started at the top of the page.",
      success: "skillcheck1_success",
      fail: "skillcheck1_fail",
    },
    skillcheck1_success: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🧮",
      grantItem: "gf_marta_item1",
      text: "There, three weeks back: a half-mark rounded up instead of down, small enough to hide, big enough to matter over a season. Whoever had done it either did not understand compounding, or understood it exactly. Marta pocketed the offending page as evidence and, from the drawer beneath the till, took out the brass rule her old master had left her, notched at every half-mark so that nobody, including her, could round in the wrong direction by accident again. She weighed it in her hand and felt steadier for it.",
      next: "afterskill",
    },
    skillcheck1_fail: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🧮",
      text: "She found nothing wrong in the sums themselves, which unsettled her more than an error would have. It meant the fault was not in her arithmetic but in something the arithmetic couldn't see: a docket that did not match the goods it claimed, or a hand she had not thought to doubt. She rubbed the ache from between her eyes, put the ledger away for the queue's sake, and told herself she would find it before the reckoning.",
      next: "afterskill",
    },
    afterskill: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🚪",
      text: "The bell above the door coughed, a Rise habit fitted specially, since the Under's own doorbells had all been requisitioned for scrap two winters back, and in came a man Marta had been half expecting since the discrepancy first showed itself: a clerk from Old Custom and Sons, debt-factors of good repute on the Rise and no repute at all on Ferrow Lane. He wore a coat the colour of unpaid rent and carried a folder tied in green ribbon, which in her experience was never a good sign, ribbon being cheaper than honesty and rather more common. 'Notice of inspection,' he said, before she had finished serving the woman in front of him. 'Quarterly reckoning. We hear your book don't balance, Mistress Marta.'",
      next: "gate_combat",
    },
    gate_combat: {
      type: "combat",
      qLevel: 2,
      emoji: "⚖️",
      enemy: { name: "Clerk Fessup", emoji: "🎩", hits: 2, toHit: 8, hitText: "Fessup taps his ribboned folder and quotes a clause at you that may or may not exist.", strikeText: "You answer him sum for sum and watch the smugness leave his face by degrees.", defeatText: "Fessup's argument collapses the way bad arithmetic always does, all at once and with no dignity left over." },
      text: "He did not shout. Men like Fessup never needed to. He laid his folder on her counter, untied the ribbon with theatrical patience, and began reading out clauses about 'unsound accounts' and 'provisional receivership' in a voice built for exactly this. Every street he had ever visited with that folder had ended up owing Old Custom and Sons considerably more than it had started the morning owing anyone. Marta had one quarter-day to prove her book was sound, and Fessup intended to make that as difficult as possible before he left.",
      victory: "gate_victory",
      retreat: "gate_retreat",
    },
    gate_victory: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🎩",
      text: "She met every clause with a sum of her own, faster and cleaner, until Fessup ran out of borrowed words and had to fall back on his own, which were considerably less impressive. He retied his ribbon with the particular stiffness of a man who has just been out-argued by a grocer and does not intend to admit it. 'Quarter-day,' he said again, as if repeating it made it more his idea. 'We'll be back.' He left without buying anything, which Marta counted as a second small victory.",
      next: "crossroads",
    },
    gate_retreat: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🎩",
      text: "She could not spare the time to win the argument outright, not with three customers waiting and the fog due within the hour, so she let him have the last clause and simply agreed to have her book ready by quarter-day. Fessup seemed satisfied with that, which told her rather more about his actual instructions than anything he had read aloud. He left the ribbon's mark pressed into her counter like a bruise. She had a quarter-day now, and a great deal to do in it.",
      next: "crossroads",
    },
    crossroads: {
      type: "choice",
      emoji: "🗺️",
      text: "Four marks does not lose itself. It goes somewhere, or someone puts it somewhere, and either way there was a short list of places to look before quarter-day: Dill Corrigan's account, which had looked odd to her for a month; the delivery register, which the carter filled in by lamplight and the eye of faith; and young Perren, the shop-boy, who had been standing rather too close to the till on collection days for a boy who claimed no interest in numbers.",
      options: [
        { label: "Go over Dill Corrigan's chalk, line by line", next: "routeA_setup", preview: "A discount, and the order it was applied in." },
        { label: "Check the delivery register against the scale", next: "routeB_setup", preview: "Trust the numbers, or trust the die." },
        { label: "Ask Perren directly what he's been doing near the till", next: "routeC_setup", preview: "A conversation that may not stay friendly." },
      ],
    },
    routeA_setup: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "📖",
      text: "Dill Corrigan wove cloth for half the Under and owed Marta for most of the thread that went into it, paid down slowly on the quarter-day terms she gave anyone steady enough to trust with them: one-eighth off the standing total if it was cleared within the quarter. He came in looking like a man who had already lost an argument with himself. 'Your book says I owe more than I make sense of,' he said, and put his own tally slate on the counter, which was, Marta noted, the single most useful thing any customer had done for her all month.",
      next: "routeA_puzzle",
    },
    routeA_puzzle: {
      type: "puzzle",
      emoji: "🧮",
      text: "Marta laid Dill's tally slate next to her own ledger and worked through his terms out loud, so he could follow every step of it himself.",
      question: "Dill's chalk stood at 240 marks before any discount. Marta's quarter-day terms take one-eighth off for anyone who clears within the quarter, and Dill cleared his in time. He then bought one more loaf on credit, adding 15 marks to what he owed. What should his new chalk total read?",
      options: ["195 marks", "210 marks", "225 marks", "240 marks"],
      answer: 2,
      hint: "Take one-eighth off the original 240 marks first, then add the 15-mark loaf afterwards, in that order.",
      solution: "One-eighth of 240 is 30, so the discount brings the chalk down to 240 minus 30, which is 210. Adding the 15-mark loaf gives 210 plus 15, which is 225 marks.",
      good: "routeA_good",
      mid: "routeA_mid",
    },
    routeA_good: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🧮",
      text: "225 marks, and not the 240 the ledger under Marta's own hand currently showed. She turned the page round so Dill could watch her work it, and he did, visibly relieved to find the fault was not in his memory. Somebody had recorded his loaf before applying his discount instead of after, which meant that every steady customer paying down a chalk on quarter-day terms had been quietly overcharged the moment they bought anything after their discount fell due. It was not much on any single page. Across a whole street, over a whole quarter, it was considerably more than four marks. She thanked Dill, corrected his account on the spot, and kept the page.",
      next: "route_converge",
    },
    routeA_mid: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🧮",
      text: "She talked it through with Dill twice and still came out somewhere she wasn't confident in, which was not a feeling Marta enjoyed having about her own arithmetic. She corrected his account to the figure that felt soundest, apologised for the trouble, and made a note to come back to the order-of-operations question when she had a clearer head and fewer customers watching her think.",
      next: "route_converge",
    },
    routeB_setup: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🐎",
      text: "The carter, a broad silent woman named Ossa who never wrote a word she could avoid writing, filled in the delivery register each outfall-day from memory and a stub of chalk, then handed it over for Marta to make sense of. Six carts had come through this quarter and any one of them might have been miscounted at the weighbridge, over-counted at the door, or simply guessed at when the fog made the numbers on the sacks illegible. Marta could not check all six by quarter-day. She could check two, chosen fairly, and she needed the die to do it without anyone accusing her of picking on Ossa's least favourite customer.",
      next: "routeB_dice",
    },
    routeB_dice: {
      type: "dice",
      die: 6,
      target: 4,
      emoji: "🎲",
      predict: {
        question: "Marta rolls two six-sided dice to pick which two carts get checked, and she needs Cart Three flagged for at least one of them, since that's the cart Dill's missing loaf came in on. What is the probability that at least one of the two dice shows a 3?",
        options: ["1 in 6", "11 in 36", "1 in 3", "25 in 36"],
        answer: 1,
        why: "The chance neither die shows a 3 is five-sixths times five-sixths, which is 25 in 36. So the chance at least one die does show a 3 is 1 minus 25 in 36, which is 11 in 36.",
      },
      text: "She set the two dice on the counter, the way she always had for anything that needed deciding fairly rather than conveniently, and asked herself honestly what the odds were before she threw them.",
      success: "routeB_good",
      fail: "routeB_mid",
    },
    routeB_good: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🎲",
      text: "The dice obliged: Cart Three came up flagged, same as the numbers had wanted all along. Against the weighbridge slip, Ossa's tally for that delivery ran four sacks short of what the ledger claimed had come in, sacks that had apparently walked off somewhere between the outfall grate and the storeroom door. Ossa swore several times, none of them at Marta, and re-signed the register there and then with considerably more care than the first time. It was not the whole four marks, but it was a good part of it, and it was in writing.",
      next: "route_converge",
    },
    routeB_mid: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🎲",
      text: "Cart Three never came up. She checked the two carts the dice did give her anyway, out of thoroughness, and found both registers sound, which told her only that the fault, wherever it was, hadn't been generous enough to be caught by chance today. Ossa signed the register again regardless and went back out into the fog looking faintly insulted on the horses' behalf.",
      next: "route_converge",
    },
    routeC_setup: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🧒",
      text: "Perren had worked the counter on collection days for eight months and had, until recently, seemed entirely uninterested in the arithmetic of it, which was itself unusual for a boy sharp enough to make change without counting on his fingers. Marta had watched him linger near the till twice this month when he had no reason to. She sent the shop empty on a pretext, latched the door, and asked him straight out what he thought he was doing.",
      next: "routeC_combat",
    },
    routeC_combat: {
      type: "combat",
      qLevel: 2,
      emoji: "💢",
      enemy: { name: "Perren", emoji: "🧒", hits: 2, toHit: 8, hitText: "Perren swears he's done nothing, loudly enough to be heard in the street.", strikeText: "You lay the half-mark discrepancy on the counter between you and wait him out.", defeatText: "Perren runs out of denials at roughly the same moment he runs out of nerve." },
      text: "He denied it before she had finished the question, which told her most of what she needed to know, and kept denying it with increasing volume as she laid the ledger open between them and started asking him, quite calmly, to explain specific numbers on specific dates. A boy telling the truth generally slows down when you press him. A boy telling a lie generally speeds up.",
      victory: "routeC_good",
      retreat: "routeC_mid",
    },
    routeC_good: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🧒",
      text: "He cracked on the third date, not the first, which was almost admirable. He hadn't been skimming, not exactly: he'd been quietly rounding his own wages up by a half-mark here and there whenever he totted the till at close, on the theory that nobody checked a boy's arithmetic as carefully as they checked a customer's. He was right about that, which was rather the problem. Marta didn't shout. She made him write out, in his own hand, every entry he'd touched for the past month, told him the half-marks were coming out of his pay until the book balanced, and said she'd be checking his sums herself every night, for as long as it took her to trust him again.",
      next: "route_converge",
    },
    routeC_mid: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🧒",
      text: "He held his denial all the way to the door and out into the street, and Marta let him go rather than drag a scene out onto Ferrow Lane where every window would be watching by morning. She wasn't certain, only suspicious, and suspicion wasn't proof, whatever the Rise thought about people like her and people like him. She'd be watching the till more closely regardless. That would have to do for now.",
      next: "route_converge",
    },
    route_converge: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "📄",
      grantComponent: "gf_marta_comp_page",
      text: "Between Dill's discount, Ossa's missing sacks and whatever Perren had or hadn't been doing to his own wages, Marta had enough now to write a single clean page: every figure re-derived from first principles, every discount applied in the right order, every sack accounted for or honestly marked as missing. She called it, privately, the corrected page, and it was worth more to her than the till itself, because a book that could show its own working was a book nobody could easily call unsound. She still needed to account for the weights on the scale and the running total the clerk had quoted her, but this was a start, and a start with paper behind it.",
      next: "comp2_setup",
    },
    comp2_setup: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "⚖️",
      text: "The scale itself came next. If Old Custom and Sons meant to argue her weights were wrong as well as her sums, she wanted to know before they did. A joint delivery of coke had come in that quarter for two households sharing a cart to save on the carter's fee, and the split between them had been argued over at the counter for a full ten minutes before Marta had simply written down a number to make them both go home. She was no longer confident it was the right one.",
      next: "comp2_puzzle",
    },
    comp2_puzzle: {
      type: "puzzle",
      emoji: "🪙",
      text: "Marta pulled out the delivery note for the shared cart and set it flat on the counter, determined to get the split right this time rather than simply splitting it down the middle again.",
      question: "A cart of coke was shared between the Pallisers and Widow Tarn's boarding house, invoiced together as a single chalk of 54 marks. The Pallisers took twice as much coke as Widow Tarn did. How many of the 54 marks should sit on Widow Tarn's account?",
      options: ["12 marks", "18 marks", "27 marks", "36 marks"],
      answer: 1,
      hint: "A 2 to 1 ratio splits the total into three equal shares. Widow Tarn takes one of those shares.",
      solution: "The 2 to 1 ratio means the coke, and the cost, divides into three equal shares. 54 divided by 3 is 18 marks per share. Widow Tarn takes one share, so she owes 18 marks, and the Pallisers take two shares, so they owe 36 marks.",
      good: "comp2_good",
      mid: "comp2_mid",
    },
    comp2_good: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🪙",
      grantComponent: "gf_marta_comp_ratio",
      text: "Eighteen marks to Widow Tarn, thirty-six to the Pallisers, and not the even split of twenty-seven and twenty-seven Marta had scrawled down in a hurry that day to stop the arguing. She corrected both accounts, wrote the ratio out properly this time in figures nobody could dispute, and set the corrected split alongside the corrected page. It would not by itself satisfy a clerk from the Rise, but a book that mended its own mistakes when shown them was, in her considerable experience, the only kind worth trusting.",
      next: "comp3_setup",
    },
    comp2_mid: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🪙",
      text: "She talked herself round to an answer but not to a confident one, and rather than write down a figure she wasn't sure of twice in the same account, she left the even split standing for now, marked the page with a query in the margin, and told herself she'd settle it properly once quarter-day was behind her. It wasn't the cleanest choice she'd made all week. It also wasn't the worst.",
      next: "comp3_setup",
    },
    comp3_setup: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🕯️",
      text: "One thing remained: the actual figure Fessup had quoted at her, the running arrears he claimed her book showed building over six weeks, the number his whole 'unsound accounts' argument hung on. If she couldn't account for it exactly, ratio and rounding errors and light-fingered shop-boys would count for nothing at quarter-day. She lit a second candle, which she resented on principle, and pulled Tessa Brindle's fair copy of the ledger alongside her own to check them line for line.",
      next: "comp3_skillcheck",
    },
    comp3_skillcheck: {
      type: "skillcheck",
      stat: 0,
      dc: 13,
      emoji: "🔢",
      text: "Six weeks of entries, added twice, by two different hands on two different nights, and somewhere in that column Fessup had found a number worth building a buyout on. Marta ran her finger down the marks column the way she had a thousand times before, adding as she went, refusing to let herself hurry even with the candle burning down and the Engine's next venting already due.",
      success: "comp3_success",
      fail: "comp3_fail",
    },
    comp3_success: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🔢",
      grantComponent: "gf_marta_comp_carry",
      text: "There it was, three lines from the bottom: a nine and a one swapped in the carrying, nineteen written where ninety-one belonged, an old man's cramped hand and a young clerk's eagerness to finish a column making exactly the kind of error a debt-factor with a folder full of ribbon would be thrilled to find and never think to question. She corrected it, initialled it, and set the true figure beside Tessa's fair copy, which agreed with her to the mark. Two independent hands, the same number. That was worth more than any single sum, however carefully done.",
      next: "forge",
    },
    comp3_fail: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🔢",
      text: "She added the column four times and got a figure she trusted well enough, close to what Fessup had quoted but not identical to it, and could not for the life of her find where the difference lived. She copied out her own total, noted honestly where it departed from his, and decided that showing her working plainly would have to serve where certainty wouldn't. It was, she reflected, roughly the situation most of Ferrow Lane found itself in most of the time. She was no stranger to making do.",
      next: "forge",
    },
    forge: {
      type: "forge",
      emoji: "📜",
      text: "Quarter-day was hours off now, and Marta laid what she had on the counter by candlelight: the corrected discount, the corrected ratio, the corrected carry, if she had all three, or gaps where they should be, if she didn't.",
      forged: "She laid all three pages out on the counter by the last of the candlelight: the corrected discount, the corrected ratio, the corrected carry, each one initialled, dated and cross-checked against Tessa's fair copy. Together they did not just answer Fessup's question, they answered it better than he had asked it, showing exactly where every mark of the supposed arrears had gone and exactly who owed exactly what. She bound the three pages into the front of the ledger itself, ahead of the accounts proper, so that anyone who opened her book from now on would see the working before they saw a single balance. A book that showed its own mistakes and its own corrections was not, whatever Old Custom and Sons wanted to call it, unsound.",
      notforged: "She had the discount sorted and the carry sorted and a query mark still sitting over the coke split, which was not the clean sweep she'd wanted going into quarter-day but was, she told herself firmly, considerably more than she'd had a fortnight ago. She wrote up what she was sure of, flagged what she wasn't, and decided that an honest gap in a ledger had always been worth more to her than a confident lie. She would simply have to say so, plainly, to Fessup's face, and trust that plain honesty counted for something even on the Rise.",
      grantItem: "gf_marta_item3",
      next: "finale",
    },
    finale: {
      type: "story",
      emoji: "🌫️",
      text: "Fessup came back on quarter-day exactly as he'd promised, ribbon and all, and found a woman with a ledger open to its own working rather than its balance, which was evidently not what Old Custom and Sons trained its clerks to expect. He read her corrections twice, looking for the seam he was used to finding in books like hers, and did not find one, because there wasn't one left to find. He retied his ribbon for the second time that month with the air of a man doing sums of his own, about how much trouble a street this well-kept was actually worth to his employers, and left without a word about receivership. Outside, the Engine ticked its afternoon knock and vented its fog exactly on schedule, indifferent as ever to anyone's arithmetic, and the queue at Marta's counter reformed itself as if nothing had happened, because on Ferrow Lane nothing much ever did, until it very nearly did, and then somebody like Marta simply added the column again until it came right.",
      next: "end",
    },
    end: {
      type: "end",
      emoji: "🧮",
      goodText: "The corrected page went into every account on the street eventually, discount by discount, and Ferrow Lane's chalk carried on exactly as it always had, which is to say precariously, honestly, and entirely on Marta's own terms. Old Custom and Sons did not come back that year. When they did, the following winter, they found the same woman with the same rule notched at every half-mark, and considerably less patience for ribbon.",
      midText: "Ferrow Lane's chalk survived quarter-day, which was the main thing, even with a query mark still sitting over the coke split and a shop-boy paying down his half-marks in silence at the counter every evening. Marta kept the corrected page pinned inside the front cover where she could see it, less as a trophy than as a reminder that a book only stays sound for as long as somebody keeps checking it, and she had never once, in eleven years, stopped.",
      grantItem: "gf_marta_item2",
    },
  },
},

gf_jory: {
  id: "gf_jory",
  title: "The Wager of the Unmarked Chalk",
  tagline: "Jory Fenwick has never lost an argument he cared enough to finish.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_jory_item1", name: "The Steward's Loupe", stat: 2, how: "found", desc: "+1 Logic. Won with a sharp Logic check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_jory_item2", name: "A Fenwick Watch, Reset", stat: 4, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Speed. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_jory_item3", name: "The Unmarked Chalk", stat: 2, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Logic. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { chalk_stub: "Contention Chalk", borrowed_lemma: "The Borrowed Lemma", second_seat: "The Second's Seat" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "📜", text: "Jory Fenwick's proof came back from the Reckoning Hall with a single word chalked in the margin: FLAWED. Not WRONG, which he could have argued with. FLAWED, which meant somebody had read it all the way through.\n\nHe had proved, to his own satisfaction and his tutor's signature, that the sum of two odd numbers is always even. He had shown it for three and five. He had shown it for seven and nine. Both times the answer had come out even, obligingly, and Jory had considered the matter settled the way the Rise generally considered matters settled, which was to say without much further checking.\n\nThe clerk's desk at the foot of the Reckoning Hall stairs was not where he expected to find the person who had written FLAWED. It was smaller than his family's boot cupboard, and behind it sat a girl in a darned Under coat, copying figures into a ledger with the unhurried patience of someone who has never once been in a hurry on somebody else's behalf.", next: "dispute" },
    dispute: { type: "story", emoji: "🧾", text: "'You wrote this,' Jory said, laying the proof on her desk as though it might be contagious.\n\n'I read it,' said the clerk, not looking up. 'Lark Hollis. I check the disputed marks, the ones the tutors sign without finishing.' She turned the page towards him and tapped two lines with a pencil worn down to a stub. 'Three and five. Seven and nine. That's two sums, Mr Fenwick, not a proof. A proof has to hold for every odd number there is, not just the two you were bothered enough to try.'\n\n'It's obviously true for all of them.'\n\n'Obviously isn't a word that appears anywhere on your statute,' said Lark, 'and it certainly isn't one I'm paid to accept from a Wrought boy who tried two examples and got tired.' She said it without heat, which somehow made it worse. 'Write it general, or the mark stands.'", next: "skillcheck1" },
    skillcheck1: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "✏️", stat: 2, dc: 11,
      text: "Jory reached for his pencil, irritated to find his hand less steady than his opinion of himself. If two odd numbers were 2n+1 and 2m+1 for some whole numbers n and m, he needed their sum to come out even, in general, on the spot, in front of a clerk with a stub of pencil and no patience for showing off.",
      success: "sc1_success", fail: "sc1_fail" },
    sc1_success: { type: "story", emoji: "🔍", grantItem: "gf_jory_item1", text: "'2n+1 plus 2m+1,' Jory said slowly, writing as he spoke, 'is 2n+2m+2, which is 2 times the whole quantity n+m+1. Whatever n and m are, that's two times a whole number. Which is even. Always.' He looked up, faintly startled by his own working.\n\nLark studied it, unhurried, and gave the smallest possible nod, the kind that costs nothing and means something anyway. 'That's a proof,' she said. 'Pity it took a clerk's desk to get one out of you.' She slid a cracked brass loupe across the desk, the kind the Hall used for checking smudged workings line by line. 'Keep it. You'll want to read your own writing more carefully than you have been.'\n\nThe Steward's Loupe is yours! (+1 Logic, kept forever.)", next: "gate_fight" },
    sc1_fail: { type: "story", emoji: "😤", text: "Jory's pencil hovered over 2n+1 and produced, after a longer silence than he would ever admit to, a smear and a crossed-out line and a general proof that generalised nothing at all. He set the pencil down with more force than the desk had done anything to deserve.\n\n'The mark stands,' said Lark, not unkindly, which was its own kind of insult. 'Come back when you've done the working instead of the sulking.'", next: "gate_fight" },
    gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "⚖️", qLevel: 2,
      text: "Stung, Jory did the one thing a Fenwick could always do: he invoked statute. Any disputed mark, pressed far enough, became a formal Contention, tried before the Steward of Reckoning on the public slate in the Slate Court. He filed the challenge before he'd finished being angry about it, which is usually when people file the worst decisions of their lives.\n\nFirst, though, statute required a qualifying viva, set by the Steward's own clerk of entries, to prove the challenger wasn't wasting the Court's chalk.",
      enemy: { name: "The Clerk of Entries", emoji: "🕯️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Jory's answer landed clean and complete, and the clerk's tally marks ticked over without comment, which from a clerk of entries was practically applause.",
        strikeText: "Jory's answer wandered, hedged, and arrived at roughly the right place by roughly the wrong road, and the clerk noted the wobble in a hand that would be shown to the Steward later.",
        defeatText: "The clerk closed the entry book with a small, final snap. 'Qualified,' she said, in the tone of someone recording a fact rather than awarding a prize. 'Whether that's good news is between you and your stake, Mr Fenwick.'" },
      victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "📋", text: "The docket was stamped, the Contention entered, the date set for the following market day. Jory walked out of the Reckoning Hall feeling, for perhaps the first time that week, entirely in control of events, which is generally how these things start to go wrong.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "📋", text: "The clerk qualified him anyway, in the end, on the grounds that turning away a Fenwick was more paperwork than letting one through. The docket was stamped all the same, though Jory left with the distinct sense of having scraped rather than sailed it.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🕰️", text: "Six days to the Contention, and Jory had precisely one general proof to his name. He needed a great deal more than that, and not much time to find it in.", options: [
      { label: "Send for Master Quill, the family tutor", next: "routeA_setup", preview: "Quick, expensive, and exactly the kind of shortcut a Fenwick is used to taking." },
      { label: "Spend the nights in the Proof Hall stacks", next: "routeB_setup", preview: "Slower, and it means being seen studying like anyone else." },
      { label: "Send Fettle Grey to dig up something on Lark Hollis", next: "routeC_setup", preview: "Ugly, and there's no guarantee it tells him anything about proofs at all." },
    ] },
    routeA_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🎩", text: "Master Quill arrived within the hour, because that was what retainers were for, and unrolled a method he called 'the family shortcut' with the pride of a man who had never once had to justify it to anyone poorer than himself. 'Half these so-called proofs,' he said, 'collapse the moment you substitute properly instead of guessing. Try this pair, and mind you do the substitution, not the guessing.'", next: "routeA_challenge" },
    routeA_challenge: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "✏️", text: "Quill set the slate down without ceremony, the way a man does when he expects to be right.", question: "Solve the simultaneous equations 3x + 2y = 16 and x − y = 2. What is the value of x + y?", options: ["6", "8", "4", "10"], answer: 0,
      hint: "Rearrange the second equation to write x in terms of y, then substitute into the first.",
      solution: "From x − y = 2, x = y + 2. Substituting: 3(y+2) + 2y = 16, so 3y + 6 + 2y = 16, so 5y = 10, so y = 2 and x = 4. Then x + y = 4 + 2 = 6.",
      good: "routeA_good", mid: "routeA_mid" },
    routeA_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧮", grantComponent: "chalk_stub", text: "Jory worked it clean, substitution first and no guessing at all, and Quill permitted himself something close to warmth. 'There,' he said. 'That's the family method, properly used.' He produced, from an inside pocket, a stick of chalk stamped with the university crest, the kind issued only to contenders who file through a retained tutor. 'Statute requires you bring your own. Don't lose it.'\n\nComponent found: Contention Chalk.", next: "converge2" },
    routeA_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🧮", setMid: true, grantComponent: "chalk_stub", text: "Jory got there in the end, by a slower and more embarrassed road than substitution, and Quill sighed the particular sigh of a tutor billing by the hour. 'Adequate,' he said, which from Quill was practically a public flogging. He handed over the stamped chalk anyway, because the fee had already been paid whether Jory earned it or not.\n\nComponent found: Contention Chalk.", next: "converge2" },
    routeB_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Proof Hall stacks at night were mostly Under students and the occasional insomniac don, and Jory sat among them feeling conspicuous in a coat that cost more than the shelving. The archivist, a Laachmi clerk named Ossett with a voice like a well-oiled hinge, pointed him at the case of contested proofs without asking why a Fenwick wanted it. 'Six volumes,' Ossett said. 'Only two are shelved where they claim to be. The other four have been reasoned about so badly for so long that nobody's fixed the label. Draw carefully.'", next: "routeB_challenge" },
    routeB_challenge: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 3,
      text: "'Before you draw,' said Ossett, not unkindly, 'tell me the odds, or I'll not trust you to read what's inside the case once you've got it open.'",
      predict: { question: "The case holds six volumes. Only two are correctly shelved under Reckoning proofs. If Jory draws one volume at random, what is the probability it is one of the two correct ones?", options: ["1/3", "1/2", "2/3", "1/6"], answer: 0,
        why: "Two correct volumes out of six total gives a probability of 2/6, which simplifies to 1/3." },
      success: "routeB_good", fail: "routeB_mid" },
    routeB_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📖", grantComponent: "chalk_stub", text: "Jory named the odds correctly and drew true on the first try: a slim, badly bound volume titled Proofs That Were Never Quite Finished, its spine cracked from being reshelved by people who couldn't understand it and refiled by people who hadn't tried. Tucked inside the back cover, someone had left a spare stick of university-stamped chalk, the kind the stacks kept for students who could prove they'd read the thing properly.\n\nComponent found: Contention Chalk.", next: "converge2" },
    routeB_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📖", setMid: true, grantComponent: "chalk_stub", text: "Jory drew wrong twice before he drew right, which Ossett noted in a ledger with the placid cruelty of someone who charges by the attempt. The right volume turned up eventually, chalk stick and all, but Jory paid a late fine for the privilege that no Fenwick had ever had to pay before, and found he minded it more than he expected to.\n\nComponent found: Contention Chalk.", next: "converge2" },
    routeC_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🕵️", text: "Fettle Grey worked out of a laundry doorway two streets below the Rise's cliff, and dealt in the sort of information that was true but not necessarily kind. 'You want dirt on a Reckoning clerk,' Fettle said, turning the coin Jory offered over twice, checking the mint mark rather than the amount, 'or you want to know if she'll flinch before she has to prove anything. Those are different orders, Mr Fenwick, and only one of them's for sale here.' Fettle's eyes had already started measuring him back.", next: "routeC_challenge" },
    routeC_challenge: { type: "combat", emoji: "🗣️", qLevel: 2,
      text: "Fettle folded both arms and turned the conversation, without warning, into a test of its own, the kind the Under ran on Wrought clients before deciding whether they were worth the risk of being seen with. Every question was a small trap dressed as arithmetic.",
      enemy: { name: "Fettle Grey", emoji: "🧷", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Jory answered fast and correct, and Fettle's expression didn't change, but the questions got a shade less contemptuous.",
        strikeText: "Jory answered too quickly and wrongly, and Fettle let the silence sit there a moment longer than comfortable before moving on.",
        defeatText: "'Well,' said Fettle at last, unfolding, 'you can count, whatever else you can't do.' A stub of stamped chalk appeared on the counter, produced from nowhere in particular, in the manner of everything Fettle produced. 'Court chalk. Don't ask where from. Consider it professional respect, which I don't hand out for free, and don't mistake it for liking you.'" },
      victory: "routeC_good", retreat: "routeC_mid" },
    routeC_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧷", grantComponent: "chalk_stub", text: "Fettle never did produce a single fact about Lark Hollis, and Jory left with the odd, unfamiliar feeling of having been assessed rather than served. He'd got his chalk. He hadn't got what he came for, and for once he noticed the difference.\n\nComponent found: Contention Chalk.", next: "converge2" },
    routeC_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🧷", setMid: true, grantComponent: "chalk_stub", text: "Jory scraped through the questioning by the skin of his teeth and Fettle's evident boredom, and got the chalk anyway, tossed across the counter like something not worth the argument of withholding. 'Go on then,' Fettle said. 'You've wasted enough of my evening for one coin.'\n\nComponent found: Contention Chalk.", next: "converge2" },
    converge2: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "Whichever road he'd taken, Jory arrived home with a stick of stamped chalk and four days left, and the beginning of an unpleasant suspicion that having the chalk was the easy part of the statute. He still didn't have a single proof technique he trusted further than the length of his own arm.", next: "lemma_puzzle" },
    lemma_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧮", text: "He went back to the Proof Hall stacks, this time by daylight, and worked through the same shelf of contested proofs Lark checked for a living, trying to see the world the way a copy-clerk was forced to: exactly, and all the way to the end of the working.",
      question: "For any whole number n, what is always true about (n+1)² − n²?", options: ["It is always odd", "It is always even", "It is always a multiple of 4", "It is always a perfect square"], answer: 0,
      hint: "Expand (n+1)² fully before you subtract n².",
      solution: "(n+1)² − n² = (n² + 2n + 1) − n² = 2n + 1. Since 2n is always even, 2n + 1 is always one more than an even number, which makes it odd, whatever whole number n happens to be.",
      good: "lemma_good", mid: "lemma_mid" },
    lemma_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📐", grantComponent: "borrowed_lemma", text: "The working came out clean and general on the first pass, no examples required, and Jory sat back from the slate feeling something he was reluctant to name as satisfaction. He copied the lemma onto a fresh strip of paper in his neatest hand: the difference between consecutive squares is always odd, and here is why, in full, for every whole number there is.\n\nComponent found: The Borrowed Lemma.", next: "second_seek" },
    lemma_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📐", setMid: true, grantComponent: "borrowed_lemma", text: "It took three false starts and one genuinely humiliating attempt to prove it 'by example' again before the general working finally sat right on the slate. Jory copied it out anyway, a little raggedly, and told himself the strip of paper didn't need to look proud of itself to be correct.\n\nComponent found: The Borrowed Lemma.", next: "second_seek" },
    second_seek: { type: "story", emoji: "🧑‍⚖️", text: "Statute required one more thing before the Slate Court would hear him: a second, a witness of standing to vouch the challenge was made in good faith and not merely because a Fenwick could afford the docket. Most Wrought seconds wanted no part of a contention against a Reckoning clerk, on the grounds that clerks occasionally won and nobody wanted to have backed the losing side in public. Jory found, to his mild disgust, that the only candidate willing to even discuss it was a scholarship boy he vaguely recognised from the quad.", next: "second_check" },
    second_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "⏳", stat: 4, dc: 12,
      text: "Rooke looked him over in the manner of someone pricing a coat, not a person. 'You want me to stand second for a Fenwick against a Reckoning clerk,' he said, 'which means if you lose, I've been seen backing the boy who tried to buy his way out of a fair flag. What's in it for me, exactly, besides the pleasure of your company?' The docket office closed at noon, and Jory had perhaps four sentences left to make this worth Rooke's time.",
      success: "second_success", fail: "second_fail" },
    second_success: { type: "story", emoji: "🤝", grantComponent: "second_seat", text: "'The flag was fair,' Jory said, before he could talk himself out of it. 'She caught a real hole in the proof. I'm not contesting that she's right. I'm contesting whether I can do better than I did the first time, in front of the Steward, which is a different question.'\n\nRooke considered this with the flat, assessing patience of someone used to being lied to and recognising, for once, that he wasn't. 'Docket fee, paid whatever happens,' he said. 'Not because I like you. Because that's what standing second costs, and I don't do it for nothing.' He signed the paper without further comment.\n\nComponent found: The Second's Seat.", next: "forge" },
    second_fail: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", setMid: true, grantComponent: "second_seat", text: "Jory led with the family name instead of the honest answer, out of old habit rather than any real choice, and watched it land exactly as badly as it deserved to. 'Find someone else,' Rooke said, and went back to his ledger without heat, the way you'd close a door on a draught.\n\nA proctor's clerk agreed to stand second instead, for a docket fee steep enough to sting even a Fenwick, and considerably less interested in whether Jory's cause was just than in whether his cheque would clear.\n\nComponent found: The Second's Seat.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🖋️", text: "The night before the Contention, Jory laid out what he had on the desk in Aster Row: a stick of stamped chalk, a strip of paper with a general proof copied out in his best hand, and a signed docket naming his second. Tanner Voss, the instrument-fitter the university kept on retainer for exactly this purpose, turned all three over without much interest and reached for his tools.",
      forged: "Voss fitted the chalk stub into a brass holder engraved with the Statute of Contention, lacquered the folded lemma into a slim compartment in the handle, and rolled the signed docket into the base where it couldn't be lost or disputed. 'There,' he said, handing it back. 'Now it's a proper kit and not just a boy with some paper.' The chalk sat oddly heavy in Jory's hand, for something so small.\n\nThe Unmarked Chalk is yours! (+1 Logic, kept forever.)",
      notforged: "Voss counted the components on the desk, found the gaps, and didn't bother pretending otherwise. 'Statute wants all three or it isn't a filed Contention, it's a boy holding some chalk,' he said. 'Go and finish the finding. The Slate Court isn't going anywhere, and neither is the paperwork.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
      grantItem: "gf_jory_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "⚖️", text: "The Slate Court filled early, mostly with people who had come to watch a Fenwick lose money in public, which was rarer entertainment than the Rise liked to admit. Lark Hollis stood at her slate in the same darned coat, chalk already in hand, and did not look nervous, which Jory found more unsettling than if she had.\n\nThe Steward read the statute, named the stakes aloud (twenty marks a side, pocket change to Aster Row and, Jory now understood, close to a term's rent to a Reckoning clerk), and set the question: prove, generally, that the product of any two consecutive odd numbers is one less than a perfect square.\n\nJory wrote fast and clean, using every general trick he'd bled for that week: odd numbers as 2n+1, full expansion, no shortcuts, no examples standing in for proof. It was, he knew as he wrote it, the best working he had ever produced in his life.\n\nLark's slate, when the Steward called time, was cleaner still: the same substitution, one fewer line, one elegant step he hadn't thought to take, arrived at with the unhurried certainty of someone who did this for a living because she had to, not because it was expected of her.\n\nThe Steward examined both slates for a long time, turned them side by side, and ruled that the flag stood: Jory's original proof had indeed been flawed, and Lark Hollis had been right to say so, then and now.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end", grantItem: "gf_jory_item2",
      goodText: "Jory paid the twenty marks without a word of complaint, which surprised the room and surprised him rather more. Outside the Slate Court, he found Lark counting the stake into a purse that clearly had further to travel than his ever had.\n\n'Where did you learn to write it that clean?' he asked, and meant it, which was the strange part.\n\n'Copying other people's mistakes for a living,' said Lark, not looking up from the counting. 'You see a lot of ways to get it wrong. Eventually you notice the one way to get it right.' She pocketed the purse and looked at him properly for the first time all week. 'You did better than I expected, for what that's worth.'\n\n'It's worth something,' Jory said, and was faintly startled to find he meant that too.\n\nHe didn't apologise for the family name, or Quill, or Fettle, or any of it, because that would have been a different story than the one he was in. But he walked back up to Aster Row turning her proof over in his head instead of his own, and that, for a Fenwick, was practically an earthquake.\n\nA Fenwick Watch, Reset is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
      midText: "Jory paid the twenty marks, grudgingly, and told himself the loss didn't sting because the flag had already stood once before. Lark took the stake without comment and left without waiting to see whether he had anything else to say, which told him plainly enough what she thought his silence was worth.\n\nHe walked back to Aster Row alone, chalk holder heavy in his pocket, turning over the uncomfortable arithmetic of a week in which the cleverest person he'd met had also been the poorest, and finding he didn't have a general proof for why that should have surprised him at all.\n\nA Fenwick Watch, Reset is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)" },
  },
},

gf_ratchet: {
  id: "gf_ratchet",
  title: "The Ninth-Bell Run",
  tagline: "A sealed quittance, a debt of his own he can't shake, and the ninth bell closing in fast.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_ratchet_item1", name: "Second-Hand Watch", stat: 4, how: "found", desc: "+1 Speed. Won with a sharp Speed check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_ratchet_item2", name: "The Runner's Marker", stat: 2, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_ratchet_item3", name: "The Counted Second", stat: 0, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { watch_glass: "Cracked Watch-Glass", mainspring: "Bent Mainspring", latch_pin: "Filed Latch-Pin" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🕰️", text: "The back room over the Anchor smelled of oil, spilled gin and paper going soft with damp. Silas Vane sat where he always sat, one boot up on the table among the strata sheets, reading something upside down with the ease of a man who had long since stopped needing to hide what he already knew.\n\n'Ratchet,' he said, without looking up, which was somehow worse than if he had. 'Quick job. You're quick.'\n\nHe set a small wax-sealed chit on the table between them: a quittance, the kind that says a debt is settled and done, not owing, not shamed, finished. On the front, in a clerk's careful hand, one name: Etta Coombe.\n\n'She scraped it together over eight months,' Vane said. 'Every mark of it, honest. I want it logged at the Sixmark counter before ninth bell. After ninth bell the Long Lane vent seals the counter's window till morning, and her name sits on tomorrow's strata list still marked owing, when it isn't. One more day of the street looking at that list and thinking what it thinks.'\n\nHe did look up then, and there was nothing kind in his face, and nothing cruel either. Just a man doing arithmetic with someone else's dignity, and knowing exactly what he was doing.\n\n'You've got until the ninth bell vents Long Lane,' he said. 'That's the whole of the job. Don't be clever. Be quick.'", next: "briefing" },
    briefing: { type: "story", emoji: "🗒️", text: "Ratchet pocketed the chit before Vane had finished the sentence, because that was the trick of the whole business: move first, feel it later, if you had time left over for feeling.\n\nHe knew what he was, working for a man who lent money nobody was ever really pressed to repay quickly, which was worse, not better, because it meant you never finished owing. Ratchet still owed nine marks himself, taken on at sixteen for a debt that had long since stopped being about the marks. He knew the shape of what he was carrying in his pocket, both directions at once: relief for Etta Coombe, and one more small, useful kindness that kept the whole rotten machine looking almost decent.\n\n'Ninth bell,' Vane said again, to his back. 'Not a count after.'\n\nOutside, Gifford's clocks were already arguing about how close to ninth bell they actually were, the way they always did. Ratchet didn't wait to hear who won.", next: "skillcheck1" },
    skillcheck1: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🏃", stat: 4, dc: 12,
      text: "The Anchor's side door let out onto Gullet Row, where the Rise's Boundary Watch changed shift on a schedule that was supposed to be a secret and had been an open one for years. Time it right and the Row was empty for the length of a held breath. Time it wrong and it was full of men with dockets to check and nowhere to be for the next twenty minutes, which was worse for Ratchet than any vent.\n\nHe stood in the doorway working out the gap in his head: how long since the last change, how long the changes usually ran, how far the ninth bell actually was by the crooked church clock over the tanner's yard. He'd have to trust his own count more than the clocks. Everyone quick learned that eventually.",
      success: "watch_found", fail: "watch_missed" },
    watch_found: { type: "story", emoji: "⏱️", grantItem: "gf_ratchet_item1", text: "The gap was exactly as wide as he'd guessed and he was through it before the Watch had finished changing, which was the sort of small private victory nobody else would ever know to applaud.\n\nOn the step outside the Anchor's side door, dropped by some previous runner in a hurry that hadn't ended well, lay a second-hand watch on a snapped chain, its face cracked but its hands still going, patient and exact as anything in Gifford ever managed to be. Ratchet turned it over once, decided against wondering too hard about whose it had been, and pocketed it.\n\nThe Second-Hand Watch is yours. (+1 Speed, kept forever.)", next: "warden_gate" },
    watch_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "He judged the gap wrong by a handful of counts and had to flatten himself into a doorway while the tail end of the old shift grumbled past, close enough that he could smell the tallow on their coats. Nobody saw him. That was the whole of the good news.\n\nWhatever some previous runner had dropped on that step was long gone by the time he could safely move, and there was no time now to go looking for what wasn't his to begin with.", next: "warden_gate" },
    warden_gate: { type: "combat", emoji: "🛡️", qLevel: 2,
      text: "The footbridge at Cutter's Seam was the only quick way from the Under up onto the Rise side of Long Lane, and one of Ashcombe's Boundary Wardens was standing square in the middle of it, docket book open, going through everyone's papers with the slow, thorough patience of a man three hours from the end of a long shift.\n\nHe wasn't cruel about it. He was worse than cruel: he was fair, and thorough, and would check Ratchet's papers exactly as carefully as anyone else's, and Ratchet's papers were exactly good enough to survive a glance and nowhere near good enough to survive being read.\n\n'Evening,' the Warden said, not unfriendly, holding out a hand for the docket that didn't exist.",
      enemy: { name: "The Boundary Warden", emoji: "🥾", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Ratchet answered fast and easy, the sort of answer that sounds like a man with nothing to hide, and the Warden's attention slid past him half a step, which was all the room he needed.",
        strikeText: "The Warden's eyes sharpened on a wrong number and Ratchet had to talk faster than he liked, feeling the whole crossing tip against him for a moment.",
        defeatText: "The Warden finally sighed, stamped a blank corner of his own docket book out of pure procedural habit, and waved Ratchet through with the air of a man who had decided that whatever this was, it wasn't worth the last three hours of his shift." },
      victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌉", text: "Ratchet came off the far side of the bridge at a fast walk that wanted badly to be a run, counting under his breath the way he always did when the counting was the only thing keeping the panic honest. Long Lane opened out ahead of him, busy, ordinary, entirely unaware that its outfall was about to make a liar of the whole afternoon.\n\nSomewhere behind him the Warden had already gone back to his book, and forgotten Ratchet's face before Ratchet had finished forgetting his.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The Warden wasn't having it. Ratchet backed off the bridge with an apology that cost him nothing and a docket book entry that would cost somebody else a headache later, and dropped down onto the towpath instead, adding a good five minutes and a wet boot to a day that had no five minutes going spare.\n\nHe came up onto Long Lane from the towpath steps, breathing hard, having learned nothing except that Ashcombe trained his Wardens properly, which was, infuriatingly, to his credit.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🗺️", text: "Long Lane split three ways before it reached the Sixmark Exchange, and somewhere down each of them the outfall was already clearing its throat. Ratchet stood at the fork doing the sum he always hated doing: not which way was safest, but which way was fastest, because fastest was the only column that mattered before ninth bell.\n\nThe Clatterlift cable ran high and straight over Fenning Gap, quick if his nerve held. The old flood culverts ran low and hidden under the Under, slower but out of sight of every patrol on the surface. And the rooftops over Bramwell's yard cut the corner entirely, if he didn't mind going over a certain workshop he had reasons, complicated ones, for not minding at all.", options: [
      { label: "Take the Clatterlift cable over Fenning Gap", next: "cable", preview: "A straight, fast, nerve-shredding crossing on a bare wire. A real timing puzzle against the vent." },
      { label: "Drop into the flood culverts", next: "culvert", preview: "Slower, hidden, timed to the drip. A probability call on catching the gap between pulses." },
      { label: "Cross the rooftops over Bramwell's yard", next: "rooftop", preview: "Quick, but it means going over a workshop where somebody Ratchet knows might see him. A close scrape with the night watchman." },
    ] },
    cable: { type: "story", emoji: "🪢", text: "The Clatterlift cable at Fenning Gap wasn't built for running. It was built for cargo cradles and the occasional maintenance man with a harness and a great deal of patience, neither of which described Ratchet at all. But it went straight across, two hundred yards dead level, and straight was what he needed.\n\nBelow the gantry the outfall grate had already started to breathe, a thin curl of steam nosing up between the slats like something waking slowly and in no particular hurry to be reasonable about it.", next: "cable_puzzle" },
    cable_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⏳", text: "Ratchet went hand over hand along the cable at a dead run, the whole wire singing under him, and made himself do the sum properly instead of just hoping, because hoping had never once got anyone across a gap on time.\n\nTwo hundred yards of cable. Five yards a count, flat out, if his legs held the pace. He was already sixty yards in when the first real hiss came up from the grate below, the sound every Gifford runner knew meant the walkway above would seal in forty counts flat, vent or no vent.", question: "The cable spans 200 yards and Ratchet holds a steady 5 yards per count. He is already 60 yards across when the outfall hisses, and the walkway seals 40 counts after that hiss. If he keeps the same pace, how many counts to spare does he have when he reaches the far side?", options: ["12 counts to spare", "8 counts to spare", "4 counts short", "20 counts to spare"], answer: 0,
      hint: "Work out the distance still to cover, then how long that takes at 5 yards a count. Compare that time to the 40 counts he has before the seal.",
      solution: "There are 200 - 60 = 140 yards left to cross. At 5 yards a count that takes 140 ÷ 5 = 28 counts. The walkway seals 40 counts after the hiss, so Ratchet finishes with 40 - 28 = 12 counts to spare.",
      good: "cable_good", mid: "cable_mid" },
    cable_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "watch_glass", text: "Ratchet counted it out as he ran, adjusted nothing because nothing needed adjusting, and came off the far gantry with twelve clean counts still in hand and his heart going considerably faster than his legs had.\n\nWedged in the gantry's rivets, left by some maintenance man's dropped kit years back, was a small disc of cracked watch glass, ground thin and true. He almost left it. He had never once in his life left a spare part lying about when he had a use for one, and he wasn't about to start now.\n\nComponent found: Cracked Watch-Glass.", next: "citycrossed" },
    cable_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", setMid: true, text: "Ratchet's sum came out wrong under pressure, close but wrong, and the last stretch of cable turned into a graceless, undignified scramble that got him across with his coat torn and his dignity considerably more damaged than his schedule. He'd cut it fine. Finer than he liked to think about.\n\nHe still had the sense, halfway along in the scramble, to snatch up a small disc of cracked watch glass wedged in the gantry rivets, because old habits paid for themselves eventually, and this looked exactly like the kind of thing that might.\n\nComponent found: Cracked Watch-Glass.", next: "citycrossed" },
    culvert: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", text: "The flood culverts ran under the Under itself, brick throats slick with a hundred years of runoff, lit only by whatever daylight leaked down through the grates above. Nobody official ever checked the culverts, which was exactly why every runner in Gifford who valued their knees used them anyway.\n\nThe trouble was the outfall pulses: short bursts of scalding backwash that came up through the side vents on no schedule anyone had ever pinned down for certain, except that they came, and that standing in the wrong spot when one arrived was not a mistake you got to make twice.", next: "culvert_dice" },
    culvert_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 3, text: "Ratchet crouched at the mouth of the narrow stretch, timing the pulses by ear the way every culvert runner learned to, and picked his moment. Roll 3 or higher on a six-sided die to catch the gap clean and get through dry.", predict: { question: "Before he moves: what is the probability of rolling 3 or higher on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/3", "1/3", "5/6"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 3, 4, 5 and 6 all clear the gap. That is four outcomes out of six equally likely ones, and 4/6 simplifies to 2/3. Working the odds out properly before he moves steadies his footing: +1 to his roll." },
      success: "culvert_good", fail: "culvert_bad" },
    culvert_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "watch_glass", text: "He went through the gap at a dead sprint and came out the far side dry, boots barely wet, just as the pulse behind him hit the brick with a sound like a kettle losing an argument.\n\nCaught in the grate he'd just cleared, snagged there who knew how long, was a small disc of cracked watch glass, ground thin and true, exactly the kind of thing a careful runner picks up out of pure reflex.\n\nComponent found: Cracked Watch-Glass.", next: "citycrossed" },
    culvert_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "💦", setMid: true, text: "He misjudged the gap by a heartbeat and the pulse caught him across the boots and shins, scalding through his socks in a way that would leave him limping for a day and cursing culverts for considerably longer. He came out the far side soaked, furious and moving slower than he could afford.\n\nHe still had the presence of mind to snatch a small disc of cracked watch glass out of the grate on his way past, because a runner who stops noticing useful things has stopped being a runner.\n\nComponent found: Cracked Watch-Glass.", next: "citycrossed" },
    rooftop: { type: "story", emoji: "🏚️", text: "The rooftops over Bramwell's yard cut clean across the last stubborn corner of Long Lane, if you didn't mind a climb, a drop and the very real chance of being seen by somebody who mattered more to you than the job did.\n\nThrough a skylight thick with soot Ratchet caught one bright second of the workshop below: lamp glow, a boiler half stripped down, Marrow bent over a bench with a spanner and that particular set to his shoulders that meant he was concentrating on something and pretending he wasn't tired. Ratchet didn't stop. He never stopped, not when he was on a job, not when it was Marrow down there and every part of him wanted five minutes over roasted chestnuts instead of a chit burning a hole in his pocket. That was the job too: wanting the five minutes, and not taking it.", next: "rooftop_fight" },
    rooftop_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🔦", qLevel: 2,
      text: "Old Fenner did the yard's night rounds the same way he'd done them for eleven years, lamp in one hand, temper in the other, and he was not a man who missed a shape moving where no shape had business moving. He came up onto the flat roof by the ladder just as Ratchet was crossing it, lamp swinging round.\n\n'Oi!' Fenner didn't sound angry so much as put out, a man whose evening had just acquired an unwelcome complication. 'This is Bramwell's roof, not the parish common!'\n\nHe wasn't wrong, and he wasn't trying to hurt anyone, and none of that made him any easier to get past.",
      enemy: { name: "Old Fenner, the Night Watchman", emoji: "🏮", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Ratchet talked fast and moved faster, ducking a stack of guttering while Fenner's lamp swung past a half-second behind him.",
        strikeText: "Fenner's lamp caught him full in the face for a moment, blinding and hot, and Ratchet nearly put a foot straight through a rotten section of felt.",
        defeatText: "Fenner gave up the chase at the roof's far edge, wheezing, and settled for shouting something about respectable premises after Ratchet's disappearing back, which Ratchet privately agreed with rather more than he'd ever admit." },
      victory: "rooftop_good", retreat: "rooftop_bad" },
    rooftop_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "watch_glass", text: "Ratchet dropped off the far gutter into the alley behind the yard, breathing hard, grinning despite himself the way he always did after outrunning someone whose job it had been to catch him.\n\nCaught in the gutter's ironwork, rattling loose as he swung past, was a small disc of cracked watch glass. He pocketed it without breaking stride, the way you pocket anything useful when you've spent your whole life owning very little.\n\nComponent found: Cracked Watch-Glass.", next: "citycrossed" },
    rooftop_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🩹", setMid: true, text: "Fenner's lamp caught him properly that time and Ratchet went down the last stretch of roof more by luck than by any plan, landing in the alley in a heap that would ache tomorrow and possibly the day after. Nothing broken. Everything bruised, including, quietly, his pride.\n\nHe still had the wit to grab a small disc of cracked watch glass out of the gutter ironwork on the way down, mostly because his hand closed on it before his brain had finished deciding whether that was sensible.\n\nComponent found: Cracked Watch-Glass.", next: "citycrossed" },
    citycrossed: { type: "story", emoji: "🌆", text: "Whichever way he'd come, Ratchet came out onto the top of Sixmark Street with the same thought hammering behind his ribs: the light. The light over the Exchange's counter window was still burning gold, which meant the window was still open, which meant he wasn't finished yet, only close.\n\nThe ninth bell hadn't sounded. Not yet. But Gifford's outfalls didn't wait for anyone's arithmetic to catch up, and Ratchet had learned a long time ago that 'not yet' was the most dangerous phrase in the whole city.", next: "second_check" },
    second_check: { type: "dice", emoji: "🛒", die: 6, target: 4, text: "Sixmark Street ran thick with barrow-sellers packing up for the night, and there was no straight line through any of it, only gaps that opened and shut like a bellows. Ratchet picked his moment at a stack of crates blocking the direct route. Roll 4 or higher on a six-sided die to slip the gap before it closed.", predict: { question: "Before he moves: what is the probability of rolling 4 or higher on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "1/3", "2/3", "5/6"], answer: 0, why: "Rolls of 4, 5 and 6 succeed. That is three outcomes out of six equally likely ones, and 3/6 simplifies to 1/2. Even odds, same as a coin toss. Working that out steadies his nerve: +1 to his roll." },
      success: "second_good", fail: "second_bad" },
    second_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "mainspring", text: "Ratchet threaded the gap a half-step before the crates closed it, spinning past a barrow-seller who swore at him with real warmth, the way Sixmark folk swore at anyone quick enough to earn it.\n\nLoose in the gutter where the barrow had just been stood, small and coiled and easily missed, lay a bent mainspring, the kind that comes out of a watch too cheap to be worth mending properly. Ratchet scooped it up without slowing.\n\nComponent found: Bent Mainspring.", next: "third_check" },
    second_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🥴", setMid: true, text: "The gap shut a half-step before he got there and Ratchet caught a crate corner hard across the hip, staggering out the far side of the crowd rather less gracefully than he'd have liked, to the barrow-seller's considerable amusement.\n\nNothing in the gutter caught his eye on the way past. He didn't have time to go back and check properly, and some things you only get one clean chance at.", next: "third_check" },
    third_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔑", stat: 2, dc: 12,
      text: "The Exchange's side alley was gated for the night, a proper iron gate with a proper Exchange lock, the kind meant to keep out exactly the sort of person Ratchet currently was. But the gate had a trick to it, if you knew the mechanism: three pins, only one of which actually caught, and no time to try them one at a time.\n\nHe'd watched a locksmith work this exact gate once, months back, for reasons that had nothing to do with tonight, and some part of his brain had filed the logic of it away without being asked to. Now was the moment to find out if that part of his brain had done its job properly.",
      success: "third_good", fail: "third_bad" },
    third_good: { type: "story", emoji: "⚙️", grantComponent: "latch_pin", text: "He worked it through properly instead of guessing: the middle pin, always the middle pin, because the outer two were dummies fitted to waste a burglar's evening. The gate gave with a clean, embarrassed little click, as if it too thought the whole arrangement was a bit much.\n\nFallen in the gate's own mechanism, filed thin by whoever had built the trick pins in the first place, was a slim brass latch-pin, exactly the sort of small precise thing a good clock is built around. Ratchet took it, and took the shortcut through, in that order.\n\nComponent found: Filed Latch-Pin.", next: "forge" },
    third_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", setMid: true, text: "He guessed instead of reasoning, tried the wrong pin first, and lost the better part of two minutes he did not have to a gate that had, in the end, no interest in his schedule. It gave eventually. Gates generally do, if you're patient and lucky in roughly equal measure.\n\nWhatever had been loose in the mechanism stayed loose, and Ratchet didn't have the time to go fishing for it.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "The Exchange counter's back room kept a bench for exactly this sort of thing, because half of Sixmark Street's business ran on repaired timepieces and nobody trusted a runner who couldn't keep to a schedule he could actually read. Old Press, who minded the bench most nights for a cut of whatever got mended on it, looked up at Ratchet with the particular unimpressed patience of a man who had seen every kind of desperate walk through that door.\n\n'Cracked glass, a bent spring, a filed pin,' Press said, turning them over one at a time under his lamp. 'You've been busy. Want me to make something of it, or just admire it?'",
      forged: "Press worked fast and said nothing while he did it, which from Press was practically a benediction. The glass went in true, the spring wound tight and even, the pin seated with one clean click, and what came off the bench was a watch that actually kept the time it claimed to, which in Gifford was rarer than it had any right to be.\n\n'The Counted Second,' Press said, handing it over. 'Keeps its own time. Doesn't care what the church clocks think.'\n\nThe Counted Second is yours! (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)",
      notforged: "Press laid out what Ratchet had and counted it twice, the way careful men count things that matter. 'Short a piece,' he said, not unkindly, nudging the gaps into a neat little row. 'A watch wants all three or it's just bits admiring each other. Come back with the rest of it. The bench isn't going anywhere, and neither, worryingly, is the ninth bell.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
      grantItem: "gf_ratchet_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "The counter window was still lit when Ratchet finally slapped the wax-sealed chit down in front of the Exchange clerk, breathing like a man who had personally argued with the whole of Sixmark Street and mostly won.\n\nThe clerk unfolded it, read the name, checked the seal against the ledger, and logged it in a hand as unhurried as if there were no such thing as a ninth bell anywhere in the world. Etta Coombe, quit and settled, struck off tomorrow's strata list before it had even been drawn up.\n\nSomewhere out over Long Lane, right on cue, the outfall let go with a long scalding sigh, and the fog came up thick and orange under the gaslight, sealing the route Ratchet had just spent the whole evening racing. He watched it through the counter window, in no hurry now at all, and let himself feel, just for a moment, exactly what he'd been putting off feeling since the back room over the Anchor: that he'd done one decent thing today, on behalf of an organisation that mostly didn't deal in those, and that the two facts sat together in his chest without cancelling each other out, the way they always did, the way he'd stopped expecting them not to.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end",
      goodText: "Ratchet made it back to the Anchor with breath to spare and the whole run's timing still sharp enough in his head to recite, which he did, at length, to anyone who'd listen and several who wouldn't. Vane heard the report without much comment, which from Vane was as close to satisfied as the job ever paid out.\n\nWord came back within the week, the way it always did on Sixmark Street: Etta Coombe had gone to the market the morning after with her head up for the first time in eight months, and nobody there had known enough to make anything of it, which was rather the point. Ratchet never met her. He didn't need to. He'd done the sum, and the sum had come out right, and for one evening at least that was allowed to be enough.",
      midText: "Ratchet got the chit to the counter in the end, scraped and out of breath and rather more battered than the job strictly called for, and the clerk logged it anyway, unbothered by the state of the runner who'd delivered it. Vane heard the report and grunted something that might, in poor light, have passed for approval.\n\nThe job got done. It nearly didn't, twice, and Ratchet spent the walk home doing the sum on that too: how close was too close, and whether he'd trust himself with a margin that thin again. He decided, on balance, that he would, because the alternative was admitting he wasn't as quick as he liked to think, and some sums a person simply declines to finish.",
      grantItem: "gf_ratchet_item2" },
  },
},

gf_colworth: {
  id: "gf_colworth",
  title: "The Interest That Was Never Owed",
  tagline: "A widow's chalk debt has grown teeth over eleven unwitnessed years, and someone patient had better read the statute all the way to the bottom.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_colworth_item1", name: "The Loupe of Small Print", stat: 2, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Logic. Won with a sharp Logic check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_colworth_item2", name: "The Corrected Writ",       stat: 0, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_colworth_item3", name: "The Quick Reckoner",       stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { old_docket: "The First Signature", chalk_ledger: "Strick's Own Ledger", statute_leaf: "The Uncut Eleventh Clause" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "⚖️", text: "Magistrate Colworth's court sat in the back half of the Sump Bench, a low brick room in the Under with one window too grimed to let much through and a stove that argued with itself all winter. On a good day it heard boundary squabbles, unpaid writs and the odd dispute over a stolen barrow. On a bad day it heard the same, only slower.\n\nColworth liked it that way. Liking is perhaps too warm a word. Colworth found it tolerable, which for Colworth amounted to the same thing. He worked through the morning's stack of dockets the way a mill works through grain: steadily, without curiosity and without much caring what came out the other end, provided it came out in the right shape.\n\nThe stack that morning held the usual: two rent disputes, a chalk debt and a complaint about a goat. He was three dockets in and entirely unmoved by any of them when the chalk debt came round again, this time with a Distress Writ pinned to its corner and a number on it that made even his tired eye pause for exactly one second longer than habit allowed.\n\nOne second. That was where this whole business started.", next: "docket_check" },
    docket_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔍", stat: 2, dc: 12,
      text: "The writ was Strick versus Loom: an old grocer's tab against Sarn Loom, widow of a lamplighter, for a debt that had begun, eleven years back, at three sovereigns and had somehow grown into something Strick's covering note called 'compounded per the usual custom, five in the hundred, every year, as is proper.'\n\n'As is proper' was doing a great deal of work for four words. Colworth had read ten thousand dockets in his time and had learned, the slow way, that whenever a phrase worked that hard, it was usually carrying something it did not want carried openly.\n\nHe did not reach for his gavel. He reached for the drawer where the old clerk before him had kept a magnifying loupe, on the grounds that if a phrase was going to lie to him, it could at least do so in large print.",
      success: "loupe_found", fail: "loupe_missed" },
    loupe_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🔎", grantItem: "gf_colworth_item1", text: "The loupe was still there, wedged behind a box of nib cases nobody had thrown out in a decade, its brass gone dull but its glass true. Through it, the covering note gave up its small print without complaint: a footnote, half a line, citing 'the Common Floor' as the fallback rate for any tab not properly renewed.\n\nColworth did not know yet what the Common Floor was. He knew only that a footnote citing it had been included, and that nobody in the room seemed to want him reading it. In his experience, that was usually the whole case, sitting quietly in six-point type, waiting to be noticed.\n\nThe Loupe of Small Print is yours. (+1 Logic, kept forever.)", next: "advocate_dispute" },
    loupe_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "📄", text: "The note's small print stayed small, and Colworth's eye, tired and unaided, slid past the footnote the way eyes do when nothing has told them to stop. Something about the phrasing nagged at him anyway, the way a wrong note nags at a piano tuner who cannot quite place which string.\n\nHe set the drawer's contents back as he had found them and made a private note, in the margin of his own patience, to read more carefully next time.\n\n(The Loupe of Small Print stays lost, this time. Another run through this case may find it.)", next: "advocate_dispute" },
    advocate_dispute: { type: "combat", emoji: "🗣️", qLevel: 2,
      text: "The door of the Sump Bench opened before Colworth had finished the footnote, and Advocate Grieve came through it at the speed of a man who has practised looking unhurried and failed.\n\n'Magistrate. I trust we're stamping the Loom writ today. My client has waited eleven years for his money and rather feels that's enough patience for one lifetime.'\n\nColworth did not look up immediately, which in a lesser man might have been read as rudeness, and in Colworth was simply the shape of a thought finishing. 'Your client's covering note cites the Common Floor,' he said, eventually. 'I should like to know what that is before I stamp anything.'\n\nGrieve's smile did not so much falter as recalculate. 'A formality, Magistrate. Standard practice. Nothing that need trouble the bench.'\n\n'Then it will not trouble me for long,' said Colworth, and reached for his own copy of the Act.",
      enemy: { name: "Advocate Grieve", emoji: "🗣️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Colworth answered plainly and precisely, and Grieve's fluent certainty took a visible dent, like a well-oiled door that has just met a step it did not know was there.",
        strikeText: "Grieve came back with three precedents at once, spoken fast and confident, and for a moment the whole room seemed to agree with him simply because he had said it first.",
        defeatText: "Grieve ran out of precedents before he ran out of confidence, which happens to advocates more often than they enjoy admitting, and settled for looking wounded instead, which cost him nothing and proved less." },
      victory: "dispute_won", retreat: "dispute_retreat" },
    dispute_won: { type: "story", emoji: "🧾", text: "'The writ is stayed,' said Colworth, 'pending a proper review of the debt's history. One week.'\n\nGrieve opened his mouth to object, considered the last five minutes and shut it again with the particular dignity of a man deciding this was not the hill. 'A week, Magistrate,' he said, and left at something considerably less than his entering speed.\n\nIt was, by the standards of the Sump Bench, a small thing: a stay, not a ruling, granted on a hunch about a footnote. But Colworth found, to his mild and entirely unwelcome surprise, that he was curious what the footnote actually said. He could not remember the last time a docket had made him curious about anything.", next: "crossroads" },
    dispute_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🧾", setMid: true, text: "Grieve's precedents came thick and fast enough that the stay Colworth managed to carve out was smaller than he would have liked: three days, not a week, and a pointed reminder from Grieve that the Bench's patience with delay was, in his professional opinion, 'not unlimited, Magistrate, whatever the footnotes say.'\n\nThree days was not much. It was, however, more than none, and Colworth had learned long ago that a magistrate who waited for ideal conditions never ruled on anything at all.\n\n(The clock is running short. The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "Three days, or a week: either way, the question was the same. Somewhere behind Strick's tidy compounding sat the truth of what Sarn Loom actually owed, and somewhere behind that sat the reason nobody had wanted Colworth reading footnotes in the first place.\n\nThere were three ways to find it. He could walk to Strick's shop himself and go through the ledger line by line. He could send to the Roll Office for whatever original paperwork had survived eleven years of filing. Or he could have Strick's own clerk brought in and asked, plainly, to explain himself.\n\nNone of the three appealed to him more than the others. Colworth did not, as a rule, have preferences. He picked the one that seemed most likely to produce an answer that would hold up.", options: [
        { label: "Walk to Strick's shop and audit the ledger yourself", next: "shop_route", preview: "A hard arithmetic puzzle about how often the tab should have been renewed guards the truth." },
        { label: "Send to the Roll Office for the original paperwork", next: "archive_route", preview: "A dice challenge through the archive's pigeonholes guards the truth." },
        { label: "Summon Strick's clerk and question him directly", next: "chambers_route", preview: "A tense cross-examination, framed as a dispute to resolve, guards the truth." },
      ] },
    shop_route: { type: "story", emoji: "🏚️", text: "Strick's shop smelled of paraffin and old flour, and Strick himself, a heavy, not unkind man with chalk dust worked permanently into the creases of his knuckles, watched Colworth come through the door with the particular wariness shopkeepers reserve for anyone carrying a ledger of their own.\n\n'Magistrate. If this is about the Loom account, I've done nothing but what's customary. Five in the hundred, every year, same as everyone charges.'\n\n'Custom is not the same word as lawful,' said Colworth, 'though I grant they are often seen together. Show me the docket. The original, if you still have it.'", next: "shop_puzzle" },
    shop_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧮", text: "Strick produced a single yellowed sheet from beneath the counter, signed once at the top in a hand that had since gone shaky with age, and blank all the way down. 'That's the original,' he said. 'Same one as ever. Why would it need more than one signature?'\n\nThe Act, as Colworth recalled it, required a Renewal Sitting, and a fresh signature from both parties, every ninety days for any tab left open. Sarn's tab had run, entirely unrenewed, for exactly three thousand nine hundred and sixty days.", question: "The Chalk Act requires a fresh Renewal signature every 90 days for any open tab. Sarn's tab has run unrenewed for exactly 3,960 days. To the letter of the Act, how many Renewal signatures should the docket carry by now?", options: ["40", "44", "45", "36"], answer: 1,
      hint: "Divide the total number of days the tab has run by the length of one Renewal period.",
      solution: "3,960 ÷ 90 = 44 exactly, so the docket ought to carry 44 Renewal signatures, one every ninety days without a gap. Strick's docket carries one: the first, and last.",
      good: "shop_good", mid: "shop_mid" },
    shop_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📜", grantComponent: "old_docket", text: "Strick did the sum on his own counter in chalk, checked it, and went a colour Colworth associated with men remembering something they had hoped to forget. 'Forty-four,' he said, quietly. 'I never renewed it. Never occurred to me I had to. Everyone does it this way.'\n\n'Everyone,' said Colworth, 'is not, on this evidence, doing it lawfully.' He took the single signed sheet, wrapped it with the care he gave to anything that might later be needed in a hurry, and left Strick standing behind his own counter looking, for the first time in the conversation, rather smaller than his shop.\n\nComponent found: The First Signature.", next: "converge" },
    shop_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", setMid: true, text: "Strick's counting slipped, twice, and came out first at forty and then at thirty-six, and by the third attempt Colworth simply worked the division himself, aloud, slowly, the way he did everything: three thousand nine hundred and sixty divided by ninety, giving forty-four, exactly, with nothing left over.\n\nStrick took the correction better than most. 'Forty-four,' he agreed, unhappily. He handed over the original sheet readily enough once the arithmetic had been settled for him rather than by him, but the moment of finding it himself, and what that moment might have made him say unprompted, had already gone.\n\n(The tab yields its secret slowly rather than cleanly. The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "converge" },
    archive_route: { type: "story", emoji: "🗄️", text: "The Roll Office kept eleven years of Sump Bench paperwork the way a magpie keeps shiny things: thoroughly, and without any system a sane person would recognise as such. Pigeonholes ran floor to ceiling in a room lit by whatever daylight the one high window could be persuaded to part with.\n\nThe clerk on duty, a Laachmi woman easily two heads taller than Colworth even seated, looked at his request with the weary patience of someone who has heard 'it should be filed under Loom, or possibly Strick, or possibly the year' a great many times before. 'Somewhere in there,' she said, gesturing at eleven years of boxes. 'Good luck. Mind the ninth shelf, it's held a grudge since the flood.'", next: "archive_dice" },
    archive_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 4, text: "Roll 4 or more on a six-sided die to spot the right box among eleven years of nearly identical grey files, before the light through the high window goes entirely.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 4 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/2", "2/3", "5/6"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 4, 5 and 6 succeed: that is 3 outcomes out of 6, and 3/6 = 1/2. An even chance, in a room that does not believe in even chances. Predicting the odds correctly steadies the search: +1 to your roll." },
      success: "archive_good", fail: "archive_fail" },
    archive_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📜", grantComponent: "old_docket", text: "The box was exactly where a sensible filing system would have put it, which in the Roll Office counted as a minor miracle: Loom, under L, in the correct year, containing a single sheet nobody had touched since the day it was first filed.\n\nColworth read it twice, out of habit rather than doubt, and found what he had expected to find: one signature, one date and eleven years of silence afterward where forty-four Renewal signatures ought to have accumulated like dust.\n\nComponent found: The First Signature.", next: "converge" },
    archive_fail: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", setMid: true, text: "The ninth shelf lived up to its reputation, shedding a box of unrelated tannery disputes across the floor at the worst possible moment, and by the time the mess was sorted and apologised for, the light through the high window had gone entirely.\n\nThe Loom docket turned up eventually, the following morning, delivered by an apologetic runner who had clearly drawn the short straw. It was the right sheet. It simply arrived a day later than it might have, and a day, in a case already running on borrowed time, is not nothing.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "converge" },
    chambers_route: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "Clerk Sowerby kept Strick's books the way some men keep secrets: close, careful, and with a visible flinch whenever anyone asked to see them. He arrived at the Sump Bench already sweating, sat where he was told to sit, and answered the first three questions with the fluent unhelpfulness of a man who has rehearsed exactly three answers.\n\n'I only keep the sums, Magistrate. Mr Strick tells me the rate, I write it down. Five in the hundred, compounded, same as always. I don't ask about renewals. Not my place.'\n\n'It is entirely your place,' said Colworth. 'You are the one who wrote it down.'", next: "chambers_fight" },
    chambers_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🗯️", qLevel: 2,
      text: "Sowerby dug in the way frightened men do, which is to say badly and at length, repeating 'not my place' with mounting volume as if saying it louder might make it truer.\n\nColworth did not raise his voice. He never did. He simply went through the ledger's own numbers, one column at a time, out loud, until the numbers themselves started disagreeing with Sowerby rather more effectively than Colworth ever could.",
      enemy: { name: "Clerk Sowerby", emoji: "😰", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "The correct working landed flatly, and Sowerby's 'not my place' came out a half-tone higher and considerably less convinced of itself.",
        strikeText: "Sowerby produced a fresh, hurried excuse about missing pages, delivered fast enough that for a moment it nearly passed for an actual answer.",
        defeatText: "Sowerby ran out of excuses before Colworth ran out of patience, which was, by his own account afterwards, the single most frightening quarter of an hour of his working life." },
      victory: "chambers_win", retreat: "chambers_retreat" },
    chambers_win: { type: "story", emoji: "📜", grantComponent: "old_docket", text: "'It's in the strongbox,' Sowerby said at last, in the flat voice of a man who has decided that whatever happens next is less bad than another minute of this. 'The original. Only ever signed the once. I said as much to Mr Strick, years back. He said everyone does it this way.'\n\n'Everyone,' said Colworth, not for the first time that week, 'is not, on this evidence, doing it lawfully.' The strongbox, when fetched, held exactly what Sowerby had promised: one sheet, one signature, eleven years old and entirely alone.\n\nComponent found: The First Signature.", next: "converge" },
    chambers_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🥀", setMid: true, text: "Sowerby held to 'not my place' with the sheer stubborn endurance of a man defending the only position he had, and after the better part of an hour Colworth concluded, with some regret, that a magistrate has other cases and cannot spend a whole morning on one clerk's nerves, however deserved the spending might feel.\n\nSowerby went home considerably more shaken than he arrived and, as it later turned out, promptly told his wife everything, who told the woman next door, who told the greengrocer, so that by suppertime half the Under knew roughly what the Sump Bench was looking into anyway. Information, Colworth reflected afterward, finds its own way through a city, whatever the strongbox says.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "converge" },
    converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "Whichever road had produced it, Colworth now had the original docket in hand and the shape of the case clear in his mind: eleven years, one signature, forty-four Renewals that had never happened. That was the fact. What he still needed was the sum, and the law, set down properly enough that no advocate could argue either one away.\n\nFor the sum, he would need Strick's own ledger, however reluctantly produced. For the law, he would need the Act itself, in full, not the summary everyone quoted at each other in place of reading it.", next: "ledger_puzzle" },
    ledger_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📒", text: "Strick brought the ledger to the Bench himself this time, under a summons rather than a request, and set it down with the resigned care of a man handing over evidence against himself. The compounding was all there, worked correctly by its own crooked rule: three sovereigns, eleven years, five in the hundred, compounded annually, running out at a little over five sovereigns owed.\n\n'That's fair reckoning,' Strick said, not quite meeting anyone's eye. 'That's how compounding works.'\n\n'It is,' Colworth agreed. 'It is also not what an unrenewed tab earns under the Common Floor, which is simple interest only, on the original sum, and nothing more inventive than that.'", question: "Sarn's tab began at 3 sovereigns, eleven years ago. Under the Common Floor, an unrenewed tab earns simple interest only: a fixed 5% of the ORIGINAL principal, added once for every year that has passed. What does Sarn properly owe, in sovereigns, after eleven years?", options: ["4.65 sovereigns", "5.13 sovereigns", "6.30 sovereigns", "1.65 sovereigns"], answer: 0,
      hint: "Simple interest adds the same amount every year: a fixed percentage of the ORIGINAL principal, not of a growing balance. Work out one year's interest, multiply by the number of years, then add the original principal back on.",
      solution: "One year's simple interest is 5% of the original 3 sovereigns, which is 0.15 sovereigns. Over eleven years that is 11 × 0.15 = 1.65 sovereigns of interest. Added to the original principal, the debt properly owed is 3 + 1.65 = 4.65 sovereigns, not the 5.13 sovereigns Strick's compounding produced, and not the 6.30 sovereigns a doubled rate would give either.",
      good: "ledger_good", mid: "ledger_mid" },
    ledger_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📒", grantComponent: "chalk_ledger", text: "Colworth worked the sum through once, slowly, the way he worked everything, and then a second time to be sure, because being sure was the entire point of the exercise. Four sovereigns and sixty-five hundredths. Not five and thirteen hundredths. The difference was less than half a sovereign, which was, Colworth thought, a remarkably small hinge for a whole distress writ to be swinging on.\n\nStrick copied the correct sum down without argument, which was either honesty or exhaustion, and did not ask to keep the ledger. Colworth took it anyway.\n\nComponent found: Strick's Own Ledger.", next: "statute_check" },
    ledger_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", setMid: true, text: "Strick's own arithmetic muddied the water for a good half hour, arriving first at five sovereigns and thirteen hundredths, then, when pressed, at something closer to six and thirty, before Colworth simply took the slate from him and worked it through himself, aloud, step by step, until the number sat still.\n\nStrick accepted the correction, eventually, the way people accept things they have stopped having the energy to argue with. He did hand the ledger over, in the end, though the handing over had cost the morning rather more patience than Colworth had budgeted for it.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "statute_check" },
    statute_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "📚", stat: 2, dc: 13,
      text: "The Roll Office kept three separate printed copies of the Chalk Act, all bound in the same drab green cloth, all apparently identical, and all, on close inspection, subtly different: one summary for shopkeepers, one summary for clerks and one, filed two shelves higher than either and considerably dustier, the Act in full.\n\nOnly the full copy still carried its eleventh clause, the one setting out the Common Floor in exact words. The two summaries had trimmed it down to a single unhelpful line: 'interest as customary.' Colworth had to work out, from binding dates and catalogue marks alone, which of the three copies on the desk in front of him was the one nobody had shortened.",
      success: "statute_success", fail: "statute_fail" },
    statute_success: { type: "story", emoji: "📜", grantComponent: "statute_leaf", text: "The catalogue mark gave it away in the end: the full copy had been bound a full year before either summary existed, which meant it could not possibly be a copy of either of them, only the other way round. Colworth opened it to the eleventh clause and read it through twice, the actual words this time, not the tidy paraphrase everyone in Gifford had apparently agreed to trust instead.\n\nIt said, in language rather plainer than he had expected, exactly what he had begun to suspect: an unrenewed tab earns the Common Floor, and the Common Floor is simple interest, full stop, no further clauses attached. He tore the page free, carefully, along its original fold, on the grounds that a page people needed to actually read was of more use loose than shelved.\n\nComponent found: The Uncut Eleventh Clause.", next: "verdict_choice" },
    statute_fail: { type: "story", emoji: "🗄️", setMid: true, text: "The three green copies gave up their differences slowly, and Colworth worked through two entirely wrong guesses, tracing one summary's lineage back to the wrong parent volume, before the catalogue marks finally lined up correctly on the third attempt.\n\nBy then the light had gone and the Roll Office clerk was visibly keen to lock up for the night, so Colworth copied the eleventh clause out by hand instead of taking the original page, word for word, checked twice against the source. It would serve. It simply cost him an evening he had not planned on spending, and a hand cramped from careful copying.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "verdict_choice" },
    verdict_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "⚖️", text: "Colworth had, by his own quiet reckoning, everything he needed: the original, unrenewed docket, the ledger showing exactly how the wrong sum had been reached, and the Act's own words showing what the right one should be. What remained was how to say so.\n\nHe could read the ruling out in open court, in full, with the reasoning laid bare for anyone in the Under who cared to hear precisely how a widow's debt had nearly trebled itself on a technicality nobody had bothered to check. Or he could simply file the corrected writ through the clerks, quiet and proper, and let the paperwork speak for itself, as paperwork usually did in his court, and usually preferred to.\n\nNeither option troubled the law. One of them troubled Colworth rather more than he expected, which was itself worth noting.", options: [
        { label: "Read the ruling aloud, in open court", next: "public_reading", preview: "A d20 roll, with advantage, on nerve he does not usually need. Success earns bonus stars and a heart restored." },
        { label: "File the corrected writ quietly through the clerks", next: "quiet_filing", preview: "No risk, no reward. The ruling stands either way." },
      ] },
    public_reading: { type: "dice", emoji: "📢", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "The Sump Bench filled up faster than Colworth had ever seen it, word having travelled the way word travels in the Under, which is to say entirely and immediately. He stood to read the ruling aloud for the first time in longer than he cared to calculate, and found his own voice considerably less steady than his arithmetic.\n\nRoll 11 or more on a twenty-sided die to hold the room and get the whole reasoning out clearly. You roll with ADVANTAGE: roll twice and keep the higher, because Colworth has, for once, actually prepared.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher, what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fail. Each fails half the time, so both fail 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4 of the time, meaning you succeed 3/4 of the time. Advantage helps a nervous man considerably more than nerve alone would. +1 to your roll." },
      success: "public_good", fail: "public_fail" },
    public_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 2, bonusHearts: 1, text: "Colworth read the whole of it through, clause and sum and all, in a voice that found its footing somewhere around the third sentence and did not lose it again. The room, which had come expecting either a formality or a scandal, got instead a magistrate working through a division sum out loud and refusing to leave until everyone present understood exactly why it mattered.\n\nSarn Loom, at the back of the room, did not cheer. She simply sat very still, the way people sit when a thing they had stopped hoping for turns out to be arriving anyway. Somewhere near the door, Strick looked considerably relieved not to owe considerably more than he had thought he might.\n\nBonus: +2 ⭐ at the end of the adventure, and one heart restored.", next: "forge" },
    public_fail: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", setMid: true, text: "Colworth's voice held for the first two sentences and then went, quite suddenly, entirely procedural, flattening the whole careful reasoning into a recitation that lost half the room somewhere around the compounding. He finished it anyway, correctly, because correctness had never been the part in doubt.\n\nThe ruling stood. It simply landed with rather less weight than it might have, delivered in a voice that gave the room nothing to hold onto beyond the bare figures, and several people afterward asked Sarn Loom to explain what had actually just happened, because the magistrate certainly hadn't managed to.\n\n(The run slips to the middling path.)", next: "forge" },
    quiet_filing: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "Colworth wrote the corrected writ out by his own hand, checked it against all three components twice, and sent it through the clerks in the ordinary way, with no more ceremony than any other piece of paper crossing his desk that week.\n\nIt was, he told himself, the more proper channel. It was also, if he was honest with himself, which he tried to be roughly as often as anyone manages, rather easier. The corrected sum would reach Sarn Loom either way. Only the manner of its arriving was in question, and Colworth had never much rated manner over substance.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "⚙️", text: "Instrument-Warden Coyle kept a bench behind the Roll Office where broken measuring devices went to be either mended or honestly buried, and looked at the three items Colworth laid out on it: one old signature, one crooked ledger, one torn statute leaf, with the particular interest of someone who enjoys being asked to build something nobody has requested before.\n\n'A reckoner, is it,' she said, turning the pieces over. 'One that keeps the difference between compounded and simple in its bones, so nobody has to work it out from first principles every single time. Give me the evening.'",
      forged: "The reckoner Coyle handed back the next morning was a small brass thing, no bigger than a pocket watch, with two dials instead of one: a plain sum on the left face, the same sum properly floored on the right, so that anyone holding it could see at a glance exactly how far a claim had wandered from the law.\n\nColworth turned it over once, tested it against a sum he already knew the answer to, and found it correct to the last hundredth. It would not, he suspected, make the next case any less tedious. It would simply make the tedium considerably harder to argue with.\n\nThe Quick Reckoner is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
      notforged: "Coyle laid the pieces out on the bench and counted them twice, which did not take long, there being rather fewer than three.\n\n'Short a piece or two,' she said, not unkindly. 'A reckoner wants the whole set or it's just a pretty dial with nothing behind it. Come back when you've the full three. The bench isn't going anywhere, and neither, unfortunately, is the arithmetic.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
      grantItem: "gf_colworth_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "⚖️", text: "The corrected writ went out under Colworth's own seal: four sovereigns and sixty-five hundredths, payable at a rate Sarn Loom could actually meet without losing her rooms over it, and a note appended to Strick's shop licence reminding him, in the driest language the Act allowed, that custom is not law and never has been, however comfortably the two sit together when nobody checks.\n\nIt was, in the grand ledger of the Sump Bench, a small case. Three sovereigns, some interest, a widow, a grocer and a clerk who would think rather more carefully about strongboxes from now on. Nobody outside the Under would ever hear of it. Colworth filed it away himself, in the ordinary manner, and found, somewhat to his own surprise, that he minded rather less than usual doing so.\n\nHe would hear larger cases before the year was out, cases with rather more at stake than four sovereigns and a widow's rooms, cases that would take considerably longer than an afternoon and matter considerably more than chalk. One of them, when it finally reached his lectern, would concern a young Sirrath and a Calibration result that did not add up either, though that was a docket for another day, and today's was smaller, and simpler, and entirely his own.\n\nFor now, Colworth closed the Loom file, set it on the shelf where closed files went, and reached, without any particular feeling one way or the other, for the next docket in the stack. It was, he noted, considerably more legible than the last one. Small mercies. He had learned to take them where the stack allowed.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end",
      goodText: "The Sump Bench's clerks spoke of the Loom ruling for months afterward, in the careful, approving way clerks speak of paperwork that finally came out right. Sarn Loom kept her rooms, paid down the true debt at a rate that left her enough for coal and sent Colworth, entirely against custom, a small parcel of dried lavender with no note attached, which he kept in his desk drawer and never once explained to anyone who asked.\n\nStrick amended his practice, grudgingly at first and then, as the months passed, rather more thoroughly than the law strictly required, on the grounds that a second visit from the Sump Bench was not an experience he cared to repeat. The Uncut Eleventh Clause went back to the Roll Office, properly catalogued this time, under its own listing, so that the next magistrate who needed it would not have to hunt through two decoy summaries to find it. Colworth would never have called any of this satisfying. But he read his dockets rather more carefully after that, footnotes and all, and something in the reading had, very quietly, stopped being nothing at all.",
      midText: "The ruling held, and Sarn Loom kept her rooms, though the case left the Sump Bench a good deal more tired than it needed to and Colworth privately suspected he had made rather harder work of a straightforward sum than the sum itself deserved. Strick paid the corrected debt without further argument, Sowerby went back to his ledgers considerably more nervous about strongboxes and the Uncut Eleventh Clause was filed away, correctly if a little late.\n\nIt was not the clean, quick resolution Colworth privately preferred, on the rare occasions he preferred anything. It was, nonetheless, the right one, arrived at the long way round, which he supposed, turning back to the next docket in the stack, was still arriving.",
      grantItem: "gf_colworth_item2" },
  },
},

gf_pip: {
  id: "gf_pip",
  title: "Given a Face on Purpose",
  tagline: "It is Chalk-Day in the Under, and small Pip must find his family's place in a queue of hundreds, all on his own.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_pip_item1", name: "Rooke's Knotted Tally-Cord", stat: 0, how: "found", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won with a sharp Arithmetic check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_pip_item2", name: "The Fair-Hearth Docket", stat: 2, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_pip_item3", name: "The Named Docket-Tag", stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { sound_chalk: "A Sound Chalk-Stub", steady_peg: "A Steady Queue-Peg", true_weight: "A True Copper Ounce" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "Chalk-Day came round on Ferrow Lane every fortnight, rain or shine or Turn-fog, and it was the day the corner shop's slate got settled and the coal ration got counted at Cindergate Hall in the Under. It used to be Rooke who walked him there, holding his hand tight through the worst of the crowd and doing all the sums in her head before the clerk had even finished asking. Rooke was at the university now, learning things Pip could not picture, and today, for the first time in his life, it was Pip and nobody else who carried the family's chalk-slate and the little cloth purse of grist and cogs, and Pip who would have to make the count come right. He was seven, and small even for seven, and he had practised tying the purse-string forty times the night before so it would not come loose in a crowd. He set off down Ferrow Lane with his coat buttoned to the chin and the whole weight of Number Twelve's fortnight balanced in one small fist.", next: "skillcheck1" },
    skillcheck1: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🪙", stat: 0, dc: 11, text: "At Widderscombe's corner shop the slate above the counter was a scrawl of chalk numbers three weeks deep, and old Widderscombe herself, gap-toothed and not unkind, told Pip plainly that she could not read her own writing any more and would take whatever he counted out as the truth of it, since he was clearly a careful sort of boy. Pip tipped his purse onto the counter, a little heap of grist and cogs and one precious mark, and set himself to counting it against the scrawled total before the queue behind him lost patience.", success: "item1_found", fail: "item1_missed" },
    item1_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🧵", grantItem: "gf_pip_item1", text: "The count came right on the first try, eleven cogs and four grist and the one mark besides, exactly matching the smudged total, and Widderscombe swept it into her tin without another word, which from Widderscombe was practically a blessing. Reaching into his coat pocket for a scrap of paper to note the shop paid in full, Pip's fingers found something he had forgotten was there: a length of soft old cord, knotted at careful intervals, worn smooth from handling. Rooke's counting-cord, the one she used to run through her fingers when a sum got knotty in her head, left in the pocket of a coat she'd once outgrown and passed down to him. He held it a moment, then knotted it twice round his wrist and tucked it under his sleeve, and it sat there warm as a hand.", next: "gate_fight" },
    item1_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The coins would not come right no matter how many times Pip counted them, and Widderscombe, sighing, took the lot and called it settled enough, which was Widderscombe's way of being kind without saying so. Pip's coat pocket held nothing but a boiled sweet gone soft and a receipt he could not read. No matter. There was still the queue to face, and the coal to count, and a boy does not need a lucky cord to do sums, only patience and a clear head.", next: "gate_fight" },
    gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🐕", qLevel: 2, text: "The alley gate to Cindergate Hall had an iron turnstile that had not turned properly since before Pip was born, and wedged half through it, in the manner of a small determined obstacle, was Ha'penny, the queue-terrier, who considered the turnstile her own personal toy and would not be moved by reason, only by counting, which was the one thing that ever calmed her down.", enemy: { name: "Ha'penny the Queue-Terrier", emoji: "🐕", hits: 2, toHit: 8, hitText: "Ha'penny darts between Pip's ankles and the turnstile gives an alarming clank, nearly toppling him.", strikeText: "Pip crouches and counts her wagging tail-beats out loud, slow and steady, the way you'd count sheep at bedtime.", defeatText: "Somewhere around the ninth count Ha'penny's ears go soft and her tail slows to match Pip's counting exactly, and she wriggles free of the turnstile and trots off pleased with herself, leaving the gate to swing open at last." }, victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The turnstile clanked open properly for the first time in memory, and Pip slipped through into the yard before Cindergate Hall, where the queue for the coal-count already stretched further than he could see the end of. He squared his shoulders the way he had seen Rooke do, and went to find where a Halloway was meant to stand.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🍃", text: "Ha'penny was in no mood to be talked round just yet, so Pip did the sensible thing and squeezed himself through the gap where the turnstile did not quite meet the post, coat buttons and all. No harm done, and the queue for the coal-count stretched out ahead of him regardless, further than he could see the end of.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🪧", text: "The yard before Cindergate Hall was cut into three ragged lanes by a dry old fountain nobody had seen work in living memory, and a chalked board nailed to its rim read FERROW, GULLIVER, ASHDOWN in letters meant to sort the whole queue by street. But the crowd today was thicker than Pip had ever seen it, Turn-fog due within the hour by the smell of the wind, and nobody was quite standing where the board said they ought. He would have to find his own way through to a place that made sense.", options: [
      { label: "The Weighing Trestle", next: "routeA_setup", preview: "Where the grain sacks are weighed out, and the sums decide who eats what." },
      { label: "The Slick Steps", next: "routeB_setup", preview: "The quickest way up to the Hall doors, if your feet can be trusted on wet stone." },
      { label: "The Cinder Yard", next: "routeC_setup", preview: "Round the back, where a barrow has got loose among the crates." },
    ] },
    routeA_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "⚖️", text: "At the Weighing Trestle a tired weighmaster was trying to split three sacks of grain fairly between two waiting families, the Ashworths and the Pembertons, and getting nowhere, because the Pembertons had twice as many little ones and everybody agreed, in the vague way people agree on fairness, that they ought to get more, without anybody agreeing how much more. 'You look like a boy who can do sums,' the weighmaster said, not very hopefully. 'Prove it, and there's a chalk-stub in it for you. I dropped mine somewhere in this sack and haven't the patience left to dig for it.'", next: "routeA_challenge" },
    routeA_challenge: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🌾", text: "The weighmaster tipped out the scales and waited, chalk-stained and hopeful.", question: "There are 36 pounds of grain to share between the Ashworth and Pemberton families in the ratio 1:2, because the Pembertons have twice as many children. How many pounds does the Pemberton family get?", options: ["24 pounds", "12 pounds", "18 pounds", "30 pounds"], answer: 0, hint: "Add the ratio numbers together to find how many equal parts the grain splits into, then work out what one part weighs.", solution: "The ratio 1:2 makes 1+2=3 equal parts. Each part is 36 divided by 3, which is 12 pounds. The Pembertons take 2 parts, so 2 times 12 is 24 pounds.", good: "routeA_good", mid: "routeA_mid" },
    routeA_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", grantComponent: "sound_chalk", text: "'Twenty-four pounds to the Pembertons, twelve to the Ashworths, and nobody short-changed,' said Pip, and both families nodded at once in the particular way people do when a sum has settled an argument nobody wanted to keep having. The weighmaster dug about in the sack and came up with a stub of good sound chalk, worn but honest, and pressed it into Pip's hand. 'That's yours fair and square. Mind you keep your own counts as tidy as that one.'", next: "converge1" },
    routeA_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "Pip's numbers came out lopsided, and the weighmaster, not unkindly, worked it out himself while Pip stood by feeling his ears go hot. 'Never mind, lad, you were close enough to be worth teaching properly one day.' The chalk-stub stayed lost somewhere in the sack of grain, and Pip moved on with his ears still warm.", next: "converge1" },
    routeB_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🪜", text: "The Slick Steps ran up the side of Cindergate Hall, worn hollow by a thousand fortnights of queueing feet and slippery with the damp that always came before Turn-fog. A queue-warden with a battered die stood at the bottom, letting folk try their luck up the quick way rather than the long way round. 'Three or better and you're up dry,' he said, tossing Pip the die. 'Less than that and you're up wet. Your choice, small one, but think before you throw, that's the whole trick of it.'", next: "routeB_challenge" },
    routeB_challenge: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 3, text: "Pip weighed the die in his palm and thought about his odds before he threw it up the Slick Steps.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 3 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "2/3", "1/3", "5/6"], answer: 1, why: "Rolls of 3, 4, 5 and 6 all succeed, that is 4 outcomes out of 6, and 4/6 = 2/3. Predicting the odds correctly steadies you: +1 to your roll." }, success: "routeB_good", fail: "routeB_mid" },
    routeB_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", grantComponent: "sound_chalk", text: "The die came up kind, and Pip went up the steps two at a time, dry as a stored biscuit, right into a clerk hurrying down the other way who dropped a stub of chalk in his flurry and didn't stop to notice. Pip called out to give it back, but the clerk was already gone into the crowd, so he pocketed it instead, reasoning that lost things found honestly were fair game, and sound chalk was sound chalk wherever it came from.", next: "converge1" },
    routeB_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "💧", text: "The die came up unkind, and Pip's feet went from under him on the third step, landing him soundly on the damp stone with his dignity more bruised than anything else. He picked himself up, checked the coins were still tied tight, and took the long way round instead, chalkless but unharmed.", next: "converge1" },
    routeC_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🛒", text: "Round the back of the Hall, in the Cinder Yard where the empty coal-sacks were stacked to dry, a hand-barrow had slipped its brake on the cobbled slope and was rattling downhill entirely of its own accord, aimed with terrible enthusiasm at a teetering stack of crates. Someone had left a writing-slate box balanced on top of those very crates, and through the crack in its lid Pip could see a whole tin of spare chalk-stubs.", next: "routeC_challenge" },
    routeC_challenge: { type: "combat", emoji: "🛒", qLevel: 2, text: "The runaway barrow trundled and clattered downhill, and Pip would have to time his dash exactly right to reach the crates and steady them before the barrow arrived, or the whole stack, chalk-box and all, would go over.", enemy: { name: "the Loose Coal-Barrow", emoji: "🛒", hits: 2, toHit: 8, hitText: "The barrow clips the corner of the stack and a crate wobbles alarmingly, nearly catching Pip's shoulder.", strikeText: "Pip counts the barrow's wheel-clatters against his own running steps, working out exactly when to plant himself and shove.", defeatText: "On the count of four Pip plants both feet and shoves the barrow's nose sideways into a heap of empty sacks, soft as anything, where it comes to a harmless, sighing stop. The crates stand steady. Nothing broke, and nobody so much as scraped a knee." }, victory: "routeC_good", retreat: "routeC_mid" },
    routeC_good: { type: "story", emoji: "📦", grantComponent: "sound_chalk", text: "With the barrow safely beached in the sacks, Pip climbed the steadied crates and lifted down the writing-slate box, its lid catching on a splinter. Inside sat a dozen stubs of good sound chalk, and he took one, leaving the rest for whichever clerk had misplaced the box, with a note scrawled in his best careful letters: FOUND SAFE, ASK AT THE TRESTLE.", next: "converge1" },
    routeC_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The barrow got away from him after all, thumping softly into the sack-pile of its own accord a moment after Pip dived clear, and though no harm was done, the crates shifted and the chalk-box tumbled into a gap between two coal-sheds too narrow for a boy to follow. Pip dusted himself off, chalkless for now, and pressed on toward the Hall doors.", next: "converge1" },
    converge1: { type: "story", emoji: "🌆", text: "Whichever way he had come, Pip found himself at last at the foot of the Hall steps, coat a little the worse for the morning and the queue around him thicker and closer than ever, the smell of Turn-fog now unmistakable on the wind. Somewhere ahead, behind the great doors, sat the ledger that would decide whether Number Twelve, Ferrow Lane, got its fair fortnight of coal. But first, he remembered, there was the queue-rope to cross, strung wobbling across the yard to keep the lanes from tangling into one great confused knot of children.", next: "dice2_setup" },
    dice2_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🪢", text: "The queue-rope ran waist-high across the yard on a row of pegs, and one peg near the middle had worked loose in the crush, letting the whole rope sag dangerously close to the mud. A queue-warden, harried and shorthanded, called out that whoever could steady the rope and cross without bringing the whole lane down could keep the loose peg for their trouble, and welcome to it.", next: "dice2" },
    dice2: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 4, text: "Pip judged he would need a firm push to reset the peg and a steady step to cross after, a 4 or better on the warden's worn old counting-die.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 4 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/3", "1/2", "2/3"], answer: 2, why: "Rolls of 4, 5 and 6 succeed, that is 3 outcomes out of 6, and 3/6 = 1/2. Predicting the odds correctly steadies you: +1 to your roll." }, success: "comp2_good", fail: "comp2_mid" },
    comp2_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔩", grantComponent: "steady_peg", text: "The peg went home with a satisfying thunk and the rope drew taut and true along the whole lane, and three separate queues sighed with relief all at once. The warden, delighted, told Pip to keep the old peg as a thank-you, since a boy who could set a queue straight had earned a keepsake of it. Pip turned the little steady peg over in his fingers, notched and worn, and pocketed it beside the chalk-stub.", next: "puzzle3_setup" },
    comp2_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🌧️", setMid: true, text: "The peg slipped from Pip's fingers at the worst possible moment and the rope sagged into the mud after all, and it took three grown men to sort the tangle that followed while Pip stood well back, red-eared and apologetic. The steady peg went into someone else's pocket in the confusion. No matter. There was still the coal to count, and the day was not done with him yet.", next: "puzzle3_setup" },
    puzzle3_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "Inside the Hall doors at last, in the press and the lamp-smoke, Pip found the coal-scale itself, a great iron balance overseen by a countkeeper with brass-rimmed spectacles and the air of somebody who had weighed out fairness for thirty years and trusted it about as far as she could throw it. Three mudlark children from the riverside, all cold and none of them related, were arguing fiercely over a single relief-sack of coal meant to be split between their three hearths according to how many little ones huddled at each. 'Settle it,' said the countkeeper to Pip, not looking up from her ledger, 'and there's a true copper ounce-weight in it for you. I haven't the patience left this morning, and you look like you might.'", next: "puzzle3" },
    puzzle3: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "The countkeeper laid out the numbers without looking up.", question: "A relief-sack holds 45 pounds of coal, to be shared between three households in the ratio 4:3:2, matching how many children shelter in each. How many pounds does the household with the largest share receive?", options: ["20 pounds", "15 pounds", "25 pounds", "18 pounds"], answer: 0, hint: "Add the ratio numbers together to find how many equal parts the coal splits into, then find what one part weighs before giving the biggest household its share.", solution: "The ratio 4:3:2 adds up to 4+3+2=9 equal parts. Sharing 45 pounds into 9 parts gives 45 divided by 9, which is 5 pounds per part. The largest share is 4 parts, so 4 times 5 is 20 pounds.", good: "comp3_good", mid: "comp3_mid" },
    comp3_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🟤", grantComponent: "true_weight", text: "'Twenty, fifteen and ten, and that's fair by anybody's fire,' said Pip, and the three mudlarks, checking it against their own fingers, found they could not argue with it, which for three mudlarks was practically a miracle. The countkeeper allowed herself half a smile, the first Pip had seen on anybody all morning, and slid a small true copper ounce-weight across the counter to him, cool and exact in his palm. 'Keep your counting that honest,' she said, 'and you'll do.'", next: "forge" },
    comp3_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", setMid: true, text: "Pip's ratio came out uneven, and the mudlarks, sharp as knives about their own hunger, spotted it before he'd finished speaking and fell to arguing worse than before, until the countkeeper sorted it herself with a weary sigh and kept the copper weight for someone steadier. Pip slunk off toward the ledger desk, feeling smaller than seven years old usually felt.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔨", text: "By the ledger desk, mending the queue-rope's worn pegs with enormous patient paws, sat Old Sull, a Sirrath line-warden with a grey-striped hide gone soft at the muzzle and a voice like a kettle just before the boil. She looked over whatever Pip had gathered with one lazy amber eye. 'A chalk-stub, a queue-peg, a copper weight,' she rumbled. 'Odd sort of hoard for a boy your size. Show me, and if it's the whole of it, I'll make you something worth having, so nobody in this yard forgets your name by dinnertime.'", forged: "Old Sull turned the sound chalk-stub, the steady peg and the true copper weight over in one huge striped paw, weighed them thoughtfully against nothing at all, and set to work with a small brass punch and a scrap of tag-leather from her apron. Three taps, a careful stamp, and a length of waxed cord threaded through a hole no bigger than a pinprick, and she pressed into Pip's palm a small brass tag, stamped not with a number but with his own name, HALLOWAY, P, in proper copperplate. 'A Named Docket-Tag,' she said. 'Wear it where the desk clerks can see it, and you'll never again be filed as a headcount when you're plainly a boy.'", notforged: "Old Sull turned over whatever little Pip had managed to gather and shook her great striped head, not unkindly. 'Two out of three won't stamp a proper tag, small one, no more than two legs make a good queue-rope. Come back when you've the whole of it, and mind you don't lose what you've got in the meantime, this yard eats loose chalk for breakfast.'", grantItem: "gf_pip_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The Turn chose that exact moment to vent, as it did four times every day whether or not the Under was ready for it, and a wall of hot white fog rolled down between the Hall and the yard, and the whole queue surged forward at once in the particular panic of people who cannot see where the front of the line has got to. Coats and caps and satchels all went sideways together, and somewhere in the crush a harried clerk, unable to see faces for fog, began simply counting grey coats aloud and waving them toward a roped-off corner to wait for someone to claim them. Pip felt himself lifted half off his feet by the crowd and set down again facing the wrong way entirely, and before he had drawn one more breath he was standing in the corner with perhaps forty other small children in identical charity grey, all of them somebody's, none of them, for the moment, anybody in particular. He held very still, the way you do when you are trying not to become more lost than you already are, and closed his fist tight around the little brass tag at his collar.\n\n'Forty-one,' the clerk was saying to nobody, not unkindly, only tiredly, ticking a slate. 'Forty-one grey coats, small, various.'\n\nIt was a Laachmi clerk who found him properly, a tall horned woman named Nettle who kept the main ledger and had, Pip now remembered, once given Rooke a peppermint for getting a whole column of sums right three years running. She was working her way along the roped corner peering at faces rather than coats, the way almost nobody bothered to any more, and when she reached Pip she stopped.\n\n'I know you,' she said, and it was not a question. 'Halloway, isn't it. The little one who used to trail after that sharp-tongued sister of his and correct the ledger from the queue, cheeky article.' She crouched to his height, which for a Laachmi meant folding a considerable distance, and read the brass tag at his collar without needing to be shown it. 'Pip Halloway. There. That's better than small, various, I should think.'\n\nPip found he could breathe again all at once, in the way you can only once you notice you had stopped.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end", goodText: "Nettle walked him back through the fog herself, one warm dry hand on his shoulder the whole way, and at the ledger desk she wrote HALLOWAY, NO.12 FERROW LANE, HEARTH OF FIVE against the coal ration in her own careful copperplate, not a headcount tally but a name with a street and a hearth attached to it, the way it ought always to have been done. She pressed the Fair-Hearth Docket into his hand along with a peppermint from the same tin, for old times' sake, and told him to give her regards to his sister when he next wrote to her, since she rather thought the university was wasting a girl who could count a queue that fast. Pip walked home through the thinning fog with the docket safe in one fist and the little brass tag warm against his collar, already working out, quite privately, exactly how he meant to tell Rooke about all of it, every last true number of it, the very next time he saw her.", midText: "It took a soaking on the steps, a lost chalk-stub or two, and one thoroughly frightening minute among forty strangers in identical grey coats, but Nettle found him all the same, and walked him to the ledger desk, and wrote his name against the family's coal instead of leaving him a number in a headcount. Some of the finer things stayed lost this morning, a peg down a gap, a weight kept by a tired countkeeper, waiting for a steadier day. But the docket was fair and the name was his own, and Pip walked home through the last of the fog turning that over and over, deciding it had, on the whole, been a good sort of terrifying, and one he would tell Rooke every detail of, the moment he next had the chance.", grantItem: "gf_pip_item2" },
  },
},

gf_adavoss: {
  id: "gf_adavoss",
  title: "The Weight of a Good Reason",
  tagline: "A second marking season, a second paper that will not add up the way it is supposed to, and Second Examiner Ada Voss has to decide, again, what a good reason is worth.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_adavoss_item1", name: "The Marginal Loupe", stat: 0, how: "found", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won with a sharp Arithmetic check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_adavoss_item2", name: "The Second Chop", stat: 2, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_adavoss_item3", name: "The Weighed Verdict", stat: 3, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Science. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { corroboration: "The Corroborated Timeline", primer: "The Boiler-Row Primer", archive_tally: "The Five-Year Tally" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "Second Examiner Ada Voss had marked eleven sittings of the Calibration since the one everybody in the examiners' room still called, carefully, 'the business with the boy from Ferrow Lane,' and not one word more than that. Eleven sittings, and she had learned to read a script the way you read weather: for the shape of the thing, not just the numbers written on it.\n\nThe script in front of her now belonged to a girl called Sorrel Kettle, stratum not yet posted, boiler row, Cutlers' Wynd. Fourteen years old. Marked once, properly, by a First Examiner who had initialled every page in a hand steady enough to trust. Twenty-six answers right out of thirty. Four wrong.\n\nIt was the four that had been sent up to Ada's desk in a folder with a Tallow Street docket clipped to the front, because somebody downstairs had noticed what Ada was now noticing herself, and had not liked the look of it enough to sign off alone.\n\nThe four wrong answers were not random. They were wrong by a small amount that was always in the same direction and always explained by the same habit: rounding at the end of every step instead of once at the very end. It took Ada three read-throughs to be sure she was not imagining it, and a fourth to be sure she was not hoping to.", next: "loupe_check" },
    loupe_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔍", stat: 0, dc: 12,
      text: "A First Examiner initials a script when they trust their own arithmetic. That does not mean nobody else's arithmetic is owed a second look, which is the entire reason the title 'Second Examiner' exists and the entire reason the Office pretends, most weeks, that it does not matter.\n\nAda set the four wrong workings side by side under the good lamp and did each sum four separate ways, on four separate scraps of paper, refusing to let her pencil agree with itself out of laziness.\n\nIf the pattern was going to survive a properly hostile check, it had better survive Ada's own first.",
      success: "loupe_find", fail: "loupe_miss" },
    loupe_find: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantItem: "gf_adavoss_item1", text: "It survived. Four times over, in ink, then in pencil, then in the back of her own head with her eyes shut for good measure: every one of Sorrel Kettle's wrong answers was short of the true answer by a whole unit, worked out by rounding after every step instead of once at the end. That is not carelessness. Carelessness is never that polite about where it goes wrong.\n\nIn the drawer under the good lamp, where three generations of Second Examiners had kept whatever oddments made the job survivable, Ada found the loupe her own predecessor had left her: brass, chipped at one edge, magnifying nothing so much as it magnified the habit of looking twice. She had not used it in a year. She used it now.\n\nThe Marginal Loupe is yours. (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)", next: "gate_contention" },
    loupe_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "The sum would not sit still for her. Ada tried it four ways and got four faintly different confirmations, close enough to be the pattern and loose enough that a hostile examiner, a real one, the kind who wanted the answer to be nothing at all, could pick the whole thing apart in a single sentence.\n\nShe put the loupe back in the drawer where it had sat gathering dust since her predecessor's retirement, and told herself the pattern would still be there in the morning, whether or not she had it proven to the standard she wanted.\n\n(The Marginal Loupe stays in the drawer this time. Another run through this adventure may find it.)", next: "gate_contention" },
    gate_contention: { type: "combat", emoji: "🔒", qLevel: 2,
      text: "Scripts under discretionary review did not simply sit on a Second Examiner's desk. They lived, officially, in the Sealed Script Room three floors down, and getting one back out after hours required a signature from Warden Fitch, who had held the Sealed Script Room's one good lock for nineteen years and treated every request against it as a personal insult to the lock.\n\n'Reopening a closed script,' Fitch said, not looking up from his ledger, 'is a Contention, same as any other. Want it back out past the hour of closing, you argue it out past the hour of closing. Rules don't care whose desk the folder was found on.'\n\nAda had won Contentions against harder men than Fitch. She had simply never enjoyed winning one less.",
      enemy: { name: "Warden Fitch", emoji: "🔒", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Ada's working came out clean and quick, and Fitch's objection, whatever it had been going to be, folded itself back up and went into his pocket.",
        strikeText: "Fitch found a clause Ada had not accounted for and read it out at her with real satisfaction, and for a moment the whole Contention tipped his way.",
        defeatText: "Fitch closed his ledger, which from Fitch counted as total surrender, and slid the signature across the counter without another word." },
      victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🗝️", text: "'Midnight,' Fitch said, stamping the release docket harder than the docket had strictly earned. 'Back by midnight, signed in, or it's my job as well as yours, and I've less patience for losing mine than you have for losing yours.'\n\nIt was not friendliness. Ada had stopped expecting friendliness from the Sealed Script Room some years ago. It was, however, a signature, and a signature was the only currency that mattered on this floor of the building.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "Fitch won the Contention on a technicality so narrow Ada could have posted a letter through it, and the script stayed exactly where it was, three floors down, behind his one good lock.\n\nThere were other ways into a locked room than through its warden, though, slower ones and less official ones, and Ada had never once in nineteen years found Fitch's rulebook covering everything.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🧭", text: "A script cannot simply be re-marked on a hunch, even a hunch with four scraps of pencil working behind it. A Second Examiner who wants a pattern to survive contact with the Office needs it corroborated from outside the paper itself, in at least one place the Office cannot wave away as an examiner's private opinion.\n\nThree doors stood open to her, and none of them looked comfortable.\n\nThrough the first, the invigilator who had watched over Sorrel Kettle's row on the day, and might remember more than her own log admitted to. Through the second, the script itself, held up to a proper light and a proper eye for what its ink was actually saying. Through the third, five years of the Rustbound archive, and whoever in the record office had decided that was theirs to guard.", options: [
      { label: "Call on the invigilator, Mrs Grieve", next: "route_invig", preview: "Question the woman who watched the row. A probability puzzle about her spot-checks stands between you and a straight answer." },
      { label: "Examine the script itself, ink and working", next: "route_script", preview: "Read the paper the way a Second Examiner should. A real Calibration-style maths puzzle guards what the arithmetic actually proves." },
      { label: "Face down the Rustbound archive clerk", next: "route_archive", preview: "Argue your way past a man guarding five years of records he was never meant to have opinions about. A Contention, not a chat." },
    ] },
    route_invig: { type: "story", emoji: "☕", text: "Mrs Grieve had invigilated the boiler-row sitting for six years and kept a log neat enough to eat your dinner off, which was more than could be said for most things in the Under. She did not want a Second Examiner in her kitchen, and said so, and put the kettle on anyway, which in the Under is not a contradiction. It is a system.\n\n'Spot-checks,' she said, once Ada finally got the question out straight. 'Four rounds, that sitting. I check one desk in three, each round, at random, same as always. Girl's desk got checked twice out of the four. Nothing on her, nothing near her. If she'd had so much as a slide-rule under that desk, I'd have had it.'\n\n'Would you, though,' Ada said, not unkindly. 'Truly. Every single time.'\n\nMrs Grieve's mouth went thin, the way mouths do when a fair question lands somewhere fairer than the asker meant it to. 'Sit down,' she said, 'and I'll show you the arithmetic of it, because I've done this six years and I still don't like the answer half so much as I ought to.'", next: "invig_dice" },
    invig_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 4,
      text: "'Each round I check one desk in three, at random,' Mrs Grieve said, setting out four teacups like a diagram. 'So for any one desk, a check that round catches trouble two times in three, and misses it one time in three. Her desk got checked twice. Work out, before you write anything in that docket of yours, what your own two checks were actually worth.' Roll 4 or more on a six-sided die to hold your nerve while you say the answer out loud, in front of a woman who has done this arithmetic in her head for six years and will know at once if you get it wrong.",
      predict: { question: "A spot-check catches a hidden device with probability 2/3, and misses it with probability 1/3. Two independent spot-checks are made on the same desk. What is the probability that BOTH checks miss it, so nothing at all gets caught, even though something was there?", options: ["2/3", "1/9", "4/9", "1/3"], answer: 1, why: "Each check misses independently with probability 1/3. Two independent misses multiply: 1/3 × 1/3 = 1/9. So even with two checks at these odds, there is still a one in nine chance a hidden device would go completely undetected, which is a real gap worth reckoning with, not a certainty either way." },
      success: "invig_good", fail: "invig_mid" },
    invig_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧵", grantComponent: "corroboration", text: "Ada said it plainly, one in nine, no hedging, and watched Mrs Grieve's face ease by exactly that much.\n\n'One in nine,' Mrs Grieve repeated, trying the number on for size. 'Small. Not nothing. I've turned that gap over for six years and never once had anyone else say it out loud to me first.' She poured a fifth cup nobody had asked for. 'For what it's worth, examiner, small as it is, I'd stake my kettle on there being nothing under that desk. She didn't need a device. Some children just count faster than the rest of us, and always have.'\n\nIt was not proof. It was, though, a first honest thread pulled loose from a story the Office would much rather stayed knotted.\n\nThe Corroborated Timeline is one thread closer.", next: "converge2" },
    invig_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🧵", setMid: true, grantComponent: "corroboration", text: "Ada's answer came out close but not clean, a nine mistaken for a six in the saying of it, and Mrs Grieve corrected her, patiently, the way a woman corrects a kettle she has boiled a thousand times before.\n\n'One in nine, near enough,' Mrs Grieve said, once they had worked it through properly between them. 'You'd have had it exact on a steadier morning. Still. Near enough is where most true answers start.'\n\nIt was a scrappier thread than Ada would have liked, pulled loose with help rather than alone, but a thread all the same, and threads do not care how tidily they were found.\n\nThe Corroborated Timeline is one thread closer, however it got there.", next: "converge2" },
    route_script: { type: "story", emoji: "💡", text: "The Sealed Script Room lent Ada exactly one hour and exactly one lamp, and the lamp was not generous. She laid Sorrel Kettle's four wrong answers flat on the table and read them the way the loupe had taught her: not for the number at the bottom, but for the arithmetic breathing underneath it.\n\nQuestion nineteen of the paper was the clearest. It concerned a boiler losing pressure, which was, Ada reflected, about as Under a question as the Calibration ever dared to set.\n\nThe mark scheme's model answer worked the loss in one clean run of multiplication, start to finish. Sorrel Kettle's working, marked wrong by the scheme's own reckoning, rounded the pressure to the nearest whole unit at the end of every single hour before carrying on. It was not the taught method. Ada meant to prove, by doing the sum properly herself, in front of witnesses, exactly what that method had cost the girl and exactly why.", next: "script_puzzle" },
    script_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚙️", text: "The question sat on the desk in the First Examiner's own careful hand, mark scheme clipped beneath it. 'Take your time,' Ada told herself, in the voice she used on nervous candidates, and meant it for once.",
      question: "A boiler holds pressure at 620 units. A slow leak drops the pressure by 12% of its current value every hour. Worked exactly, with no rounding until the very end, what is the pressure after 3 hours, to the nearest whole unit?",
      options: ["423", "422", "460", "406"], answer: 0,
      hint: "Losing 12% each hour leaves 88% of the previous hour's pressure. Multiply by 0.88 three times running, and round only at the very last step.",
      solution: "Exactly: 620 × 0.88 × 0.88 × 0.88 = 620 × 0.681472 = 422.51264, which rounds to 423. Sorrel Kettle's working rounded to the nearest whole unit after every hour instead of once at the end: 620 → 546 → 480 → 422, landing one whole unit short of the true answer, every single time she used the method, in the same direction, for the same reason. That is not a mistake behaving like a mistake. Mistakes wander. This one walks in a straight line.",
      good: "script_good", mid: "script_mid" },
    script_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧵", grantComponent: "corroboration", text: "Ada worked it through twice under the ungenerous lamp and got 423 both times, clean, no hedging. Sorrel Kettle's script said 422, plain as anything, wrong by exactly one, in exactly the direction premature rounding always errs.\n\nShe checked the other three disputed answers the same way, hour by careful hour, and found the same straight line each time: not a girl who could not do the sum, but a girl who had been taught to do it a different way, a way that cost her one whole unit here and there and never once cost her the shape of the answer.\n\nThat is arithmetic behaving exactly like arithmetic. Nobody smuggled a straight line into a script. Straight lines are what a method looks like from a distance.\n\nThe Corroborated Timeline is one thread closer.", next: "converge2" },
    script_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🧵", setMid: true, grantComponent: "corroboration", text: "Ada's first pass came out at 422, the same as the script, and for one cold minute she thought she had proved the examiners right instead of the girl. It was only on the second pass, forcing herself to carry every decimal through to the very last step instead of tidying as she went, that the true 423 turned up and stayed turned up.\n\nIt was a slower thread than she would have liked, and it had made her doubt herself in a way she did not enjoy remembering, but a thread all the same, once she had finally stopped rounding along with the very habit she was meant to be catching.\n\nThe Corroborated Timeline is one thread closer, however long it took to pull.", next: "converge2" },
    route_archive: { type: "story", emoji: "🗄️", text: "The Rustbound archive lived in a basement under the record office that the record office pretended, most weeks, not to have a key to. Getting into it properly meant going through a clerk called Under-Warden Speck, who had spent thirty years deciding what counted as somebody else's business.\n\n'Five years of boiler-row scripts,' Speck said, once Ada stated her business plainly, the way she stated everything. 'That's not a Second Examiner's business. That's an appeals matter, and appeals go through the Office, in writing, with a docket, same as always.'\n\n'I'm not appealing anything yet,' Ada said. 'I'm trying to find out whether I need to.'\n\n'Same difference, to my ledger,' said Speck, and did not move from the doorway.", next: "archive_fight" },
    archive_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🗄️", qLevel: 2,
      text: "Speck folded his arms in the doorway of the basement like a man who had folded his arms in that exact doorway a thousand times before, and won most of them.\n\n'Appropriate channels,' he said. 'There's a form. There's always a form. You'll find the form takes about as long as five years takes.'\n\n'Then we'd best begin,' Ada said, and did not reach for any form at all.",
      enemy: { name: "Under-Warden Speck", emoji: "🗄️", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Ada's reasoning came out exact enough that Speck's next objection died before it got all the way out of his mouth.",
        strikeText: "Speck found a clause about appropriate channels and read it at her twice, and for a moment the whole doorway seemed to get narrower.",
        defeatText: "Speck stepped back from the doorway at last, muttering about dockets, and let five years of numbers be his problem no longer." },
      victory: "archive_win", retreat: "archive_retreat" },
    archive_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧵", grantComponent: "corroboration", text: "Speck let her in, grudgingly, and stood over her shoulder the whole time the way basements stand over shoulders when they have decided to be helpful and resentful in exactly equal measure.\n\nFive years of boiler-row scripts gave up their pattern quickly, once Ada knew what shape to look for: the same rounding-at-each-step walk, in the same direction, in eleven other scripts across five sittings, all from children whose registered address began with the same six streets. Not one of them had been caught at the time. All eleven had been marked down for it anyway, quietly, a mark here and a mark there, never quite enough on any single paper to raise an eyebrow.\n\nAda copied the pattern out longhand, dates and names and lost marks, and did not let herself think too hard yet about what a list like that was actually a list of.\n\nThe Corroborated Timeline is one thread closer.", next: "converge2" },
    archive_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🧵", setMid: true, grantComponent: "corroboration", text: "Speck did not budge from the doorway, not for reasoning, not for rank, not for the docket Ada eventually produced and very nearly had to raise her voice over. What she got, in the end, was ten minutes standing in the doorway itself, reading upside down over his folded arms while he recited clause after clause at her like a man reading scripture to keep from having to think about what the scripture said.\n\nTen minutes was not five years. It was, however, enough to see the same rounding-at-each-step walk in three scripts on the top of the nearest pile, before Speck noticed what she was reading and closed the drawer.\n\nThree was not eleven. Three was still a pattern, and patterns do not need permission to be true.\n\nThe Corroborated Timeline is one thread closer, however narrow the door it came through.", next: "converge2" },
    converge2: { type: "story", emoji: "📋", text: "However she had come by it, Ada now had the same thread from three different directions: an invigilator's honest arithmetic, a script's own straight-line arithmetic and a basement's worth of history repeating itself. Not proof enough to walk into the Grandmaster's office and demand anything. Enough, though, to stop treating it as a hunch.\n\nTwo questions remained, and both of them mattered more than she wanted them to. Was the method Sorrel Kettle had used a real, teachable thing, and not a lucky accident dressed up as one afterwards? And had this been happening for long enough, and widely enough, that quietly fixing one girl's mark would fix nothing at all?", next: "primer_puzzle" },
    primer_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📗", text: "The trail led, eventually, to a laundry two streets from Cutlers' Wynd, where an old woman called Ivy Fettle ran the boiler, and on quiet evenings, the sums of half the street's children as well. She did not deny teaching Sorrel Kettle anything. She simply picked up a stub of chalk and a scrap of slate. 'Ask me a sum, examiner, and I'll show you the shape of what I taught her, and you can judge for yourself whether it's cheating or just old.'",
      question: "Ivy Fettle's method for multiplying two numbers close to 100 works like this: to find 97 × 96, write both as (100 − 3) and (100 − 4), then work out 100×100 − 100×4 − 100×3 + (3×4) = 10000 − 400 − 300 + 12 = 9312. Using the same method, what is 98 × 97?",
      options: ["9506", "9412", "9606", "9316"], answer: 0,
      hint: "Write 98 as (100 − 2) and 97 as (100 − 3), then work out 100×100 − 100×3 − 100×2 + (2×3), keeping every term exact until the last step.",
      solution: "98 × 97 = (100 − 2)(100 − 3) = 10000 − 300 − 200 + 6 = 9506, which checks exactly against the plain multiplication 98 × 97 = 9506. Old chalk, correctly used, gets there just as surely as any method the Calibration prefers. It is simply not the method the mark scheme was written around.",
      good: "primer_good", mid: "primer_mid" },
    primer_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", grantComponent: "primer", text: "Ada worked it through on the same slate Ivy Fettle had offered, got 9506 clean on the first pass, and set the chalk down feeling oddly steadier than she had all week.\n\n'It's old,' Ivy Fettle said, watching her check it. 'Older than the Calibration, if it comes to that. My own grandmother stoked boilers by it, and hers before her. Nobody ever called it cheating until somebody decided there was only one right way to carry a number, and that way happened to be the one taught in schools our children don't get sent to.'\n\nShe wrapped the chalk stub in a twist of paper and pressed it into Ada's hand before she could object. 'Take it. If you're going to argue my sums are honest, you'd best be able to do them yourself, in front of whoever makes you prove it.'\n\nThe Boiler-Row Primer is one piece truer.", next: "tally_check" },
    primer_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", setMid: true, text: "Ada's first attempt came out at 9412, a slipped hundred hiding in the middle of the working, and Ivy Fettle corrected her without a flicker of triumph, the way a woman corrects a sum she has taught a hundred children and expects to teach a hundred more.\n\n'Slower than my girls manage it,' she said, not unkindly, 'but you got there.' She did not offer the chalk. Some things have to be earned properly, even by examiners with good intentions.", next: "tally_check" },
    tally_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "📊", stat: 0, dc: 13,
      text: "One question was answered. The method was real, old, taught honestly, and worked correctly, whatever the mark scheme thought of it. The other question needed a night with the boiler-row registers and a great deal of coffee: how many children, across how many years, had this same straight line cost marks to, and had any of them ever climbed high enough up the strata to be worth the Office's trouble to knock back down again?\n\nAda laid five years of stratum boards next to five years of registered addresses and started counting, properly, the way the loupe had taught her: not for the number at the bottom, but for the shape underneath it.",
      success: "tally_find", fail: "tally_miss" },
    tally_find: { type: "story", emoji: "📐", grantComponent: "archive_tally", text: "The shape held. Fourteen children across five years had used some version of Ivy Fettle's method on the Calibration. Of those fourteen, six had scored well enough on the rest of their papers that the rounding cost had actually changed their stratum, every single time, by exactly one place, never more, never less, never once in the other direction.\n\nOne place. Never enough, on its own, to be worth an appeal. Always enough, added up across a whole street's worth of children over five years, to be worth noticing, if anybody with the standing to notice had ever thought to add it up.\n\nAda had thought to add it up. She sat back from the registers at four in the morning with the tally in front of her, and understood, with a coldness that had nothing to do with the hour, that she was no longer holding a hunch about one girl. She was holding evidence about a method the Office had never once had to write a conduct amendment for, because the mark scheme itself was doing the demoting, quietly, one honestly-taught rounding step at a time.\n\nThe Five-Year Tally is complete.", next: "forge" },
    tally_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🌙", text: "The registers would not stay in order past two in the morning, and somewhere around the third year Ada lost the thread of her own tally and had to start again, and did not finish before the coffee and the night both ran out on her.\n\nShe had a strong impression of the shape, and no tally she trusted enough to put her name to. An impression is not nothing. It is, however, considerably less than a Second Examiner needs, before walking into anyone's office with it.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "⚖️", text: "There is no smith for a thing like this, no bench and no bellows. What there is, in the examiners' room after everyone else has gone home, is a good lamp, a clean sheet of paper, and Ada's own steadiest hand, such as it currently is.\n\n'A Weighed Verdict, is it,' she said to the empty room, because talking to an empty room had become, this year, a habit she had stopped apologising for. 'The Corroborated Timeline, the Boiler-Row Primer, the Five-Year Tally. Show me all three, laid out properly, and I'll write you a thing worth filing.'",
      forged: "The three pieces of evidence sat under the lamp exactly where she had left them, and for once nothing about the arrangement felt like a hunch. Ada wrote it out longhand, every figure checked twice, every claim tied to a working she could defend to a hostile examiner without flinching: the invigilator's honest odds, the script's own straight-line arithmetic and the fourteen children whose strata it had quietly cost.\n\nShe did not address it to the Grandmaster. A document like this, sent upward through the ordinary channels, was exactly the sort of thing that grew a four-line conduct amendment overnight and came back down again pointed at Sorrel Kettle instead of at the mark scheme. She addressed it instead to the file itself: a formal annex, correctly witnessed and correctly dated, sitting quietly inside Sorrel Kettle's own script where nobody could remove it quietly without the gap being missed.\n\nThe Weighed Verdict is yours! (+1 Science, kept forever.)",
      notforged: "Ada counted what she had, and counted it again, the way a smith might count a forge order, or Fitch a signature.\n\nShort. A verdict wants all three threads pulled all the way through, or it is only ever a very well-organised suspicion. The lamp would still be there tomorrow night, and so, she suspected, would the arithmetic.\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
      grantItem: "gf_adavoss_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "🖋️", text: "In the morning, Ada amended Sorrel Kettle's mark by the one place the arithmetic actually owed her, initialled the change in her own hand, and filed the Weighed Verdict where the docket said an annex belonged: quietly, correctly and impossible to un-happen without somebody noticing the gap where it used to be.\n\nShe did not report Ivy Fettle. She did not report the boiler row, or the six children, or the five years. She had turned the question over a hundred times under the lamp, the way an examiner called Halloran must have turned it over once, decades before, until he found the particular kind of silence that let a man live out a whole career comfortable in it, and she had decided, in advance, the way she had once told a young officer to decide in advance, what she was actually going to do with a good reason once she had one.\n\nA good reason for doing a wrong thing quietly for a hundred years is how you get a city where four clerks and a docket slip hold up the whole arrangement. Ada could not, this year, take the whole arrangement down. She could put one honest thread back where it belonged, and leave the rest of it sitting in a file exactly where the next person with a good reason, and steadier hands than hers, would be able to find it.\n\nIt was not enough. She wrote that down too, in the margin, where nobody official would ever think to look for the truth of a thing.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end",
      goodText: "Sorrel Kettle's stratum moved up one place on the Tallow Street board, quietly, in a season when quiet was the only kind of justice on offer. Nobody thanked Ada for it, because nobody outside the examiners' room ever knew there was anything to thank her for, which was rather the point.\n\nIvy Fettle's laundry got a little busier that winter, in a way that had nothing to do with washing. Ada never asked who sent the children, and Ivy Fettle never said, and between the two of them that counted as a perfectly clear conversation.\n\nAt her desk, in the drawer under the good lamp, the Weighed Verdict sat filed exactly where a careful person, with a good reason and steadier hands than Ada's currently were, might one day go looking for it. She had stopped billing herself for the years she had not yet been that person. She had not, quite, stopped hoping to be.",
      midText: "Sorrel Kettle's mark went up by the one place the arithmetic owed her, though it took Ada three tries at the wording before the annex could not be quietly misread, and she went home each of those three nights certain she had got it wrong.\n\nShe had not got it wrong. The Weighed Verdict sat in the file, thinner than she would have liked, built out of a narrow doorway and a slipped hundred and a tally she had abandoned once and had to start again, but true in every line that was actually in it.\n\nIt was not the clean case she had wanted. Ada had learned, over eleven sittings since Ferrow Lane, that the clean case is mostly a thing examiners hope for and almost never get, and that the untidy one, filed carefully anyway, still counts.",
      grantItem: "gf_adavoss_item2" },
  },
},

gf_fenn: {
  id: "gf_fenn",
  title: "The Sixth Board",
  tagline: "Fenn Okonkwo came out sixth of eleven at the Calibration, no triumph and no disaster, and now has four days to work out in cold arithmetic what that is actually worth.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_fenn_item1", name: "The Half-Rubbed Slate", stat: 0, how: "found", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won with a sharp Arithmetic check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_fenn_item2", name: "Fenn's Reckoning Book", stat: 2, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_fenn_item3", name: "The Counted Half-Hour", stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { mainspring: "A Sound Mainspring", faceglass: "A Whole Face-Glass", minutehand: "A True Minute-Hand" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "📋", text: "Fenn Okonkwo had sat the Calibration four days ago, in a hall built for six hundred and lit like a courtroom, and had come out of it the way everyone came out of it: quiet, hungry and unable to remember a single sum he had actually written down.\n\nNow it was posting day. Tallow Street was already three deep in children who had not slept properly in a week, all of them looking up at a lintel with eleven boards under it, waiting for men in Council grey to climb the ladders and pin up the lists.\n\nFenn's mother had wanted to come. Fenn had told her no, gently, because if it went badly he did not want an audience for the part where his face did whatever it was going to do, and if it went well he wanted to be the one who told her, in his own words, at his own kitchen table.\n\nSootfall was starting, fine and black, the way it always did around the fourth bell. Fenn turned his collar up and waited with the rest of the crowd for the ladders to come out.", next: "crowd_check" },
    crowd_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🧮", stat: 0, dc: 11,
      text: "The boards weren't posted by name. They were posted by docket number, in strict order, first stratum at the top of the leftmost board and running down and across, and six hundred children trying to read six hundred numbers off eleven boards at once made for a crush that had already put one boy on the ground twice.\n\nBeside Fenn a small girl in a coat two sizes too big was reciting her own docket number under her breath like a prayer and getting no further with finding it than sheer volume was going to take her. She had the look of someone who had never had to count past a hundred in a hurry and was extremely unhappy to be starting now.\n\nSomebody was going to have to work out where on eleven crowded boards a four-digit docket number actually sat, fast, before the crush got any worse. It might as well be the boy who was good at that sort of thing.",
      success: "help_found", fail: "help_missed" },
    help_found: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", grantItem: "gf_fenn_item1", text: "Fenn did the sum properly: dockets ran in blocks of sixty per board, so a number in the four thousands sat, near enough, six boards along and forty-odd rows down, and no amount of crowd noise changes long division. He got the girl to her own board inside a minute and found her stratum (ninth, and she went white, then oddly relieved, because ninth was not what she had feared).\n\n'You did that fast,' she said, still catching her breath.\n\n'Practice,' said Fenn, which was not quite true. What was true was that he had spent the walk over doing the same sum for himself, forty different ways, and none of the answers had changed anything.\n\nThe girl's older brother, hovering behind her with the specific gratitude of someone who has been made to feel useless in public, pressed something into Fenn's hand before being towed off. It was a slate, half rubbed clean, chalk-dust still ghosting the last sum somebody had worked on it. 'Go on,' the brother said. 'You'll want it more than I will today.'\n\nFenn's Half-Rubbed Slate is yours. (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)", next: "sixth_board" },
    help_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "Fenn tried to hold the sum in his head and the crowd shouldered it straight back out again. By the time he had the girl pointed at roughly the right board, three separate strangers had trodden on his foot and the girl's own brother had already found her name by the simple expedient of asking a Council clerk, which apparently anyone was allowed to do and nobody had thought to mention.\n\nNo matter. There would be other slates. Right now there was his own docket number, and eleven boards, and a crowd that was not going to get any thinner.", next: "sixth_board" },
    sixth_board: { type: "story", emoji: "🪧", text: "Fenn found board six before he found himself on it, which was its own small mercy: enough time to get his breathing steady before his eyes landed on the number.\n\nThere it was. Not near the top, where the gilt frame caught what little gaslight the lintel offered and the crowd stood doing the particular hushed, dangerous smiling that success does to people. Not near the bottom either, where the eleventh board hung nailed flat and nobody stood near it long, because standing near it too long invited comment. Dead centre. Sixth of eleven, in a hand so ordinary the clerk had clearly stopped caring by row forty.\n\nAround him the morning did what it always did on posting day: someone shrieked with joy three boards along, someone else sat down hard on the cobbles as if the news had gone straight through their knees, and a boy near the eleventh board was quietly, thoroughly sick against the wall while his friends pretended not to notice, which was the kindest thing available.\n\nFenn stood with his finger on the board a full ten seconds after he'd found the number, not because he needed to check it again, but because it seemed, for a moment, like a thing that deserved ten seconds. Sixth of eleven. Not the top. Not the bottom. The exact, unglamorous middle, and somewhere under the sootfall a voice in him that sounded uncomfortably like his own was already asking the only question that mattered: what was that actually worth, in real coin, over a real life, starting Monday?\n\nHe had four days before the placement offices closed their books for the season. He set off to find out.", next: "gate_fight" },
    gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🎩", qLevel: 3,
      text: "He didn't get twenty yards. A thin man in a decent coat detached himself from the crowd's edge with the easy, unhurried motion of someone who had done this exact walk a hundred times, and fell into step beside him like they were old friends.\n\n'Sixth board,' the man said, not a question. 'Hard one, sixth. Not bad enough to give up on, not good enough to coast. I could help with that, for a small consideration. Call it a Contention. Sporting stakes. If your arithmetic's as good as your stratum suggests, you've nothing to lose.'\n\nHis name, it turned out over the next unpleasant minute, was Dobbin Hask, and his idea of a sporting Contention was one where the numbers moved the instant you weren't watching them closely enough.",
      enemy: { name: "Dobbin Hask, Contention Tout", emoji: "🎩", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Fenn answered a beat quicker than Hask expected, and Hask's smile lost a groat's worth of confidence.",
        strikeText: "Hask swapped the terms on him mid-sum, quick as a card trick, and by the time Fenn had caught it, two cogs had left his pocket that had never agreed to go.",
        defeatText: "Hask held both hands up, all injured innocence, the way men like him always do the moment the arithmetic stops being on their side. 'No harm meant,' he said, already drifting off toward the next stunned face coming away from the boards. There is always a next face coming away from the boards." },
      victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🌆", text: "Fenn walked on with his pockets exactly as full as they'd started, which on a day like this counted as a small, real victory. Hask would find someone else to practise his Contentions on. Tallow Street had six hundred fresh strata this morning, and every one of them was, for a few hours yet, an easy mark. Fenn intended to stop being an easy mark as soon as possible, starting now.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚶", text: "Fenn cut the conversation short and put three streets between himself and Hask before he trusted his pockets again. Two cogs, when he checked, were in fact gone, along with a length of chalk he hadn't noticed going. It was not a large loss. It was, he decided, walking faster, going to be his last one today.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🛤️", text: "Four days, three real leads, and a mother who would ask him at supper whether he had a plan, in the tone of someone who already suspected the honest answer was not yet.\n\nThe Engine Yard on Founder's Row was taking a handful of sixth-to-eighth-stratum boys onto its millwright apprenticeship this season: slow, respectable, four years to a proper writ. The Signal Office by the clatterlift terminus had more applicants than posts and was settling the matter by lot, which paid at once if it paid at all. And the vent-warden crews down in the Under were short-handed again after last winter, training being short and the work being exactly as dangerous as everybody already knew.\n\nFenn had four days to find out what each of them was actually worth, in coin, before he could tell his mother anything true.", options: [
      { label: "Try the Engine Yard on Founder's Row", next: "engineyard_setup", preview: "A respectable millwright apprenticeship. Slow, safe and four years long. A hard percentage puzzle guards the truth of the wage." },
      { label: "Chance the Signal Office lot", next: "signal_setup", preview: "Instant pay if you're drawn, nothing if you're not. A dice challenge on real odds." },
      { label: "Ask after the vent-warden crews", next: "ventward_setup", preview: "Fast money, real danger, short training. A Contention with the foreman's heavy guards the queue." },
    ] },
    engineyard_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🏭", text: "Founder's Row smelled of hot metal and clean oil, which after four days of sootfall and wet wool felt almost indecent. The Engine Yard's hiring clerk, a woman with ink-stained cuffs and no patience for wasted time, laid the terms out on a single sheet, the way you do for people you expect to actually read it.\n\n'Millwright's apprenticeship. Four years. First two, you're paid three marks a week and you're worth every penny less than that. Second two, once you're trusted near anything that turns, it's four marks a week. Come out the other end with your writ, and a qualified fitter earns nine marks a week for as long as his hands hold out, which round here is generally a good long while.'\n\nShe tapped the sheet. 'Everyone asks the same question at this point, so I'll ask it for you: is it worth it. That's not for me to say. That's arithmetic, and you'll want to do it properly before you sign anything.'", next: "engineyard_puzzle" },
    engineyard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚙️", text: "The clerk pushed the sheet across and tapped the two wage lines with one ink-stained nail, waiting.", question: "The Engine Yard's millwright apprenticeship pays 4 marks a week in its final two years of training, before the qualified fitter's wage of 9 marks a week begins. What percentage increase is the qualified wage over the final training wage?", options: ["44%", "56%", "125%", "225%"], answer: 2,
      hint: "Find the increase first (qualified wage minus training wage), then divide that increase by the training wage, the ORIGINAL amount, not the new one.",
      solution: "The increase is 9 − 4 = 5 marks. As a percentage of the starting wage: 5 ÷ 4 = 1.25, or 125%. (Dividing by 9 instead of 4 gives the wrong, smaller answer of about 56%, because a percentage increase is always measured against where you started, not where you end up.)",
      good: "engineyard_good", mid: "engineyard_mid" },
    engineyard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔧", grantComponent: "mainspring", text: "'A hundred and twenty five per cent,' Fenn said, and the clerk's eyebrows went up a fraction, which for a hiring clerk was practically a fanfare.\n\n'More than double,' she said. 'Most lads round here can tell you it's a big jump without being able to tell you how big. Knowing the actual number's worth something to me.' She reached under the counter and came up with a mainspring, salvaged from a scrapped test-gauge, coiled tight and sound as the day it was wound. 'Keep that. Sign or don't, no charge either way. Somebody who reckons a wage properly before he takes it is worth the spring.'\n\nComponent found: A Sound Mainspring.", next: "converge" },
    engineyard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📐", setMid: true, grantComponent: "mainspring", text: "Fenn got as far as knowing the increase was large, and further than that his figures kept slipping. The clerk took the pencil off him without any particular unkindness and walked the sum through properly, twice, until it sat still.\n\n'Wrong today doesn't mean wrong Monday,' she said, which was as close to comfort as she apparently came. On her way back to the ledger she tossed him a mainspring anyway, off the same scrapped gauge. 'For the walking. Not for the sum. Get the sum right before you sign anything, mind. I won't always be the one asking it.'\n\nComponent found: A Sound Mainspring.", next: "converge" },
    signal_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🎟️", text: "The Signal Office queue ran three times round the terminus yard, under the brass cables where the clatterlifts hauled up and down the face of the Rise all day, full of people who had never had to queue for anything in their lives. Forty two names had gone in for three open posts this season, a clerk announced, bored, and the drawing would be by lot, fair and square and entirely out of anyone's hands.\n\n'Five marks a week from the first Monday, if you're drawn,' said the man next to Fenn, who had clearly explained this to several people already and was starting to enjoy it. 'No apprenticeship, no writ, no waiting about. Course, it stays five marks a week more or less forever, and there's forty one other names in that box hoping exactly as hard as you are.'\n\nThe lot-drum sat on the counter, brass and turning, and somewhere inside it Fenn's name was one small folded paper among forty two.", next: "signal_dice" },
    signal_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 4, text: "'Roll to see how the office's luck runs today,' the clerk said, not unkindly, sliding a die across because apparently that was how the Signal Office liked to dramatise a very ordinary piece of paperwork. Roll 4 or more on a six-sided die and your name gets a fair crack of the drum this season.",
      predict: { question: "Forty two names go into the lot-box for three open posts, one name per post, none drawn twice. What is the probability that Fenn's name is one of the three drawn?", options: ["1/42", "1/14", "3/45", "1/3"], answer: 1, why: "Three posts are drawn from forty two names, and Fenn's is one of them, so the chance is 3 out of 42, which cancels down (divide top and bottom by 3) to 1 out of 14. Grim odds, correctly known, are still worth more than hope. +1 to your roll." },
      success: "signal_good", fail: "signal_fail" },
    signal_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🚡", grantComponent: "mainspring", text: "The die came up kind, and so, three weeks later, did the drum: Fenn's name, out of forty two, one of only three. Five marks a week, starting the first Monday, no training and no writ and precisely nothing beyond this season promised about the Monday after that.\n\nThe clerk stamped his docket without looking up. Behind the counter, wedged into the gears of the lot-drum itself where some earlier winner's paperwork must have gone astray, sat a small coiled spring, the kind that made the drum turn true instead of sticking on the same three names every week. Fenn worked it free while nobody was watching and pocketed it. Seemed a fair trade for what the drum had just cost forty one other families.\n\nComponent found: A Sound Mainspring.", next: "converge" },
    signal_fail: { type: "story", emoji: "🌧️", setMid: true, grantComponent: "mainspring", text: "The die came up unkind, and so, three weeks later, did the drum. Somebody else's name, then another's, then a third that wasn't Fenn's either. He stood in the yard afterwards for a while, not because he'd expected differently (he'd done the sum, he knew exactly how thin one in fourteen was) but because knowing the odds and feeling them land are two different arithmetics entirely.\n\nOn the way out he stopped to help the man behind him lever a jammed die out of the counter's edge, and it came away with a small coiled mainspring caught round its rim, dropped by nobody knew who. He took it. Waste not.\n\nComponent found: A Sound Mainspring.", next: "converge" },
    ventward_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The vent-warden yard sat right up against Outfall Nine, where the Turn breathed its scalding fog four times a day and somebody had to be close enough to time it or the whole lane downstream got cooked for nothing. Forty counts to fill a lane, near enough a hundred and fifty to clear it: numbers every vent-warden carried in his bones whether he'd ever written a sum down in his life.\n\n'Two years' training, four marks a week while you learn to count fog without flinching,' said the foreman, a burned looking man with a foreman's contempt for wasted breath. 'Come out the other side qualified, it's seven marks a week for as long as your nerve holds, which round here is not always as long as your contract.' He looked Fenn over. 'Short training because there's not much to teach past the counting and the not flinching. Long odds on the not flinching, mind. Ask around.'\n\nThere was a queue for that too, and at the front of it stood a boy built like a coal cart, thinning it out by hand.", next: "ventward_fight" },
    ventward_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "💨", qLevel: 3,
      text: "'Foreman pays me by the head I turn away,' Bosk said, not troubling to sound sorry about it. 'Count wrong on the outfall timing and you don't just lose your place in this queue, you lose a hand, or worse, so he likes it thinned before the real training even starts. Prove your counting's fast enough to matter, or go home. Either way, I get paid.'\n\nHe didn't wait for an answer. He just started the count.",
      enemy: { name: "Corrin Bosk, the Foreman's Heavy", emoji: "💨", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Fenn's count landed exact and unhurried, and Bosk's confident sneer slipped half a beat, same as the men he usually turned away never managed.",
        strikeText: "Bosk called the numbers faster than fair and shouldered in on the miscount, and Fenn went down hard against the outfall rail, breath knocked half out of him by grate-heat that hadn't even properly vented yet.",
        defeatText: "Bosk stepped back at last, rubbing a knuckle, weighing up whether this one was worth the bruise report. 'Fine,' he said. 'You can count. Doesn't mean you won't flinch. That part's not on me to teach.'" },
      victory: "ventward_good", retreat: "ventward_retreat" },
    ventward_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔧", grantComponent: "mainspring", text: "The foreman had watched the whole exchange from the doorway with his arms folded, and gave the smallest possible nod, the kind that in this yard passed for a warm welcome. 'Counting's the easy half,' he said. 'Come back tomorrow if you still want the other half.'\n\nUnderfoot, kicked loose from the outfall rail in the scuffle, lay a small coiled mainspring, the kind salvaged off any of a dozen broken pressure gauges the yard went through weekly. Fenn picked it up before anyone thought to charge him for it.\n\nComponent found: A Sound Mainspring.", next: "converge" },
    ventward_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🩹", grantComponent: "mainspring", text: "Fenn backed off before Bosk got a second swing in, which was the sensible thing to do and did not feel sensible at all, standing there afterwards with his ears ringing and his place in the queue gone to somebody else. The foreman hadn't so much as looked over.\n\nHe was most of the way back down the lane before he noticed the mainspring stuck to the sole of his boot, kicked there in the scuffle and travelling with him ever since, small and coiled and entirely indifferent to whether he'd won anything. He kept it anyway. A morning that costs you something should at least leave you holding something.\n\nComponent found: A Sound Mainspring.", next: "converge" },
    converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🧭", text: "Three leads, three mornings, one mainspring salvaged three different ways, and Fenn was no closer to knowing which of them added up to a life he could actually run a household on. Numbers on their own don't tell you anything. It's what you do with them, set side by side, that starts to.\n\nHe knew someone who did that sort of sum for a living, for a fee, over her own counter, and he had just enough chalk credit left to ask her properly.", next: "marta_setup" },
    marta_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "Marta's shop smelled of lamp oil and other people's debts. She kept the chalk tab for half the street on a board behind the counter, and she kept a second, unofficial ledger in her head for anyone who asked her nicely and paid in something other than good manners.\n\n'Sixth stratum,' she said, when Fenn had laid it all out: the Engine Yard, the Signal lot, the vent-warden crews. 'That's not nothing and it's not much, and you already know that, or you wouldn't be standing in my shop looking like a boy doing sums in his sleep.' She pulled a slate towards her. 'Let's put two of them next to each other properly, since half of doing sums right is refusing to look at them one at a time.'", next: "marta_puzzle" },
    marta_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📖", text: "Marta chalked two figures on the slate and turned it to face him.", question: "The Engine Yard pays 3 marks a week during training. The vent-warden apprenticeship pays 4 marks a week during its (shorter) training. Fenn's household needs 8 marks a week to cover rent, food and the chalk tab. By how many percentage points more of the household's needs does the vent-warden wage cover, compared to the Engine Yard wage?", options: ["6.25 percentage points", "12.5 percentage points", "25 percentage points", "37.5 percentage points"], answer: 1,
      hint: "Turn each wage into a percentage of 8 marks separately, then subtract one percentage from the other.",
      solution: "3 marks out of 8 is 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375, or 37.5%. 4 marks out of 8 is 4 ÷ 8 = 0.5, or 50%. The difference between them is 50% − 37.5% = 12.5 percentage points.",
      good: "marta_good", mid: "marta_mid" },
    marta_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔍", grantComponent: "faceglass", text: "'Twelve and a half,' Marta said, checking his working against her own without much surprise, the way people check sums they already trust. 'That's your whole answer, right there, in one number: the vent-warden money starts covering more of what your house actually needs, from week one, and the Engine Yard's still catching it up two years on. Doesn't make the vent yard the right choice. Makes it the honest one to weigh against the safe one, which is more than most people manage.'\n\nShe slid a small disc of glass across the counter, cut clean and whole, salvaged off some clock too far gone to save otherwise. 'For getting the sum right in my shop instead of guessing in someone else's. Go on, before I charge you rent for the stool.'\n\nComponent found: A Whole Face-Glass.", next: "sconce_setup" },
    marta_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📏", setMid: true, text: "Fenn's numbers wobbled somewhere between the two wages, and Marta let him sit with the wrong answer for a moment before taking the slate back and chalking the working out herself, clean and unhurried, the way she did everything that involved other people's money.\n\n'Three eighths and a half, side by side, is a twelfth and a half's difference,' she said, tapping each figure. 'Learn to see that at a glance, because nobody in a hiring office is going to slow down and show you twice.' She did not offer him anything for the counter this time, which felt, somehow, like the more honest response of the two.\n\nHe left with the sum right in his head at last, if not in time to have won anything for it.", next: "sconce_setup" },
    sconce_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🕰️", text: "Old Sconce mended clocks and, by his own account, consciences, whichever the customer could pay for first, out of a shopfront so narrow you had to turn sideways to reach the counter. Fenn's father's watch had sat in his good coat pocket for three years, stopped, because a stopped watch that had once been his father's was easier to carry than a working one that reminded you your father was two hundred miles down a coastal engine line and increasingly unreliable about writing.\n\n'I don't mend sentiment,' Sconce said, turning the watch over in fingers gone permanently black at the nail. 'I mend movements. This one wants a mainspring, a face-glass and a minute-hand, and I happen to be short of all three this week, so unless you're carrying spares, we're both stuck.'\n\nHe nodded at the counter, where his own accounts sat open, badly. 'While you're here, my eyes aren't what they were. Check that column for me. I haven't the patience for it today.'", next: "sconce_check" },
    sconce_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "⏱️", stat: 0, dc: 12,
      text: "The column ran forty two entries deep, chalk tabs and small repairs and one customer who plainly hadn't paid in eleven months, and Sconce's total at the bottom didn't match the entries even a careless glance could add up. Somewhere in forty two lines a figure had been carried wrong, and finding exactly which one, fast, without a fresh page to work it out on, was the kind of thing that either came naturally to you or didn't.",
      success: "sconce_found", fail: "sconce_missed" },
    sconce_found: { type: "story", emoji: "⚙️", grantComponent: "minutehand", text: "Row seventeen, carried as nine marks instead of six, three marks conjured out of nowhere and quietly bleeding into every total below it ever since. Fenn found it in under a minute, corrected the running total, and did not comment on how long it had apparently been wrong, which seemed like the more useful kind of tact.\n\nSconce checked the correction twice, said nothing for a moment, then reached into a drawer that plainly did not get opened for just anyone and came up with a minute-hand, thin as a whisker and dead straight. 'Row seventeen's been wrong since March,' he admitted, grudgingly. 'Keep that. Anyone who can find one wrong figure in forty two has earned a straight hand at least.'\n\nComponent found: A True Minute-Hand.", next: "forge" },
    sconce_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "Fenn ran the column twice and came up with the same wrong total Sconce already had, which told him something was off without telling him what. Sconce took the slate back, unbothered, muttering that the light in the shop had never been kind to figures and probably never would be.\n\n'No matter,' he said, though the ledger would go on quietly wrong for a good while yet. 'Bring me the parts, or don't. The watch keeps its own time either way, which is to say, none at all.'", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Sconce laid the three finds out on the counter under his one good lamp: the mainspring, coiled and sound, the face-glass, cut clean, the minute-hand, thin and true, if Fenn had come with all three, or a counter with gaps in it, if he hadn't.\n\n'Your father's watch,' Sconce said, 'or somebody's. Doesn't much matter whose, in the end. A stopped watch is just a very small, very stubborn lie. Let's see if we can't make it tell the truth again.'",
      forged: "Sconce worked without hurrying, the way people do when hurrying has cost them things before: mainspring wound and seated, glass set true, the new minute-hand balanced so fine it barely needed the second's help to move. He closed the case and held it out.\n\nIt ticked. An ordinary, unremarkable, entirely reliable tick, the kind that had been happening in good watches for as long as there had been watches, and that felt, after four days of arithmetic with nothing solid at the end of it, like the single most trustworthy sound Fenn had heard all week.\n\nThe Counted Half-Hour is yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
      notforged: "Sconce laid the gaps out plainly, the way he laid out everything. 'A watch wants all three or it's just a case with opinions in it,' he said. 'Come back when you've the full set. The shop isn't going anywhere, and neither, unhelpfully, is the time you're trying to keep.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
      grantItem: "gf_fenn_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "🕰️", text: "That night Fenn laid it all out on the kitchen table for his mother the way Marta had taught him to, side by side instead of one at a time: the Engine Yard, slow and safe, four years to nine marks a week and a writ nobody could take off him afterwards. The Signal lot, fast if it landed and worth nothing in the meantime, one chance in fourteen, honestly counted. The vent-warden crews, faster money and a real hand on the household's needs from week one, and a foreman who paid Bosk to thin his queue because the work cost more than wages some winters.\n\nHis mother listened to the whole of it without once telling him what to think, which from her was its own kind of respect.\n\n'The vent yard pays quicker,' Fenn said at last. 'I worked that properly. It's not close, the first two years, it's genuinely not close.'\n\n'And the other,' she said.\n\n'Is slower. And it doesn't stop. I asked round the yard, they had a man start the same year as your cousin, and he's fitting still, marks in his pocket every week for eleven years and counting, nothing that outfall work's ever promised anybody.' He turned the watch over once, listening to it tick, the way you check a thing is still telling the truth. 'Sixth stratum doesn't get me the exciting answer, whichever I pick. I've done that sum every way I know how. It gets me a decent one, if I choose carefully and don't waste the four days feeling sorry for the boy on board six instead of the one who has to actually turn up on Monday.'", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end",
      goodText: "Fenn signed the Engine Yard's papers on the last morning the books were open, three marks a week starting Monday and a writ waiting for him four slow years off, and walked home past the Signal Office queue (still three deep, still hoping, still one in fourteen) without a flicker of regret he could locate. It was not the fast money. It was the sum he'd checked most carefully, and the one that stopped being an emergency and started being a life. His mother put the Counted Half-Hour on the mantel where the stopped watch used to sit, and it kept, from that day, extremely good time.\n\nSome Calibrations end in gilt frames and some end nailed flat under the eleventh board, and most, like Fenn's, end somewhere in the ordinary, unglamorous middle, which turns out, if you reckon it properly and refuse to be ashamed of it, to be worth rather more than the boards ever bothered to post.",
      midText: "Fenn signed the Engine Yard's papers in the end, three marks a week starting Monday, though it took him longer to trust his own working than it should have, and he went back to Marta's counter twice more before he'd stake his name on the sum. His mother said the wait had made him careful rather than slow, which was her way of saying she was proud without spending a word more on it than the sentence needed.\n\nThe middle of eleven boards is not a story anyone tells at a mantel for years afterwards. But Fenn kept the Counted Half-Hour running true in his coat pocket every single day after, and turned up early for every shift the Engine Yard ever gave him, which is its own quiet, unglamorous kind of a life well reckoned.",
      grantItem: "gf_fenn_item2" },
  },
},

gf_delphine: {
  id: "gf_delphine",
  title: "The Name in the Margin",
  tagline: "A strongbox has gone missing from Ashcombe Yard, a runner boy is taking the blame for it, and Delphine Ashworth has never in her life let a good scandal go uninvestigated.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_delphine_item1", name: "The Pilfered Pince-Nez", stat: 4, how: "found", desc: "+1 Speed. Won with a sharp Speed check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_delphine_item2", name: "The Reeve's Nod", stat: 2, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_delphine_item3", name: "The Cross-Filed Docket", stat: 0, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { torn_chit: "Torn Shift-Chit", wax_seal: "Broken Wax Seal", coin_tally: "Recounted Coin Stack" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🧐", text: "Delphine Ashworth was, by her own cheerful estimate, the best-informed officer at Ashcombe Yard, and she had the duty room's full attention to prove it. She was mid-anecdote about a Reeve's daughter and a borrowed hat when the sergeant came in at a pace that meant something had actually happened, which was rare enough to be worth noticing on its own.\n\nThe counting room strongbox had gone in the night. Forty-odd grist in collected conduct fines, gone clean out of a locked room with one officer meant to be watching the corridor outside it. Standing already accused, wedged between two constables twice his size, was a Under runner boy called Fossey, who delivered post along that corridor most evenings and had, unhelpfully for himself, been seen doing exactly that the evening before.\n\n'Convenient,' murmured Delphine, not quite quietly enough.\n\nAcross the room, Corvain, three years her senior and twice as tired, raised one eyebrow at her without looking up from her own paperwork. 'Something to add, Ashworth, or just enjoying the view?'\n\n'Both,' said Delphine, and drifted, with the unhurried confidence of someone who has never once doubted she was allowed to be somewhere, over to the roster pinned by the door.", next: "eavesdrop_check" },
    eavesdrop_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "👂", stat: 4, dc: 11,
      text: "The roster was the sergeant's usual scrawl, name after name in a hand like a bad-tempered fence, right up until one line: WYNDHAM, marked absent the night of the theft in the sergeant's own ink, and then, underneath it, a small neat tick and a circle round the name, in a hand that belonged to nobody who had ever written anything else on that board.\n\nDelphine had exactly as long as it took the sergeant to stop talking to Fossey and turn round to decide whether that circle was worth remembering. Being fast, for once, mattered more than being charming.", success: "pincenez_found", fail: "pincenez_missed" },
    pincenez_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🧐", grantItem: "gf_delphine_item1", text: "She had it memorised before the sergeant's head came round, and stepped back from the board with the serene look of a woman who has been standing there admiring the woodwork the whole time.\n\nOn her way out, ducking under the arm of a constable carrying evidence bags, she liberated a pair of pince-nez from the lost-and-found tray by the door, purely, she told herself, for the look of the thing. Sharp, unhurried and thoroughly pleased with herself, she pocketed them and went to find out who owned that circle.\n\nThe Pilfered Pince-Nez is yours. (+1 Speed, kept forever.)", next: "gate_fight" },
    pincenez_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The sergeant turned round a half-second too soon, and Delphine had to spend a genuinely uncomfortable thirty seconds admiring the woodwork of the door for real, in case anyone asked. She got the gist of the circled name, but not the fine detail, and the lost-and-found tray stayed exactly as cluttered as she'd found it.\n\n(The Pilfered Pince-Nez stays lost, for now. Another run through this adventure may find it.)", next: "gate_fight" },
    gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "⚔️", qLevel: 3,
      text: "She had barely made the stairwell when a figure came pelting the other way, shoulder first, and knocked her satchel clean off her arm. A scavenger, by the look of him, one of the sort who followed strongbox rumours the way gulls followed fishing boats, hoping something worth pocketing had spilled loose in the confusion.\n\n'Watch it,' said Delphine, in the tone she usually reserved for people who trod on her boots at parties, and put her own boot rather deliberately in his path instead.",
      enemy: { name: "The Rumour-Chaser", emoji: "🥾", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "The answer landed clean, and the scavenger staggered back into a stack of empty crates with a noise like an argument losing.",
        strikeText: "He shoved past harder than expected, and Delphine's perfectly turned-out coat picked up a smear of stairwell grime that was going to require Words with the laundress.",
        defeatText: "He gave up the fight, and the satchel, and vanished back down the stairs muttering about people who weren't even carrying anything worth the bruise." },
      victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🏛️", text: "Delphine retrieved her satchel, checked her reflection in a window for form's sake, and decided the coat could be forgiven the grime in exchange for the small, satisfying fact that she had won. Word of a missing strongbox had apparently reached the Under before it had reached most of the Yard's own officers, which was itself worth remembering.\n\nAhead, three separate leads suggested themselves, each promising to explain that circled name, and none of them, Delphine felt, likely to be dull.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "Discretion, Delphine reflected, backing smartly through the stairwell door and letting it swing shut behind her, was also a kind of victory, provided nobody wrote it down that way. The satchel was gone, along with whatever the scavenger had actually been after, but she still had the one thing that mattered: a circled name and three ways to chase it down.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🧭", text: "Three routes suggested themselves, the way they always did once you started actually asking questions instead of merely enjoying other people's.\n\nThe Yard's own archive held the posting rosters going back years, tedious but exact. The counting-house across the square kept the ledgers of who had signed in and out of every corridor in the building, guarded by a night clerk who did not care for visitors. And the Brindle Chop-House, three streets down, was where junior officers went to lose money at cards, and where, if the gossip Delphine had spent years cultivating meant anything at all, somebody would eventually say too much.", options: [
      { label: "Search the Yard's archive", next: "archive_intro", preview: "Work out the corridor watch rotation from the archive rosters. A logic puzzle guards the first piece of evidence." },
      { label: "Slip into the counting-house at night", next: "counting_intro", preview: "Sneak a look at the sign-in ledger past a clerk who turns round like clockwork. A dice challenge guards the first piece of evidence." },
      { label: "Go drinking at the Brindle Chop-House", next: "chop_intro", preview: "Loosen a card-game crony's tongue, or at least his grip. This one is a fight." },
    ] },
    archive_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "📚", text: "The Yard's archive smelled of dust and other people's mistakes, and was minded by a Laachmi clerk named Ilsevet, tall, horned and entirely unmoved by rank. 'Rosters,' Ilsevet said, when Delphine explained what she wanted, 'exist to be checked, not gossiped about. Though I don't suppose that's ever stopped anyone.'\n\nThe corridor watch outside the counting room, it turned out, rotated among four officers on a strict posting cycle: Colque, then Pell, then Wyndham, then Ashe, then back to Colque again, over and over, without exception, for as long as anyone had kept the roster at all.", next: "archive_puzzle" },
    archive_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📜", text: "'The posting cycle began on what the log calls night one, with Colque,' said Ilsevet, sliding the ledger across without much hope. 'The strongbox went missing on night forty-three of that same cycle. If you're so clever, tell me whose watch that was meant to be, and I'll believe half of what they say about you.'", question: "The corridor watch rotates among four officers in strict repeating order: Colque, then Pell, then Wyndham, then Ashe, then back to Colque. The cycle began on night one, with Colque. Whose watch was scheduled for night 43?", options: ["Colque", "Pell", "Wyndham", "Ashe"], answer: 2,
      hint: "Divide 43 by 4 and look at the remainder. That remainder tells you how far into the four-name cycle night 43 falls, counting Colque as remainder 1.",
      solution: "43 = 4 × 10 + 3, so night 43 lands on the third name in the cycle. Counting Colque as first, Pell as second and Wyndham as third, the watch on night 43 belonged to Wyndham exactly as the sergeant's own roster said. The rotation was never in doubt. It was the tick beside his name that had been added later, by someone else's hand entirely.",
      good: "archive_good", mid: "archive_mid" },
    archive_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🗂️", grantComponent: "torn_chit", text: "'Well,' said Ilsevet, with the closest thing to admiration a Laachmi clerk permits itself, and turned the ledger round to show her the torn corner of the original duty chit, the one the tick had been pasted over. Wyndham's own hand, from earlier that same week, was nothing like the tidy little correction beside his name. 'Someone wanted it to look decided. It was already decided. That's the interesting part.'\n\nComponent found: Torn Shift-Chit.", next: "converge2" },
    archive_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🗂️", setMid: true, grantComponent: "torn_chit", text: "Delphine's arithmetic wobbled somewhere around night thirty, and Ilsevet, sighing in the manner of clerks the world over, worked the remainder out with a claw on the dusty desk while she watched, faintly mortified, faintly delighted anyway.\n\n'You'd have got there,' Ilsevet said, not entirely convincingly, and tore the corner off the original chit to save her the trouble of coming back. 'Everyone flatters clerks eventually. You just did it earlier than most.'\n\nComponent found: Torn Shift-Chit.", next: "converge2" },
    counting_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🏦", text: "The counting-house at night belonged to a single clerk with a lamp, a ledger the size of a paving slab, and a habit of turning to check the corridor at almost exactly the same interval, like a man who had once been startled badly and never quite recovered.\n\nThe sign-in ledger, if Delphine could reach it, would show every officer's name against every hour for the whole week of the theft. It would also, if the clerk turned round at the wrong moment, show her name against a rather different kind of entry entirely.", next: "counting_dice" },
    counting_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 5, text: "Roll 5 or more on a six-sided die to flip to the right page, read it, and set the ledger back before the clerk turns round again.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 5 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/2", "1/3", "2/3", "1/6"], answer: 1, why: "Only the faces 5 and 6 count as a success, out of six equally likely faces. That is 2 outcomes out of 6, which simplifies to 1/3. Rather worse odds than they sound, said out loud. Predicting them correctly steadies your hand anyway: +1 to your roll." },
      success: "counting_good", fail: "counting_mid" },
    counting_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🗂️", grantComponent: "torn_chit", text: "The page turned, the entry read itself into memory, and the ledger was back on its shelf a full second before the clerk came round, humming, none the wiser. Wyndham's name sat against the corridor watch for that night in the sergeant's ink, nothing added, nothing circled: exactly what the archive had already told her.\n\nSlipped between two pages, forgotten by whoever had been careless enough to leave it there, was a torn corner of paper in a hand she was starting to recognise.\n\nComponent found: Torn Shift-Chit.", next: "converge2" },
    counting_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🗂️", setMid: true, grantComponent: "torn_chit", text: "The ledger slid, the lamp wobbled, and the clerk turned round a great deal faster than clockwork usually allows. Delphine did not panic, because panicking spoils a good coat, and instead spent four extremely charming minutes explaining that she had simply been admiring his penmanship, which was, she assured him, remarkable.\n\nIt worked, as it generally did. Flattered nearly to tears, the clerk fetched the entry himself, tore off the corner she pointed to entirely without being asked, and saw her out with directions to the nearest chop-house for her trouble.\n\nComponent found: Torn Shift-Chit.", next: "converge2" },
    chop_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🍺", text: "The Brindle Chop-House was warm, loud and thick with the particular smell of young officers losing money to each other on purpose. Delphine found Wyndham's usual card partner, a broad, cheerful sort called Merritt, three drinks into a story about his cousin's card party, the one held, he was saying, the very night the strongbox went missing, wasn't that a thing.\n\n'Funny old world,' Delphine agreed, sitting down uninvited, 'that a man can be at a card party and on a corridor watch at the very same hour.' Merritt's cheerfulness left his face rather quickly after that.", next: "chop_fight" },
    chop_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "⚔️", qLevel: 3,
      text: "'You want to leave that alone,' said Merritt, not cheerful at all now, and got up fast enough to send his chair over backwards, which is not generally how innocent men respond to idle chat about card parties.\n\n'I really don't think I do,' said Delphine, and did not get up nearly as fast, because standing your ground is easier when you're fairly sure you're right.",
      enemy: { name: "Merritt, Rattled", emoji: "🃏", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "A sharp answer, sharper than his temper, and Merritt sat back down whether he meant to or not.",
        strikeText: "He caught her wrist hard enough to bruise, hissing something about people who ought to mind their own business.",
        defeatText: "He let go all at once, the fight gone out of him as quickly as it had arrived, and slumped back into his chair looking mostly relieved that someone else was finally going to decide what happened next." },
      victory: "chop_win", retreat: "chop_retreat" },
    chop_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🗂️", grantComponent: "torn_chit", text: "Merritt talked, in the end, the way frightened people do once the fighting stops being an option: too fast, too much, and mostly true. Wyndham had been at that card party all night, every hour of it, while a torn corner of his own genuine, honestly-written duty chit, the one that should have gone into the roster as ABSENT, sat forgotten in Merritt's coat pocket, because Merritt had been the one meant to file it and had, out of simple friendship, not quite managed to.\n\nComponent found: Torn Shift-Chit.", next: "converge2" },
    chop_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🗂️", setMid: true, grantComponent: "torn_chit", text: "Delphine decided, on reflection, that dignity mattered more than finishing a fight nobody was going to remember fondly, and stepped back out of Merritt's reach with her chin up and her wrist already bruising. As he turned to shove past her towards the door, she lifted the torn, crumpled chit clean out of his coat pocket, a small and entirely satisfying piece of revenge for the bruise.\n\nComponent found: Torn Shift-Chit.", next: "converge2" },
    converge2: { type: "story", emoji: "🧵", text: "Whichever way she'd come at it, the answer arrived looking the same: Wyndham's watch, Wyndham's own honest chit marking himself absent, and somebody else's small neat circle laid on top of it afterwards, turning an admission into an alibi.\n\nBut a torn corner of paper, however damning, was not proof enough to clear a Under boy standing accused with two constables' hands on his shoulders. Delphine needed the seal on the strongbox itself, and whatever the recount inside it could tell her.", next: "seal_check" },
    seal_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🕯️", stat: 2, dc: 12,
      text: "The Yard sealed its strongboxes fresh each dawn with wax mixed in one of four colours, rotating in strict order through the week so that any seal could be checked against the day it claimed. The box in evidence bore a seal in the wrong colour entirely for the day it was supposedly locked, which meant somebody had opened it and pressed a fresh seal after the fact, hoping nobody with a head for patterns would ever think to check.\n\nSomebody with a head for patterns was, as it happened, already looking.", success: "seal_found", fail: "seal_missed" },
    seal_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🔏", grantComponent: "wax_seal", text: "It took Delphine roughly four seconds to work out the colour was wrong, and considerably longer to stop looking pleased about it in front of the duty sergeant, who had not asked for her opinion and was getting it anyway. She eased the broken wax free of the box lid with a fingernail, whole enough to show its mismatched colour to anyone who cared to look.\n\nComponent found: Broken Wax Seal.", next: "tally_intro" },
    seal_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "She was two sentences into explaining the wax-colour rotation to a passing junior constable, entirely for the pleasure of being the one explaining it, when the evidence officer swept the box away for formal storage, seal and all, before she'd actually prised it free.\n\nShowing off, Delphine reflected, was occasionally its own punishment.", next: "tally_intro" },
    tally_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🪙", text: "The formal recount was ordered for that same afternoon, in the counting room itself, with the accused boy Fossey stood miserably to one side and Reeve Ashcombe presiding with the weary patience of a man who had hoped never to need to. Every grist in the strongbox had to be tallied and stacked before the hearing could properly begin.\n\nThe clerk doing the counting was neat to the point of anxiety, and had used, he announced, the fewest possible stacks to store the recovered coin: some stacks of eight grist, some of fifteen, nothing loose, nothing left over.", next: "tally_puzzle" },
    tally_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🧮", text: "'Two hundred and eighty-eight grist, exactly,' the clerk said, rather proud of himself, 'in whole stacks of eight and fifteen, fewest stacks I could manage.' Reeve Ashcombe looked expectantly round the room, and, entirely to her own delight, it was Delphine he happened to be looking at.", question: "A strongbox holds exactly 288 grist, made up entirely of whole stacks of 8 grist and whole stacks of 15 grist, with no coins left over. The fewest possible number of stacks is used. How many stacks of 15 grist are there?", options: ["0", "8", "16", "24"], answer: 2,
      hint: "Every valid split must satisfy 8a + 15b = 288 for whole numbers a and b. Try different whole-number values of b, keep only the ones that leave a a whole number, and compare the total number of stacks a + b for each.",
      solution: "The whole-number solutions to 8a + 15b = 288 are b = 0 (a = 36, 36 stacks), b = 8 (a = 21, 29 stacks) and b = 16 (a = 6, 22 stacks); no larger b keeps a whole and non-negative. The fewest total stacks come from b = 16: 16 stacks of 15 grist (240) plus 6 stacks of 8 grist (48) makes 288 grist in 22 stacks, the smallest total of the three.",
      good: "tally_good", mid: "tally_mid" },
    tally_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🪙", grantComponent: "coin_tally", text: "'Sixteen,' said Delphine, before the clerk had finished totting it up on his own fingers, and had the very great pleasure of watching a room full of people who had spent the morning treating her like decoration reassess her rather quickly.\n\nSixteen stacks of fifteen matched, to the coin, the exact amount Merritt had let slip Wyndham had actually taken from the card table that night to cover a debt he could no longer afford to lose. The recount wasn't just a recount. It was a confession, in coin.\n\nComponent found: Recounted Coin Stack.", next: "forge" },
    tally_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🪙", setMid: true, text: "Delphine guessed eight, confidently, and was wrong, which she covered by immediately asking a great many detailed questions about the clerk's counting method until nobody quite remembered she'd been wrong in the first place.\n\nThe true figure of sixteen stacks came out anyway, from the clerk's own careful working, chalked up on the counting room slate for the whole hearing to see. She hadn't found it herself this time, but she'd have it soon enough, one way or another.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🗃️", text: "Clerk Ilsevet, summoned across the square to make the case airtight before Ashcombe's hearing reconvened, laid the torn chit, the mismatched wax and the coin tally out on the counting room table like a hand of cards nobody was going to be able to argue with.\n\n'Bring me all three,' Ilsevet said, 'and I will bind you a docket that closes this case whether Wyndham likes it or not. Show me anything less, and it's just gossip with good posture.'",
      forged: "Ilsevet cross-filed the three pieces of evidence into a single bound docket, page after page, each fact pinned against the next until the whole account of that night stood up on its own without needing Delphine, or anyone, to vouch for it.\n\nThe Cross-Filed Docket is yours! (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)",
      notforged: "Ilsevet counted the evidence laid out on the table, then counted it again, which took no time at all and made the gap perfectly clear.\n\n'Short,' said Ilsevet, tapping the empty space where the missing piece should have been. 'A docket wants all three or it's just paper with opinions. Come back when you've the full set.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
      grantItem: "gf_delphine_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "⚖️", text: "Reeve Ashcombe read the docket from first page to last without once looking up, while Wyndham stood very still and Fossey stood very still in an entirely different way, the way people stand when they have only just realised the ground under them was ever going to hold.\n\n'Dereliction,' Ashcombe said at last, 'not theft. A distinction that will matter to you a great deal more than it will to anyone else in this room, Wyndham, and I'd advise you to remember why.' He turned to Fossey next, and something in his face eased. 'You're free to go. With the Yard's apology, such as it's worth.'\n\nCorvain caught Delphine's eye across the counting room and gave her the smallest possible nod, which from Corvain was practically a parade. Delphine, for her part, allowed herself exactly one triumphant smile before it occurred to her, rather unwelcome, that Fossey had stood accused for two full days before anyone had thought to check whether the paperwork protecting him was even honest. It was not a comfortable thought. She decided to keep it anyway.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end",
      goodText: "The story of how Delphine Ashworth had unpicked a cover-up with nothing but a torn chit, a wrong-coloured seal and a head for remainders went round the Yard by suppertime, gaining several flattering embellishments along the way, none of which she bothered to correct. She was, after all, mid-anecdote about it before the ink on Ashcombe's report had even dried.\n\nBut she kept the docket's true final page to herself for a while longer than the rest of the story: a short, private note that the next time somebody's name got quietly circled on a roster, innocent until proven otherwise ought to run both ways, Wrought and Under alike, and that she, of all people, had never once had cause to wonder whether it did.",
      midText: "The case closed well enough. Wyndham was disciplined, Fossey was cleared, and Delphine's part in it was noted, filed, and largely forgotten by everyone except Delphine, who made quite sure it wasn't forgotten by her.\n\nShe told the story rather more often than the facts strictly required, though rather less often, this time, without at least one honest aside about how close it had come to going the other way for a Under boy who'd never had a circled name of his own to save him.",
      grantItem: "gf_delphine_item2" },
  },
},

gf_growl: {
  id: "gf_growl",
  title: "The Weight of the Round",
  tagline: "Vane wants an example made on Cinder Row today. Growl means to choose who it gets made of.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_growl_item1", name: "The Scald-Reader's Glove", stat: 3, how: "found",  desc: "+1 Science. Won with a sharp Science check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_growl_item2", name: "The Straight Tally",       stat: 2, how: "quest",  desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_growl_item3", name: "The Quickset Knuckles",    stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { axle_bearing: "Scavenged Axle Bearing", tan_strap: "Strip of Cured Tan-Hide", valve_coil: "Wound Valve Coil" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🐯", text: "Vane had given Growl the easy half of the job and the boy the hard half, which was Vane's idea of training.\n\n'Take Pitch round with you,' Vane had said, not looking up from the ledger. 'Cinder Row. The Sirrath girl, the one whose father reckoned at Aster Row before his lungs gave out. Three marks owing since spring, and word's got round that Ma Sindry's crew are running chalk cheaper than we are. I want it seen paid, one way or another, and I want it seen by teatime.' A pause, the kind Vane was good at. 'Let the boy do the seeing to.'\n\nPitch was twelve, or near enough, and had been running errands for the Ashlight for eleven days. He had not yet broken anything that mattered to anyone but a shopkeeper. Growl had watched that particular education happen to other children more times than he cared to count, and had once, a long time ago, been the child it happened to.\n\n'What's she owe it on?' Pitch asked, trotting to keep up.\n\n'Medicine. Her mother's chest.' Growl did not slow down. 'And she's got one thing in that flat worth breaking in front of a street full of witnesses, and it's a clockwork sparrow her father built her before the fog took him. Vane wants it seen. He didn't say what.'\n\nPitch went a little pale, which was the correct response, and said nothing else the whole way down Tallow Street. Growl let him think.", next: "valve_check" },
    valve_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔧", stat: 3, dc: 12,
      text: "The stairwell up to Tansy's landing ran past a busted junction pipe that nobody in the building had a writ to mend and nobody outside it had any interest in mending for free. It hissed white and constant, and the wall beside it was warm enough to raise a blister if you leaned on it without looking.\n\nMa Sindry's crew would be coming the same way, and coming fast, and there was exactly one dry step past that pipe if you read the venting right and exactly one scalded shin if you didn't.\n\nSomebody with a proper feel for pressure and steam had better go first.", success: "valve_find", fail: "valve_miss" },
    valve_find: { type: "story", emoji: "🧤", grantItem: "gf_growl_item1", text: "Growl watched the hiss thicken, thin, thicken again, and stepped through the gap between the second thickening and the third the way you'd step between two people arguing who hadn't noticed you yet. Dry, and unscalded, and three seconds ahead of where the pipe would have had him.\n\nWedged behind the loose brick where the pipe met the wall, left by some long-gone tenant with the same idea, was a heavy leather glove, scorched black across the palm but sound. The sort a reckoner wears to read a gauge too hot to touch bare-handed.\n\n'Put your feet where I put mine,' Growl told Pitch, and the boy did, and made it through without so much as a singed cuff.\n\nThe Scald-Reader's Glove is yours. (+1 Science, kept forever.)", next: "gate_fight" },
    valve_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "💨", setMid: true, text: "Growl misjudged the third thickening by half a breath and took the edge of the vent across one forearm, which raised a welt he'd be feeling for a week and did nothing for his temper.\n\nWhatever had been wedged behind that brick stayed wedged. Pitch made it up the stairs on his own reading of the hiss, which was worse than Growl's but not fatal, and arrived at the top looking faintly proud of himself, which Growl decided not to spoil.\n\n(The Scald-Reader's Glove stays lost this time. Another run through this adventure may find it.)", next: "gate_fight" },
    gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🥊", qLevel: 3,
      text: "Ma Sindry's crew had beaten them to the landing after all, two of them, blocking the door with the particular confidence of people who get paid the same whether the debt is theirs to collect or not.\n\n'Ashlight's slow today,' said the bigger one, not moving. 'Might be we collect first. Might be we collect different.'\n\nGrowl had spent nineteen years being the reason other people's crews thought twice about doorways. He put Pitch behind him without appearing to, which was a skill in itself, and did not raise his voice.\n\n'Move,' he said. 'Or don't, and I'll move you.'",
      enemy: { name: "Ma Sindry's Collectors", emoji: "🥊", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Growl's answer landed clean and one of the pair folded round it with a grunt that had real respect buried in it somewhere.",
        strikeText: "A fist came in low and fast, and Growl took it on the forearm rather than let it past him toward the stairs where Pitch stood.",
        defeatText: "The second collector looked at the first, looked at the stairs, and decided that Ma Sindry's coin wasn't worth this particular landing. They went the way they'd come, quickly, and without the dignity of a proper retreat." },
      victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "The landing was theirs. Pitch stood very still against the wall, breathing like he'd been the one fighting, and looked at Growl with something that wasn't quite fear and wasn't quite anything else Growl had a name for yet.\n\n'You didn't have to hit them that hard,' Pitch said.\n\n'No,' Growl agreed. 'I didn't.' He didn't explain the difference between hard enough and too hard, not yet. There'd be time, or there wouldn't, and either way explaining it now would only make it sound cleverer than it was.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🩹", text: "The collectors got the better of that particular exchange, and Growl pulled Pitch back down half a flight before either of them lost more than pride.\n\n'We're not going up that way,' Growl said, pressing a hand flat against his ribs. 'We're going round. Might take the hour we don't have. Might take longer.' He didn't sound worried about it, which worried Pitch more than any amount of worrying would have.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🕯️", text: "An hour, then, before they went up for good, and Growl meant to spend every minute of it. Vane wanted the debt seen paid. Fine. Growl intended to make sure what got seen and what actually happened were two different arrangements entirely, and for that he needed something heavy enough to pass for iron in a hurried tally and nobody's business how he came by it.\n\nThree places within the hour might give him that. The scrapyard on Founder's Cut, where the furnace-men played a game with the leftover plates that Growl had never once beaten. The boiler stacks, where the fourth venting of the day was due and the shortcut behind them was only safe on a good roll of the dice. Or Tanner's Row, where a debt of his own, years old and never called in, might finally be worth calling.", options: [
      { label: "Try the scrapyard's furnace game", next: "scrapyard_setup", preview: "A hard reasoning puzzle about a furnace that only ever spits out one number. Logic is Growl's weak stat, so this one will make him work." },
      { label: "Cut behind the boiler stacks", next: "stacks_setup", preview: "Time the dash between vents on a roll of the dice. Miss it and the fog does not care whose fault that was." },
      { label: "Call in the debt on Tanner's Row", next: "tannery_setup", preview: "An old grudge with fists attached. This one is a fight." },
    ] },
    scrapyard_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "⚖️", text: "The furnace-men of Founder's Cut ran a game older than any of them: two scrap plates go into the furnace, one plate comes out, weighing exactly the difference between the two. Do that enough times, over a whole heap of plates, and eventually there's only one plate left in the yard.\n\nBet on what that last plate weighs and you could walk off with a fortnight's worth of scrap for nothing. Bet wrong and the yard keeps your stake. Growl had lost three marks to this game as a much younger, much stupider Sirrath, and had never once worked out why.", next: "scrapyard_puzzle" },
    scrapyard_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "🔩", text: "Twenty plates lay in tonight's heap, stamped 1 through 20 in grist-weight. The foreman chalked up the wager on a slate and turned to Growl with the look of a man who has taken this exact bet from a great many confident people.\n\n'Fuse away two at a time, difference each time, till there's one plate left,' he said. 'You tell me which of these it absolutely cannot be, and the whole heap's yours to call scrap against whatever you're settling tonight.'", question: "The furnace fuses any two plates of weight a and b into one plate of weight |a - b|, over and over, until one plate remains from the heap of twenty plates weighing 1 to 20 grist. Which of these weights can the final plate NOT possibly be?", options: ["6 grist", "9 grist", "14 grist", "20 grist"], answer: 1,
      hint: "Forget trying to trace every possible fusion. Ask instead what happens to the parity, odd or even, of the total weight of the whole heap each time two plates are fused into one.",
      solution: "The plates 1 to 20 sum to 210, which is even. Each fusion removes a and b from the heap's total and adds back |a - b| instead. Since a + b and |a - b| always differ by 2 x the smaller of the two, they share the same parity, so the total weight of everything left in the yard never changes from odd to even or back. It starts even, so it must always stay even, and the very last plate carries that whole total by itself. It has to be even. Of the four options, only 9 is odd, so 9 is the one weight the final plate absolutely cannot be.",
      good: "scrapyard_good", mid: "scrapyard_mid" },
    scrapyard_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🔩", grantComponent: "axle_bearing", text: "The foreman's face did something complicated. 'Nobody's ever answered that one straight off,' he said, and it was almost an accusation. 'You count like a man who's had it done to him.'\n\n'Something like that,' said Growl, and took the heap: scrap iron, a rusted axle bearing among it, weighty and dull and exactly the sort of thing that looks like three marks of settled debt to anybody not counting closely.\n\nComponent found: Scavenged Axle Bearing.", next: "converge" },
    scrapyard_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", setMid: true, text: "Growl talked himself round the parity twice and lost the thread of it both times, which needled him more than losing the stake did. Logic had never been his edge, and the furnace-men of Founder's Cut had built an entire trade out of exactly that gap in people like him.\n\n'Come back when you've slept on it,' the foreman said, not unkindly, sweeping the heap back into a pile. 'The maths isn't going anywhere. Neither's the furnace.'", next: "converge" },
    stacks_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🌫️", text: "The shortcut behind the boiler stacks ran directly under the Engine's fourth venting of the day, a corridor of scalding fog on a schedule nobody controlled and everybody in the Under had learned to read in their bones.\n\nTime it right and you crossed clean between the third rumble and the fourth. Time it wrong and you spent the walk home explaining a burn nobody would believe was an accident. Growl had crossed it a hundred times. He still watched the die in his hand like it owed him something.", next: "stacks_dice" },
    stacks_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 5, text: "Roll 5 or more on a six-sided die to catch the gap between vents clean and come out the far side of the stacks dry.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 5 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/3", "1/2", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "Only the rolls of 5 and 6 succeed. That is 2 outcomes out of 6, and 2/6 simplifies to 1/3. Not good odds for a corridor full of scalding fog, but Growl has crossed on worse. Predicting them correctly steadies his stride: +1 to the roll." },
      success: "stacks_good", fail: "stacks_miss" },
    stacks_good: { type: "story", emoji: "💨", grantComponent: "axle_bearing", text: "Growl went through the gap between the third rumble and the fourth without breaking stride, fog curling shut behind him like a door closing on somebody else's business.\n\nHalf-buried in the ash at the far end, thrown clear of some engine housing years back and never claimed, sat a scavenged axle bearing, rusted solid but sound underneath, weighty enough to fool a hurried tally.\n\nComponent found: Scavenged Axle Bearing.", next: "converge" },
    stacks_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🥵", setMid: true, text: "The fourth venting came half a breath early, or Growl went half a breath late, and the difference was a scald along one arm that would blister by morning and a great deal of very quiet cursing in the old Sirrath tongue.\n\nWhatever had been lying in that ash stayed in it. Growl came out the far side of the stacks with nothing but the burn to show for the attempt, and went on with the hour ticking down harder than before.", next: "converge" },
    tannery_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🏭", text: "Tanner's Row smelled of hide and lye and old debts. Somewhere in a back room a man named Coop had once owed Growl a favour for a beating he'd never had to take, and Growl had never once called it in, on the theory that an uncollected debt is worth more than a collected one.\n\nThat theory ran out the moment he walked in and found the door held not by Coop but by a Clockwork Bailiff on hire from the tannery guild, sent to make sure nobody's old favours interfered with the day's proper business.", next: "tannery_fight" },
    tannery_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "⚙️", qLevel: 3,
      text: "The Bailiff was steam-driven and stamped-sealed, built to enforce a schedule rather than an opinion, and it did not recognise Growl, Coop, or the concept of an old favour.\n\n'REGISTERED VISIT ONLY,' it clanked, arms unfolding into something with more edges than a greeting needs. 'STATE YOUR WRIT.'\n\n'Haven't got one,' said Growl, and put his weight on the back foot. 'Going to have to make do with the alternative.'",
      enemy: { name: "The Clockwork Bailiff", emoji: "⚙️", hits: 2, toHit: 9,
        hitText: "The answer went in at a joint the Bailiff's maker hadn't reinforced, and something inside it hissed in protest.",
        strikeText: "A stamped brass arm swept round in a slow arc, and Growl caught it on the shoulder rather than let it catch Pitch by the door." ,
        defeatText: "The Bailiff's boiler note dropped, then dropped again, and it folded down onto its own chassis with the dignity of a clock running out of wind halfway through striking the hour." },
      victory: "tannery_good", retreat: "tannery_bad" },
    tannery_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧵", grantComponent: "axle_bearing", text: "Coop came out from the back room at the sound of the Bailiff giving up, took one look at Growl, and had the grace not to ask why it had taken nineteen years for that favour to come calling.\n\n'Whatever you need,' Coop said, and meant it, and what Growl needed turned out to be sitting in a crate of guild scrap by the door: a scavenged axle bearing, heavier than it looked, exactly the kind of dead weight that reads as settled debt to anyone not counting closely.\n\nComponent found: Scavenged Axle Bearing.", next: "converge" },
    tannery_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🩹", setMid: true, text: "The Bailiff got the better exchange of it, and Growl pulled back rather than let it land a third blow with Pitch standing in the doorway watching.\n\nCoop shouted something about coming back with proper paperwork next time, which was Coop's way of saying he was sorry, and the crate of guild scrap stayed exactly where the Bailiff's chassis had fallen against it, unreachable and unclaimed.", next: "converge" },
    converge: { type: "story", emoji: "🛤️", text: "Whichever way the hour had gone, Growl came out of it with something heavy enough to fool a tally, a boy who'd stopped asking why, and considerably less time than he'd started with.\n\nTwo more things and he'd have what he needed to build Pitch something better to remember than a broken sparrow. Peg's yard would take him the rest, if Peg's yard was still open, and Peg's yard closed the moment the shutters came down for the four o'clock venting.", next: "counter_puzzle" },
    counter_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⏱️", text: "It was twenty minutes to four by the tannery clock, which ran three minutes fast and had done for years, so call it twenty minutes true. The vent sounded at four o'clock sharp and Peg's shutters came down with it, no exceptions, not even for Ashlight.\n\nPeg's counter was 900 metres off along Cinder Row. Growl could cover 75 metres a minute at a proper march, but the Row was thick with the four o'clock crowd, heading home before the fog, and it would cut his pace to four-fifths of that.", question: "Growl has 20 minutes until the shutters come down. Peg's counter is 900 metres away. The crowded street slows his pace to four-fifths of his usual 75 metres per minute. Does he reach the counter before the shutters close, and if so, with how many minutes to spare?", options: ["No, he's 3 minutes short", "Yes, with 5 minutes to spare", "Yes, with 8 minutes to spare", "Yes, with 12 minutes to spare"], answer: 1,
      hint: "Work out his actual speed after the crowd slows him down first, then divide the distance by that speed, then compare the time it takes against the 20 minutes he has.",
      solution: "Four-fifths of 75 metres per minute is 60 metres per minute. Covering 900 metres at 60 metres per minute takes 900 / 60 = 15 minutes. He has 20 minutes before the shutters close, so 20 - 15 leaves 5 minutes to spare.",
      good: "counter_good", mid: "counter_mid" },
    counter_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧵", grantComponent: "tan_strap", text: "Growl came round the last corner with time enough to breathe before knocking, which felt like luxury after the day so far. Peg was already halfway through pulling the shutter down and stopped it with one boot, unimpressed and unsurprised in the way of someone who has been doing this since before Growl was born.\n\n'Five minutes,' Peg said, checking the clock. 'Generous of you.' A strip of cured tan-hide came off a hook by the door, tough as old leather gets, meant for binding a grip that took a beating. 'That's for whatever you're building, not whatever you're planning to hit with it. I know the difference in your face, Sirrath, even if you don't.'\n\nComponent found: Strip of Cured Tan-Hide.", next: "spring_check" },
    counter_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", setMid: true, text: "Growl made the door with the shutter already halfway down and no argument left in Peg's face that would raise it back up. Rules were rules on venting days, even Peg's own rules, especially Peg's own rules.\n\n'Tomorrow,' Peg called through the gap, not unkindly, and the shutter finished its descent with a clang that meant exactly what it sounded like.", next: "spring_check" },
    spring_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "⚡", stat: 4, dc: 13,
      text: "Round the side of Peg's yard, half-forgotten under a tarpaulin, a valve coil worked loose from an old compressor and began, slowly and then not slowly at all, to unspool itself toward the drain grate at the yard's low corner.\n\nWound tight, that coil was worth having. Halfway down a drain, it was worth nothing to anybody. Somebody quick had better get a hand on it before gravity finished the argument.", success: "spring_find", fail: "spring_miss" },
    spring_find: { type: "story", emoji: "🌀", grantComponent: "valve_coil", text: "Growl's hand closed round the coil a hair's breadth from the grate, quicker than the thing had any right to expect from something his size. It was still warm, and it hummed faintly with stored tension, the way anything does when it's been wound tighter than it was ever meant to sit.\n\nPitch, watching from the yard gate, said, 'I didn't even see you move,' which was the first thing all day he'd said like he meant it as a compliment rather than a warning to himself.\n\nComponent found: Wound Valve Coil.", next: "forge" },
    spring_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🕳️", setMid: true, text: "The coil went down the grate a half-second ahead of Growl's fingers, unspooling into the dark with a diminishing rattle that sounded, if anything, faintly pleased with itself.\n\n'Leave it,' Growl said, straightening up, before either of them could suggest going in after it. Some things you only get one clean reach at, and the drains of Cinder Row keep whatever they're given.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "story", emoji: "🔨", text: "Peg's forge glowed low and steady behind the yard, the one part of the day that had kept to its schedule.\n\n'Knuckle-guard, is it,' Peg said, turning the components over one by one on the bench: the axle bearing, the tan-hide, the coil, if all three had made it this far. 'For a Sirrath who already hits like a dropped anvil. Bold choice.'\n\n'Not for hitting harder,' Growl said. 'For hitting exactly as hard as I mean to. There's a difference and I'd like to stop finding it out the day after.'",
      forged: "Peg set the bearing into a housing, wound the coil through the grip for give, and bound the whole thing tight in tan-hide until it sat on Growl's knuckles like something built rather than something worn.\n\n'Won't stop a blow landing,' Peg said. 'Will stop it landing all the way through. That's the whole of the trick, most days.'\n\nThe Quickset Knuckles are yours! (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
      notforged: "Peg counted what was on the bench and shook his head slowly. 'Short a piece or two,' he said, not unkindly. 'A guard wants the full set or it's just leather with opinions. Come back when you've the rest. The forge isn't going anywhere.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
      grantItem: "gf_growl_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "🐦", text: "Tansy's door, at last, with the street already gathering the way streets do when they can smell an example coming.\n\nGrowl set the axle bearing down on her table with a clang loud enough for the landing to hear, and made a great show of weighing three marks of scrap against three marks of chalk on Vane's borrowed scale, muttering about interest and lateness for the benefit of anyone listening at the door. Underneath the noise, quieter than the scale, he counted out the actual remainder of the debt from his own coat, in mark and cog, and told Tansy to chalk it paid and say nothing about who'd settled it.\n\nThe clockwork sparrow sat on the shelf behind her the whole time, wings folded, untouched. Nobody outside that room would ever know it had been in danger at all, which was rather the point.\n\n'That's it?' Pitch asked, once they were back on the stairs. 'That's the lesson?'\n\n'The lesson,' said Growl, 'is that you can make a street believe you've broken something without breaking it. Remember that longer than you remember anything else I teach you.' He didn't say the rest, which was that somebody had once done something similar for a Sirrath boiler-hand a few streets over, steering him toward a workshop instead of a recruitment, and that Growl still didn't know if that had been mercy or just a man placing a bet against himself years too late. Some lessons you pass on before you've finished learning them yourself.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end",
      goodText: "Word got back to Vane, as word always did, that the Cinder Row debt had been seen paid and seen paid hard, and Vane was satisfied in the specific way of a man who only ever checks that the noise was loud enough, never what it was made of.\n\nTansy's sparrow kept its wings. Pitch walked home a different shape of quiet than he'd walked out in, turning something over that he wasn't ready to say out loud yet and might not be for years. Growl went back to the Ashlight's ledgers that night the same enforcer he'd been that morning, on paper, in the eyes of everyone who mattered to his wages. Not redeemed. Not finished with the job. Just, once again, angry on somebody else's behalf, and quietly certain that particular anger had landed somewhere useful this time.",
      midText: "The debt got paid down further than Vane had any right to expect and not so far as Growl would have liked. The sparrow came through with one dented wing, quick work with a pair of pliers before anyone in the street got close enough to see, and Tansy's mother never learned how near it had come to worse.\n\nPitch went home rattled and mostly silent, which was its own kind of lesson learned, just a rougher one than Growl had hoped to teach on a first round. Growl clocked back in with Vane that night the same as ever: still the Ashlight's fist, still working for people he had no illusions about, still counting, in his own slow way, exactly how hard was hard enough.",
      grantItem: "gf_growl_item2" },
  },
},

gf_pemberton: {
  id: "gf_pemberton",
  title: "The Transposed Row",
  tagline: "A tired hand swapped two digits on two dockets, and swapped two futures along with them. Pemberton has until the ink-run bell to swap them back.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_pemberton_item1", name: "The Nine-Column Slate", stat: 0, how: "found", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Won with a sharp Arithmetic check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_pemberton_item2", name: "The Corrected Return", stat: 2, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_pemberton_item3", name: "The Quick-Set Stamp", stat: 4, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Speed. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { torn_carbon: "The Scorched Carbon Copy", runner_chit: "The Runner's Chit", true_stamp: "The Uncompromised Stamp" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🗄️", text: "Friday reconciliation. Pemberton liked Fridays the way a man likes the smaller of two available headaches: the boards came down off the wall, the carbons came up out of the drawers, and for two quiet hours nobody wanted anything from him except correct sums, which he could reliably provide.\n\nIt had been a fortnight since a Wrought officer with a level voice had asked him four polite questions and walked out knowing about a room he was not supposed to have mentioned. He had tried, and failed, to get the vault's cipher changed before she could use what she'd learned. Nobody had come for him about it yet. He checked the corridor before he checked anything else, these days, and told himself it was just thoroughness.\n\nThoroughness was, as it happened, how he found it. Board Nine, Stratum V, second column: SLACK, P. 52 marks. Pemberton frowned at the name the way you frown at a coat that doesn't fit the peg. He'd stamped that girl's paper himself, three weeks back, and remembered thinking, with the small private pleasure of a man who loves a clean sum, that whoever P. Slack was, she wrote like someone who'd never once been allowed to be lazy about a fraction.\n\n52 did not sound like that girl. 52 sounded like a mistake wearing her name.", next: "arithmetic_check" },
    arithmetic_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🧮", stat: 0, dc: 11,
      text: "The master tally sheet ran to nine hundred rows in a clerk's hand gone ragged somewhere around row six hundred, the point in any triple shift where digits stop meaning anything and start just being shapes you're owed home for. Pemberton laid two dockets side by side under the lamp: SLACK, P., number 4179, and two rows below it, DOSS, C., number 4197.\n\nSame four digits. Different order, near the end. The kind of thing a wide-awake clerk catches in a blink and an exhausted one copies faithfully, twice, onto two different boards, and never thinks of again.\n\nSomebody exact needed to check every digit against the carbon stubs before deciding this was worth the trouble it was about to become.", success: "slate_found", fail: "slate_missed" },
    slate_found: { type: "story", emoji: "📏", grantItem: "gf_pemberton_item1", text: "Digit by digit, column by column, it held. 4-1-7-9 against 4-1-9-7, the seven and the nine sitting in each other's chairs like two men who'd swapped coats in a dark cloakroom and gone home wearing the wrong one without either of them noticing.\n\nWedged behind the tally sheet, forgotten by whoever had last used the drawer, Pemberton found a nine-column slate, chalk-dust worn into every groove, the sort of thing a truly careful predecessor keeps for exactly this. It fit his hand like it had been waiting for someone who'd actually use it properly.\n\nThe Nine-Column Slate is his. (+1 Arithmetic, kept forever.)", next: "gate_fight" },
    slate_missed: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "The columns blurred rather than resolved. Pemberton went over the digits three times, got three faintly different answers, and had to admit, quietly, to the empty room, that his own hand was shaking slightly, which was not a condition conducive to arithmetic.\n\nHe was almost certain it was a transposition. Almost certain is not a phrase that survives contact with a Contention fee. He would have to be surer than this, and quickly, and without the slate he'd have liked to have found.", next: "gate_fight" },
    gate_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "📋", qLevel: 3,
      text: "The Long Gallery's counter hatch was manned that hour by Under-Clerk Vance, a thin, correct man three years Pemberton's junior who had memorised the entire Procedures for the Handling of Calibration Returns and quoted it the way frightened people quote scripture.\n\n'Boards are collated for the Bindery, Clerk Pemberton,' Vance said, not unkindly, already reaching for the requisition ledger. 'Nothing comes off the wall without a form eleven-C and a countersignature, and I don't have your countersignature, and it's gone four, and I am not,' his voice climbed half a register, 'having today be the day I get this wrong.'\n\nHe wasn't being difficult. He was being exactly as frightened of the office as Pemberton was, from the other side of the same hatch, and that made him no easier to get past at all.",
      enemy: { name: "Under-Clerk Vance", emoji: "📋", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Pemberton answered the figure clean and quick, and Vance's certainty wobbled a visible inch.",
        strikeText: "Vance recited a sub-clause at speed, word-perfect and entirely correct, and Pemberton's nerve took the hit instead of his argument.",
        defeatText: "Vance ran out of clauses before Pemberton ran out of correct answers, and stood back from the hatch with the stunned, faintly grateful look of a man who has just been allowed to stop being right about the wrong thing." },
      victory: "gate_win", retreat: "gate_retreat" },
    gate_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🔓", text: "'You'll minute this,' Vance said, already reaching for a fresh form, because some men make peace with a crisis by giving it paperwork. 'Every step, mind. If this goes wrong I want it written down whose idea it was.'\n\n'It'll be written down,' Pemberton said, and meant it as comfort, and heard, as it left his mouth, how little comfort it actually was.\n\nHe was through. Board Nine hung ahead of him under its brass frame, SLACK, P. still reading 52, still wrong, still ticking down towards six o'clock same as everything else in the building.", next: "crossroads" },
    gate_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "Pemberton backed off from the hatch rather than fight a frightened colleague to a standstill in front of the whole Gallery queue, which did him credit and cost him nine minutes he did not have.\n\nHe went round by the side stair instead, past the pigeonholes, past a Laachmi clerk three heads taller than him who didn't look up from her filing, and came out on Board Nine's landing with dust on his cuffs and considerably less patience than he'd started the afternoon with.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🧾", text: "Suspicion was not proof, and Pemberton had watched enough Petitions of Error die in committee to know the difference intimately. He needed something that would still be true after somebody hostile had looked at it twice: Peony Slack's actual marked script, recovered whole from wherever the archive had buried it.\n\nThree ways down to where the old scripts slept: the Archive proper, three floors under the Gallery, where nothing was ever thrown away and almost nothing was ever findable either. The service stair, steep and dim, that the runners used to move dockets between floors at a run. Or straight down through the Bindery floor itself, past whoever was on shift starting the day's binding early.", options: [
      { label: "Search the Archive stacks", next: "archive_setup", preview: "Find Peony's original script buried in the paper stacks. A real arithmetic puzzle guards the proof." },
      { label: "Take the service stair", next: "stair_setup", preview: "A fast, risky run down the runners' stair. A dice roll decides whether you keep your footing." },
      { label: "Cut through the Bindery floor", next: "floor_setup", preview: "The most direct route, and the most exposed. This one is a confrontation." },
    ] },
    archive_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🗃️", text: "The Archive smelled of dust and old glue, and the Archivist, a stooped Sirrath woman named Coll who had catalogued every scrap in the building for thirty years and trusted precisely none of it, made Pemberton sign for the key before she'd even asked what he wanted.\n\n'Slack, P., Calibration this season,' she said, not moving. 'Original script, not the carbon. That's a proper request, that is. Most people just want the carbon, because the carbon's easier to lose quietly.' She found the box in under a minute, which told Pemberton everything about how little she trusted the rest of the building to do the same.", next: "archive_puzzle" },
    archive_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "📜", text: "Peony's rough working papers, tucked loose in the same box as her marked script, gave two of her three section marks in her own small, exact hand. Coll set them on the table and folded her arms, the posture of someone who has decided to watch rather than help.", question: "The Calibration paper is marked out of 120 in three sections: Section One out of 40, Section Two out of 50, Section Three out of 30. Peony's rough papers show 34 for Section One and 41 for Section Two. The disputed ledger row gives her grand total as 87. If 87 truly belongs to her, what must her Section Three mark have been?", options: ["9", "12", "15", "19"], answer: 1,
      hint: "Add the two marks you already know, then see what's left of the total of 87.",
      solution: "34 + 41 = 75. The disputed total is 87, so the missing Section Three mark is 87 − 75 = 12. Coll turns up Peony's actual scorched script from the bottom of the box: Section Three, 12 out of 30, in the examiner's own red ink. It matches exactly. The 87 was never Cutter Doss's. It was always hers.",
      good: "archive_good", mid: "archive_mid" },
    archive_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧾", grantComponent: "torn_carbon", text: "Coll unfolded her arms, which for Coll was roughly equivalent to a standing ovation. 'Twelve out of thirty,' she read aloud, tapping the scorched corner of the script where the ink had run in some old spill. 'Same hand as the rough working. Same girl.'\n\nShe let Pemberton take the scorched carbon copy of the script itself, corner burned but the marks perfectly legible, and stamped his requisition without being asked twice. 'Don't lose that,' she said. 'It's the only version of the truth this building's going to believe.'\n\nComponent found: the Scorched Carbon Copy.", next: "converge" },
    archive_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🔍", setMid: true, text: "Pemberton got there in the end, but slowly, working the sum twice on the back of a requisition slip while Coll watched with the patient, faintly disappointed expression of a woman who has seen a great many people arrive at the truth by the scenic route.\n\n'You'll want to be quicker than that downstairs,' she said, not cruelly, and handed him the scorched carbon copy anyway, because being slow at arithmetic is not a crime, whatever it feels like under a clock. 'Go on. The bell won't wait for you to feel clever about it.'\n\nComponent found: the Scorched Carbon Copy.", next: "converge" },
    stair_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🪜", text: "The runners' stair was steep, dim, and built for people half Pemberton's age moving twice his speed, which described precisely nobody currently on it. He could hear a boy's boots clattering down three floors below, fearless in the way only people who have never yet broken anything important can afford to be.\n\nSpeed was not Pemberton's strength. He knew this about himself the way you know your own handwriting: without pride, without much hope of it changing, and with the settled certainty that today was going to ask for it anyway.", next: "stair_dice" },
    stair_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 5, text: "The stair narrowed at the second landing where a loose flag had never been fixed. Roll 5 or more on a six-sided die to take it at a run without losing your footing.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 5 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/3", "1/2", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "Only the rolls of 5 and 6 succeed. That's 2 outcomes out of 6, and 2/6 = 1/3. Not good odds for a man who is neither young nor sure-footed, but knowing the odds exactly is the only advantage he's got. +1 to your roll." },
      success: "stair_good", fail: "stair_mid" },
    stair_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🏃", grantComponent: "torn_carbon", text: "Pemberton took the loose flag at a dead run and somehow, astonishingly, stayed upright, arriving at the Archive landing with his heart going like a badly wound clock and his ledger clutched so tight to his chest it had left a mark.\n\nColl had the box already half open, drawn by the noise. 'Slack, P.?' she said, and when Pemberton nodded, wheezing, she found the scorched script in under a minute and handed it over without a word, the way you hand a drowning man a rope: quickly, and without commentary.\n\nComponent found: the Scorched Carbon Copy.", next: "converge" },
    stair_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "🩹", setMid: true, text: "The loose flag did exactly what loose flags do. Pemberton went down it rather than over it, landed hard on the next step, and spent an ugly, undignified minute establishing that nothing was broken except his composure.\n\nHe finished the climb down at a limp, considerably slower and considerably more honest with himself about his own speed than he'd been at the top. Coll took one look at him, said nothing about it, and fetched the scorched script anyway.\n\nComponent found: the Scorched Carbon Copy.", next: "converge" },
    floor_setup: { type: "story", emoji: "🏭", text: "The Bindery floor was heat and leather and the low iron smell of hot wax, and it was already busy: two binders working the day's first ledgers into their covers, and standing over them, checking the seals as they went, Overseer Marsh, a broad, weary woman who had run this floor for eleven years and had never once let a document leave it improperly bound.\n\nShe clocked Pemberton the second he came through the door, uninvited, unannounced, and read him correctly in about four seconds flat: a records clerk with a problem and no proper authorisation to be standing where he was standing.\n\n'This floor's for binding, not for browsing,' Marsh said, planting herself between him and the archive door beyond. 'You want something off it, you go through the front like everyone else. Rules are rules, especially on a day when everything else is coming loose.'", next: "floor_fight" },
    floor_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "⚖️", qLevel: 3,
      text: "Marsh wasn't going to hit anyone, and neither was Pemberton, whatever the tension in the room suggested. What she was going to do was ask him, precisely and repeatedly, to justify every single thing about his presence on her floor, in the flat, relentless manner of someone who has spent eleven years being the only obstacle between chaos and a properly bound ledger.\n\n'Authorisation,' she said. 'Purpose. Time of departure.' Each question landed like a closing door, and Pemberton had to answer every one correctly, and fast, or lose the argument entirely.",
      enemy: { name: "Overseer Marsh", emoji: "⚖️", hits: 3, toHit: 9,
        hitText: "Pemberton answered clean and exact, and Marsh's flat expression cracked, very slightly, towards something like respect.",
        strikeText: "A question landed he didn't have a ready answer for, and Marsh's silence afterwards did more damage than shouting would have." ,
        defeatText: "Marsh ran out of questions before Pemberton ran out of correct answers, folded her arms, and stepped, deliberately, one pace out of the doorway." },
      victory: "floor_good", retreat: "floor_retreat" },
    floor_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🗝️", grantComponent: "torn_carbon", text: "'Archive's through there,' Marsh said, jerking a thumb at the door she'd been blocking. 'Coll'll have what you want. And Clerk Pemberton,' she added, as he went past her, 'if this is what I think it is, you'd better be right, because I'm not unsealing anything twice for the same mistake.'\n\nColl had the scorched script waiting before he'd finished explaining himself, having heard the whole exchange through the wall with the professional interest of a woman who missed nothing that happened near her boxes.\n\nComponent found: the Scorched Carbon Copy.", next: "converge" },
    floor_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚫", setMid: true, text: "Marsh didn't move, didn't shout, didn't need to. Pemberton retreated up the corridor with his dignity in worse condition than his prospects, and had to go the long way round through the Archive's front counter after all, ten precious minutes poorer for the detour.\n\nColl took one look at his face and didn't ask. She just fetched the scorched script, because whatever had happened on the Bindery floor, the truth in the box hadn't changed.\n\nComponent found: the Scorched Carbon Copy.", next: "converge" },
    converge: { type: "story", emoji: "⏳", text: "Whichever way he'd come, Pemberton stood now in the Gallery with the scorched carbon copy of Peony Slack's true script in his ledger, next to the folded periodical he never quite found time to read and had long since stopped pretending he would. Proof, at least, he now had.\n\nProof was not enough. Correcting a sealed board without proper standing invited exactly the kind of question he could least afford to answer this season, and the boards went down to the Bindery at the six o'clock bell whether he was ready or not. He needed a runner sent ahead to hold the binding on Board Nine and Board Two, and he needed it now.", next: "chit_dice" },
    chit_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 2, text: "The nearest runner boy was young, fast, and thoroughly bribeable with the last of Pemberton's lunch money. Roll 2 or more on a six-sided die to catch him before he set off on his next round.", predict: { question: "Before you roll: what is the chance of rolling 2 or more on a fair six-sided die?", options: ["1/6", "1/2", "5/6", "1"], answer: 2, why: "Only a roll of exactly 1 fails. That leaves 5 outcomes out of 6 that succeed, so the chance is 5/6. Good odds, for once, on a day that hasn't been offering many. Predicting them correctly steadies your hand: +1 to your roll." },
      success: "chit_good", fail: "chit_bad" },
    chit_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🏃‍♂️", grantComponent: "runner_chit", text: "The boy caught the coin, caught the instruction, and was gone down the corridor at a speed Pemberton hadn't managed on his best day as a child, let alone this one. He came back four minutes later with a scrawled chit from the Bindery clerk on duty, holding both boards for exactly twenty minutes, no more, signed and countersigned.\n\nThe Runner's Chit was real, was legal, and bought him time he had not had five minutes ago.\n\nComponent found: the Runner's Chit.", next: "stamp_check" },
    chit_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "😟", setMid: true, text: "The boy had already gone, off down some other corridor on some other errand, and the next runner Pemberton found was slower, less certain, and came back nine minutes later with nothing but an apologetic shrug and the news that the Bindery clerk on duty wanted it in writing, properly, through channels, which was precisely the thing there was no time left for.\n\nPemberton would have to move faster on his own account now, with no chit and no extra minutes bought.", next: "stamp_check" },
    stamp_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔏", stat: 2, dc: 12,
      text: "Any amendment made after posting needed the office's amendment stamp, and the amendment stamp drawer held eleven of the things, seven retired, three reissued, and exactly one currently live, logged out under a code Pemberton would have to work out from the ledger's own numbering, because the label had worn illegibly smooth sometime around the reign of somebody's grandfather.\n\nUsing the wrong stamp, on a day like this, would not look like an honest mistake. It would look exactly like the thing Corvain was still, somewhere, quietly investigating. Somebody with real Logic needed to work out which stamp was the live one, and be certain of it, not hopeful.", success: "stamp_good", fail: "stamp_bad" },
    stamp_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✅", grantComponent: "true_stamp", text: "It came out the way good reasoning does: the retired stamps were all logged with an even code and a strike-through date, the reissued ones carried last season's mark, and that left exactly one drawer, second from the left, with a code that matched this season's ledger header precisely.\n\nHe drew it out, checked it a third time out of pure habit, and found it uncompromised: clean, current, entirely his to use.\n\nThe Uncompromised Stamp is his to carry. Component found: the Uncompromised Stamp.", next: "risk_choice" },
    stamp_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🗄️", setMid: true, text: "The codes swam. Two drawers looked equally plausible, and Pemberton, sweating properly now, did not trust himself to guess between them with a Contention fee riding on the answer.\n\nHe would have to go on without a stamp he could be fully certain of, which meant the amendment, if it happened at all, would happen the harder way: countersigned in person by whoever was overseeing the Bindery, on the strength of the proof alone.", next: "risk_choice" },
    risk_choice: { type: "choice", emoji: "🕰️", text: "The Gallery clock said quarter to six. Two paths remained: sprint straight down to the Bindery now, cut through the last of the paperwork on the move, and gamble on catching Board Nine and Board Two before the wax went on. Or take the slower, correct route through the Deputy Registrar's office first, get a proper countersignature, and arrive with everything in perfect order, if not necessarily in time.\n\nPemberton, who had spent twenty-eight years being exactly the second kind of man, discovered he did not much like how that plan felt today.", options: [
      { label: "Sprint for the Bindery now (risky, with a reward)", next: "risk_dice", preview: "A hard d20 roll, but you roll it with advantage. Success buys extra minutes and settles your nerve for the confrontation ahead." },
      { label: "Go through the Deputy Registrar first (safe and correct)", next: "safe_route", preview: "No risk, no reward, and a countersignature nobody can argue with later." },
    ] },
    risk_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🌙", die: 20, target: 11, advantage: true, text: "Pemberton ran, which was not a thing he did often or well, down three flights and along the pipe gallery, ledger clamped to his chest. With adrenaline doing the work his legs weren't built for, roll with ADVANTAGE: roll the twenty-sided die twice and keep the higher. You need 11 or more to reach the Bindery floor before the wax cart does.", predict: { question: "One roll of a d20 succeeds (11 or more) half the time. Rolling TWICE and keeping the higher, what is the chance of success now?", options: ["Still 1/2", "3/4", "1/4", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "You only fail if BOTH rolls fail. Each roll fails half the time, so both fail 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4 of the time, which means you succeed the other 3/4 of the time. Advantage is worth exactly that much, and knowing the number steadies your footing: +1 to your roll." },
      success: "risk_good", fail: "risk_bad" },
    risk_good: { type: "story", emoji: "✨", bonusStars: 2, text: "Pemberton arrived at the Bindery floor with a stitch in his side, dust on his knees from one bad step near the pipe gallery, and, astonishingly, five whole minutes to spare, the wax cart still two boards away from Nine and Two.\n\nHe had never in his working life moved that fast for anything, and the small, unfamiliar feeling sitting under his ribs alongside the stitch took him a moment to identify. It was, he realised with some surprise, something close to pride.\n\nBonus: +2 ⭐ at the end of the adventure.", next: "final_fight" },
    risk_bad: { type: "story", emoji: "🥵", setMid: true, text: "Pemberton's legs gave out on the second flight, not dramatically, just completely, and he arrived at the Bindery floor two minutes later than he'd hoped, wheezing, having lost his hat somewhere near the pipe gallery and most of his composure with it.\n\nTwo minutes is not nothing, on a day measured in them. He would have less room to argue than he'd wanted.", next: "final_fight" },
    safe_route: { type: "story", emoji: "🖋️", text: "The Deputy Registrar's office was three corridors out of the way and the Deputy Registrar himself was, as ever, correct, unhurried, and entirely unmoved by the phrase 'the bell's about to go.' He read the proof twice, asked one sharp question Pemberton was grateful he could actually answer, and signed.\n\nPemberton walked to the Bindery at a steady, sensible pace, countersignature in hand, arriving with rather less margin than he'd like and rather more paper backing him up than the sprint would have bought. Different risk, same clock.", next: "final_fight" },
    final_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🕯️", qLevel: 3,
      text: "Bindery-Master Coyle was standing over Board Nine with the wax pot already hot, watching the clock rather than the ledger, the way a man watches something he'd rather not be responsible for. He was not unkind. He was tired, and it was six minutes to the bell, and Pemberton was asking him to unravel two hours of careful binding on the strength of a scorched piece of carbon and a clerk he barely knew.\n\n'Everything you're telling me might be true,' Coyle said, not moving the wax pot an inch. 'But I bind what's put in front of me correctly, and I've bound thousands of these, and I am not stopping the whole floor on a hunch.' He wasn't wrong to be careful. That was, infuriatingly, rather the point of him.",
      enemy: { name: "Bindery-Master Coyle", emoji: "🕯️", hits: 4, toHit: 10,
        hitText: "Pemberton laid the proof out clean and exact, and Coyle's caution gave an inch, then another.",
        strikeText: "Coyle raised a fair, procedural objection Pemberton hadn't quite prepared for, and the clock kept moving while he found an answer.",
        defeatText: "Coyle looked at the last figure, looked at the clock, and set the wax pot down with the air of a man who has just decided, against considerable professional caution, to be persuaded." },
      victory: "final_win", retreat: "final_retreat" },
    final_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🔨", text: "'Right,' said Coyle, and it cost him visibly to say it, a man giving up eleven years of careful habit in a single word. 'Show me the stamp. Show me the chit. And God help you both if you're wrong.'\n\nHe was not wrong. Board Nine, Board Two, the whole matter, sat waiting on the counter in front of him, six minutes from being unfixable, and entirely fixable still.", next: "forge" },
    final_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "⏰", text: "Coyle wouldn't be moved without more than Pemberton had brought to the fight, and the argument ran down to nothing with the bell four minutes off. Pemberton stepped back, breathing hard, and made himself think rather than plead, which had never once in his life been the wrong order to do those two things.\n\nWhatever he had left, proof, chit, stamp, whichever of them he'd actually managed to gather, it would have to be enough on its own, laid out plainly and quickly, because there was no more time to win Coyle over by force of argument.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🖋️", text: "Pemberton spread what he had across the Bindery counter under Coyle's watchful eye: the scorched carbon of Peony's true script, the runner's chit holding the boards, the uncompromised stamp still warm from his own pocket. Three things, each one proof of nothing much on its own, and together the whole shape of the truth.\n\n'Well,' said Coyle, looking at the three of them laid out in a row. 'Get on with it, then. Bell's not getting any further off.'",
      forged: "Pemberton worked fast and clean, faster than he had ever worked in his life: struck through the wrong total on Board Two, struck through the wrong total on Board Nine, wrote the true figures in his own careful hand, and brought the uncompromised stamp down on both corrections with the chit as his authority, one minute inside the borrowed twenty and four minutes ahead of the bell.\n\nCoyle checked every line twice, found nothing wrong with any of it, and reached for the wax pot with something that was almost, cautiously, respect. The Quick-Set Stamp went back into Pemberton's pocket warm, and stayed there.\n\nThe Quick-Set Stamp is his. (+1 Speed, kept forever.)",
      notforged: "Coyle looked at the gap on the counter where a component should have been, and did not soften. 'You've brought me two of three,' he said. 'I bind what's complete and correct, and this isn't, not yet. Bell's going, Pemberton. Come back with the whole set, or come back next season.'\n\nReplay the adventure to find the missing components.",
      grantItem: "gf_pemberton_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "🔔", text: "The bell went at six exactly, same as it had every evening for as long as anyone in the building could remember, indifferent to what had or hadn't been settled underneath it. Coyle sealed Board Nine and Board Two into the bound ledger with SLACK, P. and DOSS, C. carrying the marks that actually belonged to them, and locked the Bindery for the night without another word.\n\nPemberton walked home the long way, for once, past the tannery district where a girl he had never met and would probably never meet was, at that exact moment, entirely unaware that a thin, damp, anxious clerk had spent his whole afternoon being braver than he felt on her behalf, over four digits nobody but him had thought worth the trouble.\n\nHe did not think of it as bravery. He thought of it, doing the sum honestly as he always did, as simply the job, done properly, for once, all the way through.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end",
      goodText: "By Monday, word had come back through the ordinary channels, which is to say slowly and without fuss: the Instrument-Makers' Guild had confirmed Peony Slack's apprenticeship on the strength of a Stratum III return, properly bound, properly true. Nobody at Tallow Street made a thing of it. Pemberton preferred it that way, and told himself so rather firmly, twice.\n\nCutter Doss, for his part, was quietly moved to the stratum his actual marks supported, and nobody explained to him exactly why, which Pemberton privately thought was its own small unkindness, if a gentler one than the alternative. He ate his lunch alone that day, as usual, and read four pages of the folded periodical in his ledger, and found, for the first time in a fortnight, that he wasn't checking the corridor quite so often.",
      midText: "The correction went through in the end, though it took a Petition of Error lodged the following week to finish what the Bindery floor hadn't quite settled outright, and a longer, more anxious wait than anyone involved would have chosen. It came out true all the same: Peony Slack's marks were hers, properly, on the record, for good.\n\nPemberton spent the intervening week doing his ordinary work with his ordinary caution, said nothing to anyone about any of it, and found that the waiting suited him rather less well than the running had. When the Petition finally cleared, he allowed himself exactly one small, private moment of satisfaction, standing alone at the counter, before going back to the next row of figures. There was always a next row of figures.",
      grantItem: "gf_pemberton_item2" },
  },
},

gf_maude: {
  id: "gf_maude",
  title: "Five Parts Water",
  tagline: "The medicine came in short and the queue came in long, and someone has to make the numbers hold.",
  items: [
    { id: "gf_maude_item1", name: "The Steady Dropper", stat: 3, how: "found", desc: "+1 Science. Won with a sharp Science check early in the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_maude_item2", name: "The Ledger of Doses", stat: 2, how: "quest", desc: "+1 Logic. Awarded for completing the adventure." },
    { id: "gf_maude_item3", name: "The Apothecary's Beam", stat: 0, how: "forged", desc: "+1 Arithmetic. Forged from three components hidden along the way." },
  ],
  components: { brass_beam: "A True Brass Beam", grain_weight: "The Grain Counterweight", glass_pan: "A Steady Glass Pan" },
  start: "intro",
  scenes: {
    intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🩺", text: "Sister Maude had been running the infirmary on Sallow Row for eleven years, which was long enough to have opinions about weather, water and the general untrustworthiness of anything shipped in from the Rise, in that order.\n\nThe fog had come down twice in the night instead of the usual once, which meant the Turn was working harder than it should for early autumn, which meant the alleys had gone scalding and wet by turns for six hours straight. By morning half of Sallow Row was coughing the particular wet, rattling cough that Maude had a private name for and never used it in front of patients, because patients did not need to know their doctor thought of their illness as a joke about kettles.\n\nThe crate from the Rise apothecaries' guild arrived at eight, three hours late and, when she got the lid off with the flat of a bread knife, plainly wrong. Forty vials of prepared feverbane tincture had been ordered and paid for. What she found, packed in straw between two cracked flasks, was concentrate: raw, unmixed, dangerous at full strength and nowhere near enough of it.\n\nLittle Ivy Coles was already on the second cot from the door, six years old and breathing like a door with a bad hinge. Maude looked at her, looked at the crate, and did the sum in her head before she had properly finished being angry about it, because the sum mattered more than the anger and always would.", next: "dose_check" },
    dose_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🔍", stat: 3, dc: 12, text: "Before she mixed anything she checked the concentrate's proof mark against the guild's manifest, because a tincture at the wrong strength was worse than no tincture at all, and she had exactly one chance to get the first batch right with a ward full of people watching her hands.", success: "dose_found", fail: "dose_miss" },
    dose_found: { type: "story", emoji: "💧", grantItem: "gf_maude_item1", text: "The proof mark was faint, half-scraped by careless handling, but it was there, and it did not match the manifest's claim. The guild had sent last season's stronger stock, not this season's standard mix, a full third more potent than the label pretended.\n\nCaught early, that was nothing. Caught late, in a hurried dose for a six-year-old, that would have been a different sort of morning entirely.\n\nTucked in the straw where the true mark had been checked against the false one, someone before her had left a fine glass dropper, graduated in careful grains along its side. Its last owner, a dispensary clerk who had clearly cared about exactly this kind of mistake, had no more use for it. Maude did.\n\nItem found: The Steady Dropper.", next: "factor_fight" },
    dose_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "🫗", text: "The first test measure went in cloudier than it should have, and by the time Maude worked out why, a good ounce of concentrate had gone down the drain rather than into anyone's chest. Last season's stock, stronger than the label claimed, nearly caught her the way it would have caught someone less careful.\n\nShe wasted no time being cross with herself. There was no dropper in the straw worth keeping, and there was a ward full of coughing people who did not care whose fault the crate was.", next: "factor_fight" },
    factor_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "🧾", qLevel: 3,
      text: "Factor Grieve came at ten, docket in hand, to collect Maude's signature on delivery and the balance of the account. He was a grey, tired man who had clearly signed a great many dockets in a great many wards that did not have enough of anything, and he did not enjoy this part of his work so much as endure it, the way a lot of the Rise's business got done in the Under: politely, and at the poor end's expense.\n\n'Forty vials, prepared, as invoiced,' he said, tapping the docket. 'Sign here and we're square.'\n\n'There are two flasks of raw concentrate in that crate and a great deal of straw,' said Maude. 'We are not square. We are considerably short, and I'd like that written down before you leave, not after.'",
      enemy: { name: "Factor Grieve", emoji: "🧾", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Maude set the manifest weight against what the crate actually held and read the shortfall aloud, number by number, until Grieve's pencil stopped defending the invoice and started correcting it.",
        strikeText: "Grieve produced a second docket, older and messier, and for a moment its tangle of adjusted totals nearly buried the plain fact that the crate was short. 'These things average out over the season,' he said, and it very nearly sounded reasonable.",
        defeatText: "Grieve ran out of forms to produce. He looked at the crate, looked at Ivy Coles's cot, and wrote SHORT DELIVERY, CONFIRMED across the docket in a hand rather less steady than his first one." },
      victory: "factor_win", retreat: "factor_retreat" },
    factor_win: { type: "story", emoji: "📝", text: "He signed the correction without further argument, which was, in its small grey way, the most honest thing Factor Grieve had done all morning. It would not put a single extra vial in Maude's hands today, but it would mean a proper credit against the next crate, and next time was a thing worth planning for even when this time was already going wrong.\n\n'For what it's worth,' Grieve said, not quite meeting her eye, 'the guild board doesn't ask where the shortfalls land. Only that the numbers on their side come out even.'\n\n'They usually land here,' said Maude. 'You may tell them that too, if you like. I shan't hold my breath.'\n\nHe left. She turned back to the crate, and to the numbers that still needed to work whether the guild's did or not.", next: "crossroads" },
    factor_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚪", text: "Ivy coughed, wet and long, from the second cot, and Maude decided the docket could wait and Grieve's conscience could keep. She signed what he put in front of her without reading it twice, because arguing arithmetic with a company man while a child fought for breath ten feet away was an argument for a calmer day.\n\nGrieve took the signature and went, faster than was strictly dignified, plainly relieved not to have to defend the guild's numbers after all.\n\nNo credit, then, and no admission on paper. Only the crate, exactly as short as it had been an hour ago, and rather less time than there had been an hour ago to do anything clever about it.", next: "crossroads" },
    crossroads: { type: "choice", emoji: "🗺️", text: "By noon the list had grown to sixty-three names and the crate had not grown at all. Maude set down her pen and considered, with the flat unsentimental patience of a woman who had done this particular sum before and would do it again: there were three places in Sallow Row that might have what she was short of, and no time to try all three.", options: [
      { label: "Try the Drip-Row dispensary", next: "apoth_intro", preview: "Widow Prudhoe deals fair, if the working is exact. A hard ratio puzzle guards a true brass beam." },
      { label: "Try Coop's still on the cistern stair", next: "cistern_intro", preview: "A grey-market gamble on an unopened batch. A probability call, rolled for, guards the same beam." },
      { label: "Answer the queue outside the door", next: "fen_intro", preview: "A frightened father wants his daughter seen first. An argument, not a fight, guards the same beam." },
    ] },
    apoth_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🏪", text: "Drip-Row ran two streets over, a narrow dispensary kept by Widow Prudhoe, who salvaged what broke elsewhere and sold it on for less than the Rise ever would, provided you could prove you deserved the discount.\n\n'Everybody wants my cupboard today,' she said, not looking up from her ledger. 'I don't give it away for a sad story, Sister. Never have. Show me the sum's right and I'll show you the shelf.'", next: "apoth_puzzle" },
    apoth_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚗️", text: "Prudhoe set her slate on the counter and waited, unmoved, for the working.", question: "Two flasks of feverbane concentrate survived the crate intact, holding 80 ml each. The concentrate must be diluted at a ratio of 1 part tincture to 4 parts water before use. Each patient's dose is 20 ml of the diluted mixture. How many patients can be dosed from the concentrate that survived?", options: ["40", "8", "32", "48"], answer: 0,
      hint: "First work out how much concentrate actually survived. Then use the 1:4 ratio to find the total diluted volume (that's 5 parts in total for every 1 part of concentrate). Finally divide by the size of one dose.",
      solution: "Two flasks survived: 2 x 80 ml = 160 ml of concentrate. Diluted 1 part concentrate to 4 parts water is 5 parts in total, so 160 x 5 = 800 ml of usable mixture. At 20 ml per dose, 800 divided by 20 gives 40 patients.",
      good: "apoth_good", mid: "apoth_mid" },
    apoth_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "brass_beam", text: "Prudhoe checked the working twice against her own slate, found nothing to correct, and allowed herself something close to approval.\n\n'Forty, and not a drop wasted arguing about it,' she said. 'That's a fair sum and a fair trade.' From beneath the counter she brought out a true brass beam, salvaged whole from a scale some careless apprentice had dropped years back, and set it on the counter between them. 'Somebody'll need this more than my dust does.'\n\nComponent found: A True Brass Beam.", next: "converge" },
    apoth_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", grantComponent: "brass_beam", text: "The first pass came out wrong, twenty patients short of the true answer, and Prudhoe corrected it aloud without a shred of gentleness, tapping each step with a chalk-white nail until the sum sat right. 'There,' she said. 'Forty. That's the actual number, whatever you had before.'\n\nShe handed the true brass beam over anyway, muttering something about wasted breath being better spent than wasted argument. 'Get it right next time, Sister. I shan't always be in a generous mood.'\n\nComponent found: A True Brass Beam.", next: "converge" },
    cistern_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "🛢️", text: "Coop kept a still on the cistern stair and a reputation for stock that had fallen off the back of somewhere respectable. He dealt in flasks nobody official would admit existed, at prices that assumed you were desperate, because you generally were.\n\n'Got a fresh case in,' he said, thumbing a crate that had definitely not come through any guild docket. 'Carrier's manifest says one in six of these arrives cracked this time of year, roads being what they are. Open one blind and take your chances, or walk away with nothing. Your call, Sister.'", next: "cistern_dice" },
    cistern_dice: { type: "dice", emoji: "🎲", die: 6, target: 2, text: "Roll 2 or more on a six-sided die. Only a roll of exactly 1 means the flask Coop hands over is the cracked, wasted one.",
      predict: { question: "If 1 flask in every 6 in this batch typically arrives cracked, what is the probability that the next flask opened is safe to use?", options: ["1/6", "5/6", "1/3", "2/3"], answer: 1, why: "1 in 6 cracked means 5 in 6 arrive safe. Knowing the odds are firmly in your favour before you open it steadies the hand that opens it: +1 to your roll." },
      success: "cistern_good", fail: "cistern_fail" },
    cistern_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "brass_beam", text: "The seal held. The flask inside was whole, and better yet, wedged in the same crate under a layer of old rag, Coop turned up a brass beam off some scale he claimed to have 'come by honestly', a claim Maude did not examine too closely, because a clinic that asked too many questions about the Ashlight's supply lines tended not to stay a clinic for long.\n\n'Call it a courtesy,' Coop said, which in his dialect meant it wasn't, quite, but nobody was going to say so.\n\nComponent found: A True Brass Beam.", next: "converge" },
    cistern_fail: { type: "story", emoji: "🌊", text: "The flask came out cracked at the neck, its contents already sour on the straw, exactly as one in six of them apparently did. Coop shrugged, entirely unbothered by another man's bad luck. 'Some days the odds just don't run your way. No refunds on chance, Sister, you know that.'\n\nShe left with nothing to show for the visit but a wasted hour and a slightly firmer opinion of Coop's business practices.", next: "converge" },
    fen_intro: { type: "story", emoji: "😨", text: "Warrick Coles was still at the door when Maude came back, exactly where she'd left him, wearing the particular grey look of a man doing sums about his own daughter and not liking any of the answers.\n\n'She's fifty-third on that list,' he said, before Maude had got her coat off. 'Fifty-third, and you've got medicine for forty if you're lucky. I've watched you work the numbers on other people's children all morning and I want to know where mine lands in them, and I want her moved up while there's still time to matter.'", next: "fen_fight" },
    fen_fight: { type: "combat", emoji: "😨", qLevel: 3,
      text: "It was not a fight, not really, though it had every shape of one: a man frightened past politeness, and a woman who had exactly one fair way to run a ward and intended to keep running it that way even now.\n\n'I'm not moving her because you shouted loudest,' Maude said. 'I'll move her if the numbers say she needs moving, same as anybody. Sit down and let me show you the list, or stand there and waste both our time.'",
      enemy: { name: "Warrick Coles", emoji: "😨", hits: 2, toHit: 8,
        hitText: "Maude laid the true numbers out flat, Ivy's fever chart against the other fifty-two, and Coles's anger had nowhere left to stand once the figures were in front of him.",
        strikeText: "'You don't know what it's like,' he said, and for one bad moment the whole queue behind him took up the same fear at once, loud enough that Maude nearly lost the thread of her own argument.",
        defeatText: "Coles ran out of things to be angry at that weren't the fog, the Turn or the guild three streets up, none of which were in the room to take it. He sat down hard on the step, all at once looking a great deal more tired than dangerous." },
      victory: "fen_win", retreat: "fen_retreat" },
    fen_win: { type: "story", emoji: "🧰", grantComponent: "brass_beam", text: "'She's eleventh,' Maude told him at last, turning the ledger round so he could read the working himself. 'Not first. Eleventh, by fever and breath alone, same as I'd rank anyone's child. You'll have her seen within the hour, and you'll have it because the numbers put her there, not because you shouted, and I'd thank you to remember the difference next time you're frightened.'\n\nSomething in him gave way, quietly, the way frightened men do once the fright has somewhere useful to go. He dug in his coat and pressed a brass fitting into her hand, a beam off his mother's own dispensary scales, kept against harder days and rent he couldn't cover.\n\n'Take it,' he said, gruff. 'You'll need it more than I need what it'd fetch. Just get her seen.'\n\nComponent found: A True Brass Beam.", next: "converge" },
    fen_retreat: { type: "story", emoji: "🚶", text: "He would not sit, would not hear the ledger, would not be talked down today, and Maude, who had buried enough patience arguing with frightened parents to know when arguing further only burned daylight, let him vent himself out on the step instead of on the numbers.\n\nHe left eventually, no calmer and no richer for the exchange, and Ivy stayed exactly where the fever chart put her, eleventh, whether her father had heard the number or not.\n\nMaude made a private note to check on the Coles household again before nightfall, whatever the ledger said. Some sums you finish later, off the page.", next: "converge" },
    converge: { type: "story", emoji: "📒", text: "By the time Maude got back to the cots, the list had crept to sixty-three and showed no sign of stopping, and the mixture she had, true brass beam or none, still would not stretch to cover everyone on it by simple wishing.\n\nShe sat down with the ledger, the concentrate and a very short pencil, and did the fairest thing she knew how to do with numbers that would not be kind: she pooled the lot, and started dividing it evenly, because a smaller fair share beat a larger unfair one every single time she had tried the alternative.", next: "share_puzzle" },
    share_puzzle: { type: "puzzle", emoji: "⚖️", text: "The ward monitor, old Hollis, folded his arms and waited to be shown the working before he'd unlock anything.", question: "Maude has 45 vials of prepared tincture and 63 patients now on the ward list. She decides to pool every vial and share the total equally among all 63 patients rather than give some a full vial and others none. What fraction of a full vial does each patient receive, written in its simplest form?", options: ["5/7", "7/5", "3/4", "2/3"], answer: 0,
      hint: "Write it as a fraction: vials over patients. Then find the highest common factor of 45 and 63 and divide both numbers by it.",
      solution: "45 vials shared among 63 patients gives each patient 45/63 of a vial. The highest common factor of 45 and 63 is 9: 45 divided by 9 is 5, and 63 divided by 9 is 7. So each patient receives 5/7 of a vial, and the fraction cannot be simplified any further.",
      good: "share_good", mid: "share_mid" },
    share_good: { type: "story", emoji: "🧮", grantComponent: "grain_weight", text: "The ward monitor, a stooped old Rustbound clerk called Hollis who checked her arithmetic the way some men check a bridge before they'll walk across it, went over the fraction twice and found nothing to fault.\n\n'Five in seven,' he said. 'That's proper sharing, that. Not the sort of sum most Rise doctors bother learning.' He unlocked a drawer and set out a small set of true grain-weights he'd been keeping back 'for emergencies', and allowed that this, finally, counted as one.\n\nComponent found: The Grain Counterweight.", next: "bottle_check" },
    share_mid: { type: "story", emoji: "📉", text: "Her instinct for fairness was sound; her first pass at the fraction was not, and Hollis corrected it without much patience, tapping the highest common factor out with one bony finger until five-sevenths sat right on the page.\n\n'Fair's no good to anyone if the sum's wrong,' he said, and kept his grain-weights locked away this round, not from meanness, only from a clerk's plain refusal to reward a mistake twice in one afternoon.", next: "bottle_check" },
    bottle_check: { type: "skillcheck", emoji: "🧪", stat: 2, dc: 13, text: "Two bottles stood together on the back shelf, near enough identical to have been mixed up by anyone in a hurry: one true concentrate, one a dead flask of something else entirely, mislabelled years ago by a less careful hand than hers. Only one of them belonged anywhere near a patient, and there was no time to test either the slow way.", success: "bottle_found", fail: "bottle_miss" },
    bottle_found: { type: "story", emoji: "🥃", grantComponent: "glass_pan", text: "The wax seals told the story, once she looked properly: one a shade paler than the manifest described, the dust undisturbed around it for months, while the other's seal matched exactly and had clearly been handled inside the week. Simple elimination, nothing mystical about it, the true bottle was the one that had actually been used.\n\nClearing the dead flask off the shelf to make room, she found behind it a small steady glass dish, precisely the shape a proper beam scale wanted for weighing out grains. Somebody had shelved it and forgotten it years ago, the way useful things get forgotten right up until the day they're needed.\n\nComponent found: A Steady Glass Pan.", next: "forge" },
    bottle_miss: { type: "story", emoji: "👃", text: "In the end she went by smell and the shape of the cork, which happened to be right, and told herself firmly that happening to be right was not the same as knowing, and that she would not be trying that particular shortcut again on a day with less luck in it.\n\nNo dish turned up behind the wrong bottle this time. Some shelves keep their secrets whether you guess correctly or not.", next: "forge" },
    forge: { type: "forge", emoji: "🔥", text: "Old Bramwell's workshop sat two doors down, cluttered with the innards of machines nobody else in Sallow Row could still name, and Bramwell himself grinning at the sight of a true brass beam the way other men grinned at good news.\n\n'A scale, is it,' he said, already clearing bench space with one forearm. 'Bring me the makings and I'll true it up proper. Grains don't lie, Sister, not once the beam's honest. It's people that lie. Machines just get built wrong.'",
      forged: "He set the true brass beam across its fulcrum, hung the steady glass pan from one arm and weighed it dead level against the grain counterweight on the other, adjusting by shavings of brass no thicker than a hair until the beam held perfectly still on its own. 'There,' he said, satisfied. 'Honest to the grain. Weigh anything on that and you'll know exactly what you're giving, which is rather the whole point of the exercise, when you think about it.'\n\nMaude took it in both hands. It felt, for the first time all day, like a number she could finally trust.",
      notforged: "He worked with what she'd brought him, which was not quite everything the job wanted, and said so plainly rather than pretend otherwise. 'It'll weigh true enough for government work,' he said, tapping the beam so it settled a fraction off level and stayed there, 'but I'll not tell you it's honest to the last grain, because it isn't, and you'd find out the hard way at the worst possible moment. Mind the difference and you'll be fine.'\n\nMaude took it anyway. An imperfect scale beat no scale at all, and she had never once in eleven years had the luxury of only using perfect tools.",
      grantItem: "gf_maude_item3", next: "finale" },
    finale: { type: "story", emoji: "🕯️", text: "By evening the ward had its rhythm back: dose measured, dose given, name crossed off the list in a hand too tired to be neat about it. Ivy Coles took her feverbane at the correct strength, slept through till morning without the rattle, and woke asking, with the particular outrage of the very young, why nobody had brought her anything to eat yet.\n\nWarrick Coles came back at dusk, sober-faced, and did not apologise, because Maude did not deal in apologies and had made that plain to him some years before this morning ever happened. He simply sat by his daughter's cot until the shift changed, which was, from him, the same thing said differently.\n\nOn the empty crate by the door, half-hidden under packing straw nobody had bothered to clear, was a small chalked mark Maude recognised and did not mention to anyone: the sort of mark that meant a certain someone's money had quietly covered the guild's shortfall invoice, the same as it covered a great deal else in Sallow Row that everybody knew about and nobody said aloud. She scrubbed it off the crate before the next delivery boy could see it and think about it too hard, and went back to counting vials, because the sums still needed doing whoever, in the end, had paid for them.", next: "end" },
    end: { type: "end",
      goodText: "By the week's turn the Kettle Cough had run its course through Sallow Row without a single name added to the churchyard's own ledger, which was not nothing, in a winter like this one. Bramwell's true beam proved so exact that the parish surveyor came by twice to borrow it for his own work, and Maude let him, on the understanding it came straight back. She banked one whole vial's worth of concentrate against the next crate's certain shortfall, because there would be a next crate, and there would be a next shortfall, and a woman who had done this sum enough times stopped being surprised by either.",
      midText: "Most of Sallow Row pulled through, which mattered more than the arithmetic of it being tidy. A handful of the later names on the list spent two uncomfortable, feverish nights waiting their turn, and Maude did not pretend that was the same as everyone being fine, only that everyone being fine had never actually been on offer, not with a crate that short. She kept her promises to every name on the list, in order, and considered that, on balance, the better use of a short week than pretending the numbers had come out kinder than they had.",
      grantItem: "gf_maude_item2" },
  },
},

};
const INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS = {};
INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.surdsAndIndices = {
  title: "Surds & Indices: exact answers, no rounding",
  minutes: 18,
  order: 3,
  prereq: ["algebraicManipulation"],
  intro: "A surd is a square root (or other root) that doesn't simplify to a whole number — √2, √7, √50. Rather than rounding it to a decimal and losing accuracy, this lesson shows you how to simplify, combine and rationalise surds so the answer stays exact, plus the index laws that let you handle fractional and negative powers the same clean way. Go slowly through the 'why' sections — once you've SEEN why a rule is true, you stop needing to memorise it.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Simplifying a surd", body: [
      "Start with something you can check by hand. Is √4 × √9 the same as √(4×9)? Work out each side on its own.",
      "First deal with √4 × √9:\n√4 = 2 and √9 = 3, so\n√4 × √9 =\n2 × 3 =\n6.",
      "Then look at √(4×9):\n4×9 =\n36, so\n√(4×9) =\n√36 =\n6.",
      "Both sides give 6 — they match. That wasn't luck.",
      "The same thing is true for ANY two numbers under the roots, not just 4 and 9 — here's the proof. Let a and b stand for any two numbers that are zero or positive (square roots of negative numbers aren't needed at GCSE level).",
      "Squaring undoes a square root. Square the left side:\n(√a × √b)² =\n(√a)² × (√b)² =\na × b.",
      "Now square the right side:\n(√(a×b))² =\na × b (by definition of squaring a root).",
      "Both squarings give exactly a × b. Since a and b are positive, there's only one positive number that squares to give any particular result, so the two original (unsquared) quantities must have been equal all along. That's the whole proof of the rule √a × √b = √(a×b), and it's the entire engine behind everything in this section.",
      "Now use that rule backwards. Instead of starting with two roots and multiplying them, start with ONE ugly root and split the number underneath into two friendlier factors.",
      "√50 isn't a whole number, but:\n50 =\n25 × 2, so\n√50 =\n√(25×2) =\n√25 × √2\n(using the rule above).",
      "√25 IS a whole number (5), so it walks out from under the root sign, leaving 5√2.",
      "The whole skill is choosing the right split. Any factor pair of 50 lets you use the rule, but only a perfect-square factor (a number that is itself something squared: 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49...) lets a whole number actually escape.",
      "Split 50 as 10×5 instead:\n√50 =\n√(10×5) =\n√10 × √5\n— true, but useless, since neither 10 nor 5 is a perfect square, so nothing comes out from under either root.",
      "Always hunt for the LARGEST perfect square that divides the number, so the job is done in one go.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Simplify √72.", steps: [
        "List perfect squares to test against, largest first, so you only need to check a couple: 64, 49, 36, 25, 16, 9, 4.",
        "Does 64 divide 72 exactly? 72 ÷ 64 is not a whole number, so no.",
        "Does 36 divide 72 exactly? 72 ÷ 36 = 2 — yes. 36 is a perfect square (6×6=36), so this is the split to use.",
        "Rewrite the number under the root using that factor:\n72 =\n36 × 2, so\n√72 =\n√(36 × 2).",
        "Apply the splitting rule:\n√(36 × 2) =\n√36 × √2.",
        "√36 is a whole number (6), so it comes out from under the root:\n√72 =\n6√2.",
      ], answer: "6√2" },
      { q: "Simplify √180 (a bigger number, to check the method still works).", steps: [
        "Test the larger perfect squares first: is 180 divisible by 100? No. By 81? No (180 ÷ 81 isn't whole). By 36? 180 ÷ 36 = 5 — yes.",
        "So:\n180 =\n36 × 5, giving\n√180 =\n√(36 × 5) =\n√36 × √5\n(splitting rule again).",
        "√36 = 6, so √180 = 6√5.",
        "Final check: does 5 itself hide another perfect-square factor? No — 5 is prime, so it can't be split any further. 6√5 is fully simplified.",
      ], answer: "6√5" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Simplify √48.", answer: "4√3. The largest perfect-square factor of 48 is 16 (check: 48 ÷ 16 = 3), so √48 = √16 × √3 = 4√3. Since 3 is prime, it can't be split any further." } },
    { h: "2. Rationalising the denominator", body: [
      "Here's a pattern worth checking by hand first: what is √5 × √5? Using the splitting rule from Section 1 in reverse:\n√5 × √5 =\n√(5×5) =\n√25 =\n5\n— a whole number, no root left at all.",
      "Try it again with a different number to make sure it's not a one-off:\n√11 × √11 =\n√(11×11) =\n√121 =\n11.",
      "Multiplying any surd by an exact copy of itself always clears the root completely. Here's why, in general: let n stand for whatever number sits under the root (5, 11, or anything else). Multiplying √n by itself is really just computing √(n²), and squaring then square-rooting a positive number gets you straight back to that same number, n.",
      "That single fact is the whole secret of 'rationalising'. A fraction like 1/√5 isn't mathematically wrong, but a root sitting on the bottom of a fraction is awkward to compare, add, or estimate by eye, so it's treated as untidy.",
      "To clean it up, multiply the WHOLE fraction by √5/√5. Why is that allowed? Because √5/√5 is 'something divided by itself', which always equals exactly 1, and multiplying any number by 1 never changes its value, only how it's written.",
      "Once you multiply through:\n1/√5 =\n(1×√5) / (√5×√5) =\n(1×√5) / 5\n(since √5×√5 = 5, a whole number, as shown above) =\n√5/5\n— the exact same value as before, just rearranged so the root sits harmlessly on top.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Rationalise 1/√5.", steps: [
        "Multiply the fraction by √5/√5 — allowed because √5/√5 equals 1, so the value of the fraction doesn't change:\n(1/√5) × (√5/√5).",
        "Multiply the numerators:\n1 × √5 =\n√5.",
        "Multiply the denominators:\n√5 × √5 =\n5\n(a surd times an identical copy of itself always gives a whole number, as shown above).",
        "Write the new numerator over the new denominator:\n√5/5.",
      ], answer: "√5/5" },
      { q: "Rationalise 6/√8 (the denominator isn't in simplest form yet, so there's an extra step first).", steps: [
        "Simplify the surd on the bottom before doing anything else:\n√8 =\n√(4×2) =\n√4 × √2 =\n2√2\n(Section 1's method).",
        "Rewrite the fraction using that simplified root:\n6/√8 =\n6/(2√2).",
        "Simplify the whole-number part of the fraction first:\n6 ÷ 2 =\n3, leaving\n3/√2.",
        "Now rationalise as before, multiplying top and bottom by √2:\n(3×√2) / (√2×√2).",
        "The denominator becomes √2×√2 = 2. The numerator becomes 3√2.",
        "Final answer: 3√2/2.",
      ], answer: "3√2/2" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Rationalise 7/√7 and simplify fully.", answer: "√7. Multiplying top and bottom by √7 gives (7×√7)/7 = 7√7/7, and the 7s cancel exactly, leaving just √7." } },
    { h: "3. Fractional and negative indices", body: [
      "Let n stand for any positive number, the base of the power about to be examined. Where does n^(1/2) actually come from? It isn't a new rule to memorise, it falls straight out of the ordinary law for multiplying powers (add the indices when the base is the same):\nn^(1/2) × n^(1/2) =\nn^(1/2 + 1/2) =\nn^1 =\nn.",
      "So whatever number n^(1/2) actually is, squaring it gets you back to n. But 'the number that squares to give n' is exactly the definition of √n. So n^(1/2) simply means √n.",
      "The same logic stretches to any root:\nn^(1/3) × n^(1/3) × n^(1/3) =\nn^(1/3 + 1/3 + 1/3) =\nn^1 =\nn,",
      "so n^(1/3) is the number that CUBES to give n, the cube root. In general, letting q stand for any whole number, n^(1/q) means the qth root of n, because multiplying it by itself q times always lands back on n^1.",
      "What about a fraction like n^(p/q), with a numerator as well — here p just stands for another whole number, sitting on top of q? The denominator q says 'take this root'; the numerator p says 'then raise to this power', and the order doesn't actually matter, since:\n(n^(1/q))^p =\nn^(p/q) =\n(n^p)^(1/q)\n(indices multiply either way round).",
      "In practice, always take the root FIRST: it keeps the numbers small, and THEN raising that small result to a power is easy arithmetic instead of a huge one.",
      "Negative indices come from a different index law, but the same style of reasoning. Let k stand for any whole number, the power involved. Multiplying powers with a matching base adds the indices, so:\nn^k × n^(-k) =\nn^(k + (-k)) =\nn^0 =\n1\n(anything to the power 0 equals 1 — check it yourself: 5³×5⁻³ must equal 5⁰=1).",
      "If n^k multiplied by n^(-k) always gives exactly 1, then n^(-k) must be the reciprocal of n^k, i.e. n^(-k) = 1/n^k. The minus sign was never a mysterious symbol, it's simply 'take the reciprocal', and it drops straight out of the same index law used everywhere else.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Evaluate 27^(-2/3).", steps: [
        "Deal with the negative sign first, using n^(-k) = 1/n^k:\n27^(-2/3) =\n1 / (27^(2/3)).",
        "Now evaluate 27^(2/3) by taking the root first (the denominator, 3, means cube root):\n27^(1/3) = 3\n(since 3×3×3=27).",
        "Raise that result to the numerator's power:\n27^(2/3) =\n(27^(1/3))² =\n3² =\n9.",
        "Put this back under the reciprocal from step 1:\n27^(-2/3) =\n1/9.",
      ], answer: "1/9" },
      { q: "Evaluate (1/8)^(-2/3) (a fraction base, to check the rules combine correctly).", steps: [
        "Handle the negative index first — it flips the base upside down and removes the minus sign:\n(1/8)^(-2/3) =\n8^(2/3).",
        "Take the root first, using the denominator 3:\n8^(1/3) = 2\n(since 2×2×2=8).",
        "Raise that to the numerator's power:\n8^(2/3) =\n(8^(1/3))² =\n2² =\n4.",
      ], answer: "4" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Evaluate 16^(3/4).", answer: "8. Take the root first: 16^(1/4) = 2, since 2×2×2×2=16 (the fourth root). Then raise to the numerator's power: 2³ = 8." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.numberTheoryDivisibility = {
  title: "Number Theory: factors, multiples and remainders",
  minutes: 13,
  order: 1,
  prereq: [{ module: "primary", key: "factorsMultiplesPrimes" }],
  intro: "Underneath a lot of Kangaroo and GCSE questions sits the same toolkit: breaking a number into its prime building blocks, finding what two numbers have in common (HCF), what they both divide into (LCM), and what's left over after division (modular arithmetic). This lesson builds all three from the same idea, prime factorisation. Go slowly through the worked checks in each section: once you've seen a rule fail when done wrong, you understand why the correct version works.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Prime factorisation", body: [
      "Try building 12 two different ways. Start with:\n12 = 2 × 6, then split\n6 = 2 × 3, giving\n12 = 2 × 2 × 3.",
      "Now start differently:\n12 = 3 × 4, then split\n4 = 2 × 2, giving\n12 = 3 × 2 × 2. Same three primes both times (2, 2 and 3), just multiplied in a different order.",
      "That's not a coincidence for 12, it's true for every whole number greater than 1: however you break it into factors, you always end up with exactly the same list of primes. This is called the fundamental theorem of arithmetic, and it's the reason 'the prime factorisation of a number' is a single, meaningful thing to ask for, not one of several possible answers.",
      "To find it in practice, divide by the smallest prime that fits, write down what's left, and repeat with the smallest prime that fits that new number. Keep going until the number left over is itself prime.",
      "The most common slip is stopping too early, for example giving up on 45 because 2 doesn't divide it, without trying the next prime up. If a prime doesn't divide exactly, move on to the next one (3, then 5, then 7...) rather than assuming the job is finished.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Write 60 as a product of primes.", steps: [
        "Start with the smallest prime, 2. Does 2 divide 60 exactly? 60 ÷ 2 = 30, yes.",
        "So 60 = 2 × 30. Now factorise 30 the same way: does 2 divide 30? 30 ÷ 2 = 15, yes.",
        "So 30 = 2 × 15. Does 2 divide 15? No, 15 is odd, so move up to the next prime, 3. 15 ÷ 3 = 5, yes.",
        "So 15 = 3 × 5, and 5 is itself prime, so the process stops here.",
        "Collect every prime used:\n60 =\n2 × 2 × 3 × 5 =\n2² × 3 × 5.",
      ], answer: "2² × 3 × 5" },
      { q: "Write 180 as a product of primes (a bigger number, to check the method still works).", steps: [
        "Start with the smallest prime, 2. 180 ÷ 2 = 90, yes, so 180 = 2 × 90.",
        "Divide 90 by 2 again: 90 ÷ 2 = 45, yes, so 90 = 2 × 45.",
        "Try 2 on 45: 45 is odd, so 2 doesn't divide it. Move up to the next prime, 3. 45 ÷ 3 = 15, yes.",
        "Divide 15 by 3 again: 15 ÷ 3 = 5, yes, and 5 is prime, so stop.",
        "Collect every prime used:\n180 =\n2 × 2 × 3 × 3 × 5 =\n2² × 3² × 5.",
      ], answer: "2² × 3² × 5" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Write 84 as a product of primes.", answer: "2² × 3 × 7. Dividing by 2 twice gives:\n84 =\n2 × 2 × 21 =\n4 × 21, and 21 = 3 × 7, neither of which can be split further since 3 and 7 are both prime." } },
    { h: "2. HCF and LCM from prime factors", body: [
      "Before going any further, pin down exactly what the two names mean, using letters so the definition covers any pair of numbers, not just the ones about to be used. Let a and b be two whole numbers. When we write HCF(a, b), we mean the Highest Common Factor of a and b: the largest whole number that divides both a and b exactly, with nothing left over. When we write LCM(a, b), we mean the Lowest Common Multiple of a and b: the smallest whole number that both a and b divide into exactly, with nothing left over either. Both are easiest to find by writing each number as a product of primes, using the method from Section 1.",
      "Take 24 = 2³ × 3 and 36 = 2² × 3². The HCF has to be a number that divides BOTH 24 and 36 exactly. Try taking the higher power of 2 instead of the lower one, just to see what goes wrong: 2³ × 3 = 24. Does 24 divide 36 exactly? 36 ÷ 24 = 1.5, not a whole number, so 24 fails as a common factor of 36. The problem is that 36 only contains 2² worth of 2s (that's 4), not 2³ (8), so anything using a full 2³ is asking 36 for more 2s than it actually has.",
      "That's why the HCF always takes the LOWER power of each shared prime: it can never demand more copies of a prime than the number with fewer copies of it can supply. Using the lower powers here, 2² × 3¹ = 12, and 12 does divide both 24 (24 ÷ 12 = 2) and 36 (36 ÷ 12 = 3) exactly.",
      "The LCM works the opposite way round: it has to be a multiple of BOTH 24 and 36 (both numbers divide into it exactly). Try using the lower power of 3 instead of the higher one, again to see the failure: 2³ × 3¹ = 24. Is 24 a multiple of 36? No, 24 is smaller than 36, so 36 can't possibly divide into it. Taking too low a power of a prime leaves the candidate too small to be a genuine multiple of the number that needed the higher power.",
      "That's why the LCM always takes the HIGHER power of every prime that appears in either number:\n2³ × 3² =\n8 × 9 =\n72.",
      "Check it divides evenly: 72 ÷ 24 = 3 and 72 ÷ 36 = 2, both whole numbers, so 72 really is a common multiple, and it's the smallest one, because using anything less than the higher power leaves at least one of the original numbers unable to divide in.",
      "A handy shortcut: HCF × LCM always equals the product of the two original numbers (here 12 × 72 = 864, and 24 × 36 = 864 too), which is a good way to check an answer, though not a substitute for working out the prime factors properly. One common pitfall: if a prime appears in only ONE of the two numbers, it still counts for the LCM (you need enough of it to be a genuine multiple) but contributes nothing to the HCF, since the two numbers don't share it.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find the HCF and LCM of 12 and 18.", steps: [
        "Write both numbers as products of primes: 12 = 2² × 3, and 18 = 2 × 3².",
        "For the HCF, take the lower power of each prime that appears in both: the lower power of 2 is 2¹ (12 has 2² but 18 only has 2¹), and the lower power of 3 is 3¹ (18 has 3² but 12 only has 3¹).",
        "Multiply those lower powers together:\nHCF =\n2 × 3 =\n6.",
        "For the LCM, take the higher power of each prime that appears in either number: 2² (from 12) and 3² (from 18).",
        "Multiply those higher powers together:\nLCM =\n4 × 9 =\n36.",
        "Check:\nHCF × LCM =\n6 × 36 =\n216, and 12 × 18 = 216. They match.",
      ], answer: "HCF = 6, LCM = 36" },
      { q: "Find the HCF and LCM of 24 and 36.", steps: [
        "Write both numbers as products of primes: 24 = 2³ × 3, and 36 = 2² × 3².",
        "For the HCF, take the lower power of each shared prime: 2² (24 has 2³ but 36 only has 2²) and 3¹ (36 has 3² but 24 only has 3¹).",
        "Multiply those together:\nHCF =\n4 × 3 =\n12.",
        "For the LCM, take the higher power of each prime: 2³ (from 24) and 3² (from 36).",
        "Multiply those together:\nLCM =\n8 × 9 =\n72.",
        "Check:\nHCF × LCM =\n12 × 72 =\n864, and 24 × 36 = 864. They match.",
      ], answer: "HCF = 12, LCM = 72" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the HCF and LCM of 20 and 30.", answer: "HCF = 10, LCM = 60. Since 20 = 2² × 5 and 30 = 2 × 3 × 5, the HCF uses the lower powers of the shared primes (2¹ × 5¹ = 10), while the LCM uses the higher powers of every prime that appears in either number (2² × 3 × 5 = 60); the 3 only appears in 30, so it doesn't count for the HCF but does count for the LCM." } },
    { h: "3. Modular arithmetic (remainders)", body: [
      "'N mod m' means the remainder when N is divided by m. Here N and m are just two whole numbers: N is the number being divided, and m (called the modulus) is what it's divided by. Check this against a clock: it's 14:00, and the clock reads that as 2:00, not 14:00, because clocks reset every 12 hours.",
      "That's exactly 14 mod 12:\n14 =\n1 × 12 + 2, so\n14 mod 12 =\n2.",
      "A useful pattern is that remainders repeat in a cycle. Check the powers of 2 mod 5:\n2¹ mod 5 = 2\n2² mod 5 = 4\n2³ mod 5 = 8 mod 5 = 3\n2⁴ mod 5 = 16 mod 5 = 1.",
      "Try the next one:\n2⁵ mod 5 =\n32 mod 5 =\n2, the same remainder as 2¹. The cycle (2, 4, 3, 1) has just started repeating, with length 4.",
      "That cycle lets you jump straight to a huge power without working it out directly. To find 2 to some large power mod 5, divide the exponent by the cycle length (4) and use the remainder to find the matching spot in the cycle, since the pattern simply repeats from there.",
      "Be careful not to confuse 'N mod m' with just subtracting m once. If N is much bigger than m, you may need to subtract several multiples of m (or divide fully) before what's left is smaller than m and counts as the true remainder.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "What is 100 mod 7?", steps: [
        "Divide 100 by 7 and find the whole number part: 100 ÷ 7 = 14.28..., so 14 whole times.",
        "Multiply back to see how much that accounts for: 14 × 7 = 98.",
        "Subtract to find what's left over: 100 − 98 = 2.",
        "So 100 mod 7 = 2 (this is a genuine remainder since 2 is smaller than 7).",
      ], answer: "2" },
      { q: "What is 2¹⁰ mod 5, using the cycle pattern from above (a case where working out the power directly would be tedious)?", steps: [
        "First find the cycle of remainders for powers of 2 mod 5:\n2¹ mod 5 = 2\n2² mod 5 = 4\n2³ mod 5 = 8 mod 5 = 3\n2⁴ mod 5 = 16 mod 5 = 1.",
        "Check the next one to confirm the cycle really repeats:\n2⁵ mod 5 =\n32 mod 5 =\n2, the same as 2¹ mod 5, so the cycle length is 4 (2, 4, 3, 1, then repeat).",
        "To find 2¹⁰ mod 5 without working out 2¹⁰ directly, divide the exponent 10 by the cycle length 4: 10 = 2 × 4 + 2, so the remainder of that division is 2.",
        "A remainder of 2 means 2¹⁰ lands on the same spot in the cycle as 2², which is 4.",
        "Check directly: 2¹⁰ = 1024, and 1024 ÷ 5 = 204 remainder 4 (204 × 5 = 1020, 1024 − 1020 = 4). It matches.",
      ], answer: "4" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "What is 50 mod 6?", answer: "2. Since 8 × 6 = 48 and 50 − 48 = 2, which is smaller than 6, so it's the genuine remainder." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.algebraicManipulation = {
  title: "Algebraic Manipulation: expanding, factorising, simplifying",
  minutes: 15,
  order: 2,
  prereq: [{ module: "junior", key: "multiExpr" }],
  intro: "Almost every harder algebra question starts with tidying up an expression before you can actually solve anything. This lesson covers the three core moves, expanding brackets, factorising back the other way, and spotting the difference-of-two-squares pattern that shows up constantly. Each rule here is checked with real numbers first, so you can see exactly why it works rather than just accepting it.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Expanding double brackets", body: [
      "Start with something familiar: does 3 × (4+5) equal 3×4 + 3×5? Left side: 3 × 9 = 27. Right side: 12 + 15 = 27. They match, this is the distributive law, multiplying a number onto a bracket means multiplying it onto every term inside.",
      "Now check what happens with TWO brackets: does (2+3) × (4+5) equal 2×4 + 2×5 + 3×4 + 3×5? Left side: 5 × 9 = 45. Right side: 8 + 10 + 12 + 15 = 45. They match again, so with two brackets, every term in the first bracket has to multiply every term in the second, not just a matching pair.",
      "Expanding (ax+b)(cx+d) is exactly the same process, just with letters standing in for some of the numbers: x is the unknown, and a, b, c and d are just ordinary numbers, the coefficients and constants of each bracket (they could be positive, negative, or even fractions, the method doesn't change). FOIL (First, Outer, Inner, Last) is simply a memory aid for the order to do those four multiplications in, so none get missed.",
      "The most common mistake is a lost sign when a bracket contains a minus. Each sign travels together with the term it belongs to, so 3 × (x−4) means 3 × x and 3 × (−4), not 3 × x and 3 × 4.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Expand (x+2)(x+3).", steps: [
        "Multiply the First terms: x × x = x².",
        "Multiply the Outer terms: x × 3 = 3x.",
        "Multiply the Inner terms: 2 × x = 2x.",
        "Multiply the Last terms: 2 × 3 = 6.",
        "Add all four results together: x² + 3x + 2x + 6.",
        "Collect the like terms (3x and 2x are both 'x' terms): x² + 5x + 6.",
      ], answer: "x² + 5x + 6" },
      { q: "Expand (2x+3)(x-4) (this time one bracket has a minus in it).", steps: [
        "Multiply the First terms: 2x × x = 2x².",
        "Multiply the Outer terms: 2x × (−4) = −8x. The minus sign travels with the 4, since the term is really +(−4).",
        "Multiply the Inner terms: 3 × x = 3x.",
        "Multiply the Last terms: 3 × (−4) = −12.",
        "Add all four results together: 2x² − 8x + 3x − 12.",
        "Collect the like terms:\n−8x + 3x =\n−5x, giving\n2x² − 5x − 12.",
      ], answer: "2x² - 5x - 12" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Expand (3x-1)(x+5).", answer: "3x² + 14x - 5. First: 3x × x = 3x². Outer: 3x × 5 = 15x. Inner: −1 × x = −x. Last: −1 × 5 = −5. Adding: 3x² + 15x − x − 5 = 3x² + 14x − 5." } },
    { h: "2. Factorising a quadratic", body: [
      "Check what happens when you expand (x+3)(x+4) using the method from Section 1:\nFirst x², Outer 4x, Inner 3x, Last 12, giving\nx² + 7x + 12.",
      "Notice that the middle coefficient (7) is the SUM of 3 and 4, and the constant (12) is their PRODUCT.",
      "That pattern always holds, not just for 3 and 4. Let p and q stand for any two numbers, whatever numbers happen to end up going into the brackets. Expanding (x+p)(x+q) in general, by the same First-Outer-Inner-Last method as above, gives x² + (p+q)x + pq: the middle coefficient is always p+q, and the constant is always p×q. So whenever a quadratic is written as x²+bx+c, where b just stands for whatever number sits in front of x and c stands for the constant term on its own, you're really looking for exactly this pair in reverse: two numbers that multiply to give c and add to give b.",
      "In practice, list the factor pairs of c and check which pair adds to b, keeping an eye on signs: if c is positive and b is positive, both numbers are positive; if c is positive and b is negative, both are negative; if c is negative, the two numbers must have opposite signs (since a positive times a negative gives a negative product).",
      "A common slip is picking a factor pair that multiplies correctly but forgetting to check the sum, or the reverse. Always check BOTH conditions (the product and the sum) before writing down the brackets.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Factorise x² + 7x + 12.", steps: [
        "You need two numbers that multiply to give 12 (the constant term) and add to give 7 (the coefficient of x).",
        "List the factor pairs of 12: 1 and 12, 2 and 6, 3 and 4.",
        "Check which pair adds to 7: 1+12=13 (no), 2+6=8 (no), 3+4=7 (yes).",
        "So the two numbers are 3 and 4, and they slot straight into the brackets: (x+3)(x+4).",
        "Check by expanding back:\n(x+3)(x+4) =\nx²+4x+3x+12 =\nx²+7x+12. It matches.",
      ], answer: "(x+3)(x+4)" },
      { q: "Factorise x² - x - 20 (trickier, since the constant is negative).", steps: [
        "You need two numbers that multiply to give −20 and add to give −1 (the coefficient of x, since x²−x means the coefficient is −1).",
        "Since the product is negative, the two numbers must have opposite signs.",
        "List factor pairs of 20 to try with opposite signs: 1 and 20, 2 and 10, 4 and 5.",
        "Try 4 and 5 with opposite signs: −5 and 4 multiply to −20, and add to −1. That matches both targets.",
        "So the two numbers are −5 and 4, giving (x−5)(x+4).",
        "Check by expanding back:\n(x−5)(x+4) =\nx²+4x−5x−20 =\nx²−x−20. It matches.",
      ], answer: "(x-5)(x+4)" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Factorise x² - 3x - 10.", answer: "(x-5)(x+2). You need two numbers multiplying to -10 and adding to -3: -5 and 2 work, since -5×2=-10 and -5+2=-3." } },
    { h: "3. Difference of two squares", body: [
      "Let a and b stand for any two numbers or expressions at all here, they could be plain numbers, a letter like x, or something like 3x, it won't change the pattern about to appear.",
      "Expand (a−b)(a+b) using the same method as Section 1:\nFirst a×a=a², Outer a×b=ab, Inner −b×a=−ab, Last −b×b=−b².",
      "Adding them:\na² + ab − ab − b², and\nthe ab and −ab are opposites, so they cancel exactly, leaving\na² − b². No middle term survives.",
      "Check it with actual numbers: let a=5, b=2.",
      "Left side:\n(5−2)(5+2) =\n3×7 =\n21.",
      "Right side:\na²−b² =\n25−4 =\n21.",
      "They match.",
      "Because (a−b)(a+b) always simplifies to a²−b², the process reverses cleanly too: whenever you SEE an expression of the form 'a perfect square minus a perfect square', you can write down the two brackets immediately, with no searching for numbers the way Section 2 required, since there's no middle term to match.",
      "To spot it, recognise perfect squares: numbers like 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49..., and with algebra, terms like x² or (3x)²=9x². The one condition that matters is that it must be a DIFFERENCE (a minus sign) between the two squares, a SUM like x²+49 does not factorise this way at all.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Factorise x² - 49.", steps: [
        "Check whether both terms are perfect squares: x² is a perfect square (it's x×x), and 49 = 7×7, so 49 is a perfect square too.",
        "Since it's a DIFFERENCE of two squares (x² minus 49, not a sum), the a²−b² rule applies directly, with a=x and b=7.",
        "Write it straight into the two brackets: x² − 49 = (x−7)(x+7).",
        "Check by expanding back:\n(x−7)(x+7) =\nx²+7x−7x−49 =\nx²−49. The middle terms cancel exactly as shown above, and it matches.",
      ], answer: "(x-7)(x+7)" },
      { q: "Factorise 9x² - 25 (this time a number multiplies the x² term).", steps: [
        "Check whether both terms are perfect squares: 9x² = (3x)² (since 3x×3x=9x²), and 25 = 5×5, so both are perfect squares.",
        "This is a difference of two squares with a=3x and b=5.",
        "Write it into the brackets: 9x² − 25 = (3x−5)(3x+5).",
        "Check by expanding back:\n(3x−5)(3x+5) =\n9x²+15x−15x−25 =\n9x²−25. The middle terms cancel again, and it matches.",
      ], answer: "(3x-5)(3x+5)" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Factorise 4x² - 81.", answer: "(2x-9)(2x+9). Since 4x²=(2x)² and 81=9², this is a²-b² with a=2x and b=9." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.simultaneousEquations = {
  title: "Simultaneous Equations: two unknowns, two clues",
  minutes: 14,
  order: 5,
  prereq: ["algebraicManipulation", { module: "junior", key: "systemWord" }, { module: "primary", key: "twoUnknowns" }],
  intro: "Whenever a problem gives you two separate facts about two unknown quantities, you need simultaneous equations, a pair of equations solved together, not one at a time. This lesson covers elimination (the fastest method for linear pairs) and substitution (essential once a quadratic gets involved). Both methods rely on a single idea worth checking carefully first: that you're always allowed to combine two true equations together.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Elimination", body: [
      "Suppose x+y=10 and x−y=4 are both true for the same x and y. Check with x=7, y=3: 7+3=10 and 7−3=4, both check out. Because the left side of each equation equals its own right side, adding the two left sides together must give exactly the same total as adding the two right sides together: (x+y)+(x−y) must equal 10+4. That's just 'equal amounts add to equal amounts', nothing extra is smuggled in.",
      "Working out the left side confirms it:\n(x+y)+(x−y) =\nx+y+x−y =\n2x, since the y and −y cancel.",
      "So:\n2x =\n14, giving\nx = 7, which matches the x we already knew. The method works especially well here because the y-coefficients are opposite (+1 and −1), so adding the equations removes y entirely, leaving one equation in one unknown. This whole technique is called elimination, because the step of adding (or subtracting) the two equations makes one of the letters disappear completely — it has been eliminated — leaving something simple enough to solve directly.",
      "Sometimes the letter you want to remove already has coefficients that are equal rather than opposite (like the 3x in both equations of the example below), in which case SUBTRACTING one whole equation from the other removes it instead. Other times a coefficient needs to be forced to match first, by multiplying one whole equation by a number, which is allowed for exactly the same reason: multiplying both sides of an equation by the same number keeps them equal.",
      "The easiest mistake is applying an operation to only PART of an equation, for example doubling the left side but forgetting the right. Always treat the whole equation, both sides together, as one package when adding, subtracting or scaling.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Solve x + y = 10 and x - y = 2.", steps: [
        "Both equations already have matching coefficients of x (just 'x' in each), so adding them will eliminate y, since +y and −y are opposites.",
        "Add the left sides together, and separately add the right sides together: (x+y)+(x−y) = 10+2.",
        "Simplify the left side:\nx+y+x−y =\n2x (the y and −y cancel), so\n2x = 12.",
        "Divide both sides by 2: x = 6.",
        "Substitute x=6 back into the first equation (that is, replace x with the value 6 wherever it appears):\n6+y = 10, so\ny = 4.",
        "Check in the second equation: 6−4=2. It matches, so the solution is correct.",
      ], answer: "x = 6, y = 4" },
      { q: "Solve 3x + 2y = 18 and 3x - y = 9.", steps: [
        "Both equations have exactly 3x, so subtracting one equation from the other will eliminate x entirely.",
        "Subtract the second equation from the first, doing it to both sides: (3x+2y) − (3x−y) = 18 − 9.",
        "Simplify the left side carefully, remembering to subtract the WHOLE second bracket:\n3x+2y−3x+y =\n3y, so\n3y = 9.",
        "Divide both sides by 3: y = 3.",
        "Substitute y=3 back into the second equation: 3x − 3 = 9.",
        "Add 3 to both sides then divide by 3:\n3x =\n12, so\nx = 4.",
        "Check in the first equation:\n3(4)+2(3) =\n12+6 =\n18. It matches.",
      ], answer: "x = 4, y = 3" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Solve x + 2y = 12 and x - 2y = 4.", answer: "x = 8, y = 2. Adding the equations gives:\n2x =\n16, so\nx = 8, then substituting into x+2y=12 gives:\n2y =\n4, so\ny = 2." } },
    { h: "2. Substitution", body: [
      "Suppose y=x+2 is true. If x=1, then y=3. Check that 3 really is 'x+2' when x=1: 1+2=3, yes. So wherever y appears in another true statement about the same x and y, replacing it with 'x+2' cannot change whether that statement is true, you're not changing the VALUE, only how it's written down.",
      "That's the whole idea behind substitution: take the equation that already isolates one letter (like y=x+2), and replace that letter with its expression everywhere it appears in the OTHER equation. That turns two equations in two unknowns into a single equation in one unknown, which can then be solved as usual.",
      "This is also the ONLY method that works once one equation is a curve, such as y=x², rather than a straight line. Elimination relies on matching or scaling coefficients across two equations that behave the same way at every point; a curve's 'coefficient' of x isn't constant in that sense, so there's nothing consistent to add or subtract away.",
      "Once a quadratic is involved, there will usually be TWO solutions for x, since factorising a quadratic gives two brackets. This uses factorising, the same technique from Algebraic Manipulation: finding two numbers that multiply to give the constant term and add to give the coefficient of x, exactly as in the worked example below. A common pitfall is solving for x and stopping, forgetting to go back and find the matching y-value for EACH x, and forgetting to check both pairs in the original equations.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Solve y = 2x and 3x + y = 15.", steps: [
        "The first equation already gives y on its own, so substitute '2x' in place of y in the second equation: 3x + (2x) = 15.",
        "Simplify the left side:\n3x+2x =\n5x, so\n5x = 15.",
        "Divide both sides by 5: x = 3.",
        "Use y=2x to find y:\ny =\n2×3 =\n6.",
        "Check in the second equation:\n3(3)+6 =\n9+6 =\n15. It matches.",
      ], answer: "x = 3, y = 6" },
      { q: "Solve y = x + 2 and x² + y = 8 (a quadratic this time, so expect two solutions).", steps: [
        "The first equation gives y on its own, so substitute 'x+2' in place of y in the second equation: x² + (x+2) = 8.",
        "Rearrange so everything is on one side:\nx² + x + 2 − 8 =\n0, which simplifies to\nx² + x − 6 = 0.",
        "Factorise: find two numbers multiplying to −6 and adding to 1, those are 3 and −2, so (x+3)(x−2) = 0.",
        "This gives two possible values: x = −3 or x = 2, since a quadratic can have two solutions and both must be followed through.",
        "Find y for x=−3, using y=x+2:\ny =\n−3+2 =\n−1.",
        "Find y for x=2, using y=x+2:\ny =\n2+2 =\n4.",
        "Check both pairs in x²+y=8:\n(−3)²+(−1) =\n9−1 =\n8, and\n(2)²+4 =\n4+4 =\n8. Both work.",
      ], answer: "(x,y) = (-3,-1) or (2,4)" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Solve y = 2x and y = x² - 3.", answer: "x=3,y=6 or x=-1,y=-2. Substituting gives:\nx²-2x-3 =\n0, which factorises to\n(x-3)(x+1) = 0, so x=3 or x=-1, and y=2x gives the matching y-values." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.quadratics = {
  title: "Quadratics: solving, completing the square, and the graph",
  minutes: 16,
  order: 6,
  prereq: ["algebraicManipulation", "surdsAndIndices"],
  intro: "A quadratic is any expression that can be written as ax² + bx + c, where a, b and c stand for fixed numbers: a is the coefficient of x² (the number multiplying x², which can never be 0, or there'd be no x² term left and it wouldn't be a quadratic any more), b is the coefficient of x, and c is the constant term - the number left on its own with no x attached at all. This lesson covers completing the square (which reveals the graph's turning point and turns out to be the hidden engine behind the quadratic formula), solving by factorising or the formula, and reading off what the discriminant tells you about how many solutions exist, all without needing to memorise any of it as an unexplained fact.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Completing the square", body: [
      "This section works with the simplest case, where a=1 - just x²+bx+c, with no separate number multiplying x². The case where a is something other than 1 comes back in Section 2, when the quadratic formula is introduced.",
      "Expand (x+3)² using the method from the algebra lesson:\nFirst x², Outer 3x, Inner 3x, Last 9, giving\nx² + 6x + 9.",
      "Notice that the 9 is exactly (half of 6)², since half of 6 is 3 and 3²=9.",
      "That's the trick used in reverse to 'complete the square'. Given x²+6x+5, first build the bracket that matches the x²+6x part: (x+3)² = x²+6x+9.",
      "But the target only wants +5, not +9, so subtract the 9 that came along for free and add back the 5 that's actually wanted:\nx²+6x+5 =\n(x+3)² − 9 + 5 =\n(x+3)² − 4.",
      "Check by expanding:\n(x+3)²−4 =\nx²+6x+9−4 =\nx²+6x+5, which matches.",
      "In general, for x²+bx+c, halve b, square that half, and use it the same way: x²+bx+c = (x+b/2)² + (c − (b/2)²).",
      "This form reveals the graph's turning point directly. A squared term like (x+3)² can never be negative, its smallest possible value is 0, and that happens exactly when x=−3. So for y=(x+3)²−4, the minimum value of y is −4, at x=−3, giving the turning point (−3,−4).",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Write x² + 6x + 5 in completed-square form.", steps: [
        "Take half of the coefficient of x: half of 6 is 3.",
        "Square that half: 3² = 9. This tells you (x+3)² expands to x²+6x+9 (First x², Outer 3x, Inner 3x, Last 9).",
        "Compare that to the expression actually wanted, x²+6x+5: same x²+6x part, but +5 instead of +9.",
        "Adjust for the difference: subtract the unwanted 9 and add the wanted 5: x²+6x+5 = (x+3)² − 9 + 5.",
        "Simplify the numbers:\n−9+5 =\n−4, giving\n(x+3)² − 4.",
        "Check by expanding back:\n(x+3)² − 4 =\nx²+6x+9−4 =\nx²+6x+5. It matches.",
      ], answer: "(x+3)² - 4" },
      { q: "Write x² - 8x + 10 in completed-square form, and use it to state the turning point of y = x² - 8x + 10.", steps: [
        "Take half of the coefficient of x: half of −8 is −4.",
        "Square that half: (−4)² = 16, so (x−4)² expands to x²−8x+16 (First x², Outer −4x, Inner −4x, Last 16).",
        "Compare to the target x²−8x+10: same x²−8x part, but +10 instead of +16, so subtract 16 and add 10: x²−8x+10 = (x−4)² − 16 + 10.",
        "Simplify:\n−16+10 =\n−6, giving\n(x−4)² − 6.",
        "Check by expanding:\n(x−4)² − 6 =\nx²−8x+16−6 =\nx²−8x+10. It matches.",
        "Read off the turning point: (x−4)² can never be negative, so its smallest value is 0, at x=4, where:\ny =\n0−6 =\n−6. The turning point is (4,−6).",
      ], answer: "(x-4)² - 6; turning point (4, -6)" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the turning point of y = x² + 10x + 7.", answer: "(-5, -18). Completing the square: half of 10 is 5, squared is 25, so:\nx²+10x+7 =\n(x+5)² - 25 + 7 =\n(x+5)² - 18, giving a minimum at x=-5, y=-18." } },
    { h: "2. Solving by factorising and the formula", body: [
      "If x²+bx+c factorises neatly - using the numbers-that-multiply-and-add method from the algebra lesson to find a pair of numbers p and q with p×q=c and p+q=b, so x²+bx+c=(x+p)(x+q) - that's the fastest way to solve x²+bx+c=0. Once it's written as (x+p)(x+q)=0, at least one bracket must equal zero, because the only way two numbers can multiply together to give exactly 0 is if one of them already IS 0. So x+p=0 or x+q=0, giving x=−p or x=−q.",
      "But not every quadratic factorises into nice whole numbers, which is where completing the square (Section 1) earns its keep. Take x²+6x+5 again: Section 1 rewrote it as (x+3)²−4.",
      "Setting the whole thing to 0:\n(x+3)²−4 =\n0, so\n(x+3)² = 4.",
      "Square-rooting both sides gives x+3=±2 (both +2 and −2 square to give 4, so both count as valid square roots - that's where the ± comes from), so x=−1 or x=−5.",
      "The same completing-the-square idea works even when a isn't 1 - for the fully general quadratic ax²+bx+c=0. Divide every term by a first, so the x² term has coefficient 1 again, matching Section 1's method exactly:\nx² + (b/a)x + c/a = 0.",
      "Complete the square exactly as in Section 1: halve the coefficient of x, which is now b/a, giving b/(2a), then square that half, giving b²/(4a²):\nx² + (b/a)x + c/a =\n(x + b/(2a))² − b²/(4a²) + c/a.",
      "Set the whole thing to 0 and move the plain-number terms to the other side:\n(x + b/(2a))² =\nb²/(4a²) − c/a.",
      "Combine the two fractions on the right over the same denominator, 4a² (multiplying c/a's top and bottom by 4a so the denominators match):\nb²/(4a²) − c/a =\nb²/(4a²) − 4ac/(4a²) =\n(b² − 4ac)/(4a²).",
      "Square-root both sides, exactly as with (x+3)²=4 above - this again gives two possibilities, the ±, for the same reason (a positive and a negative square root both square back to the same value). The square root of 4a² is 2a, since 4a² is already a perfect square, being (2a)²:\nx + b/(2a) = ±√(b² − 4ac) / (2a).",
      "Subtract b/(2a) from both sides. The two fractions on the right already share the denominator 2a, so they combine directly:\nx =\n−b/(2a) ± √(b² − 4ac)/(2a) =\n(−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / (2a).",
      "That's the quadratic formula, x=(−b±√(b²−4ac))/2a - and nothing about it was invented separately from Section 1. It's the exact same completing-the-square process, just carried out once symbolically on the letters a, b and c instead of being redone by hand for every new quadratic.",
      "Check it against x²+6x+5=0: matching ax²+bx+c against x²+6x+5 gives a=1 (there's no number written in front of x², which always means the coefficient is 1), b=6, c=5. The expression under the root works out as:\nb²−4ac =\n6²−4(1)(5) =\n36−20 =\n16, and √16=4, so\nx =\n(−6±4)/2, giving\nx=−1 or x=−5, exactly the same two answers found by squaring and rooting directly, above.",
      "This is also why the formula always works, even when factorising fails completely, for example when the solutions involve surds, since it's built from the same square-completing steps as Section 1.",
      "The most common formula slip is losing a negative sign when b is already negative. For x²−6x+5=0, b=−6, so −b becomes +6, not −6. Always substitute the whole value of b, sign included, before applying the extra minus sign in front of it.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Solve x² - 5x + 6 = 0.", steps: [
        "Look for two numbers that multiply to give 6 and add to give −5: −2 and −3 (since −2×−3=6 and −2+−3=−5).",
        "Write the factorised form: (x−2)(x−3) = 0.",
        "Since the two brackets multiply to give 0, at least one of them must itself be 0: either x−2=0 or x−3=0.",
        "Solve each: x−2=0 gives x=2; x−3=0 gives x=3.",
      ], answer: "x = 2 or x = 3" },
      { q: "Solve x² + 2x - 4 = 0 using the quadratic formula (this one doesn't factorise into whole numbers).", steps: [
        "Identify a, b and c by comparing to ax²+bx+c=0: a=1, b=2, c=−4.",
        "Work out the discriminant first, b²−4ac:\n2² − 4(1)(−4) =\n4 − (−16) =\n4+16 =\n20.",
        "Since the discriminant is positive, there are two real solutions (more on this in Section 3).",
        "Substitute into the formula x=(−b±√(b²−4ac))/2a: x = (−2 ± √20)/2.",
        "Simplify √20 by pulling out its largest perfect-square factor:\n√20 =\n√(4×5) =\n2√5, so\nx = (−2 ± 2√5)/2.",
        "Divide every term by the denominator, 2: x = −1 ± √5.",
      ], answer: "x = -1 + √5 or x = -1 - √5" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Solve x² - 2x - 5 = 0 using the formula (leave your answer in surd form).", answer: "x = 1 + √6 or x = 1 - √6. Discriminant =\n(-2)² - 4(1)(-5) =\n4+20 =\n24, so\nx =\n(2 ± √24)/2 =\n(2 ± 2√6)/2 =\n1 ± √6." } },
    { h: "3. The discriminant", body: [
      "The expression that appeared under the square root in Section 2's derivation, b²−4ac, has a name: the discriminant. Look back at Section 2's examples: x²+6x+5=0 had discriminant 16, a positive perfect square, giving two clean whole-number solutions. x²+2x−4=0 had discriminant 20, positive but not a perfect square, giving two surd solutions. Either way, a positive discriminant produced two real solutions.",
      "Now check the boundary case. x²−6x+9=0 factorises as:\n(x−3)(x−3) =\n(x−3)² =\n0, so the only solution is x=3, repeated.",
      "Its discriminant is:\nb²−4ac =\n(−6)²−4(1)(9) =\n36−36 =\n0.",
      "A discriminant of exactly 0 means the formula's ± adds or subtracts nothing, √0=0, so there's only ONE value of x rather than two, a repeated root.",
      "Now check what happens when the discriminant is negative, using x²+4x+7=0:\nb²−4ac =\n16−28 =\n−12.",
      "The formula would need √(−12), but no real number multiplied by itself gives a negative result, so there is no real value of x that solves the equation. The graph of y=x²+4x+7 never touches the x-axis at all.",
      "Knowing the discriminant's sign before solving anything is useful for checking work: a positive discriminant should produce two different answers, zero should produce one repeated answer, and negative should produce none at all, so an answer that doesn't match is a sign a mistake crept in somewhere.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "How many real solutions does x² - 6x + 9 = 0 have?", steps: [
        "Identify a, b, c: a=1, b=−6, c=9.",
        "Work out the discriminant:\nb²−4ac =\n(−6)² − 4(1)(9) =\n36 − 36 =\n0.",
        "A discriminant of exactly 0 means the formula's ± adds or subtracts nothing, so there's only ONE value of x, not two.",
        "Check by factorising instead:\nx²−6x+9 =\n(x−3)(x−3) =\n(x−3)², so the only solution is x=3, repeated.",
      ], answer: "1 real solution (a repeated root, x = 3)" },
      { q: "How many real solutions does x² + 4x + 7 = 0 have?", steps: [
        "Identify a, b, c: a=1, b=4, c=7.",
        "Work out the discriminant:\nb²−4ac =\n4² − 4(1)(7) =\n16 − 28 =\n−12.",
        "The discriminant is negative, which would require √(−12), but no real number squares to give a negative result, so no real value of x exists.",
      ], answer: "0 real solutions" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "How many real solutions does x² + 3x - 2 = 0 have?", answer: "2 real solutions. The discriminant is:\n3² - 4(1)(-2) =\n9+8 =\n17, which is positive, so there are two distinct real solutions (though they won't be whole numbers, since 17 isn't a perfect square)." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.percentageAndCompoundGrowth = {
  title: "Percentages & Compound Growth: multipliers, not just fractions",
  minutes: 16,
  order: 4,
  prereq: [],
  intro: "The single most useful percentage skill for GCSE and Kangaroo alike is thinking in MULTIPLIERS rather than 'find 10%, then add it on'. A 15% increase means multiply by 1.15. A 15% decrease means multiply by 0.85. This lesson doesn't just hand you that rule, it builds it from scratch so you can see why it works, then extends it to compound growth over several years and to working backwards from an after-value to a before-value.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. The multiplier method", body: [
      "Increase £80 by 15% the way you probably learned first: find 15% of £80, then add it on. 10% of £80 = £8, and 5% of £80 = £4, so\n15% of £80 =\n£8 + £4 =\n£12.\nAdding that on: £80 + £12 = £92.",
      "Now look closely at what that calculation actually did. £80 + £12 is the same as £80 × 1 + £80 × 0.15 (since £12 is exactly 0.15 × £80). Factor out the £80:\n£80 × 1 + £80 × 0.15 =\n£80 × (1 + 0.15) =\n£80 × 1.15.\nThe two-step 'find it, then add it' process and the one-step 'multiply by 1.15' process give the exact same £92, because they are the exact same arithmetic, just written differently.",
      "That works for any starting amount and any percentage, not just 15% of £80. Let r stand for that percentage (just the plain number, like 15, not '15%'). Increasing by r% means:\nnew =\nold + (r/100) × old =\nold × (1 + r/100).\nThe number (1 + r/100) is called the multiplier, and it packages the whole 'find the percentage, then add' process into a single multiplication.",
      "Decreasing works the same way, with a subtraction instead of an addition. Decrease £80 by 15%: £80 - £12 = £68. Written as a multiplication, that's:\n£80 × 1 - £80 × 0.15 =\n£80 × (1 - 0.15) =\n£80 × 0.85,\nand £80 × 0.85 = £68 too. So decreasing by r% means multiplying by (1 - r/100).",
      "The common slip is mixing up which way the multiplier moves: an increase always gives a multiplier ABOVE 1, a decrease always gives one BELOW 1. If a calculation produces a multiplier like 0.85 for something that should be growing, that's a sign of a sign error, not a valid answer.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Increase £80 by 15%.", steps: [
        "Turn the percentage into a decimal:\n15% =\n15/100 =\n0.15.",
        "Since this is an increase, add 1 to get the multiplier: 1 + 0.15 = 1.15.",
        "Multiply the original amount by the multiplier: £80 × 1.15.",
        "£80 × 1.15 = £92.",
      ], answer: "£92" },
      { q: "Decrease £340 by 8% (bigger, messier numbers to check the method still works).", steps: [
        "Turn the percentage into a decimal:\n8% =\n8/100 =\n0.08.",
        "Since this is a decrease, subtract from 1 to get the multiplier: 1 - 0.08 = 0.92.",
        "Multiply the original amount by the multiplier: £340 × 0.92.",
        "£340 × 0.92 = £312.80.",
      ], answer: "£312.80" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Decrease £120 by 30%.", answer: "£84, using the multiplier 1 - 0.30 = 0.70, since £120 × 0.70 = £84." } },
    { h: "2. Compound growth over several years", body: [
      "Suppose £500 sits in an account paying 4% interest a year. Simple interest would add the SAME flat amount every single year:\n4% of £500 =\n£20, so after 3 years you'd have\n£500 + £20 + £20 + £20 =\n£560.\nCompound interest is different: it pays 4% of the CURRENT balance each year, not the original £500, so the amount being added grows too.",
      "Working it out year by year with the multiplier from Section 1:\nafter year 1, £500 × 1.04 =\n£520.00.\nAfter year 2, that new balance grows again:\n£520.00 × 1.04 =\n£540.80.\nAfter year 3:\n£540.80 × 1.04 =\n£562.432, so £562.43 to the nearest penny.",
      "Multiplying by 1.04 three times in a row, one after another, is exactly the same as multiplying by 1.04³ in one go, since 1.04 × 1.04 × 1.04 = 1.04³.\nChecking:\n1.04³ =\n1.124864, and\n£500 × 1.124864 =\n£562.432,\nmatching the year-by-year answer exactly.",
      "That gives the general rule: letting n stand for however many years the growth continues, growing repeatedly by r% for n years means multiplying by (1 + r/100)ⁿ. Shrinking repeatedly (depreciation) works identically, using (1 - r/100)ⁿ instead.",
      "The classic mistake is confusing this with simple interest, i.e. multiplying the PERCENTAGE by the number of years (4% × 3 = 12%, then applying that once) instead of raising the MULTIPLIER to a power. Those give different answers (£560 vs £562.43 above) because they describe different situations, and only the power method matches genuine year-on-year compounding.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "£500 is invested at 4% compound interest for 3 years. What is it worth?", steps: [
        "Find the multiplier for one year: 1 + 4/100 = 1.04.",
        "Growing for 3 years means applying that multiplier 3 times in a row, i.e. raising it to the power 3: 1.04³.",
        "Work out 1.04³: 1.04 × 1.04 × 1.04 = 1.124864.",
        "Multiply by the original amount: £500 × 1.124864 = £562.432.",
        "Round to the nearest penny: £562.43.",
      ], answer: "£562.43" },
      { q: "A machine worth £8,000 depreciates by 12% per year. What is it worth after 4 years?", steps: [
        "Find the multiplier for one year: since this is a decrease, 1 - 12/100 = 0.88.",
        "Depreciating for 4 years means applying that multiplier 4 times: 0.88⁴.",
        "Work out 0.88² first: 0.88 × 0.88 = 0.7744.",
        "Square that result to get 0.88⁴: 0.7744 × 0.7744 = 0.59969536.",
        "Multiply by the original value: £8,000 × 0.59969536 = £4,797.56 (to the nearest penny).",
      ], answer: "£4,797.56" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A car worth £12,000 depreciates by 20% per year. What is it worth after 2 years?", answer: "£7,680, using the multiplier 1 - 0.20 = 0.80 applied twice:\n£12,000 × 0.8² =\n£12,000 × 0.64 =\n£7,680." } },
    { h: "3. Reverse percentages", body: [
      "It's tempting to think that if a 20% increase gives £60, you can just take 20% off £60 to get back to the original price. Test that:\n20% of £60 =\n£12, so\n£60 - £12 =\n£48.\nBut check it the other way: does £50 increased by 20% actually give £60?\n20% of £50 =\n£10, and\n£50 + £10 =\n£60.\nYes it does, yet the 'take 20% off the after-value' method gave £48, not £50. The two directions are NOT simple opposites of each other.",
      "The reason is that '20% of £60' and '20% of £50' are 20% of two DIFFERENT numbers. In multiplier terms, increasing by 20% then decreasing by 20% means multiplying by 1.20 then by 0.80, and 1.20 × 0.80 = 0.96, not 1. So going up 20% and then down 20% leaves you 4% below where you started, not back at the start.",
      "The reliable method uses the multiplier the same way Sections 1 and 2 did: after = before × multiplier. If you know the AFTER value and the multiplier, undo the multiplication with a division: before = after ÷ multiplier.",
      "So for a 20% increase ending at £60: the multiplier is 1.20, and\nbefore =\n£60 ÷ 1.20 =\n£50,\nmatching the value confirmed above by forward-checking. The rule in general: never apply the percentage to the after-value; always divide the after-value by the multiplier.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "After a 20% increase, a price is £60. What was the original price?", steps: [
        "Write the relationship using the multiplier: after = before × multiplier.",
        "Find the multiplier for a 20% increase: 1 + 0.20 = 1.20.",
        "Substitute the known after value: 60 = before × 1.20.",
        "Divide both sides by 1.20 to isolate before: before = 60 ÷ 1.20.",
        "60 ÷ 1.20 = 50.",
        "Check: £50 × 1.20 = £60. This matches the given after-value, confirming the answer.",
      ], answer: "£50" },
      { q: "After a 15% decrease, a laptop costs £221. What was the original price?", steps: [
        "Write the relationship using the multiplier: after = before × multiplier.",
        "Find the multiplier for a 15% decrease: 1 - 0.15 = 0.85.",
        "Substitute the known after value: 221 = before × 0.85.",
        "Divide both sides by 0.85 to isolate before: before = 221 ÷ 0.85.",
        "221 ÷ 0.85 = 260.",
        "Check: £260 × 0.85 = £221. This matches, confirming the answer.",
      ], answer: "£260" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "After a 10% increase, a bag of sweets costs £4.40. What was the original price?", answer: "£4.00, since dividing £4.40 by the multiplier 1.10 gives:\nbefore =\n£4.40 ÷ 1.10 =\n£4.00." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.surdicModularNumberTheory = {
  title: "Modular Arithmetic & Surd Identities",
  minutes: 17,
  order: 10,
  prereq: ["surdsAndIndices", "numberTheoryDivisibility"],
  intro: "This lesson goes a level deeper into two ideas from Number Theory and Surds & Indices: spotting which fractions terminate as decimals (and which recur forever), and using the (a+b)(a-b) surd-conjugate trick to clear roots entirely from an expression, before finishing with modular exponentiation, remainders of very large powers.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Which fractions terminate?", body: [
      "Divide 1 by 4 by hand: 1 ÷ 4 = 0.25 exactly, and the division stops. That's because 1/4 can be rewritten with a denominator of 100 (a power of 10): 1/4 = 25/100, and any fraction over a power of 10 is just digits placed after the decimal point, so it terminates.",
      "Now divide 1 by 3: 1 ÷ 3 = 0.333... and it never stops, no matter how many more 3s you compute. The reason is that no whole number multiplied by 3 ever lands exactly on a power of 10 (10, 100, 1000, ...), so 1/3 can never be rewritten with a power-of-10 denominator the way 1/4 could.",
      "This next step leans on prime factorisation from Number Theory & Divisibility: breaking a number down into the prime numbers that multiply together to make it (so 12 = 2×2×3, for example). Since 10 = 2 × 5, every power of 10 is built ONLY from the prime factors 2 and 5. A fraction can be rewritten over a power of 10 exactly when its (fully simplified) denominator's only prime factors are 2 and/or 5 too. If the denominator contains any other prime factor (3, 7, 11, ...), it can never be scaled up to match a power of 10, so the division carries on forever and the decimal recurs.",
      "That gives the working rule: a fraction terminates exactly when its fully simplified denominator has no prime factors other than 2 and 5. Any other prime factor in the denominator means the decimal recurs.",
      "The word 'simplified' matters. Check the denominator's prime factors only AFTER cancelling anything the numerator and denominator share. For example 3/6 looks like it has a 3 in the denominator's factorisation (6 = 2×3), but 3/6 simplifies to 1/2 first, and 1/2 terminates perfectly well, since the 3 cancelled away before it could cause trouble.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Does 7/16 terminate?", steps: [
        "Check whether 7/16 is already fully simplified: 7 is prime and does not divide into 16, so yes, it is already in simplest form.",
        "Find the prime factors of the denominator:\n16 =\n2×2×2×2 =\n2⁴.",
        "The only prime factor present is 2, which is allowed, so 7/16 terminates.",
        "Confirm by dividing: 7 ÷ 16 = 0.4375, which does stop.",
      ], answer: "Yes - 7/16 = 0.4375" },
      { q: "Does 21/56 terminate? (needs simplifying first)", steps: [
        "Check whether 21/56 is fully simplified: 21 = 3×7 and 56 = 8×7, so both share a factor of 7 - it is not yet simplified.",
        "Cancel the shared factor of 7: 21/56 = 3/8.",
        "Find the prime factors of the simplified denominator:\n8 =\n2×2×2 =\n2³.",
        "Only the prime factor 2 appears, so 3/8 (and therefore the original 21/56) terminates.",
        "Confirm by dividing: 3 ÷ 8 = 0.375.",
      ], answer: "Yes - 21/56 = 3/8 = 0.375" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Does 5/12 terminate?", answer: "No - 12 = 2² × 3, and the leftover 3 means it recurs (5/12 = 0.41666...)." } },
    { h: "2. The surd conjugate trick", body: [
      "Let x and y stand for any two numbers or algebraic terms - nothing here depends on them being surds yet. Expand (x+y)(x-y) fully using FOIL, keeping every term rather than jumping to the answer.\n(x+y)(x-y) =\nx×x + x×(-y) + y×x + y×(-y) =\nx² - xy + xy - y².",
      "Look at the middle two terms: -xy and +xy are exact opposites (multiplication doesn't care about the order you write x and y in), so they cancel to zero, leaving x² - y². That's the whole 'difference of two squares' identity, and it comes from watching the middle terms destroy each other, not from a rule handed down with no explanation.",
      "The two expressions being multiplied, (x+y) and (x-y), contain exactly the same two terms but with the sign in the middle flipped. A pair like that is called a conjugate pair, and multiplying a conjugate pair together like this is exactly the trick this section is named after.",
      "Now bring surds into it. Let a and b be two numbers that aren't negative, so that √a and √b are real numbers, and set x = √a, y = √b - a conjugate pair built from surds instead of plain letters. The same cancellation happens: (√a+√b)(√a-√b) = (√a)² - (√b)². From Surds & Indices, squaring undoes a square root, so (√a)² = a and (√b)² = b, and the whole expression collapses to a - b, with no surds left anywhere.",
      "Check it on numbers where both sides are easy to verify directly: let a=9, b=4.\n(√9+√4)(√9-√4) =\n(3+2)(3-2) =\n5×1 =\n5,\nand\na-b =\n9-4 =\n5.\nBoth routes agree.",
      "Once you recognise the (√a+√b)(√a-√b) shape - a conjugate pair of surds - skip the expansion and jump straight to a-b. The one condition to watch for: the SAME pair of terms must appear with opposite signs. (√a+√b)(√a+√b) is a completely different calculation (that's a square of a sum, not a difference of squares, and not a conjugate pair), so check the signs before applying the shortcut.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Simplify (√11+√3)(√11-√3).", steps: [
        "Recognise the pattern: this is (x+y)(x-y) with x=√11 and y=√3, which always equals x² - y².",
        "Work out x²: (√11)² = 11, since squaring undoes the root.",
        "Work out y²: (√3)² = 3.",
        "Subtract:\nx² - y² =\n11 - 3 =\n8.",
      ], answer: "8" },
      { q: "Simplify (5+√6)(5-√6) (a whole number paired with a surd).", steps: [
        "Recognise the pattern: this still matches (x+y)(x-y), with x=5 and y=√6 - x does not need to be a surd itself for the identity to apply.",
        "Work out x²: 5² = 25.",
        "Work out y²: (√6)² = 6.",
        "Subtract:\nx² - y² =\n25 - 6 =\n19.",
      ], answer: "19" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Simplify (√15+√2)(√15-√2).", answer: "13, since this matches x² - y² with x=√15 and y=√2, giving 15 - 2 = 13." } },
    { h: "3. Modular exponentiation", body: [
      "Let a and m be two whole numbers, with m greater than zero (m is called the modulus). 'a mod m' just means the remainder left when a is divided by m. Check:\n17 ÷ 5 =\n3 remainder 2, so\n17 mod 5 =\n2.\nAnother check:\n20 ÷ 5 =\n4 remainder 0, so\n20 mod 5 =\n0, since 5 divides 20 exactly.",
      "For small numbers, finding aᵇ mod m is direct: work out the power aᵇ FIRST, then divide by m and read off the remainder (the power always happens before the mod). For example:\n3⁴ =\n81, and\n81 ÷ 7 =\n11 remainder 4\n(since 11×7 = 77 and 81-77 = 4), so\n3⁴ mod 7 =\n4.",
      "For a LARGE exponent b, computing aᵇ directly becomes impossible, the number of digits explodes long before you can divide it by anything. Instead, track only the remainder after each multiplication, and watch for it starting to repeat. List the remainders of successive powers of 3 mod 7:\n3¹ mod 7 = 3,\n3² mod 7 =\n9 mod 7 =\n2,\n3³ mod 7 =\n27 mod 7 =\n6,\n3⁴ mod 7 =\n81 mod 7 =\n4\n(matching above),\n3⁵ mod 7 =\n(4×3) mod 7 =\n12 mod 7 =\n5,\n3⁶ mod 7 =\n(5×3) mod 7 =\n15 mod 7 =\n1.",
      "3⁶ mod 7 landing back on 1 is the signal to stop: multiplying by 3 again just restarts the same sequence, since\n3⁷ mod 7 =\n(1×3) mod 7 =\n3,\nidentical to 3¹ mod 7. So the remainders cycle forever in a block of length 6: 3, 2, 6, 4, 5, 1, then repeat.",
      "To find a huge power like 3²⁰ mod 7 without ever computing 3²⁰, work out where exponent 20 falls inside that length-6 cycle:\n20 ÷ 6 =\n3 remainder 2, so 3²⁰ sits in the same position as 3² in the list, giving\n3²⁰ mod 7 =\n2.\nThe cycle length isn't always guessable in advance, it has to be found by generating remainders until one repeats a value seen before.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find 3⁴ mod 7.", steps: [
        "Compute the actual power:\n3⁴ =\n3×3×3×3 =\n81.",
        "Divide by 7 and find the remainder: 81 ÷ 7 = 11 remainder 4, since 11×7 = 77 and 81-77 = 4.",
        "So 3⁴ mod 7 = 4.",
      ], answer: "4" },
      { q: "Find 3²⁰ mod 7 without computing 3²⁰ directly.", steps: [
        "List the remainders of successive powers of 3 mod 7 until they repeat: 3¹→3, 3²→2, 3³→6, 3⁴→4, 3⁵→5, 3⁶→1.",
        "3⁶ mod 7 = 1 signals the cycle restarting, since multiplying by 3 again reproduces 3¹'s remainder. So the cycle has length 6: 3, 2, 6, 4, 5, 1, repeating.",
        "Find where exponent 20 falls in a cycle of length 6: 20 ÷ 6 = 3 remainder 2, so exponent 20 matches the same position as exponent 2.",
        "Read off the remainder at position 2 from the list: 2.",
      ], answer: "2" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find 5¹⁰⁰ mod 3 by spotting a short cycle.", answer: "1. Since 5 mod 3 = 2, the remainders of powers of 2 mod 3 cycle as 2, 1, 2, 1, ... with cycle length 2. Because 100 is even, it matches the same position as exponent 2, which gives remainder 1." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.ratioProportionAlgebraic = {
  title: "Proportion as Algebra: y=kx and y=k/x",
  minutes: 15,
  order: 7,
  prereq: [{ module: "primary", key: "ratioBasics" }, "algebraicManipulation"],
  intro: "'Directly proportional' and 'inversely proportional' are really just two equation shapes: y=kx (direct) and y=k/x (inverse). In both, k stands for a fixed number that doesn't change across matching pairs of x and y drawn from the SAME relationship - though a different problem will generally have a different value of k. Once you spot which shape a problem fits, working out what k actually equals for that problem unlocks the whole question.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Direct proportion: y = kx", body: [
      "Suppose y = 4x. Build a table and check the ratio y/x every time: x=1 gives y=4 (ratio 4/1=4), x=5 gives y=20 (ratio 20/5=4), x=10 gives y=40 (ratio 40/10=4). No matter which pair you pick, y/x always comes out as the same number, 4. That fixed ratio is what 'directly proportional' actually means.",
      "The 4 in y=4x is only fixed for THIS particular relationship - a different proportional relationship would have a different fixed number sitting in its place. Rather than write out a specific number every time, use the letter k to stand for 'whichever fixed number happens to apply here'. So the general shape of every direct proportion is y=kx, with k equal to 4 for the relationship above, but free to be a completely different number in a different problem.",
      "Where does the value of k actually come from, for a given problem? Start from y=kx and divide both sides by x (this is allowed as long as x isn't 0):\ny/x =\nkx/x =\nk.\nSo k is just y divided by x - work it out from any one matching pair of x and y, and it comes out the same from every other pair in that relationship. Checking this on the example above: for y=4x,\ny/x =\n4x/x =\n4,\nmatching the 4 already spotted in the table.",
      "That's exactly why the method works: since y=kx holds for EVERY matching pair of x and y in a relationship, and k stays fixed throughout, any single known pair can be used to find k by computing k=y/x as just derived - and once k is known, it applies to every other pair from that same relationship.",
      "It also explains the doubling behaviour: if x becomes 2x, then y becomes:\nk×(2x) =\n2×(kx) =\n2y.\nWhatever happens to x happens to y as well, doubling causes doubling, tripling causes tripling, because k stays fixed throughout.",
      "To use this on a problem: spot the language ('proportional to', 'varies directly as') or a graph that's a straight line through the origin, find k from one given pair using k=y/x, write the specific equation y=kx (or y=kx² etc, matching whatever power is stated), then substitute to answer the rest.",
      "Watch out for relationships that LOOK proportional but aren't: y=kx passes through the origin (x=0 gives y=0), but y=x+5 does not, so it is not truly proportional even though y still increases as x increases. A genuine proportion has no '+constant' added on.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "y is proportional to x. When x=5, y=20. Find y when x=8.", steps: [
        "Since y is directly proportional to x, y = kx for some fixed constant k.",
        "Use the known pair to find k: k = y/x, so\n20/5 =\n4.",
        "Write the specific equation: y = 4x.",
        "Substitute x=8:\ny = 4×8 =\n32.",
      ], answer: "32" },
      { q: "y is proportional to x². When x=3, y=45. Find y when x=5.", steps: [
        "Since y is directly proportional to x², the equation is y = kx² (not y = kx - the power matters).",
        "Use the known pair to find k: k = y/x², so\n45/3² =\n45/9 =\n5.",
        "Write the specific equation: y = 5x².",
        "Substitute x=5:\ny = 5×5² =\n5×25 =\n125.",
      ], answer: "125" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "y is proportional to x. When x=7, y=21. Find y when x=12.", answer: "36, since k = y/x, so\n21/7 =\n3,\ngiving y = 3x, so\ny = 3×12 =\n36." } },
    { h: "2. Inverse proportion: y = k/x", body: [
      "Suppose xy = 60 always. Build a table: x=4 gives y=15 (product 60), x=6 gives y=10 (product 60), x=12 gives y=5 (product 60). The product xy stays fixed at 60 every time - that's what 'inversely proportional' means.",
      "This comes directly from the general shape y = k/x, where k again just stands for whichever fixed number applies to this particular relationship (exactly as in Section 1) - only this time it's the PRODUCT xy that stays fixed, not the ratio. Multiply both sides of y=k/x by x:\ny × x =\n(k/x) × x =\nk,\nso xy = k for any matching pair drawn from the same relationship. Checking against the example above, xy=60 throughout, so k=60 here.",
      "Check the halving behaviour with real numbers: x=4 gives y=15, and doubling x to 8 gives:\ny = 60/8 =\n7.5,\nexactly half of 15. Confirm the product still holds: 8×7.5 = 60. So doubling x halves y, the opposite behaviour to direct proportion.",
      "To use this on a problem: find k = xy from one known pair, write the specific equation y = k/x (or y = k/x² etc, matching the power stated), then substitute to answer the rest.",
      "The trap is applying direct-proportion thinking by mistake: in an inverse relationship, doubling x does NOT double y, it halves it. Always check which shape the problem actually describes before assuming either one.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "y is inversely proportional to x. When x=4, y=15. Find y when x=6.", steps: [
        "Since y is inversely proportional to x, xy = k for some fixed constant k.",
        "Use the known pair to find k: k = xy, so\n4×15 =\n60.",
        "Write the specific equation: y = 60/x.",
        "Substitute x=6:\ny = 60/6 =\n10.",
      ], answer: "10" },
      { q: "y is inversely proportional to x³. When x=2, y=10. Find y when x=5.", steps: [
        "Since y is inversely proportional to x³, the equation is x³y = k (not xy = k - the power matters).",
        "Use the known pair to find k: k = x³×y, so\n2³×10 =\n8×10 =\n80.",
        "Write the specific equation: y = 80/x³.",
        "Substitute x=5:\ny = 80/5³ =\n80/125 =\n0.64.",
      ], answer: "0.64" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "y is inversely proportional to x². When x=2, y=9. Find y when x=3.", answer: "4, since k = x²y, so\n2²×9 =\n36,\nand\ny = 36/3² =\n36/9 =\n4." } },
    { h: "3. Spotting which shape a real-world problem fits", body: [
      "Test the speed and time relationship with actual numbers first. Driving a FIXED distance of 120 miles: at 60mph it takes 120/60 = 2 hours, but at 30mph (half the speed) it takes 120/30 = 4 hours (double the time). Speed halved, time doubled - that's the signature of inverse proportion, and it makes sense as:\nspeed × time =\ndistance =\n120,\na constant.",
      "Compare that with density. For a FIXED material, mass and volume are directly proportional: double the volume of the same substance and you get exactly double the mass, since mass = density × volume, and the density (the constant) doesn't change when you take more or less of the same stuff.",
      "Reading the real-world situation correctly matters more than the algebra itself. Ask: if one quantity doubles, does the other quantity also double (direct proportion) or does it halve (inverse proportion)? Getting that answer right tells you immediately whether to reach for y=kx or y=k/x.",
    ], example: { q: "A car travels a fixed distance of 180 miles. At 45mph the journey takes 4 hours. How long does it take at 60mph?", steps: [
      "Decide the shape: for a FIXED distance, speed and time are inversely proportional (a faster speed needs less time), so speed × time = k, a constant equal to the distance.",
      "Find k from the known pair:\nk = 45×4 =\n180\n(the fixed distance in miles).",
      "Write the specific equation: time = 180/speed.",
      "Substitute speed=60:\ntime = 180/60 =\n3 hours.",
    ], answer: "3 hours" },
      tryit: { q: "A recipe uses a fixed ratio of butter to flour. 300g of flour needs 150g of butter. How much butter is needed for 500g of flour, using the same ratio?", answer: "250g. Butter is directly proportional to flour for a fixed recipe ratio:\nk = 150/300 =\n0.5, so\nbutter = 0.5×500 =\n250g." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.algebraicProof = {
  title: "Algebraic Proof: showing it's ALWAYS true",
  minutes: 18,
  order: 11,
  prereq: ["algebraicManipulation"],
  intro: "A proof has to work for every possible number, not just the ones you happen to try. This lesson covers the standard building blocks, expressing 'any even number' as 2n, 'consecutive integers' as n, n+1, n+2, and shows exactly why testing a handful of examples can never be enough, before finishing with a full worked proof.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Representing number types algebraically", body: [
      "Try the claim 'the sum of two consecutive integers is always odd' on two actual numbers first. n=3: the integers are 3 and 4, sum = 7 (odd). n=100: the integers are 100 and 101, sum = 201 (odd). Both worked, but neither one, nor both together, proves it for every integer, there's no way to write down a general argument yet, only two checks.",
      "To argue about EVERY integer at once, algebra uses a single letter, say n, to stand for 'any integer whatsoever' - not one particular number, but a placeholder for every integer at once. Any even number can then be written 2n. That works because every even number is, by definition, exactly 2 times some whole number, so as n runs through every integer in turn (..., -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, ...), the expression 2n runs through every even number, and only even numbers: n=1 gives 2, n=2 gives 4, n=0 gives 0, n=-1 gives -2.",
      "Any odd number is 2n+1. Odd numbers sit exactly one more than an even number, so taking the same 2n and adding 1 shifts every even number up to the odd number right next to it: n=0 gives 1, n=1 gives 3, n=2 gives 5. Consecutive integers are written n, n+1, n+2, and consecutive EVEN numbers are 2n, 2n+2, 2n+4 (each one 2 more than the last).",
      "This is why algebra can prove something a handful of examples cannot: n is not one fixed number, it's a placeholder standing for ANY integer simultaneously. A calculation carried out using n (rather than a specific value) is therefore true for every integer at once, the moment the algebra is finished, the proof is finished too.",
      "One care point: n ranges over ALL integers, including zero and negative numbers, not just the positive counting numbers you'd list first. Check 2n+1 still behaves correctly at n=-1: 2×(-1)+1 = -1, which is indeed odd, so the representation holds even at the edge cases.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Prove that the sum of two consecutive integers is always odd.", steps: [
        "Represent the two consecutive integers algebraically: let them be n and n+1, where n stands for any integer.",
        "Add them: sum = n + (n+1).",
        "Simplify: n + (n+1) = 2n+1.",
        "2n+1 matches the general form for an odd number, 2 times something plus 1, so the sum is odd.",
        "Because n could have been any integer, this holds for every possible pair of consecutive integers, including the n=3 and n=100 cases checked above, and every other integer besides.",
      ], answer: "2n+1 is always odd, so the sum of two consecutive integers is always odd, true for every integer n." },
      { q: "Prove that the sum of three consecutive integers is always a multiple of 3.", steps: [
        "Represent three consecutive integers algebraically: n, n+1, n+2.",
        "Add them: sum = n + (n+1) + (n+2).",
        "Collect like terms: three n's give 3n, and 1+2 gives 3, so the sum is 3n+3.",
        "Factorise: 3n+3 = 3(n+1).",
        "3(n+1) is 3 times a whole number, so it is a multiple of 3 by definition, true for every integer n.",
      ], answer: "3(n+1), a multiple of 3 for every integer n." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Show that any even number added to any odd number gives an odd number.", answer: "Odd. Represent the even number as 2n and the odd number as 2m+1, using a DIFFERENT letter m since the two numbers need not be related. The sum is 2n+(2m+1) = 2(n+m)+1, which matches the form for an odd number, true for all integers n and m." } },
    { h: "2. Why one example is never a proof", body: [
      "The earlier claim, that the sum of two consecutive integers is always odd, was checked at n=3 (giving 7) and n=100 (giving 201), and both came out odd. But does that prove it for n=4, or n=57, or n=-12? Not yet, each new number would need its own fresh check, and since there are infinitely many integers, no finite list of checks could ever cover them all.",
      "Compare that with the algebraic version from Section 1. The single line 'n+(n+1) = 2n+1' already CONTAINS n=3 and n=100 as special cases: substitute n=3 and it reads 2(3)+1=7, substitute n=100 and it reads 2(100)+1=201. The same algebraic step produces both instantly, along with every other integer, because n was never pinned to a particular value while the working was carried out.",
      "That's the real difference between verifying and proving. Verifying checks specific cases and can only ever build confidence. Proving gives an argument that must hold before you even know which number someone is going to pick, that's the only way to cover an infinite set of cases with a finite amount of writing.",
      "Be careful the general argument really does cover every case honestly. n² is even for n=2 (4) and n=4 (16), but n² is NOT even for n=3 (9), so a pattern that looks solid on a couple of examples can still fail elsewhere. Algebra only protects you from this if the working is genuinely general, substituting a few sample values into your final algebraic form is a good habit for catching a proof that secretly only works part of the time.",
    ] },
    { h: "3. A full worked proof", body: [
      "Check a bracket expansion concretely before trusting it in a bigger proof.\nTake (n+1)² at n=2: directly,\n(2+1)² =\n3² =\n9.\nUsing the expansion n²+2n+1 at n=2: 4+4+1 = 9. Both routes agree, confirming the expansion is safe to use algebraically.",
      "(Steps (2) and (3) below lean directly on skills from Algebraic Manipulation - expanding brackets, collecting like terms, and factorising. That's the lesson to revisit first if any of those feel shaky.) The standard proof recipe has four steps: (1) represent the number type(s) involved algebraically, (2) expand any brackets, (3) simplify by collecting like terms and factorising where possible, (4) explicitly say WHY the final form proves the claim.",
      "Step (4) is the one people skip, and it's the step that actually finishes the proof. Reaching an expression like 8n+8 doesn't prove anything on its own until you point out that 8n+8 = 8(n+1), which is 8 times a whole number, and therefore a multiple of 8 by definition.",
      "Take particular care with signs when subtracting one whole expanded bracket from another, as in the worked example below. Expand each bracket completely and separately FIRST, then subtract term by term, rather than trying to subtract before expanding, that's where sign errors creep in.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Prove that the square of any odd number is odd.", steps: [
        "Represent any odd number algebraically: 2n+1.",
        "Square it: (2n+1)² = (2n+1)(2n+1).",
        "Expand using FOIL: 2n×2n + 2n×1 + 1×2n + 1×1 = 4n² + 2n + 2n + 1.",
        "Collect like terms: 4n² + 4n + 1.",
        "Factor 2 out of the terms that allow it: 4n² + 4n + 1 = 2(2n² + 2n) + 1.",
        "2(2n² + 2n) is 2 times a whole number, so the whole expression is 2×(something)+1, exactly the form for an odd number, true for every integer n.",
      ], answer: "2(2n²+2n)+1 is always odd, so the square of any odd number is odd." },
      { q: "Prove that the difference between the squares of two consecutive odd numbers is always a multiple of 8.", steps: [
        "Represent two consecutive odd numbers algebraically: 2n+1 and 2n+3 (each odd number is 2 more than the last).",
        "Write the difference of their squares: (2n+3)² - (2n+1)².",
        "Expand (2n+3)² using FOIL:\n(2n+3)(2n+3) =\n4n² + 6n + 6n + 9 =\n4n² + 12n + 9.",
        "Expand (2n+1)² using FOIL:\n(2n+1)(2n+1) =\n4n² + 2n + 2n + 1 =\n4n² + 4n + 1.",
        "Subtract the second expansion from the first, keeping the whole bracket together: (4n² + 12n + 9) - (4n² + 4n + 1).",
        "The 4n² terms cancel, leaving 12n + 9 - 4n - 1.",
        "Collect like terms: 8n + 8.",
        "Factorise: 8n + 8 = 8(n+1).",
        "8(n+1) is 8 times a whole number, so it is a multiple of 8 by definition, true for every integer n.",
      ], answer: "8(n+1) is always a multiple of 8, proving the claim for every pair of consecutive odd numbers." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Prove that the difference between the squares of two consecutive integers equals the sum of those two integers.", answer: "True for every integer n: with integers n and n+1,\n(n+1)² - n² =\n(n²+2n+1) - n² =\n2n+1,\nand n+(n+1) also equals 2n+1, so the two expressions are identical for every integer n." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.functionsAndIteration = {
  title: "Functions & Iteration: machines that feed into themselves",
  minutes: 14,
  order: 12,
  prereq: ["algebraicManipulation", { module: "junior", key: "numberMachine" }],
  intro: "A function is a rule that takes an input and produces an output - the same 'number machine' idea from Junior (numbers go in, get processed by the rule, and come out changed), just written with algebraic notation instead of a picture of a machine. This lesson covers composite functions (chaining two functions together), inverse functions (undoing a function), and iteration (feeding a function's output back in as the next input, over and over) - used to home in on solutions that can't be found exactly.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Composite functions", body: [
      "Try it out on numbers before worrying about notation. Let f(x) = 2x+1 and g(x) = x-3. Work out g(5) first:\ng(5) =\n5-3 =\n2.\nNow feed that answer into f:\nf(2) =\n2(2)+1 =\n5.\nSo doing g then f on the number 5 gives 5.",
      "Now do the two functions in the OTHER order on the same starting number, to see whether order matters:\nf(5) =\n2(5)+1 =\n11.\nFeed that into g:\ng(11) =\n11-3 =\n8.\nDoing f then g on 5 gives 8, not 5. The two answers are different, so which function goes first genuinely changes the result - order matters.",
      "This is exactly what the notation fg(x) is built to tell you. Read it as a sentence: 'f of g of x'. The function written closest to x, here g, is the one that acts on x first; its output then becomes the input to the next function out, here f. So fg(x) means f(g(x)), and the calculation above (g first, giving 2, then f, giving 5) is precisely fg(5) = 5. The other order, f then g, is a completely different function called gf(x) = g(f(x)).",
      "The technique, then, is always to work from the inside out: whichever function is written nearest to x, do that one first, then apply the next function to whatever number comes out.",
      "The most common mistake is reading fg(x) left to right, like reading English, and doing f first. Always look at which letter is touching the bracket around x - that's the one that goes first.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "f(x) = 2x+1, g(x) = x-3. Find fg(5).", steps: [
        "In fg(x), the function touching the bracket is g, so g acts on x first.",
        "Work out g(5):\ng(5) =\n5 - 3 =\n2.",
        "Feed that result into f:\nf(2) =\n2(2) + 1 =\n5.",
        "So fg(5) =\nf(g(5)) =\n5.",
      ], answer: "5" },
      { q: "Using the same f(x) = 2x+1 and g(x) = x-3, find gf(x) as a single expression in x, then check it against the gf(5) = 8 found earlier.", steps: [
        "In gf(x), the function touching the bracket is f, so f acts on x first.",
        "Write out f(x): f(x) = 2x + 1.",
        "Feed this whole expression into g in place of x: g(f(x)) = (2x+1) - 3.",
        "Simplify: 2x + 1 - 3 = 2x - 2. So gf(x) = 2x - 2.",
        "Check the formula against the number worked out earlier:\ngf(5) =\n2(5) - 2 =\n10 - 2 =\n8, matching the 8 found directly. The formula is confirmed correct.",
      ], answer: "gf(x) = 2x - 2" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "f(x) = x+4, g(x) = 3x. Find fg(2).", answer: "10. In fg(x), g acts first:\ng(2) =\n3(2) =\n6.\nThen f(6) =\n6+4 =\n10." } },
    { h: "2. Inverse functions", body: [
      "Take f(x) = 3x-4. Work out f(3):\nf(3) =\n3(3)-4 =\n5.\nAn inverse function's whole job is to undo this - to take the 5 back to the 3 it came from. Whatever process does that job is called f⁻¹, read as 'f inverse'. That little -1 is just a label meaning 'the inverse of f' - it is NOT a power, and f⁻¹(x) does NOT mean 1/f(x), even though it's written similarly to power notation. Whatever process f⁻¹ turns out to be, it must satisfy f⁻¹(5) = 3.",
      "Here's the method for finding that process algebraically. Write the function as y = 3x-4, thinking of x as the input and y as the output. An inverse just swaps which one you know and which one you're solving for - so swap the letters: x = 3y-4. This new equation describes exactly the same input-output pairs, but now reversed: you're told the old output (renamed x) and asked to recover the old input (renamed y).",
      "Now rearrange this swapped equation to make y the subject again, using ordinary equation-solving steps. Add 4 to both sides:\nx+4 =\n3y.\nThen divide both sides by 3:\ny =\n(x+4)/3.\nThat gives f⁻¹(x) = (x+4)/3.",
      "Check it does what an inverse should:\nf⁻¹(5) =\n(5+4)/3 =\n9/3 =\n3\n- exactly the input that produced 5 in the first place. That check (feeding f's output back into f⁻¹ and getting the original input) is always worth doing once you've found an inverse.",
      "One thing to watch for: the swap-and-rearrange method needs the original equation to be fully isolated as y = ... before you swap, and you must finish by isolating y again afterwards - stopping halfway through the rearrangement is the most common way marks are lost.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find the inverse of f(x) = 3x - 4, then check it against f(3) = 5.", steps: [
        "Write the function as y = 3x - 4.",
        "Swap x and y: x = 3y - 4.",
        "Add 4 to both sides: x + 4 = 3y.",
        "Divide both sides by 3: y = (x+4)/3.",
        "So f⁻¹(x) = (x+4)/3.",
        "Check:\nf(3) =\n3(3)-4 =\n5,\nand f⁻¹(5) =\n(5+4)/3 =\n9/3 =\n3,\nrecovering the original input. Confirmed.",
      ], answer: "f⁻¹(x) = (x+4)/3" },
      { q: "Find the inverse of f(x) = (2x+5)/3, and check it against f(2).", steps: [
        "Write the function as y = (2x+5)/3.",
        "Swap x and y: x = (2y+5)/3.",
        "Multiply both sides by 3 to clear the fraction: 3x = 2y+5.",
        "Subtract 5 from both sides: 3x-5 = 2y.",
        "Divide both sides by 2: y = (3x-5)/2. So f⁻¹(x) = (3x-5)/2.",
        "Check:\nf(2) =\n(2(2)+5)/3 =\n9/3 =\n3,\nand f⁻¹(3) =\n(3(3)-5)/2 =\n4/2 =\n2,\nrecovering the original input 2. Confirmed.",
      ], answer: "f⁻¹(x) = (3x-5)/2" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the inverse of f(x) = (x-2)/5.", answer: "f⁻¹(x) = 5x+2. Swap:\nx =\n(y-2)/5, multiply both sides by 5:\n5x =\ny-2, add 2:\ny =\n5x+2.\nCheck:\nf(7) =\n(7-2)/5 =\n1,\nand f⁻¹(1) =\n5(1)+2 =\n7,\nmatching." } },
    { h: "3. Iteration to approximate a solution", body: [
      "Some equations, like x² = 5, don't have a nice whole-number or fraction answer - the solution is an infinite, non-repeating decimal. Iteration is a way of getting as close as you like to that decimal, by repeating a simple calculation over and over, each time feeding the previous answer back in to generate a better one.",
      "To keep track of which attempt is which, every approximation gets a subscript number: x_0 is the very first guess, x_1 is the result after doing the calculation once, x_2 is the result after doing it twice, and so on. In general, x_n means 'the approximation after n repeats', and x_(n+1) simply means 'the next one after that'. So a rule written as x_(n+1) = (something involving x_n) is just an instruction: take whichever approximation you currently have, do the calculation, and the answer is the next one in the list.",
      "Rearranging x² = 5 gives x = 5/x, and averaging x with 5/x turns out, when applied repeatedly, to home in on the actual answer. Written with the subscript notation just introduced, that rule is:\nx_(n+1) =\n(x_n + 5/x_n)/2\n- meaning 'to get the next approximation, average the current one with 5 divided by the current one'.",
      "Check what happens if you could feed in the exact answer, √5 ≈ 2.2361, itself:\n(2.2361 + 5/2.2361)/2 =\n(2.2361 + 2.2361)/2 =\n2.2361.\nNothing changes - the true solution is a 'fixed point' of this process, meaning the formula leaves it unchanged. Writing the rearranged equation as x = g(x) (here g(x) = 5/x, g just being a name for whatever expression ends up on the right after rearranging), a fixed point is exactly a value of x for which x = g(x) is already true.",
      "Starting from a number that ISN'T the exact answer, each application of the formula happens to move the result closer to that fixed point rather than further away, for this particular rearrangement. Start at x_0 = 2 (a sensible guess, since 2²=4 and 3²=9, so √5 must lie between 2 and 3):\nx_1 =\n(2+5/2)/2 =\n2.25,\nalready much closer to the true value 2.2361 than the starting guess of 2 was.",
      "The technique in general: rearrange the original equation into the form x = g(x), pick a sensible starting value x_0 (often given, or found by noticing which two whole numbers the answer must lie between), then repeatedly substitute each output back in as the next input, keeping full decimal accuracy throughout.",
      "Two pitfalls. First, not every rearrangement of the same equation converges - some send the values further and further away instead, so always check your first couple of iterations are actually getting closer together, not further apart. Second, rounding too early and carrying the rounded value forward compounds the error at every step - keep several extra decimal places during the working, and only round the final answer.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Use x_(n+1) = (x_n + 5/x_n)/2 with x_0=2 to find x_1 and x_2 (this iteration converges toward √5).", steps: [
        "Substitute x_0 = 2 into the formula to find x_1: x_1 = (2 + 5/2)/2.",
        "Work out 5/2 = 2.5.",
        "Add: 2 + 2.5 = 4.5.",
        "Divide by 2: 4.5/2 = 2.25. So x_1 = 2.25.",
        "Substitute x_1 = 2.25 into the formula to find x_2: x_2 = (2.25 + 5/2.25)/2.",
        "Work out 5/2.25 = 2.2222 (to 4 dp).",
        "Add: 2.25 + 2.2222 = 4.4722.",
        "Divide by 2: 4.4722/2 = 2.2361 (to 4 dp). So x_2 = 2.2361.",
        "Compare to √5 = 2.2361 (to 4 dp) - already matching after just two steps.",
      ], answer: "x_1 = 2.25, x_2 ≈ 2.2361 (√5 to 4 dp)" },
      { q: "Use x_(n+1) = (x_n + 10/x_n)/2 with x_0=3 to find x_1, x_2 and x_3 (converging toward √10, a bigger and messier target).", steps: [
        "Substitute x_0 = 3 into the formula: x_1 = (3 + 10/3)/2.",
        "Work out 10/3 = 3.3333 (to 4 dp).",
        "Add: 3 + 3.3333 = 6.3333.",
        "Divide by 2: 6.3333/2 = 3.1667 (to 4 dp). So x_1 = 3.1667.",
        "Substitute x_1 = 3.1667 into the formula: x_2 = (3.1667 + 10/3.1667)/2.",
        "Work out 10/3.1667 = 3.1579 (to 4 dp).",
        "Add and halve:\n(3.1667 + 3.1579)/2 =\n6.3246/2 =\n3.1623 (to 4 dp). So x_2 = 3.1623.",
        "Substitute x_2 = 3.1623 into the formula:\nx_3 =\n(3.1623 + 10/3.1623)/2 =\n(3.1623 + 3.1623)/2 =\n3.1623 (to 4 dp).",
        "x_2 and x_3 agree to 4 decimal places, so the iteration has settled down: √10 = 3.1623 (to 4 dp).",
      ], answer: "x_1 = 3.1667, x_2 ≈ 3.1623, x_3 ≈ 3.1623 (√10 to 4 dp)" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Use x_(n+1) = (x_n + 7/x_n)/2 with x_0=3 to find x_1 (this iteration converges toward √7).", answer: "x_1 = 2.6667.\nx_1 =\n(3 + 7/3)/2 =\n(3+2.3333)/2 =\n5.3333/2 =\n2.6667,\nalready fairly close to √7 ≈ 2.6458 after just one step." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.sequencesAndSeries = {
  title: "Sequences: finding the rule and the nth term",
  minutes: 13,
  order: 8,
  prereq: [{ module: "junior", key: "customCount" }, { module: "junior", key: "bouncing" }],
  intro: "A sequence is a list of numbers following a rule. This lesson covers spotting arithmetic sequences (constant difference), geometric sequences (constant ratio), and quadratic sequences (where the SECOND difference is constant), plus how to write the nth term formula for each.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Arithmetic sequences", body: [
      "Look at 5, 8, 11, 14. Subtract each term from the next: 8-5=3, 11-8=3, 14-11=3. The difference is the same (3) every time - that constant gap is what makes this an arithmetic sequence.",
      "To describe this precisely, give three things names. Call the constant gap between consecutive terms d, for 'difference' (here d=3). Call the very first term of the sequence a (here a=5). And use n for the POSITION of a term in the list - n=1 for the first term, n=2 for the second, n=3 for the third, and so on - so 'the nth term' means 'whichever term sits in position n': one formula written in terms of n that produces every term at once, instead of listing them one by one.",
      "Think about what building the sequence term by term actually involves: term 2 is term 1 plus one lot of d; term 3 is term 1 plus two lots of d; term 4 is term 1 plus three lots of d. By the time you reach term n, you've added d exactly (n-1) times - one fewer than the term number, because the first term doesn't need any adding at all. That's the whole reason the formula for the nth term is a + (n-1)d and not a + nd.",
      "Check it on the example: a=5, d=3, so the formula gives:\nterm 1 = 5+(0)(3) = 5,\nterm 2 = 5+(1)(3) = 8,\nterm 3 = 5+(2)(3) = 11,\nterm 4 = 5+(3)(3) = 14\n- matching the original list exactly.",
      "To use it: find d by subtracting any term from the one after it, find a as the first term, then substitute both into a+(n-1)d and expand the brackets to tidy it into the form (something)n + (something).",
      "The most common slip is writing nd instead of (n-1)d - always double check by testing the formula against n=1, which must give back the first term a.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find the nth term of 5, 8, 11, 14, ...", steps: [
        "Find the common difference by subtracting consecutive terms: 8-5=3, 11-8=3, 14-11=3. d=3.",
        "Identify the first term: a=5.",
        "Substitute into the formula:\nnth term =\na+(n-1)d =\n5+(n-1)(3).",
        "Expand the bracket: 5+3n-3.",
        "Simplify: 3n+2.",
        "Check against n=1: 3(1)+2=5, matching the first term. Confirmed.",
      ], answer: "3n + 2" },
      { q: "Find the nth term of 20, 15, 10, 5, ..., then use it to find the 15th term.", steps: [
        "Find the common difference: 15-20=-5, 10-15=-5, 5-10=-5. d=-5 (the sequence is decreasing).",
        "Identify the first term: a=20.",
        "Substitute into the formula: nth term = 20+(n-1)(-5).",
        "Expand the bracket: 20-5n+5.",
        "Simplify: 25-5n.",
        "Check against n=1: 25-5(1)=20, matching. Confirmed the formula.",
        "Find the 15th term by substituting n=15:\n25-5(15) =\n25-75 =\n-50.",
      ], answer: "nth term = 25-5n; 15th term = -50" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the nth term of 9, 13, 17, 21, ...", answer: "4n+5. Common difference d=4, first term a=9, so:\nnth term =\n9+(n-1)(4) =\n9+4n-4 =\n4n+5.\nCheck: n=1 gives 4+5=9, matching." } },
    { h: "2. Quadratic sequences", body: [
      "Look at 3, 8, 15, 24, 35. The first differences are 8-3=5, 15-8=7, 24-15=9, 35-24=11 - these aren't constant, so this isn't an arithmetic sequence. But look at the differences OF those differences: 7-5=2, 9-7=2, 11-9=2. These second differences ARE constant. Whenever that happens, the sequence is quadratic - its nth term includes an n² term.",
      "Where does the size of that n² term come from? Check the second differences of some pure (number)×n² sequences - call the number multiplying n² in each case k, so the three tried here are k=1, k=2 and k=3. n² itself (k=1) gives 1,4,9,16,25,...; first differences 3,5,7,9; second differences 2,2,2 - always 2. Now try 2n² (k=2): 2,8,18,32,50,...; first differences 6,10,14,18; second differences 4,4,4 - always 4. And 3n² (k=3): 3,12,27,48,75,...; first differences 9,15,21,27; second differences 6,6,6 - always 6. The pattern is exact: kn² always has constant second difference 2k. So if a sequence's constant second difference is known, the coefficient of n² is that number halved.",
      "Apply that here: the constant second difference was 2, so the n² coefficient is 2÷2=1, meaning the nth term starts with n² and then something extra (call it the 'leftover').",
      "To find the leftover, subtract n² from each term of the original sequence and see what's left: 3-1=2, 8-4=4, 15-9=6, 24-16=8. The leftover 2,4,6,8 is itself a plain arithmetic sequence (difference 2, first term 2), with nth term 2n by the method from Section 1. Add the n² part and the leftover part together to get the full nth term: n²+2n.",
      "Always finish by checking the formula against a couple of the original terms - here n=3 gives 9+6=15, matching. The main pitfall is subtracting n² itself when the coefficient isn't 1 - subtract the FULL term (coefficient × n²), not just n².",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find the nth term of 3, 8, 15, 24, 35, ...", steps: [
        "Find the first differences: 8-3=5, 15-8=7, 24-15=9, 35-24=11. So first differences are 5,7,9,11 - not constant, so this isn't arithmetic.",
        "Find the second differences: 7-5=2, 9-7=2, 11-9=2. These ARE constant, so the sequence is quadratic - the nth term will include an n² term.",
        "The coefficient of n² is always half the constant second difference: 2÷2 = 1. So the nth term has the form n² + (something linear).",
        "Subtract n² from each term to find the leftover:\nterm1: 3-1² = 3-1 = 2.\nterm2: 8-2² = 8-4 = 4.\nterm3: 15-3² = 15-9 = 6.\nterm4: 24-4² = 24-16 = 8.",
        "The leftover sequence 2,4,6,8 is arithmetic with common difference 2 and first term 2, so its nth term is 2n.",
        "Add the leftover back to the n² part: nth term = n² + 2n.",
        "Check against the original sequence: n=1: 1+2=3, correct. n=2: 4+4=8, correct. n=3: 9+6=15, correct. n=4: 16+8=24, correct.",
      ], answer: "n² + 2n" },
      { q: "Find the nth term of 5, 16, 33, 56, 85, ... (bigger numbers, and this time the n² coefficient isn't 1).", steps: [
        "Find the first differences: 16-5=11, 33-16=17, 56-33=23, 85-56=29 - not constant.",
        "Find the second differences: 17-11=6, 23-17=6, 29-23=6 - constant, so the sequence is quadratic.",
        "The coefficient of n² is half the constant second difference: 6÷2=3. So the nth term has the form 3n² + (something linear).",
        "Subtract 3n² (not just n²) from each term:\nterm1: 5-3(1²) = 5-3 = 2.\nterm2: 16-3(2²) = 16-12 = 4.\nterm3: 33-3(3²) = 33-27 = 6.\nterm4: 56-3(4²) = 56-48 = 8.",
        "The leftover 2,4,6,8 is arithmetic with common difference 2 and first term 2, so its nth term is 2n.",
        "Add the leftover to the 3n² part: nth term = 3n² + 2n.",
        "Check against the 5th term: n=5 gives:\n3(25)+2(5) =\n75+10 =\n85,\nmatching the original sequence. Confirmed.",
      ], answer: "3n² + 2n" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the nth term of 4, 10, 18, 28, 40, ...", answer: "n²+3n. First differences 6,8,10,12 aren't constant, but second differences are all 2, so the n² coefficient is 2÷2=1; subtracting n² from each term leaves 3,6,9,12, which is 3n, so nth term = n²+3n." } },
    { h: "3. Geometric sequences", body: [
      "Look at 3, 6, 12, 24. Divide each term by the one before it: 6/3=2, 12/6=2, 24/12=2. The ratio is the same (2) every time - that constant multiplier is what makes this a geometric sequence.",
      "As in Section 1, give the things involved names. Call this constant multiplier r, for 'ratio' (here r=2), and reuse a for the first term (here a=3) and n for the position of a term in the list, exactly as before.",
      "Think about building the sequence term by term: term 2 is term 1 multiplied by r once; term 3 is term 1 multiplied by r twice (once to get to term 2, again to get to term 3); term 4 is term 1 multiplied by r three times. By term n, r has been multiplied in exactly (n-1) times - one fewer than the term number, for the same reason as the arithmetic case. That's why the formula is a×r^(n-1).",
      "Check it:\na=3, r=2, so:\nterm1 = 3×2⁰ = 3×1 = 3,\nterm2 = 3×2¹ = 6,\nterm3 = 3×2² = 12,\nterm4 = 3×2³ = 24\n- matching the list.",
      "To use it: find r by dividing any term by the one before it, find a as the first term, then substitute into a×r^(n-1), being careful to write the power as (n-1) and not n.",
      "Watch the brackets carefully when r is negative or a fraction - r^(n-1) needs the whole ratio raised to that power, not just part of it, and an even power always turns a negative ratio positive.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find the 6th term of the geometric sequence 3, 6, 12, 24, ...", steps: [
        "Find the ratio by dividing a term by the one before it: 6÷3=2, 12÷6=2 - confirming r=2.",
        "Identify the first term: a=3.",
        "Substitute into the formula for the 6th term:\nterm6 =\na×r^(6-1) =\n3×2⁵.",
        "Work out 2⁵ = 32.",
        "Multiply: 3×32=96.",
      ], answer: "96" },
      { q: "Find the 7th term of the geometric sequence 8, -4, 2, -1, ... (a negative, fractional ratio this time).", steps: [
        "Find the ratio: -4÷8=-1/2, 2÷(-4)=-1/2 - confirming r=-1/2.",
        "Identify the first term: a=8.",
        "Substitute into the formula for the 7th term:\nterm7 =\na×r^(7-1) =\n8×(-1/2)⁶.",
        "Work out (-1/2)⁶: an even power makes a negative number positive, so this equals (1/2)⁶ = 1/64.",
        "Multiply:\n8×(1/64) =\n8/64 =\n1/8.",
      ], answer: "1/8" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the 5th term of the geometric sequence 2, 6, 18, 54, ...", answer: "162. Ratio:\nr =\n6÷2 =\n3,\nfirst term a=2, so:\n5th term =\na×r⁴ =\n2×3⁴ =\n2×81 =\n162." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.graphsAndRatesOfChange = {
  title: "Graphs & Rates of Change",
  minutes: 13,
  order: 9,
  prereq: [{ module: "junior", key: "coordGeom" }, "simultaneousEquations"],
  intro: "A graph's gradient tells you how fast one quantity changes compared to another - speed on a distance-time graph, acceleration on a velocity-time graph. This lesson covers finding gradients, reading key features off a graph, and using the area under a graph.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Gradient between two points", body: [
      "Check the formula against a line you already know: y=2x passes through (1,2) and (3,6) (since 2×1=2 and 2×3=6). Using the gradient formula on these two points:\n(6-2)/(3-1) =\n4/2 =\n2\n- exactly matching the 2 in y=2x. The formula recovers the gradient you already knew was there.",
      "Here's why that always works, for ANY straight line, not just y=2x. Every straight line's equation can be written in the general form y=mx+c, where m and c stand for two fixed numbers particular to that one line (for y=2x, matching against y=mx+c gives m=2 and c=0). Exactly what m and c represent geometrically is unpacked fully in Section 2 - for now, all that matters is that they're just two fixed numbers that don't change as x and y vary along the same line. Any two points on such a line can be written (x₁, mx₁+c) and (x₂, mx₂+c) - the small 1 and 2 just label 'point 1' and 'point 2', and each point's y-value comes from putting its own x-value into y=mx+c. Subtracting the y-values:\ny₂-y₁ =\n(mx₂+c)-(mx₁+c) =\nm(x₂-x₁)\n- the c's cancel completely. Dividing both sides by (x₂-x₁) gives (y₂-y₁)/(x₂-x₁) = m. So whichever two points you pick on a straight line, this calculation always returns the same value m - the gradient is a genuine property of the whole line, not of the particular points chosen.",
      "To use it: label one point (x₁,y₁) and the other (x₂,y₂), subtract the y's, subtract the x's in the SAME order, then divide.",
      "The most common mistake is mixing up the order - subtracting y₂-y₁ on top but x₁-x₂ on the bottom. That flips the sign of the answer. Whichever point you call 'point 1', use it as the first term in both subtractions.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find the gradient of the line through (1,3) and (4,15).", steps: [
        "Label the points: (x₁,y₁)=(1,3), (x₂,y₂)=(4,15).",
        "Find the change in y: 15-3=12.",
        "Find the change in x, using the same order as the y-subtraction: 4-1=3.",
        "Divide:\ngradient =\n12/3 =\n4.",
      ], answer: "4" },
      { q: "Find the gradient of the line through (-2,7) and (4,-5).", steps: [
        "Label the points: (x₁,y₁)=(-2,7), (x₂,y₂)=(4,-5).",
        "Find the change in y: -5-7=-12.",
        "Find the change in x, same order:\n4-(-2) =\n4+2 =\n6.",
        "Divide:\ngradient =\n-12/6 =\n-2.",
      ], answer: "-2" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the gradient of the line through (2,-1) and (5,8).", answer: "3.\nChange in y =\n8-(-1) =\n9.\nChange in x (same order) =\n5-2 =\n3.\nGradient =\n9/3 =\n3." } },
    { h: "2. Reading a line's equation", body: [
      "Take y=2x+1. Substitute x=0:\ny =\n2(0)+1 =\n1\n- matching the constant term, 1. Now substitute x=1:\ny =\n2(1)+1 =\n3.\nUsing the gradient formula from Section 1 between (0,1) and (1,3):\n(3-1)/(1-0) =\n2/1 =\n2\n- matching the coefficient of x, 2. Both numbers in the equation turn out to have a direct, checkable meaning.",
      "That's not a coincidence for this example - it's true of every line written in the y=mx+c form introduced in Section 1. Setting x=0 makes the mx term vanish entirely, leaving y=c, so c is always the y-value where the line crosses the y-axis (the y-intercept). And from the proof in Section 1, the coefficient m is always the gradient of the line, however it was found. Once an equation is written in this form, m and c can simply be read straight off, without plotting anything or picking two points.",
      "To use this: if the equation isn't already arranged as y=mx+c (for example, it might be written as 2y=4x+6, or 3x+y=5), rearrange it into that form first. Only once y is completely isolated on one side can the coefficient of x and the constant term be read off safely as m and c.",
      "In real contexts, m and c usually mean something concrete. m is the amount added for every extra unit of x (a rate: pounds per mile, pence per minute...), while c is whatever the quantity equals when x=0 - often a fixed starting charge or fee that applies before any usage at all.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "For the line y = 4x - 7, state the gradient and y-intercept, then check them.", steps: [
        "Compare y=4x-7 to the general form y=mx+c: m=4, c=-7.",
        "Check c by substituting x=0:\ny =\n4(0)-7 =\n-7,\nmatching c=-7 - this is where the line crosses the y-axis.",
        "Check m by finding a second point, substituting x=1:\ny =\n4(1)-7 =\n-3.",
        "Use the gradient formula between (0,-7) and (1,-3):\n(-3-(-7))/(1-0) =\n4/1 =\n4,\nmatching m=4. Confirmed.",
      ], answer: "gradient = 4, y-intercept = -7" },
      { q: "A taxi firm charges C = 2.50 + 1.20d, where C is the cost in pounds and d is the distance in miles. What do the numbers 2.50 and 1.20 represent, and what does a 6-mile journey cost?", steps: [
        "Compare C=2.50+1.20d to y=mx+c, with C playing the role of y and d playing the role of x: m=1.20, c=2.50.",
        "c is the value when d=0, i.e. before any distance is travelled - so £2.50 is a fixed charge just for getting in the taxi.",
        "m is the extra cost added for each additional mile - so £1.20 is added to the fare for every mile driven.",
        "Find the cost of a 6-mile journey by substituting d=6: C=2.50+1.20(6).",
        "Work out 1.20×6=7.20.",
        "Add:\nC =\n2.50+7.20 =\n9.70.",
      ], answer: "£2.50 is the fixed charge, £1.20 is the cost per mile; a 6-mile journey costs £9.70" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A phone plan costs C = 15 + 0.05t, where t is minutes used. What do 15 and 0.05 represent, and what is the cost for 200 minutes?", answer: "£15 is the fixed monthly charge (the cost when t=0), £0.05 is the cost per minute; for 200 minutes:\nC =\n15+0.05×200 =\n15+10 =\n£25." } },
    { h: "3. Area under a velocity-time graph", body: [
      "Start with the simplest case: an object moving at a constant 10 m/s for 4 seconds. By the familiar formula:\ndistance =\nspeed × time =\n10×4 =\n40 metres.\nNow look at the graph of this journey: velocity is a flat horizontal line at height 10, from time 0 to time 4 - a rectangle. Its area is:\nheight×width =\n10×4 =\n40.\nThe two calculations, done completely differently, give exactly the same number.",
      "That match isn't a coincidence - it's built into the units. Area under this kind of graph is always height×width, and here height is measured in m/s while width is measured in s, so height×width is measured in (m/s)×s = m - metres, exactly the unit distance is measured in. Whatever shape the region under the graph happens to be, working out its area in the ordinary geometric sense produces a quantity in metres, which is distance travelled.",
      "This still works even when the velocity is changing, so the top edge of the region is sloped or curved rather than flat, because the graph can be thought of as made of enormously many very short, near-constant-velocity strips side by side. Each thin strip behaves like the flat case above (a very short constant speed for a very short time), and 'the area under the graph' is exactly the sum of all those strips added together - so it still equals total distance.",
      "In practice: identify what shape the region under the line makes (rectangle for constant velocity, triangle for velocity rising steadily from zero, trapezium for velocity rising steadily from a non-zero starting value, or several of these joined together), then use the matching area formula.",
      "Two things to watch: this rule is specific to velocity-time graphs (area under a distance-time graph doesn't represent anything physical), and if part of the graph dips below the time-axis (negative velocity, meaning travel in the opposite direction), that area represents distance in the opposite direction - read the question carefully to see whether it wants total distance or overall displacement.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A velocity-time graph shows speed rising steadily from 0 to 20 m/s over 8 seconds. What distance was covered?", steps: [
        "The velocity rises steadily from 0 to 20 m/s over 8 seconds, so the region under the graph is a triangle with base 8 and height 20.",
        "Area of a triangle = ½×base×height.",
        "Substitute: ½×8×20.",
        "Work out 8×20=160, then half of that is 80.",
      ], answer: "80 metres" },
      { q: "A velocity-time graph shows speed rising steadily from 5 m/s to 15 m/s over 6 seconds. What distance was covered?", steps: [
        "The velocity doesn't start at 0, so the region under the graph is a trapezium: two parallel sides of length 5 and 15 (the starting and ending velocities), with width 6 (the time).",
        "Area of a trapezium = ½×(sum of parallel sides)×width.",
        "Sum of the parallel sides: 5+15=20.",
        "Substitute: ½×20×6.",
        "Work out 20×6=120, then half of that is 60.",
      ], answer: "60 metres" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A cyclist travels at a constant 12 m/s for 10 seconds. Using the area under the velocity-time graph, find the distance travelled.", answer: "120 metres. Constant velocity makes a rectangle, so:\narea =\nheight×width =\n12×10 =\n120,\nmatching distance=speed×time." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.advancedProbability = {
  title: "Advanced Probability: AND, OR, and without replacement",
  minutes: 14,
  order: 15,
  prereq: ["combinatoricsAndCounting", { module: "primary", key: "fractionArithmetic" }],
  intro: "Beyond a single spin or roll, most real probability questions combine several events. This lesson covers the AND rule (multiply for independent events), drawing without replacement (where probabilities change after each draw), and conditional probability (given some information is already known).",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Independent events: the AND rule", body: [
      "List every equally likely outcome of flipping a coin and rolling a die together: 2 coin results × 6 die results = 12 equally likely pairs in total. Exactly one of those pairs is (heads, 6). Write P(event) as shorthand for 'the probability that event happens' - a number between 0 (impossible) and 1 (certain), found here just by counting: (favourable outcomes) ÷ (total equally likely outcomes). So P(heads AND a 6) = 1/12 by direct counting.",
      "Compare that to multiplying the two separate probabilities: P(heads)=1/2, P(a 6)=1/6, and 1/2×1/6=1/12 - exactly matching the count. This isn't a coincidence: because the coin and die don't affect each other, EVERY one of the coin's 2 outcomes can be paired with EVERY one of the die's 6 outcomes, giving 2×6=12 equally likely combined outcomes in total, of which exactly 1×1=1 is the combination wanted. Dividing favourable by total, (1×1)/(2×6), is the same calculation as (1/2)×(1/6) rearranged - multiplying the separate probabilities together always gives the right answer, provided the events genuinely don't influence each other.",
      "This only works for independent events - ones where knowing the outcome of one tells you nothing about the other. To use the rule: confirm the events are independent, then simply multiply their individual probabilities.",
      "The danger is applying this same multiplying shortcut to events that DO affect each other (see the next section), where the probabilities themselves change once the first event has happened.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A fair coin is flipped and a fair die is rolled. What is P(heads AND a 6)?", steps: [
        "Check the events are independent: the coin landing on heads has no effect on what the die shows, and vice versa.",
        "Find P(heads) = 1/2.",
        "Find P(a 6) = 1/6.",
        "Multiply: 1/2×1/6=1/12.",
      ], answer: "1/12" },
      { q: "Two fair dice are rolled and a fair coin is flipped. Find P(the two dice sum to 7 AND the coin shows heads).", steps: [
        "Check independence: the coin doesn't affect the dice, and the dice don't affect the coin.",
        "Find P(the two dice sum to 7): the pairs that work are (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1) - 6 pairs out of 36 equally likely pairs total, so:\nP =\n6/36 =\n1/6.",
        "Find P(coin shows heads) = 1/2.",
        "Multiply: 1/6×1/2=1/12.",
      ], answer: "1/12" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Two independent spinners are used: spinner A has 4 equal sections numbered 1-4, spinner B has 3 equal sections coloured red, blue, green. Find P(spinner A shows 3 AND spinner B shows green).", answer: "1/12. Independent events, so multiply the separate probabilities: P(3)=1/4, P(green)=1/3, giving 1/4×1/3=1/12." } },
    { h: "2. Without replacement", body: [
      "Take a tiny bag with 2 red counters (call them R1 and R2 to tell them apart) and 1 blue counter (B), and draw 2 counters out one after another without putting the first one back. Listing every equally likely ordered result: (R1,R2), (R1,B), (R2,R1), (R2,B), (B,R1), (B,R2) - 6 outcomes altogether (3 choices for the first draw, then 2 remaining choices for the second). Exactly 2 of these 6 outcomes are 'both red': (R1,R2) and (R2,R1). So:\nP(both red) =\n2/6 =\n1/3,\nfound by direct counting.",
      "Now compare that to working it out via probabilities: P(1st red) = 2/3 (2 reds out of 3 counters). But for the second draw, one red counter and one counter overall have already gone, leaving only 2 counters, 1 of them red. This second probability needs a new piece of language: write P(B given A) to mean the probability that B happens, worked out using only the outcomes where A has already happened - it's called a conditional probability, and 'given' is the word that introduces the condition. Here, P(2nd red, given the first was red) means exactly: 'assuming the first counter really was red, what fraction of what's left is red?' - and that fraction is 1/2 (1 red counter left, out of 2 counters left). Multiplying:\n2/3×1/2 =\n2/6 =\n1/3\n- matching the count exactly.",
      "That match reveals what's different here from Section 1: once a counter is removed and not replaced, both the number left in total AND the number left of the type just drawn are reduced by one for the next draw. The second probability genuinely depends on what happened first - the events are no longer independent - so the numbers used for the second draw must be adjusted before multiplying.",
      "In general: work out the first draw's probability as normal. For the second draw, reduce the total (the denominator) by 1, and reduce the count of whatever was actually drawn first (the relevant numerator) by 1 as well, before writing the second fraction. Then multiply, exactly as before.",
      "One more rule is worth naming here, since the second example below needs it: when a result can happen via two or more different routes that can't both occur at once - like 'red-then-blue' OR 'blue-then-red' - work out the probability of each route separately and then ADD the routes together. This addition is safe exactly because the routes are non-overlapping (getting red-then-blue on a given pair of draws rules out getting blue-then-red on that same pair), so nothing is being double-counted. This is the OR rule, and it's the opposite move to the AND rule from Section 1, which multiplies rather than adds.",
      "The single most common mistake is forgetting to reduce the total on the second draw (using the original total instead of one fewer) - always ask 'how many counters are left in the bag altogether now?' before writing the second fraction.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A bag has 5 red and 3 blue counters. Two are drawn without replacement. Find P(both red).", steps: [
        "Find P(1st red): 5 red out of 8 counters total, so P=5/8.",
        "One red counter and one counter overall are now gone, leaving 7 counters, 4 of them red.",
        "Find P(2nd red, given the 1st was red): 4/7.",
        "Multiply: 5/8×4/7=20/56.",
        "Simplify: 20/56=5/14 (dividing top and bottom by their highest common factor, 4 - the same simplifying technique as in fraction arithmetic).",
      ], answer: "5/14" },
      { q: "The same bag (5 red, 3 blue, 8 total) has 2 counters drawn without replacement. Find P(one red and one blue, in either order).", steps: [
        "'One red and one blue' can happen in two different orders: red-then-blue, or blue-then-red - these are separate, non-overlapping cases, so (by the OR rule above) work each out and add.",
        "Red then blue: P(1st red)=5/8. One counter is now gone (a red one), leaving 7 counters, still all 3 blues. P(2nd blue given 1st red)=3/7. Multiply: 5/8×3/7=15/56.",
        "Blue then red: P(1st blue)=3/8. One counter is now gone (a blue one), leaving 7 counters, still all 5 reds. P(2nd red given 1st blue)=5/7. Multiply: 3/8×5/7=15/56.",
        "Add the two cases together, since either order satisfies the question: 15/56+15/56=30/56.",
        "Simplify: 30/56=15/28.",
      ], answer: "15/28" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A bag has 4 green and 2 yellow counters. Two are drawn without replacement. Find P(both yellow).", answer: "1/15.\nP(1st yellow) =\n2/6 =\n1/3.\nOne yellow and one counter overall are now gone, leaving 5 counters with 1 yellow, so P(2nd yellow given 1st yellow)=1/5. Multiply: 1/3×1/5=1/15." } },
    { h: "3. At-least-one, via the complement", body: [
      "Flip two fair coins and list all 4 equally likely outcomes: HH, HT, TH, TT. Count how many contain at least one head: HH, HT and TH all do - that's 3 out of 4, so P(at least one head)=3/4 by direct counting.",
      "Now work it out a different way: P(no heads at all) means both flips are tails, which is just the single outcome TT, so P(no heads)=1/4. Subtracting from 1: 1-1/4=3/4 - exactly matching the count.",
      "That match is guaranteed, not lucky. 'At least one head' and 'no heads at all' are opposites: every possible outcome falls into exactly one of these two categories, with nothing left over and no overlap between them. Two categories that between them cover every possibility, with no overlap, must have probabilities that add up to exactly 1. Rearranging P(at least one)+P(none)=1 gives P(at least one)=1-P(none).",
      "This is most useful when 'none happening' is much easier to calculate directly than 'at least one happening' - which is almost always true once there are more than two events or trials, since 'at least one' would otherwise mean adding up lots of separate messy cases (exactly one, exactly two, exactly three...), whereas 'none' is just one multiplication.",
      "The pitfall to avoid: P(at least one) is NOT found by adding the individual probabilities of each trial together - that method overcounts (and can even produce an answer bigger than 1 once there are enough trials). Always go via the complement, P(none), instead.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Two fair coins are flipped. Find P(at least one head).", steps: [
        "'At least one head' is the opposite of 'no heads at all', i.e. both coins landing tails.",
        "Find P(a single coin shows tails) = 1/2.",
        "Since the coins are independent:\nP(both tails) =\n1/2×1/2 =\n1/4.",
        "P(at least one head) =\n1 - P(both tails) =\n1 - 1/4 =\n3/4.",
        "Check by listing: HH, HT, TH, TT - 3 of these 4 equally likely outcomes contain at least one head, giving 3/4. Matches.",
      ], answer: "3/4" },
      { q: "P(rain on a given day) = 0.3. Find P(at least one rainy day in 2 independent days).", steps: [
        "P(no rain on a single day) =\n1-0.3 =\n0.7.",
        "Since the two days are independent:\nP(no rain on either day) =\n0.7×0.7 =\n0.49.",
        "P(at least one rainy day) =\n1 - P(no rain at all) =\n1-0.49 =\n0.51.",
      ], answer: "0.51" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A biased coin has P(heads)=0.6, and is flipped twice (independent flips). Find P(at least one tail).", answer: "0.64. P(no tails at all) means both flips are heads: 0.6×0.6=0.36. So:\nP(at least one tail) =\n1-0.36 =\n0.64." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.combinatoricsAndCounting = {
  title: "Combinatorics: counting without listing",
  minutes: 13,
  order: 14,
  prereq: [{ module: "primary", key: "combinatoricsCounting" }, { module: "junior", key: "seating" }],
  intro: "Combinatorics is about counting how many ways something can happen, without having to list every single possibility by hand. This lesson covers permutations (order matters), combinations (order doesn't matter), and the classic handshake-style counting problem. Go slowly through the 'why' paragraphs in each section - once you've seen why a counting rule works, you stop needing to memorise it.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Permutations: order matters", body: [
      "Start with something small enough to list by hand. How many ways can 3 people, A, B and C, stand in a queue? Write every arrangement out: ABC, ACB, BAC, BCA, CAB, CBA. That's 6 arrangements, and no others are possible - any attempt to write a 7th will just repeat one you already have.",
      "Notice how that count of 6 also falls out of a simple slot-filling argument: there are 3 choices for who stands first. Once that person is fixed, only 2 people are left for the second slot. Once that's fixed, only 1 person is left for the third slot. Multiplying the choices at each stage gives 3 × 2 × 1 = 6, exactly matching the list. This 'multiply the choices at each stage' idea is called the multiplication principle, and it's the engine behind every count in this lesson.",
      "Now generalise it. If you're arranging r items chosen from n distinct items INTO ORDER, the first slot has n choices, the second has (n-1) choices (one item is used up), the third has (n-2), and so on, for r slots in total. This count is written nPr (\"n permute r\"): nPr = n × (n-1) × (n-2) × ... down to r terms.",
      "The one thing to watch is where the multiplication stops. It runs for exactly r terms, not all the way down to 1, unless r happens to equal n. If you're placing 3 out of 8 runners into 1st, 2nd and 3rd, you multiply 8 × 7 × 6 (three terms, one per medal) and then STOP - there's no reason to keep going down to 5, 4, 3... because only 3 positions exist.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "How many ways can 3 runners finish 1st, 2nd and 3rd out of 8 racers?", steps: [
        "This is an arrangement into order (1st, 2nd, 3rd are different positions), so it's a permutation problem.",
        "There are 3 positions to fill, so the multiplication chain will have exactly 3 terms, starting from 8.",
        "8 choices for who finishes 1st.",
        "Once 1st is decided, 7 racers remain, so 7 choices for 2nd.",
        "Once 1st and 2nd are decided, 6 racers remain, so 6 choices for 3rd.",
        "Multiply the three stage-counts together: 8 × 7 × 6 = 336.",
      ], answer: "336" },
      { q: "How many ways can 4 contestants out of 10 be awarded 1st, 2nd, 3rd and 4th place (a harder version with more positions)?", steps: [
        "There are 4 positions this time, so the chain needs exactly 4 terms, starting from 10.",
        "10 choices for 1st place.",
        "9 remaining choices for 2nd place.",
        "8 remaining choices for 3rd place.",
        "7 remaining choices for 4th place.",
        "Multiply all four stage-counts:\n10 × 9 = 90, then\n90 × 8 = 720, then\n720 × 7 = 5040.",
      ], answer: "5040" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "5 sprinters race. In how many ways can gold, silver and bronze medals be awarded?", answer: "60. Three positions to fill from 5 sprinters: 5 × 4 × 3 = 60, stopping after three terms because there are only three medals." } },
    { h: "2. Combinations: order doesn't matter", body: [
      "Compare two different questions about the same 3 people, A, B and C. Question 1: how many ORDERED pairs can you make (like picking a captain then a vice-captain)? List them: AB, BA, AC, CA, BC, CB - that's 6, matching nPr with n=3, r=2 (3×2=6). Question 2: how many ways can you choose an UNORDERED pair (like picking 2 people for a team, where nobody has a special role)? List them: AB, AC, BC - that's only 3, because AB and BA are now the SAME choice (the same two people).",
      "Look at the ratio between those two counts: 6 ÷ 3 = 2. That 2 isn't a coincidence, and it's worth defining a new piece of notation to describe it properly. For a whole number n, write n! (read 'n factorial') to mean n multiplied by every smaller whole number down to 1: n! = n × (n-1) × (n-2) × ... × 2 × 1. So 2! = 2 × 1 = 2, and 3! = 3 × 2 × 1 = 6. The ratio just found, 2, is exactly 2!: it's the number of ways to reorder any 2 chosen items (AB can only become BA, one swap, so there are 2! = 2 orderings). Every unordered pair, like {A,B}, corresponds to exactly 2 of the ordered pairs (AB and BA), so the ordered count is always r! times bigger than the unordered count, for a selection of size r. That gives the rule: nCr = nPr ÷ r!.",
      "nPr can also be written using factorial notation instead of a running product, and it's worth checking the two forms really agree before using either. Take the running example again, n=3, r=2: n! = 3! = 3×2×1 = 6, and (n-r)! = (3-2)! = 1! = 1 (when the countdown reaches 1, there's nothing smaller left to multiply by, so 1! is just 1). Dividing:\nn!/(n-r)! =\n6/1 =\n6,\nthe same 6 as nPr = 3×2 computed directly above. This works in general, not just for these numbers: n! is the full product n×(n-1)×...×(n-r+1)×(n-r)×(n-r-1)×...×1, and dividing by (n-r)! cancels exactly that second half of the chain - the tail running from (n-r) down to 1 - leaving only the first r factors, n×(n-1)×...×(n-r+1). So nPr = n!/(n-r)!, and dividing that by r! (to remove the ordering, exactly as just shown) gives the full combination formula: nCr = n!/(r!×(n-r)!).",
      "In practice it's often quickest to compute nPr first as a short multiplication chain, then divide by r! at the end, cancelling where you can rather than working out huge factorials.",
      "The key question to ask before choosing which formula to use is: are the chosen items being given different roles (positions, medals, ordered slots) or are they just being grouped together as an unordered set (a team, a committee, a handful of toppings)? Different roles mean permutations; an unordered group means combinations, and you must remember to divide out the r! or you'll overcount.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "How many ways can a pair of 2 representatives be chosen from a class of 5 (no distinct roles, just 2 people)?", steps: [
        "This is a combination, since the 2 chosen people aren't given different roles.",
        "First find the permutation count nPr with n=5, r=2: 5 × 4 = 20.",
        "The 2 chosen items can be reordered in 2! = 2 ways, and each of those 2 orderings was counted separately in the permutation count.",
        "Divide to remove the overcounting: 20 ÷ 2 = 10.",
      ], answer: "10" },
      { q: "How many ways can a team of 3 be chosen from 8 people (a harder version with a bigger group)?", steps: [
        "This is a combination: a 'team' has no internal order, so all 3 members play an equivalent role.",
        "First find nPr with n=8, r=3: 8 × 7 × 6 = 336.",
        "The 3 chosen items can be reordered in:\n3! =\n3 × 2 × 1 =\n6\nways, all counted as the same team inside the 336.",
        "Divide to remove the overcounting: 336 ÷ 6 = 56.",
      ], answer: "56" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "How many ways can 3 pizza toppings be chosen from a menu of 6 toppings?", answer: "20. Permutation count 6 × 5 × 4 = 120, and 3! = 6 (since the 3 chosen toppings have no order), so:\n120 ÷ 6 =\n20." } },
    { h: "3. The handshake problem", body: [
      "Take a small group of 4 people, A, B, C and D, and have everyone shake hands with everyone else exactly once. List every handshake: AB, AC, AD, BC, BD, CD. That's 6 handshakes, and listing any more would just repeat a pair (BA is the same handshake as AB).",
      "Look closely at what a handshake actually is: it's simply an unordered choice of 2 people out of the group. That means the number of handshakes in a group of n people is exactly nC2 - the same quantity from section 2, just wearing a different costume.",
      "Since nCr = nPr ÷ r!, setting r = 2 gives:\nnC2 =\nnP2 ÷ 2! =\n[n × (n-1)] ÷ 2.\nFor the group of 4, that's:\n(4 × 3) ÷ 2 =\n12 ÷ 2 =\n6,\nmatching the list exactly.",
      "The division by 2 is doing real work here, not just decoration: without it you'd be counting 'A shakes B's hand' and 'B shakes A's hand' as two separate events, when they're actually the same physical handshake. Dividing by 2! is exactly how section 2 taught you to remove that kind of double-count.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "At a small meeting of 6 people, everyone shakes hands with everyone else once. How many handshakes in total?", steps: [
        "A handshake is an unordered choice of 2 people, so this is nC2 with n=6.",
        "Ordered pair count: 6 × 5 = 30.",
        "Divide by 2! = 2 to remove the double-count (A-then-B and B-then-A are the same handshake):\n30 ÷ 2 =\n15.",
      ], answer: "15" },
      { q: "At a party of 12 people, everyone shakes hands with everyone else once (a harder version with a bigger group). How many handshakes?", steps: [
        "Again this is nC2, now with n=12.",
        "Ordered pair count: 12 × 11 = 132.",
        "Divide by 2! = 2:\n132 ÷ 2 =\n66.",
      ], answer: "66" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "In a round-robin chess tournament with 9 players, every player plays every other player exactly once. How many games are played?", answer: "36. Each game is an unordered choice of 2 players out of 9:\n(9 × 8) ÷ 2 =\n72 ÷ 2 =\n36." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.invariantsAndParity = {
  title: "Invariants & Parity: what never changes",
  minutes: 12,
  order: 16,
  prereq: ["numberTheoryDivisibility"],
  intro: "An invariant is something about a puzzle that stays fixed no matter what moves are made - often the parity (odd/evenness) of a total. Spotting the right invariant can solve a puzzle instantly, without checking every possible case. Go slowly through the demonstrations in each section - once you've seen a rule proved on real numbers, you won't need to just trust it.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Odd + odd, odd x even, and the rules of parity", body: [
      "Parity, whether a number is odd or even, is really just divisibility by 2 - the same idea covered in general in the divisibility prerequisite lesson, just specialised down to the only two possible remainders when dividing by 2: 0 (even, divides exactly with nothing left over) or 1 (odd, one left over).",
      "Check the basic rules on real numbers before trusting them. 4 + 6 = 10 (even + even = even). 3 + 5 = 8 (odd + odd = even). 3 + 4 = 7 (odd + even = odd). 3 × 4 = 12 (odd × even = even).",
      "These aren't accidents, and it's worth seeing exactly why, using letters to stand for 'any whole number, not a fixed one'. Any even number can be written as 2a for some whole number a, and any odd number as 2b+1 for some whole number b. Every letter used below - a, b, c, d, k and so on - works the same way: it just means 'some whole number', and a fresh letter is used whenever a fresh, possibly-different number is needed (there's no reason to assume two different even numbers are secretly equal, for instance, so they get two different letters).",
      "Adding two evens, calling them 2a and 2c so they're allowed to be different numbers: 2a + 2c = 2(a+c), which is 2 × (a whole number, since a+c is a whole number whenever a and c both are), so it's even. Adding two odds, calling them 2b+1 and 2d+1:\n(2b+1) + (2d+1) =\n2(b+d) + 2 =\n2(b+d+1),\nagain even, since b+d+1 is a whole number. Adding an odd and an even: (2b+1) + 2a = 2(a+b) + 1, which is 2 × (a whole number) + 1, the exact definition of odd.",
      "The same approach handles multiplication. Odd × even: (2b+1) × 2a = 2 × [a(2b+1)], even, since there's a factor of 2 sitting outside. Odd × odd:\n(2b+1)(2d+1) =\n4bd + 2b + 2d + 1 =\n2(2bd+b+d) + 1,\nwhich is odd. Subtraction behaves exactly like addition for parity purposes too (odd - even = odd, odd - odd = even, and so on), since taking away a number has the same effect on parity as adding it back would.",
      "This lets you predict the parity of a long calculation without doing any of the actual arithmetic - just track odd/even through each operation in turn, applying multiplication before addition, exactly as the normal order of operations requires.",
      "The one real trap is doing the tracking in the wrong order. Parity must be worked out in the same sequence you'd calculate the real answer (brackets and multiplication before addition and subtraction), otherwise you can easily flip the final result.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Is 15 + 22 odd or even?", steps: [
        "15 is odd, 22 is even.",
        "Odd + even = odd (from the rule above).",
      ], answer: "Odd" },
      { q: "Is 37 x 48 + 15 odd or even (a harder version mixing multiplication and addition)?", steps: [
        "Order of operations: the multiplication must be resolved first.",
        "37 is odd, 48 is even, so 37 × 48 is odd × even = even.",
        "Now add 15 (odd) to that even result: even + odd = odd.",
      ], answer: "Odd" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Is 29 x 13 - 8 odd or even?", answer: "Odd. 29 and 13 are both odd, and odd × odd = odd, giving an odd result. Subtracting 8 (even) from an odd number keeps it odd, since odd - even = odd, the same rule as addition." } },
    { h: "2. Famous invariant results", body: [
      "Build up the pattern for summing odd numbers rather than just being told it:\n1 = 1².\n1+3 =\n4 =\n2².\n1+3+5 =\n9 =\n3².\n1+3+5+7 =\n16 =\n4².\nEvery time, adding the next odd number lands exactly on the next perfect square.",
      "This pattern can be proved for any number of terms at all, not just the four checked above, using a letter to stand for 'which position in the list you've reached'. Let k stand for any whole number counting a position: k=1 means the 1st odd number, k=2 the 2nd, k=3 the 3rd, and so on. The odd numbers themselves follow a simple rule tied to their position: the 1st is 1 = 2(1)-1, the 2nd is 3 = 2(2)-1, the 3rd is 5 = 2(3)-1, the 4th is 7 = 2(4)-1 - doubling the position and subtracting 1 always gives that position's odd number. So in general, the kth odd number is (2k-1).",
      "Now suppose the sum of the first (k-1) odd numbers is already known to equal (k-1)² - true for the small cases just checked, for instance with k-1=3: 1+3+5=9=3². The very next odd number in the list, the kth one, is (2k-1) as just shown. Adding it to that running total:\n(k-1)² + (2k-1) =\nk² - 2k + 1 + 2k - 1 =\nk².\nThe -2k and +2k cancel, and the +1 and -1 cancel, leaving exactly k². So no matter how far the list has already got, adding the next odd number always lands exactly on the next perfect square. Since this step works for every whole number k, repeating it from k=1 all the way up proves the sum of the first n odd numbers is always n², for any n.",
      "The even numbers have their own pattern, built the same way:\n2 =\n1×2.\n2+4 =\n6 =\n2×3.\n2+4+6 =\n12 =\n3×4.\nThe sum of the first n even numbers is always n(n+1).",
      "This also has a quick proof: the first n even numbers are just 2×1, 2×2, ..., 2×n, so their sum is:\n2×(1+2+...+n) =\n2 × [n(n+1)/2] =\nn(n+1),\nusing the standard result that 1+2+...+n = n(n+1)/2. That result itself comes from pairing terms from opposite ends of the list: pair 1 with n, 2 with (n-1), 3 with (n-2), and so on. Every such pair adds up to exactly n+1, and there are n/2 pairs in total, giving n/2 × (n+1) = n(n+1)/2 altogether.",
      "Both shortcuts turn a long addition into a single multiplication, which is exactly the point of spotting an invariant pattern - once you know the rule holds for every n, you never need to add term by term again.",
      "The easiest mistake to make is confusing n (how many terms you're adding - the same role k played above, just renamed for the general count) with the value of the LAST term. The nth odd number is (2n-1), not n itself - 'the first 10 odd numbers' means n=10, but the list actually runs up to 19 (= 2×10 - 1), not up to 10.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find 1+3+5+7+9+11 (the first 6 odd numbers) using the shortcut.", steps: [
        "Count the terms: there are 6 of them, so n=6.",
        "Apply the rule: sum of first n odd numbers = n².",
        "6² = 36.",
        "Check by direct addition:\n1+3 = 4,\n+5 = 9,\n+7 = 16,\n+9 = 25,\n+11 = 36.\nMatches.",
      ], answer: "36" },
      { q: "What is 1+3+5+7+...+19 (the first 10 odd numbers, a harder version with more terms)?", steps: [
        "Confirm 19 really is the 10th odd number using the nth-term rule: 2×10 - 1 = 19. It matches, so n=10.",
        "Apply the rule: sum of first n odd numbers = n².",
        "10² = 100.",
        "This avoids adding all 10 numbers by hand.",
      ], answer: "100" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "What is 2+4+6+8+10+12+14 (the first 7 even numbers)?", answer: "56. Using the shortcut n(n+1) with n=7: 7×8 = 56. Check by direct addition: 2+4+6+8+10+12+14 = 56." } },
    { h: "3. Using parity to rule out impossible puzzles", body: [
      "Try a concrete puzzle first. 5 coins all start heads-up. Each move flips exactly 2 coins of your choosing (heads become tails, tails become heads). Could all 5 coins ever end up tails-up? Look at what one move does to the total number of heads: flipping 2 heads turns them both to tails, so heads decreases by 2; flipping 2 tails turns them both to heads, so heads increases by 2; flipping 1 head and 1 tail swaps their roles, so heads stays the same. Every move changes the heads-count by -2, 0 or +2 - always an even amount.",
      "That means the PARITY (odd or even) of the heads-count can never change, no matter how many moves are made or which coins are chosen. It starts at 5, which is odd, so it stays odd forever. All-tails means 0 heads, which is even. Since odd can never become even by adding even amounts, this puzzle is impossible - and no amount of trying different move sequences will ever find a way, because the invariant rules it out completely.",
      "This is the general technique: find some quantity that only ever changes by amounts that preserve a property (usually its parity), work out that property at the start, and compare it with what the target would require. If they don't match, no sequence of moves, however long, can bridge the gap.",
      "The reasoning is airtight because it doesn't depend on HOW MANY moves are made or in what order - each individual move preserves the parity, so by repeating that fact move after move, the parity at every future point is forced to match the parity at the start. It's a proof about ALL possible move sequences at once, not just the ones you happened to try.",
      "Matching parity is not the same as proving something possible. If the start and target parities agree, this particular invariant simply fails to rule the puzzle out - you'd still need to actually find a working sequence of moves (or a different invariant) to be sure it can be done. Also double-check that every type of allowed move really does preserve the property you're relying on; missing one exceptional move can silently break the whole argument.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A light switch starts OFF. Each flick toggles it (OFF becomes ON, ON becomes OFF). After 15 flicks, could the switch be ON?", steps: [
        "After 1 flick: ON. After 2 flicks: OFF. After 3 flicks: ON. The state alternates with every single flick.",
        "So after an ODD number of flicks the switch is ON, and after an EVEN number of flicks it is OFF - the state is entirely determined by the parity of the flick-count.",
        "15 is odd.",
        "So after 15 flicks the switch is ON.",
      ], answer: "Yes, ON. This is a case where the parities match up, so the target is genuinely reachable (unlike the examples that follow, where a mismatch rules the target out)." },
      { q: "A frog starts at 0 on a number line and jumps either +3 or +5 each time (its choice). After exactly 7 jumps, could it be at position 30 (a harder version using an impossibility argument)?", steps: [
        "Both jump sizes, 3 and 5, are odd numbers.",
        "The frog's total displacement after 7 jumps is the sum of 7 odd numbers.",
        "A sum of k odd numbers always has the same parity as k itself. This follows from the addition rules proved in Section 1: start from 0 (even, since an empty sum has nothing in it), then add the odd numbers on one at a time - each single addition of an odd number flips the running total's parity (even+odd=odd, or odd+odd=even, either way it flips). Flipping the parity k times in total lands back on even if k is even, and lands on odd if k is odd.",
        "Here k=7, which is odd, so the total displacement after 7 jumps must be odd, regardless of which mix of +3s and +5s was used.",
        "30 is even, but the displacement is forced to be odd - a mismatch.",
      ], answer: "No, impossible. No combination of 7 jumps of +3 or +5 can ever total an even number like 30." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A counter starts at 0 and each move adds either 3 or 7 (both odd). After 4 moves, could it show 15?", answer: "No. The total after 4 moves is a sum of 4 odd numbers, and a sum of an even number of odd numbers is always even. 15 is odd, so it can never be reached after exactly 4 moves." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.logicAndDeduction = {
  title: "Logic & Deduction: truth-tellers, liars and clues",
  minutes: 13,
  order: 17,
  prereq: [{ module: "junior", key: "truthLiars" }, { module: "junior", key: "pigeonhole" }],
  intro: "Logic puzzles give you a set of statements and ask what must be true. The key technique is testing an assumption (say, 'assume person A is telling the truth') and following the consequences through - if you hit a contradiction, that assumption was wrong. Go slowly through each assumption in turn; the whole method depends on checking BOTH branches properly, not stopping as soon as one seems to work.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Truth-tellers and liars", body: [
      "These puzzles build on the basic truth-teller-and-liar setup from the truth-tellers-and-liars prerequisite lesson (a truth-teller always says true things, a liar always says false things), extending it to several statements at once. They all rely on one core method: assumption-testing. Pick one of the possibilities the puzzle allows (say, 'assume this person is a truth-teller'), work out everything that possibility FORCES every statement to mean, and check whether those forced meanings clash with the possibility you started with. If they do clash - if the assumption ends up forcing something that contradicts itself - that's called a contradiction, and it proves that assumption cannot be the real situation. If nothing clashes, the assumption survives as a genuine possibility, at least until it's tested against any other statements in the puzzle.",
      "See the method in its purest form first. Suppose one person, Max, says: 'I am a liar.' Try assuming Max is a truth-teller: then his statement must be true (since truth-tellers only say true things), meaning Max IS a liar - but that directly contradicts the assumption just made, that he's truthful. Now try the other possibility, that Max is a liar: then his statement must be false (since liars only say false things), meaning Max is NOT a liar - which contradicts the assumption that he's a liar. Both of the only two possibilities collapse into contradictions, which shows this particular statement could never actually be spoken by either a truth-teller or a liar - it's a paradox, not a puzzle with a real answer.",
      "In a solvable puzzle (unlike Max's paradox above), exactly one assumption about who is truthful will survive the check, and the other assumption will hit a genuine contradiction. The general procedure: assume one person is the truth-teller, work out what that FORCES every statement to mean, and see if everything stays consistent; then do exactly the same for the alternative assumption.",
      "For puzzles with several statements, work through the consequences of each assumption systematically, one statement at a time, rather than jumping to a conclusion. Write down what each person's statement would have to mean (true or false) under the assumption you're testing, then check that this matches what their type (truth-teller or liar) requires.",
      "The one habit to enforce every time: don't stop the moment one assumption looks consistent. Always finish checking the OTHER assumption too, and confirm it genuinely leads to a contradiction (not just that it 'seems less likely'). A well-posed puzzle guarantees exactly one assumption survives; if you find that both seem to survive, that's a sign to re-read the statements rather than guess.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Two people, Tom and Wendy. Exactly one of them always tells the truth and the other always lies. Tom says: 'At least one of us is a liar.' Who is the truth-teller?", steps: [
        "Assume Tom is the truth-teller (so Wendy is the liar).",
        "Then Tom's statement must be true. Check it: is at least one of them a liar? Yes, Wendy is. So the statement is true - consistent, no contradiction.",
        "Now assume Wendy is the truth-teller (so Tom is the liar).",
        "Then Tom's statement must be false, since Tom lies. For 'at least one of us is a liar' to be false, NEITHER person can be a liar - but this branch assumes Tom IS a liar. That's a direct contradiction.",
        "Only the first assumption survives.",
      ], answer: "Tom is the truth-teller." },
      { q: "Two people, Priya and Raj. Exactly one always tells the truth. Priya says: 'Raj is a liar.' Raj says: 'Priya and I are both liars.' (A harder version with two statements.) Who is the truth-teller?", steps: [
        "Assume Priya is the truth-teller (so Raj is the liar).",
        "Priya's statement 'Raj is a liar' must then be true - and it is, by assumption. Consistent so far.",
        "Raj's statement must be false, since Raj lies. Check: is 'Priya and I are both liars' actually false? Priya is truthful (not a liar) in this branch, so 'both are liars' is indeed false. Consistent - no contradiction anywhere.",
        "Now assume Raj is the truth-teller (so Priya is the liar).",
        "Raj's statement 'Priya and I are both liars' must then be true - but that would mean Raj himself is a liar, directly contradicting the assumption that Raj is truthful.",
        "Only the first assumption survives.",
      ], answer: "Priya is the truth-teller." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Jo says: 'Jo and Kim are both liars.' Exactly one of them always tells the truth. Who is the truth-teller?", answer: "Kim. If Jo were truthful, Jo would be admitting to being a liar, a contradiction. So Jo lies, meaning the statement 'both are liars' is false, so Kim must be truthful." } },
    { h: "2. The pigeonhole principle", body: [
      "Check a small case directly. Take 3 socks and only 2 colours available (say red and blue). Could all 3 socks have different colours from each other? There are only 2 colours to choose from, so with 3 socks, at least two of them are forced to share a colour - there simply aren't enough colours to go round without a repeat.",
      "Let N stand for the total number of items being placed, and k for the number of containers they're going into. That's the whole idea generalised: if N items are placed into k containers, and N is bigger than k, at least one container must hold more than one item. More precisely, at least one container is guaranteed to hold at least ⌈N/k⌉ items, where ⌈⌉ means 'round up to the next whole number'.",
      "To use it, imagine spreading the items as EVENLY as possible across the containers - that's the best case for avoiding a big pile-up. If N doesn't divide evenly by k, the leftover items must go somewhere, forcing at least one container one item higher than the even split. Rounding N/k up to the next whole number captures exactly that forced leftover.",
      "Two things catch people out: rounding down instead of up (⌈4.1⌉ is 5, not 4), and forgetting that the principle only guarantees THAT some container is overloaded, not WHICH one.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "In a room of 13 people, must at least 2 of them share a birth month (there are 12 months)?", steps: [
        "N = 13 people (items), k = 12 months (containers).",
        "Compute N/k:\n13/12 =\n1.0833...",
        "Round up: ⌈13/12⌉ = 2.",
      ], answer: "Yes - at least 2 people must share a birth month." },
      { q: "23 socks are placed into 5 drawers (a harder version with bigger numbers). What's the minimum guaranteed number in the fullest drawer?", steps: [
        "N = 23 socks, k = 5 drawers.",
        "Compute N/k:\n23/5 =\n4.6.",
        "Round up: ⌈23/5⌉ = 5.",
      ], answer: "5" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "37 pigeons roost in 6 pigeonholes. What is the minimum guaranteed number in the fullest hole?", answer: "7. 37/6 = 6.1666..., and rounding up gives ⌈37/6⌉ = 7 (since 6 holes with 6 pigeons each only accounts for 36, the 37th pigeon forces one hole up to 7)." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.diophantineEquations = {
  title: "Diophantine Equations: integer-only solutions",
  minutes: 13,
  order: 13,
  prereq: ["numberTheoryDivisibility", "simultaneousEquations"],
  intro: "A Diophantine equation is one where only WHOLE NUMBER solutions count - no fractions or decimals allowed. This restriction, surprisingly, is what makes these puzzles solvable by careful reasoning even when there are two unknowns and only one equation. Go slowly through the 'why' paragraphs - the tests in this lesson only work because of exactly how whole numbers behave.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. When does a solution exist at all?", body: [
      "Start with something you can check by hand, before any letters get involved. Look at the equation 6x + 9y, where x and y can be ANY whole numbers, including negative ones and zero. Since 6 = 3×2, the first term is:\n6x =\n3×2×x =\n3×(2x),\nwhich is 3 times a whole number, whatever x is (2x is a whole number because x is, and 2 is). Since 9 = 3×3, the second term is 9y = 3×(3y), also 3 times a whole number. Adding them:\n6x + 9y =\n3(2x) + 3(3y) =\n3(2x + 3y).\nThis is 3 times a whole number no matter which integers x and y are picked - it is FORCED to be a multiple of 3, always.",
      "That pattern generalises to any two numbers, not just 6 and 9, but it needs some notation defined carefully first, one piece at a time, before it can be written down.",
      "Let a and b be two whole numbers. When we write HCF(a, b) = h, we mean that h is the Highest Common Factor of a and b: the largest whole number that divides both a and b exactly, with nothing left over either time.",
      "For example, HCF(12, 18) = 6, because 6 divides both 12 and 18 exactly (12÷6=2 and 18÷6=3, both whole numbers), and no bigger number manages that (7 doesn't divide 12 at all, and nothing between 7 and 11 divides both).",
      "Since h divides a exactly, a must be h multiplied by some other whole number - call that number p. So:\na =\nh × p.\nSimilarly, since h divides b exactly, b must be h multiplied by some other whole number too - call that one q. So:\nb =\nh × q.",
      "The letters p and q are not special or mysterious. They are simply whatever is left over once a and b have each been divided by h:\np = a ÷ h,\nq = b ÷ h.",
      "Check this against the example above, where a=12, b=18 and h=6:\n12 = 6 × 2, so p = 2.\n18 = 6 × 3, so q = 3.\nThat's the whole of p and q - just the two answers you get from doing 12÷6 and 18÷6.",
      "Now look at the expression a×x + b×y, where x and y are allowed to be ANY integers - positive, negative, or zero, it doesn't matter which. Since a = h×p and b = h×q, replace a and b in that expression with those:\na×x + b×y =\n(h×p)×x + (h×q)×y =\nh×p×x + h×q×y.",
      "Both terms on the right contain a factor of h, so h can be pulled outside a bracket, exactly the way 3 was pulled out at the very start of this section:\nh×p×x + h×q×y =\nh×(p×x + q×y).",
      "The part sitting inside the brackets, p×x + q×y, has to be a whole number itself, because p, q, x and y are all whole numbers, and multiplying whole numbers together, or adding them, always gives another whole number - there's no way for a fraction to sneak in.",
      "So the entire expression a×x + b×y is equal to h multiplied by some whole number. That is exactly what it means for something to be 'a multiple of h'. And since h is HCF(a,b), this proves that a×x + b×y is ALWAYS a multiple of HCF(a,b), no matter which integers are chosen for x and y.",
      "Check this conclusion on the running example too, where a=12, b=18, h=6, p=2, q=3:\n12x + 18y =\n6×2×x + 6×3×y =\n6×(2x + 3y).\nThis is always 6 times a whole number, so 12x + 18y is always divisible by 6, whichever integers x and y turn out to be.",
      "The consequence: if c is not a multiple of HCF(a,b), then a×x + b×y = c can never hold for any integers x and y, because the left side is forced to be a multiple of HCF(a,b) by the argument just given, and the right side, c, isn't. Remarkably, the reverse is also guaranteed to be true (a result called Bezout's identity): whenever HCF(a,b) DOES divide c, at least one integer solution definitely exists. So this single divisibility check is a complete yes/no test, not just a way to rule things out.",
      "In practice: find HCF(a,b) (by listing factors, or using the Euclidean algorithm for bigger numbers), then check whether it divides c exactly.",
      "This test only tells you WHETHER a solution exists, not what it actually is - finding the actual x and y values is the job of section 2.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Does 6x + 9y = 20 have integer solutions?", steps: [
        "Find HCF(6,9): the common factors of 6 and 9 are 1 and 3, so HCF(6,9) = 3.",
        "Check whether 3 divides 20 exactly: 20 ÷ 3 = 6.666..., not a whole number.",
        "Since HCF(6,9) does not divide 20, no integer solution can exist.",
      ], answer: "No solution exists" },
      { q: "Does 4x + 6y = 10 have integer solutions (a case where the answer is yes)?", steps: [
        "Find HCF(4,6): the common factors of 4 and 6 are 1 and 2, so HCF(4,6) = 2.",
        "Check whether 2 divides 10 exactly: 10 ÷ 2 = 5, a whole number.",
        "Since HCF(4,6) divides 10, an integer solution must exist.",
        "Confirm with an actual pair: x=1, y=1 gives:\n4(1) + 6(1) =\n4 + 6 =\n10.\nIt works.",
      ], answer: "Yes - for example x=1, y=1" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Does 8x + 12y = 30 have integer solutions?", answer: "No. HCF(8,12) = 4, and 30 ÷ 4 = 7.5, not a whole number, so 4 does not divide 30 exactly - no integer solution can exist." } },
    { h: "2. Finding solutions by substitution", body: [
      "Once you know a solution exists, the most reliable method is systematic trial. For 3x + 4y = 25, isolate x: x = (25 - 4y)/3, and simply test small whole-number values of y, starting from 0, until the right-hand side comes out as a whole number. Try y=0:\nx =\n25/3 =\n8.33...,\nnot whole - reject it. Try y=1:\nx =\n(25-4)/3 =\n21/3 =\n7,\na whole number - success.",
      "In general, to solve ax + by = c, isolate one variable, x = (c - by)/a, and test y = 0, 1, 2, 3... in turn (increasing, or trying small values in both directions if negative numbers are allowed) until the numerator divides exactly by a.",
      "Once one solution is found, there's a neat trick for generating others: if (x, y) is a solution, then (x+b, y-a) is also a solution, since:\na(x+b) + b(y-a) =\nax + ab + by - ab =\nax + by,\ncompletely unchanged. This shows a single found solution is really just one member of an entire family.",
      "Always finish by substituting your (x, y) pair back into the ORIGINAL equation to confirm it really works, and search systematically (increasing y from 0) rather than guessing at random, so you don't miss the first valid pair or waste time re-testing values.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find a positive integer solution to 3x + 4y = 25.", steps: [
        "Isolate x: x = (25 - 4y) / 3.",
        "Try y=0:\nx =\n25/3 =\n8.33...,\nnot a whole number - reject.",
        "Try y=1:\nx =\n(25-4)/3 =\n21/3 =\n7,\na whole number - success.",
        "Check:\n3(7) + 4(1) =\n21 + 4 =\n25.\nCorrect.",
      ], answer: "x=7, y=1" },
      { q: "Find a positive integer solution to 5x + 8y = 61 (a harder version needing more trials).", steps: [
        "Isolate x: x = (61 - 8y) / 5.",
        "Try y=1:\nx =\n(61-8)/5 =\n53/5 =\n10.6,\nnot a whole number - reject.",
        "Try y=2:\nx =\n(61-16)/5 =\n45/5 =\n9,\na whole number - success.",
        "Check:\n5(9) + 8(2) =\n45 + 16 =\n61.\nCorrect.",
      ], answer: "x=9, y=2" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find a positive integer solution to 4x + 9y = 46.", answer: "x=7, y=2. Trying y=1 gives:\nx =\n(46-9)/4 =\n9.25\n(reject); trying y=2 gives:\nx =\n(46-18)/4 =\n7,\na whole number. Check:\n4(7)+9(2) =\n28+18 =\n46." } },
    { h: "3. Digit puzzles as Diophantine equations", body: [
      "Start with what a two-digit number actually means. The number 47 is really 10×4 + 7 - the tens digit contributes 10 times its face value, and the units digit contributes just its face value. Any two-digit number can be written as 10t + u, where t is the tens digit and u is the units digit.",
      "That means clues about 'digit sum' or 'digit difference' are really just clues about t and u, and turn straight into ordinary linear equations - exactly like any other pair of simultaneous equations, just with the extra restriction that t and u must be whole numbers from 0 to 9 (and t can't be 0, or it wouldn't be a two-digit number).",
      "Solve them exactly as you would any simultaneous equations: for instance, if you have both t+u and t-u, adding the two equations cancels u and leaves 2t, which can be divided down to find t directly, then u follows by substitution.",
      "Always check the final digits actually make sense (each between 0 and 9, with the tens digit not zero) - the algebra by itself doesn't know these are digits, only that they're numbers satisfying two equations, so a result outside 0-9 would mean no valid two-digit number fits the clues.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A two-digit number has digit sum 9 and its tens digit is 3 more than its units digit. Find it.", steps: [
        "Let the tens digit be t and the units digit be u. The number is 10t+u.",
        "Digit sum clue: t + u = 9.",
        "Digit difference clue: t - u = 3.",
        "Add the two equations to eliminate u: (t+u) + (t-u) = 9+3, giving:\n2t =\n12, so\nt =\n6.",
        "Substitute back:\n6 + u =\n9, so\nu =\n3.",
        "Both digits are valid (0-9, tens digit not 0), so the number is 63.",
      ], answer: "63" },
      { q: "A two-digit number has digit sum 14 and its tens digit is 4 more than its units digit (a harder version with bigger digits). Find it.", steps: [
        "Let the tens digit be t and the units digit be u.",
        "Digit sum clue: t + u = 14.",
        "Digit difference clue: t - u = 4.",
        "Add the two equations:\n2t =\n18, so\nt =\n9.",
        "Substitute back:\n9 + u =\n14, so\nu =\n5.",
        "Both digits are valid, so the number is 95.",
      ], answer: "95" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A two-digit number has digit sum 12 and its tens digit is 2 more than its units digit. Find it.", answer: "75. Adding t+u=12 and t-u=2 gives:\n2t =\n14, so\nt =\n7,\nthen u=5. Both digits are valid, giving the number 75." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.optimisationAndExtremal = {
  title: "Optimisation: finding the best possible value",
  minutes: 16,
  order: 18,
  prereq: ["algebraicManipulation", "quadratics", { module: "junior", key: "productOpt" }],
  intro: "Optimisation asks 'what's the biggest (or smallest) this can possibly be?' The single most useful rule for a fixed perimeter or fixed sum: the extreme value happens when the parts are as EQUAL as possible. This lesson proves that rule with real numbers rather than just stating it, then shows what happens when a constraint changes the rules of the game.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Fixed sum, maximise the product", body: [
      "Suppose two positive whole numbers must add up to 20. Try several different splits and compare their products side by side: 10+10=20, product 10×10=100. 9+11=20, product 9×11=99. 8+12=20, product 8×12=96. 5+15=20, product 5×15=75. 1+19=20, product 1×19=19. Every row adds to 20, but the products fall further and further away from 100 as the two numbers get further apart. The pattern is unmistakable: the closer together the two numbers are, the bigger the product.",
      "Here's why this always happens, using algebra instead of just a table of examples. Let d stand for 'how far each number has moved away from the equal split of 10' - so d=0 means both numbers are still exactly 10 and 10, d=1 means one number went up by 1 and the other down by 1 (giving 11 and 9), d=5 gives 15 and 5, and so on. Write the two numbers as 10+d and 10-d. Check first that this really does capture every split adding to 20, whatever d is chosen:\n(10+d) + (10-d) =\n10 + d + 10 - d =\n20,\nsince the +d and -d cancel out completely - so this way of writing the two numbers is completely general, not a special case that happens to work.",
      "Now multiply the two numbers together, expanding the brackets term by term (the same bracket expansion covered in algebraicManipulation):\n(10+d)(10-d) =\n10×10 - 10×d + d×10 - d×d =\n100 - 10d + 10d - d² =\n100 - d².\nThe middle two terms, -10d and +10d, are opposites, so they cancel exactly - that's why only the two squared terms survive. Two matching brackets like this, with opposite signs, always collapse this way, leaving just a difference of two squares (also covered in quadratics) - that's the name for this exact pattern.",
      "Since d² is zero when d=0 and gets bigger the further d moves from 0 in either direction (d² can never be negative, whether d itself is positive or negative), 100-d² is largest exactly when d=0 - i.e. when the two numbers are equal. This is the same shape of reasoning as in quadratics: subtracting a squared term that's always zero-or-positive can only ever shrink a number or leave it unchanged, never grow it, so the biggest possible result comes at the one point where that squared term is zero. Moving away from equal in either direction can only subtract more, never less, since d² is never negative.",
      "So for a fixed sum, hunt for the split where the two numbers are equal (or as close to equal as the integers allow). Divide the total by 2: if that gives a whole number, that's your two equal parts. If it doesn't, round to the two whole numbers on either side of it.",
      "Watch out for two traps: the numbers must be positive (0 and 20 gives a product of 0, the worst possible split, even though it looks extreme), and if the question asks for integers, you cannot split an odd total into two equal integers, so you must use the two nearest whole numbers instead - e.g. 15 splits as 7 and 8, not 7.5 and 7.5.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Two positive integers add to 20. What's the greatest possible product?", steps: [
        "20 is even, so it splits into two equal whole numbers: 20÷2 = 10 and 10.",
        "By the equal-split rule just proved, this is the split that maximises the product.",
        "10 × 10 = 100.",
      ], answer: "100" },
      { q: "Two positive integers add to 15. What's the greatest possible product? (15 is odd, so it can't split into two equal integers - what's the best you can do?)", steps: [
        "15÷2 = 7.5, which isn't a whole number, so an exactly-equal split isn't possible with integers.",
        "The equal-split rule says the true maximum (allowing non-integers) would be at 7.5 and 7.5, so the best integer split is the pair closest to that: 7 and 8.",
        "Check both are positive integers adding to 15: 7+8=15. Correct total.",
        "7 × 8 = 56.",
        "Compare with the next-closest split to confirm: 6+9=15, 6×9=54, which is smaller than 56 - confirming 7 and 8 really is the best.",
      ], answer: "56" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Two positive integers add to 13. What's the greatest possible product?", answer: "42. 13÷2=6.5 isn't a whole number, so use the closest integers either side, 6 and 7: 6×7=42 (compare with 5×8=40, which is smaller, confirming 6 and 7 is best)." } },
    { h: "2. Fixed perimeter, maximise the area", body: [
      "A rectangle's perimeter is 2×(length+width). If the perimeter is fixed, then length+width is also fixed (it's just half the perimeter) - which means finding the maximum area is exactly the fixed-sum, maximise-the-product problem from Section 1, just with length and width playing the roles of the two numbers. Check this with a perimeter of 28, so length+width=14: 7+7=14, area 7×7=49. 6+8=14, area 6×8=48. 5+9=14, area 5×9=45. 3+11=14, area 3×11=33. 1+13=14, area 1×13=13. Exactly the same pattern as before: the closer the two sides are to equal, the bigger the area.",
      "This isn't a coincidence needing a separate proof - it's literally the same algebra as Section 1, with length+width (=14 here) playing the role of the fixed sum, and length and width playing the role of the two numbers. Using the same idea as Section 1's d - the distance each side has moved away from the equal split, now 7 either way - write length as 7+d and width as 7-d; these still add to 14 for any d, by the identical cancellation shown in Section 1. Expanding exactly as before:\n(7+d)(7-d) =\n49 - 7d + 7d - d² =\n49 - d².\nThis is largest when d=0, i.e. when length=width and the rectangle is a square - for the same reason as Section 1: d² can never be negative, so subtracting it can never increase the result.",
      "So for a fixed perimeter, halve it to get length+width, then split that as evenly as possible - a square (or the closest integer rectangle to a square) always wins.",
      "Don't halve the perimeter twice. The perimeter is 2(length+width), so length+width is HALF the perimeter, not a quarter. A common slip is dividing by 4, which would only be correct for finding a single side length of an actual square, not the length+width sum.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A rectangle has perimeter 28. What integer side lengths give maximum area?", steps: [
        "Halve the perimeter to get length+width: 28÷2=14.",
        "By the equal-split rule from Section 1, this sum is maximised as a product when split evenly: 14÷2=7 and 7.",
        "Area =\n7×7 =\n49.",
      ], answer: "49 (sides 7 and 7)" },
      { q: "A rectangle has perimeter 50. What integer side lengths give the maximum area, and what is it?", steps: [
        "Halve the perimeter: 50÷2=25.",
        "25 is odd, so it can't split into two equal integers - use the closest whole numbers either side: 12 and 13.",
        "Check they add to 25: 12+13=25. Correct.",
        "Area =\n12×13 =\n156.",
        "Confirm this beats the next-closest split: 11+14=25, so:\narea =\n11×14 =\n154, which is smaller - so 12 and 13 is best.",
      ], answer: "156 (sides 12 and 13)" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A rectangle has perimeter 22. What integer side lengths give the maximum area?", answer: "30 (sides 5 and 6). Half the perimeter is 22÷2=11, which is odd, so the closest integer split is 5 and 6 (5+6=11): 5×6=30, which beats 4×7=28." } },
    { h: "3. Optimisation with a real-world constraint", body: [
      "Every rule so far assumed all sides need building material (fencing, wall, perimeter). Real problems often break that assumption - for example, a rectangular pen built against an existing wall doesn't need fencing along the side that touches the wall. That changes the relationship between the fixed amount of material and the sides, so the make-it-a-square rule can no longer be assumed to still apply - it has to be checked from scratch.",
      "Label the side parallel to the wall (and opposite it) as the length, l, and the two sides running from the wall out to meet it as the width, w. Only 3 sides need fencing: two widths and one length, so the fencing formula is l+2w = (total fencing), not 2(l+w) = (total fencing) as it would be for a fully-fenced rectangle. Because w now appears twice in the formula but l only once, the equal-is-best rule from Sections 1 and 2 - which relied on both sides mattering equally - no longer points to l=w.",
      "With one variable's formula changed, the safest approach at this level is the same table method used in Section 1: fix the total fencing, express l in terms of w, then compute the area for values of w close to a sensible middle guess and see which gives the biggest number.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "40m of fencing is used to build a rectangular pen against an existing wall (the wall forms one side, so no fencing is needed there). What integer width and length maximise the area, and how does this compare with using the same 40m of fencing with no wall at all?", steps: [
        "No-wall case first, for comparison: all four sides need fencing, so:\nlength+width =\n40÷2 =\n20. By the equal-split rule the best is 10+10, giving area 10×10=100.",
        "Wall case: only 2 widths and 1 length need fencing, so:\nl + 2w =\n40, which rearranges to\nl =\n40 - 2w.",
        "Area =\nl × w =\n(40-2w) × w. Try w=10 (the value that worked before):\nl =\n40-20 =\n20,\narea =\n20×10 =\n200.",
        "Test whether moving away from w=10 does better or worse, as in Section 1's table: w=9 gives l=22, area=198. w=11 gives l=18, area=198. w=8 gives l=24, area=192. w=12 gives l=16, area=192.",
        "The areas rise to a peak at w=10 and fall away on both sides, so w=10, l=20 is the maximum - but notice l=20 is DOUBLE w=10, not equal to it, unlike the no-wall case.",
        "Compare the two results: with the wall, the maximum area is 200 m², exactly double the no-wall maximum of 100 m², using the identical 40m of fencing - because the wall provides one side for free, and the optimal shape shifts away from a square towards a rectangle twice as long as it is wide.",
      ], answer: "200 m² (width 10m, length 20m) - double the no-wall maximum of 100 m², with the optimal shape no longer a square because only 3 sides need fencing." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "60m of fencing builds a rectangular pen against a wall (no fencing needed on the wall side). What width and length maximise the area?", answer: "450 m² (width 15m, length 30m - the length is double the width, following the same pattern as the worked example: l+2w=60, and testing values near w=15 confirms it's the peak: w=14 gives area 448, w=16 gives area 448, both less than 450)." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.proofTechniques = {
  title: "Proof Techniques: what actually counts as proof",
  minutes: 15,
  order: 19,
  prereq: ["algebraicProof"],
  intro: "Not every argument that LOOKS convincing is actually a valid proof. This lesson covers the most common ways a proof attempt goes wrong - checking only one example, circular reasoning, and confusing 'a solution exists' with 'this is the only solution' - plus what a genuinely watertight proof looks like.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. One example is never enough", body: [
      "Showing a claim works for n=5 proves nothing about n=6, or any other value.",
      "Here's how convincing - and wrong - a one-example proof can look. Suppose someone claims 'every prime number is odd', and checks it: 3 is odd and prime, 5 is odd and prime, 7 is odd and prime - three examples in a row, all fitting the pattern. It looks proven. But the claim is false, and the worked example below shows exactly where the one-example approach breaks down.",
      "A real proof needs an argument that covers every possible case at once - usually through algebra (see Algebraic Proof) or through a general logical argument that doesn't depend on which specific number you happen to have tried.",
      "The number of examples doesn't matter - checking 3, 30 or 300 cases and finding they all fit is still not a proof, since the very next untested case could be the exception. The only way to be sure is to argue about all cases in one go, or to test literally every case if there are only finitely many (and even then, you must genuinely check every single one, not just most of them).",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Someone claims 'every prime number is odd' and checks n=3, 5, 7 to 'prove' it. Show this proof attempt is wrong.", steps: [
        "Check the claim against the examples given: 3 is prime and odd. 5 is prime and odd. 7 is prime and odd. All three fit, which is why the argument looks convincing.",
        "But three examples only cover three numbers, not all prime numbers - the claim 'every prime is odd' is about every single one of infinitely many primes, so the checked cases are a tiny fraction, and none of them can rule out an exception elsewhere.",
        "Test the smallest prime number of all, which the example happened to skip: 2.",
        "2 is prime (its only factors are 1 and 2), but 2 is even, not odd.",
        "So the claim is false, and the reason the proof missed it is precisely because it only ever checked a few chosen examples instead of arguing about every prime.",
      ], answer: "False - 2 is a prime number that is even, disproving the claim. The one-example check missed it because checking a few cases can never rule out an exception that wasn't tried." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A claim states 'every multiple of 6 is even', and someone 'proves' it by checking 6, 12, 18, 24 - all even. Explain in one or two sentences why this checking process is not a valid general proof, even though the claim happens to be true.", answer: "Checking four examples only tells you about those four multiples, not all infinitely many - even though this particular claim is true, checking specific cases can never rule out a future exception. A proper proof notes that any multiple of 6 can be written as 6k = 2×(3k), which is 2 times a whole number for every integer k, so it's even for every single case at once, not just the four tested." } },
    { h: "2. Circular reasoning", body: [
      "A circular argument - the mistake this section is named after, 'circular reasoning' - secretly assumes the very thing it's trying to prove, somewhere in the middle of the proof.",
      "It always sounds plausible on a first read, which is exactly what makes it dangerous - the fault is usually hidden in the middle of a chain of steps that all sound reasonable on their own.",
      "To spot one, work through the argument step by step and ask, for every single step: does this step rely only on things already established (definitions, earlier proven facts, or the given information), or does it quietly lean on the very conclusion we're trying to reach? The moment a step needs the conclusion to already be true, the argument has gone circular.",
      "Circular arguments are especially easy to write by accident when a claim feels obviously true - it's tempting to use the claim itself as a stepping stone without noticing, because it doesn't feel like an assumption, it feels like common sense.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Spot the flaw in this argument: 'Prove that the angles of a triangle add up to 180°. We know a straight line is 180°. Since the three angles of a triangle can be rearranged to form a straight line, they must add up to 180°, because a triangle's angles always add up to 180°.'", steps: [
        "Read the argument looking for what it is trying to establish: that the three angles of a triangle sum to 180°.",
        "Now check the justification given for the key step (that the three angles can be rearranged to form a straight line): the argument justifies this by saying 'because a triangle's angles always add up to 180°'.",
        "That justification is exactly the statement the argument set out to prove in the first place - the conclusion has been used as its own reason.",
        "This is circular: strip out the phrase that sounds like a justification, and there is no actual reasoning left connecting 'rearranged into a straight line' to '180°' other than assuming the answer.",
        "A genuine proof of this fact instead uses parallel lines and alternate angles to show the rearrangement is forced by geometry, never assuming the 180° total along the way.",
      ], answer: "Circular - the argument justifies 'the angles form a straight line' by re-asserting 'a triangle's angles add up to 180°', which is the very thing being proved. A valid proof must reach 180° using facts established beforehand, such as angle facts on parallel lines, not the conclusion itself." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Spot the circular step: 'Every even number greater than 2 can be written as the sum of two primes, because every even number greater than 2 is a sum of two primes.'", answer: "Circular - the second half of the sentence simply restates the claim itself as its own justification, rather than giving an independent reason. (This particular claim, Goldbach's Conjecture, is actually still unproven, which is exactly why no one is allowed to just assert it as a 'because'.)" } },
    { h: "3. Existence vs uniqueness", body: [
      "Two different questions get mixed up more often than you'd think: 'DOES a solution exist?' (existence) and 'Is this THE ONLY solution, or the best one?' (uniqueness). Existence asks whether at least one thing satisfying some condition can be found at all. Uniqueness asks whether there is exactly one such thing - or, when the condition is 'the biggest/best', whether exactly one arrangement reaches that top value, with nothing else tying or beating it.",
      "Finding ONE arrangement that gives a large value proves existence - it shows that value is achievable. It says nothing about uniqueness or maximality: whether that's the ONLY good arrangement, or the BEST one, needs a separate, further argument.",
      "This is exactly the trap the optimisation examples avoided by checking neighbouring splits every time. Finding that one split of a fixed sum gives a big product only proves existence for that value - it says nothing about whether some other split does even better (or ties it), unless every other option has also been ruled out.",
      "So 'a solution exists' and 'this is the best, or the only, solution' are two separate claims, and both need proving separately. Existence needs just one example. Showing something is the maximum - or the unique best answer - needs either a check of every alternative, or a general argument (like the difference-of-squares argument in the Optimisation lesson) that rules all of them out at once.",
      "Watch for the phrase 'I found a value that works, so it must be the best' - finding a value only ever establishes existence. It says nothing about uniqueness or optimality, and the gap between those claims is exactly where invalid proofs hide.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Someone claims: 'For two positive integers adding to 20, the greatest possible product is 96, because 8×12=96 is a large product and I can't think of a bigger split.' Show why this reasoning is flawed, without redoing the full proof.", steps: [
        "Identify the claim actually being made: not just that 96 is achievable (true - 8+12=20 and 8×12=96), but that 96 is the greatest possible product.",
        "'I can't think of a bigger one' is not a check of every other split - it's just a report of which splits were or weren't considered.",
        "Test a split the claim didn't consider: 10+10=20, and 10×10=100.",
        "100 is bigger than 96, so the claim that 96 is the maximum is false - only the weaker claim, that 96 is achievable, was actually true.",
      ], answer: "False - 10×10=100 beats 96, so 96 is only a possible product, not the maximum. Finding one large value never proves it's the biggest without checking that nothing else beats it." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A rectangle has perimeter 24. Someone says 'a 4 by 8 rectangle has area 32, so 32 must be the maximum area.' Is this reasoning valid? Find the actual maximum to check.", answer: "No - checking one rectangle only shows 32 is achievable, not that it's the biggest. Half the perimeter is 24÷2=12, so the equal split 6 and 6 gives area:\n6×6 =\n36,\nwhich beats 32. The maximum is 36, not 32." } },
    { h: "4. Counterexamples", body: [
      "To disprove a claim that something is ALWAYS true, you only need ONE counterexample - a single case where it fails.",
      "This is the mirror image of Section 1's lesson: one example is never enough to prove a general claim, but one example is always enough to disprove one, because 'always true' is broken by even a single failure, however rare.",
      "This is much easier than proving something always holds, which needs a fully general argument covering every case - finding a counterexample only needs you to find one single failing case.",
      "The practical approach is to test small cases first, in order, since counterexamples to well-known nearly-true patterns often show up quickly, as the worked example below demonstrates.",
      "A counterexample must satisfy every condition in the original claim. If the claim is about positive integers, your counterexample must be a positive integer too - finding a failure using a negative number or a fraction doesn't count.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Disprove the claim 'n² + n + 1 is always prime for positive integers n' by finding the smallest counterexample.", steps: [
        "n=1: 1+1+1=3, prime.",
        "n=2: 4+2+1=7, prime.",
        "n=3: 9+3+1=13, prime.",
        "n=4:\n16+4+1 =\n21 =\n3×7, NOT prime.",
      ], answer: "n=4 disproves the claim, since 21 is not prime." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Disprove the claim 'all odd numbers greater than 1 are prime' by finding the smallest counterexample.", answer: "9. Check in order: 3 is prime, 5 is prime, 7 is prime, but 9 = 3×3, which is not prime since it has a factor other than 1 and itself. So 9 is the smallest counterexample." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.speedAndRelativeMotion = {
  title: "Speed & Relative Motion",
  minutes: 17,
  order: 20,
  prereq: [{ module: "junior", key: "multiRate" }, "algebraicManipulation"],
  intro: "Speed = distance ÷ time is the foundation, but the harder questions involve TWO moving objects at once - chasing each other, or moving toward each other. The key idea both times is the same: combine the two objects' separate speeds into one single number describing how fast the gap between them is changing. That combined number gets two different names depending on the situation - the closing speed when the two objects move toward each other (Section 2), and the relative speed when one is chasing the other (Section 3). Both sections build up to those names from the concrete numbers first, so don't worry about memorising the terms yet.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. The basic formula, rearranged three ways", body: [
      "Speed = distance ÷ time. This one formula secretly contains three formulas, depending on which quantity is unknown - and confusing which rearrangement to use is one of the most common exam mistakes.",
      "Check this with real numbers first. A car travels 100 miles in 2 hours, so its speed is 100÷2=50mph. Now flip the question round: if you're told the car travels at 50mph for 2 hours, how far does it go? Common sense says:\ndistance =\nspeed × time =\n50×2 =\n100 miles, which matches. And if you're told it travelled 100 miles at 50mph, how long did it take?\ntime =\ndistance ÷ speed =\n100÷50 =\n2 hours, matches again. All three versions describe the exact same underlying relationship, just rearranged to solve for whichever quantity is missing.",
      "This works because speed×time=distance is just speed=distance÷time with both sides multiplied by time - the same kind of equation rearranging covered in algebraicManipulation: doing the same operation to both sides keeps the equation balanced, so rearranging like this never changes what it means, only which letter it's solved for.",
      "A quick way to keep all three straight: distance is always on its own (distance=speed×time, or speed=distance÷time, or time=distance÷speed) - distance never gets divided BY something else, it only gets divided INTO something else, or multiplied out.",
      "The most common slip is dividing the wrong way round, e.g. writing time=speed÷distance. If a rearrangement gives a 'time' that's smaller for a longer distance, or a 'speed' that increases as time increases, that's the signal something's flipped.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A cyclist travels 45km in 3 hours at a constant speed. (a) Find the speed. (b) Using that speed, find how far she'd travel in 5 hours. (c) Using that speed, find how long it would take her to travel 60km.", steps: [
        "(a) Speed =\ndistance÷time =\n45÷3 =\n15km/h.",
        "(b) Distance =\nspeed×time =\n15×5 =\n75km.",
        "(c) Time =\ndistance÷speed =\n60÷15 =\n4 hours.",
        "Sanity check each answer against common sense: going for longer (5h vs 3h) at the same speed should cover more ground (75km > 45km) - correct. Covering a bit more distance (60km vs 45km) at the same speed should take a bit longer (4h > 3h) - correct.",
      ], answer: "(a) 15km/h (b) 75km (c) 4 hours" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A train travels at 80km/h for 3.5 hours. How far does it travel, and how long would it take to cover 200km at the same speed?", answer: "280km, and 2.5 hours to cover 200km.\nDistance =\nspeed×time =\n80×3.5 =\n280;\ntime =\ndistance÷speed =\n200÷80 =\n2.5." } },
    { h: "2. Moving toward each other: add the speeds", body: [
      "Picture two trains 300km apart, travelling toward each other, one at 60km/h and the other at 90km/h. In the first hour, the 60km/h train covers 60km toward the other, and the 90km/h train covers 90km toward the first. Together, the gap between them has shrunk by 60+90=150km in that one hour - even though neither train travelled 150km itself, the distance between them dropped by that much, because both trains' movement works to close the same gap.",
      "This works for any two speeds moving toward each other: in one hour, object A eats up a distance equal to A's speed worth of the gap, and object B eats up a distance equal to B's speed worth of the same gap, from the other end. Since both are shrinking the same single gap simultaneously, the total shrink per hour is simply their speeds added together - this combined value is called the closing speed.",
      "Once you have the closing speed, treat the whole problem as one object closing a single gap: time to meet = (starting distance) ÷ (sum of the two speeds).",
      "Don't average the two speeds - averaging (75km/h here) would be the speed of a single object needing to cover the whole 300km alone, not the combined rate at which two objects shrink the gap between them. Averaging always under-counts here, since it ignores that both objects are moving at once.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Two trains, 300km apart, travel toward each other at 60km/h and 90km/h. How long until they meet?", steps: [
        "Combined closing speed =\n60+90 =\n150 km/h (the gap shrinks by 150km every hour, as shown above).",
        "Time =\ndistance ÷ closing speed =\n300 ÷ 150 =\n2 hours.",
      ], answer: "2 hours" },
      { q: "Two friends start 96km apart and walk toward each other, meeting after 2.4 hours. One walks at 15km/h. How fast does the other walk?", steps: [
        "Together, they must close the whole 96km gap in 2.4 hours, so:\ntheir combined closing speed =\n96÷2.4 =\n40km/h.",
        "The closing speed is the SUM of both walking speeds, so: 15 + (other speed) = 40.",
        "Other speed =\n40 - 15 =\n25km/h.",
        "Check: combined speed 15+25=40km/h, and 40×2.4=96km, matching the original gap - correct.",
      ], answer: "25km/h" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Two cyclists start 117km apart and cycle toward each other at 26km/h and 13km/h. How long until they meet?", answer: "3 hours.\nClosing speed =\n26+13 =\n39km/h,\ntime =\n117÷39 =\n3 hours." } },
    { h: "3. Chasing: subtract the speeds", body: [
      "Now picture a car at 70km/h chasing another car at 50km/h, both travelling the same direction, starting 40km apart. In one hour, the front car moves 50km further away along the road, and the chasing car moves 70km further along the same road. Since both distances are measured along the same direction, the gap between them shrinks by the difference: 70-50=20km in that hour, not by 70+50 - the front car is still moving away as it's being chased, so its own speed works against the chaser, not with it.",
      "This is the opposite situation to Section 2: there, both speeds worked together to shrink the gap, so they added. Here, one speed shrinks the gap (the chaser's) while the other speed tries to keep rebuilding it (the car in front pulling further away), so the net shrink per hour is the difference between the two speeds, called the relative speed.",
      "Time to catch up = (starting gap) ÷ (difference between the two speeds) - always subtract the slower speed from the faster one, so the answer comes out positive.",
      "If the chasing object's speed isn't actually bigger than the one it's chasing, it will never catch up at all - always check that the chaser's speed exceeds the other's before doing the subtraction, otherwise the gap grows forever instead of closing.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A car at 70km/h chases another at 50km/h, starting 40km behind. How long to catch up?", steps: [
        "Relative speed =\n70-50 =\n20 km/h (the gap shrinks by 20km every hour, as shown above).",
        "Time =\ngap ÷ relative speed =\n40 ÷ 20 =\n2 hours.",
      ], answer: "2 hours" },
      { q: "A cyclist 15km ahead is caught after 3 hours by a second cyclist riding at 22km/h. How fast was the first cyclist riding?", steps: [
        "The 15km gap is closed entirely in 3 hours, so:\nthe relative speed =\n15÷3 =\n5km/h.",
        "Relative speed is the DIFFERENCE between the two speeds: 22 - (first cyclist's speed) = 5.",
        "First cyclist's speed =\n22 - 5 =\n17km/h.",
        "Check: relative speed 22-17=5km/h, and 5×3=15km, matching the original gap - correct.",
      ], answer: "17km/h" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A cheetah runs at 100km/h chasing a gazelle running at 80km/h, starting 0.5km (500m) behind. How long until the cheetah catches up?", answer: "0.025 hours, which is 1.5 minutes (90 seconds).\nRelative speed =\n100-80 =\n20km/h;\ntime =\n0.5÷20 =\n0.025 hours =\n90 seconds." } },
    { h: "4. Average speed over a whole journey", body: [
      "Average speed is ALWAYS total distance ÷ total time - never the average of the individual speeds, since more time is spent at the slower speed.",
      "Check this with real numbers. Suppose a journey is 120 miles total, split into two equal 60-mile halves: the first half driven at 30mph, the second at 60mph. Naive instinct says average the speeds: (30+60)÷2=45mph. But actually work out the time for each half:\nfirst half takes 60÷30 =\n2 hours,\nsecond half takes 60÷60 =\n1 hour,\nso the whole 120 miles takes 2+1 =\n3 hours in total.\nTrue average speed =\ntotal distance÷total time =\n120÷3 =\n40mph, not 45mph.",
      "The naive average of the two speeds silently assumes equal time in each half, but here the equal-distance halves actually took different amounts of time (2 hours at the slow speed, only 1 hour at the fast speed) - more of the total time was spent at the slower speed, which drags the true average down below the halfway point between 30 and 60.",
      "Whenever a journey is split into stages, work out the distance covered and time taken for each stage separately, add up both totals, then divide - never average the speeds directly unless you've separately confirmed equal time (not equal distance) was spent at each speed.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A journey is 120 miles: the first 60 miles at 30mph, the second 60 miles at 60mph. Find the true average speed for the whole journey, and compare it with simply averaging 30 and 60.", steps: [
        "Naive average of the two speeds: (30+60)÷2 = 45mph.",
        "Time for the first half: 60÷30 = 2 hours.",
        "Time for the second half: 60÷60 = 1 hour.",
        "Total distance =\n60+60 =\n120 miles.\nTotal time =\n2+1 =\n3 hours.",
        "True average speed =\ntotal distance ÷ total time =\n120÷3 =\n40mph.",
        "40mph (true) is less than 45mph (naive average) - because twice as much time (2 hours vs 1 hour) was spent travelling at the slower 30mph, pulling the true average down toward 30 more than toward 60.",
      ], answer: "40mph (not 45mph) - more time was spent at the slower speed, so the true average sits closer to 30mph than a simple average of the two speeds would suggest." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A cyclist rides 40km at 20km/h, then a further 40km at 40km/h. Find her average speed for the whole 80km.", answer: "80÷3 ≈ 26.7km/h (not 30km/h).\nTime for the first 40km =\n40÷20 =\n2 hours;\ntime for the second 40km =\n40÷40 =\n1 hour;\ntotal time = 3 hours;\naverage speed =\n80÷3 ≈\n26.7km/h - closer to 20km/h since twice as long was spent at that slower speed." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.estimationAndBounds = {
  title: "Estimation & Bounds: how wrong could a rounded number be?",
  minutes: 15,
  order: 21,
  prereq: [{ module: "primary", key: "roundingEstimate" }, { module: "junior", key: "estimation" }],
  intro: "Every rounded measurement hides a small range of possible true values. This lesson covers finding upper and lower bounds, using them to bound a calculation, and the standard form / significant-figure skills used for quick estimation.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Upper and lower bounds", body: [
      "A measurement rounded to the nearest whole number could really be anywhere within 0.5 either side. Rounded to the nearest 10, the true value is within 5 either side, and so on - half of whatever unit it was rounded to.",
      "Check why half a unit either side is exactly right, not just a rule of thumb. If a length rounds to 24cm to the nearest whole cm, then 24.4cm would also round to 24 (since 24.4 is closer to 24 than to 25), but 24.6cm would round UP to 25 instead. The rounding boundary sits exactly halfway between 24 and 25, at 24.5 - so the largest value that still rounds down to 24 is anything up to (but not including) 24.5. The same logic going down means anything from 23.5 upward rounds to 24. That's why the true value can be anywhere from 23.5 up to just under 24.5: half a unit either side of the rounded value.",
      "The same idea applies to any rounding unit: rounding to the nearest 10 means the true value is within 5 either side (half of 10); rounding to the nearest 0.1 means within 0.05 either side (half of 0.1). Always take half of whatever unit the number was rounded to.",
      "Lower bound = rounded value minus half the unit. Upper bound = rounded value plus half the unit.",
      "The upper bound is technically not quite reachable (24.5cm would round UP to 25, not down to 24), but at GCSE/JMC level it's standard to still write the upper bound as exactly 24.5 - just be aware the true value is always strictly less than the upper bound, even though it can equal the lower bound.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A length is given as 24cm, to the nearest cm. Find the upper and lower bounds.", steps: [
        "The length was rounded to the nearest whole cm, so half a unit = 0.5cm either side.",
        "Lower bound =\n24 - 0.5 =\n23.5.",
        "Upper bound =\n24 + 0.5 =\n24.5.",
      ], answer: "23.5cm to 24.5cm" },
      { q: "A crowd size is reported as 3,400 people, to the nearest 100. Find the upper and lower bounds.", steps: [
        "The number was rounded to the nearest 100, so:\nhalf a unit here =\n100÷2 =\n50.",
        "Lower bound =\n3400 - 50 =\n3350.",
        "Upper bound =\n3400 + 50 =\n3450.",
        "Check the boundary logic: 3350 rounds up to 3400, while 3449 still rounds down to 3400, but 3450 would round up to 3500 - confirming the range 3350 to 3450.",
      ], answer: "3350 to 3450 people" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A journey distance is given as 60km, to the nearest 10km. Find the upper and lower bounds.", answer: "55km to 65km. Rounded to the nearest 10, half a unit = 5, so:\nlower bound =\n60-5 =\n55,\nupper bound =\n60+5 =\n65." } },
    { h: "2. Bounding a calculation", body: [
      "Let a and b stand for any two rounded measurements, each already given its own upper and lower bound the way Section 1 describes. To find the maximum possible result of adding them, a+b, add their UPPER bounds. For the maximum of a subtraction, a−b, don't pair upper with upper - instead use the upper bound of a and the LOWER bound of b, since making b as small as possible makes the gap as large as possible.",
      "Check the addition rule with real numbers. Suppose a=24cm (bounds 23.5 to 24.5) and b=10cm to the nearest cm (bounds 9.5 to 10.5). The biggest possible value of a+b uses the biggest possible a AND the biggest possible b at the same time: 24.5+10.5=35. Using anything smaller for either one can only make the total smaller, never bigger - so the true maximum of a+b is exactly (upper bound of a)+(upper bound of b).",
      "Subtraction behaves differently, because a bigger b makes a-b SMALLER, not bigger. To make a-b as large as possible, you want a as big as possible (upper bound of a) AND b as small as possible (lower bound of b) at the same time - taking as much as possible while subtracting as little as possible gives the biggest possible gap. So maximum of (a-b) = (upper bound of a) - (lower bound of b).",
      "By the same logic, the SMALLEST possible value of a-b uses the smallest possible a and the largest possible b: minimum of (a-b) = (lower bound of a) - (upper bound of b).",
      "It's tempting to always pair upper with upper, but that's only correct for addition (and multiplication of positives). For subtraction (and division), the maximum mixes the upper bound of one measurement with the LOWER bound of the other - work out which combination genuinely gives the biggest number before calculating, rather than pattern-matching.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A plank has length a=150cm (to the nearest cm) and a piece of length b=35cm (to the nearest cm) is cut from it. Find the maximum possible length of the remaining plank, a−b.", steps: [
        "Find the bounds of a: rounded to the nearest cm, half a unit=0.5, so a is between 149.5 and 150.5.",
        "Find the bounds of b: likewise, b is between 34.5 and 35.5.",
        "To make a−b as large as possible, start with as much plank as possible (upper bound of a=150.5) and cut away as little as possible (lower bound of b=34.5) - any other combination either starts with less plank or cuts away more, both of which shrink the remainder.",
        "Maximum of a−b =\n150.5 − 34.5 =\n116.",
      ], answer: "116cm (using the upper bound of a, 150.5cm, minus the lower bound of b, 34.5cm)" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "x=80 (to the nearest 10) and y=12 (to the nearest 1). Find the minimum possible value of x−y.", answer: "62.5. Minimum of x−y uses the smallest possible x (lower bound 75, since x is rounded to the nearest 10, half unit=5) and the largest possible y (upper bound 12.5, half unit=0.5): 75−12.5=62.5." } },
    { h: "3. Standard form and estimation", body: [
      "Standard form writes any number as a × 10ⁿ, where a is a number with only one non-zero digit before the decimal point, so 1≤a<10 (call this the digit part), and n is a whole number - positive, negative, or zero - saying how many places the decimal point has shifted (call this the power part). Standard form makes very large or small numbers easy to estimate with: round each number to 1 significant figure first, then multiply the digit parts together and add the power parts together.",
      "Check why rounding each number separately before multiplying still gives a sensible estimate. 412×58 actually equals 23,896. Rounding each factor to 1 significant figure first, 412→400 and 58→60, then multiplying the digit parts, 400×60=24,000, is very close to the real answer, and vastly easier to calculate by hand or in your head.",
      "This works well WITH standard form because standard form splits a number into a digit part (between 1 and 10) and a power part (a power of 10) - e.g. 400 = 4×10², 60=6×10¹. Multiplying two numbers in standard form means multiplying the digit parts together and adding the powers of ten:\n4×6 =\n24 (the digit parts), and\n10²×10¹ =\n10³ (the power parts), giving\n24×10³ =\n24,000, matching the estimate exactly.",
      "Rounding EACH number separately before multiplying is safe, but rounding the ANSWER at the end to the same number of significant figures as the least-precise input is what makes the estimate meaningful - don't report an estimate to more significant figures than the original rounding actually supports.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Estimate 412 × 58 by rounding to 1 significant figure first.", steps: [
        "412 rounds to 400 (1 significant figure). 58 rounds to 60 (1 significant figure).",
        "400 × 60 = 24,000.",
      ], answer: "≈ 24,000 (actual value is 23,896)" },
      { q: "Estimate 6,150,000 ÷ 305 by rounding to 1 significant figure and using standard form.", steps: [
        "Round each number to 1 significant figure: 6,150,000 → 6,000,000. 305 → 300.",
        "Write both in standard form: 6,000,000 = 6×10⁶. 300 = 3×10².",
        "Divide the simple digit parts: 6÷3=2. Subtract the powers of ten (since dividing subtracts indices): 10⁶÷10²=10⁴.",
        "Combine: 2×10⁴ = 20,000.",
      ], answer: "≈ 20,000 (actual value is about 20,164)" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Estimate 0.00089 × 5,200 by rounding to 1 significant figure first.", answer: "≈4.5 (actual value is about 4.628). Rounding: 0.00089→0.0009=9×10⁻⁴, 5200→5000=5×10³; multiply digit parts:\n9×5 =\n45, add powers:\n10⁻⁴×10³ =\n10⁻¹, giving\n45×10⁻¹ =\n4.5." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.coordinateGeometry = {
  title: "Coordinate Geometry: lines on a grid",
  minutes: 14,
  order: 22,
  prereq: [{ module: "junior", key: "coordGeom" }, "simultaneousEquations"],
  intro: "Every straight line has a gradient (how steep it is) and an equation (y = mx + c) that describes every point on it. This lesson covers finding the gradient and midpoint between two points, writing a line's equation, and the special relationship between gradients of parallel and perpendicular lines.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Gradient between two points", body: [
      "A point on a grid is written as a pair of coordinates (x, y): x tells you how far across from the origin, y tells you how far up. To talk about the gradient between two DIFFERENT points, it helps to give them labels so they don't get muddled up. Call the first point's coordinates x₁ and y₁ (say 'x-one' and 'y-one'), and the second point's coordinates x₂ and y₂ ('x-two' and 'y-two'). The little subscript numbers don't mean anything mathematically - they're just labels for 'first point' and 'second point', nothing more.",
      "The gradient measures steepness: how many units up (or down) the line goes for every unit you move across. It's found from 'change in y' divided by 'change in x'. The change in y is how far you moved vertically going from the first point to the second, which is y₂ − y₁. The change in x is how far you moved horizontally, x₂ − x₁. So:\ngradient =\n(change in y) ÷ (change in x) =\n(y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁).",
      "Try this on a concrete pair of points: (1, 2) and (4, 8). Label them so x₁=1, y₁=2, x₂=4, y₂=8. Change in y = y₂ − y₁ =\n8 − 2 =\n6. Change in x = x₂ − x₁ =\n4 − 1 =\n3. So the gradient =\n6 ÷ 3 =\n2 - the line climbs 2 units up for every 1 unit across.",
      "It doesn't matter which point you call 'first' and which you call 'second', as long as you're consistent - swapping them flips the sign of both the change in y and the change in x, and those two minus signs cancel out, giving the exact same gradient either way.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find the gradient of the line through (1, 2) and (4, 8).", steps: [
        "Label the points: x₁=1, y₁=2, x₂=4, y₂=8.",
        "Change in y = y₂ − y₁ =\n8 − 2 =\n6.",
        "Change in x = x₂ − x₁ =\n4 − 1 =\n3.",
        "Gradient =\n6 ÷ 3 =\n2.",
      ], answer: "2" },
      { q: "Find the gradient of the line through (2, 3) and (5, −1) (a harder version with a negative, fractional result).", steps: [
        "Label the points: x₁=2, y₁=3, x₂=5, y₂=−1.",
        "Change in y = y₂ − y₁ =\n−1 − 3 =\n−4.",
        "Change in x = x₂ − x₁ =\n5 − 2 =\n3.",
        "Gradient =\n−4 ÷ 3 =\n−4/3.",
      ], answer: "−4/3" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the gradient through (0, 5) and (2, 1).", answer: "−2, since change in y = 1 − 5 = −4, change in x = 2 − 0 = 2, and −4 ÷ 2 = −2." } },
    { h: "2. Midpoint of two points", body: [
      "The midpoint is the single point sitting exactly halfway between two other points - the same distance from each. You find it by averaging the two x-coordinates to get the midpoint's x, and separately averaging the two y-coordinates to get the midpoint's y:\nmidpoint =\n((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2).",
      "Check this makes sense on a simple case first: the midpoint of (2, 3) and (8, 7). Midpoint x = (2+8)/2 =\n10/2 =\n5. Midpoint y = (3+7)/2 =\n10/2 =\n5. So the midpoint is (5, 5) - exactly halfway between x=2 and x=8, and exactly halfway between y=3 and y=7.",
      "Negative coordinates work exactly the same way - just be careful adding a negative number, since adding a negative is the same as subtracting.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find the midpoint of (2, 3) and (8, 7).", steps: [
        "Midpoint x = (2+8)/2 =\n10/2 =\n5.",
        "Midpoint y = (3+7)/2 =\n10/2 =\n5.",
      ], answer: "(5, 5)" },
      { q: "Find the midpoint of (−3, 4) and (6, −9) (a harder version with negative coordinates).", steps: [
        "Midpoint x = (−3+6)/2 =\n3/2 =\n1.5.",
        "Midpoint y = (4+(−9))/2 =\n−5/2 =\n−2.5.",
      ], answer: "(1.5, −2.5)" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the midpoint of (7, −2) and (−1, 10).", answer: "(3, 4), since midpoint x = (7+(−1))/2 = 3 and midpoint y = (−2+10)/2 = 4." } },
    { h: "3. Equation of a line: y = mx + c", body: [
      "Every straight line (that isn't vertical) can be written in the form y = mx + c. Here m is the gradient (exactly the same idea as in section 1 - how steep the line is), and c is the y-intercept: the y-value where the line crosses the vertical axis, which is the point where x=0.",
      "If you already know the gradient m and just ONE point (x, y) that lies on the line, you can find c: substitute the gradient and the point's coordinates into y = mx + c, and the only unknown left is c, so solve for it.",
      "For example, a line has gradient 3 and passes through (2, 11). Substitute m=3, x=2, y=11 into y = mx + c:\n11 =\n3×2 + c =\n6 + c,\nso c = 11 − 6 =\n5. The line's equation is y = 3x + 5.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A line has gradient 3 and passes through (2, 11). Find its equation.", steps: [
        "Substitute into y = mx + c: 11 = 3(2) + c.",
        "11 =\n6 + c, so\nc =\n5.",
      ], answer: "y = 3x + 5" },
      { q: "A line has gradient −2 and passes through (−3, 7) (a harder version with negative numbers). Find its equation.", steps: [
        "Substitute into y = mx + c: 7 = −2(−3) + c.",
        "7 =\n6 + c, so\nc =\n1.",
      ], answer: "y = −2x + 1" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A line has gradient 1/2 and passes through (4, 1). Find its equation.", answer: "y = 0.5x − 1, since 1 = 0.5(4) + c gives 1 = 2 + c, so c = −1." } },
    { h: "4. Parallel and perpendicular lines", body: [
      "Parallel lines point in exactly the same direction and never meet, however far they're extended. Since the gradient measures direction, two lines are parallel exactly when they have the SAME gradient.",
      "Perpendicular lines meet at a right angle (90°). Their gradients are always negative reciprocals of each other. A reciprocal means 'flip the fraction upside down' (the reciprocal of 2/3 is 3/2, and the reciprocal of a whole number n is 1/n, since n is really n/1). 'Negative reciprocal' means flip it AND change its sign. The test that always works: multiply the two gradients together, and if the lines are perpendicular, the answer is always exactly −1.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Are the lines y = 2x + 5 and y = 2x − 3 parallel?", steps: [
        "Compare gradients: both lines have m = 2.",
        "Same gradient means same direction, so the lines are parallel (they never meet, since their different y-intercepts, 5 and −3, mean they're not the same line either).",
      ], answer: "Yes, they are parallel" },
      { q: "A line has gradient 2/3. What is the gradient of a line perpendicular to it? (a harder version, needing the flip-and-negate rule)", steps: [
        "Flip the fraction 2/3 upside down (find its reciprocal): 3/2.",
        "Negate it (change its sign) to get the negative reciprocal: −3/2.",
        "Check by multiplying the two gradients:\n(2/3) × (−3/2) =\n−1.\nCorrect.",
      ], answer: "−3/2" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "What gradient is perpendicular to a line with gradient −4?", answer: "1/4, since −4 = −4/1, flipping gives −1/4, and negating that gives 1/4. Check: (−4) × (1/4) = −1." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.similarShapesAndScaleFactors = {
  title: "Similar Shapes: scaling lengths, areas and volumes",
  minutes: 12,
  order: 23,
  prereq: [{ module: "primary", key: "ratioBasics" }, { module: "primary", key: "shapeProperties" }],
  intro: "Two shapes are similar when one is an exact scaled-up (or down) copy of the other — same angles, same proportions, just a different size. The single most important idea in this lesson is that length, area and volume DON'T scale by the same amount when a shape is enlarged.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Scale factor for length", body: [
      "When two shapes are similar, every side on one shape has a matching side on the other, in the same relative position going round the shape - these are called corresponding sides. The scale factor is the number you multiply every length on the ORIGINAL shape by, to get the matching length on the NEW shape. You find it by dividing a length on the new shape by the corresponding length on the original:\nscale factor =\n(new length) ÷ (matching original length).",
      "This uses ratio: comparing two quantities by dividing one by the other, the same idea used for comparing amounts in ratio basics - here the 'amounts' happen to be two lengths instead of two quantities of something.",
      "For example, if triangle A has a side of 4cm and the similar triangle B has the matching side at 10cm, the scale factor from A to B is 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5 - every length on A gets multiplied by 2.5 to give the matching length on B. Going the other way, from B to A, would use the reciprocal instead (4 ÷ 10 = 0.4), since that direction shrinks rather than enlarges.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Triangle A has a side of 4cm. The similar triangle B has the matching side at 10cm. What is the scale factor from A to B?", steps: [
        "Scale factor = new length ÷ matching original length.",
        "Scale factor =\n10 ÷ 4 =\n2.5.",
      ], answer: "2.5" },
      { q: "Rectangle P has a side of 12cm. The similar rectangle Q has the matching side at 9cm (a harder version where the scale factor is a reduction). Find the scale factor from P to Q.", steps: [
        "Scale factor = matching length on Q ÷ corresponding length on P.",
        "Scale factor =\n9 ÷ 12 =\n0.75 (that is, 3/4).",
      ], answer: "0.75 (3/4)" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Shape A has a side of 6cm. The similar shape B has the matching side at 9cm. Find the scale factor from A to B.", answer: "1.5, since 9 ÷ 6 = 1.5." } },
    { h: "2. Area scales by the SQUARE of the scale factor", body: [
      "Area is always a length multiplied by a length (think of a rectangle: area = length × width). So if every length on a shape is multiplied by a scale factor k, the area gets multiplied by k TWICE over - once for each length involved - which means area scales by k².",
      "Check this on a simple square first. A square of side 2cm has area 2×2 = 4cm². Scale every length by k=3: the new side is 2×3 = 6cm, so the new area is 6×6 = 36cm². Compare the two areas: 36 ÷ 4 = 9, and 9 is exactly 3² - the area scale factor is the square of the length scale factor, exactly as claimed.",
      "So whenever a length scale factor k is given, the area scale factor is k², and you multiply the original area by k² to get the new area.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Two similar rectangles have a length scale factor of 3. The smaller rectangle has area 8cm². What is the area of the larger one?", steps: [
        "Area scale factor = k² =\n3² =\n9.",
        "New area = 8 × 9 =\n72cm².",
      ], answer: "72cm²" },
      { q: "Two similar shapes X and Y have a length scale factor from X to Y of 2/5 (a harder version where Y is smaller than X). Shape X has area 50cm². Find the area of Y.", steps: [
        "Area scale factor = k² =\n(2/5)² =\n4/25.",
        "New area = 50 × 4/25 =\n200/25 =\n8cm².",
      ], answer: "8cm²" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Two similar shapes have a length scale factor of 5. If the smaller has area 6cm², find the larger's area.", answer: "150cm², since 5² = 25 and 6 × 25 = 150." } },
    { h: "3. Volume scales by the CUBE of the scale factor", body: [
      "Volume is a length multiplied by a length multiplied by a length (think of a box: volume = length × width × height). So scaling every length by k multiplies the volume by k three times over - volume scales by k³.",
      "Check this on a simple cube. A cube of side 1cm has volume 1×1×1 = 1cm³. Scale every length by k=2: the new side is 2cm, so the new volume is 2×2×2 = 8cm³. Compare: 8 ÷ 1 = 8, and 8 is exactly 2³ - confirming volume scales by the cube of the length scale factor.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Two similar solids have a length scale factor of 2. The smaller has volume 5cm³. Find the larger's volume.", steps: [
        "Volume scale factor = k³ =\n2³ =\n8.",
        "New volume = 5 × 8 =\n40cm³.",
      ], answer: "40cm³" },
      { q: "Two similar solids have a length scale factor of 3/2 (a harder version with a fractional scale factor). The smaller has volume 16cm³. Find the larger's volume.", steps: [
        "Volume scale factor = k³ =\n(3/2)³ =\n27/8.",
        "New volume = 16 × 27/8 =\n432/8 =\n54cm³.",
      ], answer: "54cm³" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Two similar solids have a length scale factor of 4. The smaller has volume 2cm³. Find the larger's volume.", answer: "128cm³, since 4³ = 64 and 2 × 64 = 128." } },
    { h: "4. Working backwards from area or volume", body: [
      "Sometimes you're given an area ratio or a volume ratio and asked to find the length scale factor instead - this means undoing the squaring or cubing from the last two sections. The square root of a number is the value which, multiplied by itself, gives that number back (so undoing 'squared' means taking a square root). The cube root of a number is the value which, multiplied by itself three times, gives that number back (so undoing 'cubed' means taking a cube root).",
      "So: given an area ratio, take its square root to recover the length scale factor. Given a volume ratio, take its cube root.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Two similar shapes have areas 9cm² and 25cm². Find the scale factor of their lengths.", steps: [
        "Area ratio = 25/9.",
        "Length scale factor = √(25/9) =\n5/3.",
      ], answer: "5/3" },
      { q: "Two similar solids have volumes 8cm³ and 125cm³ (a harder version, working back from a volume ratio). Find the scale factor of their lengths.", steps: [
        "Volume ratio = 125/8.",
        "Length scale factor = ∛(125/8) =\n5/2 (since 5³=125 and 2³=8).",
      ], answer: "5/2" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Two similar shapes have areas 16cm² and 49cm². Find the scale factor of their lengths.", answer: "7/4, since √(49/16) = 7/4 (7²=49, 4²=16)." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.circleTheoremsAndTangents = {
  title: "Circle Theorems: angle facts inside a circle",
  minutes: 15,
  order: 24,
  prereq: [{ module: "junior", key: "angleParallel" }, { module: "junior", key: "angleIso" }],
  intro: "Circles hide a handful of guaranteed angle facts, wherever the points happen to sit on the circumference. This lesson covers the angle in a semicircle, the angle at the centre versus the circumference, angles in the same segment, cyclic quadrilaterals, and tangent-radius facts.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Angle in a semicircle is 90°", body: [
      "A few words first, since they get used constantly in this lesson. The centre is the single point exactly in the middle of the circle. A radius is a straight line from the centre out to any point on the circle's edge - it's always the same length, wherever it's drawn. A diameter is a straight line that passes right through the centre, touching the circle on both sides - it's exactly twice the length of the radius. The circumference is the round boundary line of the circle itself - every point 'on the circle' means a point sitting on this boundary.",
      "Angles get named using three letters, like 'angle BAC'. The middle letter always names the corner (vertex) where the angle is actually measured - so 'angle BAC' means the angle sitting at corner A, opening out towards B on one side and C on the other.",
      "The theorem: take any triangle where one side is a diameter of the circle, and the third corner sits anywhere else on the circle's circumference. That triangle is always guaranteed to have a right angle (90°) exactly at that third corner - whichever point on the circle it happens to be.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "AB is a diameter of a circle, and C is a point on the circle. Angle BAC = 35°. Find angle ACB and angle ABC.", steps: [
        "Angle ACB = 90° (angle in a semicircle, since AB is the diameter and C is the third corner).",
        "A triangle's angles always sum to 180°, so angle ABC =\n180 − 90 − 35 =\n55°.",
      ], answer: "ACB = 90°, ABC = 55°" },
      { q: "AB is a diameter of a circle, and C is a point on the circle. Angle ABC = 52° (a harder version with a different angle given). Find angle ACB and angle BAC.", steps: [
        "Angle ACB = 90° (angle in a semicircle).",
        "A triangle's angles sum to 180°, so angle BAC =\n180 − 90 − 52 =\n38°.",
      ], answer: "ACB = 90°, BAC = 38°" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "AB is a diameter of a circle, and C is a point on the circle. Angle BAC = 61°. Find angle ABC.", answer: "29°, since angle ACB = 90° (semicircle), and 180 − 90 − 61 = 29." } },
    { h: "2. Angle at the centre is TWICE the angle at the circumference", body: [
      "An arc is just a section of the circle's circumference lying between two points on the circle. Suppose P and Q are two points on the circle, marking the ends of an arc. From a third point, you can draw lines to P and Q and measure the angle they make - this is called the angle 'standing on' (or subtended by) that arc. The third point could be the centre O, giving angle POQ, or it could be some other point R on the circumference, giving angle PRQ - both are standing on the same arc PQ, just measured from different places.",
      "The theorem: whenever a centre-angle and a circumference-angle are both standing on the SAME arc, the centre-angle is always exactly double the circumference-angle.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "The angle at the centre of a circle standing on arc PQ is 100°. Find the angle at the circumference standing on the same arc.", steps: [
        "Centre angle = 2 × circumference angle.",
        "100 = 2 × circumference angle, so circumference angle =\n100 ÷ 2 =\n50°.",
      ], answer: "50°" },
      { q: "The angle at the circumference of a circle standing on arc PQ is 38° (a harder version, working the other way round). Find the angle at the centre standing on the same arc.", steps: [
        "Centre angle = 2 × circumference angle.",
        "Centre angle =\n2 × 38 =\n76°.",
      ], answer: "76°" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "The angle at the circumference of a circle standing on arc PQ is 47°. Find the angle at the centre standing on the same arc.", answer: "94°, since the centre angle is always double the circumference angle: 2 × 47 = 94." } },
    { h: "3. Angles in the same segment are equal", body: [
      "A chord is a straight line joining any two points on the circle - unlike a diameter, it doesn't have to pass through the centre. Drawing a chord splits the inside of the circle into two regions, and each region is called a segment (usually one bigger 'major' segment and one smaller 'minor' segment).",
      "'Angles in the same segment' means this: take a chord PQ, then pick two more points that both lie in the SAME one of the two segments, and from each of them draw lines to P and Q. The angle formed at each of those points is always equal to the angle formed at the other one - they don't need the centre at all, just both points sitting on the same side of the chord.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "PQ is a chord of a circle. R and S are two points on the circle, both in the same segment (the same side of PQ). Angle PRQ = 40°. Find angle PSQ.", steps: [
        "Angles PRQ and PSQ both stand on chord PQ from within the same segment, so they must be equal.",
        "Angle PSQ = 40°.",
      ], answer: "40°" },
      { q: "PQ is a chord of a circle. R and S are two points in the same segment. Angle PRQ = (3x+10)° and angle PSQ = (x+50)° (a harder version using algebra). Find x and the size of the equal angles.", steps: [
        "Angles in the same segment are equal, so: 3x+10 = x+50.",
        "Subtract x from both sides and subtract 10 from both sides:\n3x+10 = x+50\n2x =\n40, so\nx =\n20.",
        "Substitute back to find the angle: 3(20)+10 =\n60+10 =\n70°. Check the other expression too: 20+50 =\n70°. Both match.",
      ], answer: "x = 20, angles = 70°" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "PQ is a chord of a circle. R and S are two points in the same segment. Angle PRQ = 63°. Find angle PSQ.", answer: "63°, since angles in the same segment are always equal." } },
    { h: "4. Cyclic quadrilaterals: opposite angles sum to 180°", body: [
      "A quadrilateral is just a four-sided shape. It's called cyclic when all four of its corners lie exactly on a single circle. Labelling the corners P, Q, R, S in order round the shape, 'opposite angles' means pairs of corners that don't share a side - angle P is opposite angle R, and angle Q is opposite angle S.",
      "The theorem: in any cyclic quadrilateral, each pair of opposite angles always adds up to 180° (they're supplementary).",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "In cyclic quadrilateral PQRS, angle P = 85°. Find angle R.", steps: [
        "Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°.",
        "Angle R =\n180 − 85 =\n95°.",
      ], answer: "95°" },
      { q: "In cyclic quadrilateral PQRS, angle Q = (2x+10)° and angle S = (3x−5)° (a harder version using algebra). Find x and both angles.", steps: [
        "Q and S are opposite angles, so they sum to 180°: (2x+10) + (3x−5) = 180.",
        "Simplify the left side:\n2x+10+3x−5 =\n5x+5,\nso 5x+5 = 180.",
        "Subtract 5 then divide by 5:\n5x =\n175, so\nx =\n35.",
        "Angle Q =\n2(35)+10 =\n80°. Angle S =\n3(35)−5 =\n100°. Check: 80+100 =\n180°.",
      ], answer: "x = 35, Q = 80°, S = 100°" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "In cyclic quadrilateral PQRS, angle P = 112°. Find angle R.", answer: "68°, since 180 − 112 = 68." } },
    { h: "5. Tangent-radius: always perpendicular", body: [
      "A tangent is a straight line that touches the circle at exactly one point, without crossing into its interior. The single point where it touches is called the point of contact.",
      "Fact 1: a tangent always meets the radius drawn to its point of contact at exactly 90°.",
      "Fact 2: if you pick a point outside the circle (an external point) and draw the two possible tangent lines from it to the circle, those two tangent lines are always exactly equal in length.",
      "Because fact 1 guarantees a right angle, any triangle built from a tangent, a radius, and the line joining the centre to an external point is right-angled - which means Pythagoras' theorem (the one covered in the Pythagoras lesson) can be used directly to find a missing length in it.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A tangent touches a circle at point T. The radius OT is drawn, and a line from the external point A to T makes angle OTA. What is angle OTA?", steps: [
        "The tangent meets the radius at exactly 90° (fact 1).",
        "Angle OTA = 90°.",
      ], answer: "90°" },
      { q: "A circle has centre O and radius 5cm. From external point A, a tangent touches the circle at T, with AT = 12cm (a harder version combining both tangent facts with Pythagoras). Find the distance OA, and state the length of the other tangent from A, which touches the circle at S.", steps: [
        "Angle OTA = 90° (fact 1), so triangle OTA is right-angled at T, with OA as its hypotenuse.",
        "Apply Pythagoras' theorem:\nOA² =\nOT² + AT² =\n5² + 12² =\n25 + 144 =\n169.",
        "OA =\n√169 =\n13cm.",
        "By fact 2, the two tangents from the same external point A are equal in length, so AS = AT =\n12cm.",
      ], answer: "OA = 13cm, AS = 12cm" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A circle has centre O and radius 3cm. From external point B, a tangent touches the circle at T, with BT = 4cm. Find OB.", answer: "5cm, since angle OTB = 90° gives OB² = 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25, so OB = √25 = 5." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.trigonometryAdvanced = {
  title: "Trigonometry: beyond right-angled triangles",
  minutes: 15,
  order: 25,
  prereq: [{ module: "junior", key: "pythagQuest" }, "similarShapesAndScaleFactors"],
  intro: "SOHCAHTOA solves right-angled triangles, but the sine rule, cosine rule and the ½ab sin C area formula extend that to ANY triangle — no right angle required. This lesson builds up from the basic ratios to all three.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. SOHCAHTOA — right-angled triangles", body: [
      "In a right-angled triangle, once you pick one of the two non-right angles to focus on, the three sides get special names relative to that angle. The hypotenuse is the longest side, always the one opposite (directly across from) the right angle - it's easy to spot since it never touches the right angle. The opposite side is the side directly across from the angle you've chosen to focus on. The adjacent side is the side that touches your chosen angle but ISN'T the hypotenuse. (If you picked the OTHER non-right angle instead, the opposite and adjacent sides would swap - which side is 'opposite' or 'adjacent' always depends on which angle you're using.)",
      "Sin, cos and tan are just names for three particular ratios (fractions) between these sides: sin(angle) = opposite ÷ hypotenuse, cos(angle) = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse, tan(angle) = opposite ÷ adjacent. SOHCAHTOA is simply a memory aid built from the first letters of those three lines (Sin-Opposite-Hypotenuse, Cos-Adjacent-Hypotenuse, Tan-Opposite-Adjacent) - it isn't new maths, just a way to remember the three ratios above.",
      "Used forwards, if you know an angle and one side, rearrange the matching ratio to find another side. Used backwards, if you know two sides, you can find the angle: for instance if tan(angle) = 0.75, 'undo' the tan using the inverse tan function on a calculator, written tan⁻¹, so angle = tan⁻¹(0.75). The same idea works with sin⁻¹ and cos⁻¹.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A right-angled triangle has hypotenuse 10cm and one angle of 30°. Find the side opposite that angle.", steps: [
        "sin(angle) = opposite ÷ hypotenuse, so rearranged: opposite = hypotenuse × sin(angle).",
        "Opposite =\n10 × sin(30°) =\n10 × 0.5 =\n5cm.",
      ], answer: "5cm" },
      { q: "A right-angled triangle has an opposite side of 7cm and an adjacent side of 9cm (a harder version, finding the angle instead of a side). Find the angle between the hypotenuse and the adjacent side.", steps: [
        "tan(angle) = opposite ÷ adjacent, so:\ntan(angle) =\n7 ÷ 9 ≈\n0.778.",
        "Undo the tan using the inverse function on a calculator:\nangle =\ntan⁻¹(0.778) ≈\n37.9°.",
      ], answer: "≈37.9°" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A right-angled triangle has an adjacent side of 12cm and an angle of 25° next to it. Find the hypotenuse.", answer: "≈13.2cm, since cos(25°) = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse, so hypotenuse = 12 ÷ cos(25°) = 12 ÷ 0.906 ≈ 13.2cm." } },
    { h: "2. The sine rule: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C", body: [
      "For any triangle (not just right-angled ones), label its three corners A, B and C. Then label each SIDE with the lowercase version of the letter at the corner OPPOSITE it: side a is opposite angle A, side b is opposite angle B, and side c is opposite angle C. This labelling convention is what makes the sine rule readable - without it, 'a' and 'A' would look unrelated instead of being a matching pair.",
      "The sine rule says the ratio (side) ÷ sin(its opposite angle) comes out the SAME no matter which of the three pairs you pick - a strikingly similar idea to the constant scale factor between similar shapes covered earlier: there, one constant ratio linked every pair of matching lengths; here, one constant ratio links every side to the sine of its opposite angle, within a single triangle.",
      "Use the sine rule when you know a side and its opposite angle, plus one more side or angle to complete the picture.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "In triangle ABC, a = 8cm, angle A = 40°, angle B = 60°. Find side b.", steps: [
        "Use a/sin A = b/sin B:\n8/sin(40°) =\nb/sin(60°).",
        "Rearrange to make b the subject:\nb =\n8 × sin(60°) ÷ sin(40°) ≈\n8 × 0.866 ÷ 0.643 ≈\n10.8cm.",
      ], answer: "≈10.8cm" },
      { q: "In triangle ABC, a = 10cm, angle A = 35°, c = 14cm (a harder version, finding an angle rather than a side). Find angle C.", steps: [
        "Use a/sin A = c/sin C, rearranged to make sin C the subject:\nsin C =\nc × sin A ÷ a.",
        "Substitute the numbers:\nsin C =\n14 × sin(35°) ÷ 10 ≈\n14 × 0.574 ÷ 10 ≈\n0.803.",
        "Undo the sin using the inverse function:\nC =\nsin⁻¹(0.803) ≈\n53.4°.",
        "Quick sanity check: A + C ≈ 35 + 53.4 = 88.4°, comfortably under 180°, so this angle fits inside a real triangle.",
      ], answer: "≈53.4°" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "In triangle ABC, angle A = 50°, a = 9cm, angle B = 70°. Find side b.", answer: "≈11.0cm, since b = a × sin B ÷ sin A = 9 × sin(70°) ÷ sin(50°) ≈ 9 × 0.940 ÷ 0.766 ≈ 11.0cm." } },
    { h: "3. The cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A", body: [
      "This uses the same side-labelling convention as the sine rule (side a opposite angle A, and so on). When two sides and the angle SANDWICHED between them are known, that middle angle is called the included angle - it's the angle you need for this formula.",
      "There's a link back to Pythagoras' theorem here: if angle A happens to be exactly 90°, then cos(90°) = 0, so the last term vanishes and the formula becomes a² = b² + c² - exactly Pythagoras' theorem. The cosine rule is really Pythagoras' theorem extended to work for ANY angle, not just a right angle.",
      "Use the cosine rule to find a missing side when you know two sides and the included angle between them, as in the formula above. To find a missing ANGLE instead, when all three sides are known, rearrange the formula to make cos A the subject:\ncos A =\n(b² + c² − a²) ÷ (2bc),\nthen undo the cos with cos⁻¹.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Two sides of a triangle are 7cm and 9cm, with an included angle of 50°. Find the third side.", steps: [
        "Use a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A with b=7, c=9, A=50°:\na² =\n7² + 9² − 2(7)(9)cos(50°) =\n49 + 81 − 126×0.643.",
        "Work out the last term:\n126 × 0.643 ≈\n81.0, so\na² ≈\n49 + 81 − 81.0 =\n49.0.",
        "a ≈\n√49.0 ≈\n7.0cm.",
      ], answer: "≈7.0cm" },
      { q: "A triangle has sides a=5cm, b=7cm, c=8cm (a harder version, finding an angle from three known sides). Find angle A.", steps: [
        "Use the rearranged cosine rule:\ncos A =\n(b² + c² − a²) ÷ (2bc) =\n(7² + 8² − 5²) ÷ (2×7×8).",
        "Work out the top and bottom separately:\ntop =\n49 + 64 − 25 =\n88.\nbottom =\n2×7×8 =\n112.",
        "cos A =\n88 ÷ 112 ≈\n0.786.",
        "Undo the cos using the inverse function:\nA =\ncos⁻¹(0.786) ≈\n38.2°.",
      ], answer: "≈38.2°" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Two sides of a triangle are 4cm and 5cm, with an included angle of 70°. Find the third side.", answer: "≈5.2cm, since a² = 4² + 5² − 2(4)(5)cos(70°) = 16 + 25 − 40×0.342 ≈ 27.3, so a ≈ √27.3 ≈ 5.2cm." } },
    { h: "4. Area of any triangle: ½ab sin C", body: [
      "This formula finds a triangle's area directly from two sides and their included angle (the angle sandwiched between them, same meaning as in the cosine rule) - no height measurement needed.",
      "One thing worth noticing: sin stays positive for every angle between 0° and 180°, including obtuse angles (bigger than 90°), so this formula keeps giving a sensible positive area even when the included angle is obtuse.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A triangle has two sides of 6cm and 8cm with an included angle of 45°. Find its area.", steps: [
        "Area = ½ × 6 × 8 × sin(45°).",
        "Area =\n24 × 0.707 ≈\n17.0cm².",
      ], answer: "≈17.0cm²" },
      { q: "A triangle has two sides of 10cm and 13cm with an included angle of 110° (a harder version with an obtuse included angle). Find its area.", steps: [
        "Area = ½ × 10 × 13 × sin(110°).",
        "Area =\n65 × 0.940 ≈\n61.1cm².",
      ], answer: "≈61.1cm²" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A triangle has two sides of 5cm and 6cm with an included angle of 60°. Find its area.", answer: "≈13.0cm², since area = ½ × 5 × 6 × sin(60°) = 15 × 0.866 ≈ 13.0cm²." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.threeDGeometryAndNets = {
  title: "3D Shapes: volume, surface area and nets",
  minutes: 13,
  order: 26,
  prereq: [{ module: "junior", key: "cubeProps" }, { module: "junior", key: "shapeFold" }],
  intro: "A net is a 2D shape that folds up into a 3D solid: every face of the solid is drawn out flat and joined along the edges where it folds. This builds directly on shape-folding (shapeFold) and cube facts (cubeProps) from earlier - if nets still feel unfamiliar, it's worth a quick look back at those first. This lesson covers surface area via nets, volume of prisms, volume of cylinders, and how to recognise which net folds into which solid.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Surface area = the total area of every face in the net", body: [
      "A solid's surface area is the total area of its outside surface - every face, added up. The net is exactly this surface unfolded flat, so surface area is simply the AREA OF THE WHOLE NET: add up the area of each flat face shown in it.",
      "Picture a cube's net: six identical squares arranged so that folding along their shared edges closes them up into a cube with no gaps and no overlaps (one common arrangement is a strip of four squares in a row, with one more square attached above one of them and one more below one of them - folding the strip round into a tube gives four side faces, and folding the extra two flat gives the top and the bottom).",
      "A cuboid has three edge lengths meeting at each corner, usually called its length (l), width (w) and height (h) - which edge gets which name doesn't matter, as long as it's used consistently from here on. A cuboid's net is six rectangles, but they come in three matching PAIRS, because opposite faces of a cuboid are always identical rectangles: two faces measuring l×w, two measuring l×h, and two measuring w×h.",
      "Adding up all six faces: Surface area = 2×(l×w + l×h + w×h). The '2×' is there because each of the three different rectangle sizes appears exactly twice - once on each of two opposite faces.",
      "Check this on a cuboid measuring 4cm×3cm×2cm, so l=4, w=3, h=2:\nl×w =\n4×3 =\n12,\nl×h =\n4×2 =\n8,\nw×h =\n3×2 =\n6.\nAdding these three and doubling:\nSurface area =\n2×(12+8+6) =\n2×26 =\n52cm².",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find the surface area of a cuboid measuring 4cm × 3cm × 2cm.", steps: [
        "Identify l=4, w=3, h=2 (any assignment of the labels works, as long as it's consistent).",
        "Find each pair's area:\nl×w = 4×3 = 12,\nl×h = 4×2 = 8,\nw×h = 3×2 = 6.",
        "Add the three and double, since each rectangle appears on two opposite faces:\nSurface area =\n2×(12+8+6) =\n2×26 =\n52cm².",
      ], answer: "52cm²" },
      { q: "Find the surface area of a cuboid measuring 6cm × 5cm × 3cm (bigger numbers, same method).", steps: [
        "Identify l=6, w=5, h=3.",
        "Find each pair's area:\nl×w = 6×5 = 30,\nl×h = 6×3 = 18,\nw×h = 5×3 = 15.",
        "Add the three and double:\nSurface area =\n2×(30+18+15) =\n2×63 =\n126cm².",
      ], answer: "126cm²" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the surface area of a cube with side length 7cm (a cube is just a cuboid where l=w=h).", answer: "294cm². A cube has 6 identical square faces, each 7×7=49cm², so surface area = 6×49 = 294cm² (the same formula as a cuboid, just with l=w=h=7, so all three pair-areas equal 49)." } },
    { h: "2. Volume of a prism = cross-sectional area × length", body: [
      "A prism is a 3D solid with the same flat shape - called its cross-section - repeated all the way through, like a stack of identical slices glued together. The cross-section is whatever shape you'd see if you sliced straight across the prism, perpendicular to its length.",
      "Volume of a prism = cross-sectional area × length, where 'length' means the distance the cross-section extends through the solid (sometimes called its depth, depending on which way it's sitting). This works because the prism is literally that cross-sectional area, stacked up 'length' times over - the same idea as stacking identical sheets of paper to build a block.",
      "A cube is a special case of a prism: its cross-section is a square, and its 'length' equals its side too - which is exactly why a cube's volume formula (side×side×side, from cubeProps) is just this same prism rule in disguise.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A triangular prism has a cross-section of area 15cm² and length 10cm. Find its volume.", steps: [
        "Volume = cross-sectional area × length.",
        "Volume = 15 × 10 = 150cm³.",
      ], answer: "150cm³" },
      { q: "A prism's cross-section is a right-angled triangle with base 6cm and height 8cm, and the prism is 12cm long. Find its volume.", steps: [
        "First find the cross-sectional area. A triangle's area is half its base times its height:\nArea =\n½ × 6 × 8 =\n½ × 48 =\n24cm².",
        "Volume = cross-sectional area × length:\nVolume =\n24 × 12 =\n288cm³.",
      ], answer: "288cm³" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A prism has a cross-sectional area of 20cm² and length 7cm. Find its volume.", answer: "140cm³, since volume = cross-sectional area × length = 20 × 7 = 140cm³." } },
    { h: "3. Volume of a cylinder = πr²h", body: [
      "A cylinder is a prism whose cross-section is a circle, so the same rule applies: Volume = cross-sectional area × length. For a cylinder the 'length' runs up its axis and is usually called its height, h, and the cross-section is a circle of radius r (the distance from the centre of the circle out to its edge).",
      "The area of a circle is π×r², where π (pronounced 'pi') is a fixed number, roughly 3.14159..., equal to a circle's circumference divided by its diameter - it turns up whenever a circle's measurements are involved. For estimating by hand, π ≈ 3.14 is close enough.",
      "Putting the circle's area into the prism rule:\nVolume =\ncross-sectional area × height =\n(π×r²) × h =\nπr²h.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Find the volume of a cylinder with radius 3cm and height 10cm (use π ≈ 3.14).", steps: [
        "Cross-sectional area = π×r²:\nπ × 3² =\nπ × 9 ≈\n3.14 × 9 =\n28.26cm².",
        "Volume = cross-sectional area × height:\n28.26 × 10 =\n282.6,\nso volume ≈ 283cm³ (rounded).",
      ], answer: "≈283cm³" },
      { q: "Find the volume of a cylinder with radius 5cm and height 12cm (use π ≈ 3.14).", steps: [
        "Cross-sectional area = π×r²:\nπ × 5² =\nπ × 25 ≈\n3.14 × 25 =\n78.5cm².",
        "Volume = cross-sectional area × height:\n78.5 × 12 =\n942cm³.",
      ], answer: "942cm³" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Find the volume of a cylinder with radius 4cm and height 9cm (use π ≈ 3.14).", answer: "≈452cm³. Cross-sectional area = π×4² ≈ 3.14×16 = 50.24cm², so volume ≈ 50.24×9 = 452.16, rounding to 452cm³." } },
    { h: "4. Matching a net to its solid", body: [
      "Checking a net has two parts. First, count: does it have the right NUMBER of faces, of the right SHAPES, for the solid? A cube needs exactly 6 squares. A triangular prism needs 2 triangles (the two ends) plus 3 rectangles (the sides). A square-based pyramid needs 1 square plus 4 triangles.",
      "Second, fold it mentally (or on paper): the right number of the right shapes isn't enough on its own - they also have to fold up edge-to-edge with no gaps and no overlaps. Two different arrangements of the same six squares can give one net that folds perfectly into a cube and another that leaves two squares landing on top of each other.",
      "For example, a strip of 4 squares in a row, with 1 more square attached above any one of the 4 and another attached below any one of the 4, always folds into a valid cube: the strip of 4 wraps round into a tube (the four side faces), and the extra two fold flat to become the top and the bottom, wherever along the strip they were attached. This is exactly the kind of check practised with shapeFold earlier.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A square-based pyramid has 1 square base and 4 triangular side faces. How many faces, and of what shapes, should its net show?", steps: [
        "Count the faces on the solid itself: 1 square (the base) + 4 triangles (the sloping sides) = 5 faces in total.",
        "So the net must show exactly 1 square and exactly 4 triangles - any other combination of shapes or count is automatically wrong, even before folding it.",
      ], answer: "5 faces: 1 square + 4 triangles" },
      { q: "A net has six squares: a straight row of four squares, with a fifth square attached to the TOP edge of the leftmost square, and a sixth square also attached to the TOP edge of the rightmost square. Does this fold into a valid cube?", steps: [
        "Face count is right: 6 squares, matching a cube's 6 faces - so the count check alone doesn't rule it out.",
        "Fold the row of four into a tube: it forms the four side faces of the cube.",
        "Both extra squares are attached on the SAME side (top) of the strip. When folded up, both try to become the same face (the top) at once - they land on top of each other instead of one becoming the top and the other the bottom.",
        "Result: two faces overlap and the bottom face is left completely uncovered, so this net does NOT fold into a valid cube, even though the face count was correct.",
      ], answer: "No - both flaps fold onto the same face (the top), leaving the bottom uncovered, even though the face count (6 squares) looked right." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A net has 2 triangles and 3 rectangles, correctly shaped and sized to match. Which solid could this be the net of?", answer: "A triangular prism - it needs exactly 2 triangular end faces plus 3 rectangular side faces, which matches this net's face count and shapes exactly." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.multiStepGeometryProof = {
  title: "Geometry Chains: combining facts step by step",
  minutes: 14,
  order: 27,
  prereq: ["circleTheoremsAndTangents", "trigonometryAdvanced", "algebraicProof"],
  intro: "The hardest geometry questions don't use one rule - they chain two or three together, where the answer to one step becomes the input to the next. This lesson is about the STRATEGY of chaining, not a new rule of its own: spot every shape hiding in the diagram, extract one fact at a time using a rule you can name (from earlier lessons like circleTheoremsAndTangents, trigonometryAdvanced or algebraicProof, or from basic angle facts), and let each one unlock the next.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Find every shape hiding in the diagram", body: [
      "Complex diagrams are almost always several simple shapes overlapping or sharing a side - a triangle sharing a side with a parallelogram, or an isosceles triangle formed by two radii of a circle. Before touching any angles, trace each individual shape's outline on its own.",
      "A reminder of what 'isosceles' means: a triangle with (at least) two sides the same length. Whenever two sides in a diagram are equal - very often because they're both radii of the same circle, since every radius of a circle is the same length - the triangle they form is isosceles, and its two base angles (the angles opposite the two equal sides) are automatically equal too.",
      "Naming the shapes you find matters, because each shape brings its own rule with it: a triangle brings 'angles sum to 180°', a parallelogram brings 'opposite angles are equal' and 'co-interior angles between the parallel sides sum to 180°', and a circle brings the circle theorems (from circleTheoremsAndTangents) - such as the fact that a tangent is always perpendicular to the radius drawn to the point where it touches the circle.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A diagram shows quadrilateral ABCD with diagonal AC drawn in. What two shapes can you see once the diagonal is added, and what do they share?", steps: [
        "The diagonal AC splits the quadrilateral into two separate triangles: triangle ABC and triangle ACD.",
        "They share the side AC - so any angle or length worked out using AC in one triangle can be reused directly in the other.",
      ], answer: "Triangle ABC and triangle ACD, sharing the side AC." },
      { q: "A circle has centre O. A and B are points on the circumference, and a tangent to the circle touches it at A. What separate, nameable shapes and facts can you pick out of this diagram, involving O, A and B?", steps: [
        "OA and OB are both radii of the same circle, so OA = OB - this makes triangle OAB isosceles, with equal base angles at A and B.",
        "The tangent at A and the radius OA meet at a right angle - this is the tangent-radius theorem (from circleTheoremsAndTangents): a tangent is always perpendicular (90°) to the radius drawn to its point of contact.",
        "So this one diagram actually contains an isosceles triangle (OAB) PLUS a separate 90° fact (between the tangent and OA) - two independent tools, ready to be chained together if a question asks for an angle involving the tangent.",
      ], answer: "An isosceles triangle OAB (since OA = OB are radii) and a 90° angle between the tangent at A and the radius OA (the tangent-radius theorem)." },
    ] },
    { h: "2. Work from what you're GIVEN, not toward what you WANT", body: [
      "Start at the angle or length you already know, and ask 'what does this fact force?' - rather than staring at the target angle hoping for inspiration. Each individual step should be a single, simple rule: base angles of an isosceles triangle are equal, angles on a straight line sum to 180°, angles round a point sum to 360°, angles in a triangle sum to 180°, or a circle theorem.",
      "Two rules used repeatedly below: the isosceles triangle base angle theorem (the two angles opposite a triangle's two equal sides are equal), and angles on a straight line (angles on one side of a straight line, meeting at a single point, always add up to 180°).",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "Triangle ABC is isosceles with AB = AC and angle BAC = 40°. BC is extended to a point D. Find angle ACD (the exterior angle at C).", steps: [
        "Base angles of the isosceles triangle are equal (the isosceles triangle base angle theorem), and the triangle's three angles sum to 180°:\nangle ABC = angle ACB =\n(180 − 40) ÷ 2 =\n140 ÷ 2 =\n70°.",
        "Angle ACD and angle ACB lie on the straight line BD, meeting at point C, so together they sum to 180° (angles on a straight line):\nangle ACD =\n180 − 70 =\n110°.",
      ], answer: "110°" },
      { q: "O is the centre of a circle, and A, B, C are points on the circumference with C on the major arc AB (the longer way round, not the short arc between A and B). OA and OB are radii, and angle AOB = 100°. Find (a) angle OAB and (b) angle ACB.", steps: [
        "OA and OB are both radii, so OA = OB - triangle OAB is isosceles, with equal base angles at A and B. Using the triangle's angle sum of 180°:\nangle OAB = angle OBA =\n(180 − 100) ÷ 2 =\n80 ÷ 2 =\n40°.",
        "That answers (a): angle OAB = 40°.",
        "For (b), use the angle at centre theorem (from circleTheoremsAndTangents): the angle at the centre is always twice the angle at the circumference, when both stand on the same arc AB. Here the centre angle AOB = 100°, so:\nangle ACB =\n100 ÷ 2 =\n50°.",
      ], answer: "(a) 40°  (b) 50°" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Triangle PQR is isosceles with PQ = PR and angle QPR = 50°. QR is extended to a point S. Find angle PRS.", answer: "115°. Base angles: angle PQR = angle PRQ = (180−50)÷2 = 65°. Angle PRS and angle PRQ lie on the straight line QS, so angle PRS = 180 − 65 = 115°." } },
    { h: "3. Label every angle as you find it", body: [
      "Write each newly-found angle directly onto (or next to) the diagram immediately, as soon as you find it. A chain of four or five steps is easy to lose track of otherwise, and a diagram with every known angle labelled lets you spot the next available step at a glance, rather than re-deriving something you already worked out.",
      "One more rule appears below: the angles inside any quadrilateral (a four-sided shape) always sum to 360° - the quadrilateral angle sum theorem. It follows from the triangle angle sum theorem, since any quadrilateral can be split into two triangles by one diagonal (exactly as in section 1's example), each contributing 180°.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "In triangle ABC, angle A = 55° and angle B = 65°. Find angle C, and label it before moving on to anything else.", steps: [
        "The three angles of a triangle sum to 180°:\nangle C =\n180 − 55 − 65 =\n60°.",
        "Label angle C = 60° on the diagram immediately - now every angle in this triangle is visible at a glance for the next step of any longer question.",
      ], answer: "60°" },
      { q: "Quadrilateral ABCD has angle A = 80°, angle B = 100°, angle C = x and angle D = 2x. Find x, then label angle C and angle D in turn.", steps: [
        "The angles of a quadrilateral sum to 360° (the quadrilateral angle sum theorem):\n80 + 100 + x + 2x =\n360.",
        "Simplify the left side:\n180 + 3x =\n360.",
        "Solve for x:\n3x =\n360 − 180 =\n180, so\nx =\n60.",
        "Label angle C = x = 60° on the diagram, then use it immediately to get angle D = 2x = 2×60 = 120°, and label that too.",
      ], answer: "x = 60°, so angle C = 60° and angle D = 120°." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Triangle DEF has angle D = 48° and angle E = 2 × angle D. Find angle F.", answer: "36°. Angle E = 2×48 = 96°. The triangle's angles sum to 180°, so angle F = 180 − 48 − 96 = 36°." } },
    { h: "4. Sanity-check the final answer", body: [
      "Angles in a triangle should sum to 180°, angles on a straight line to 180°, and angles round a point to 360° (or a quadrilateral's angles to 360°, as in section 3). If a completed chain of reasoning leads to a total that breaks one of these known facts, a step was mis-applied somewhere along the chain - go back and check each link.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A student calculates the three angles of a triangle as 50°, 65° and 70°. Use the angle sum check to decide if this is possible.", steps: [
        "Add the three angles:\n50 + 65 + 70 =\n185°.",
        "A triangle's angles must sum to exactly 180°, not 185° - so at least one of these three angles must be wrong, and it's worth re-checking the working that produced them.",
      ], answer: "Not possible - the three angles add to 185°, not 180°, so a mistake was made somewhere." },
      { q: "Four angles meeting at a single point are calculated as 90°, 85°, 100° and 90°. Use the angle sum check, then suggest what a corrected second angle would need to be for everything else to stay the same.", steps: [
        "Add the four angles:\n90 + 85 + 100 + 90 =\n365°.",
        "Angles round a point must sum to exactly 360°, not 365° - so something is 5° too big somewhere.",
        "If only the second angle (85°) was mis-calculated, the corrected value would need to be:\n85 − 5 =\n80°,\nsince\n90 + 80 + 100 + 90 =\n360°, which checks out.",
      ], answer: "The original total (365°) is 5° too many; correcting the second angle to 80° gives a valid total of 360°." },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "Two angles on a straight line are calculated as 115° and 60°. Use the angle sum check to decide if this is possible.", answer: "Not possible - 115 + 60 = 175°, not 180°, so the two angles are inconsistent with lying on a straight line; at least one needs to be re-checked." } },
  ],
};

INTERMEDIATE_LESSONS.statisticsAdvanced = {
  title: "Advanced Statistics: box plots, cumulative frequency and histograms",
  minutes: 14,
  order: 28,
  prereq: [{ module: "junior", key: "meanPuzzle" }],
  intro: "Beyond mean, median and mode (mean was covered in meanPuzzle) - GCSE and Kangaroo questions test three specific diagrams for summarising a whole dataset at a glance: box plots (spread and quartiles), cumulative frequency graphs (running totals), and histograms (frequency density for unequal class widths). Each diagram comes with its own vocabulary, defined from scratch below with a small worked dataset threaded through each one.",
  sections: [
    { h: "1. Box plots: five key numbers", body: [
      "Suppose 11 students' test scores, already sorted from smallest to largest, are:\n4, 7, 9, 12, 14, 15, 18, 20, 23, 25, 30.\nThe MEDIAN is the middle value once the data is sorted like this. With 11 values, the middle one sits at position (11+1)÷2 = 6, so counting in six places: the median is 15.",
      "The LOWER QUARTILE (written Q1) is the median of just the lower half of the data - the values below the overall median. Here the lower half is 4, 7, 9, 12, 14 (5 values), whose middle value is 9. So Q1 = 9. The UPPER QUARTILE (Q3) is the median of just the upper half - here 18, 20, 23, 25, 30 (5 values), whose middle value is 23. So Q3 = 23.",
      "(If a half itself contains an even number of values, its quartile is the MEAN of the two middle values of that half - the same rule used for finding the median of any even-sized list.)",
      "The INTERQUARTILE RANGE (IQR) is Q3 − Q1 - the width of the middle 50% of the data:\nIQR =\n23 − 9 =\n14.\nA box plot draws a box from Q1 to Q3 (with a line inside it at the median), and then a thin line called a WHISKER stretching out from each end of the box to the minimum and the maximum. So for this dataset, the box runs from 9 to 23 with a line at 15, and whiskers reach out to the minimum (4) and maximum (30).",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A box plot has minimum 5, lower quartile Q1 = 12, median 18, upper quartile Q3 = 25, maximum 40. Find the interquartile range.", steps: [
        "The interquartile range is the upper quartile minus the lower quartile:\nIQR =\nQ3 − Q1 =\n25 − 12 =\n13.",
      ], answer: "13" },
      { q: "Using the 11 sorted test scores 4, 7, 9, 12, 14, 15, 18, 20, 23, 25, 30, find the median, Q1, Q3 and the interquartile range.", steps: [
        "Median: the middle (6th) value out of 11 is 15.",
        "Lower half (the 5 values below the median): 4, 7, 9, 12, 14 - its middle value is Q1 = 9.",
        "Upper half (the 5 values above the median): 18, 20, 23, 25, 30 - its middle value is Q3 = 23.",
        "Interquartile range:\nIQR =\nQ3 − Q1 =\n23 − 9 =\n14.",
      ], answer: "Median = 15, Q1 = 9, Q3 = 23, IQR = 14" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "9 sorted values are: 2, 5, 6, 9, 11, 13, 15, 18, 20. Find Q1, Q3 and the interquartile range.", answer: "Q1 = 5.5, Q3 = 16.5, IQR = 11. The median (5th value) is 11. The lower half is 2, 5, 6, 9 (4 values, even), so Q1 is the mean of its two middle values: (5+6)÷2 = 5.5. The upper half is 13, 15, 18, 20, so Q3 = (15+18)÷2 = 16.5. IQR = 16.5 − 5.5 = 11." } },
    { h: "2. Cumulative frequency: running totals", body: [
      "CUMULATIVE FREQUENCY just means a RUNNING TOTAL: keep adding each new class's frequency onto the total of everything before it. For example, suppose 80 students' exam scores are grouped like this:\n0-20: frequency 10\n20-40: frequency 20\n40-60: frequency 30\n60-80: frequency 15\n80-100: frequency 5.\nRunning that total: after the first class, 10; after the second,\n10 + 20 =\n30; after the third,\n30 + 30 =\n60; after the fourth,\n60 + 15 =\n75; after the fifth,\n75 + 5 =\n80 - which correctly reaches the full total of 80 students by the end, as it always must.",
      "A cumulative frequency GRAPH plots these running totals against the upper boundary of each class, and is always increasing (or flat) - it can never go down, since a running total never shrinks.",
      "Reading UP from a chosen cumulative frequency value to the plotted curve, then ACROSS and DOWN to the horizontal axis, gives the data value below which that many items lie. The median sits at HALF the total frequency; the lower quartile Q1 sits at a QUARTER (¼) of the total; the upper quartile Q3 sits at THREE-QUARTERS (¾) of the total. With the 80-student example above, the median is read off at cumulative frequency\n80 ÷ 2 =\n40.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A cumulative frequency graph has a total frequency of 80. At what cumulative frequency value do you read off the median?", steps: [
        "The median sits at HALF the total frequency.",
        "80 ÷ 2 = 40.",
      ], answer: "40" },
      { q: "A cumulative frequency graph has a total frequency of 120. At what cumulative frequency values do you read off the lower quartile (Q1) and the upper quartile (Q3)?", steps: [
        "Q1 sits at a QUARTER of the total:\n120 ÷ 4 =\n30.",
        "Q3 sits at THREE-QUARTERS of the total:\n3 × 120 ÷ 4 =\n360 ÷ 4 =\n90.",
      ], answer: "Q1 at cumulative frequency 30, Q3 at cumulative frequency 90" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A cumulative frequency graph has a total frequency of 200. At what cumulative frequency values do you read off the median and Q3?", answer: "Median at 200 ÷ 2 = 100; Q3 at 3 × 200 ÷ 4 = 150." } },
    { h: "3. Histograms: frequency density, not frequency", body: [
      "A histogram is a bar chart for grouped, continuous data. When every class has the SAME width, plotting frequency as the bar height works fine. But when class widths are UNEQUAL, plotting raw frequency as height is misleading - a wide class can pile up a big frequency just because it's wide, not because the data is densely packed there.",
      "The fix is to plot FREQUENCY DENSITY instead, defined as:\nfrequency density =\nfrequency ÷ class width.\nThis keeps the bar's AREA (not its height) proportional to the number of data points, however wide or narrow the class is - since area = height × width = frequency density × class width = frequency, exactly recovering the count.",
      "For example, three classes with frequencies 20, 24 and 24 but widths 5, 10 and 6 respectively have frequency densities:\n20 ÷ 5 =\n4,\n24 ÷ 10 =\n2.4,\n24 ÷ 6 =\n4.\nNotice the first and third classes have the same frequency density (4) despite different frequencies (20 vs 24) - that's because their widths differ too (5 vs 6), and density accounts for that.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A histogram class has frequency 24 and class width 6. Find its frequency density.", steps: [
        "Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width.",
        "24 ÷ 6 = 4.",
      ], answer: "4" },
      { q: "A histogram class has frequency 45 and class width 9. Find its frequency density.", steps: [
        "Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width.",
        "45 ÷ 9 = 5.",
      ], answer: "5" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A histogram bar has frequency density 5 over a class width of 8. Find the frequency it represents.", answer: "40, since frequency = frequency density × class width = 5 × 8 = 40." } },
    { h: "4. Reading frequency back out of a histogram bar", body: [
      "Rearranging the frequency density formula (frequency density = frequency ÷ class width) to make frequency the subject:\nfrequency =\nfrequency density × class width.\nThis is exactly how you recover an actual COUNT of data points from a histogram bar's height (its frequency density) and width (its class width) - the reverse direction of section 3.",
    ], examples: [
      { q: "A histogram bar has frequency density 4 and class width 5. Find the frequency it represents.", steps: [
        "Frequency = frequency density × class width.",
        "Frequency = 4 × 5 = 20.",
      ], answer: "20" },
      { q: "A histogram bar has frequency density 3.5 and class width 12. Find the frequency it represents.", steps: [
        "Frequency = frequency density × class width.",
        "Frequency = 3.5 × 12 = 42.",
      ], answer: "42" },
    ],
      tryit: { q: "A histogram bar has frequency density 2.5 and class width 20. Find the frequency it represents.", answer: "50, since frequency = frequency density × class width = 2.5 × 20 = 50." } },
  ],
};

